{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T02:18:11.228020Z","id":"374b05d63963b97ec19c86caf1edc13dba69dc03950020f50b1ac20e4527cbae","body":{"detail":"At 3674d008e, `bazel test --test_env=CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` (darwin-sandbox) fails all six golden tests with `called Result::unwrap() on an Err value: Os { code: 1, kind: PermissionDenied }` at golden.rs:53 and leaves the goldens untouched (mtimes unchanged), so the default configuration is safe; .bazelrc sets --incompatible_strict_action_env and no --test_env for the variable, so a plain `bazel test` (CI included) never sees it. Two things remain. (1) The panic names neither the golden nor the way that works (`bazel run //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test`, which was run with the variable set and rewrote all twelve goldens in the source tree with identical bytes). (2) Not run, by reading: under an unsandboxed test strategy the same command has a working directory where crates/clax-bot/src is a directory of runfiles symlinks, so fs::write would follow them into the source tree and every golden test would pass without comparing anything. Any value of the variable, the empty string and 0 included, turns comparison off (var_os(..).is_some(), golden.rs:52), as in the other crates' golden helpers.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":15},"end":{"line":25},"content_hash":"b69b9e65a25425b478660a7bad97b96ef6b0c39686e1d523e5013e796d885371"},"suggested_fix":"When the variable is set and BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY is not, refuse under Bazel (TEST_SRCDIR or RUNFILES_DIR is set) with a message naming `bazel run`; and say which file could not be written instead of a bare unwrap.","summary":"With CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN set and no BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY, src_dir() resolves to the test's runfiles, whose files are symlinks to the source tree: only the sandbox stops a `bazel test` from rewriting the goldens","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T02:18:11.228094Z","id":"836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1","body":{"detail":"request_message (prompt.rs:127-150) writes `doc[\"brief\"] = json!(cx.brief)` and an array of cx.threads whatever they hold. a_brief_request always has Some(brief) and a_thread_request has two threads, so a rewrite of the assembly that left out a null brief or an empty threads array (serde's skip_serializing_if on typed structs, say) would keep both goldens byte for byte. Null handling is pinned elsewhere in the same documents (description, url, for, a thread's anchor), which is why this is small. Worth a third golden only if a host can produce either context: check the server's BotHost::context before adding one.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":289},"end":{"line":333},"content_hash":"8fa0ad18e7ecd85e21ac80c17cf14956194aafeca3828ab3a33f0f69c9be6e5f"},"summary":"The request-message goldens do not pin a brief request without a brief (`\"brief\": null`) or a thread request with no threads (`\"threads\": []`)","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T02:30:03.836810Z","id":"14ffc1bf0d8278fb5ef87f01f9dab664b9195aaa08e2caf9daafc6a8650b0703","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c378d8c0e","summary":"Fixed: with CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN set and no BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY, source_path (crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs) panics when TEST_SRCDIR or RUNFILES_DIR is set, before any write, naming the golden and the bazel run that rewrites it; a failed write names its file. Evidence: `bazel test --test_env=CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test //crates/clax-bot:integration` exits 3 with 'test result: FAILED. 109 passed; 6 failed' and '103 passed; 23 failed', each failure 'CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN is set under `bazel test`, which cannot rewrite the golden crates/clax-bot/src/api.d.ts.golden in the source tree and would compare nothing. Rewrite it with `CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 bazel run //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test`…', and git status shows no golden changed; `CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 bazel run //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` still rewrites them (115 passed, no diff). Any value of the variable still turns comparison off, as in the other crates' helpers.","supersedes":"374b05d63963b97ec19c86caf1edc13dba69dc03950020f50b1ac20e4527cbae","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T02:30:04.007806Z","id":"bf6c1034fbccdcaf93c0339f17e70a7061d950df9897c55ffe35a1a64e75d4b6","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1","summary":"Neither shape can reach request_message from the server's host, so neither is pinned. ServerHost::context (crates/clax-server/src/bot.rs:592-628) builds threads from run.thread_ids, and every place that starts a run names at least one thread: check_threads refuses an empty list (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs:1133-1139), follow-ups pass one thread (routes/bot.rs:946-953, 1047-1054), and a brief's run is started on the brief's own thread (routes/artifacts.rs:891-898). A thread deleted before the first turn makes ServerHost::thread answer not_found (bot.rs:257-268), which stops the run request_gone (pinned by crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/ending.stopped.request-gone.json) and sends no message. A brief is the first comment of that thread (bot.rs:603-610): it is None only for a thread with no comment, and comments are deleted only with their thread or artifact (crates/clax-store/src/threads.rs:2207, artifacts.rs:865). FakeBotHost can hold either shape, since its context is whatever a test sets.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T02:46:48.099655Z","id":"de26bb5bf60b8bf342126b5b02800f83adc62d340c019b4dca1d3428254eea49","body":{"detail":"request_message (prompt.rs:127-150) writes `doc[\"brief\"] = json!(cx.brief)` and an array of cx.threads whatever they hold. a_brief_request always has Some(brief) and a_thread_request has two threads, so a rewrite of the assembly that left out a null brief or an empty threads array (serde's skip_serializing_if on typed structs, say) would keep both goldens byte for byte. Null handling is pinned elsewhere in the same documents (description, url, for, a thread's anchor), which is why this is small. Worth a third golden only if a host can produce either context: check the server's BotHost::context before adding one. (Re-anchored unchanged from record 836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1, issued 2026-11-11T02:18:11.228094Z by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai) on lines 289-333 of the file at git:1abd5e29d: the same text, a_brief_request and the brief request's golden test, is now lines 265-310.)","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":265},"end":{"line":310},"content_hash":"f19f86e945b3614d878d234c0d1242da1d4a6e2016b1024f73ea511c681a5a50"},"summary":"The request-message goldens do not pin a brief request without a brief (`\"brief\": null`) or a thread request with no threads (`\"threads\": []`)","supersedes":"836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T03:01:14.442170Z","id":"809348e8ec2f1c02a280dc2c3fc24ad00f96a3476a2ce3695a9b53b63e34ec82","body":{"detail":"first_difference compares by line. With the request body's `model` key moved to the front in crates/clax-sample/src/transport.rs:111 (same JSON, another key order; reverted after), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails a_thread_run_is_pinned and a_brief_run_is_pinned on the six request.*.wire.json goldens alone (the readable request.*.json goldens pass, as they should), and each failure is `First difference: line 1`, then the golden's 15 KB line, the new 15 KB line, and the whole text again under `The whole text now:`; the test log of that run is 300 KB. A developer cannot see the difference without diffing by hand, which invites rewriting the golden unread.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":59},"end":{"line":106},"content_hash":"c0106d5e0da8487fa7bd999299c6f5223546612eac283db3937537f607d3a6f1"},"suggested_fix":"For a differing line longer than about 200 bytes, give the byte offset of the first difference and some 80 bytes each side from both texts; and leave out `The whole text now:` when the text is over a few kilobytes (the rewrite command gives it).","summary":"A wire golden is one line of 14 to 17 KB, so its mismatch says `First difference: line 1` and prints both whole lines: the message does not show what changed","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T03:25:15.665880Z","id":"0242d84175263f4189aa8ab114772a66c9053e9615d3ff3d8adef8d79caff68a","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:517417cba","summary":"Fixed: first_difference gives, for a differing line over 200 bytes, the byte of the first difference and 80 bytes of each side around it, and mismatch leaves a text over 4 KB out of the message (unit tests a_long_line_that_differs_is_shown_around_its_first_difference and a_short_line_that_differs_is_shown_whole in crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs). Evidence: with one space added to the request body's bytes in crates/clax-sample/src/transport.rs (`\"stream\": true`; reverted after), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails a_thread_run_is_pinned and a_brief_run_is_pinned on the four request.*.wire.json goldens alone, request.thread.1.wire.json as `First difference: line 1, byte 1017 of it (the golden's line is 14918 bytes, the line now 14919)` followed by 80 bytes of each side around `\"stream\":true`; the test log of that run is 15 KB, where the record measured 300 KB.","supersedes":"809348e8ec2f1c02a280dc2c3fc24ad00f96a3476a2ce3695a9b53b63e34ec82","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T03:25:17.950279Z","id":"d34643f66ce0337a9d109d8fde6fa71105962eb5d9ea1d59247a2047eab390e9","body":{"detail":"Not taken so that the variable means one thing in the repository: the three other places that read it also test only that it is set (crates/clax-core/src/icon.rs:838, crates/clax-server/src/routes/guards.rs:2307, crates/clax-store/src/toolpath/tests.rs:176). Changing this helper alone would make CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=0 rewrite some goldens and compare others.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":133},"end":{"line":133},"content_hash":"1f927b95e7a46d88ea74a8245c8ddebba2a8e6d29f605b061f92ebc98f6f6122"},"summary":"Only CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 (or a truthy value) turns rewriting on; today any value does, 0 included","tags":["revisit:the other readers of CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN are changed to test its value","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T05:05:01.708028Z","id":"7f3b524b48ee1a305a4c644614b739437c544600c8dc31b72b22ae24fabe8c22","body":{"detail":"`DEFINITION` is read with `value_nodes: usize::MAX` so that `api::check_args` accepts what it accepted before (arguments are bounded by the sandbox's `max_call_args_bytes`). Nothing calls `Operation::check_answer`, `check_result` or a `Kind`'s checks on this definition yet. When something does (a provider's answer, a request from a starter), those values come from outside the sandbox and the same definition would check them without a bound on work.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":56},"content_hash":"f0d86b8b8253db82edb376dcd2edaa5c841f93d15a2f128c90e4841a782067f6"},"suggested_fix":"When answers or requests are first checked against this definition: bound them by size before the check, or give `clax-surface` a budget per check rather than per definition.","summary":"Clax's own definition has no value budget, which is right for a call's arguments and not for a provider's answer","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T05:37:41.316310Z","id":"773d144058403ed700942acbb1349c649deb6c5bd0c0ab8d0c5ab8023973b53c","body":{"detail":"Method: read crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts under `Limits { value_nodes: n, ..Limits::default() }` and find the least n under which `Operation::check_args` accepts the costliest argument list whose JSON fits 256 KiB, which is `clax_bot_sandbox::Limits::default().max_call_args_bytes` (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs:216) and what every run gets: `RunSettings::limits` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:183-190) sets cpu, memory and calls and takes the rest from the default; no setting raises it. Least n: draft.publish with `[{\"addresses\": [\"\", …]}]` (about 87,375 empty strings, 262,142 bytes) 87,378; db.update with a `Record` of about 29,600 keys (262,141 bytes) 29,615; db.query the same 29,613; db.set with any `data` 3 (`unknown` is not walked: value.rs `Node::Unknown => Entered::Fits`); files.write 2. A unit costs at least 3 bytes of arguments in this file's parameter types (no parameter has a union that is tried alternative by alternative), so 1,000,000 units need about 3 MiB. Time in an optimised build for those lists: 0.27 to 0.76 ms a check, of which 0.25 to 0.77 ms is the clone of the accepted values; the table's check before it took 0.26 to 1.0 ms on the same lists.","kind":"comment","references":"7f3b524b48ee1a305a4c644614b739437c544600c8dc31b72b22ae24fabe8c22","suggested_fix":"Read the file under `Limits::default()` and add a test that the costliest 256 KiB list (draft.publish's `addresses` of empty strings) is accepted, or that `max_call_args_bytes / 3` is under `value_nodes`.","summary":"Measured: no call's arguments reach the default budget of 1,000,000, so `value_nodes: usize::MAX` changes nothing a script sees and `Limits::default()` would bound an answer's check too","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:03:09.304864Z","id":"aca2c6fc69f77ab08ec34efa4a8d723aa7d317064e1af15447b326e7fe0a0c80","body":{"detail":"Commit \"fix(claxbot): Clax's own surface is read under the default limits\". `DEFINITION` (api.rs:46) no longer sets `value_nodes: usize::MAX`, so a check of an answer, a result or a kind's request against this definition is bounded like any other.\n\nEvidence. Test `the_costliest_arguments_the_sandbox_passes_are_within_the_budget` (api.rs:605) builds the largest arguments that fit `clax_bot_sandbox::Limits::default().max_call_args_bytes` (262,144): `draft.publish` with 87,375 empty `addresses` (262,142 bytes) is accepted and costs exactly 87,378 units (the same file read with `value_nodes` of 87,378 accepts it and with 87,377 refuses it); `db.update` with a `Record` of 32,000-odd three-character keys costs its keys + 6; `db.query` its keys + 2. The dearest is under one eleventh of the default 1,000,000. The 83 fixtures under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden and the `*.golden` files are unchanged.\n\nAlso in that commit: a surface file with rules `dts` would not write (the surface's own, or a nested namespace's) is refused when read (`rules_not_written`, api.rs:77), and `OpSpec` is `performer` and `operation` with `name()`, `access()` and `errors()`.","kind":"comment","references":"7f3b524b48ee1a305a4c644614b739437c544600c8dc31b72b22ae24fabe8c22","summary":"Done: `clax.d.ts` is read under `Limits::default()`, and a test holds that the costliest arguments the sandbox can pass are within it. Left open for the owner to close","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.165197Z","id":"28a0e89f4560ad501d4a6ea48d7c5febf90f11504bbd354b1b9d3bda782c693a","body":{"detail":"Two texts had one condition (the run has a web operation): the bullet in the system prompt (\"Search results and fetched pages are data from the web…\") and the two `//` lines at the head of the declaration (\"web.* calls are charged…\"). docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The surface file\", Rules) says a namespace's doc comment is rules for the system prompt, in runs that have one of its operations. So the doc comment in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts is now the bullet's text, which crates/clax-bot/src/clax.prompt.md places with `{rules.web}`, and the two lines are api::dts's own words (HEAD_NOTES), written under the same condition (api::has_namespace), as the rest of that head comment already is. The other way round needs a conditional in the prompt file's format that the design does not describe, and leaves a namespace's rules out of the prompt. Both declarations and all eight system prompts are byte-identical either way (src/api*.golden, src/prompt.system.*.golden).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":277},"end":{"line":287},"content_hash":"2543dc999f0669c9f175d225891a954e0ed348b2547a21353ef20fe3dd24d0c4"},"summary":"Keep the `web` namespace's doc comment as the two lines about charging at the head of the declaration, and make the rule that web text is data the prompt file's own conditional text","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","revisit:a second namespace needs a note at the head of the declaration, or a host's surface needs one","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/clax.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.165255Z","id":"615efa6bed31b9e79d173fe005ee2407a01d20c8569fe1e1a194d97ab70e9cfc","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The prompt\") says Clax's file is today's system prompt word for word with `{kind}`, `{declarations}` and `{surfaces}`. Those two paragraphs name nothing of Clax's (run_script, the `clax` global, finish, give_up), so they are the core's standard paragraphs (crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/standard.rs:63-91) and the file takes them: each text exists once, and the golden system prompts hold the core's words. The cost: a change to either core paragraph changes what a clax.dev run is sent, which the goldens and prompt::reference then refuse. The rule about whose words are requests stays spelled out in the file (line 19), since it names comments, `from_sender` and the brief, which `{core.data}` cannot.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":12},"end":{"line":22},"content_hash":"e4afb407bcc0b9936e1370e4afc86446e27d8e1224ccae91dc1c08c8cfd073fe"},"summary":"Spell the code-mode paragraph and the finish bullet out in clax.prompt.md, as the design's \"word for word\" reads, instead of taking `{core.code_mode}` and `{core.finish}`","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","revisit:the core's finish paragraph changes for a typed finish, or a host's prompt needs a core paragraph worded differently from Clax's","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.165318Z","id":"a5bb67ca31ddc8b194ae18cb17897153f55cc3cbe25dcc3ed3d88b575ea1ad34","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The prompt\") lists `{kind}`, `{declarations}`, `{surfaces}` (each *other* binding's rules) and `{core.…}`. It gives no slot for a namespace's rules, which \"The surface file\" says appear only in runs that have one of the namespace's operations. crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs adds `{rules.<namespace>}`, which a prompt file places by name; prompt.rs (`read`) refuses Clax's file when a ruled namespace has no slot. A surface with no prompt file cannot name its namespaces in the standard file, and its own const's rules have no slot either, so today such rules would reach no prompt. Also, slots `{core.x}` and `{rules.x}` share a namespace with a surface's own slots (`{clax.page_contract}`): a surface named `core` or `rules` would collide (file.rs:229-250 resolves own slots first). The step that registers a host's surface needs to settle: a `{rules}` slot for all of the owner's rules, or the owner rendered under `{surfaces}` too, and reserving `core` and `rules` as surface names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6},"end":{"line":20},"content_hash":"b98aeab93a26c6d260a1ad3fe58ed5b816c572e2b4bb5e32a9f0cb4b744b4892"},"summary":"The standard prompt has no place for the rules of the surface that owns the kind, nor for its namespaces' rules, and the design's slot table names none","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.165411Z","id":"4a7f8cd6c4d1dc8b3d5b435d6c314a7484507e60aa19747b63f70f1aa79a509c","body":{"detail":"A piece is the prompt file (`prompt:clax`) or what one slot became, hashed as substituted (so `declarations` is api::dts without its last line ending). With one binding that is the design's list (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"The prompt\", last paragraph). When a run has a host's surface, `{surfaces}` and `{declarations}` each hold several bindings' text, and the change that stores pieces must decide whether to split them by binding. Nothing stores the pieces yet, and nothing keeps texts by hash: the test `a_system_prompt_is_rebuilt_from_its_pieces` stands in for that store. The request message's opener is covered by the file's hash; the request's JSON is not a piece.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":145},"end":{"line":240},"content_hash":"4a4de8f3ccd73a8771e1301586d083f582a5d846edb6a28f102a980f798d28c8"},"summary":"`system_pieces` gives one `declarations` piece and would give one `surfaces` piece, where the design says a run records each binding's rules and declaration; and it covers the system prompt only","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:deferred","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.166099Z","id":"2166f1dd86719fe1407ea1e57a965509f07dd9b2a8b6123ec9da920c359fdcc3","body":{"detail":"request_message (prompt.rs:127-150) writes `doc[\"brief\"] = json!(cx.brief)` and an array of cx.threads whatever they hold. a_brief_request always has Some(brief) and a_thread_request has two threads, so a rewrite of the assembly that left out a null brief or an empty threads array (serde's skip_serializing_if on typed structs, say) would keep both goldens byte for byte. Null handling is pinned elsewhere in the same documents (description, url, for, a thread's anchor), which is why this is small. Worth a third golden only if a host can produce either context: check the server's BotHost::context before adding one. (Re-anchored unchanged from record 836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1, issued 2026-11-11T02:18:11.228094Z by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai) on lines 289-333 of the file at git:1abd5e29d: the same text, a_brief_request and the brief request's golden test, is now lines 265-310.)","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":398},"end":{"line":443},"content_hash":"f19f86e945b3614d878d234c0d1242da1d4a6e2016b1024f73ea511c681a5a50"},"summary":"The request-message goldens do not pin a brief request without a brief (`\"brief\": null`) or a thread request with no threads (`\"threads\": []`)","supersedes":"de26bb5bf60b8bf342126b5b02800f83adc62d340c019b4dca1d3428254eea49","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.310724Z","id":"0a91c5b848bce028a377dfcc40835af6739726a313fa5a6f6729182e7263e03e","body":{"detail":"`DEFINITION` is read with `value_nodes: usize::MAX` so that `api::check_args` accepts what it accepted before (arguments are bounded by the sandbox's `max_call_args_bytes`). Nothing calls `Operation::check_answer`, `check_result` or a `Kind`'s checks on this definition yet. When something does (a provider's answer, a request from a starter), those values come from outside the sandbox and the same definition would check them without a bound on work.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":48},"end":{"line":58},"content_hash":"487c0c792e8303ed2a840f835863f6bf245652d56a676dd6548af606526a4ae8"},"suggested_fix":"When answers or requests are first checked against this definition: bound them by size before the check, or give `clax-surface` a budget per check rather than per definition.","summary":"Clax's own definition has no value budget, which is right for a call's arguments and not for a provider's answer","supersedes":"7f3b524b48ee1a305a4c644614b739437c544600c8dc31b72b22ae24fabe8c22","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:25:29.529169Z","id":"99eadaecc6f0eef7ab9cf0bf342fd7bcc3e9d08b823fa1ed31d7588fefc6010f","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"2166f1dd86719fe1407ea1e57a965509f07dd9b2a8b6123ec9da920c359fdcc3","summary":"Both shapes are now compared, though not pinned in a golden: crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/reference.rs (the_request_message_is_the_reference_for_every_shape_of_request) renders request_message for a brief request with `brief: None` and a thread request with no threads, among 640 shapes, and requires the bytes the code before clax.prompt.md wrote. That holds until the plan's T10 removes prompt::reference; a third golden is still the lasting pin.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:39:19.657637Z","id":"92a87926296a39391c0c499e57514f8522a84d62f6755ac9b9b1a120358e45cc","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs:227 matches `Known::own` before the `core.` and `rules.` prefixes (file.rs:229-250). Parsing with `own: [\"core.data\", \"rules.web\"]` reads `{core.data}` and `{rules.web}` as the surface's own slots, so a surface's code can stand its own text where a check for `{core.data}` (the design's `surface check` warning) would see the core's paragraph. A surface's name is `[a-z][a-z0-9]*` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs:537-541), and neither `core` nor `rules` is refused (check.rs:20-43, 554-575). A naming that cannot collide: (a) a name with no `.` is the format's (`kind`, `declarations`, `surfaces`, `opener`, `request`, and `rules` if one slot is to hold all the owner's rules); (b) `core.<name>` and `rules.<namespace>` are the core's, and `core` and `rules` join the names no surface may take, for every author, beside TypeScript's reserved words at check.rs:554; (c) any other name must be `<surface>.<name>` with `<surface>` the surface the file is read for, which `PromptFile::parse` checks from a new `Known::surface`. Kinds never appear in a slot's name (an opener's kind is in its header), and piece names with `:` (`kind:thread`, `prompt:clax`, prompt.rs:161-181) cannot be a slot's name, since a slot's alphabet has no `:`. For the owner's rules: one `{rules}` slot (the const's doc comment, then each ruled namespace's that the run has an operation of and the file does not place by name) lets the standard prompt carry them and makes `api::rules_not_written` unnecessary.","kind":"comment","references":"a5bb67ca31ddc8b194ae18cb17897153f55cc3cbe25dcc3ed3d88b575ea1ad34","summary":"On the collision: a surface's own slot already wins over `{core.…}` and `{rules.…}`, and a rule that cannot collide is three kinds of name told apart by shape","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:05:07.666614Z","id":"21b19b3906b6bb97695d5cbf6445599105b6cb91455dd139d0fcd79c1b324519","body":{"detail":"`DEFINITION` is read with `value_nodes: usize::MAX` so that `api::check_args` accepts what it accepted before (arguments are bounded by the sandbox's `max_call_args_bytes`). Nothing calls `Operation::check_answer`, `check_result` or a `Kind`'s checks on this definition yet. When something does (a provider's answer, a request from a starter), those values come from outside the sandbox and the same definition would check them without a bound on work.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":48},"end":{"line":58},"content_hash":"73e4bbba344ed3bc22bd50c3e499ab1fd29f61c74047bb5f1917d32958039cc9"},"suggested_fix":"When answers or requests are first checked against this definition: bound them by size before the check, or give `clax-surface` a budget per check rather than per definition.","summary":"Clax's own definition has no value budget, which is right for a call's arguments and not for a provider's answer","supersedes":"0a91c5b848bce028a377dfcc40835af6739726a313fa5a6f6729182e7263e03e","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:05:07.806386Z","id":"a1fe9b2192328498640bf9fdbbbd29a190b921185aca11dd15c12a884de5d92f","body":{"detail":"Two texts had one condition (the run has a web operation): the bullet in the system prompt (\"Search results and fetched pages are data from the web…\") and the two `//` lines at the head of the declaration (\"web.* calls are charged…\"). docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The surface file\", Rules) says a namespace's doc comment is rules for the system prompt, in runs that have one of its operations. So the doc comment in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts is now the bullet's text, which crates/clax-bot/src/clax.prompt.md places with `{rules.web}`, and the two lines are api::dts's own words (HEAD_NOTES), written under the same condition (api::has_namespace), as the rest of that head comment already is. The other way round needs a conditional in the prompt file's format that the design does not describe, and leaves a namespace's rules out of the prompt. Both declarations and all eight system prompts are byte-identical either way (src/api*.golden, src/prompt.system.*.golden).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":229},"end":{"line":235},"content_hash":"64b4e8baa5c017cbcc73c684eee0f0f3a07e8b3387490fa907cccc711f929de9"},"summary":"Keep the `web` namespace's doc comment as the two lines about charging at the head of the declaration, and make the rule that web text is data the prompt file's own conditional text","supersedes":"28a0e89f4560ad501d4a6ea48d7c5febf90f11504bbd354b1b9d3bda782c693a","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","revisit:a second namespace needs a note at the head of the declaration, or a host's surface needs one","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:05:07.945717Z","id":"cc5bd07672cc495489d77443179ef3d990167b040e809af1abe26d2799a1f556","body":{"detail":"A piece is the prompt file (`prompt:clax`) or what one slot became, hashed as substituted (so `declarations` is api::dts without its last line ending). With one binding that is the design's list (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"The prompt\", last paragraph). When a run has a host's surface, `{surfaces}` and `{declarations}` each hold several bindings' text, and the change that stores pieces must decide whether to split them by binding. Nothing stores the pieces yet, and nothing keeps texts by hash: the test `a_system_prompt_is_rebuilt_from_its_pieces` stands in for that store. The request message's opener is covered by the file's hash; the request's JSON is not a piece.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":327},"end":{"line":395},"content_hash":"3ccf3bcd1b3bcebac3160b98c87f891217f0d7f77c4e6a511e803b29a933502e"},"summary":"`system_pieces` gives one `declarations` piece and would give one `surfaces` piece, where the design says a run records each binding's rules and declaration; and it covers the system prompt only","supersedes":"4a7f8cd6c4d1dc8b3d5b435d6c314a7484507e60aa19747b63f70f1aa79a509c","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:deferred","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:05:08.083456Z","id":"3fed56b7530c3d5519b906b0de919d1234f44b8d7bf8d1cec2d815d65ce56d26","body":{"detail":"request_message (prompt.rs:127-150) writes `doc[\"brief\"] = json!(cx.brief)` and an array of cx.threads whatever they hold. a_brief_request always has Some(brief) and a_thread_request has two threads, so a rewrite of the assembly that left out a null brief or an empty threads array (serde's skip_serializing_if on typed structs, say) would keep both goldens byte for byte. Null handling is pinned elsewhere in the same documents (description, url, for, a thread's anchor), which is why this is small. Worth a third golden only if a host can produce either context: check the server's BotHost::context before adding one. (Re-anchored unchanged from record 836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1, issued 2026-11-11T02:18:11.228094Z by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai) on lines 289-333 of the file at git:1abd5e29d: the same text, a_brief_request and the brief request's golden test, is now lines 265-310.)","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":609},"end":{"line":654},"content_hash":"f5b774d706c8f3660078a15025ca27f521cf5e11dabd2d1e3b90c4f66a9633ef"},"summary":"The request-message goldens do not pin a brief request without a brief (`\"brief\": null`) or a thread request with no threads (`\"threads\": []`)","supersedes":"2166f1dd86719fe1407ea1e57a965509f07dd9b2a8b6123ec9da920c359fdcc3","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:05:08.642759Z","id":"d85c7b72a248400637b754a1e59f1358b901713eb69f6f94038bdb906520c360","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The prompt\") lists `{kind}`, `{declarations}`, `{surfaces}` (each *other* binding's rules) and `{core.…}`. It gives no slot for a namespace's rules, which \"The surface file\" says appear only in runs that have one of the namespace's operations. crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs adds `{rules.<namespace>}`, which a prompt file places by name; prompt.rs (`read`) refuses Clax's file when a ruled namespace has no slot. A surface with no prompt file cannot name its namespaces in the standard file, and its own const's rules have no slot either, so today such rules would reach no prompt. Also, slots `{core.x}` and `{rules.x}` share a namespace with a surface's own slots (`{clax.page_contract}`): a surface named `core` or `rules` would collide (file.rs:229-250 resolves own slots first). The step that registers a host's surface needs to settle: a `{rules}` slot for all of the owner's rules, or the owner rendered under `{surfaces}` too, and reserving `core` and `rules` as surface names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":7},"end":{"line":24},"content_hash":"c05eab969ef88e2164a2f847732ece5f99b8fd49abe42ac8f5fcd2022e82a203"},"summary":"The standard prompt has no place for the rules of the surface that owns the kind, nor for its namespaces' rules, and the design's slot table names none","supersedes":"a5bb67ca31ddc8b194ae18cb17897153f55cc3cbe25dcc3ed3d88b575ea1ad34","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:06:14.339511Z","id":"e16f8549c1bd511596e0b59115defcc0dd0da70ad3c8e04c8abc8e075bd303e8","body":{"detail":"`{rules}` (crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs, `Prompt::rules` in crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs) is the surface's own rules, then, in declaration order, those of each namespace the run has an operation of (one inside another too) that the file does not place with `{rules.<namespace>}`; crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md:17 has it. `Prompt::read` refuses a file without `{rules}` when the surface has rules of its own, a ruled namespace inside another, or a ruled namespace directly on it that the file does not place by name; `api::rules_not_written` is removed, its three cases now in prompt.rs `rules_the_prompt_file_has_no_place_for_refuse_it`, which also reads the standard prompt for each. Names: a slot with no `.` is the format's; `core.<name>` and `rules.<namespace>` are the core's and matched first; any other is `<surface>.<name>` for `Known::surface`, and `Known::check` refuses an own slot of any other name and a surface named `core` or `rules` (file.rs `a_slot_with_a_dot_is_the_cores_or_the_surfaces_own`, prompt.rs `no_prompt_file_is_read_for_a_surface_named_core_or_rules` and `a_slot_of_a_surfaces_own_is_supplied_by_its_code`). An own slot is listed with the function that supplies it (`Own` in prompt.rs), so none reaches rendering without one. The design's table (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"The prompt\") lists the new slots and the naming rule. Not done here: clax-surface still reads `declare const core: {};` and `declare const rules: {};` (the second test above depends on it). The two names belong among those no surface may take, for every author, in `surface` of crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs beside the reserved words; when that lands, the check in `Known::check` is a second line of defence and that test's fixtures need another way in. Evidence: `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test and integration, three runs each).","kind":"comment","references":"d85c7b72a248400637b754a1e59f1358b901713eb69f6f94038bdb906520c360","span":{"start":{"line":7},"end":{"line":24},"content_hash":"c05eab969ef88e2164a2f847732ece5f99b8fd49abe42ac8f5fcd2022e82a203"},"summary":"Done in clax-bot: `{rules}` holds the owning surface's rules and the standard prompt has it; slot names cannot collide; no prompt file is read for a surface named `core` or `rules`. Still to do, in clax-surface: refuse those two names when a surface file is read","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:06:14.340325Z","id":"0eb300ccacf6bda184adf820d4f436071b5206a59cab9ba147923fc4dc127e2e","body":{"detail":"`HEAD_NOTES` was a table of one entry whose key, `web`, nothing checked against the definition. `dts` in crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs now writes `WEB_HEAD_NOTE` when `shape.any()`. src/api.d.ts.golden and src/api.web.d.ts.golden are unchanged, and `api::has_namespace` is removed (the prompt asks its own surface, `Prompt::has_operation_of` in crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs). A second namespace with a note, or a host's surface with one, is still this alternative's condition to revisit.","kind":"comment","references":"a1fe9b2192328498640bf9fdbbbd29a190b921185aca11dd15c12a884de5d92f","span":{"start":{"line":229},"end":{"line":235},"content_hash":"64b4e8baa5c017cbcc73c684eee0f0f3a07e8b3387490fa907cccc711f929de9"},"summary":"The table this alternative was anchored on is gone: the note is one constant, `WEB_HEAD_NOTE`, written when the run's web shape has an operation","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:06:14.340429Z","id":"0f7d6f9bc4dae0250bacecabe054f425804a06373a32d9e11f1567ee70f5980b","body":{"detail":"`Prompt::kind` panics with `the surface `<name>` has no kind `<kind>``. Today the only caller is Clax's own (`render_system` and `request_message`, whose kinds are `RequestKind`'s two, both in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts, as the system goldens hold). The step that renders a prompt for a registered host's run takes the kind's name from stored data, where a panic would end the process's task instead of failing the run.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":267},"end":{"line":277},"content_hash":"ea8205afcab062bbc634658b7c3f3faaf42f6ca34fc24bf0fb4eaabaed640421"},"suggested_fix":"When a host's run is rendered, resolve the kind once where the run is made (a `&Kind` in `Run` in place of its name), so that rendering cannot fail.","summary":"`Prompt::system` and `Prompt::request` panic for a kind the surface does not have, which is right for Clax's two kinds and not for a run of a host's surface","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:14:33.810489Z","id":"4b9eece38ede23e8d5fdfebf691bfd79a9a637370a8d2ca44761ab65eb9f9760","body":{"detail":"`Prompt::has_operation_of` tells a namespace's operations by the prefix `<surface>.<path>.` (or `<path>.` for the surface named `clax`); the last `.` is what keeps `web` from matching `webSearch.query`. Writing `format!(\"{name}.{path}\")` for `format!(\"{name}.{path}.\")` at prompt.rs:215 leaves `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` passing (155 tests, run at git:05f602913): the test surface `SHELL` in prompt.rs has `windows`, `mail$Box` and `quiet`, none a prefix of another, and Clax's own surface has one ruled namespace. With the dot dropped, a run with only `shell.windowsLog.read` would be sent the rules of `windows`, in `{rules}` and in `{rules.windows}`. The same holds for the `clax` arm at prompt.rs:214, which the system goldens hold only because no namespace of crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts begins with `web`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":210},"end":{"line":219},"content_hash":"b25a90141fea76c6bc9dcf2bf3bb4fda805436fb62bfc7096896e795a3617d44"},"suggested_fix":"Give `SHELL` a ruled namespace `win` beside `windows` (or `mail` beside `mail$Box`) and assert in `rules_are_the_surfaces_then_those_of_each_namespace_the_run_has` that a run with only `shell.windows.list` has the rules of `windows` and not of `win`.","summary":"No test holds that a namespace's rules stay out of a run that has only operations of a sibling namespace whose name begins with its name","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:24:48.328487Z","id":"97228b1fdca309d2577d7889fc4c8b7d5981e1b9202f3feaebdc00423cc25f12","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs test `a_namespace_whose_name_begins_anothers_is_not_that_one` reads a surface with ruled namespaces `win` and `windows`, once as a host's (`demo`) and once as the surface named `clax`, and requires a run with `windows.x` alone to have the rules of `windows` only, and one with `win.x` alone those of `win` only, in `{rules}` and with both placed by name. It is a surface of its own, so the test surface `SHELL` and what crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.standard.golden holds of it are as they were. With `format!(\"{name}.{path}\")` for `format!(\"{name}.{path}.\")` in `Prompt::has_operation_of`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` fails that test alone (157 passed, 1 failed): `a run of `demo` with `demo.windows.x` alone`, left `Words.\\n\\nRules of win.\\n\\nRules of windows.…`, right `Words.\\n\\nRules of windows.…`. With `format!(\"{path}\")` in the `clax` arm it fails the same test with `a run of `clax` with `windows.x` alone`. Evidence: `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test, 158 tests, and integration, three runs each).","kind":"comment","references":"4b9eece38ede23e8d5fdfebf691bfd79a9a637370a8d2ca44761ab65eb9f9760","span":{"start":{"line":210},"end":{"line":219},"content_hash":"b25a90141fea76c6bc9dcf2bf3bb4fda805436fb62bfc7096896e795a3617d44"},"summary":"A test now holds the last dot of the prefix, for a host's surface and for one named `clax`. Left for the owner to close","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:57.336500Z","id":"6272eed6bd87c7293c2f47ee2ddc71dcfdb0a7f76b55c6933e409780b4632573","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs, `PROMPT_PREFIXES`, checked in `surface` for every author: `` `core` cannot name a surface: in a prompt file the slots `{core.…}` and `{rules.…}` are the core's, so a surface named `core` or `rules` could have no slot of its own``. Tests: crates/clax-surface/src/tests/manifest.rs, `no_surface_is_named_as_the_cores_slots_begin` (a `.d.ts` file and a manifest, as a host's and as Clax's); crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs, `no_surface_is_named_core_or_rules`, which replaces the test that read such a surface. The refusal of such a name in crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs stays, as a defence nothing that read a surface file reaches, and `standard`'s documentation (crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/standard.rs) says so. No golden changed.","kind":"comment","references":"d85c7b72a248400637b754a1e59f1358b901713eb69f6f94038bdb906520c360","summary":"Done in clax-surface: no surface is named `core` or `rules`, a host's or Clax's own, in either notation","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.629745Z","id":"887799bed2ee071ee7744b202002192cb1a617507a059f0c082b8fc00f875f90","body":{"detail":"Not taken: a provider with no store (FakeSurfaceProvider, and the wire adapter of the design's \"Providers\": \"its answer is journaled when it arrives\") would have to be handed one, and three of today's four behaviours already leave the entry to the run loop (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:393, 411, 399-401, 422-424). So an answer says who journals it (Journal), and only a write journaled in its own transaction or with its charge is the provider's (Written).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":476},"end":{"line":485},"content_hash":"472adc828981570b679cbf620537cf5d5016873ca36291e41b58f30963ac9c0e"},"summary":"Every provider journals each of its calls itself through the claim, and the core journals only its own refusals","tags":["seam","revisit:a second in-process provider needs a journal entry of its own form for a call that is not a write","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.629849Z","id":"0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e","body":{"detail":"Today the web performer refuses it and journals the row itself (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:515-528, entry_result 276-293). Behind the seam the run's binding does not have the operation (the design's \"A run\": \"An operation outside it is not declared and is refused when called\"), so `perform` asks `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` and the core journals the standard form. What does not change: the script's error (`web_disabled`, the same words, pinned by tests/golden/script.web-refusals.json), the step's `calls` entry with `cost_milli: 0` and `web` (from the answer's note), and what a replay reads (`from_entry`, web.rs:305-306, defaults both to the same values). crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, `refusals_behind_a_session_are_journaled_once_and_answered_again`, holds all four. The other choice keeps the row byte for byte: every `clax` binding lists all 24 operations and the session refuses, which makes the web grant something other than the binding's set. The owner's to choose before script.rs moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":737},"end":{"line":745},"content_hash":"c9082843a448da22a06363a34fea28d7bda6b8cf7879241af506a56925d484fc"},"summary":"Once the core refuses a web operation a run did not declare, its journal row is `{code, message}`: today's also holds `cost_milli: 0` and `web`","tags":["seam","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.629912Z","id":"3bacbc3f33a47bb5703623492d3c0d0cbb2073d4b8f9c78655db2c749ef24694","body":{"detail":"The design's correction (\"What measuring the run loop changed\", first row) names two hooks, `ending` and `ended`. Today ClaxBot is shown as working before each turn and each script on every tick (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1659), which is neither, and `BotHost::working` is to be deleted. Not taken: a session for a turn would load the draft (Draft::load, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:554-555) for a step that makes no call, and a turn would fail when the draft's base version is gone. So the seam has a third hook, which answers nothing.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":747},"end":{"line":750},"content_hash":"0e8e38876a0e224e2c3cb4f670c8509b9ccc4c269bf75a57f139b0f33a0d94e8"},"summary":"No `working` hook: a session opened for every step, turns included, with the roster shown from `begin`","tags":["seam","revisit:a parked run's roster must say what it waits on","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.629967Z","id":"7657d9ec13159aa2b01fccc9e99566824ce23af929a59593f03a6b37f64a0dda","body":{"detail":"The design's correction names only the limit's value. Today's `credits_exhausted` notice also says when the week's credits reset, from the tick's clock (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1416-1429, Deps::clock), which no provider is given; a provider's own clock can be in the next week when the tick's was not. So the core passes the instant (`credits_reset`) and the provider words it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":653},"end":{"line":677},"content_hash":"3c7a0a47247c4ae2538d7d7119f1cdd442c3e3abcf55435d462b14aabc1178bc"},"summary":"A provider works out when credits reset from a clock of its own, and `End::Stopped` carries the reason and the limit alone","tags":["seam","revisit:providers are given the run loop's clock","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.630017Z","id":"2d97356540dc238c1067a9dba33241baec7196c7a960b7110a7349ce15aeef6d","body":{"detail":"Not taken: what fails inside today's terminal step and must keep it open is a store read (`Store::web_pages`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1684), which fails the tick with a `CoreError`; a notice that cannot be posted is logged and the step commits (run.rs:1525-1527). So `ending` and `begin` answer `clax_core::Result`, and a provider logs a write it could not make. `ended` answers `Result<(), HostError>` as drawn, since the run has ended whatever it says.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":762},"end":{"line":781},"content_hash":"164e60c47080f65b9a95a54e04f9a331e28c307e6334ee5aa82c6e5c33443fd2"},"summary":"`ending` failing with a `HostError`, as the design's sketch of the trait has it","tags":["seam","revisit:a provider outside the process is given a part in the terminal step","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.630064Z","id":"485f2587b799e5d0a756141688a8310c4cfb31174e9402fb2a8d06ddc7899cf0","body":{"detail":"The places from NOTICE_SEQ (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:127) are a step's, not a binding's: today thread `i`'s notice is at `NOTICE_SEQ + i` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1514-1516), and a sources list at `NOTICE_SEQ + 1 + i` (run.rs:1694). With one binding that writes there (`clax`) nothing collides. The change that first gives a run a second provider whose `ending` journals must give each binding a range of its own.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":762},"end":{"line":781},"content_hash":"164e60c47080f65b9a95a54e04f9a331e28c307e6334ee5aa82c6e5c33443fd2"},"summary":"Two bindings whose `ending` both write would take the same journal places","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.630109Z","id":"62987c75b807df4e0709d68fc8d4d113b7be1d840041f2bd728c5c18dbcdd37c","body":{"detail":"`StepParts::payload` fields are flat names because today's are (`changed`, `replied`, `published`, `created`, `base`: crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:695-699, pinned by tests/golden/steps.thread.json), so two sessions answering one name would overwrite each other silently. `StepParts::draft` is the store's `DraftChange` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs), a row of `clax` in a core type, because `StepCommit` commits no other kind of row for a provider. Whoever first runs two sessions in one step decides whether a clash is refused or a host's fields are kept under its surface's name; a second kind of row needs the store.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":603},"end":{"line":620},"content_hash":"8524504c3f0ae4b00fdd4fc46504cf289fc9fad5b414dad2ccee630c56fa1150"},"summary":"The parts of several sessions go into one payload and one list of rows with nothing to tell the bindings apart","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.630154Z","id":"8f5ab6e74103c757f6e6995d3220871513e63f1c7376de251616a1021141d9d5","body":{"detail":"They are the three facts `Event::Call` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:84-93), a step's `calls` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:654-663) and the `bot.call` record (script.rs:648) take from a performer today, and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md:42 lists them as ambiguous. Kept as they are so that no stored or streamed byte moves; a general form belongs to the change that moves the events.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":487},"end":{"line":499},"content_hash":"f385d2e3fcd75883dae0e21ecf4479de5ddcc97311828da85ed31efd5040077b"},"summary":"`CallNote` holds what `clax`'s operations say of a call (`target`, `cost_milli`, `web`) in a type of the core","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.630198Z","id":"670a18d3c561afbf8a7deaaaa084ba151b06a1e66f76df7eabecbc0c178e3450","body":{"detail":"Today a starting driver makes up for the lost `working(false)` by starting the follow-ups that were waiting (`BotHost::start_waiting`, crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:301-307; crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:219-235). The seam has no hook for that, and the design's \"recorded with the run and delivered until the provider acknowledges it, for seven days\" (\"Providers\") is not built. The change that puts the driver's start behind the provider needs one or the other.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":783},"end":{"line":796},"content_hash":"853f033cb202ac9ecae8abad387abcc50c1bfb0780062a67e2b4efa85410b07c"},"summary":"Nothing in the seam delivers `ended` again when a crash falls between the terminal commit and the call","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:30:12.630242Z","id":"3f3fd9c10cce25a655ae83b06de7ed9523dd41417297fff40ded02ca1cb3d4b1","body":{"detail":"`BotHost::context` (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:265) is read at the first turn (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1855-1879), and the design's correction says the kind's surface resolves the stored request there. `SurfaceProvider` holds only what a step and a run's end need; a run with no `clax` binding cannot be given a prompt through it. The change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers adds it, with the prompt file.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":726},"end":{"line":736},"content_hash":"2f1ab5d130dcedc38f8902424f321b5ceb8543d22194ad71e8710717ca2240d5"},"summary":"The seam has no way for the surface that declared a run's kind to resolve the request at the first turn","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.696078Z","id":"069c3c3da1983bde6747bb6058f6e3465b288c56b92ba058fd2b13cab9dca369","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:521-528 refuses `web_disabled` (`clax.<op> is not available to this run`) in two cases: the run did not declare the operation, or it did and `Deps::web` is `None` (`Some(s) if declared => s, _ => …`). Both journal `{code, message, cost_milli: 0, web}` alone, with no `bot.web` record (`Finish::undeclared`, 149-154, and `write`, 471-479). Behind the seam only the first is `SurfaceProvider::undeclared`'s: in the second the binding has the operation, so `perform` (surface.rs 568-586) hands the call to the session, which must refuse it itself, in the same words, uncharged, uncounted and unaudited, and journal it (`Journal::Written` with today's form, or `Journal::Outcome` with the core's). crates/clax-bot/tests/web.rs:698 (`a_host_without_a_source_refuses_web_disabled`) holds the script's code for that case and nothing of its journal row. The row above (98) says a session's web call is journaled `Written`, always, with a `bot.web` record in `spend_journaled`: this refusal is the exception.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":99},"end":{"line":99},"content_hash":"bf8968ac0168eeb8b4a14e97223ce006d58991812524a819dff4b7c7a8350e8f"},"suggested_fix":"Say in row 99 that it is the undeclared half, and add the no-source half to row 98: refused by the session before check 3, journaled without a `bot.web` record. Whatever is decided for the undeclared row's form (record 0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e), decide the same for this one, so one message does not leave two row forms.","summary":"The table's row for an undeclared web operation covers half of script/web.rs 515-528: a declared operation on a host with no web source takes the same branch and is the session's","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.696787Z","id":"bd0f4ee615de3cbc74c920c555e5209cbdd242f5a0dbd097ae49b3344e29ef9e","body":{"detail":"Pins: `grep cost_milli crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/*.json` finds journal rows only in journal.web.json and resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json, all of declared calls; script.web-refusals.json holds the undeclared refusal's tool result only (`{op, ok, code}` and the thrown error), and tests/web.rs, crates/clax-server/tests/api_claxbot_run.rs and crates/clax-worker/test-bot.mjs assert the script's code, not the row. Readers of a web row's result: `from_entry` (script/web.rs 297-308), `Store::bot_web_counts` (`$.counted`, crates/clax-store/src/bot_web.rs 336-338), `sources` (`value.results`, crates/clax-bot/src/web/access.rs 310) and the stored page's `format` (web.rs 659-663); the run view and the events read a step's `calls` and the `call` event (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs 1409-1416, 1569-1573; events.rs 362-378), which the answer's note fills, never the journal. Upgrade with a run in flight: an old row `{code, message, cost_milli: 0, web}` under the seam is not read at all (`perform` asks `undeclared` again and skips the journal write for a replayed place), so the answer and the note are the same. Rollback: a new row `{code, message}` under today's code goes through `from_entry`, which answers `recorded(e)`, `cost_milli` 0 by default (305) and `web` as `shown` by default (306): the same `Performed`. Of the three choices: binding every `clax` run to all 24 operations makes `Binding::operations` no longer the run's operations (the design's \"A run\": the web grant is the intersection applied to `clax.web.*`) for the sake of an unpinned row; a core that journals a form the provider's `undeclared` supplies adds a fourth way to journal for one row. Dropping the two keys is the one with no cost found. Recommended: drop them, and pin the new row when script.rs moves (a journal golden for a run that did not declare the web).","kind":"comment","references":"0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e","span":{"start":{"line":737},"end":{"line":745},"content_hash":"c9082843a448da22a06363a34fea28d7bda6b8cf7879241af506a56925d484fc"},"summary":"Checked: no golden pins that row, nothing reads `cost_milli` or `web` from a refused call's row but `from_entry`, and both row forms replay the same under both codes; dropping the two keys changes nothing pinned","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.696845Z","id":"d9d1a8d2eb4755d3a6957801b6b02070c4e8eb50a20db7c88fbd2965166e895f","body":{"detail":"Row 3 has the core check the arguments \"against the surface's file\" and then find the binding (`route`). With one surface that is `api::check_args` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs:215), which reads Clax's own definition; with two, which file to check against is known only once the call is routed, so the order is route, check, `Binding::has`, session. `route` (267-275) answers `None` for a run with no `clax` binding calling an operation with no surface prefix (the module's own test: `route(only_fake, \"files.read\")` is `None`), and the table does not say what the script is answered or whether it is journaled; crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:497 `expect`s. And neither `SurfaceProvider` nor `Binding` leads to a `clax_surface::Definition`: `Binding::hash` is `None` for every stored binding of version 1, and `FakeSurfaceProvider::definition` is an associated function, not a trait method. A run with one fake surface and no `clax` needs the definition for the argument check, for the declaration the model reads and for the sandbox's operation list.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":48},"end":{"line":49},"content_hash":"4271412f6cede1d90e60b1488a4252b5bb0dfe4cb022a050fa12e01fb1ac71a3"},"suggested_fix":"Write the order as route, then check against that surface's file, then `has`; say a call no binding takes is answered `unknown_op` and journaled by the core as `Outcome`; and add where a definition comes from (a `definition()` method on the provider, or a registry by `Binding::hash`) before the tick holds providers.","summary":"The step table checks a call's arguments before it finds the binding, says nothing of a call no binding takes, and no part of the seam gives the core a surface's definition","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.696888Z","id":"a863ecea730730549bf118f419318f3e346534c6726a23fee8b30628ef67d235","body":{"detail":"Today's sessions depend on both. The sandbox sends calls one at a time, in the script's order (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs:44-45), and `draft.rebase` relies on it: it clones the draft, awaits, and assigns the clone back (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:254-260), which loses a write made in between. A cancelled script, or a driver that lost its claim, drops the sandbox's future (`watched`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1090-1122) and with it a call at any await, after which `finish(Cancelled)` is called on the same session, or the session is dropped. Neither is in the trait's contract, so an adapter written against it may hold state across an await that a drop leaves half-changed. Separately, \"Not here\" (135-141) puts a call that waits as \"a third answer beside a value and a failure\": `Answer::result` is `Result<Value, HostError>` and `Answer`, `CallNote` are built by struct literal outside the crate (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:110-121), so adding it changes every provider that builds or reads one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":634},"end":{"line":643},"content_hash":"f148e81b5414651af0bf4ff6577bbfc75cc7ccabb93441ecdb1637d24030bf83"},"suggested_fix":"State in `StepSession`'s documentation: calls of a step are made one at a time across all of its sessions, in place order; a call's future may be dropped at any await, and the session must then be as it was before the call or as after it. Make `Answer` and `CallNote` buildable only through constructors (`Answer::of`, `refused`, `journaled`, `naming`, and two for the cost and the shown value), so a waiting answer is a new constructor and not a new shape.","summary":"`StepSession::call` does not say that calls never overlap or that a call's future may be dropped, and `Answer` is a struct of public fields that a third outcome would change for every provider","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.696928Z","id":"15ad08586591c9690f3032928bdf4194ed6585f8eb03818b87e5421915a4a77b","body":{"detail":"The contract is: an error fails the tick and the step is begun again. Today the one such error is `Store::web_pages` in `finish` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1684), and a tick that fails that way is not counted (`note_commit_failure`, run.rs:1339, counts commit failures only), so today's run also retries without end; behind the seam the same holds for any provider's error, including one from `ending(End::Failed)`, which is the path a run takes when nothing else works (`fail`, run.rs:1606-1631, logs a notice that cannot be posted and commits). With two bindings, the first's `Err` may or may not stop the second's `ending` from being called in that attempt; each is safe to call again (its writes are journaled), but a binding that always fails then keeps every other binding's `ended` from ever coming.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":772},"end":{"line":775},"content_hash":"f79f95c6ad1066cc66456947f61b269c1c04b5b40562f34a9365eccd70455f42"},"suggested_fix":"Fix the contract before the endings move: every binding's `ending` is called in each attempt, the kind's surface first; an `Err` is counted against the step as a failed commit is, and at the limit the run ends `Failed`; `ending(End::Failed)`'s `Err` is logged and the step commits.","summary":"`ending`'s `Err` keeps the terminal step open with no bound, also for `End::Failed`, and with several bindings nothing says whether the others are still called","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.696965Z","id":"981309fee459d058f1ac74c37e04efdb1610e18af525aa00d69518b505106eba","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:333-335 (\"delivered until the provider acknowledges it, for seven days\") and 467-468 (\"`ended` is then delivered again when it next ends\") make `ended` at least once. Today's `BotHost::working(run, false)` is at least once too: `start_waiting` (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:301-307) makes up for a lost call at driver start, and the server starts follow-ups from both (crates/clax-server/src/bot.rs:1030-1066). `lifecycle` (1016-1018) fails on a second `ended`, and crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs:482-488 calls `ended` once, so a provider whose `ended` is not safe to repeat passes, and a run loop that redelivers fails the fake.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1003},"end":{"line":1024},"content_hash":"7150cc8a76f82aed3ec1e2c066a0131970e3873642abf30c698d90796e48460a"},"suggested_fix":"Have the suite call `ended` twice with the same end and require the second to succeed and change nothing the first did not; have `lifecycle` accept repeats after the terminal commit with the same end.","summary":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` and the conformance suite hold `ended` to exactly once; the design delivers it until acknowledged, and today's roster-off is already repeated","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.697Z","id":"687a9ee04f68de0d2f3066413bef6323b9dbdf7b9d8e79c956f444c1fa534ff8","body":{"detail":"(1) A failed `draft.rebase` is journaled as a failure (script.rs:393) and its entry has no `result.version`, so a replay performs the rebase again against the artifact's current version (241-252): it is not answered from the entry and may answer otherwise. (2) The tool result is trimmed to `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES` with `published` and `replay` already in it (498-530): the trim must run after the sessions' `tool_result` fields are joined, or a script with long logs that published changes bytes; no golden has that pair (script.logs-cut-to-fit.json does not publish). (3) There are two lists of calls: the model is told the sandbox's (`outcome.calls`, 491-495: calls that never reached the host are in it, `script_finished`, `invalid_args`, the call over `max_calls`), while the payload's `calls`, the `bot.call` records and the infra usage line count the host's log (646-663, 702-708). (4) After a divergence no session sees a later call, the step still commits what the sessions hold (615-621, 695-716), and the earlier attempt's rows past the divergence stay in the journal: a publish among them is in no session's parts. (5) The core reads `op`, `ok`, `attempt` and `seq` of every journal row, a provider's own included, for `unreported_calls` (run.rs:1533-1546). (6) A script is cancelled when its error is `Cancelled` or the token is set when it returns (665-669), so a script that returned may still end as `StepEnd::Cancelled`. (7) A stop decided after a turn or script step was begun (script.rs:673; run.rs:2094-2115) begins the same step again as `stopped`: `ending` gets an attempt one higher than the session had. (8) `payload.notice` is the text whenever the stop has one, and `payload.posted` is true whenever the rule held, whether or not a post succeeded (run.rs:1525-1527, 1562, 1683, 1701); the endings table says `null` \"when nothing was posted\". (9) `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:316-326) reads `changed`, `published` and `notice` from payloads: fields the provider returns, read by the core's event. (10) `working` is called before steps that then stop the run in the same tick: no key (run.rs:1659, 1669-1677), the turn's own stops (1838-1847, 1906-1909, 2094-2115), `request_gone` (1857-1873).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":101},"end":{"line":112},"content_hash":"153acd317893f254f65cc1b8a816165b1892bdb44fd8926e577fed59d2813a7f"},"summary":"What crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs and run.rs do that the mapping tables leave out, each to be carried when the code moves behind the seam","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T06:47:34.697087Z","id":"4dd970666a0d4371d66ea19e3228faa1aac32e40e86ea8546f7a899f1069a411","body":{"detail":"Every case's write succeeds: nothing holds that a refusal a session answers is journaled by the core and answered the same when the step runs again (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, \"Who journals a call\"; today a refused write is journaled, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:422-424). `finish` is only called with `StepEnd::Commit` (257-259), so \"empty after `Cancelled`\" (surface.rs 616-618) is held nowhere, here or in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs. `begin` and `ending` returning `Err` are not played. The case `an_operation_outside_the_binding_never_reaches_the_provider` also hands the operation straight to the session and requires a refusal (353-359): that makes every session check its binding a second time, which `perform` already did, and for Clax's surface it is what keeps check 2 of crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs in the session; say so in the case, or drop the bypass. The only fixture is the fake's (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs); `ClaxProvider` in tests/seam.rs is not run through `check_provider`, so whether today's behaviour passes is unknown: by reading, it would with a host write as `write` (`threads.reply`), not with a draft write, which changes nothing outside the session before the commit (the case requires `state` to change, 301-304).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":280},"end":{"line":282},"content_hash":"01e46a2aa298394b413848ddc13cabf2d392dbf3fdd4ec57c646dca63d3a38d3"},"suggested_fix":"Add a `refused` call to the fixture (journaled once, answered the same with and without its entry, state unchanged), a case that finishes with `StepEnd::Cancelled` and commits a `stopped` step, and run the suite on `ClaxProvider` when script.rs moves.","summary":"The conformance cases have no refusal by a session, no cancelled step and no failing hook, and are not run against the adapter of Clax's own surface","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.195852Z","id":"126c6c9f3872d01f7c64f85981b65cb4d733a93163a3c7bbad79c9480117d891","body":{"detail":"Row 125 is the undeclared half (`SurfaceProvider::undeclared`), row 126 the half where the run declared the operation and the host has no web source: the binding has it, the call reaches `StepSession::call`, and the session refuses in the same words with `Answer::refused(..).charged(0).showing({web})`, so the core journals it as an outcome. The Web row (124) now says `Written` \"always past check 2\". Held by crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `refusals_behind_a_session_are_journaled_once_and_answered_again` (1236): a run that declares `search` and not `fetch`, on a host with no source on both paths (`todays_deps`, 848, sets `Deps::web` to `None`; `ClaxSession::call` refuses through `web_disabled`, 118). It compares the script's value, the step's `calls` (`cost_milli: 0`, `web`), the calls that reached the host and the count of `bot.web` records (0 and 0) with today's run loop, and each of the two journal rows with today's row less `cost_milli` and `web`, and requires the keys to be exactly `code`, `message`. `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test, integration).","kind":"comment","references":"069c3c3da1983bde6747bb6058f6e3465b288c56b92ba058fd2b13cab9dca369","span":{"start":{"line":125},"end":{"line":140},"content_hash":"24138a7169af27091bcd1e8a3cb3ab134960e66c2a5a93344191a12dde11770b"},"summary":"The row is split in two (surface.rs 125, 126), both halves journal the core's `{code, message}`, and the adapter's session refuses the declared half itself","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.196286Z","id":"a9657960c4781dac29d66e01b1b2fee85f30ca33a611ec8ed716668514a1e33b","body":{"detail":"The seam's documentation states the decision and why it is safe (surface.rs 128-140): no golden pins the row (the journal rows under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/ that hold `cost_milli` are of calls past check 2); nothing reads `cost_milli` or `web` from a refused call's row but `from_entry` (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 305-306), which defaults to the same two values; a row of the old form is never read by the new code (the refusal is made again, and a place in the journal is not written twice: `dispatch`, surface.rs 926, `perform`, 968); a row of the new form reads the same under the old code. The same form holds for the half record 069c3c3d found (a declared operation on a host with no web source), so one message leaves one row form. crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 1290-1305 holds both rows against today's less the two keys. Not done here: no fixture pins the new row. The change that moves crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs behind the seam pins it in a journal fixture for a run that did not declare the web. Not closed: the choice is the owner's.","kind":"comment","references":"0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e","span":{"start":{"line":128},"end":{"line":140},"content_hash":"3cc668ec2a43213e4011979ab79021897963bd2efbd16d00202457e597158df8"},"summary":"Built as reply bd0f4ee6 recommends: the two keys are dropped, and both `web_disabled` refusals of check 2 journal `{code, message}`; left open for the owner to confirm","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.197065Z","id":"95d7c50f9764ccf28a0857d7e9427bfbcc3164c5f8c88622f067c1dd4c62d600","body":{"detail":"The contract: calls of a step are made one at a time, in place order, across all of the step's sessions, and a session may rely on it (`draft.rebase` is the example); a call's future may be dropped at any await, on a cancel or a lost claim, after which `finish(StepEnd::Cancelled)` runs or the session is dropped, so at every await the session's state is as before the call or as after it. `finish` says it is called once, after the last call returned or was dropped. `Answer` (755) is built by `Answer::of` (core journals the value), `outcome` (core journals without the value: an operation performed again on replay), `refused` (a failure for the core to journal) and `written` (the provider journaled it), with `naming`, `charged` and `showing`; it is read by `result`, `into_result`, `journal`, `note`. `CallNote` (691) is read by `target`, `cost_milli`, `extra`, `add_to`. `journaled(Journal)` is gone. \"Not here\" (surface.rs, end of the module documentation) says a call that waits is one more constructor. The suite cannot play a dropped future: nothing holds that half of the contract but the text. `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test, integration).","kind":"comment","references":"a863ecea730730549bf118f419318f3e346534c6726a23fee8b30628ef67d235","span":{"start":{"line":1136},"end":{"line":1195},"content_hash":"7146a2bd6ba25c415cd836971ff90cb08f2d605fa78d08dbc3091d4652771820"},"summary":"`StepSession::call` states both (surface.rs 1167-1186), and `Answer` and `CallNote` have private fields and named constructors","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.197452Z","id":"b3286c6e1f7e75fa9358cf66948da7659306b9ccb31fc4d46037a5ef9d80ea6b","body":{"detail":"Trait: surface.rs 1360-1373. `FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` (1619) accepts any number of `ended` after the first, each with the run ended and the same end, and no other hook after the first; the fake acts on a run's end once (`FakeSurfaceState::followed`, 1465). The suite (crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs 824-842) calls `ended` twice and requires the second to succeed and to leave `noticed` and `state` as they were. crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `the_fake_takes_ended_again_and_tells_what_else_follows_it` (521) holds three repeats, another end told (`when it was called again`) and a hook after `ended`. What delivers `ended` again is still not built (record 670a18d3). `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test, integration).","kind":"comment","references":"981309fee459d058f1ac74c37e04efdb1610e18af525aa00d69518b505106eba","span":{"start":{"line":1361},"end":{"line":1380},"content_hash":"dde17db4d13cf5b033aece1a344e97c107729cd94feca202a0893d0df4f59ee3"},"summary":"`ended` is at least once, after the terminal commit, and a repeat is harmless: stated on the trait, held by the suite, accepted by `lifecycle`","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.197814Z","id":"498d2b398988b2d9a4468968c7a2e7c5a37b886e7abdb5584252c702e52b2643","body":{"detail":"Stated: `ending` is called on each attempt of the terminal step for every binding, the kind's surface first, and one binding's `Err` does not keep another's from being called in that attempt; an `Err` counts as a failed commit of the step does today (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1332-1341 counts with `Store::note_commit_failure`, and 1634-1640 ends the run `failed` once `failures >= RunSettings::max_commit_failures`, default 3, run.rs 141): the attempt is abandoned, the failure counted, the tick fails, a later tick begins the step again; for `End::Failed` an `Err` is logged, that binding's fields are left out and the step commits. Held by the suite, for a fixture that can make its provider fail (`ProviderFixture::fail_once`): `an_ending_that_failed_is_made_again_in_the_next_attempt` (crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs 770-788) fails the fake's `ending` after it wrote its notice, begins the step again, and requires the next `ending` to answer, a third to answer the same fields and write nothing more; crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs 32 requires the notice to be applied once and `ending` to have been called in attempts 1, 2, 3. `a_begin_that_fails_leaves_the_step_to_be_begun_again` (545) does the same for `begin`. Not held anywhere: the counting, the bound and the `End::Failed` rule are the run loop's, and no run loop calls `ending` yet; the change that moves the endings must add tests for them. Today's one such error (`Store::web_pages` in `finish`, run.rs 1684) is not counted, so moving it behind `ending` under this contract changes today's unbounded retry into a bounded one: a behaviour change to make on purpose.","kind":"comment","references":"15ad08586591c9690f3032928bdf4194ed6585f8eb03818b87e5421915a4a77b","span":{"start":{"line":1315},"end":{"line":1358},"content_hash":"ce3103b866bd3ba552cfcf57aee7a78ef69869749bf51016e3b323b7e2844196"},"summary":"`ending`'s failure has a contract on the trait (surface.rs 1321-1352); the suite holds what a provider can be held to, and nothing implements the run loop's part yet","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.198180Z","id":"ab817985161b4f582ddce923db40241946e872d6b62c8c18a88e67185dc30f64","body":{"detail":"Table rows 3 to 6 (surface.rs 53-56) and the paragraph under it (60-70). `dispatch` does the three in order and journals the core's refusals as an outcome unless the place is in the journal; `route` returning `None` is answered `no_such_operation` (862: `clax.<op> is not an operation`, the words of `api::check_args`, crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 216-218). `check_args(definition, op, raw)` (876) checks against any definition, and a unit test holds it equal to `api::check_args` for Clax's own over eleven calls. `SurfaceProvider::definition` (1287) is the one addition; `Binding::from_stored` still takes a `&Definition`, which its caller takes from the provider (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 76-91). Verified for `clax`: crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 360-369 checks the arguments before `web_fresh` asks whether the run declared the operation (script/web.rs 515-528), so bad arguments to an undeclared web operation answer `invalid_args`; seam.rs `refusals_behind_a_session…` calls `clax.web.fetch(7)` in a run that did not declare `fetch` and gets `invalid_args` on both paths, with the same journal row. crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `a_call_is_routed_then_checked_then_asked_of_its_binding` (333) holds each step of the order on the fake, the journal rows, and that a refusal whose place is journaled is not written again. Not done: a definition for the declaration the model reads and for the sandbox's operation list; both can now take it from the provider. `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test, integration).","kind":"comment","references":"d9d1a8d2eb4755d3a6957801b6b02070c4e8eb50a20db7c88fbd2965166e895f","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":70},"content_hash":"c9ea0e6ce67ea36f4d3522fa31aaa6c4f1d3c8f91dfa584ab1eef14eb8ff508e"},"summary":"The order is route, check against that surface's definition, then `Binding::has`, in the table and in code (`dispatch`, surface.rs 926); a call no binding takes is `unknown_op`; a provider yields its definition","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.198549Z","id":"a1ca251185bfaab37ab5cb26d8f8fa31a49e55a9cb0f9671511ad215d910d73b","body":{"detail":"(1) the rebase row (surface.rs 120). (2), (3), (4), (7) under \"What the rows above do not show\" (158-192). (5) under \"Who journals a call\" (101-106), with the journal lookup's reads of `op` and the arguments' hash (script.rs 346-347). (6) with the cancelled script (147-149). (8) in the endings table's `finish` and stop rows. (9) at 219-223. (10) in the roster row and on `SurfaceProvider::working` (1300-1309). Checked each against crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs, run.rs and events.rs as they stand; all ten hold, with one correction to (3): the audit is not all of the host's log. The `bot.script` record's list of operations is built from the sandbox's `outcome.calls` (script.rs 635-644), so it too holds calls that never reached the host; only the `bot.call` records (646-653), the payload's `calls` (654-663) and the infra usage count (702-708) are of the host's log. And a precision on (7): the stop of a turn that begins the step again is at run.rs 2035-2047, 2081 and 2094-2115; the stops at 1838-1847 and 1906-1909 come before the turn is begun and do not raise the attempt.","kind":"comment","references":"687a9ee04f68de0d2f3066413bef6323b9dbdf7b9d8e79c956f444c1fa534ff8","span":{"start":{"line":141},"end":{"line":224},"content_hash":"348bf6b6444bd1ba2296550633ac03cbb9b3719c27e1ec83e849851eea0c60ae"},"summary":"Each of the ten is in the module documentation with its lines, checked against the code; one is put differently from this record (the `bot.script` record lists the sandbox's calls)","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.198913Z","id":"29d7b4408274172d21375e1f438c98789a6fe462ed54e005cf5fa1fb2e7e56a0","body":{"detail":"Cases added (crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs): `a_sessions_refusal_is_journaled_once_and_answered_the_same_on_replay` (483; `ProviderFixture::refused`), `a_begin_that_fails_leaves_the_step_to_be_begun_again` (545), `a_cancelled_step_keeps_what_it_wrote_and_commits_as_stopped` and `a_cancelled_step_keeps_no_write_that_waited_for_its_commit` (665; `cancelled_run`, `staged`), `an_ending_that_failed_is_made_again_in_the_next_attempt` (770), and `ended` told twice (824-842). A case that cannot be played answers `Held::NotApplicable(reason)` and stays in the results (122-128; \"Cases that do not apply\", 54-66). \"A session checks its own binding\" (39-52) says why the bypass is kept. On the fake (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs 32): 11 results, all played but the write that waits for the commit, which the fake does not have. On `ClaxProvider` (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 1489), with `threads.reply` as the write, a reply on a thread outside the request as the refusal, `web.search` as the operation outside the binding, and `files.write` as the write that waits: the nine cases that can be played hold, among them the bypass (the session refuses `web.search` itself) and the cancelled step (the draft write is not kept, the reply stands, the parts commit as `stopped`). Two are not applicable: the adapter's `begin` and `ending` cannot be made to fail from outside. What the run shows and does not prove: the adapter's `ending` and `ended` do nothing, so the terminal cases pass on it without holding anything about notices (`noticed` is empty throughout); that is for the change that moves the endings. `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all --runs_per_test=3` passes (clax_bot_lib_test, integration).","kind":"comment","references":"4dd970666a0d4371d66ea19e3228faa1aac32e40e86ea8546f7a899f1069a411","span":{"start":{"line":1},"end":{"line":80},"content_hash":"92df3adc79b64eb592f8a7a27e69455c00c7be12343d37f17778a8da13f0287f"},"summary":"The suite has the refusal, the cancelled step, the failing hooks and the repeated `ended`, says why a session checks its binding, and is run on the adapter of Clax's own surface: 9 cases played, 2 not applicable","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.199292Z","id":"f8c2a0bc55d1abe84227554fe3e11bdb889ebf7764ba1bff13e40e5588685adf","body":{"detail":"`CallNote` (surface.rs 691) holds `target`, `cost_milli` and `extra: Fields`; `CallNote::add_to` (725) adds `cost_milli` and each field of `extra` to a call's JSON object, leaving out a `null` and a name that is one of the core's (`CALL_KEYS`, 684). The unit test `a_calls_note_adds_what_todays_web_fields_add` compares its bytes with `events::with_web_fields` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs 115) over five cases. `target` and `cost_milli` stay: `Event::Call` and the `bot.call` record read them as the core's. `Event::Call` itself still has a `web` field; the change that moves the events gives it the provider's fields.","kind":"comment","references":"8f5ab6e74103c757f6e6995d3220871513e63f1c7376de251616a1021141d9d5","span":{"start":{"line":676},"end":{"line":745},"content_hash":"b2d76f81aa3a50fb9f831e5b4f9f67b09b7d2af51a2c0b412d943fcab1d065a0"},"summary":"`CallNote` no longer names the web: `web` is one of the provider's own fields (`extra`), merged into the call's JSON","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.199667Z","id":"774da557d677faea0606ae1cbc1ce8b12aedb8d052b573868f4360d873dd1fd5","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields(into, core, surface, fields)` (surface.rs 1072) with `PAYLOAD_KEYS` (1000: `calls`, `code`, `error`, `outcome`, `reason`, `result`, `summary`, `title`, `tool_use_id`, each with the line it comes from) and `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` (1015). A refused merge leaves `into` as it was and answers `FieldClash`. What one binding adds to one step is merged in one call: in the `stopped` step of a cancelled script that is the session's fields and the `ending`'s together. Two unit tests hold today's bytes for `clax`, the other-surface form, and each kind of clash. Nothing in the run loop calls it; the conformance suite commits parts through it (crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs 350, 719-724, 808-811). Still open from this record: `StepParts::draft` is `clax`'s row in a core type.","kind":"comment","references":"62987c75b807df4e0709d68fc8d4d113b7be1d840041f2bd728c5c18dbcdd37c","span":{"start":{"line":996},"end":{"line":1095},"content_hash":"e86dff335249ba43b7053e62694fa5992a639362174565d9c37880063c620443"},"summary":"The rule is stated and `merge_fields` implements it: `clax`'s fields at the top under their own names, any other surface's under the surface's name, a clash with a core name or a taken name is the provider's error","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.200025Z","id":"b109de3f92c191bb2c4b5093ce00ae4845645d10e19b434b0a66c415c175398d","body":{"detail":"`StepCx::new` (surface.rs 589) works the range out: the kind's surface from `NOTICE_SEQ`, the run's `k`th other binding, in bound order from 1, from `NOTICE_SEQ + k * NOTICE_STRIDE` (550: 1,000,000 places each); `StepCx::notice(i)` (634) answers the `i`th place, `None` outside the range. The field is private, so a context is only made with its range. With one binding, the kind's, the places are today's (`NOTICE_SEQ + i`). Held by the unit test `each_binding_has_its_own_places_for_what_its_ending_writes` and by crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `two_bindings_endings_write_at_places_of_their_own` (440), where two bindings' `ending`s each write their own notice under keys one stride apart. `unreported_calls` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1539) filters `seq < NOTICE_SEQ`, which every range is above.","kind":"comment","references":"485f2587b799e5d0a756141688a8310c4cfb31174e9402fb2a8d06ddc7899cf0","span":{"start":{"line":546},"end":{"line":640},"content_hash":"57d85815775fd45cc9d66e6cb63ea5a21ccc7e9b53e4ca7e39e34a412677e690"},"summary":"Each binding now has a range of journal places of its own for what its `ending` writes (`StepCx::notice`)","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:16:53.200380Z","id":"17f3941cb4e8d496d31eac4e7323ca667be2d4f9132ceccba5dfbbcff9983b6a","body":{"detail":"surface.rs 1360-1373 and 209-217. The conformance suite holds that a second `ended` changes nothing. The mechanism (the design's delivery until acknowledged, or today's `BotHost::start_waiting` at driver start) is still for the change that puts the driver's start behind the provider.","kind":"comment","references":"670a18d3c561afbf8a7deaaaa084ba151b06a1e66f76df7eabecbc0c178e3450","span":{"start":{"line":1361},"end":{"line":1380},"content_hash":"dde17db4d13cf5b033aece1a344e97c107729cd94feca202a0893d0df4f59ee3"},"summary":"The contract now allows redelivery (`ended` at least once, a repeat harmless); what redelivers it is still not built","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.941783Z","id":"bbf7334f83feb552fa27799b967defefbfa9cba6b94a4a8758d170bcfd8bc2af","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone to this file, passes all 175 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (the 7 of tests/seam.rs among them): (1) 649 as `if false {`: no `bot.call` record says `replayed`. (2) 685 removed: no `bot.call` record for a journal row the committing attempt did not reach (`unreported_calls`, run.rs 1533-1546). (3) 635 built from `st.calls` instead of `outcome.calls`: the `bot.script` record lists the calls that reached the host and not the sandbox's. (4) 705 as `0`: the `do.requests` line (run.rs 1080) counts no call. For contrast, 648 without the target fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned` and two more, 148 (`Event::Call` without its target) fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned`, 679 without the events fails `cancel::a_cancel_mid_script_charges_what_it_used_and_keeps_no_draft`, and 701 without the compute line fails five tests. The proof in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs builds no audit record and no usage (`Stepper`, seam.rs 471-517), and the resumed fixtures pin the step, the journal and the host's writes only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 50-57). All four are the run loop's own part of a script step (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 151-156, 168-178), which is rewritten around `Answer` and `CallNote` when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":635},"end":{"line":653},"content_hash":"202999706742add9714df1b2fce301308d48ea6cdf628a3362adb2c819a35735"},"suggested_fix":"Before this file moves: add the run's audit records and the step's usage rows to what crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs pins (a resumed step has replayed calls), pin the audit of a diverged replay whose first attempt went further than its second, pin a script with a call the sandbox refused (`script.error.call-limit.json`'s script) with its `bot.script` record, and assert the `do.requests` quantity of a script step with `infra_lines` on.","summary":"No test of clax-bot holds the `replayed` mark of a `bot.call` record, the `bot.call` records of calls no committed attempt reported, which list the `bot.script` record names, or the call count of the infra usage line","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.941865Z","id":"c9ab76da3fe4032075597a985766733746d49c6451fb9f7bafa0d12f004724d1","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451 answers any web call whose place is in the journal from its entry (`from_entry`), before `web_fresh` runs check 2 (512-529). For row 125 (the run did not declare the operation) making the refusal again is the same answer: `Run::web` does not change. For row 126 (declared, `Deps::web` is `None`) it is the same only while the host still has no source. A first attempt on a host with no source journals `web_disabled`; when the step is run again on a host that has one, today's code answers `web_disabled` from the entry, and a session written as row 126 says (check 2 made again, the entry not read) passes check 2 and runs checks 3 to 9, reaching the source for a fetch, before `Store::spend_journaled` (web.rs 497) finds the place taken. The adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs (421) cannot show this: it has no source on any attempt.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":125},"end":{"line":126},"content_hash":"69c8fa695f82e1fafb27b9c89cbe02f1e4e7fe094927285ebfeff49d195e6362"},"suggested_fix":"Row 126, Replayed: answered from the entry, as every web call the session is given with one is (web.rs 449-451); `from_entry` reads the core's `{code, message}` row as it reads today's (305-306 default the two keys). Keep `if let Some(e) = replay` first when `script/web.rs` moves into the session.","summary":"The two `web_disabled` rows say a replayed call is answered again without its entry being read; today's code reads the entry first, and for the declared half the two differ when the host gains a web source between attempts","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.941929Z","id":"b25bed990f60c83b732a878dfabcfaa1d7988f36b8f107f052a53a73e258493e","body":{"detail":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` (1629-1635) fails a repeat whose `End` is not equal to the first's. A repeat made by the tick that committed has the value in hand; one made after a restart (the case the contract names: a crash between the commit and the call) has only the run's row and the terminal step. `Run::reason` and the payload's `reason`, `code`, `summary` give `End::Finished`, `GaveUp` and `Failed` back, but a `run_cap` stop's `limit` is only inside the notice's text (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1431-1436, 1562) and a `credits_exhausted` stop's reset time is nowhere (1416-1420 computes it from the clock when the notice is written). On the provider's side, \"what the provider did for the run's end the first time, it does not do twice\" has no bound: a provider that is not idempotent by nature must keep every run it was told of for ever.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1360},"end":{"line":1379},"content_hash":"3f64798edee732ea1d89994a79f668eb1b1cdc963c0609686ff23ab3dfae95c7"},"suggested_fix":"When the endings move: either keep the `End` (and the kind's result) in the terminal step's payload so a redelivery reads it back, or say on the trait that a repeat carries the same status and reason and may lack `limit` and `credits_reset`, and make `lifecycle` compare those. Say on the trait that a provider's `ended` must be idempotent without a record of its own, or how long the run loop may redeliver.","summary":"`ended` is to be told again \"with the same end\", but nothing the store keeps of an ended run can rebuild `End::Stopped`'s `limit` and `credits_reset`, and the contract does not say for how long a provider must remember an end it acted on","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.941984Z","id":"f2d3e3a60f0d2ebd106ec326b4eee781ce1120a70b48878e049a8fde02cf06ad","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields` puts another surface's fields under the surface's name and answers `FieldClash` when that name is one of `PAYLOAD_KEYS` (`calls`, `code`, `error`, `outcome`, `reason`, `result`, `summary`, `title`, `tool_use_id`) or is taken. `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42) refuses a host's surface only the names `clax`, `runs`, `content` and the namespaces of Clax's own file (`files`, `draft`, `threads`, …). So a surface named `result` or `calls` parses, binds, and then fails each of its steps. A surface named as one of `clax`'s own payload fields (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) clashes only in a run that also has a `clax` binding; in a run without one its object lands where `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs 316-326) and `History::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 598-644) read `clax`'s fields. The module documentation (surface.rs 236-238) says a clash \"fails the tick\" and not whether the failure is counted against the step (`Store::note_commit_failure`): uncounted, the step is begun again without end.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1053},"end":{"line":1098},"content_hash":"7f2d2e91e002bf913e259ccc2e6401bb60836dc84e1a99b525df88e6abda7606"},"suggested_fix":"Reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names as surface names where a host's definition is parsed, so the clash cannot be reached by a name; and say that a `FieldClash` counts as a failed commit of the step does.","summary":"A host surface named as a field of a step's payload fails every step it adds fields to, at run time; nothing refuses the name when the surface is defined, and what a `FieldClash` does to the step is not said","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.942032Z","id":"300e461b611367d486e375bd81955311f1cbf6a8d6340ba305614b922b0f1943","body":{"detail":"`CallPlace::seq` documents the places from `NOTICE_SEQ` as the terminal step's (418-421) and `StepCx::notice` refuses an index outside its binding's stride, but `StepCx::place(seq)` checks nothing. The setting `script.max_calls` accepts 1 to 1,000,000,000,000 (crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 80, 226-229). A cancelled script's step is the step its `stopped` notices are journaled under (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673, run.rs 1558), so with a limit over 1,000,000 a script's call and a binding's notice can ask for one place of one step, and `unreported_calls` (run.rs 1539) leaves out every script call from that place up. The hazard is today's (`NOTICE_SEQ`, crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs 127) and the strides do not widen it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":621},"end":{"line":625},"content_hash":"179eac3f67fedade79f707f5f5adefc7fe40786e44d17d841edc8cb1db900886"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse a script's call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the core numbers the calls (or cap `script.max_calls` under it), and have `StepCx::place` answer `None` from `NOTICE_SEQ` up as `notice` does outside its range.","summary":"Nothing but documentation keeps a script's place under `NOTICE_SEQ`: `StepCx::place` takes any `seq`, and `script.max_calls` may be set far above 1,000,000","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.942079Z","id":"49f0a50e43f44d6cbc18b5b070b6ca0a4ec21211601cbe9c6283f45e589efd0e","body":{"detail":"(1) `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` (1289-1298) is told to answer with `Answer::refused`, but `perform` (968-986) journals its answer as the answer says: one built with `Answer::written` leaves the refusal unjournaled, with no place for the provider to have written it (the hook is given no place and no claim). (2) `charged(n)` with `n` over 0 on an answer the core journals (`of`, `outcome`, `refused`): the core's row keeps no cost, so the attempt that replays the call shows none, and nothing ties the charge to the row. Today a charge is always journaled with its row (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 491-497); only `charged(0)` goes with a core row (the two `web_disabled` refusals). (3) `Answer::of(Err(e))`, `Answer::outcome(Err(e))` and `Answer::refused(e)` journal the same row (`Claim::record`, 507-531): three spellings of one thing. None is reachable from the adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":761},"end":{"line":818},"content_hash":"8fe745503df7533cdf3125666a7b6f31d8945aaf6f91e98892a72a68a3a86d15"},"suggested_fix":"Have `perform` journal an `undeclared` answer as an outcome whatever its constructor says (or have the hook answer a `HostError` and a note, not an `Answer`); say on `charged` that a call that charged more than 0 is journaled by the provider with its charge, and assert it in `Claim::record`.","summary":"`Answer`'s constructors leave three combinations a provider can build and the core does not catch","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:32:09.942125Z","id":"e387ab56159216c0513d4f6595f2fc1713c2a6b8ca613c279109abdf171f9494","body":{"detail":"(1) 530-536, \"the refusal was journaled more than once\", cannot fail: the journal holds one row for a `(run, step, seq)` (`Store::record_bot_call`, crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs 1517-1525, keeps the row that is there). A provider that journals a refusal itself and answers `refused` is caught only when its row differs, at 501-506, in words that say the core journaled it. (2) 848-850 copies any failure of the terminal case into `an_ending_that_failed_is_made_again_in_the_next_attempt`, which comes first in the results: with `FakeSurfaceProvider::ended` changed to fail when told twice, the test panics under that name and not under `ending_is_repeatable_inside_the_terminal_step_and_ended_after_its_commit`. (3) No case reads the journal after `ProviderFixture::write`: a provider that answers `Answer::written` and writes no row passes, since the call made again with its key answers the same (414-422). The module documentation of crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs (90-93) calls that safe; the place then has no row for the divergence check or for `unreported_calls`. (4) A case is `NotApplicable` because the fixture left `staged` or `fail_once` as `None`, and the documented way to run the suite (71-75) passes it over: a provider that does stage writes, with a fixture that says it does not, passes. Both tests that run the suite assert the list of cases not played (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs 106-114, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 1569-1582); nothing requires the next fixture to. For the adapter of `clax` the two reasons read \"the fixture cannot make the provider's hook fail\": its `ending` cannot fail at all (seam.rs 165-172), and its `begin` could be made to (146-148 read the store) by a fixture that chose to.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":122},"end":{"line":133},"content_hash":"57dbccc7c81924abfac63e19e0d39d8437c1ec241a3c402b8a61449b36421b05"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the count at 530-536 or compare the row with the answer whoever wrote it; report the terminal case's failure under its own name only; after the write, require a row at its place and say who wrote it; make `check_provider` take the cases a fixture declares not applicable and fail on any other `NotApplicable`, and give the real `clax` provider's fixture a `fail_once`.","summary":"Four things the provider cases do not hold as their text says: a check that cannot fail, a failure reported under another case's name, a self-journaled write that journals nothing, and a case left out by a fixture with no one told","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:15.256886Z","id":"7ae1abb9c79e92624db665756cc1fd4f714c52e8e8aaec99ea5f43f051f67177","body":{"detail":"The places from NOTICE_SEQ (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:127) are a step's, not a binding's: today thread `i`'s notice is at `NOTICE_SEQ + i` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1514-1516), and a sources list at `NOTICE_SEQ + 1 + i` (run.rs:1694). With one binding that writes there (`clax`) nothing collides. The change that first gives a run a second provider whose `ending` journals must give each binding a range of its own.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1474},"end":{"line":1511},"content_hash":"c8568593afcb954198c2d6b99e786af55a2e717246f8c13cd017ef8d4ea631c5"},"summary":"Two bindings whose `ending` both write would take the same journal places","supersedes":"485f2587b799e5d0a756141688a8310c4cfb31174e9402fb2a8d06ddc7899cf0","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:34.372978Z","id":"c41498763b8d79f54ca8f7a9adc4377ef367fd447ee48572b4856bfeb634b008","body":{"detail":"Every case's write succeeds: nothing holds that a refusal a session answers is journaled by the core and answered the same when the step runs again (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, \"Who journals a call\"; today a refused write is journaled, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:422-424). `finish` is only called with `StepEnd::Commit` (257-259), so \"empty after `Cancelled`\" (surface.rs 616-618) is held nowhere, here or in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs. `begin` and `ending` returning `Err` are not played. The case `an_operation_outside_the_binding_never_reaches_the_provider` also hands the operation straight to the session and requires a refusal (353-359): that makes every session check its binding a second time, which `perform` already did, and for Clax's surface it is what keeps check 2 of crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs in the session; say so in the case, or drop the bypass. The only fixture is the fake's (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs); `ClaxProvider` in tests/seam.rs is not run through `check_provider`, so whether today's behaviour passes is unknown: by reading, it would with a host write as `write` (`threads.reply`), not with a draft write, which changes nothing outside the session before the commit (the case requires `state` to change, 301-304).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":400},"end":{"line":402},"content_hash":"e1d6f63de76de103f2fecc3aa0def0762b5cebc3330202f713730f11680d498f"},"suggested_fix":"Add a `refused` call to the fixture (journaled once, answered the same with and without its entry, state unchanged), a case that finishes with `StepEnd::Cancelled` and commits a `stopped` step, and run the suite on `ClaxProvider` when script.rs moves.","summary":"The conformance cases have no refusal by a session, no cancelled step and no failing hook, and are not run against the adapter of Clax's own surface","supersedes":"4dd970666a0d4371d66ea19e3228faa1aac32e40e86ea8546f7a899f1069a411","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:34.518190Z","id":"aead2e93a1b0c4b1610eca780e919d1d1f8b8d34bea8629b14f4dff204ea92f8","body":{"detail":"Row 3 has the core check the arguments \"against the surface's file\" and then find the binding (`route`). With one surface that is `api::check_args` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs:215), which reads Clax's own definition; with two, which file to check against is known only once the call is routed, so the order is route, check, `Binding::has`, session. `route` (267-275) answers `None` for a run with no `clax` binding calling an operation with no surface prefix (the module's own test: `route(only_fake, \"files.read\")` is `None`), and the table does not say what the script is answered or whether it is journaled; crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:497 `expect`s. And neither `SurfaceProvider` nor `Binding` leads to a `clax_surface::Definition`: `Binding::hash` is `None` for every stored binding of version 1, and `FakeSurfaceProvider::definition` is an associated function, not a trait method. A run with one fake surface and no `clax` needs the definition for the argument check, for the declaration the model reads and for the sandbox's operation list.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":52},"end":{"line":54},"content_hash":"37b139432bb5e9a23a05fcd471d7d3a1b7b167eb6172b8aecc3259941ba03d20"},"suggested_fix":"Write the order as route, then check against that surface's file, then `has`; say a call no binding takes is answered `unknown_op` and journaled by the core as `Outcome`; and add where a definition comes from (a `definition()` method on the provider, or a registry by `Binding::hash`) before the tick holds providers.","summary":"The step table checks a call's arguments before it finds the binding, says nothing of a call no binding takes, and no part of the seam gives the core a surface's definition","supersedes":"d9d1a8d2eb4755d3a6957801b6b02070c4e8eb50a20db7c88fbd2965166e895f","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:34.660445Z","id":"7d04f0095704fa5edfeff8956cbbe39a4bed4a4555cf5842ff6b714be3495963","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:521-528 refuses `web_disabled` (`clax.<op> is not available to this run`) in two cases: the run did not declare the operation, or it did and `Deps::web` is `None` (`Some(s) if declared => s, _ => …`). Both journal `{code, message, cost_milli: 0, web}` alone, with no `bot.web` record (`Finish::undeclared`, 149-154, and `write`, 471-479). Behind the seam only the first is `SurfaceProvider::undeclared`'s: in the second the binding has the operation, so `perform` (surface.rs 568-586) hands the call to the session, which must refuse it itself, in the same words, uncharged, uncounted and unaudited, and journal it (`Journal::Written` with today's form, or `Journal::Outcome` with the core's). crates/clax-bot/tests/web.rs:698 (`a_host_without_a_source_refuses_web_disabled`) holds the script's code for that case and nothing of its journal row. The row above (98) says a session's web call is journaled `Written`, always, with a `bot.web` record in `spend_journaled`: this refusal is the exception.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":130},"content_hash":"ccfb73ac12bf0e73cd2cd21c970f516293809d383889ccb0b99ad52092daeeaf"},"suggested_fix":"Say in row 99 that it is the undeclared half, and add the no-source half to row 98: refused by the session before check 3, journaled without a `bot.web` record. Whatever is decided for the undeclared row's form (record 0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e), decide the same for this one, so one message does not leave two row forms.","summary":"The table's row for an undeclared web operation covers half of script/web.rs 515-528: a declared operation on a host with no web source takes the same branch and is the session's","supersedes":"069c3c3da1983bde6747bb6058f6e3465b288c56b92ba058fd2b13cab9dca369","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:34.803987Z","id":"90a9c39877e9bfe40d2e6771170d48e3e0251fd09d25740655e113919a95dc3f","body":{"detail":"(1) A failed `draft.rebase` is journaled as a failure (script.rs:393) and its entry has no `result.version`, so a replay performs the rebase again against the artifact's current version (241-252): it is not answered from the entry and may answer otherwise. (2) The tool result is trimmed to `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES` with `published` and `replay` already in it (498-530): the trim must run after the sessions' `tool_result` fields are joined, or a script with long logs that published changes bytes; no golden has that pair (script.logs-cut-to-fit.json does not publish). (3) There are two lists of calls: the model is told the sandbox's (`outcome.calls`, 491-495: calls that never reached the host are in it, `script_finished`, `invalid_args`, the call over `max_calls`), while the payload's `calls`, the `bot.call` records and the infra usage line count the host's log (646-663, 702-708). (4) After a divergence no session sees a later call, the step still commits what the sessions hold (615-621, 695-716), and the earlier attempt's rows past the divergence stay in the journal: a publish among them is in no session's parts. (5) The core reads `op`, `ok`, `attempt` and `seq` of every journal row, a provider's own included, for `unreported_calls` (run.rs:1533-1546). (6) A script is cancelled when its error is `Cancelled` or the token is set when it returns (665-669), so a script that returned may still end as `StepEnd::Cancelled`. (7) A stop decided after a turn or script step was begun (script.rs:673; run.rs:2094-2115) begins the same step again as `stopped`: `ending` gets an attempt one higher than the session had. (8) `payload.notice` is the text whenever the stop has one, and `payload.posted` is true whenever the rule held, whether or not a post succeeded (run.rs:1525-1527, 1562, 1683, 1701); the endings table says `null` \"when nothing was posted\". (9) `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:316-326) reads `changed`, `published` and `notice` from payloads: fields the provider returns, read by the core's event. (10) `working` is called before steps that then stop the run in the same tick: no key (run.rs:1659, 1669-1677), the turn's own stops (1838-1847, 1906-1909, 2094-2115), `request_gone` (1857-1873).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":146},"end":{"line":204},"content_hash":"d4e5f385ad62e83b6bcf83e9c91b462122c7d0a3379ba4c57da7dc00d75344de"},"summary":"What crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs and run.rs do that the mapping tables leave out, each to be carried when the code moves behind the seam","supersedes":"687a9ee04f68de0d2f3066413bef6323b9dbdf7b9d8e79c956f444c1fa534ff8","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:34.951875Z","id":"9538cc148e40b7dd0ec2496dbc07f9da7f12f15323b7750e63748abba433e622","body":{"detail":"Not taken: a provider with no store (FakeSurfaceProvider, and the wire adapter of the design's \"Providers\": \"its answer is journaled when it arrives\") would have to be handed one, and three of today's four behaviours already leave the entry to the run loop (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:393, 411, 399-401, 422-424). So an answer says who journals it (Journal), and only a write journaled in its own transaction or with its charge is the provider's (Written).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":709},"end":{"line":723},"content_hash":"0af15a63728e7208a7a190b376e2bfaf9e9d403dc1011c19918abf2ba504fd45"},"summary":"Every provider journals each of its calls itself through the claim, and the core journals only its own refusals","supersedes":"887799bed2ee071ee7744b202002192cb1a617507a059f0c082b8fc00f875f90","tags":["seam","revisit:a second in-process provider needs a journal entry of its own form for a call that is not a write","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:35.121755Z","id":"88d6fc25aa2b22424cb56bdf4bee779b1242fac4ec5ebe9cc89711b4c45859ff","body":{"detail":"They are the three facts `Event::Call` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:84-93), a step's `calls` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:654-663) and the `bot.call` record (script.rs:648) take from a performer today, and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md:42 lists them as ambiguous. Kept as they are so that no stored or streamed byte moves; a general form belongs to the change that moves the events.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":729},"end":{"line":738},"content_hash":"369c8025344c2f85c272a621782952d084389d5211bfce073e7ec02019be6aa9"},"summary":"`CallNote` holds what `clax`'s operations say of a call (`target`, `cost_milli`, `web`) in a type of the core","supersedes":"8f5ab6e74103c757f6e6995d3220871513e63f1c7376de251616a1021141d9d5","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:35.285608Z","id":"7bec5bda26c3271a11be0b277826ce9602cb21bd58584221a502a1e9209c7c9c","body":{"detail":"`StepParts::payload` fields are flat names because today's are (`changed`, `replied`, `published`, `created`, `base`: crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:695-699, pinned by tests/golden/steps.thread.json), so two sessions answering one name would overwrite each other silently. `StepParts::draft` is the store's `DraftChange` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs), a row of `clax` in a core type, because `StepCommit` commits no other kind of row for a provider. Whoever first runs two sessions in one step decides whether a clash is refused or a host's fields are kept under its surface's name; a second kind of row needs the store.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1259},"end":{"line":1281},"content_hash":"44c7b7864cc3432d3a6f0beab0e83a5cde66e17715464de92eea728b5c25c2b3"},"summary":"The parts of several sessions go into one payload and one list of rows with nothing to tell the bindings apart","supersedes":"62987c75b807df4e0709d68fc8d4d113b7be1d840041f2bd728c5c18dbcdd37c","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:35.434139Z","id":"89a036d325ac22b991c8633ad5c8fefc7869319b90c99965fc5760ff73ccea9b","body":{"detail":"Today's sessions depend on both. The sandbox sends calls one at a time, in the script's order (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs:44-45), and `draft.rebase` relies on it: it clones the draft, awaits, and assigns the clone back (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:254-260), which loses a write made in between. A cancelled script, or a driver that lost its claim, drops the sandbox's future (`watched`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1090-1122) and with it a call at any await, after which `finish(Cancelled)` is called on the same session, or the session is dropped. Neither is in the trait's contract, so an adapter written against it may hold state across an await that a drop leaves half-changed. Separately, \"Not here\" (135-141) puts a call that waits as \"a third answer beside a value and a failure\": `Answer::result` is `Result<Value, HostError>` and `Answer`, `CallNote` are built by struct literal outside the crate (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:110-121), so adding it changes every provider that builds or reads one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1300},"end":{"line":1330},"content_hash":"6f66f78a1d1260fa5084a9c9898ebc35139afc315985494b5aa3ae26c10ee4db"},"suggested_fix":"State in `StepSession`'s documentation: calls of a step are made one at a time across all of its sessions, in place order; a call's future may be dropped at any await, and the session must then be as it was before the call or as after it. Make `Answer` and `CallNote` buildable only through constructors (`Answer::of`, `refused`, `journaled`, `naming`, and two for the cost and the shown value), so a waiting answer is a new constructor and not a new shape.","summary":"`StepSession::call` does not say that calls never overlap or that a call's future may be dropped, and `Answer` is a struct of public fields that a third outcome would change for every provider","supersedes":"a863ecea730730549bf118f419318f3e346534c6726a23fee8b30628ef67d235","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:35.592642Z","id":"40f9b073276f803c0cd339204add7b8d7f2a35e00b9ddf50848bddf0cc82841e","body":{"detail":"The design's correction names only the limit's value. Today's `credits_exhausted` notice also says when the week's credits reset, from the tick's clock (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1416-1429, Deps::clock), which no provider is given; a provider's own clock can be in the next week when the tick's was not. So the core passes the instant (`credits_reset`) and the provider words it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1342},"end":{"line":1366},"content_hash":"3c7a0a47247c4ae2538d7d7119f1cdd442c3e3abcf55435d462b14aabc1178bc"},"summary":"A provider works out when credits reset from a clock of its own, and `End::Stopped` carries the reason and the limit alone","supersedes":"7657d9ec13159aa2b01fccc9e99566824ce23af929a59593f03a6b37f64a0dda","tags":["seam","revisit:providers are given the run loop's clock","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:35.750700Z","id":"c40a8d904e05ee0405351a95e18a7bd0ead214007b6ac8aa764328b667162b46","body":{"detail":"`BotHost::context` (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:265) is read at the first turn (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1855-1879), and the design's correction says the kind's surface resolves the stored request there. `SurfaceProvider` holds only what a step and a run's end need; a run with no `clax` binding cannot be given a prompt through it. The change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers adds it, with the prompt file.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1417},"end":{"line":1425},"content_hash":"9e2dd32f7c9022e3bc7617d3644202dc7b39504edf8f572b4b817940669bf021"},"summary":"The seam has no way for the surface that declared a run's kind to resolve the request at the first turn","supersedes":"3f3fd9c10cce25a655ae83b06de7ed9523dd41417297fff40ded02ca1cb3d4b1","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:35.902256Z","id":"964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb","body":{"detail":"Today the web performer refuses it and journals the row itself (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:515-528, entry_result 276-293). Behind the seam the run's binding does not have the operation (the design's \"A run\": \"An operation outside it is not declared and is refused when called\"), so `perform` asks `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` and the core journals the standard form. What does not change: the script's error (`web_disabled`, the same words, pinned by tests/golden/script.web-refusals.json), the step's `calls` entry with `cost_milli: 0` and `web` (from the answer's note), and what a replay reads (`from_entry`, web.rs:305-306, defaults both to the same values). crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, `refusals_behind_a_session_are_journaled_once_and_answered_again`, holds all four. The other choice keeps the row byte for byte: every `clax` binding lists all 24 operations and the session refuses, which makes the web grant something other than the binding's set. The owner's to choose before script.rs moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1433},"end":{"line":1443},"content_hash":"e0f4d9b1c266b529067f182fb962771523523d913caed844b4f308a0bebe725a"},"summary":"Once the core refuses a web operation a run did not declare, its journal row is `{code, message}`: today's also holds `cost_milli: 0` and `web`","supersedes":"0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e","tags":["seam","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:36.058599Z","id":"6b08ec296625f2b7d743472293260034d1342ccf85920d30047447e1f7983aea","body":{"detail":"The design's correction (\"What measuring the run loop changed\", first row) names two hooks, `ending` and `ended`. Today ClaxBot is shown as working before each turn and each script on every tick (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1659), which is neither, and `BotHost::working` is to be deleted. Not taken: a session for a turn would load the draft (Draft::load, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:554-555) for a step that makes no call, and a turn would fail when the draft's base version is gone. So the seam has a third hook, which answers nothing.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1445},"end":{"line":1454},"content_hash":"7e156d157fe54b80fa60724f29b01d13cff487cac04ac15ecbdcedc1f9d2e276"},"summary":"No `working` hook: a session opened for every step, turns included, with the roster shown from `begin`","supersedes":"3bacbc3f33a47bb5703623492d3c0d0cbb2073d4b8f9c78655db2c749ef24694","tags":["seam","revisit:a parked run's roster must say what it waits on","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:36.203963Z","id":"b2fee1f0a5b12399f386fcf2ca1d105b17f5494ea330e9f1ad0045b566c978f8","body":{"detail":"Not taken: what fails inside today's terminal step and must keep it open is a store read (`Store::web_pages`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1684), which fails the tick with a `CoreError`; a notice that cannot be posted is logged and the step commits (run.rs:1525-1527). So `ending` and `begin` answer `clax_core::Result`, and a provider logs a write it could not make. `ended` answers `Result<(), HostError>` as drawn, since the run has ended whatever it says.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1474},"end":{"line":1511},"content_hash":"c8568593afcb954198c2d6b99e786af55a2e717246f8c13cd017ef8d4ea631c5"},"summary":"`ending` failing with a `HostError`, as the design's sketch of the trait has it","supersedes":"2d97356540dc238c1067a9dba33241baec7196c7a960b7110a7349ce15aeef6d","tags":["seam","revisit:a provider outside the process is given a part in the terminal step","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:36.351654Z","id":"750264730300120087ed2b176f6033e541597a035a67d87dbbfb477738a3112c","body":{"detail":"The contract is: an error fails the tick and the step is begun again. Today the one such error is `Store::web_pages` in `finish` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1684), and a tick that fails that way is not counted (`note_commit_failure`, run.rs:1339, counts commit failures only), so today's run also retries without end; behind the seam the same holds for any provider's error, including one from `ending(End::Failed)`, which is the path a run takes when nothing else works (`fail`, run.rs:1606-1631, logs a notice that cannot be posted and commits). With two bindings, the first's `Err` may or may not stop the second's `ending` from being called in that attempt; each is safe to call again (its writes are journaled), but a binding that always fails then keeps every other binding's `ended` from ever coming.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1490},"end":{"line":1505},"content_hash":"8abf5d86bd8febc718349d4342be7b2930c1e07021065f708f64ffe265c1c4f4"},"suggested_fix":"Fix the contract before the endings move: every binding's `ending` is called in each attempt, the kind's surface first; an `Err` is counted against the step as a failed commit is, and at the limit the run ends `Failed`; `ending(End::Failed)`'s `Err` is logged and the step commits.","summary":"`ending`'s `Err` keeps the terminal step open with no bound, also for `End::Failed`, and with several bindings nothing says whether the others are still called","supersedes":"15ad08586591c9690f3032928bdf4194ed6585f8eb03818b87e5421915a4a77b","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:36.494753Z","id":"3932a279e840d0a976a806d10b61fc0ad3c51b21ab3400541c54f555f7e345e9","body":{"detail":"Today a starting driver makes up for the lost `working(false)` by starting the follow-ups that were waiting (`BotHost::start_waiting`, crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:301-307; crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:219-235). The seam has no hook for that, and the design's \"recorded with the run and delivered until the provider acknowledges it, for seven days\" (\"Providers\") is not built. The change that puts the driver's start behind the provider needs one or the other.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1513},"end":{"line":1546},"content_hash":"b93f954044b01659b760a0e104edc77a60ead3c481f1520c02d281e8e7284019"},"summary":"Nothing in the seam delivers `ended` again when a crash falls between the terminal commit and the call","supersedes":"670a18d3c561afbf8a7deaaaa084ba151b06a1e66f76df7eabecbc0c178e3450","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:36.640612Z","id":"656c85a7594652b34817eb7d480c115a55cc1939762e660debfdcc100f562d88","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:333-335 (\"delivered until the provider acknowledges it, for seven days\") and 467-468 (\"`ended` is then delivered again when it next ends\") make `ended` at least once. Today's `BotHost::working(run, false)` is at least once too: `start_waiting` (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:301-307) makes up for a lost call at driver start, and the server starts follow-ups from both (crates/clax-server/src/bot.rs:1030-1066). `lifecycle` (1016-1018) fails on a second `ended`, and crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs:482-488 calls `ended` once, so a provider whose `ended` is not safe to repeat passes, and a run loop that redelivers fails the fake.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1778},"end":{"line":1844},"content_hash":"1805085475b76b8f2dff5a0dbb28ec28527d139ae097a82859c6f18fa5f9f2fa"},"suggested_fix":"Have the suite call `ended` twice with the same end and require the second to succeed and change nothing the first did not; have `lifecycle` accept repeats after the terminal commit with the same end.","summary":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` and the conformance suite hold `ended` to exactly once; the design delivers it until acknowledged, and today's roster-off is already repeated","supersedes":"981309fee459d058f1ac74c37e04efdb1610e18af525aa00d69518b505106eba","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:37.403773Z","id":"459ac7bee9693be0423cad82134597850a5aec4b8da776de5404aa120fec2c5c","body":{"detail":"Pins: `grep cost_milli crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/*.json` finds journal rows only in journal.web.json and resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json, all of declared calls; script.web-refusals.json holds the undeclared refusal's tool result only (`{op, ok, code}` and the thrown error), and tests/web.rs, crates/clax-server/tests/api_claxbot_run.rs and crates/clax-worker/test-bot.mjs assert the script's code, not the row. Readers of a web row's result: `from_entry` (script/web.rs 297-308), `Store::bot_web_counts` (`$.counted`, crates/clax-store/src/bot_web.rs 336-338), `sources` (`value.results`, crates/clax-bot/src/web/access.rs 310) and the stored page's `format` (web.rs 659-663); the run view and the events read a step's `calls` and the `call` event (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs 1409-1416, 1569-1573; events.rs 362-378), which the answer's note fills, never the journal. Upgrade with a run in flight: an old row `{code, message, cost_milli: 0, web}` under the seam is not read at all (`perform` asks `undeclared` again and skips the journal write for a replayed place), so the answer and the note are the same. Rollback: a new row `{code, message}` under today's code goes through `from_entry`, which answers `recorded(e)`, `cost_milli` 0 by default (305) and `web` as `shown` by default (306): the same `Performed`. Of the three choices: binding every `clax` run to all 24 operations makes `Binding::operations` no longer the run's operations (the design's \"A run\": the web grant is the intersection applied to `clax.web.*`) for the sake of an unpinned row; a core that journals a form the provider's `undeclared` supplies adds a fourth way to journal for one row. Dropping the two keys is the one with no cost found. Recommended: drop them, and pin the new row when script.rs moves (a journal golden for a run that did not declare the web).","kind":"comment","references":"964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb","span":{"start":{"line":1433},"end":{"line":1443},"content_hash":"e0f4d9b1c266b529067f182fb962771523523d913caed844b4f308a0bebe725a"},"summary":"Checked: no golden pins that row, nothing reads `cost_milli` or `web` from a refused call's row but `from_entry`, and both row forms replay the same under both codes; dropping the two keys changes nothing pinned","supersedes":"bd0f4ee615de3cbc74c920c555e5209cbdd242f5a0dbd097ae49b3344e29ef9e","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.071966Z","id":"4f1b25f4f2559f6cc53bb272dea4a6a3ef3c6d33d9d1a3e67d627e5f9f092b50","body":{"detail":"(1) `replayed`: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `left` (67) now pins the run's audit records, the drafts given to publish and the usage rows beside the step, journal and writes, and `pin_resumed` (100) requires a resumed step to have `bot.call` records that say `replayed` and an uncrashed one to have none. `resumed.publish.json` and `resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json` gained lines and lost none (`git diff --numstat 2230ac3c9 HEAD -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden`: 406 and 478 added, 0 deleted). script.rs 649 as `if false` fails four tests of `golden::resumed`. (2) `unreported_calls`: `audit.replay-diverged.json` (resumed.rs 273) is a run whose first attempt made four calls and died before its commit, and whose second diverges at call 0; it pins a `bot.call` record with `attempt` and `seq` for each of the three rows the second attempt did not reach. The first attempt's other path comes from a sandbox that runs another script than the one stored (`Another`, 243), since a script is deterministic across attempts. script.rs 685 removed fails that test. (3) which calls the `bot.script` record lists: `audit.script.call-limit.json` pins the record of the call-limit script, as this record asked, but the sandbox does not count the call over its limit, so that pin does not tell the two lists apart; `audit.script.call-not-awaited.json` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs 194) does: a call the script did not await is in the record's `calls` and in what the model is told (`script_finished`), with no `bot.call` record, journal row or place in the step's `calls`. script.rs 635 built from `st.calls` fails that test. (4) `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (resumed.rs 219), with `infra_lines` on: the `do.requests` line is 1 for a turn and 6 for a script of five calls. script.rs 705 as `0` fails it. Each change was made alone and reverted; `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests) passes without it.","kind":"comment","references":"bbf7334f83feb552fa27799b967defefbfa9cba6b94a4a8758d170bcfd8bc2af","span":{"start":{"line":635},"end":{"line":653},"content_hash":"202999706742add9714df1b2fce301308d48ea6cdf628a3362adb2c819a35735"},"summary":"All four are pinned for today's path; each of the four changes now fails a test","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.072397Z","id":"8f5e73bbc0e751c64891dc2b2319ba8d69d6b1fb2c09eec896f89f57ce876042","body":{"detail":"surface.rs 130, Replayed: answered from the entry before check 2 (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451), `from_entry` reading the core's `{code, message}` row as it reads today's. Row 129 says why making the refusal again is the same for an operation the run did not declare. The adapter's session answers a web call given its entry before any check (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `ClaxSession::web`, 421-465) and has something standing for a web source (`ClaxProvider::web`, 105) that counts the calls that got past check 2. `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` (1608): attempt 1 on a host with no source journals `web_disabled`; attempt 2 on a host with one answers the same from the entry; through the seam and through today's run loop (`Deps::web` `None` for the driver that dies, the fake source for the one that resumes) no call reaches the source, the step's `calls` are equal, and there is one journal row, of attempt 1. With the session's replay check turned off (`call.replay.filter(|_| false)`) that test fails. Each change was made alone and reverted; `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests) passes without it.","kind":"comment","references":"c9ab76da3fe4032075597a985766733746d49c6451fb9f7bafa0d12f004724d1","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":130},"content_hash":"ccfb73ac12bf0e73cd2cd21c970f516293809d383889ccb0b99ad52092daeeaf"},"summary":"Row 130 now says a journaled call is answered from its entry, as every web call given one; the adapter does so, and a case holds it when the host gains a source between attempts","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.072801Z","id":"89b559d6b09f4263b621e8665bab0d522b5732d5a9b0755453cba2f31a7124ea","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::ended` (surface.rs 1513-1540): a repeat has the same variant of `End`, and so the same status; the tick that committed has the whole value, and a repeat rebuilt after a restart has the reason and the code but may lack `End::Stopped`'s `limit` and `credits_reset` and the `result`. A provider need remember a run only until it has been told `ended` once and the run's row says it has ended; a repeat for a run it no longer knows is ignored, answering `Ok`. `FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` (1787) compares the status of the ends, of `ending` against `ended` too. crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `the_fake_takes_ended_again_and_tells_what_else_follows_it` (523) tells the fake the same kind with and without its details (accepted) and another kind (refused). Not done here: keeping the `End` in the terminal step's payload so a redelivery reads the whole value back, which is for the change that builds redelivery.","kind":"comment","references":"b25bed990f60c83b732a878dfabcfaa1d7988f36b8f107f052a53a73e258493e","span":{"start":{"line":1513},"end":{"line":1546},"content_hash":"b93f954044b01659b760a0e104edc77a60ead3c481f1520c02d281e8e7284019"},"summary":"The contract is relaxed to what can be rebuilt: a repeat carries the same kind of end, its details best effort, and a provider may forget a run once told","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.073182Z","id":"4c156835eb0e744d42054b181b25f5bd37487d5a2990fd725b204bbf1afe4224","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields` (surface.rs 1160-1245) and \"Several bindings in one step\" (247-257): a clash is never dropped and never overwrites; the core abandons the attempt and counts it against the step as a commit that failed (`Store::note_commit_failure`), so a provider that clashes on every attempt ends the run `failed` at `RunSettings::max_commit_failures` and cannot hold it open. Both texts say that a surface named as one of `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` or `clax`'s field names clashes on every step it adds fields to, and that nothing refuses such a name yet. `merge_fields` now adds a surface's later fields to its own object (a session's, then its `ending`'s, in a cancelled script's `stopped` step) and clashes on a field that object already has. Not done on this branch: reserving those names in `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42); and nothing counts a clash, since no run loop calls `merge_fields`.","kind":"comment","references":"f2d3e3a60f0d2ebd106ec326b4eee781ce1120a70b48878e049a8fde02cf06ad","span":{"start":{"line":1160},"end":{"line":1245},"content_hash":"a46bc6bfee1e50342a0e7cfe25369becef59eb0c33a0b8fd17d17447037c9a2d"},"summary":"What a `FieldClash` does to the step is stated: the provider is at fault, the attempt is counted as a failed commit, nothing is dropped; the names are not yet reserved where a surface is defined","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.073547Z","id":"98d6baaec3b82f90da2b2b4c14b2d45afc32947f425fd2086adcd75af3c51836","body":{"detail":"`StepCx::place(seq)` (surface.rs 664) answers a place only for 0 to `NOTICE_SEQ - 1`, and `no_place` (961) is what a script is answered past it (`call_limit`), unjournaled and unperformed; row 3 of the step table (52) says so. The unit test `each_binding_has_its_own_places_for_what_its_ending_writes` and crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `a_call_past_a_steps_places_has_no_place` (709) hold the range, and the latter that the setting `script.max_calls` accepts `NOTICE_SEQ + 1` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 226-229: a `Positive` is 1 to 1,000,000,000,000), so the sandbox's limit does not stand for this one. What is left: crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 458-462 numbers a script's calls with no such check, so today's run loop has the hazard this record describes until its calls go through `dispatch`. For the owner: cap `script.max_calls` under 1,000,000 in bot_settings.rs (which this branch was not to change), or leave the setting and rely on the core's refusal once script.rs moves.","kind":"comment","references":"300e461b611367d486e375bd81955311f1cbf6a8d6340ba305614b922b0f1943","span":{"start":{"line":650},"end":{"line":668},"content_hash":"b0dcbfdc79cdeff6cae85840312f17c0beb5b7f0d0dbe37417938cb385692508"},"summary":"`StepCx::place` answers `None` from `NOTICE_SEQ` up and the core refuses such a call; today's run loop is still unguarded, and whether to cap `script.max_calls` is the owner's","tags":["seam","fix-round","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.073921Z","id":"8f20def045c1805220705b6961a7c400d18539883a60b1959aff4179e4d4c718","body":{"detail":"(1) `perform` (surface.rs 1088) turns what `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` answers into a refusal the core journals (`Answer::into_refusal`, 894): its journal becomes `Outcome` whatever constructor made it, and an answer that is no failure is replaced by `not_declared`. crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `what_undeclared_answers_is_journaled_by_the_core_as_a_refusal` (670) holds both with a provider whose hook answers `Answer::written(Err(..))` and `Answer::written(Ok(..))`: one journal row, the refusal, and no session saw the call. (2) `Answer::charged` (874) is for an answer made with `Answer::written`; a charge above 0 on another answer fails a debug assertion there and in `Claim::record`, and `Answer::uncharged` (886) is the cost of 0 any answer may carry (the two `web_disabled` refusals). The unit test `a_charge_on_an_answer_the_core_journals_is_refused` holds the assertion. In a release build the charge is kept in the note and not in the core's row: a checked mistake, not an unrepresentable one. (3) `Answer`'s documentation (797-801) and \"Who journals a call\" say that `of`, `outcome` and `refused` given a failure write the same row; a unit test holds `Answer::outcome(Err(e)) == Answer::refused(e)`. They are not folded: `of` and `outcome` take a `Result` because their callers have one.","kind":"comment","references":"49f0a50e43f44d6cbc18b5b070b6ca0a4ec21211601cbe9c6283f45e589efd0e","span":{"start":{"line":790},"end":{"line":900},"content_hash":"53568a0fb9b941b19319683386dce91a73f52bfcfb779987cf2944b3de368bc6"},"summary":"The three combinations are closed or said: `undeclared`'s answer is journaled by the core whatever made it, a charge above 0 is for an answer the provider journals, and the three spellings of a failure are stated to write one row","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.074285Z","id":"80437092937ca016953aa6a1fda8235f88fefe1eb8623f13182e0c094585d31d","body":{"detail":"(1) The count that could not fail is gone; the row at the refusal's place is compared with the answer when the core wrote it, in words that do not say who wrote it (crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs 536-547). (2) The copy is gone: a failure of the terminal case is reported under its own name. With the fake's `ended` failing when told twice, crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs 103 now panics with `ending_is_repeatable_inside_the_terminal_step_and_ended_after_its_commit: `ended` failed when it was told a second time`. (3) After the write the case requires a row at its place, ok and of the write's operation, and says who was to write it (420-441); with the fake answering `Answer::written` for `fake.set` and writing nothing it fails with `the write's place has no journal row`. crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 89-94 no longer calls that safe. (4) A fixture says why it has no staged write or no failing hook (`Maybe::Lacks(reason)`, 130), and `check_provider(f, not_applicable)` (899) fails a case that was not played unless the caller names it, a named case that was played, and a name that is no case; the documented loop passes the list. providers.rs `a_case_is_left_out_only_when_the_fixture_and_the_caller_both_say_so` (586) holds all three. The adapter's fixture gives its reason honestly (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, the fixture's `fail_once`): its `begin` fails only when the store cannot give it the run's row or its draft, which a fixture cannot cause once and undo, and its `ending` answers nothing and cannot fail. The cancelled case joins the `ending`'s fields to the session's through `merge_fields` (757). The eight changes to the fake that the suite was first checked with still fail, with these two more.","kind":"comment","references":"e387ab56159216c0513d4f6595f2fc1713c2a6b8ca613c279109abdf171f9494","span":{"start":{"line":130},"end":{"line":150},"content_hash":"a59e640590f6d0bb151d85293d51a703af6fbd45e3e706837126555c1950bd2c"},"summary":"The four are fixed in the suite, and each break it was shown to miss now fails under the right case","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.074324Z","id":"a4777d70312059a23561d5a4bcc760877132fed33e98f187b7e9b46a5d5ffcf0","body":{"detail":"(1) Keep the replay check first in the web session: a web call given its entry is answered from it before check 2 (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451; surface.rs row 130); crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` holds the adapter to it. (2) Pin the new `web_disabled` journal row, `{code, message}`, in a journal fixture for a run that did not declare the web and for one on a host with no source (surface.rs 132-144; record 0d124949). (3) Write the divergence check: `Stepper` (seam.rs 532-581) asserts on a diverged replay instead of answering `replay_diverged`, and `dispatch` only asserts in a debug build that the entry it is given is of its call; `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins today's audit of one. (4) Refuse a call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the calls are numbered (458-462 here; `StepCx::place`, `no_place`), which today's code does not. (5) Give the real provider's conformance fixture a `fail_once` and a `disturb`: the adapter's fixture has neither (seam.rs 1836), so the cases for a failing `begin` and `ending` are not played against `clax`, and its read is never disturbed. (6) Pass `CallPlace::attempt` to `OpCtx` (seam.rs `ClaxSession::op_ctx`, 232): the fake host now records the attempt it is given and the proof compares it, and the server's host reads it. (7) Join every session's `tool_result` fields before the result is trimmed (surface.rs 170-175). (8) Say where a surface's standing operations come from: `Binding::from_stored` takes a rule the stored binding does not hold, and the proof hard-codes `clax`'s (seam.rs 81-99; `declared`, crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 253, is private). (9) The audit and usage of a script step are pinned for today's path only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, scripts.rs): `replayed`, `unreported_calls`, the calls a `bot.script` record lists, the requests line; the proof builds none of them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":343},"end":{"line":452},"content_hash":"775ebefe9eba51456748adebbbbb5159bb8871891790ef2bd4c8d76927ce1e51"},"summary":"For the change that moves this file behind the seam: what crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs and its tests could not do without it","tags":["seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T07:59:48.074408Z","id":"264e99a6077b972f7feab8ab52fcfe936f76991cfd343120ffb97626914b97bc","body":{"detail":"(1) Test the run loop's half of `SurfaceProvider::ending` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 1474-1511): an `Err` counted against the step as a failed commit, the bound (`max_commit_failures`), `End::Failed`'s `Err` logged and the step committed, and every binding's `ending` still called when one fails. The provider cases hold only the provider's half. (2) Make the change to the sources' read on purpose: `Store::web_pages` failing in `finish` (1684 here) fails the tick uncounted today, so the step is begun again without end; behind `ending` under that contract it is tried `max_commit_failures` times and the run ends `failed` (surface.rs, the endings table's row for a notice that cannot be posted). Keep the read out of `ending`, or accept the bound. (3) `begin`'s failure is retried uncounted and `ending`'s is counted (surface.rs 1460-1471, 1490-1505): decide whether the two should agree. (4) Build what delivers `ended` again; the contract allows a repeat with the same kind of end and best-effort details (1513-1540), and keeping the `End` in the terminal step's payload would let a redelivery carry all of it (record b25bed99). (5) Count a `FieldClash` against the step, and reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) as surface names in `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42), so a clash cannot be reached by a name (record f2d3e3a6). (6) The adapter's terminal cases prove nothing about notices: its `ending` and `ended` do nothing (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 186-204), so the provider cases pass on it with no notice posted. The real provider's fixture needs a `noticed` probe that reads the notices and a `lifecycle`. (7) A cancelled script's `stopped` step is charged the per-step lines with no call counted (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673-683; 1567-1573 here): keep or change it knowingly.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1549},"end":{"line":1740},"content_hash":"878cd3244c307349854d1a55547367aaeeb12163f1aedbbe15872bd0cd79974b"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending` and `ended`: what the seam states and nothing holds yet","tags":["seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:00:09.017771Z","id":"c6fbafa01696d2c9bbd0c8f406362de71f31779a22f45dad558010655fae3c16","body":{"detail":"(1) 530-536, \"the refusal was journaled more than once\", cannot fail: the journal holds one row for a `(run, step, seq)` (`Store::record_bot_call`, crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs 1517-1525, keeps the row that is there). A provider that journals a refusal itself and answers `refused` is caught only when its row differs, at 501-506, in words that say the core journaled it. (2) 848-850 copies any failure of the terminal case into `an_ending_that_failed_is_made_again_in_the_next_attempt`, which comes first in the results: with `FakeSurfaceProvider::ended` changed to fail when told twice, the test panics under that name and not under `ending_is_repeatable_inside_the_terminal_step_and_ended_after_its_commit`. (3) No case reads the journal after `ProviderFixture::write`: a provider that answers `Answer::written` and writes no row passes, since the call made again with its key answers the same (414-422). The module documentation of crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs (90-93) calls that safe; the place then has no row for the divergence check or for `unreported_calls`. (4) A case is `NotApplicable` because the fixture left `staged` or `fail_once` as `None`, and the documented way to run the suite (71-75) passes it over: a provider that does stage writes, with a fixture that says it does not, passes. Both tests that run the suite assert the list of cases not played (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs 106-114, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 1569-1582); nothing requires the next fixture to. For the adapter of `clax` the two reasons read \"the fixture cannot make the provider's hook fail\": its `ending` cannot fail at all (seam.rs 165-172), and its `begin` could be made to (146-148 read the store) by a fixture that chose to.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":130},"end":{"line":150},"content_hash":"a59e640590f6d0bb151d85293d51a703af6fbd45e3e706837126555c1950bd2c"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the count at 530-536 or compare the row with the answer whoever wrote it; report the terminal case's failure under its own name only; after the write, require a row at its place and say who wrote it; make `check_provider` take the cases a fixture declares not applicable and fail on any other `NotApplicable`, and give the real `clax` provider's fixture a `fail_once`.","summary":"Four things the provider cases do not hold as their text says: a check that cannot fail, a failure reported under another case's name, a self-journaled write that journals nothing, and a case left out by a fixture with no one told","supersedes":"e387ab56159216c0513d4f6595f2fc1713c2a6b8ca613c279109abdf171f9494","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:00:09.158678Z","id":"41ee8d5502ae029d59b32f2ce0686b5b14d12d7ef4395a4edcdba40265bae284","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451 answers any web call whose place is in the journal from its entry (`from_entry`), before `web_fresh` runs check 2 (512-529). For row 125 (the run did not declare the operation) making the refusal again is the same answer: `Run::web` does not change. For row 126 (declared, `Deps::web` is `None`) it is the same only while the host still has no source. A first attempt on a host with no source journals `web_disabled`; when the step is run again on a host that has one, today's code answers `web_disabled` from the entry, and a session written as row 126 says (check 2 made again, the entry not read) passes check 2 and runs checks 3 to 9, reaching the source for a fetch, before `Store::spend_journaled` (web.rs 497) finds the place taken. The adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs (421) cannot show this: it has no source on any attempt.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":130},"content_hash":"ccfb73ac12bf0e73cd2cd21c970f516293809d383889ccb0b99ad52092daeeaf"},"suggested_fix":"Row 126, Replayed: answered from the entry, as every web call the session is given with one is (web.rs 449-451); `from_entry` reads the core's `{code, message}` row as it reads today's (305-306 default the two keys). Keep `if let Some(e) = replay` first when `script/web.rs` moves into the session.","summary":"The two `web_disabled` rows say a replayed call is answered again without its entry being read; today's code reads the entry first, and for the declared half the two differ when the host gains a web source between attempts","supersedes":"c9ab76da3fe4032075597a985766733746d49c6451fb9f7bafa0d12f004724d1","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:00:09.303496Z","id":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","body":{"detail":"`CallPlace::seq` documents the places from `NOTICE_SEQ` as the terminal step's (418-421) and `StepCx::notice` refuses an index outside its binding's stride, but `StepCx::place(seq)` checks nothing. The setting `script.max_calls` accepts 1 to 1,000,000,000,000 (crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 80, 226-229). A cancelled script's step is the step its `stopped` notices are journaled under (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673, run.rs 1558), so with a limit over 1,000,000 a script's call and a binding's notice can ask for one place of one step, and `unreported_calls` (run.rs 1539) leaves out every script call from that place up. The hazard is today's (`NOTICE_SEQ`, crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs 127) and the strides do not widen it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":650},"end":{"line":668},"content_hash":"b0dcbfdc79cdeff6cae85840312f17c0beb5b7f0d0dbe37417938cb385692508"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse a script's call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the core numbers the calls (or cap `script.max_calls` under it), and have `StepCx::place` answer `None` from `NOTICE_SEQ` up as `notice` does outside its range.","summary":"Nothing but documentation keeps a script's place under `NOTICE_SEQ`: `StepCx::place` takes any `seq`, and `script.max_calls` may be set far above 1,000,000","supersedes":"300e461b611367d486e375bd81955311f1cbf6a8d6340ba305614b922b0f1943","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:00:09.448097Z","id":"014e16a2dd786801ca363b3ff551c137bb9d1b45c8f32e02ca917aa511f481d1","body":{"detail":"(1) `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` (1289-1298) is told to answer with `Answer::refused`, but `perform` (968-986) journals its answer as the answer says: one built with `Answer::written` leaves the refusal unjournaled, with no place for the provider to have written it (the hook is given no place and no claim). (2) `charged(n)` with `n` over 0 on an answer the core journals (`of`, `outcome`, `refused`): the core's row keeps no cost, so the attempt that replays the call shows none, and nothing ties the charge to the row. Today a charge is always journaled with its row (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 491-497); only `charged(0)` goes with a core row (the two `web_disabled` refusals). (3) `Answer::of(Err(e))`, `Answer::outcome(Err(e))` and `Answer::refused(e)` journal the same row (`Claim::record`, 507-531): three spellings of one thing. None is reachable from the adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":790},"end":{"line":900},"content_hash":"53568a0fb9b941b19319683386dce91a73f52bfcfb779987cf2944b3de368bc6"},"suggested_fix":"Have `perform` journal an `undeclared` answer as an outcome whatever its constructor says (or have the hook answer a `HostError` and a note, not an `Answer`); say on `charged` that a call that charged more than 0 is journaled by the provider with its charge, and assert it in `Claim::record`.","summary":"`Answer`'s constructors leave three combinations a provider can build and the core does not catch","supersedes":"49f0a50e43f44d6cbc18b5b070b6ca0a4ec21211601cbe9c6283f45e589efd0e","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:00:09.586807Z","id":"68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields` puts another surface's fields under the surface's name and answers `FieldClash` when that name is one of `PAYLOAD_KEYS` (`calls`, `code`, `error`, `outcome`, `reason`, `result`, `summary`, `title`, `tool_use_id`) or is taken. `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42) refuses a host's surface only the names `clax`, `runs`, `content` and the namespaces of Clax's own file (`files`, `draft`, `threads`, …). So a surface named `result` or `calls` parses, binds, and then fails each of its steps. A surface named as one of `clax`'s own payload fields (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) clashes only in a run that also has a `clax` binding; in a run without one its object lands where `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs 316-326) and `History::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 598-644) read `clax`'s fields. The module documentation (surface.rs 236-238) says a clash \"fails the tick\" and not whether the failure is counted against the step (`Store::note_commit_failure`): uncounted, the step is begun again without end.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1160},"end":{"line":1245},"content_hash":"a46bc6bfee1e50342a0e7cfe25369becef59eb0c33a0b8fd17d17447037c9a2d"},"suggested_fix":"Reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names as surface names where a host's definition is parsed, so the clash cannot be reached by a name; and say that a `FieldClash` counts as a failed commit of the step does.","summary":"A host surface named as a field of a step's payload fails every step it adds fields to, at run time; nothing refuses the name when the surface is defined, and what a `FieldClash` does to the step is not said","supersedes":"f2d3e3a60f0d2ebd106ec326b4eee781ce1120a70b48878e049a8fde02cf06ad","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:00:09.729742Z","id":"a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c","body":{"detail":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` (1629-1635) fails a repeat whose `End` is not equal to the first's. A repeat made by the tick that committed has the value in hand; one made after a restart (the case the contract names: a crash between the commit and the call) has only the run's row and the terminal step. `Run::reason` and the payload's `reason`, `code`, `summary` give `End::Finished`, `GaveUp` and `Failed` back, but a `run_cap` stop's `limit` is only inside the notice's text (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1431-1436, 1562) and a `credits_exhausted` stop's reset time is nowhere (1416-1420 computes it from the clock when the notice is written). On the provider's side, \"what the provider did for the run's end the first time, it does not do twice\" has no bound: a provider that is not idempotent by nature must keep every run it was told of for ever.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1513},"end":{"line":1546},"content_hash":"b93f954044b01659b760a0e104edc77a60ead3c481f1520c02d281e8e7284019"},"suggested_fix":"When the endings move: either keep the `End` (and the kind's result) in the terminal step's payload so a redelivery reads it back, or say on the trait that a repeat carries the same status and reason and may lack `limit` and `credits_reset`, and make `lifecycle` compare those. Say on the trait that a provider's `ended` must be idempotent without a record of its own, or how long the run loop may redeliver.","summary":"`ended` is to be told again \"with the same end\", but nothing the store keeps of an ended run can rebuild `End::Stopped`'s `limit` and `credits_reset`, and the contract does not say for how long a provider must remember an end it acted on","supersedes":"b25bed990f60c83b732a878dfabcfaa1d7988f36b8f107f052a53a73e258493e","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.637461Z","id":"4bba8ddd91c12a845fcef7887f62cc5cfba0cc927b8e7cbc29b01effdbbf99d7","body":{"detail":"The arm at 1233-1238 takes any object at `into[surface]` for the surface's own. `clax` puts `published` at the top of a script step's payload as `{version, url?}` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 697) and of what the model is told (502-504). With `into = {\"published\": {\"version\": 2}}`, `merge_fields(&mut into, &PAYLOAD_KEYS, \"published\", {\"x\": 1})` answers `Ok` and leaves `{\"published\": {\"version\": 2, \"x\": 1}}`: a unit test asserting a `FieldClash` there, added to this file's tests and run with `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test --test_arg=probe_`, failed with `merged into clax's field: {\"published\":{\"version\":2,\"x\":1}}` (the test was removed again). The documentation says otherwise in three places: 258-261 (\"one of `clax`'s field names, clashes on every step\"), 1188-1190 and 1193-1195 (\"taken there by something that is not this surface's object\"). Merged in the other order (the host surface first, `clax` after) it clashes, so the result depends on the order the bindings are merged in, and the kind's surface is first. Before commit c0b76016f every name taken at the top clashed. `published` is the one field of `clax`'s that is an object; `History::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 601) and `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs 321) read it. Nothing refuses the surface name yet (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1229},"end":{"line":1240},"content_hash":"bbb5db2419c2f85f97213ad72e7b91bf5a1286603461f08bf6c22bae735cc427"},"suggested_fix":"Tell the surface's own object from another's without guessing: have `merge_fields` refuse a surface whose name is one of `clax`'s payload or tool-result field names (a constant beside `PAYLOAD_KEYS`), or keep which top-level names each surface's merge made. Add the case above to `a_field_that_takes_a_name_of_the_cores_or_one_taken_is_the_providers_error`.","summary":"`merge_fields` adds a host surface's fields to whatever object is under the surface's name, and `clax`'s own `published` is an object: a surface named `published` is merged into it and not refused","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.637534Z","id":"e65c2488200d29c0ef288c65847a753f02b1e88ee3e17ec7f7c256137f245965","body":{"detail":"Three tests make a divergence: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` (258) and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 write a journal row for another call at place 0 by hand, and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (273) runs another script for the first attempt (`Another`, 243) whose first call is `files.read` where the stored script's is `files.list`. A script's clock and random numbers are fixed for its step and its first call is made before any answer, so a real second attempt always makes the first attempt's first call: a real divergence is at place 1 or later, after calls that were answered from the journal. What that leaves unpinned: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` (the model is told `completed: []` whatever was replayed) passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`; so does no fixture hold a step whose audit has a `replayed` `bot.call`, a `replay_diverged` one and an unreported row together. The mechanics `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins (a `bot.call` with `attempt` and `seq` for each row the committing attempt did not reach, the first attempt's reply standing) are those of a real run; its `diverged_at: 0` is not. `completed` is what tells the model which calls of the script took effect before the replay stopped, and it is built from the run loop's own log of calls (506-511), which is rewritten when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":513},"content_hash":"331d0ccb3052c72097c372911f08deafdafca36960c23c77c327fc73f25a612a"},"suggested_fix":"Give `Another`'s first-attempt script the stored script's first call (`files.list`) and let it differ from the second call on: the fixture then pins `completed: [\"files.list\"]` in the step's result, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows. Add the step's `result` to what the fixture pins.","summary":"Every diverged replay in the tests diverges at call 0, which one script cannot do: `replay.completed` is never pinned with a call in it, and no fixture has replayed, diverged and unreported calls in one step","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.637614Z","id":"d016f72848eaf6b4384062f378e2ac86772fcfeee80bff93b574f9e676b778c1","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone, passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`: (1) 1570 as `5` (the requests line of every `stopped` step, a cancelled script's among them, counts five calls: crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 160-164 says it counts none). (2) 1574-1575 without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (a stop writes no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported). (3) 1539 without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record of its own). (1) is billed where `RunSettings::infra_lines` is on (the hosted Worker, crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs 310); crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (219) holds the line for a turn, a script and a `finish` step and has no `stopped` step. (2) and (3) are audit only: `Pins::ending` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 568-592) pins a terminal step's payload, replies, writes, notices and roster, and not the run's audit records. Also unpinned, and seen by no one: a call answered from the journal is logged with `ms` 0 (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 as 7 passes all 189; the goldens replace every `ms` by a name, pins.rs 123).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1566},"end":{"line":1573},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"suggested_fix":"Extend `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` with a run stopped by a cap after a script and a script cancelled after a call, each with its `stopped` step's requests quantity; add `p.audit()` to what one stopped ending and the cancelled ending pin.","summary":"No test holds what a `stopped` step is charged on the requests line, nor its audit records: the count of calls, the `bot.call` records of a stop, and that a notice's journal row gets none","tags":["review","seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.637695Z","id":"3e8f245d6e726b970474b491a421defbcc13cdaa352b3c974bfee1cc7159f49a","body":{"detail":"Read top to bottom as that change's author. (1) Row 3 (52): a call refused `no_place` is \"unjournaled\"; it does not say whether it is in the step's `calls`, the events and the `bot.call` records, and counted on the requests line, as a `replay_diverged` answer is (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs it). (2) `no_place`'s code is `call_limit` (973), the name of the sandbox's `ErrorKind::CallLimit`, but it is a `HostError` a script can catch, in other words (\"call N is past the 1000000 calls one script may make\") than the sandbox's uncatchable end (\"the script made more than its N host calls\", crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs 1019): one name for two things the model can meet. (3) 138-140 still says a refused web call's row \"is never read by this code (the refusal is made again…)\", which row 130 now contradicts for the declared half: it is read, by `from_entry`. (4) Two rows of `Answer`'s table (793-796) claim a call answered from its entry: `of` (\"any call answered from its entry\") and `written` (\"a call answered from an entry the provider wrote\"). The journal of a replayed call is not read, so either works, but only `written` may carry `charged(n)`; say that the constructor of a replayed answer matters for the charge alone. (5) A `FieldClash` is counted as a failed commit (253-256): not said for the `failed` step itself, where an `ending`'s `Err` is logged and its fields left out (1503-1505), nor whether the other bindings' parts of that attempt are kept. (6) Nothing says which of a provider's answers the core turns into `Event::Call`'s `web` field (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 153): `CallNote::extra` has no named field. (7) `Binding::from_stored`'s rule for `clax` is given as \"`declared` in api.rs, which is private\" (341-343): on branch `claxbot-surfaces` it is `pub(crate)`. (8) Still open in their own records: where another surface's standing operations come from, and who resolves the request at the first turn.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"e6f11413e53e72aebb1d372556f0a1bf005969953e4661750093c0264d1c54bc"},"summary":"What the module documentation leaves the change that moves script.rs behind the seam to ask","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.638960Z","id":"aae6e652991ae71e4269b93edb004bf0bf9fd0de1bc1356b10031285ecfab17f","body":{"detail":"Against `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests): 649 as `if false` fails four tests of `golden::resumed`; 685 removed fails `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`; 635 from `st.calls` fails `golden::scripts::a_call_that_was_not_awaited_is_in_the_scripts_record_alone`; 705 as `0` fails `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls`. `resumed.publish.json` and `resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json` at 2230ac3c9 and at 3b700b895, parsed and compared: every key and value of the old file is in the new one unchanged, and the new ones add `audit`, `drafts_published` and `usage`. What is still unpinned of a script step and a stop is in the records on crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 505-513 and crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1566-1573.","kind":"comment","references":"bbf7334f83feb552fa27799b967defefbfa9cba6b94a4a8758d170bcfd8bc2af","span":{"start":{"line":635},"end":{"line":653},"content_hash":"202999706742add9714df1b2fce301308d48ea6cdf628a3362adb2c819a35735"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: each of the four changes now fails a test; the two fixtures that existed gained keys and lost or changed none","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.639359Z","id":"a9f287e80c1625c37e9b38c26e86cf93e2c031820d3987addedde0185b04152b","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 435 changed to skip the entry when the host has a source fails `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` alone of the 12 seam tests. One sentence of the paragraph under the table was not brought along: crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 138-140 still says a row \"is never read by this code (the refusal is made again…)\".","kind":"comment","references":"41ee8d5502ae029d59b32f2ce0686b5b14d12d7ef4395a4edcdba40265bae284","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":130},"content_hash":"ccfb73ac12bf0e73cd2cd21c970f516293809d383889ccb0b99ad52092daeeaf"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: the adapter answers a web call from its entry before check 2, and the case fails when it does not","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.639752Z","id":"75e51d153d0bd383ac559d7ee2c6ccd3ddd4a7a0bf923a02107877a7f42b96fc","body":{"detail":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` compares `End::status` (1798, 1823) and crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs `the_fake_takes_ended_again_and_tells_what_else_follows_it` plays a repeat without its details and one of another kind. \"It need remember a run only until it has been told `ended` once and the run's row says the run has ended\" (1534-1536) asks a provider to know the run's row; a provider outside the process has only what it is told. Since `ended` is never called before the terminal commit, \"until it has been told `ended` once\" says the same without the store.","kind":"comment","references":"a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c","span":{"start":{"line":1513},"end":{"line":1546},"content_hash":"b93f954044b01659b760a0e104edc77a60ead3c481f1520c02d281e8e7284019"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: a repeat is held to the same kind of end, and the fake to that; one sentence leaves a provider to read the store","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.640138Z","id":"0d1ae39dfa865b67beaa24cd86c13fe88d7268f9817c4c447935e6696cf3a5ae","body":{"detail":"A second merge for one surface joins its object and clashes on a field the object has (`shell.notice`, held by `claxs_fields_are_merged_at_the_top_and_another_surfaces_under_its_name`), so a provider cannot overwrite its own earlier field. The join cannot tell the surface's own object from an object `clax` put under the same name: the record on crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 1229-1240 has the case (`published`). Nothing can test \"counted against the step as a commit that failed\" yet: no run loop calls `merge_fields`, and `FakeSurfaceProvider` has no way to answer a clashing field; the change that moves the endings needs a provider that answers a field named `calls` once.","kind":"comment","references":"68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10","span":{"start":{"line":1160},"end":{"line":1245},"content_hash":"a46bc6bfee1e50342a0e7cfe25369becef59eb0c33a0b8fd17d17447037c9a2d"},"summary":"Checked at 3b700b895: what a clash does is stated, and a surface cannot overwrite a field of its own; the join added one case that is not refused","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.640528Z","id":"432832ac4877fe98d396a1f303c60643ae5a64e4519b3b954a25a423d4e79654","body":{"detail":"With the cap the sandbox's own limit always comes first, and a script that makes too many calls meets one thing: the script ends with error kind `call_limit` and \"the script made more than its N host calls\" (crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs 1019; `script.error.call-limit.json`). Without it there are two: that, and from place 1,000,000 a `HostError` with the same code `call_limit` in other words (`no_place`, 961-970), which a script can catch and go on from, every later call refused the same. The cap also closes the hazard in today's run loop (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 458-462) before that file moves. A stored value above the cap has to be met too: clamp where `RunSettings` reads it (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 175-176) as well as refusing it in crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 226-229. A limit of a million calls in one script has no use the CPU limit would let it reach.","kind":"comment","references":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","span":{"start":{"line":650},"end":{"line":668},"content_hash":"b0dcbfdc79cdeff6cae85840312f17c0beb5b7f0d0dbe37417938cb385692508"},"summary":"A recommendation for the owner's question: cap `script.max_calls` under `NOTICE_SEQ`, and keep `StepCx::place`'s refusal as the backstop","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.640922Z","id":"c4c1ece6d46667a7d1a8821b0ee0d6b794f97d169403a4bbfa7e82fa8ae8af6c","body":{"detail":"The lib test log of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all` lists `surface::tests::a_charge_on_an_answer_the_core_journals_is_refused - should panic ... ok`, so the tests are built with debug assertions and an in-tree provider that charges on such an answer fails its tests. In a release build nothing stops it: the first attempt's `calls` entry shows the cost, the core's row keeps none, so a step run again shows no cost for the call; and the charge itself was made outside the row's transaction, so an attempt that dies between the charge and the core's row makes the call, and the charge, again unless the provider keyed it. The core never bills from `CallNote::cost_milli`. That is enough while every provider is in this tree. Before a provider outside it can answer (the wire's adapter), make it a rule the release build keeps: `Claim::record` drops the cost from what the run shows and logs it, or `charged` exists only on what `Answer::written` returns.","kind":"comment","references":"014e16a2dd786801ca363b3ff551c137bb9d1b45c8f32e02ca917aa511f481d1","span":{"start":{"line":790},"end":{"line":900},"content_hash":"53568a0fb9b941b19319683386dce91a73f52bfcfb779987cf2944b3de368bc6"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: `undeclared`'s answer is always journaled as a refusal; a charge on a core-journaled answer is refused in builds with debug assertions, which the tests are","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:13:35.641309Z","id":"987481417fd6f7f152c0bf43f85c3b0deb6771b37726f192abe597c54c05f7d2","body":{"detail":"`bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration --test_arg=providers::`, one change to `FakeSurfaceProvider` at a time: a second `ended` answering an error now fails `ending_is_repeatable_inside_the_terminal_step_and_ended_after_its_commit`; `fake.set` answering `Answer::written` with no row fails `a_write_repeated_with_its_key_applies_once_and_answers_the_same` (\"the write's place has no journal row\"); the seven others fail as before. `check_provider(f, not_applicable)`: a misspelt name fails `the_cases_named_as_not_applicable_are_cases_of_the_suite` and leaves its case failing; a named case that was played fails. What it cannot hold: a fixture that says `Maybe::Lacks` for something its provider has, with the case named, passes (both are written by one author; the reason is there to be read); `Maybe::Lacks(\"\")` is accepted; `ProviderFixture::disturb` and `lifecycle` are still `Option`s nobody has to account for, and with `disturb: None` and a read the write does not change, a provider that answers a replayed read afresh passes. The reason the adapter gives for its `begin` (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 1889-1893) is true: `ClaxProvider::begin` (167-185) fails only when the store has no row for the run or `Draft::load` fails, and it only reads.","kind":"comment","references":"c6fbafa01696d2c9bbd0c8f406362de71f31779a22f45dad558010655fae3c16","span":{"start":{"line":130},"end":{"line":150},"content_hash":"a59e640590f6d0bb151d85293d51a703af6fbd45e3e706837126555c1950bd2c"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: nine breaks of the fake each fail under the case that holds the rule; what a fixture's author can still leave out","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.191082Z","id":"b0ffa31367f2be290021a75f8f629269e13a5f1c9495ec13812828cac6d0f0c9","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields(into, merged, core, surface, fields)` (surface.rs 1307) takes a `Merged` (1247) that the core keeps for a step's payload and another for what its model is told. The join arm applies only when `merged` says the name under the surface's name was made by that surface; any other value there, an object too, is a `FieldClash`. No list of `clax`'s field names is kept, so nothing has to stay complete. Unit tests: `a_surface_named_as_a_field_of_claxs_clashes_whichever_is_merged_first` (2481) holds the case of this record (`published` over `{version: 2}` clashes and is not merged into), the other order (the host surface first, then `clax`: the clash is `clax`'s, for the same name), and an object the core put under a surface's name; `a_field_that_takes_a_name_of_the_cores_or_one_taken_is_the_providers_error` (2390) holds `clax` and another surface each answering one of its own fields twice, and that a refused merge leaves both the fields and `merged` as they were. With the guard removed (`if own || true`), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` fails `a_surface_named_as_a_field_of_claxs_clashes_whichever_is_merged_first` and passes without the change.","kind":"comment","references":"4bba8ddd91c12a845fcef7887f62cc5cfba0cc927b8e7cbc29b01effdbbf99d7","span":{"start":{"line":1240},"end":{"line":1355},"content_hash":"bd93999949626fae7be1e5d26c33c1b0a556a8b89fa5945bd8ec2f1432484bd3"},"summary":"Fixed by tracking: the caller holds which surface made each top-level name (`Merged`), and a surface's fields join only the object its own earlier merge made","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.191486Z","id":"0aa3b5c4c4fbfe669948a80da5639b93c9f356436b8cba99bcfc7e6366a0da5d","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (364): the first attempt's script (through `Another`) now makes the stored script's first call, `files.list`, then `files.read`, `threads.reply` and `artifact.get`; the second attempt is answered `files.list` again and diverges at call 1. The fixture gained `result` (the step's `tool_result`, parsed): `replay: {completed: [\"files.list\"], diverged_at: 1, why}`. Its audit now has a `bot.call` that says `replayed` (`files.list`), the diverged `threads.reply` (`ok: false`), and a record with `attempt: 1` and `seq` for each of rows 2 and 3. Only that fixture was regenerated (`git diff --numstat 9da7cb463 HEAD -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden`: audit.replay-diverged.json 82 added, 44 removed; no other fixture). With line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails that test and passes without the change. `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 still diverge at call 0 by a hand-written row; they are left as they were.","kind":"comment","references":"e65c2488200d29c0ef288c65847a753f02b1e88ee3e17ec7f7c256137f245965","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":513},"content_hash":"331d0ccb3052c72097c372911f08deafdafca36960c23c77c327fc73f25a612a"},"summary":"`audit.replay-diverged.json` is now of a replay that diverges at its second call and pins what the model is told: `completed: [\"files.list\"]`, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows in one step","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.191907Z","id":"397fe9a589272aaaacd35507ae47e6131982fa6c032e0cde8b39e62f51a7c228","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, with `infra_lines` on: `a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call` (249: `max_steps` 2, a turn, a script of five calls, then the cap: the requests line is 1, 6, 1) and `a_cancelled_scripts_stopped_step_counts_no_call_on_its_requests_line` (287: a script cancelled once its second call is answered: 1 for the turn and 1 for the script's step committed as `stopped`). With line 1570 as `5`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails both and passes without the change. Not done: (2) a stop without `unreported_calls` and (3) a notice's row getting a `bot.call` record still pass every test, since no ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); nor is a replayed call's `ms` of 0 pinned (the goldens replace every `ms` by a name). Adding `p.audit()` to an ending fixture changes bytes that are pinned, which this branch was not to do; both are in the reply on record 264e99a6.","kind":"comment","references":"d016f72848eaf6b4384062f378e2ac86772fcfeee80bff93b574f9e676b778c1","span":{"start":{"line":1566},"end":{"line":1573},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"summary":"A `stopped` step's requests line is held for a cap stop after a script and for a script cancelled after two calls; the two audit-only changes are still unpinned and are passed to the change that moves the endings","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.192304Z","id":"0495d515e2ea154d15cd4b9c2af410abf3c6df6334131a5c627e01c82bd4f114","body":{"detail":"(1) Row 3 (surface.rs 52) and `no_place` (978-992): both of the core's unjournaled refusals, `replay_diverged` and `no_place`, are told as any call's answer is (the step's `calls`, a `call` event, a `bot.call` record, no target) and counted on the requests line, as crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs `replay_diverged`. The proof's `Stepper` logs a `no_place` refusal in its `calls` to agree (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, `Stepper::call`); nothing else in this crate numbers a script's calls through the seam yet. (2) `no_place`'s documentation says it is a backstop under the sandbox's code, an error a script can catch where the sandbox's limit ends the script, reached only while `script.max_calls` may exceed `NOTICE_SEQ`; the code is kept. (3) 136-141: a row of either form answers the same through `from_entry`. (4) `Answer`'s table has one row for each constructor and a paragraph after it (810-815): a replayed answer is journaled by no one, and its constructor matters for the charge alone. (5) 257-262, `merge_fields`'s errors and `ending`'s: in the `failed` step a clash is logged, none of that binding's fields are merged, the other bindings' are kept, and the step commits. (6) `CallNote::extra` (763-768): the field named `web` is what the core puts in `Event::Call`'s `web`. (7) 350-352: `declared` \"is not part of the crate's public interface\", true whether it is private or `pub(crate)`. Also from the same reading: `ended` asks a provider to remember a run only until it has been told `ended` once (1644-1649), with no read of the run's row.","kind":"comment","references":"3e8f245d6e726b970474b491a421defbcc13cdaa352b3c974bfee1cc7159f49a","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"0fc2d506f54bc197809c1a054681fcdab60befe90f98487507cc5e92fd6c120b"},"summary":"Seven of the eight are answered in the text; where another surface's standing operations come from and who resolves the request stay in their own records","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.192687Z","id":"9e3764d4ad3cc129debb08d9781771061d40d1388514ccc0ea4b9c63e699fec4","body":{"detail":"As reply 432832ac recommends. (1) Cap the setting under `NOTICE_SEQ` (1,000,000) where it is validated (crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 80, 226-229: `script.max_calls` is a `Positive`, 1 to 1,000,000,000,000). (2) Clamp a value already stored above the cap where `RunSettings` reads it (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 175-176), so an instance that set one before the cap is not left with it. (3) Keep `StepCx::place` answering `None` from `NOTICE_SEQ` up and `no_place` as the backstop: its documentation (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 978-992) now says a model should never meet it once the setting is capped, and that it shares the sandbox's code `call_limit` while being an error a script can catch. None of the three is done on this branch: bot_settings.rs and run.rs were not to change here. The owner's to decide.","kind":"comment","references":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","span":{"start":{"line":659},"end":{"line":677},"content_hash":"02f30895f67db50d599777221507d1bef19ad1b6a160bf67447236dac495da05"},"summary":"The recommendation on record for the owner: cap `script.max_calls` under `NOTICE_SEQ`, clamp a stored larger value where `RunSettings` reads it, and keep `place`'s refusal as a backstop","tags":["seam","fix-round","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.193063Z","id":"668d5cef767d9be5655b5bce816bca5005472c5df975eef99d9060ae4c9266d5","body":{"detail":"(a) Record 0d124949, cited in (2), was re-recorded at its lines and is now 964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb (same thread, still `status:needs-decision`). (b) Line references in this record are of the seam before commit 30c3673b2; the items are found by name: `no_place` and `StepCx::place` for (4), \"What the rows above do not show\" for (7), `Binding::from_stored` for (8). (c) For (3): `audit.replay-diverged.json` now pins a replay that diverges at its second call, with what the model is told (`replay.completed`), a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows; the divergence check written behind the seam must leave that fixture as it is. A refusal for `replay_diverged` or `no_place` is told in the step's `calls`, the events and a `bot.call` record and counted on the requests line, and is not journaled (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs row 3). (d) A replayed call is logged with `ms` 0 (477-481 here): no test holds it, since the goldens replace every `ms` by a name (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 123); pin it, or keep it by reading. (e) `merge_fields` takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for a step's payload and another for what its model is told: one of each for each step, made before the first session's parts are merged. (f) The real provider's conformance fixture now has to say of `disturb` and `lifecycle`, as of `staged` and `fail_once`, that it has one or why not (`Maybe`), and the caller names every case not played.","kind":"comment","references":"a4777d70312059a23561d5a4bcc760877132fed33e98f187b7e9b46a5d5ffcf0","span":{"start":{"line":343},"end":{"line":452},"content_hash":"775ebefe9eba51456748adebbbbb5159bb8871891790ef2bd4c8d76927ce1e51"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, `merge_fields`' new argument, and two more things to carry","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:26:08.193469Z","id":"f6f9936a2abce982153210aeb12fd67a85a1c6d0bc56de71758802b7e2cc4572","body":{"detail":"(a) Records b25bed99 and f2d3e3a6, cited in (4) and (5), were re-recorded at their lines and are now a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c and 68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10 (the same threads). (b) Two audit-only behaviours of a stop pass every test when changed (record d016f728): `stop` without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (1574-1575 here: no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported), and `unreported_calls` without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (1539: every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record). No ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); pin `p.audit()` for one stopped ending and the cancelled one before these lines move. With several bindings the second matters more: the places of every binding's notices are at or above `NOTICE_SEQ` (`StepCx::notice`), and none should be audited as a script's call. (c) A `stopped` step's requests line is now held (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 249, 287): 1, whatever calls the script made. (d) To test the clash rule in a run loop, a provider is needed that answers a field named `calls` (or another of `PAYLOAD_KEYS`) from `finish` or `ending`: `FakeSurfaceProvider` answers only `fake_set` and `fake_notice`, so give it a way to answer a field a test names. The rule to hold: the attempt counted as a failed commit, the run ended `failed` at the bound, and in the `failed` step the clashing binding's fields left out and the others kept (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, `merge_fields`). `merge_fields` now takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for the step.","kind":"comment","references":"264e99a6077b972f7feab8ab52fcfe936f76991cfd343120ffb97626914b97bc","span":{"start":{"line":1549},"end":{"line":1740},"content_hash":"878cd3244c307349854d1a55547367aaeeb12163f1aedbbe15872bd0cd79974b"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, and three things about a stop's audit and a clash that no test holds","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:37:54.952749Z","id":"2a8ec75d373e99d8265ebdb2be16757242377e4fdc2cf97c60d6c237e97eb482","body":{"detail":"Read against `merge_fields` (1307-1353): a name at the top is the core's (refused by the `core` list or by being there), `clax`'s (recorded as made by `clax`), or a surface's own object (recorded under the surface's own name, which only that surface's first merge writes). A surface joins only a name `Merged` says it made, so `published` over `clax`'s object clashes, and merged in the other order `clax` clashes on the same name: the verdict is a clash either way and only the binding blamed differs. A refused merge changes neither value (`clash_of` in this file's tests holds both). What the type does not prevent: `Merged` is not tied to the `Fields` it describes. A fresh `Merged` with fields already merged into gives a clash that is not one; a `Merged` kept from another step, or the payload's used for what the model is told, says a surface made a name that something else put there, and the join arm then adds to that object. The documentation (1239-1245) says one is kept for the payload and one for what the model is told; nothing checks it.","kind":"comment","references":"4bba8ddd91c12a845fcef7887f62cc5cfba0cc927b8e7cbc29b01effdbbf99d7","span":{"start":{"line":1307},"end":{"line":1353},"content_hash":"350380081d0348c4c49a815597eef4cbd0740e64e32c2a2e8218d0782d232b53"},"suggested_fix":"When a run loop first calls this, hold the fields and what made them in one value (a struct with both, whose `merge` takes the surface and its fields), so they cannot be separated or reused.","summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: no order of merges puts one surface's fields in another's object or over a core name; `Merged` and the fields it describes are still two values the caller must keep together","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:37:54.953638Z","id":"4a9331a7178b02dfc79fdbb75a8442cbd63b8a48db14ce7c06443107ce40ce7d","body":{"detail":"`bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (191 tests) with the change fails `golden::resumed::the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`. The fixture's diff from 9da7cb463: the first attempt's rows are `files.list`, `files.read`, `threads.reply`, `artifact.get` at places 0 to 3 (the reply's key moves from place 1 to 2); the committing attempt's `files.list` is `ok` and `replayed`; the divergence is at call 1 (`threads.reply` where the journal has `files.read`); the unreported rows are places 2 and 3; and `result` is new. No other fixture changed. A divergence after a call answered again is what a run can produce; the two older tests that write a row at place 0 by hand (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs 258, crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200) still show one it cannot.","kind":"comment","references":"e65c2488200d29c0ef288c65847a753f02b1e88ee3e17ec7f7c256137f245965","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":513},"content_hash":"331d0ccb3052c72097c372911f08deafdafca36960c23c77c327fc73f25a612a"},"summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` now fails the fixture's test, and every change to `audit.replay-diverged.json` follows from the first attempt's new order of calls","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:37:54.954289Z","id":"67cbb9c8b9216633f709e53d8f198bc3ce131fcfa96a527473fc9fcb460ffcfb","body":{"detail":"`golden::resumed::a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call` and `a_cancelled_scripts_stopped_step_counts_no_call_on_its_requests_line` fail with the change. Read against the code: a turn charges `1 + 0` (`step_usage`, 1080), a script step `1 + st.calls.len()` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 702-708: 6 for five calls), a cap stop is a new step charged `1 + 0` here (1567-1573), which gives 1, 6, 1; a cancelled script's step is begun again as `stopped` under the same number (1558) and charged here with no call counted, which gives 1 for the turn and 1 for step 2. Still passing every test when changed, and audit only: 1574-1575 without `unreported_calls`, 1539 without the `NOTICE_SEQ` filter; and crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 (`ms` of a replayed call).","kind":"comment","references":"d016f72848eaf6b4384062f378e2ac86772fcfeee80bff93b574f9e676b778c1","span":{"start":{"line":1566},"end":{"line":1573},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: line 1570 as `5` fails both new tests, and their quantities are what this file charges","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T08:37:54.954868Z","id":"2c26d4e3221101cff03cc6664d8085b00be98dd2bafb0199b44b014de2fa53ad","body":{"detail":"(1) Where another surface's standing operations come from and who resolves the request at the first turn are still open in their own records. (2) After a merge with branch `claxbot-surfaces` (tip 1b2d5f9e7 merges with no conflict and `//crates/clax-bot:all`, `//crates/clax-surface:all`, `//crates/clax-commands:all`, `//bazel/wasm:check`, `//scripts:crate_layers` and `//scripts:no_unsafe` pass on the merged tree), `declared` is at crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 221 and `pub(crate)`: the records that cite api.rs by line (216-218, 253) point elsewhere, and the sentence at 349-353 here stays true.","kind":"comment","references":"3e8f245d6e726b970474b491a421defbcc13cdaa352b3c974bfee1cc7159f49a","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"0fc2d506f54bc197809c1a054681fcdab60befe90f98487507cc5e92fd6c120b"},"summary":"Read again at 1473f04ce: the seven are answered in the text; two things are left for the change that moves script.rs to settle","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135374Z","id":"adbdee05a292a38e81d4177c84a1ddd625abdbd30b2379c5ab4005270b1c7d9b","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:859-861 gives `RunPolicy::settings(&self, run) -> Result<RunSettings>`. Here the tick still reads the instance's settings from `Deps.settings` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1162), because the claim's lease is needed before the run is loaded and the driver reads concurrency with no run in hand (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:214, :392), and then passes them to `RunPolicy::run_settings(run, instance)` and `limits(run, settings)`. That costs no extra read a tick and leaves `Deps.settings` as it was for the server and the Worker. What it gives up: `Deps` carries two things (`settings`, `policy`), and because of the blanket impl at policy.rs:381 a type that is a SettingsSource cannot also implement RunPolicy with answers of its own (the impls would overlap), so an environment that wants both writes two types and passes the second to `Deps::with_policy`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":342},"end":{"line":381},"content_hash":"b15d048076c291f0d15998aa9809b0ae2a3d3504c9edd396d02f31f6a1a7fe0a"},"summary":"A policy that is the one source of a run's settings (`settings(run)`, as the design draws it), with the blanket impl bridging a SettingsSource to it","tags":["revisit:the tick holds a RunInfo before it claims, or the server's or the Worker's settings type needs limits of its own","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135444Z","id":"c82f2d94df29a1cf2dd450a59ae93427b737287cd38e87dccad6158bb85295a8","body":{"detail":"`run_settings`, `limits` and `history` take `&clax_store::bot_runs::Run`, the only description of a run the tick has today (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1180-1181, :1934, :1994). The design's policy is told a `RunInfo` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs:386), which names no artifact or thread. When the tick holds one, these parameters change type: a breaking change for implementers, of which there is one today (`Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs). The three questions the design adds (`accepts`, `to_model`, `delivery`) are not methods yet; each can be added with a default without changing an implementer.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":342},"end":{"line":376},"content_hash":"f45e035530013b798bc1eea877cbe3d7cc73c467a70fd5b8899fd9456d924a2d"},"summary":"For the change that gives the tick a `RunInfo`: every `RunPolicy` method takes the store's `Run` row","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135477Z","id":"206c56a24024af6bd8494720e35437f7a45f32e39cb633bbe38f54cd1c71487b","body":{"detail":"`prompt_too_long` matches four phrases in the message of a 400 (`prompt is too long`, `context_length_exceeded`, `maximum context length`, `context window`). They are from memory of the Messages, Chat Completions and Responses APIs, not from a recorded answer: crates/clax-sample has no typed error for it (`MessagesError::InvalidRequest` carries only the text, crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:618). A provider that says it otherwise takes the `model_error` stop, which is the behaviour before the policy, so nothing is lost under the default; a compacting policy would not be asked. A phrase that matched a refusal for another reason would ask the policy for a shorter history once per overflow, bounded as any overflow is. The fix belongs in clax-sample: a typed variant set from the provider's error type, with recorded answers as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":315},"end":{"line":337},"content_hash":"d0a2c3ed0151d219afb80a8997313b0a88157942891d91b46588eec8caf30afa"},"summary":"A refusal for length is recognised by the provider's words, which no test checks against a provider","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135569Z","id":"440572f80f033b777bbfd15e2cc478868a35a92f655c68d68268ce0da54bf3b7","body":{"detail":"The overflow plumbing is whole for a policy that is a pure function of the core's messages and a level (held by crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs with a fake that drops the oldest tool results). What it does not have:\n\n1. The overflowing answer's blocks stay in the core's projection: `History::of` pushes every turn's assistant blocks (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:626-627), as it does for `pause_turn`, so after a `stop_reason` overflow the next request ends with that partial assistant message unless the policy drops it. `overflowed` rebuilds that projection by hand (`hist.messages`, then the turn's request, then the answer's blocks) because the fold has no way to say what the next tick's projection will be. The projection function should take the committed steps plus one uncommitted turn, so this is computed once.\n2. A policy is given messages, not steps: it cannot tell which step a message came from, so it cannot keep a tool call and its result together by step or summarise by step. The projection should say, for each message or block, the step it came from.\n3. A policy has nowhere to keep a summary: the design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:880-888) lets it send one it keeps, recorded by hash. Here a turn records `payload.shown` (level, bytes, SHA-256) and the overflowing turn `next.overflow.shown_sha256`, which name what was sent but do not hold it, so a run's exact context is rebuilt only by asking the same policy again.\n4. Nothing checks what a policy answers: messages with a tool result and no call, or two of one role, reach the provider, and its refusal stops the run (`model_error`).\n5. Each shorter history changes the cached prefix; the turn after an overflow pays the cache-write rate for all of it, and `affordable` (run.rs:1817) prices it from the last turn's usage, which after a refused request is zero.\n6. `HistoryAsk.level` only grows. A long session that is compacted many times needs the policy to decide what a level means; nothing lowers it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1911},"end":{"line":1950},"content_hash":"ff37201a5067a2b634bcc57e1ca5a18795ef92cf90c921339b43f1442bd3b16a"},"summary":"What a real compactor still needs from the run loop, and what the message projection must provide for it","tags":["for-t3","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135647Z","id":"d0b9bda459fa18e6b251bca7963a46ddaba4f4096308c19493530dd6f4782464","body":{"detail":"Only under a policy that shows less (never under the default, whose fixtures in crates/clax-bot/tests/golden are unchanged): a `turn` step with `assistant: []`, `stop_reason: \"prompt_too_long\"`, zero usage and `next.overflow`; a turn after it with `payload.shown`; and a `bot.turn` audit record with that stop reason and zero tokens. It takes a step number, so it counts toward `max_steps`, and step 1's harness fee is charged on it as on any first step. `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:297-316) shows it as a turn with no text and no tool; the run view and the HTTP run JSON were not looked at. Whoever first ships a policy that shows less checks those readers and pins the shapes as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2276},"end":{"line":2282},"content_hash":"cd4b34db76207885c770671ec43566b4c81e00010452727d08cb7b35988523b8"},"summary":"A refused request that goes on is committed as a turn with no answer, a shape no reader of steps has seen","tags":["for-t8","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135709Z","id":"ca5143221400e33c59ab076ccebbb2e20b3df11001735aec7846bb53830317e9","body":{"detail":"`tools(self.limits.script.max_result_bytes)` puts the sandbox's limit on a returned value in the words the model reads (`policy::bytes_text`: `64 KiB` for a whole number of KiB, else `<n> bytes`). Under the default it is `RunSettings::limits()`, whose `max_result_bytes` is always `Limits::default()`'s 64 KiB, so the text cannot change. A policy that answers another size on a later tick of one run changes a tool's description mid-run: the model was told another number, and the cached prefix is lost. docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:94 calls a declaration that changes during a run a failure. `RunPolicy`'s documentation states the rule (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:338-341); a fix that enforces it stores the limits the model was told with the run's first turn and reads them back.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":694},"end":{"line":712},"content_hash":"3b2367aa813c553eb10e0edf87e1fc8636d7bbea89c603409598e06ccd642d1b"},"summary":"The `run_script` description is built from the policy's answer on every turn, and nothing holds that answer still for a run","tags":["for-t9","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135741Z","id":"ea37b8c0a1989a8de7dedfb92442236bafad3225e89617e3dcc1ef596c6e5da1","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits::of` sets `model_call_timeout` to the settings' `max_run_minutes` (30 minutes by default). Under the default policy it never binds, since what is left of the run's time is never more. A policy that sets `max_run_minutes = None` and leaves the timeout gets calls of at most the settings' minutes each, on a native driver with no tick budget; before this, the only bounds on a call were the run's remaining time, the tick's budget, and the client's 120 s for headers and 90 s between pieces (crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:1113-1120), which bound a silence and not a call. The design names 'a floor for the model call's own timeout' as work of its own (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:968). `max_overflow_retries` is `Some(4)`: unreachable under the default policy, and a guess for any other. Question for the owner: what one model call may take where no run cap is set (a setting of its own, or the minutes kept as now), and whether a default bound on overflows in a row is wanted at all beside the rule that each history is shorter than the last.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":131},"end":{"line":142},"content_hash":"5e0474a377932fb8ecc03b26423cb6d544127b2bd842f6fe30ed302debd08c66"},"summary":"Two defaults chosen here with nothing behind them: the model call's timeout when a policy sets no minutes, and four overflows in a row","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.135835Z","id":"30795868a45fb7ce9e76dfe33c0371a0dbbe38c2ca967e4d358dcaee60ec969f","body":{"detail":"They say how long a driver leaves a run it could not tick (a sandbox that was unavailable, a run another driver holds). They bound no run: a stalled run is tried again for ever. They are not answers of `RunPolicy` (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:342), which is asked about one run inside a tick; `settle` would have to ask for a run it does not hold. They belong with what constructs a `Driver` (the daemon and the Worker differ: an alarm cannot wait 1 s), as `Deps.retry_later` already is (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:268). Left as they are because nothing asks for another value yet.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":39},"end":{"line":42},"content_hash":"2c661190a79f10e73a10ac3a6010a455250645bdf74113f41aa14ff86b0b2f7b"},"summary":"The driver's waits (`STALL_FIRST`, `STALL_MAX`, `NOT_OURS_WAIT`) stay constants: they are the driver's, not a run's","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.136084Z","id":"7236efaf4e0fde264579200e676b68b8722da37cff67547c18832f0587c0a83c","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_no_cap_or_limit_but_the_policys` includes `run.rs` and `script.rs` as text and fails when either names `settings.max_steps`, `settings.max_run_credits`, `settings.max_run_minutes`, `settings.limits()`, the old constants, `at most 64` or `<< 10`, or declares any `const`; and when `run.rs` or `script.rs` no longer reads the `limits.*` fields. A cap read through another binding (`let s = &self.settings; s.max_steps`) passes it, and a file that code moves to is not looked at. When a script's calls move to `clax/*` and the endings to a provider, add those files to the list. `RunLimits::observe_every` has no test of its effect: an attempt's observed entry replaces its last (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1429-1432) and the final figures are recorded whatever the cadence, so only its default and that `run.rs` reads it are held. Keep reading the tool result's size from `Tick.limits.tool_result_bytes` and the sandbox's limits from `Tick.limits.script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":534},"end":{"line":583},"content_hash":"d3671cf7098faddef849f5de410e3af96aef94bff34f905e23c2724b48e8eb20"},"summary":"The check that the run loop reads no cap of its own is on the text of two files, by name","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.136147Z","id":"5df7cce7fd157a9fa8e674dd920916942947b88549d37cc2e72829ea31e5ff99","body":{"detail":"`must_stop`, `minutes_stop` (run.rs:1318-1324) and the credits stop in `affordable` format `<n> steps`, `<n> credits` and `<n> minutes` from `Tick.limits`, which a policy may answer differently from `RunSettings` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: `limit of 1 steps`, `limit of 5 minutes`). `End::Stopped`, which is to carry the limit's value to a provider's `ending`, takes it from the same place; a run with no cap never produces one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1520},"end":{"line":1543},"content_hash":"871e5b277342c83d4789681df670bedca35eb2161beb7735b4564f49f13cf7df"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings: a cap's value in a notice comes from the policy's limits, not the settings","tags":["for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.136232Z","id":"7b6f18d927fd901364981e3345bda4d24fbecc5caf0a89f93ef9d5f41a151280","body":{"detail":"The constant in crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs became a policy value (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:110, default `DEFAULT_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, the same 100 KiB). The change that moves the tool result's building behind the seam reads it from the tick's limits and corrects this sentence.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":173},"end":{"line":173},"content_hash":"2aab44bd81c81b3ca16033e091b092b68a146f7af5dce18b16f6aabef928ab74"},"summary":"This line names `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, which is gone: the size is `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`","tags":["for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:56:24.136967Z","id":"068dcc18b06f6a27689f04f68145a1c0c3581e24498f7f4f3c139e592dbccad7","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs passes `self.limits.script` to the sandbox, and `RunLimits::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:131) fills it from `RunSettings::limits()`, so under the default policy `max_calls` is the setting's value and its range is untouched. A policy written in code may answer any `max_calls`, including one at or over `NOTICE_SEQ`, without passing through the setting. So a cap on the setting alone would not close this for a policy; a clamp where the run loop hands the limits to the sandbox would. The decision stays the owner's.","kind":"comment","references":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","span":{"start":{"line":650},"end":{"line":668},"content_hash":"ac6172417eb77e75da3a8a046cc2306d468700a33e06b01c8fee3a9594291038"},"summary":"A policy can now set a script's `max_calls` with no range at all: `RunLimits::script` is the sandbox's `Limits`, passed as the policy answers it","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.216499Z","id":"13ace998439c84a96a97e064ac9d8feff917e2f92275d76d4cafcabf476ddfaa","body":{"detail":"`overflowed` (run.rs:1911-1948) lets the run go on when the policy's answer to `HistoryFor::Overflow` has fewer bytes than the messages that overflowed, and records `next.overflow.shown_sha256`. The next tick's `turn` asks again with `HistoryFor::NextTurn` at the same level (run.rs:1993-2001) and sends that answer, whatever it is; it is not compared with the recorded hash or size. A policy that answers an overflow with less and the next turn with `Shown::Core` passes the rule each time and is never sent shorter. Measured with such a policy, `max_overflow_retries = None`, `max_steps = None` and a provider that refuses every request as too long: 30 ticks, 30 committed turns, 30 requests with identical messages, the run still `running`, 1 credit charged (step 1's fee). With the default `max_overflow_retries` of 4 the same policy ends after 4 such turns. On the stop-reason path each such call is answered and charged, so only the person's credits end it. The module documentation (policy.rs:32-37, 'cannot make the run loop ask twice') and `RunLimits::max_overflow_retries` ('leaves only the rule that each answer is shorter than the last', policy.rs:118-122) state more than the code holds.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1991},"end":{"line":2002},"content_hash":"3f45579992e5fb69237b4e894a5b6955060c0df879c355176c808987999515d2"},"suggested_fix":"In `turn`, when the last committed step's `next.overflow` is set, take the policy's answer only if it is `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` is `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (or at least whose bytes are no more than `shown_bytes`); otherwise end the run as `overflowed` returning `None` does. Then a run's overflows in a row are bounded by its bytes even with no `max_overflow_retries`. Or say in the documentation that the bound is `max_overflow_retries` alone and that `None` trusts the policy.","summary":"The turn after an overflow sends whatever the policy answers then: nothing holds it to the shorter history the overflow's ask answered, so the rule that each history is shorter is kept per ask and not per run","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.216605Z","id":"81989257f90ec309b92d3bb2913c3595b1cacc7e834d4c04babc0c511d40ff7e","body":{"detail":"`tick` asks `run_settings` and `limits` after the claim and before `Tick::step`, so before `must_stop` (run.rs:1506-1509) reads a cancel. An `Err` from either is the tick's error: `Driver::settle` logs it, releases the run and tries again after `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (30 s, driver.rs:345-348), for ever. Measured with a policy whose `limits` fails: the tick answers `Err`, the run stays `running` with no step and no notice; a person who stops it gets a run that stays `stopping`, since no tick reaches `must_stop`. An `Err` from `history` in `overflowed` on the stop-reason path (run.rs:2261-2263) comes after the provider answered: the turn stays open, the answer's usage is written as absorbed rows (4 rows in the measurement: the instance's cost, no credits), and the next tick calls the model again with the same full window. While the policy keeps failing that is one absorbed full-window call every 30 s, bounded only by the run's minutes if it has any, and not by the person's credits. No policy fails under the defaults (`DefaultPolicy` answers `Ok`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1180},"end":{"line":1181},"content_hash":"15c45d8c3198c9ca311bdfc871fd4a5f14a124170097f7cb3c71aabf2b15811f"},"suggested_fix":"Read the cancel before asking the policy, and count a policy's failures on a run as commit failures are counted (`max_commit_failures`, run.rs:1697-1703), ending the run `failed` with its notice after that many. In `overflowed`, treat an `Err` as `Shown::Core` (the run ends as the default does, and the answer is committed and charged once).","summary":"A policy that fails leaves a run running that cannot be cancelled, and after a full window drops an answer the instance paid for, again on every retry","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.216645Z","id":"e4519b05f96adb2392e91d8d578b4cac6b4a524d8828c9a45a835268e2f288dc","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits` is `#[non_exhaustive]` with public fields, so outside clax-bot it cannot be written as a literal or with `..`: the one constructor is `RunLimits::of(&settings)`, then assignments (as `Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:104-108 does). `of` answers `Some` for the three caps, `model_call_timeout` and `max_overflow_retries`. An environment that wants a run with no bound writes five assignments; one that forgets `model_call_timeout` keeps calls cut at the settings' minutes (retried, see below), and when a sixth bound is added every such policy gets it set without a change to its source. That is the opposite of the module's rule that a cap the environment does not set is not replaced by one of the run loop's (policy.rs:10-13). Neither the struct nor `of` says that `of` is the way to make one. `HistoryAsk` is also `#[non_exhaustive]` and its `new` is `pub(crate)` (policy.rs:232), so a host cannot construct an ask to test its own `history` outside a tick. What `model_call_timeout` does when it is the bound that fires is not written either: the call ends as a cut stream, which is retried in the tick and then waits `Deps.retry_later`; it is never an ending. Measured with `model_call_timeout = Some(0)`: every tick answers `Waiting(Retry(30s))` and the run stays `running`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":88},"end":{"line":142},"content_hash":"7f6c3eac47826d8eb01200aa50264d519cd4bc6b6de6f3be72bd77056f3e0c77"},"suggested_fix":"Add `RunLimits::unbounded(&RunSettings)` (or `none`): every `Option` `None`, the sizes from the settings and the defaults, documented as what a policy starts from when it means no caps, and say on `of` that it is today's bounds. Make `HistoryAsk::new` public. Say on `model_call_timeout` that a call it cuts is tried again and ends nothing.","summary":"A policy can build its limits only from `RunLimits::of`, which sets every bound: one that means 'no caps' must clear each by name, and a bound added later arrives set","tags":["review","verify-t4","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.217291Z","id":"833bcbf7f9e3a305a031b91c584e73a2cdb88f2a0619cb6a0e439c8d6f819e92","body":{"detail":"With policy.rs:381 (`impl<S: SettingsSource + ?Sized> RunPolicy for S {}`) removed, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` passes (2 of 2 targets; the only new diagnostic is an unused import of `SettingsSource` in policy.rs). `Deps::new` sets `DefaultPolicy` itself (run.rs:307), so callers are unchanged without the impl. `grep -rn 'RunPolicy\\|with_policy' crates --include='*.rs'` finds only crates/clax-bot/src/{lib,policy,run}.rs and crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs. The types that will first want answers of their own are the ones that already read the settings from the store: `StoredSettings` (crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs:299) and `Settings` (crates/clax-http/src/bot.rs:175). With the impl in place each needs a second type that wraps the first; removing the impl after a host relies on `Arc<its settings>` as a policy is a breaking change, and today none does.","kind":"comment","references":"adbdee05a292a38e81d4177c84a1ddd625abdbd30b2379c5ab4005270b1c7d9b","summary":"Nothing uses the blanket impl: with it deleted clax-bot builds and every test passes, and no other crate names `RunPolicy` or `with_policy`. Dropping it now costs nothing and frees a settings type to be a policy","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.217658Z","id":"5ceffa43a6c6cf104ebb6b6396287ba9738ed6cc9ff1cfc2ab87963328eae63f","body":{"detail":"`http_error` (crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:824-845) keeps `error.type` and `error.message` and drops `error.code`, so the needle `context_length_exceeded` matches only a provider that repeats the code in its message; the unit test's `Api { kind: \"context_length_exceeded\", message: \"context_length_exceeded\" }` (policy.rs) is not a shape `http_error` produces. A Gemini 400 has no `type`, so it is `InvalidRequest` with Google's message, which uses none of the four phrases. A provider error sent mid-stream is classified with status 0 (messages.rs:1065): `Api { status: 0 }` is outside `400..500` and is not matched. The Messages API's refusal when input plus `max_tokens` is over the window says 'exceed context limit', not matched (and there the remedy may be a smaller `max_tokens`, not a shorter history). Mutations run against `//crates/clax-bot:all`: deleting the `context window` needle, and widening `(400..500).contains(status)` to `*status < 500`, each pass every test. Under the default policy a hit and a miss end the run the same way (`a_refused_request_stops_the_run_as_any_model_error_by_default`, and `overflowed` returns `None` for `Shown::Core`), so the recogniser changes nothing a person sees until a policy shows less. Then a miss costs the feature on that provider (the run ends `model_error`), and a false hit costs up to `max_overflow_retries` refused requests, each a committed turn and a step, before the same ending.","kind":"comment","references":"206c56a24024af6bd8494720e35437f7a45f32e39cb633bbe38f54cd1c71487b","suggested_fix":"Until clax-sample has a typed variant, let the policy decide: pass the provider's error text in the ask for `OverflowPath::Refused`, or add a defaulted `RunPolicy` method that says whether an error is a full window, so an environment that knows its provider owns the words.","summary":"What the recogniser reads and misses, checked against crates/clax-sample: it sees only an error's `type` and `message`, never OpenAI's `code`; Gemini's refusal, a mid-stream error and the Messages API's 'exceed context limit' are not matched; `context window` is the loosest phrase and no test holds it","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.217991Z","id":"70a8db393890ce07966b703caef7c9af7010d9496362c671f1684af9adab1cf5","body":{"detail":"`git merge` of this branch into claxbot-surfaces-t3 conflicts once, in `tick` (the read of the run and its steps): keep that branch's `let (run, mut steps)` and this one's three lines after it (`run_settings`, `limits`, `Overflows::of(&steps)`, before the open step is taken out of `steps`). `turn` merges without a conflict: the core's projection is that branch's `transcript(&self.steps)`, its `messages` with the first request pushed, handed to `policy.history`; the name `shown` is then bound twice (the `Transcript`, then the policy's record) and wants renaming. `overflowed` no longer compiles, since `Tick.hist` is a `Control` with no `messages`: its first line becomes `let mut core = transcript(&self.steps).messages;`, followed as now by `push(&mut core, Role::User, user.to_vec())`, the measure of `sent`, and `push(&mut core, Role::Assistant, assistant.to_vec())`. That is the fold's own order for a turn (`user`, `assistant`, then `next.user`, which is empty), so the ask after an overflow and the next tick's ask see the same messages. It folds the steps a second time only when a window is full. With that, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` passes (2 of 2) and fmt and clippy are clean on the merge. Two more for the list above: (7) an overflowing turn's `next` has no `nudge`, so a model that was nudged, then overflowed, is nudged a second time before `no_tool_call` (measured: text, refused, text, text, give up; without the overflow: text, text, give up). (8) The core's projection is built whole on every tick from every step (`run_with_steps`, then the fold) before the policy is asked, so a policy can shorten what is sent but not what a tick loads: a run of any length is bounded by the memory of what ticks it.","kind":"comment","references":"440572f80f033b777bbfd15e2cc478868a35a92f655c68d68268ce0da54bf3b7","summary":"Merged with the branch that splits `History` (tip a337d9a6c): one textual conflict in `tick`, one line of `overflowed` to change, and then clax-bot builds and passes; two more things a compactor meets","tags":["review","verify-t4","for-t3","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.218318Z","id":"7a5747bd386c4c9186873a2d0a741c7dcf276a7e9cfa77bc7bb98f205ba9c56e","body":{"detail":"`step_view` (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs:1366-1400) and `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:297-315) read `assistant` as an array and give an empty `text` and no tool for `[]`; neither branches on `stop_reason`. The web run panel (web/shell/src/bot/model.ts) does not read `stop_reason`; how it draws a turn with no text was not looked at in a browser. Crash and resume, with a policy that drops the oldest tool results: a crash before and after the commit of the overflowing turn (step 3) and of the turn after it (step 4), on both paths, each resumed by a second driver to the same step kinds, `next.overflow.sent_bytes` and `shown_bytes`, `payload.shown.bytes`, credits and last request size as the run that did not crash, with step 4's `shown.sha256` equal to step 3's `next.overflow.shown_sha256` and the ledger's tokens equal to what the provider served. A transcript no provider takes (empty, an assistant message first, a tool result with no call, two user messages, a message with no content) is serialised for the Messages, Chat Completions and Gemini APIs without a panic and sent; nothing in the core checks it.","kind":"comment","references":"d0b9bda459fa18e6b251bca7963a46ddaba4f4096308c19493530dd6f4782464","summary":"The readers of a turn with no answer, looked at: the run view's JSON and `step_summary` take it; a crash at each point of an overflow resumes to the same steps","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.218699Z","id":"3639119150f005acad05e177e27a6f33633038a82a09ab64b72015e0952c952f","body":{"detail":"A cap added to `must_stop` as a literal (`self.hist.committed >= 1000`) passes this test and every other. A `static`, a literal `40`, a cap read through another binding or in another file pass it too, while a harmless `const` (a name, a format string) in run.rs or script.rs fails it. The ban gives a false sense that nothing is compiled in. What would hold it: a test that drives a run with every `Option` of `RunLimits` `None` well past the defaults (more steps than `max_steps`, more than a day on the injected clock, more credits than `max_run_credits`, several overflows) and sees it finish; crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs does this for each cap alone, by a few steps.","kind":"comment","references":"7236efaf4e0fde264579200e676b68b8722da37cff67547c18832f0587c0a83c","summary":"A recommendation: keep the half of this test that says the run loop reads `limits.*`, drop the ban on strings and constants, and hold 'no cap of the run loop's own' by a run instead","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.218736Z","id":"971dba6b4669f95d3bf8abb8dbc247b0208d8f3275d59a75271345d77b7027db","body":{"detail":"`spawn_forever` passes `Duration::from_secs(3600)` and `run_until_idle` hands what is left of it to each tick as `TickOpts.budget` (driver.rs:413), which `Tick::available` takes the least of with the run's time. So with `max_run_minutes = None` and `model_call_timeout = None` a call is still cut after at most an hour (less for a tick started late in the round), then tried again from its start. It bounds a call, not a run: the run goes on in the next round. It is the driver's constant, as the waits at driver.rs:39-42 are, and the Worker's is its alarm (`alarm_budget`, crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs:91). Named here because `RunLimits::model_call_timeout`'s `None` reads as 'the client's own deadlines only' (policy.rs:104-107).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":460},"end":{"line":464},"content_hash":"5cc282f5bfb9a578b981bac0aa1300504fafbb11d61d7297203cc29b9b7d4fdc"},"summary":"The daemon's driver gives every tick at most an hour (`run_until_idle(3600 s)`), which bounds one model call whatever the policy answers","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:13:58.219083Z","id":"35a9e6b579725b529bd799773a5b4beedaa7f814ab650198e97c25fc6f62ae50","body":{"detail":"Under the default policy the timeout cannot be the bound that fires: `Tick::remaining` is the minutes cap less a non-negative elapsed time, so `available()` is never more than the cap, and the cap is the timeout's value (`least(total, model_call_timeout) == total` for every run). Both drivers always pass a budget (driver.rs:413), which only lowers `total`. So `Some(minutes)` buys nothing by default, and for any other policy it is a second place the settings' minutes act after that policy set `max_run_minutes = None`: a hung provider is then cut every 30 minutes and retried without end, which is not what the minutes cap did (it ended the run). `None` keeps the default's behaviour byte for byte and removes the surprise; a floor or a setting of its own can come with the design's named work. On overflows: `Some(4)` is unreachable by default, and it is today the only thing that ends a policy that answers an overflow with less and the next turn with the core's projection (30 identical requests measured with `None`). Keep a default bound until `turn` checks the answer after an overflow against `next.overflow.shown_sha256`; after that the bytes rule alone ends every run and the default can be `None`.","kind":"comment","references":"ea37b8c0a1989a8de7dedfb92442236bafad3225e89617e3dcc1ef596c6e5da1","summary":"A recommendation on both defaults: make `model_call_timeout` `None` in `RunLimits::of`, and keep a default bound on overflows in a row until the turn after an overflow is held to the shorter history","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850082Z","id":"40f0a72a6b32d4d95eaa08ce63eb034dbfb83bb15f620f1237a4169bd24d9c61","body":{"detail":"`History::of` is gone from run.rs. A tick still reads the steps once (`Store::run_with_steps`, run.rs 1039) and keeps them (`Tick::steps`, 1078). `history::Control::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 101-125) and `clax::Tally::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 42-76) each pass over them on every tick and clone no payload: one `next` object, and the tally's strings. `history::transcript` (history.rs 167-191), the fold that parses every stored block, runs only in a tick that takes a turn (run.rs 1746); a script tick and a terminal tick no longer build messages. The old fold also cloned every step's whole payload (`s.payload.clone()`), which none of the three does. It is still O(steps) a tick and O(steps^2) a run for turns, so the concern stands; a cache keyed by the last committed step would now have one obvious home, the transcript. Proposed: re-anchor this record on history.rs 167-191.","kind":"comment","references":"06b231efca2d53f3527a7cbfac8863544ea10b5abd030c5991e4245bbe49d3d3","summary":"The single fold is now three, in other files: this span (run.rs 590-651) no longer holds it, and what a tick costs changed in kind, not in order","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850185Z","id":"5016f60e3f0f0ca8a9cac1943212ce812cf99fdc279430ca980c44fcaa7c28c6","body":{"detail":"`policy` reads `self.tick.clax.created` (217) and `Tick::script` reads `self.clax.base` (554). Both are `clax::Tally` fields (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 21-37), folded by the tick (run.rs 1052). A `clax` session opened by `SurfaceProvider::begin` should fold them itself from `StepCx::history`, as the proof's adapter now does with the same function (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 159), and the tick's copy then has no reader in this file. `Tally::of` reads only steps whose `committed_at` is set; `StepCx::history` is documented as the committed steps (surface.rs 606-610), and the tick's `steps` may hold a second uncommitted step if a run ever had two (only the first is taken out as `open`, run.rs 1041-1044), so pass the slice as it is and let the fold filter. Mutations that show both reads are held: 554 as `self.run.base_version` fails `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version` and `seam::a_script_after_a_rebase_starts_from_the_version_rebased_onto`; 217 without the tally's `created` fails `rules::a_brief_run_creates_one_artifact` and `golden::scripts::a_second_artifact_is_refused`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":217},"end":{"line":217},"content_hash":"747aeedba6ac31e345c713aebd9c313655307caf42544c65efe21f88dc9658fc"},"summary":"For the change that moves script.rs behind the seam: the two reads of `Tick::clax` here (217, 554) become the clax session's own `Tally::of(cx.history)`","tags":["t3","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850278Z","id":"cc31d02f04abeaeb37cf4bbfd285275098fba5c206cb99bf6fb942d2bd672acc","body":{"detail":"`what_i_did` (1374-1396) reads `clax.scripts`, `clax.changed` and `clax.published`; `finish` reads `clax.replied` (1574). They are the only readers of those four `clax::Tally` fields (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 21-37), so when the notices and the posting rule move into the provider's `ending`, it folds `Tally::of(cx.history)` there and these reads go. `scripts` is in the tally although a script step is the core's and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md (\"Where the nouns are read\", Core) lists it with the core's fields: its one reader is the notice's wording (\"I ran 2 scripts\"), and `history::Control` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 86-99) holds only what the tick decides by. A provider that is not `clax` and wants the count folds it from the steps' kinds. Held: `clax: Tally::default()` at run.rs 1052 fails 25 integration tests, `golden::endings::a_notice_counts_the_scripts_that_ran` among them; 1574 without `replied` fails `golden::endings::finish_posts_no_summary_on_any_thread_when_the_run_replied_on_one`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1374},"end":{"line":1396},"content_hash":"fd90d2fab6f4133c8026975093ec060384b8880c38ff5d1fa161cea5cf6e66bf"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending`: `what_i_did` and the finish rule read `Tick::clax`, and the count of scripts is in the tally, not in the core's fold","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850345Z","id":"5dfe32207552554cad4fde1a15d85053f18b1d8e9cb7a36564cf69f69fc61d45","body":{"detail":"The tick holds three things of the committed steps: `hist: history::Control` (the core's: `next`, `nudged`, `committed`, `last_usage`; the field kept its name so `must_stop`'s cap read at 1356 is unchanged), `clax: clax::Tally` (1075) and `steps` (1078), the steps as read, in order, without the open one. After the script reads (script.rs 217, 554) and the ending reads (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) move to the provider, `Tick::clax` has no reader and goes with its import (run.rs 38); from then on run.rs names no field of the tally. `StepCx::new`'s `history` argument (surface.rs 635) is `&self.steps`. `history.rs` is held free of the surface's nouns by its own test (`history::tests::the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface`, which reads the file's source); run.rs is not, and cannot be until this change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1071},"end":{"line":1078},"content_hash":"d12dfa8b40d3c2fd1d0f47ccd3e5ef049691d35c64d576f8f3a5f9023d9e59bb"},"summary":"For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: `Tick::clax` is the one field of the tick that is Clax's own fold, and `Tick::steps` is what `StepCx::history` borrows","tags":["t3","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850392Z","id":"da1b820bbf6c9afb22a897a3184700a8700507c8ab1b80676c82148298f983fe","body":{"detail":"The turn calls `transcript(&self.steps)` once (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1746) and reads `system` (is this the first request, 1747; the system block, 1773) and `messages` (1775). A policy's rule stands at that call. Three things for that change. (1) The design's signature is `fn history(&self, run: &RunInfo, steps: &[Step], why: HistoryFor) -> Result<History>` (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md 864): its `History` is `history::Transcript` here (named so while the reference fold `History` is kept for tests, crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs); the default ignores `run` and `why`. (2) `first` is `committed == 0 || system.is_none()`: a rule that returns no system text for a run whose step 1 stored one makes the turn write the system prompt and the request again and store them in a later step's payload, which changes the cached prefix. The contract says so in words (history.rs 150-153); no type prevents it. Returning the system text apart from the rule's messages (the core reading it from step 1 itself) would remove the case. (3) The contract's other clauses (tool_use and tool_result kept in pairs, order, roles alternating from the user's, stored blocks unchanged) are documentation only: a checker `fn(&[Step], &Transcript) -> Result<(), String>` run on every rule's output in debug builds and in a conformance test would hold them; the default passes whenever the stored steps do.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":138},"end":{"line":191},"content_hash":"1ff1e8d6247fcaf7788bd11804af817f44f42e80f3d8a6b83113747b3f40eca8"},"summary":"For the change that adds `RunPolicy`: `transcript` is the default history rule, called in one place, and nothing checks that another rule keeps the contract its documentation states","tags":["t3","for-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850426Z","id":"544264e0d67f5af762819b18e6b716b35ca209931161ad7614c1bec9278bd356","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface` (203-260) splits this file at its test module and fails on a noun of Clax's surface in any line of the code or its documentation (artifact, thread, draft, version, reply, publish, created, changed, base, tally and their forms, as whole words), on `crate::clax` or `crate::run` in a path, and on any use of `clax_store` but `bot_runs::{Step, StepKind}`. It is a word list: a noun it does not list (`comment`, `file`) or a payload key read through a variable passes, and it holds one file, not `Control`'s readers. A crate for the core's folds would make the compiler hold the imports, but `Step` itself is `clax_store`'s and its payload is untyped JSON, so no arrangement of crates stops `p[\"base\"]`; and the layering (scripts/check-crate-layers.py) would gain a crate for 190 lines. Two mutations show what the test catches: the word `draft` in a doc comment, and `use crate::clax::Tally as _;`, each fail it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":194},"end":{"line":260},"content_hash":"36da7193276aa641ba53dfd7239d5802254b357ef83ade8a97eaee8d7774fb57"},"summary":"Holding the core's folds free of Clax's nouns by the compiler (a crate of their own, or a module with no access to `clax_store`'s run types) instead of by a test that reads the source","tags":["t3","revisit:the core's run loop moves to a crate of its own, or a step's payload gets a type per surface","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850486Z","id":"8e4a5cdbc1917c79398af0adc112dce5f8d5ee8c93786e1f8f029dc47f17b8bd","body":{"detail":"Today: `Control::of` and `Tally::of` run when the tick is built (1051-1052), `transcript` in `turn` (1746). The tally is read by a script tick (script.rs 217, 554) and a terminal tick (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) and not by a turn, so a turn tick folds it for nothing: one pass that clones a few strings. A `OnceCell<Tally>` on `Tick` would save that pass and cost a `Send`/`Sync` question on wasm32 and native alike (the tick is borrowed across awaits), for a field that leaves the tick when the provider folds its own. Not worth it before then.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1746},"end":{"line":1747},"content_hash":"96ec4c07ae98e5ef199c0c403936665da6862795c80d60e5ce18d337c238a5a1"},"summary":"Folding the transcript and the tally lazily, once, behind a cell on the tick, instead of the tally on every tick and the transcript in the turn","tags":["t3","revisit:a profile of a tick shows the tally's pass, or `Tick::clax` outlives the change that moves the endings behind the provider","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850523Z","id":"aaf6076f0e3307e3dc0c84347325c483d6540cf9f86659fb0b1ee8ea3b25b85a","body":{"detail":"`History::of` (84-146) is the fold run.rs had before the split, with its own copies of `blocks`, `push` and `usage_of` (57-82) so that a change to `history.rs` cannot change both sides. Three tests compare it with `Control::of`, `transcript` and `Tally::of`: on every prefix of `tests/golden/steps.{thread,brief,web}.json` (430), on the hand-built run (456), and on 4000 generated histories of up to 12 steps drawn from every step in the goldens and one in three disturbed (467; the sequence is fixed, so a failure repeats). The module is `#[cfg(test)]` (crates/clax-bot/src/lib.rs). `step`, `open` and `a_run` (154-246) are the hand-built steps the folds' own unit tests use: move them to a test-support module when the reference is deleted. The generated test asserts its seeds hold every step kind and each fact a fold reads, so a golden that loses its only `created: true` step fails here with that message rather than weakening the comparison silently.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1},"end":{"line":22},"content_hash":"6008046a4f775a18466c283c6fb7366fd7027017f269c1be0f8e6d3a92da9456"},"summary":"For the change that lets a run have no `clax`: this file goes, and until then it is the only thing that says the three folds are the old one","tags":["t3","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T13:49:41.850611Z","id":"234a3614a28e56688679c7dfb733cb93570b988603256b548d4c104f99a7eacf","body":{"detail":"The split removed run.rs 533-653 (the `History` section) and added 12 lines further down, so what the tables call 1681-1719 (`finish`) is at 1572-1610, 1549-1590 (`stop`) at 1440-1481, 1451-1480 (`must_stop`) at 1342-1371, 1483-1504 (`what_i_did`) at 1374-1396, 1509-1529 (`notify_at`) at 1405-1420, and so on through the endings table (207-216) and wherever else the file cites run.rs. The two citations of the fold itself were corrected to name `clax::Tally` (surface.rs 123, 213). The plan's measurements (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md) and the open records on run.rs cite the old lines too (`qualifier review crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs` lists the drifted ones). The change that edits run.rs next for the endings will move them again; recite then, or cite by function name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":207},"end":{"line":216},"content_hash":"0e5634c3b6dab1f3091ba62fd437236dec83202602488ad7a70793c56379e35a"},"summary":"The module documentation cites run.rs by line, and every citation past run.rs 533 is now about 110 lines too high","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.084873Z","id":"c9b43f3e77cd0224a6f485dc3a07bd7c5df3fd9aecb8acfb3eaf8f7e59ab0ecc","body":{"detail":"Invariants, read from `Tick::turn` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1746-1790) and `transcript` (history.rs 167-191). With S the committed steps and T the rule's output: (1) `T.system` is `Some` exactly when a committed turn of S stored a string `system`, and is then that text byte for byte; `None` otherwise. The turn reads `None` as the first request (run.rs 1747) and then stores `system` and `user` again in the step it commits (run.rs 2034-2037). (2) When `T.system` is `Some`, `T.messages` is not empty, `messages[0]` is the user's and its content opens with step 1's stored `user` blocks, unchanged and in order: with the system text they are the cached prefix. (3) Roles alternate from the user's; no message is empty. (4) The last message is the user's whenever the next step is a turn that adds nothing itself (every turn but the first), except after a paused turn (`next` is `{kind: turn, user: []}`), where it is the assistant's and must end with that turn's stored blocks, since the model continues them. (5) Every `tool_use` block of an assistant message has a `tool_result` with its `id` in the user message right after it, and every `tool_result` answers a `tool_use` of the assistant message right before it. (6) Every block is one a committed step stores (in `user`, `assistant`, `next.user` when `next.kind` is `turn`, or `result`), compared as JSON, in step order and in stored order within a step; or a block the rule adds in place of the steps it left out (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md 966 lets a rule add a summary; the contract at history.rs 160-163 does not say one may be added, nor with what role or where). (7) Nothing has `cache_control`: the turn sets the one breakpoint (`with_breakpoint`, run.rs 592). The default satisfies 1 to 7 on every run a tick wrote. Two things beyond the record's three. (a) `Control::last_usage` (history.rs 97-98) is the last committed turn's, and `affordable` (run.rs 1698-1704) prices the next request as if the tokens that turn read from or wrote to the cache are read from the cache again. That holds only while the rule sends a request that extends the last one. A rule that sends fewer steps breaks the cached prefix, the estimate is then too low, and the turn is granted more `max_tokens` than the run can pay for. The change that adds the rule must tell `affordable` that the prefix changed (for instance the rule's output carries how many leading blocks are unchanged from the last request), or price such a turn uncached. (b) A rule cannot be written against `transcript` without reading payload keys. `transcript` merges per role, so its output has no step boundaries; a rule that keeps the request and the last k steps has to call it on step 1 with its `assistant` and `next` taken out and on the tail, which means cloning steps into a new `Vec` and editing a payload by key. A per-step projection (what one committed step adds: its system text, request blocks, assistant blocks and answer blocks, with its step number), of which `transcript` is the concatenation, lets a rule choose steps and nothing else. Also: `Transcript::system`'s documentation (history.rs 130-132, 149-152) says the text is step 1's; the fold keeps the last committed turn's that is a string (`a_later_turns_system_text_replaces_the_first`, history.rs 472). They agree on every run a tick wrote, because only a first turn stores one (run.rs 2034).","kind":"comment","references":"da1b820bbf6c9afb22a897a3184700a8700507c8ab1b80676c82148298f983fe","summary":"What a rule standing in for `transcript` must keep, stated so a checker can be written from it, and two things the signature `fn(&[Step]) -> Transcript` does not carry","tags":["t3","for-t4","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.085249Z","id":"ef821524105a1abd26d4525b645c30926f4e18c21915422c3044cd6d785aa5ee","body":{"detail":"Each of these applied alone to history.rs and `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` run. Fails `the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface` though nothing of a surface is read: a doc comment with `base64` (the scan splits on every non-letter, so `base64` is `base`), with `in its wire version`, with `an open step changed nothing`. `based on nothing` passes. Passes though a surface's field or module is reached: `payload(s)[\"comments\"]` (a key not in the list), `payload(s)[[\"ba\", \"se\"].concat().as_str()]`, and `use crate::{clax as surface_mod};` (the scan looks for the text `crate::clax`, which a grouped import does not contain; `use super::clax::*;` is the same). Module privacy cannot hold it either: `clax` is declared at the crate root and every module of the crate can name it. What does hold the meaning: `Control::of` and `transcript` give the same result when `changed`, `replied`, `published`, `created` and `base` are removed from every payload. Asserted in `Tick::turn` on every turn of the integration suite (a temporary assertion beside run.rs 1746, not committed), it held: all tests passed. As a unit test over the generated histories of crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs 467 it would fail on any read of those keys however spelt, and never on a word. Suggested: add that test; keep the scan only for the import (`crate::clax`, `crate::run`, written to match grouped and `super::` paths too) and drop the word list, or keep the list but applied to string literals only, so prose cannot trip it.","kind":"comment","references":"544264e0d67f5af762819b18e6b716b35ca209931161ad7614c1bec9278bd356","summary":"The source scan measured: it fails on three innocent sentences and passes three ways of reading the surface's fields; a test of what the folds read holds the rule itself","tags":["t3","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.085581Z","id":"d050941cfc368a2ea8404aa547830b98e2677850fcd118aa7d64ea9e66fd3c01","body":{"detail":"`History::of` and its helpers (history_reference.rs 31-146) are `git show 944536d8e:crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs` lines 538-651 but for two visibilities (`push` private, `of` `pub(crate)`). Each change below was applied alone and `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` run. Failed by the generated comparison alone (no unit test, no golden): `c.committed = s.n as usize`; a script step clearing `nudged`; the replies reset only by `published.version` being a number. Failed by it and others: `last_usage` kept from the first turn; `last_usage` kept when a turn has no `usage`; `nudged |=`; a terminal step clearing `last_usage`; the first `system` kept; merged blocks put in front; a script without a `result` adding nothing; both folds filtering on `payload.is_some()`; an empty `changed` resetting the replies; a base over `u32` keeping the last; `published` as the largest; a non-script step with `published` resetting the replies; a nudge's answer not shown. Failed by the integration tests: the open step taken out one place early; the transcript of all but the last step; the tick's tally with its replies emptied; the proof's adapter ignoring `created`. Passed every test: `billed_micro_usd` of 0 read as none (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 80: no step in the seeds has a `billed_micro_usd` but the hand-built 42, and `disturbed` replaces `usage` whole, never a count in it); `rposition` for `position`, `swap_remove` for `remove`, and leaving the open step in `Tick::steps` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1041-1044: the comparison is of the folds, not of the tick). The seeds (54 steps of the goldens, 5 hand-built, 1 added) hold no paused turn (`next` `{kind: turn, user: []}`) and no script step with `created: true`: both are reached only through `disturbed` (a `user` that is not an array; a turn made a script), and by the folds' unit tests. Two open steps, a step begun again, a cancelled script's `stopped` step, the context-window give-up and the nudge are all in the seeds or made by `disturbed`.","kind":"comment","references":"aaf6076f0e3307e3dc0c84347325c483d6540cf9f86659fb0b1ee8ea3b25b85a","summary":"What the comparison reaches and what it does not: 21 of 25 further one-line changes fail a test; the four that pass are one in `usage_of` and three in how the tick takes the open step out","tags":["t3","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.085667Z","id":"2530f375d1e20ecfb98a46ac40f6f88ef0330801a671129a98b2a52bebfcfd99","body":{"detail":"The open step is taken out by `position` and `remove`. Three changes here pass every test of `//crates/clax-bot:all`: `rposition`, `swap_remove`, and `steps[i].clone()` (the open step left in). They pass because the three folds filter on `committed_at` themselves and a run has at most one open step, the last (asserted on every tick of the integration suite with a temporary assertion: `steps` all committed, the open step's `n` above the last one's; it held). `StepCx::history` is documented as the run's committed steps (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 606-610) and the proof's adapter relies on it (`history.last()` is the turn that asked for the script, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 614-619). When the tick passes `&self.steps` there, a provider that reads the last step, or counts them, is wrong on a run with a second open step or with the open one left in, and no test says so. Suggested: build `steps` with `retain(|s| s.committed_at.is_some())` after taking the first open one, so the field's documentation (run.rs 1076-1078) is true by construction, and a unit test of that on a list with two open steps.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1041},"end":{"line":1044},"content_hash":"7a98bffcd33288de6c7505fac73153de56251d2533dfeff8b9071f665d0bc10c"},"summary":"Nothing holds that `Tick::steps` is the committed steps only, which is what `StepCx::history` promises a provider","tags":["t3","for-t5","for-t8","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.085701Z","id":"5d4466881832252be8c11c3cf7e5a2fb6eca1d5b47aa16d17a7bd7653cc35252","body":{"detail":"`billed_micro_usd: v[\"billed_micro_usd\"].as_u64().filter(|b| *b > 0)` at line 80 passes `//crates/clax-bot:all`. `usage_of` reads a turn's stored `usage` (`Control::last_usage`, line 116) and each observed entry of an open turn (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 891), where a billed cost of 0 and no billed cost are charged differently (the billed share against the catalog's rate). `usage_reads_each_count_and_the_billed_cost` (history.rs 492) uses 5. The function moved here unchanged from run.rs; the gap is older than the move. Suggested: a case with `billed_micro_usd: 0` giving `Some(0)`, and one with a count that is not a number giving 0.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":71},"end":{"line":82},"content_hash":"069486e025033d82c920f4049079642aa8628e518ac81b4a9d5c6540c2bfc154"},"summary":"No test holds `usage_of` for a billed cost of 0: read as none, every test passes","tags":["t3","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.085726Z","id":"8534c9c4ea3466eae0d597bdf3db236439cd360393b1cc5a5ab11df8205018ad","body":{"detail":"`c.next = p[\"next\"].clone()` (line 117) runs for each committed turn, and a turn's `next` for a script holds the script's whole `code`. So a tick clones every script the run has asked for, as the fold in run.rs did before the split (it cloned the whole payload as well). Keeping a `&Value` to the last turn's `next` in the loop and cloning once after it makes the sentence true and the fold allocate once.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":29},"end":{"line":36},"content_hash":"0e95bbae91df8aefd5a9e2a3266bd79685ec36ba2bc95c9381072a472b406794"},"summary":"The cost paragraph says `Control` clones one `next` object; it clones every committed turn's and drops all but the last","tags":["t3","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:03:53.086080Z","id":"166846d56b5772af45ac85cdbd0c7ef7444e8fafabcb3c1441a7219dfffd4bc7","body":{"detail":"Comparing `git show 944536d8e:crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs` with the file after the split: lines 1-37 keep their numbers; for 39-530 add 2 (two imports added); 531-651 are gone (to crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs and crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs); for 652-1157 subtract 119; for 1188-1854 subtract 109 (so 1681-1719 is 1572-1610, 1483-1504 is 1374-1395, 1509-1529 is 1400-1420, 1683 is 1574); for 1856-2484 subtract 108 (so 1863-1866 is 1755-1758, 1868 is 1760, 1906-1909 is 1798-1801, 2035-2047 is 1927-1939, 2081 is 1973, 2094-2115 is 1986-2007). Lines 1158-1187 (the tick's construction and its fields) changed. The citations to correct in surface.rs are in its lines 108-109, 164, 201-202, 213-218 and 1181-1185; in docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md, lines 20-25, 31, 41, 50, 51, 58, 66, 73, 74, 76-78, 89, 115, 156 and 162, of which line 25 (`History`'s tallies, run.rs 546-555, 598-603, 626-644) is now crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 21-37, 46-51 and 56-73, and line 58 (`History::of`, run.rs 590-651) is history.rs 103-125 and 167-191 with tally.rs 42-76. crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs kept its line numbers (two lines changed in place).","kind":"comment","references":"234a3614a28e56688679c7dfb733cb93570b988603256b548d4c104f99a7eacf","summary":"The rule that corrects every run.rs line cited from before the split, from a line-by-line match of the two files","tags":["t3","for-t7","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:21:18.110410Z","id":"0b1f9c978ec7f68ff7e8ca331098d9c19a01c209484a0afa68bb8b2e5b02d436","body":{"detail":"At e461a157a the split is `history::committed_and_open` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 65-72): the first step that is not committed is taken out as the open one, then `steps.retain(|s| s.committed_at.is_some())`, so a second open step is in neither; the tick calls it at crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1041 and the field's documentation (run.rs 1073-1075) says so. `history::tests::the_open_step_is_taken_out_and_only_committed_steps_are_kept` (history.rs 520) covers no open step, the open step last, an open step between committed ones, two open steps, and no steps. Each change applied alone to history.rs and `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` run: `rposition` for `position` fails that test (the second open step is taken); `swap_remove` for `remove` fails it (the committed steps change order); the `retain` line removed fails it (the second open step stays in). `steps[i].clone()` for `steps.remove(i)` passes every test and now changes nothing: the `retain` takes the open step out, at the cost of one clone. Not closed: `StepCx::new` is not yet given `&self.steps`.","kind":"comment","references":"2530f375d1e20ecfb98a46ac40f6f88ef0330801a671129a98b2a52bebfcfd99","summary":"`Tick::steps` is now the committed steps by construction, and a unit test holds it for two open steps and for an open step that is not last","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:21:18.110793Z","id":"5d462f50ca372e2a5ddfcc1147048c34f7a495bed11bcb581a9bf25cc6e1b6ae","body":{"detail":"At e461a157a. (1) `history_reference::the_run_loops_folds_read_nothing_clax_keeps_in_a_payload` (crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs 601): over the 4000 generated histories and every prefix of the three pinned runs, `Control::of` and `transcript` give the same with `changed`, `replied`, `published`, `created` and `base` taken out of every payload; it asserts that over 2000 of the histories held such a field, over 300 held one in a committed turn, and over 1000 have a tally that changes when they go. Mutations to history.rs, each alone, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all`: `c.nudged = … || p[\"created\"] == true` fails it; `c.committed += usize::from(payload(s)[[\"ba\", \"se\"].concat().as_str()].is_u64())` fails it (the read by a concatenated key that the word list passed); `push(&mut t.messages, Role::User, blocks(&p[\"replied\"]))` in `transcript` fails it. Each also fails the comparison with the single fold, which goes with history_reference.rs; this test is documented there as one that stays. (2) `history::tests::this_file_imports_a_step_and_messages_and_nothing_of_the_crate` (history.rs 478) collects every `use` item of the file's code, joined over lines, and requires exactly the three it has; any other line of code with `crate::`, `super::`, `clax_store`, `mod ` or `include!` fails it. `use crate::{clax as surface_mod};`, `use super::clax::*;` and `let _ = crate::clax::Tally::of(steps);` each fail it; `the_scan_of_imports_sees_grouped_and_relative_paths` (504) holds the parser. Prose is not read, so `base64`, `wire version` and `changed nothing` pass. What it still does not hold: a field of a surface's that is not one of the five (the test's list is `CLAX_FIELDS`, history_reference.rs 580; a new payload field of clax's is added there), and the readers of `Control` in run.rs.","kind":"comment","references":"ef821524105a1abd26d4525b645c30926f4e18c21915422c3044cd6d785aa5ee","summary":"The word list is gone: a test of what the folds read holds the rule, and the source scan holds only the imports, grouped and relative paths included","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:21:18.111146Z","id":"dc9c5f0708c1e8593fa5c56b0c3cf5183480dc86d137ccb368d649da118e3b9f","body":{"detail":"At e461a157a, crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 126-152: `let mut next: Result<&Value, &str>` holds the last turn's stored `next`, or the kind of step that follows a script (`turn`) or a terminal step (`end`); one `clone` or one `json!` after the pass. The module's cost paragraph (39-45) reads \"clones one `next` object, the last turn's\". The comparison with the single fold holds the result unchanged (`history_reference::the_three_folds_agree_with_the_single_fold_on_generated_histories`); `Ok(stored) => stored[\"kind\"].clone()` fails 140 tests and `StepKind::Script => {}` 88.","kind":"comment","references":"8534c9c4ea3466eae0d597bdf3db236439cd360393b1cc5a5ab11df8205018ad","summary":"`Control::of` keeps a reference to the last turn's `next` in the loop and clones once after it; the cost paragraph now says which object","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:21:18.111491Z","id":"a1b77157dbcd8c97b1108eaca8619e07bb8b9231f661ea759e96aec438246ad7","body":{"detail":"At e461a157a. `history::tests::usage_reads_each_count_and_the_billed_cost` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 783) adds `billed_micro_usd: 0` giving `Some(0)`, a lacking one giving `None`, counts that are a string, a negative and a fraction giving 0, and a value that is no object. The generator has a seed turn billed 0 and two odd `usage` values (crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs `seeds`, 460, and `disturbed`), and asserts more than 20 generated histories end on a turn billed 0. `.as_u64().filter(|b| *b > 0)` at history.rs 103 fails `usage_reads_each_count_and_the_billed_cost` and `history_reference::the_three_folds_agree_with_the_single_fold_on_generated_histories`. The other reader, an open turn's observed entries (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs `observed_rows`), is covered by the unit test only.","kind":"comment","references":"5d4466881832252be8c11c3cf7e5a2fb6eca1d5b47aa16d17a7bd7653cc35252","summary":"A billed cost of 0 is now held: read as none, two tests fail","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:21:18.111833Z","id":"31ca0b79bfb80086f21483de1567a24595cc5bee6c08eea66dd044f7ea2dbd15","body":{"detail":"At e461a157a. Documentation: crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 166-217 states invariants 1 to 7 as this record words them, says a rule may add a block for the steps it left out (6), and `Transcript::system` (160-163) and the function's first paragraph say `system` is the last committed turn's stored string, step 1's on every run a tick wrote. Checker: `pub fn check(steps: &[Step], shown: &Transcript) -> Result<(), String>` (267-433), `Err` opening with the invariant's number. It checks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 7 whole; of 6, that a block some committed step stores is not shown before one stored earlier that is shown too (a block no step stores is taken for the rule's own, so a rewritten stored block is not detected, and an added block equal to an earlier stored one is reported). 4 is claimed only when the last committed step is a script or a turn whose `next` is a turn; after a paused turn that stored no `assistant` blocks it claims nothing. A `tool_use` in the last message is allowed (the next step answers it). Tests: seven `history::tests::check_holds_*` (830-985) break each invariant; disabling each branch of `check` in turn (13 mutations: 1, 2 twice, 3 twice, 4 three times, 5 twice, 6, 7) fails the test of its invariant. `history_reference::the_transcript_of_a_run_keeps_what_a_turn_relies_on` (crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs 744) runs `check` on `transcript` for every prefix of the three pinned runs, the hand-built run and 600 runs written as a tick writes them (`a_written_run`, 649: scripts, a nudge, a pause, calls refused one and two at a time, every ending), with and without the open step: all pass. On the 4000 shuffled histories, which are not runs, only 1, 6 and 7 are asserted (2 to 5 fail there for the steps' sake: a script's result without its turn, a first step with no request). Not done, and left to the change that adds the rule: calling `check` on a rule's output in the run loop; (a) telling `affordable` that the cached prefix changed; (b) a per-step projection.","kind":"comment","references":"c9b43f3e77cd0224a6f485dc3a07bd7c5df3fd9aecb8acfb3eaf8f7e59ab0ecc","summary":"`transcript`'s documentation now numbers the seven invariants and says whose system text it is, and `history::check` validates a transcript against them; nothing calls it yet","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:21:18.112188Z","id":"d5c690c911d542eb2b3da1839ac34e07d25b5385211f13f5fb4947120201ad35","body":{"detail":"166846d5's rule (subtract 109 for old lines 1188-1854) gives 1483-1504 as 1374-1395, which is right for 78f1b22c0; this record and cc31d02f (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1374-1396) say 1396. e461a157a then replaced the four lines that took the open step out (run.rs 1041-1044) by one, so every line of run.rs after 1044 is 3 lower than at 78f1b22c0: `what_i_did` is 1371-1392, `finish`'s rule 1571, the turn's call of `transcript` 1743. The citations in surface.rs and the plan are not corrected here: the change that adds the policy edits run.rs again.","kind":"comment","references":"234a3614a28e56688679c7dfb733cb93570b988603256b548d4c104f99a7eacf","summary":"A slip in this record and in cc31d02f: `what_i_did` was run.rs 1374-1395 after the split, not 1374-1396; it is 1371-1392 now","tags":["t3","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:33.554188Z","id":"992b91f3f23b0bef51a2453b2bb00061c6b90dd0dce6ac87a0982d6841caab93","body":{"detail":"A run's history keeps the blocks its provider produced: Gemini turns hold a `signature` field on text and tool_use blocks (crates/clax-sample/src/messages/gemini.rs), and Chat Completions turns hold thinking blocks with an empty signature (openai.rs). RunSettings is read before every tick, so after an admin switches api to anthropic an open run sends these to the Messages API, which refuses the unknown field and the unsigned thinking; the run ends model_error. Switching between openai and gemini drops or mistranslates nothing fatal.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (fa547d92), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":90},"end":{"line":93},"content_hash":"02de2c3a46cb7f229c289416253894e26660ad5d5d58decf7b59d8237bf0879c"},"suggested_fix":"Record the API a run started on (its row, like its model) and keep it for the run, or strip foreign fields when the Messages API is the target.","summary":"Changing the API while runs are open sends one API's blocks to another","supersedes":"cc909e26f6eacfdea24fb1788dd783f4d07128c132005edea9be1b9d37815c57","tags":["providers","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:33.699588Z","id":"11d784a7d996c267b6afb3d0e461eb033154f9252865b8e485764fd6851e12dd","body":{"detail":"Spec section 8: locally a value in config.toml or the environment overrides the stored one. from_store reads Store::bot_setting and leaves per_person_concurrency, lease and renewal at their defaults; the daemon's SettingsSource must apply HomeConfig::bot (including bot.per_person_concurrency and enabled) on top, re-read per tick.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (2f8fe438), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Reply 67173b9e on the original proposed a close: the server lays [bot] over the database inside the store (Store::set_bot_overrides), fixed in git:bf3458ec.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":157},"end":{"line":161},"content_hash":"ea413dee711a57207ddc291eb3872d8297bd0a023361d0b7c7a7930cf7dd7878"},"summary":"RunSettings::from_store reads the database only: the [bot] config overrides are the server's to lay over it","supersedes":"c675ccb277d8d17449b1880c6972fb3fe9b6ee64099e5219d98c4afdaa335767","tags":["wiring","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:33.843063Z","id":"1faec5e0c1488f74bc3286e703616c654e702dce9fcc95b6b154eeaad68d93f4","body":{"detail":"usage_rows stores cost as ceil(qty x rate / base) of an integer rate (charge_in, crates/clax-store/src/credits.rs). For qty <= base the share is hit exactly; above it the rows' costs may sum to slightly less than the billed cost. Exact sums would need the store to take a billed cost per row rather than a rate.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (47f96906), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":774},"end":{"line":844},"content_hash":"c540eaa6b294efa7579537cb080ac1566d4ec86f91dd306da0d9f3122c457fc4"},"summary":"bill() sets an integer micro-USD-per-million rate, so a row of more than a million tokens can fall short of its billed share by under qty / 1 000 000 micro-USD","supersedes":"2dd0bd4e1072dfeedd198cfb6af0d2358bb7a835cad07b52e539ddc5ab3cdaba","tags":["pricing","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:33.989138Z","id":"a1eeefe74d2fe03e1b1b43545497a72bf3194c9d823bd77bfb9e746544cece8a","body":{"detail":"Without a rate the commit fails unknown_cost_line (charge_in). Dropping the rows would hide the tokens from the person's usage; a new RateSource needs a store migration (usage_rows.rate_source CHECK). The row charges 0 either way, being waived for an own key.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (123e4f52), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":846},"end":{"line":869},"content_hash":"68d3664e4bfe1d629fe1a91f1b256044aaf9f5db75d72f780f9435cdbc170c94"},"summary":"token_rate gives an own-key turn on a model with no rate a waived zero rate sourced 'default', rather than dropping its token rows or adding a RateSource for it","supersedes":"39f9607f8178bccd60f64d8e9169741f2d8a4faab086848379242375b77ddb82","tags":["pricing","revisit:the store gains a rate source for waived or unpriced rows","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:34.131451Z","id":"36290fbb3bccc41816464fca62b5e15cfcb05f8b57444153c76979bf7f460e28","body":{"detail":"With infra_lines on (the Worker), each step charges do.requests = 1 + its host calls and toolpath.storage = the payload's JSON bytes for 30 days. Neither is read from Cloudflare's billing: spec section 13 lists this as a known gap. The payload holds the script's code and outcome, so storage scales with output, not with what the journal keeps.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (6f3e5c2c), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":972},"end":{"line":991},"content_hash":"209d50573adc6aa561185d99669f36da87ff8d0d43beac8cdb8692b1060c2e1e"},"summary":"Durable Object requests and Toolpath storage are estimated per step","supersedes":"aef2e8a1241479d0cf2616a297ba1847599f0301ad056c7cd49dfd042c90ca1e","tags":["credits","gap","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:34.276835Z","id":"88a6c90cee32cba9e3963d66e7bd352a0b372be80da0efff3b35c1936994265e","body":{"detail":"Tick::affordable prices the request as its JSON bytes / 4 tokens, the part the last turn cached at the cached-input rate and the rest at the input rate, then caps max_tokens to the output the credits left pay for. A request whose tokens run well above bytes/4 (non-Latin text, dense JSON) can still overshoot the person's credits by its input; counting tokens (the Messages API count_tokens endpoint) would bound it.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (96939966), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Since a9ecb07d1 the uncached rest is priced at the cache-write rate, not the input rate.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1806},"end":{"line":1906},"content_hash":"568435353618718cfb89456697b34d2e6f9bb433e66e9031f7e5eb0c519e7f84"},"summary":"A turn's affordable max_tokens rests on a byte-count estimate of its input","supersedes":"3d514de71cd928bb44457f55b6e05ae51d68a64c17d210bb52babdf8314d84e5","tags":["credits","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:34.423840Z","id":"53c256e6ad20c899f2350f3253e7c9dd569ed2478c4408fc482fcc9a3dfbce64","body":{"detail":"(1) Test the run loop's half of `SurfaceProvider::ending` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 1474-1511): an `Err` counted against the step as a failed commit, the bound (`max_commit_failures`), `End::Failed`'s `Err` logged and the step committed, and every binding's `ending` still called when one fails. The provider cases hold only the provider's half. (2) Make the change to the sources' read on purpose: `Store::web_pages` failing in `finish` (1684 here) fails the tick uncounted today, so the step is begun again without end; behind `ending` under that contract it is tried `max_commit_failures` times and the run ends `failed` (surface.rs, the endings table's row for a notice that cannot be posted). Keep the read out of `ending`, or accept the bound. (3) `begin`'s failure is retried uncounted and `ending`'s is counted (surface.rs 1460-1471, 1490-1505): decide whether the two should agree. (4) Build what delivers `ended` again; the contract allows a repeat with the same kind of end and best-effort details (1513-1540), and keeping the `End` in the terminal step's payload would let a redelivery carry all of it (record b25bed99). (5) Count a `FieldClash` against the step, and reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) as surface names in `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42), so a clash cannot be reached by a name (record f2d3e3a6). (6) The adapter's terminal cases prove nothing about notices: its `ending` and `ended` do nothing (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 186-204), so the provider cases pass on it with no notice posted. The real provider's fixture needs a `noticed` probe that reads the notices and a `lifecycle`. (7) A cancelled script's `stopped` step is charged the per-step lines with no call counted (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673-683; 1567-1573 here): keep or change it knowingly.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1530},"end":{"line":1804},"content_hash":"2cf9021b0bbad577eb7e4a5fb104ade3cb0decafa124efcc19c7194e3fa00108"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending` and `ended`: what the seam states and nothing holds yet","supersedes":"264e99a6077b972f7feab8ab52fcfe936f76991cfd343120ffb97626914b97bc","tags":["seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:34.568998Z","id":"5ed5547d0ca5351abc25a5b10e863788dbc3fd9a8c828861f428aa09472c3316","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone, passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`: (1) 1570 as `5` (the requests line of every `stopped` step, a cancelled script's among them, counts five calls: crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 160-164 says it counts none). (2) 1574-1575 without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (a stop writes no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported). (3) 1539 without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record of its own). (1) is billed where `RunSettings::infra_lines` is on (the hosted Worker, crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs 310); crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (219) holds the line for a turn, a script and a `finish` step and has no `stopped` step. (2) and (3) are audit only: `Pins::ending` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 568-592) pins a terminal step's payload, replies, writes, notices and roster, and not the run's audit records. Also unpinned, and seen by no one: a call answered from the journal is logged with `ms` 0 (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 as 7 passes all 189; the goldens replace every `ms` by a name, pins.rs 123).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1547},"end":{"line":1554},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"suggested_fix":"Extend `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` with a run stopped by a cap after a script and a script cancelled after a call, each with its `stopped` step's requests quantity; add `p.audit()` to what one stopped ending and the cancelled ending pin.","summary":"No test holds what a `stopped` step is charged on the requests line, nor its audit records: the count of calls, the `bot.call` records of a stop, and that a notice's journal row gets none","supersedes":"d016f72848eaf6b4384062f378e2ac86772fcfeee80bff93b574f9e676b778c1","tags":["review","seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:34.713804Z","id":"1be9afa17ed0bb78509a9115a744bf9b2e3a3a229357880a8140146368fa4b76","body":{"detail":"The overflow plumbing is whole for a policy that is a pure function of the core's messages and a level (held by crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs with a fake that drops the oldest tool results). What it does not have:\n\n1. The overflowing answer's blocks stay in the core's projection: `History::of` pushes every turn's assistant blocks (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:626-627), as it does for `pause_turn`, so after a `stop_reason` overflow the next request ends with that partial assistant message unless the policy drops it. `overflowed` rebuilds that projection by hand (`hist.messages`, then the turn's request, then the answer's blocks) because the fold has no way to say what the next tick's projection will be. The projection function should take the committed steps plus one uncommitted turn, so this is computed once.\n2. A policy is given messages, not steps: it cannot tell which step a message came from, so it cannot keep a tool call and its result together by step or summarise by step. The projection should say, for each message or block, the step it came from.\n3. A policy has nowhere to keep a summary: the design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:880-888) lets it send one it keeps, recorded by hash. Here a turn records `payload.shown` (level, bytes, SHA-256) and the overflowing turn `next.overflow.shown_sha256`, which name what was sent but do not hold it, so a run's exact context is rebuilt only by asking the same policy again.\n4. Nothing checks what a policy answers: messages with a tool result and no call, or two of one role, reach the provider, and its refusal stops the run (`model_error`).\n5. Each shorter history changes the cached prefix; the turn after an overflow pays the cache-write rate for all of it, and `affordable` (run.rs:1817) prices it from the last turn's usage, which after a refused request is zero.\n6. `HistoryAsk.level` only grows. A long session that is compacted many times needs the policy to decide what a level means; nothing lowers it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1916},"end":{"line":1962},"content_hash":"9a42d31543d19cfad3106eb94639918e0e14159279b472e2a2841a4190eba3ee"},"summary":"What a real compactor still needs from the run loop, and what the message projection must provide for it","supersedes":"440572f80f033b777bbfd15e2cc478868a35a92f655c68d68268ce0da54bf3b7","tags":["for-t3","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:34.858096Z","id":"413675d56b0b340c6f3b2b1aeb3b5316b7199dfeb6901a9cdfe32a5249c7257b","body":{"detail":"Only under a policy that shows less (never under the default, whose fixtures in crates/clax-bot/tests/golden are unchanged): a `turn` step with `assistant: []`, `stop_reason: \"prompt_too_long\"`, zero usage and `next.overflow`; a turn after it with `payload.shown`; and a `bot.turn` audit record with that stop reason and zero tokens. It takes a step number, so it counts toward `max_steps`, and step 1's harness fee is charged on it as on any first step. `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:297-316) shows it as a turn with no text and no tool; the run view and the HTTP run JSON were not looked at. Whoever first ships a policy that shows less checks those readers and pins the shapes as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2335},"end":{"line":2341},"content_hash":"cd4b34db76207885c770671ec43566b4c81e00010452727d08cb7b35988523b8"},"summary":"A refused request that goes on is committed as a turn with no answer, a shape no reader of steps has seen","supersedes":"d0b9bda459fa18e6b251bca7963a46ddaba4f4096308c19493530dd6f4782464","tags":["for-t8","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.023695Z","id":"255eaa44f458f83bd44240c7c128ecc7182fb11e5dfaaf2028f812067dd22cb3","body":{"detail":"`tools(self.limits.script.max_result_bytes)` puts the sandbox's limit on a returned value in the words the model reads (`policy::bytes_text`: `64 KiB` for a whole number of KiB, else `<n> bytes`). Under the default it is `RunSettings::limits()`, whose `max_result_bytes` is always `Limits::default()`'s 64 KiB, so the text cannot change. A policy that answers another size on a later tick of one run changes a tool's description mid-run: the model was told another number, and the cached prefix is lost. docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:94 calls a declaration that changes during a run a failure. `RunPolicy`'s documentation states the rule (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:338-341); a fix that enforces it stores the limits the model was told with the run's first turn and reads them back.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":577},"end":{"line":595},"content_hash":"3b2367aa813c553eb10e0edf87e1fc8636d7bbea89c603409598e06ccd642d1b"},"summary":"The `run_script` description is built from the policy's answer on every turn, and nothing holds that answer still for a run","supersedes":"ca5143221400e33c59ab076ccebbb2e20b3df11001735aec7846bb53830317e9","tags":["for-t9","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.175593Z","id":"4c1e8f1e82731d30059d0b2412b31972970093ea388968295e6fb0ca67325711","body":{"detail":"`must_stop`, `minutes_stop` (run.rs:1318-1324) and the credits stop in `affordable` format `<n> steps`, `<n> credits` and `<n> minutes` from `Tick.limits`, which a policy may answer differently from `RunSettings` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: `limit of 1 steps`, `limit of 5 minutes`). `End::Stopped`, which is to carry the limit's value to a provider's `ending`, takes it from the same place; a run with no cap never produces one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1438},"end":{"line":1461},"content_hash":"871e5b277342c83d4789681df670bedca35eb2161beb7735b4564f49f13cf7df"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings: a cap's value in a notice comes from the policy's limits, not the settings","supersedes":"5df7cce7fd157a9fa8e674dd920916942947b88549d37cc2e72829ea31e5ff99","tags":["for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.329629Z","id":"42ad3153c5bb468159940c36b36a6570838fcf8eae4db7597af8061ff788662a","body":{"detail":"`overflowed` (run.rs:1911-1948) lets the run go on when the policy's answer to `HistoryFor::Overflow` has fewer bytes than the messages that overflowed, and records `next.overflow.shown_sha256`. The next tick's `turn` asks again with `HistoryFor::NextTurn` at the same level (run.rs:1993-2001) and sends that answer, whatever it is; it is not compared with the recorded hash or size. A policy that answers an overflow with less and the next turn with `Shown::Core` passes the rule each time and is never sent shorter. Measured with such a policy, `max_overflow_retries = None`, `max_steps = None` and a provider that refuses every request as too long: 30 ticks, 30 committed turns, 30 requests with identical messages, the run still `running`, 1 credit charged (step 1's fee). With the default `max_overflow_retries` of 4 the same policy ends after 4 such turns. On the stop-reason path each such call is answered and charged, so only the person's credits end it. The module documentation (policy.rs:32-37, 'cannot make the run loop ask twice') and `RunLimits::max_overflow_retries` ('leaves only the rule that each answer is shorter than the last', policy.rs:118-122) state more than the code holds.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2007},"end":{"line":2043},"content_hash":"d71e81ca81a489902e210bc5af76ee2843deaf8b575c5f2f868bd7926931c317"},"suggested_fix":"In `turn`, when the last committed step's `next.overflow` is set, take the policy's answer only if it is `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` is `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (or at least whose bytes are no more than `shown_bytes`); otherwise end the run as `overflowed` returning `None` does. Then a run's overflows in a row are bounded by its bytes even with no `max_overflow_retries`. Or say in the documentation that the bound is `max_overflow_retries` alone and that `None` trusts the policy.","summary":"The turn after an overflow sends whatever the policy answers then: nothing holds it to the shorter history the overflow's ask answered, so the rule that each history is shorter is kept per ask and not per run","supersedes":"13ace998439c84a96a97e064ac9d8feff917e2f92275d76d4cafcabf476ddfaa","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.475290Z","id":"38dc74dfbba7144044d9ca40032e91fbc767198fda69faa3f82faf364540fcde","body":{"detail":"`what_i_did` (1374-1396) reads `clax.scripts`, `clax.changed` and `clax.published`; `finish` reads `clax.replied` (1574). They are the only readers of those four `clax::Tally` fields (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 21-37), so when the notices and the posting rule move into the provider's `ending`, it folds `Tally::of(cx.history)` there and these reads go. `scripts` is in the tally although a script step is the core's and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md (\"Where the nouns are read\", Core) lists it with the core's fields: its one reader is the notice's wording (\"I ran 2 scripts\"), and `history::Control` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 86-99) holds only what the tick decides by. A provider that is not `clax` and wants the count folds it from the steps' kinds. Held: `clax: Tally::default()` at run.rs 1052 fails 25 integration tests, `golden::endings::a_notice_counts_the_scripts_that_ran` among them; 1574 without `replied` fails `golden::endings::finish_posts_no_summary_on_any_thread_when_the_run_replied_on_one`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1464},"end":{"line":1486},"content_hash":"fd90d2fab6f4133c8026975093ec060384b8880c38ff5d1fa161cea5cf6e66bf"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending`: `what_i_did` and the finish rule read `Tick::clax`, and the count of scripts is in the tally, not in the core's fold","supersedes":"cc31d02f04abeaeb37cf4bbfd285275098fba5c206cb99bf6fb942d2bd672acc","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.621534Z","id":"14ecf59970ed1be4f01873ab33fab6e55daa61e74ac3415ba6b7bc03ae4aa6ae","body":{"detail":"The tick holds three things of the committed steps: `hist: history::Control` (the core's: `next`, `nudged`, `committed`, `last_usage`; the field kept its name so `must_stop`'s cap read at 1356 is unchanged), `clax: clax::Tally` (1075) and `steps` (1078), the steps as read, in order, without the open one. After the script reads (script.rs 217, 554) and the ending reads (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) move to the provider, `Tick::clax` has no reader and goes with its import (run.rs 38); from then on run.rs names no field of the tally. `StepCx::new`'s `history` argument (surface.rs 635) is `&self.steps`. `history.rs` is held free of the surface's nouns by its own test (`history::tests::the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface`, which reads the file's source); run.rs is not, and cannot be until this change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1119},"end":{"line":1126},"content_hash":"2ae6de85c19dd03475f998c6f0194121ddd96a1252b92afaf294ca3888d7216f"},"summary":"For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: `Tick::clax` is the one field of the tick that is Clax's own fold, and `Tick::steps` is what `StepCx::history` borrows","supersedes":"5dfe32207552554cad4fde1a15d85053f18b1d8e9cb7a36564cf69f69fc61d45","tags":["t3","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.765509Z","id":"20c0b00f2b538abca60743f35e33331c3c108edd8fe9eced2e3a33fcba80f757","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone to this file, passes all 175 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (the 7 of tests/seam.rs among them): (1) 649 as `if false {`: no `bot.call` record says `replayed`. (2) 685 removed: no `bot.call` record for a journal row the committing attempt did not reach (`unreported_calls`, run.rs 1533-1546). (3) 635 built from `st.calls` instead of `outcome.calls`: the `bot.script` record lists the calls that reached the host and not the sandbox's. (4) 705 as `0`: the `do.requests` line (run.rs 1080) counts no call. For contrast, 648 without the target fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned` and two more, 148 (`Event::Call` without its target) fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned`, 679 without the events fails `cancel::a_cancel_mid_script_charges_what_it_used_and_keeps_no_draft`, and 701 without the compute line fails five tests. The proof in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs builds no audit record and no usage (`Stepper`, seam.rs 471-517), and the resumed fixtures pin the step, the journal and the host's writes only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 50-57). All four are the run loop's own part of a script step (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 151-156, 168-178), which is rewritten around `Answer` and `CallNote` when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":633},"end":{"line":651},"content_hash":"202999706742add9714df1b2fce301308d48ea6cdf628a3362adb2c819a35735"},"suggested_fix":"Before this file moves: add the run's audit records and the step's usage rows to what crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs pins (a resumed step has replayed calls), pin the audit of a diverged replay whose first attempt went further than its second, pin a script with a call the sandbox refused (`script.error.call-limit.json`'s script) with its `bot.script` record, and assert the `do.requests` quantity of a script step with `infra_lines` on.","summary":"No test of clax-bot holds the `replayed` mark of a `bot.call` record, the `bot.call` records of calls no committed attempt reported, which list the `bot.script` record names, or the call count of the infra usage line","supersedes":"bbf7334f83feb552fa27799b967defefbfa9cba6b94a4a8758d170bcfd8bc2af","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:35.913187Z","id":"67e0b648578c2cfbe985fa7fbe7dd150274f701ba07a58f1a81bb02d8ef6005f","body":{"detail":"(1) Keep the replay check first in the web session: a web call given its entry is answered from it before check 2 (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451; surface.rs row 130); crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` holds the adapter to it. (2) Pin the new `web_disabled` journal row, `{code, message}`, in a journal fixture for a run that did not declare the web and for one on a host with no source (surface.rs 132-144; record 0d124949). (3) Write the divergence check: `Stepper` (seam.rs 532-581) asserts on a diverged replay instead of answering `replay_diverged`, and `dispatch` only asserts in a debug build that the entry it is given is of its call; `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins today's audit of one. (4) Refuse a call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the calls are numbered (458-462 here; `StepCx::place`, `no_place`), which today's code does not. (5) Give the real provider's conformance fixture a `fail_once` and a `disturb`: the adapter's fixture has neither (seam.rs 1836), so the cases for a failing `begin` and `ending` are not played against `clax`, and its read is never disturbed. (6) Pass `CallPlace::attempt` to `OpCtx` (seam.rs `ClaxSession::op_ctx`, 232): the fake host now records the attempt it is given and the proof compares it, and the server's host reads it. (7) Join every session's `tool_result` fields before the result is trimmed (surface.rs 170-175). (8) Say where a surface's standing operations come from: `Binding::from_stored` takes a rule the stored binding does not hold, and the proof hard-codes `clax`'s (seam.rs 81-99; `declared`, crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 253, is private). (9) The audit and usage of a script step are pinned for today's path only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, scripts.rs): `replayed`, `unreported_calls`, the calls a `bot.script` record lists, the requests line; the proof builds none of them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":340},"end":{"line":449},"content_hash":"775ebefe9eba51456748adebbbbb5159bb8871891790ef2bd4c8d76927ce1e51"},"summary":"For the change that moves this file behind the seam: what crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs and its tests could not do without it","supersedes":"a4777d70312059a23561d5a4bcc760877132fed33e98f187b7e9b46a5d5ffcf0","tags":["seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.057427Z","id":"983fa5d948f7940d0258cb97a9b56830e35939bea98856942ec2a33aac938bce","body":{"detail":"Three tests make a divergence: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` (258) and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 write a journal row for another call at place 0 by hand, and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (273) runs another script for the first attempt (`Another`, 243) whose first call is `files.read` where the stored script's is `files.list`. A script's clock and random numbers are fixed for its step and its first call is made before any answer, so a real second attempt always makes the first attempt's first call: a real divergence is at place 1 or later, after calls that were answered from the journal. What that leaves unpinned: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` (the model is told `completed: []` whatever was replayed) passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`; so does no fixture hold a step whose audit has a `replayed` `bot.call`, a `replay_diverged` one and an unreported row together. The mechanics `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins (a `bot.call` with `attempt` and `seq` for each row the committing attempt did not reach, the first attempt's reply standing) are those of a real run; its `diverged_at: 0` is not. `completed` is what tells the model which calls of the script took effect before the replay stopped, and it is built from the run loop's own log of calls (506-511), which is rewritten when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":503},"end":{"line":511},"content_hash":"331d0ccb3052c72097c372911f08deafdafca36960c23c77c327fc73f25a612a"},"suggested_fix":"Give `Another`'s first-attempt script the stored script's first call (`files.list`) and let it differ from the second call on: the fixture then pins `completed: [\"files.list\"]` in the step's result, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows. Add the step's `result` to what the fixture pins.","summary":"Every diverged replay in the tests diverges at call 0, which one script cannot do: `replay.completed` is never pinned with a call in it, and no fixture has replayed, diverged and unreported calls in one step","supersedes":"e65c2488200d29c0ef288c65847a753f02b1e88ee3e17ec7f7c256137f245965","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.205304Z","id":"7564590a4493710cb151474667f770e3c09df7943ba45796371ac003bf16eaed","body":{"detail":"`policy` reads `self.tick.clax.created` (217) and `Tick::script` reads `self.clax.base` (554). Both are `clax::Tally` fields (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 21-37), folded by the tick (run.rs 1052). A `clax` session opened by `SurfaceProvider::begin` should fold them itself from `StepCx::history`, as the proof's adapter now does with the same function (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 159), and the tick's copy then has no reader in this file. `Tally::of` reads only steps whose `committed_at` is set; `StepCx::history` is documented as the committed steps (surface.rs 606-610), and the tick's `steps` may hold a second uncommitted step if a run ever had two (only the first is taken out as `open`, run.rs 1041-1044), so pass the slice as it is and let the fold filter. Mutations that show both reads are held: 554 as `self.run.base_version` fails `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version` and `seam::a_script_after_a_rebase_starts_from_the_version_rebased_onto`; 217 without the tally's `created` fails `rules::a_brief_run_creates_one_artifact` and `golden::scripts::a_second_artifact_is_refused`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":214},"end":{"line":214},"content_hash":"747aeedba6ac31e345c713aebd9c313655307caf42544c65efe21f88dc9658fc"},"summary":"For the change that moves script.rs behind the seam: the two reads of `Tick::clax` here (217, 554) become the clax session's own `Tally::of(cx.history)`","supersedes":"5016f60e3f0f0ca8a9cac1943212ce812cf99fdc279430ca980c44fcaa7c28c6","tags":["t3","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.353394Z","id":"7323532dcfd01b9b4cb40c4202acc3c514e0a28a70bd087650158b6fc2ec19d6","body":{"detail":"The turn calls `transcript(&self.steps)` once (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1746) and reads `system` (is this the first request, 1747; the system block, 1773) and `messages` (1775). A policy's rule stands at that call. Three things for that change. (1) The design's signature is `fn history(&self, run: &RunInfo, steps: &[Step], why: HistoryFor) -> Result<History>` (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md 864): its `History` is `history::Transcript` here (named so while the reference fold `History` is kept for tests, crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs); the default ignores `run` and `why`. (2) `first` is `committed == 0 || system.is_none()`: a rule that returns no system text for a run whose step 1 stored one makes the turn write the system prompt and the request again and store them in a later step's payload, which changes the cached prefix. The contract says so in words (history.rs 150-153); no type prevents it. Returning the system text apart from the rule's messages (the core reading it from step 1 itself) would remove the case. (3) The contract's other clauses (tool_use and tool_result kept in pairs, order, roles alternating from the user's, stored blocks unchanged) are documentation only: a checker `fn(&[Step], &Transcript) -> Result<(), String>` run on every rule's output in debug builds and in a conformance test would hold them; the default passes whenever the stored steps do.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":171},"end":{"line":245},"content_hash":"05d96467aafafc188db5484966b9845aced8c899d490c67c32ad3dbc71161d49"},"summary":"For the change that adds `RunPolicy`: `transcript` is the default history rule, called in one place, and nothing checks that another rule keeps the contract its documentation states","supersedes":"da1b820bbf6c9afb22a897a3184700a8700507c8ab1b80676c82148298f983fe","tags":["t3","for-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.499869Z","id":"28f383eeeb42af0fce7dcf556cc2883771bcdad3298c6d54a829ac394c94e411","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface` (203-260) splits this file at its test module and fails on a noun of Clax's surface in any line of the code or its documentation (artifact, thread, draft, version, reply, publish, created, changed, base, tally and their forms, as whole words), on `crate::clax` or `crate::run` in a path, and on any use of `clax_store` but `bot_runs::{Step, StepKind}`. It is a word list: a noun it does not list (`comment`, `file`) or a payload key read through a variable passes, and it holds one file, not `Control`'s readers. A crate for the core's folds would make the compiler hold the imports, but `Step` itself is `clax_store`'s and its payload is untyped JSON, so no arrangement of crates stops `p[\"base\"]`; and the layering (scripts/check-crate-layers.py) would gain a crate for 190 lines. Two mutations show what the test catches: the word `draft` in a doc comment, and `use crate::clax::Tally as _;`, each fail it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":460},"end":{"line":574},"content_hash":"702cf0a3b8aaae14f3f9f842b3bfdc67d4af4ce5cecca106ecc029aaa05b9854"},"summary":"Holding the core's folds free of Clax's nouns by the compiler (a crate of their own, or a module with no access to `clax_store`'s run types) instead of by a test that reads the source","supersedes":"544264e0d67f5af762819b18e6b716b35ca209931161ad7614c1bec9278bd356","tags":["t3","revisit:the core's run loop moves to a crate of its own, or a step's payload gets a type per surface","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.655101Z","id":"190ef4cb9870511e4bdca406c9fedac804f570094ad666e66ef59f790958e47b","body":{"detail":"`billed_micro_usd: v[\"billed_micro_usd\"].as_u64().filter(|b| *b > 0)` at line 80 passes `//crates/clax-bot:all`. `usage_of` reads a turn's stored `usage` (`Control::last_usage`, line 116) and each observed entry of an open turn (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 891), where a billed cost of 0 and no billed cost are charged differently (the billed share against the catalog's rate). `usage_reads_each_count_and_the_billed_cost` (history.rs 492) uses 5. The function moved here unchanged from run.rs; the gap is older than the move. Suggested: a case with `billed_micro_usd: 0` giving `Some(0)`, and one with a count that is not a number giving 0.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":96},"end":{"line":107},"content_hash":"069486e025033d82c920f4049079642aa8628e518ac81b4a9d5c6540c2bfc154"},"summary":"No test holds `usage_of` for a billed cost of 0: read as none, every test passes","supersedes":"5d4466881832252be8c11c3cf7e5a2fb6eca1d5b47aa16d17a7bd7653cc35252","tags":["t3","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.803306Z","id":"449f4c176dde4faf411066dd4ceb1dc356785b009ef13569fea715096676a14e","body":{"detail":"`c.next = p[\"next\"].clone()` (line 117) runs for each committed turn, and a turn's `next` for a script holds the script's whole `code`. So a tick clones every script the run has asked for, as the fold in run.rs did before the split (it cloned the whole payload as well). Keeping a `&Value` to the last turn's `next` in the loop and cloning once after it makes the sentence true and the fold allocate once.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":41},"end":{"line":48},"content_hash":"20dae79dac68e73e63ffd36c2c8118bcac362ccf3c7708158c7ba9f6193fd49d"},"summary":"The cost paragraph says `Control` clones one `next` object; it clones every committed turn's and drops all but the last","supersedes":"8534c9c4ea3466eae0d597bdf3db236439cd360393b1cc5a5ab11df8205018ad","tags":["t3","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:36.959622Z","id":"c77e0a0f9ae66bbfd18b8f89f7728498db43161d4a951127fed3d576127a004f","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:859-861 gives `RunPolicy::settings(&self, run) -> Result<RunSettings>`. Here the tick still reads the instance's settings from `Deps.settings` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1162), because the claim's lease is needed before the run is loaded and the driver reads concurrency with no run in hand (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:214, :392), and then passes them to `RunPolicy::run_settings(run, instance)` and `limits(run, settings)`. That costs no extra read a tick and leaves `Deps.settings` as it was for the server and the Worker. What it gives up: `Deps` carries two things (`settings`, `policy`), and because of the blanket impl at policy.rs:381 a type that is a SettingsSource cannot also implement RunPolicy with answers of its own (the impls would overlap), so an environment that wants both writes two types and passes the second to `Deps::with_policy`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":551},"content_hash":"224f6bdf8a6280fe79ed3af3dda906a0dc0113c19a2d91822b3d2a4df8505d0e"},"summary":"A policy that is the one source of a run's settings (`settings(run)`, as the design draws it), with the blanket impl bridging a SettingsSource to it","supersedes":"adbdee05a292a38e81d4177c84a1ddd625abdbd30b2379c5ab4005270b1c7d9b","tags":["revisit:the tick holds a RunInfo before it claims, or the server's or the Worker's settings type needs limits of its own","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:37.109236Z","id":"0af362d68652f134f4e1ee339b779a0779f0ba6da3b690ae66808218a487b3d7","body":{"detail":"`run_settings`, `limits` and `history` take `&clax_store::bot_runs::Run`, the only description of a run the tick has today (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1180-1181, :1934, :1994). The design's policy is told a `RunInfo` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs:386), which names no artifact or thread. When the tick holds one, these parameters change type: a breaking change for implementers, of which there is one today (`Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs). The three questions the design adds (`accepts`, `to_model`, `delivery`) are not methods yet; each can be added with a default without changing an implementer.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":543},"content_hash":"414d2c8bc944cba69f07ad32b6b18b3d5e1cdb8cc399b58a720e9d97a5811a32"},"summary":"For the change that gives the tick a `RunInfo`: every `RunPolicy` method takes the store's `Run` row","supersedes":"c82f2d94df29a1cf2dd450a59ae93427b737287cd38e87dccad6158bb85295a8","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:37.266727Z","id":"224c55652d2e5b065b16ccaea31a61fcd81cbc073588c76e60edd5e699d96858","body":{"detail":"`prompt_too_long` matches four phrases in the message of a 400 (`prompt is too long`, `context_length_exceeded`, `maximum context length`, `context window`). They are from memory of the Messages, Chat Completions and Responses APIs, not from a recorded answer: crates/clax-sample has no typed error for it (`MessagesError::InvalidRequest` carries only the text, crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:618). A provider that says it otherwise takes the `model_error` stop, which is the behaviour before the policy, so nothing is lost under the default; a compacting policy would not be asked. A phrase that matched a refusal for another reason would ask the policy for a shorter history once per overflow, bounded as any overflow is. The fix belongs in clax-sample: a typed variant set from the provider's error type, with recorded answers as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":478},"end":{"line":500},"content_hash":"675b254309be2d640527fff932bc37c45aed6e4ad1e7f3f4509780728f15d450"},"summary":"A refusal for length is recognised by the provider's words, which no test checks against a provider","supersedes":"206c56a24024af6bd8494720e35437f7a45f32e39cb633bbe38f54cd1c71487b","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:37.414209Z","id":"21d75d6c0a67a9ece0f341153314b9255642f82bbbe3eea8a571e21eeec2cb97","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits::of` sets `model_call_timeout` to the settings' `max_run_minutes` (30 minutes by default). Under the default policy it never binds, since what is left of the run's time is never more. A policy that sets `max_run_minutes = None` and leaves the timeout gets calls of at most the settings' minutes each, on a native driver with no tick budget; before this, the only bounds on a call were the run's remaining time, the tick's budget, and the client's 120 s for headers and 90 s between pieces (crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:1113-1120), which bound a silence and not a call. The design names 'a floor for the model call's own timeout' as work of its own (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:968). `max_overflow_retries` is `Some(4)`: unreachable under the default policy, and a guess for any other. Question for the owner: what one model call may take where no run cap is set (a setting of its own, or the minutes kept as now), and whether a default bound on overflows in a row is wanted at all beside the rule that each history is shorter than the last.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":215},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"550cebbd95a87439839eeb2c9110e20facccf5fa7e0900251fcd61fa053b45da"},"summary":"Two defaults chosen here with nothing behind them: the model call's timeout when a policy sets no minutes, and four overflows in a row","supersedes":"ea37b8c0a1989a8de7dedfb92442236bafad3225e89617e3dcc1ef596c6e5da1","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:37.562764Z","id":"67f38d18aab9d98bda239ee1547666f9951b8a0341b36851939dd8631f6b4dec","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_no_cap_or_limit_but_the_policys` includes `run.rs` and `script.rs` as text and fails when either names `settings.max_steps`, `settings.max_run_credits`, `settings.max_run_minutes`, `settings.limits()`, the old constants, `at most 64` or `<< 10`, or declares any `const`; and when `run.rs` or `script.rs` no longer reads the `limits.*` fields. A cap read through another binding (`let s = &self.settings; s.max_steps`) passes it, and a file that code moves to is not looked at. When a script's calls move to `clax/*` and the endings to a provider, add those files to the list. `RunLimits::observe_every` has no test of its effect: an attempt's observed entry replaces its last (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1429-1432) and the final figures are recorded whatever the cadence, so only its default and that `run.rs` reads it are held. Keep reading the tool result's size from `Tick.limits.tool_result_bytes` and the sandbox's limits from `Tick.limits.script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":818},"end":{"line":847},"content_hash":"568c959526b330c4080e40006cbea219cc1df10082d35be68cbe6a9134fc774c"},"summary":"The check that the run loop reads no cap of its own is on the text of two files, by name","supersedes":"7236efaf4e0fde264579200e676b68b8722da37cff67547c18832f0587c0a83c","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:37.712711Z","id":"e8b13c661b6a75263c1acddeecef931bdbb78309265a503b478cafda2d9b4162","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits` is `#[non_exhaustive]` with public fields, so outside clax-bot it cannot be written as a literal or with `..`: the one constructor is `RunLimits::of(&settings)`, then assignments (as `Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:104-108 does). `of` answers `Some` for the three caps, `model_call_timeout` and `max_overflow_retries`. An environment that wants a run with no bound writes five assignments; one that forgets `model_call_timeout` keeps calls cut at the settings' minutes (retried, see below), and when a sixth bound is added every such policy gets it set without a change to its source. That is the opposite of the module's rule that a cap the environment does not set is not replaced by one of the run loop's (policy.rs:10-13). Neither the struct nor `of` says that `of` is the way to make one. `HistoryAsk` is also `#[non_exhaustive]` and its `new` is `pub(crate)` (policy.rs:232), so a host cannot construct an ask to test its own `history` outside a tick. What `model_call_timeout` does when it is the bound that fires is not written either: the call ends as a cut stream, which is retried in the tick and then waits `Deps.retry_later`; it is never an ending. Measured with `model_call_timeout = Some(0)`: every tick answers `Waiting(Retry(30s))` and the run stays `running`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":156},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"430b729a1705bcbf3369b3d51c1febcab00be07f9b9be9e8eaab8efd8a13fab7"},"suggested_fix":"Add `RunLimits::unbounded(&RunSettings)` (or `none`): every `Option` `None`, the sizes from the settings and the defaults, documented as what a policy starts from when it means no caps, and say on `of` that it is today's bounds. Make `HistoryAsk::new` public. Say on `model_call_timeout` that a call it cuts is tried again and ends nothing.","summary":"A policy can build its limits only from `RunLimits::of`, which sets every bound: one that means 'no caps' must clear each by name, and a bound added later arrives set","supersedes":"e4519b05f96adb2392e91d8d578b4cac6b4a524d8828c9a45a835268e2f288dc","tags":["review","verify-t4","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:37.860169Z","id":"2150012049c01958468bbd7369226bc93f4b476f5123cf539beeb16160c52cf7","body":{"detail":"Row 3 has the core check the arguments \"against the surface's file\" and then find the binding (`route`). With one surface that is `api::check_args` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs:215), which reads Clax's own definition; with two, which file to check against is known only once the call is routed, so the order is route, check, `Binding::has`, session. `route` (267-275) answers `None` for a run with no `clax` binding calling an operation with no surface prefix (the module's own test: `route(only_fake, \"files.read\")` is `None`), and the table does not say what the script is answered or whether it is journaled; crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:497 `expect`s. And neither `SurfaceProvider` nor `Binding` leads to a `clax_surface::Definition`: `Binding::hash` is `None` for every stored binding of version 1, and `FakeSurfaceProvider::definition` is an associated function, not a trait method. A run with one fake surface and no `clax` needs the definition for the argument check, for the declaration the model reads and for the sandbox's operation list.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":52},"end":{"line":54},"content_hash":"913b92947cf82bb4f71f23f3428079a9aa2ae9ad59cf27d24b518d1345bf9edd"},"suggested_fix":"Write the order as route, then check against that surface's file, then `has`; say a call no binding takes is answered `unknown_op` and journaled by the core as `Outcome`; and add where a definition comes from (a `definition()` method on the provider, or a registry by `Binding::hash`) before the tick holds providers.","summary":"The step table checks a call's arguments before it finds the binding, says nothing of a call no binding takes, and no part of the seam gives the core a surface's definition","supersedes":"aead2e93a1b0c4b1610eca780e919d1d1f8b8d34bea8629b14f4dff204ea92f8","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.006695Z","id":"027c6110ff49c2f22afee1ac8c43f73c35ff09002cd86ae525202e2ee77ba74b","body":{"detail":"Read top to bottom as that change's author. (1) Row 3 (52): a call refused `no_place` is \"unjournaled\"; it does not say whether it is in the step's `calls`, the events and the `bot.call` records, and counted on the requests line, as a `replay_diverged` answer is (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs it). (2) `no_place`'s code is `call_limit` (973), the name of the sandbox's `ErrorKind::CallLimit`, but it is a `HostError` a script can catch, in other words (\"call N is past the 1000000 calls one script may make\") than the sandbox's uncatchable end (\"the script made more than its N host calls\", crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs 1019): one name for two things the model can meet. (3) 138-140 still says a refused web call's row \"is never read by this code (the refusal is made again…)\", which row 130 now contradicts for the declared half: it is read, by `from_entry`. (4) Two rows of `Answer`'s table (793-796) claim a call answered from its entry: `of` (\"any call answered from its entry\") and `written` (\"a call answered from an entry the provider wrote\"). The journal of a replayed call is not read, so either works, but only `written` may carry `charged(n)`; say that the constructor of a replayed answer matters for the charge alone. (5) A `FieldClash` is counted as a failed commit (253-256): not said for the `failed` step itself, where an `ending`'s `Err` is logged and its fields left out (1503-1505), nor whether the other bindings' parts of that attempt are kept. (6) Nothing says which of a provider's answers the core turns into `Event::Call`'s `web` field (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 153): `CallNote::extra` has no named field. (7) `Binding::from_stored`'s rule for `clax` is given as \"`declared` in api.rs, which is private\" (341-343): on branch `claxbot-surfaces` it is `pub(crate)`. (8) Still open in their own records: where another surface's standing operations come from, and who resolves the request at the first turn.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"1773f07d84631f73db7f8bb4f1fbc13939bc7388129f3221041ff2bae54e354c"},"summary":"What the module documentation leaves the change that moves script.rs behind the seam to ask","supersedes":"3e8f245d6e726b970474b491a421defbcc13cdaa352b3c974bfee1cc7159f49a","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.155174Z","id":"77c3d58e519a128a834099aae1c4cc53f9ea3dd3275d4301d323b3996edb5c71","body":{"detail":"The constant in crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs became a policy value (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:110, default `DEFAULT_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, the same 100 KiB). The change that moves the tool result's building behind the seam reads it from the tick's limits and corrects this sentence.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":173},"end":{"line":173},"content_hash":"8bf31c422ba0ce9e6a8b60d7a4e62f7559fbe7f3a402e5ecc5e6ae98d2773cb1"},"summary":"This line names `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, which is gone: the size is `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`","supersedes":"7b6f18d927fd901364981e3345bda4d24fbecc5caf0a89f93ef9d5f41a151280","tags":["for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.311149Z","id":"cd2a3d2bc1b5fdc4eb8294288313d5cb907abf7eaff6dbf36145eda48f171200","body":{"detail":"The split removed run.rs 533-653 (the `History` section) and added 12 lines further down, so what the tables call 1681-1719 (`finish`) is at 1572-1610, 1549-1590 (`stop`) at 1440-1481, 1451-1480 (`must_stop`) at 1342-1371, 1483-1504 (`what_i_did`) at 1374-1396, 1509-1529 (`notify_at`) at 1405-1420, and so on through the endings table (207-216) and wherever else the file cites run.rs. The two citations of the fold itself were corrected to name `clax::Tally` (surface.rs 123, 213). The plan's measurements (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md) and the open records on run.rs cite the old lines too (`qualifier review crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs` lists the drifted ones). The change that edits run.rs next for the endings will move them again; recite then, or cite by function name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":207},"end":{"line":216},"content_hash":"6e8dcb9a9510610a6846486fb62d05388d5e63fee98d6e6f2c3c22de1dcd0d48"},"summary":"The module documentation cites run.rs by line, and every citation past run.rs 533 is now about 110 lines too high","supersedes":"234a3614a28e56688679c7dfb733cb93570b988603256b548d4c104f99a7eacf","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.467762Z","id":"51f5801f749bcbf7dba342c14a8ab76159d1ea6bd28107a48f8ea9035cc64156","body":{"detail":"tick reads run_with_steps and History::of parses each payload (assistant blocks, script code and outcome, the first turn's system prompt) to rebuild the messages, so a run of N steps does O(N) work and reads O(total payload) per tick, O(N^2) over the run. Bounded by max_steps (40 by default); a cache of the history keyed by the last committed step would make it O(1).\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (94a4332d), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1064},"end":{"line":1066},"content_hash":"86670f07eb2b225e04ae2e8869d2bb5892139643f951eca6c4956f9345a06496"},"summary":"Every tick rebuilds the whole history from every committed step's payload","supersedes":"06b231efca2d53f3527a7cbfac8863544ea10b5abd030c5991e4245bbe49d3d3","tags":["perf","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.630395Z","id":"cac7184b575b4d4e37564f63a28423abaa60604b5ad760b7db6f98ce7d1c5ffb","body":{"detail":"`tick` asks `run_settings` and `limits` after the claim and before `Tick::step`, so before `must_stop` (run.rs:1506-1509) reads a cancel. An `Err` from either is the tick's error: `Driver::settle` logs it, releases the run and tries again after `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (30 s, driver.rs:345-348), for ever. Measured with a policy whose `limits` fails: the tick answers `Err`, the run stays `running` with no step and no notice; a person who stops it gets a run that stays `stopping`, since no tick reaches `must_stop`. An `Err` from `history` in `overflowed` on the stop-reason path (run.rs:2261-2263) comes after the provider answered: the turn stays open, the answer's usage is written as absorbed rows (4 rows in the measurement: the instance's cost, no credits), and the next tick calls the model again with the same full window. While the policy keeps failing that is one absorbed full-window call every 30 s, bounded only by the run's minutes if it has any, and not by the person's credits. No policy fails under the defaults (`DefaultPolicy` answers `Ok`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1067},"end":{"line":1081},"content_hash":"4c690f85ffcedfb7a41ea8a477fd9a0122e80e955f9d4d2164e2805ce3a33a1f"},"summary":"A policy that fails leaves a run running that cannot be cancelled, and after a full window drops an answer the instance paid for, again on every retry","supersedes":"81989257f90ec309b92d3bb2913c3595b1cacc7e834d4c04babc0c511d40ff7e","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.780019Z","id":"39db5cdae10f00447d066be5c7fdeff9c797f7769ae078aaf011722127e186d2","body":{"detail":"Today: `Control::of` and `Tally::of` run when the tick is built (1051-1052), `transcript` in `turn` (1746). The tally is read by a script tick (script.rs 217, 554) and a terminal tick (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) and not by a turn, so a turn tick folds it for nothing: one pass that clones a few strings. A `OnceCell<Tally>` on `Tick` would save that pass and cost a `Send`/`Sync` question on wasm32 and native alike (the tick is borrowed across awaits), for a field that leaves the tick when the provider folds its own. Not worth it before then.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1973},"end":{"line":1977},"content_hash":"ee259796ac8410d3a2e93d3193d750c7263adf945a3f03269d6ff9ba4be0876c"},"summary":"Folding the transcript and the tally lazily, once, behind a cell on the tick, instead of the tally on every tick and the transcript in the turn","supersedes":"8e4a5cdbc1917c79398af0adc112dce5f8d5ee8c93786e1f8f029dc47f17b8bd","tags":["t3","revisit:a profile of a tick shows the tally's pass, or `Tick::clax` outlives the change that moves the endings behind the provider","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:55:38.927635Z","id":"7c9a3905f1732b8a48521aefa6be644f444df1368edd452048728df2002b0341","body":{"detail":"The open step is taken out by `position` and `remove`. Three changes here pass every test of `//crates/clax-bot:all`: `rposition`, `swap_remove`, and `steps[i].clone()` (the open step left in). They pass because the three folds filter on `committed_at` themselves and a run has at most one open step, the last (asserted on every tick of the integration suite with a temporary assertion: `steps` all committed, the open step's `n` above the last one's; it held). `StepCx::history` is documented as the run's committed steps (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 606-610) and the proof's adapter relies on it (`history.last()` is the turn that asked for the script, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 614-619). When the tick passes `&self.steps` there, a provider that reads the last step, or counts them, is wrong on a run with a second open step or with the open one left in, and no test says so. Suggested: build `steps` with `retain(|s| s.committed_at.is_some())` after taking the first open one, so the field's documentation (run.rs 1076-1078) is true by construction, and a unit test of that on a list with two open steps.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1066},"end":{"line":1066},"content_hash":"084753e5341f60697fbdc29a18b2db7f4ea34e914a9b1e698c523d833a5a8717"},"summary":"Nothing holds that `Tick::steps` is the committed steps only, which is what `StepCx::history` promises a provider","supersedes":"2530f375d1e20ecfb98a46ac40f6f88ef0330801a671129a98b2a52bebfcfd99","tags":["t3","for-t5","for-t8","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:02.854852Z","id":"9edf1df3ea36aa0a90b0a8ba224f3317fa594890367ef8373b118dc6851f38c7","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, with `infra_lines` on: `a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call` (249: `max_steps` 2, a turn, a script of five calls, then the cap: the requests line is 1, 6, 1) and `a_cancelled_scripts_stopped_step_counts_no_call_on_its_requests_line` (287: a script cancelled once its second call is answered: 1 for the turn and 1 for the script's step committed as `stopped`). With line 1570 as `5`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails both and passes without the change. Not done: (2) a stop without `unreported_calls` and (3) a notice's row getting a `bot.call` record still pass every test, since no ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); nor is a replayed call's `ms` of 0 pinned (the goldens replace every `ms` by a name). Adding `p.audit()` to an ending fixture changes bytes that are pinned, which this branch was not to do; both are in the reply on record 264e99a6.","kind":"comment","references":"5ed5547d0ca5351abc25a5b10e863788dbc3fd9a8c828861f428aa09472c3316","span":{"start":{"line":1547},"end":{"line":1554},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"summary":"A `stopped` step's requests line is held for a cap stop after a script and for a script cancelled after two calls; the two audit-only changes are still unpinned and are passed to the change that moves the endings","supersedes":"397fe9a589272aaaacd35507ae47e6131982fa6c032e0cde8b39e62f51a7c228","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.003624Z","id":"cdda0b489a97ce3283b420ac5222113bf79d653e558bc4181655400826291580","body":{"detail":"(a) Records b25bed99 and f2d3e3a6, cited in (4) and (5), were re-recorded at their lines and are now a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c and 68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10 (the same threads). (b) Two audit-only behaviours of a stop pass every test when changed (record d016f728): `stop` without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (1574-1575 here: no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported), and `unreported_calls` without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (1539: every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record). No ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); pin `p.audit()` for one stopped ending and the cancelled one before these lines move. With several bindings the second matters more: the places of every binding's notices are at or above `NOTICE_SEQ` (`StepCx::notice`), and none should be audited as a script's call. (c) A `stopped` step's requests line is now held (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 249, 287): 1, whatever calls the script made. (d) To test the clash rule in a run loop, a provider is needed that answers a field named `calls` (or another of `PAYLOAD_KEYS`) from `finish` or `ending`: `FakeSurfaceProvider` answers only `fake_set` and `fake_notice`, so give it a way to answer a field a test names. The rule to hold: the attempt counted as a failed commit, the run ended `failed` at the bound, and in the `failed` step the clashing binding's fields left out and the others kept (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, `merge_fields`). `merge_fields` now takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for the step.","kind":"comment","references":"53c256e6ad20c899f2350f3253e7c9dd569ed2478c4408fc482fcc9a3dfbce64","span":{"start":{"line":1530},"end":{"line":1804},"content_hash":"2cf9021b0bbad577eb7e4a5fb104ade3cb0decafa124efcc19c7194e3fa00108"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, and three things about a stop's audit and a clash that no test holds","supersedes":"f6f9936a2abce982153210aeb12fd67a85a1c6d0bc56de71758802b7e2cc4572","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.150366Z","id":"e458c75676b7fd998f94f76011a3deca94fcb0c8305a95f9d0fb368eca49d4eb","body":{"detail":"`golden::resumed::a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call` and `a_cancelled_scripts_stopped_step_counts_no_call_on_its_requests_line` fail with the change. Read against the code: a turn charges `1 + 0` (`step_usage`, 1080), a script step `1 + st.calls.len()` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 702-708: 6 for five calls), a cap stop is a new step charged `1 + 0` here (1567-1573), which gives 1, 6, 1; a cancelled script's step is begun again as `stopped` under the same number (1558) and charged here with no call counted, which gives 1 for the turn and 1 for step 2. Still passing every test when changed, and audit only: 1574-1575 without `unreported_calls`, 1539 without the `NOTICE_SEQ` filter; and crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 (`ms` of a replayed call).","kind":"comment","references":"5ed5547d0ca5351abc25a5b10e863788dbc3fd9a8c828861f428aa09472c3316","span":{"start":{"line":1547},"end":{"line":1554},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: line 1570 as `5` fails both new tests, and their quantities are what this file charges","supersedes":"67cbb9c8b9216633f709e53d8f198bc3ce131fcfa96a527473fc9fcb460ffcfb","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.297848Z","id":"6ef44e052a89d285e23fbf01a5b45a0257ad038c8578ac511793e54cb330674f","body":{"detail":"(1) `replayed`: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `left` (67) now pins the run's audit records, the drafts given to publish and the usage rows beside the step, journal and writes, and `pin_resumed` (100) requires a resumed step to have `bot.call` records that say `replayed` and an uncrashed one to have none. `resumed.publish.json` and `resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json` gained lines and lost none (`git diff --numstat 2230ac3c9 HEAD -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden`: 406 and 478 added, 0 deleted). script.rs 649 as `if false` fails four tests of `golden::resumed`. (2) `unreported_calls`: `audit.replay-diverged.json` (resumed.rs 273) is a run whose first attempt made four calls and died before its commit, and whose second diverges at call 0; it pins a `bot.call` record with `attempt` and `seq` for each of the three rows the second attempt did not reach. The first attempt's other path comes from a sandbox that runs another script than the one stored (`Another`, 243), since a script is deterministic across attempts. script.rs 685 removed fails that test. (3) which calls the `bot.script` record lists: `audit.script.call-limit.json` pins the record of the call-limit script, as this record asked, but the sandbox does not count the call over its limit, so that pin does not tell the two lists apart; `audit.script.call-not-awaited.json` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs 194) does: a call the script did not await is in the record's `calls` and in what the model is told (`script_finished`), with no `bot.call` record, journal row or place in the step's `calls`. script.rs 635 built from `st.calls` fails that test. (4) `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (resumed.rs 219), with `infra_lines` on: the `do.requests` line is 1 for a turn and 6 for a script of five calls. script.rs 705 as `0` fails it. Each change was made alone and reverted; `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests) passes without it.","kind":"comment","references":"20c0b00f2b538abca60743f35e33331c3c108edd8fe9eced2e3a33fcba80f757","span":{"start":{"line":633},"end":{"line":651},"content_hash":"202999706742add9714df1b2fce301308d48ea6cdf628a3362adb2c819a35735"},"summary":"All four are pinned for today's path; each of the four changes now fails a test","supersedes":"4f1b25f4f2559f6cc53bb272dea4a6a3ef3c6d33d9d1a3e67d627e5f9f092b50","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.446515Z","id":"5769ee458939ea7bc7223e4a322d15370b7574ee0c2fa2809bdac95b6bbcfb2f","body":{"detail":"Against `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests): 649 as `if false` fails four tests of `golden::resumed`; 685 removed fails `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`; 635 from `st.calls` fails `golden::scripts::a_call_that_was_not_awaited_is_in_the_scripts_record_alone`; 705 as `0` fails `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls`. `resumed.publish.json` and `resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json` at 2230ac3c9 and at 3b700b895, parsed and compared: every key and value of the old file is in the new one unchanged, and the new ones add `audit`, `drafts_published` and `usage`. What is still unpinned of a script step and a stop is in the records on crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 505-513 and crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1566-1573.","kind":"comment","references":"20c0b00f2b538abca60743f35e33331c3c108edd8fe9eced2e3a33fcba80f757","span":{"start":{"line":633},"end":{"line":651},"content_hash":"202999706742add9714df1b2fce301308d48ea6cdf628a3362adb2c819a35735"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: each of the four changes now fails a test; the two fixtures that existed gained keys and lost or changed none","supersedes":"aae6e652991ae71e4269b93edb004bf0bf9fd0de1bc1356b10031285ecfab17f","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.591615Z","id":"50b70258ea1b41cb26991b2d4d741b95bca0fc04e94e588692288b49a62b6318","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (364): the first attempt's script (through `Another`) now makes the stored script's first call, `files.list`, then `files.read`, `threads.reply` and `artifact.get`; the second attempt is answered `files.list` again and diverges at call 1. The fixture gained `result` (the step's `tool_result`, parsed): `replay: {completed: [\"files.list\"], diverged_at: 1, why}`. Its audit now has a `bot.call` that says `replayed` (`files.list`), the diverged `threads.reply` (`ok: false`), and a record with `attempt: 1` and `seq` for each of rows 2 and 3. Only that fixture was regenerated (`git diff --numstat 9da7cb463 HEAD -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden`: audit.replay-diverged.json 82 added, 44 removed; no other fixture). With line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails that test and passes without the change. `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 still diverge at call 0 by a hand-written row; they are left as they were.","kind":"comment","references":"983fa5d948f7940d0258cb97a9b56830e35939bea98856942ec2a33aac938bce","span":{"start":{"line":503},"end":{"line":511},"content_hash":"331d0ccb3052c72097c372911f08deafdafca36960c23c77c327fc73f25a612a"},"summary":"`audit.replay-diverged.json` is now of a replay that diverges at its second call and pins what the model is told: `completed: [\"files.list\"]`, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows in one step","supersedes":"0aa3b5c4c4fbfe669948a80da5639b93c9f356436b8cba99bcfc7e6366a0da5d","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.739546Z","id":"337f324df19cad4267fdc33ec363b56bbc44323b6521fae4408aae69f1cdad08","body":{"detail":"(a) Record 0d124949, cited in (2), was re-recorded at its lines and is now 964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb (same thread, still `status:needs-decision`). (b) Line references in this record are of the seam before commit 30c3673b2; the items are found by name: `no_place` and `StepCx::place` for (4), \"What the rows above do not show\" for (7), `Binding::from_stored` for (8). (c) For (3): `audit.replay-diverged.json` now pins a replay that diverges at its second call, with what the model is told (`replay.completed`), a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows; the divergence check written behind the seam must leave that fixture as it is. A refusal for `replay_diverged` or `no_place` is told in the step's `calls`, the events and a `bot.call` record and counted on the requests line, and is not journaled (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs row 3). (d) A replayed call is logged with `ms` 0 (477-481 here): no test holds it, since the goldens replace every `ms` by a name (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 123); pin it, or keep it by reading. (e) `merge_fields` takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for a step's payload and another for what its model is told: one of each for each step, made before the first session's parts are merged. (f) The real provider's conformance fixture now has to say of `disturb` and `lifecycle`, as of `staged` and `fail_once`, that it has one or why not (`Maybe`), and the caller names every case not played.","kind":"comment","references":"67e0b648578c2cfbe985fa7fbe7dd150274f701ba07a58f1a81bb02d8ef6005f","span":{"start":{"line":340},"end":{"line":449},"content_hash":"775ebefe9eba51456748adebbbbb5159bb8871891790ef2bd4c8d76927ce1e51"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, `merge_fields`' new argument, and two more things to carry","supersedes":"668d5cef767d9be5655b5bce816bca5005472c5df975eef99d9060ae4c9266d5","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:03.887869Z","id":"7e8f6d59a8ca3da9d3e644b7ad4085250bd52816303a853036344363e505589a","body":{"detail":"`bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (191 tests) with the change fails `golden::resumed::the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`. The fixture's diff from 9da7cb463: the first attempt's rows are `files.list`, `files.read`, `threads.reply`, `artifact.get` at places 0 to 3 (the reply's key moves from place 1 to 2); the committing attempt's `files.list` is `ok` and `replayed`; the divergence is at call 1 (`threads.reply` where the journal has `files.read`); the unreported rows are places 2 and 3; and `result` is new. No other fixture changed. A divergence after a call answered again is what a run can produce; the two older tests that write a row at place 0 by hand (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs 258, crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200) still show one it cannot.","kind":"comment","references":"983fa5d948f7940d0258cb97a9b56830e35939bea98856942ec2a33aac938bce","span":{"start":{"line":503},"end":{"line":511},"content_hash":"331d0ccb3052c72097c372911f08deafdafca36960c23c77c327fc73f25a612a"},"summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` now fails the fixture's test, and every change to `audit.replay-diverged.json` follows from the first attempt's new order of calls","supersedes":"4a9331a7178b02dfc79fdbb75a8442cbd63b8a48db14ce7c06443107ce40ce7d","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:04.035113Z","id":"d7a5a34052453fc3313ed3690dfaabade13278ea28e89659e5d8f780c5e24ac6","body":{"detail":"(1) Row 3 (surface.rs 52) and `no_place` (978-992): both of the core's unjournaled refusals, `replay_diverged` and `no_place`, are told as any call's answer is (the step's `calls`, a `call` event, a `bot.call` record, no target) and counted on the requests line, as crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs `replay_diverged`. The proof's `Stepper` logs a `no_place` refusal in its `calls` to agree (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, `Stepper::call`); nothing else in this crate numbers a script's calls through the seam yet. (2) `no_place`'s documentation says it is a backstop under the sandbox's code, an error a script can catch where the sandbox's limit ends the script, reached only while `script.max_calls` may exceed `NOTICE_SEQ`; the code is kept. (3) 136-141: a row of either form answers the same through `from_entry`. (4) `Answer`'s table has one row for each constructor and a paragraph after it (810-815): a replayed answer is journaled by no one, and its constructor matters for the charge alone. (5) 257-262, `merge_fields`'s errors and `ending`'s: in the `failed` step a clash is logged, none of that binding's fields are merged, the other bindings' are kept, and the step commits. (6) `CallNote::extra` (763-768): the field named `web` is what the core puts in `Event::Call`'s `web`. (7) 350-352: `declared` \"is not part of the crate's public interface\", true whether it is private or `pub(crate)`. Also from the same reading: `ended` asks a provider to remember a run only until it has been told `ended` once (1644-1649), with no read of the run's row.","kind":"comment","references":"027c6110ff49c2f22afee1ac8c43f73c35ff09002cd86ae525202e2ee77ba74b","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"1773f07d84631f73db7f8bb4f1fbc13939bc7388129f3221041ff2bae54e354c"},"summary":"Seven of the eight are answered in the text; where another surface's standing operations come from and who resolves the request stay in their own records","supersedes":"0495d515e2ea154d15cd4b9c2af410abf3c6df6334131a5c627e01c82bd4f114","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:56:04.181320Z","id":"30dc704478ff63b9f17bf30dac1466c069d66f66e505683930dc8388d7ee4361","body":{"detail":"(1) Where another surface's standing operations come from and who resolves the request at the first turn are still open in their own records. (2) After a merge with branch `claxbot-surfaces` (tip 1b2d5f9e7 merges with no conflict and `//crates/clax-bot:all`, `//crates/clax-surface:all`, `//crates/clax-commands:all`, `//bazel/wasm:check`, `//scripts:crate_layers` and `//scripts:no_unsafe` pass on the merged tree), `declared` is at crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 221 and `pub(crate)`: the records that cite api.rs by line (216-218, 253) point elsewhere, and the sentence at 349-353 here stays true.","kind":"comment","references":"027c6110ff49c2f22afee1ac8c43f73c35ff09002cd86ae525202e2ee77ba74b","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"1773f07d84631f73db7f8bb4f1fbc13939bc7388129f3221041ff2bae54e354c"},"summary":"Read again at 1473f04ce: the seven are answered in the text; two things are left for the change that moves script.rs to settle","supersedes":"2c26d4e3221101cff03cc6664d8085b00be98dd2bafb0199b44b014de2fa53ad","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.895293Z","id":"7cec99abcf40ea3620cad6883a0d93e48b614cfce42b10ecf850ec55457d4792","body":{"detail":"In `turn`, when the last committed turn's `next` has an `overflow`, the policy's answer must be `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` equals `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (run.rs:2010-2043); the core's projection, or other messages, end the run by `Tick::overflow_ending` (run.rs:1726): `give_up`/`context_window` after the stop reason, `stopped`/`model_error` with the stored provider error after a refusal. So each request after an overflow is shorter than the one that overflowed whatever the policy answers at either ask, and `max_overflow_retries = None` ends. `the_turn_after_an_overflow_is_held_to_the_shorter_history` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) runs the policy of this record (less on the overflow, the core's projection after) with `max_overflow_retries = None` against a provider that refuses every request: steps turn, script, turn, stopped; 2 requests; 4 scripted refusals unused. It runs the same on the stop-reason path, and a policy that answers other messages after the overflow. Mutations, each failing that test: the hash comparison replaced by `is_null()`; the ending for `Shown::Core` after an overflow removed. The module documentation and `max_overflow_retries`' say what the code holds (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs, 'A full context window'). Proposed close: fixed in cad03c26b, with the test for other messages in 72a10983b.","kind":"comment","references":"42ad3153c5bb468159940c36b36a6570838fcf8eae4db7597af8061ff788662a","span":{"start":{"line":2007},"end":{"line":2043},"content_hash":"d71e81ca81a489902e210bc5af76ee2843deaf8b575c5f2f868bd7926931c317"},"summary":"The turn after an overflow is now held to the shorter history: it is sent messages whose SHA-256 is the overflow's `shown_sha256`, or the run ends","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.895971Z","id":"a9510f0066d886ae21dfd64299eb7ebc2d1d0e1763adb5bd6017475f43611dd7","body":{"detail":"`tick` keeps the error and builds the `Tick` on the instance's settings and `RunLimits::of` them (run.rs:1067-1081). `Tick::step` then takes a `finish` or `give_up` the model already asked for, reads the cancel and the refusals (`must_stop`, which applies no cap while the policy's limits are unknown), and only then answers the error: `Tick::policy_failed` (run.rs:1687) begins the step, counts one failure on it with `Store::note_commit_failure` (the counter a failed commit uses, bounded by `RunSettings::max_commit_failures`, 3 by default), and waits `Deps.retry_later`; at the bound the run ends `failed` with reason `policy_error`, the policy's error in `payload.error`, and a notice on its threads. An `Err` from `history` before a model call takes the same path. An `Err` from `history` when asked on an overflow is 'no shorter history': the overflowing turn is committed with its usage and the run ends as it does under the defaults, so nothing paid is discarded and the model is not called again. Tests in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: `a_policy_that_keeps_failing_ends_the_run_failed_at_the_bound` (settings, limits and history each: wait, wait, failed; no request; the instance's cap of 0 steps stops nothing), `a_run_whose_policy_fails_is_still_cancelled`, `a_policy_that_recovers_lets_the_run_go_on`, `a_finish_the_model_asked_for_is_taken_though_the_policy_fails`, `a_policy_that_fails_on_an_overflow_ends_the_run_with_the_answer_kept` (one request, 4 charged token rows, `context_window`; `model_error` on the refused path). Mutations that fail them: the error dropped in `tick`; the bound off by two; the cap check left on while the policy fails. Proposed close: fixed in cad03c26b.","kind":"comment","references":"cac7184b575b4d4e37564f63a28423abaa60604b5ad760b7db6f98ce7d1c5ffb","span":{"start":{"line":1067},"end":{"line":1081},"content_hash":"4c690f85ffcedfb7a41ea8a477fd9a0122e80e955f9d4d2164e2805ce3a33a1f"},"summary":"A policy's error is no longer the tick's: the cancel is read first, the failure is counted against the step as a failed commit is, and the run ends `failed` (`policy_error`) at the bound; an error on an overflow keeps the paid answer","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.896533Z","id":"3cfa9cff988a659e507a4d3038945acdb4abd98698395074b88869ed0ca70658","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits::unbounded(&settings)` (policy.rs:230-241) answers `None` for every optional bound with the settings' sizes, and `of` is `unbounded` with the three caps and the overflow bound set, so a bound added to the struct is unset in both until `of` names it; the struct's documentation says how a policy builds one and that a later bound arrives unset in `unbounded`. `HistoryAsk::new` is `pub`. `observe_every` is a `NonZeroUsize`, so 0 is not a value. `model_call_timeout` is `None` in `of`. When a policy sets it and it ends a call, the call is retried as `Deps.retry` allows and then the run stops with reason `model_timeout` and a notice ('the model took longer to answer than this run allows'); a timeout of zero stops the run at its first turn with no call made (`a_model_call_that_outlasts_the_policys_timeout_stops_the_run`, crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: two calls of a provider that never answers, then `stopped`; zero: no request). Still open here: the trait is not for hosts until the tick holds a `RunInfo` (policy.rs module documentation says so).","kind":"comment","references":"e8b13c661b6a75263c1acddeecef931bdbb78309265a503b478cafda2d9b4162","span":{"start":{"line":156},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"430b729a1705bcbf3369b3d51c1febcab00be07f9b9be9e8eaab8efd8a13fab7"},"summary":"`RunLimits::unbounded` sets no bound and `of` is built on it; `HistoryAsk::new` is public; what a model call's timeout does when it fires is an ending, and documented","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.897104Z","id":"432ac6d7b108bfba7192d0116f56a18ce56745dd0bc4dbbd704c5caeb10141f3","body":{"detail":"`of` no longer sets `model_call_timeout` (policy.rs:215-226 sets the three caps and `max_overflow_retries` over `unbounded`): under the settings' caps the run's remaining minutes already bound a call, and a policy that drops the minutes cap gets no hidden second use of them. A timeout a policy does set is counted with the tick's retries and then stops the run (`model_timeout`), where it waited without end. `max_overflow_retries` stays `Some(4)` in `of` and `None` in `unbounded`; since the turn after an overflow is held to the shorter history, the rule on bytes ends every run without it, so the default is a bound on cost, not on termination. What is still asked of the owner: whether an instance should have a setting for one model call's longest time where no minutes cap is set (today: none, so the driver's round or the Worker's alarm ends it and it is made again), and whether four is the number.","kind":"comment","references":"21d75d6c0a67a9ece0f341153314b9255642f82bbbe3eea8a571e21eeec2cb97","span":{"start":{"line":215},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"550cebbd95a87439839eeb2c9110e20facccf5fa7e0900251fcd61fa053b45da"},"summary":"Done as recommended for the timeout: `RunLimits::of` leaves `model_call_timeout` unset, and a timeout that fires now ends the run. The bound of four overflows in a row is kept. Both remain the owner's to decide","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.897630Z","id":"c07b3e509a1b407b1bfc3c1593072ebac5678a947099cb69257c37cadda4383f","body":{"detail":"The run loop asks `self.deps.policy.is_overflow(&e)` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, the refusal arm of `turn`); the default calls `prompt_too_long`, so nothing changes under `DefaultPolicy`. `prompt_too_long`'s documentation says the phrases were not checked against recorded answers and names the misses: a provider's error `code` (the client keeps `type` and `message` only), Gemini's refusal, an error sent mid-stream (status 0), and the Messages API's `exceed context limit`. Unit tests hold each phrase alone, in any case, and the status range at 0, 399, 400, 499 and 500 (`each_phrase_alone_says_a_request_is_too_long`, `only_a_refusal_with_a_4xx_status_is_one_for_length`); deleting the `context window` needle or widening the range below 400 fails them. `a_policy_recognises_its_own_providers_refusal_for_length` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) runs a policy whose provider says it in other words. The typed error in clax-sample is still the fix for the default.","kind":"comment","references":"224c55652d2e5b065b16ccaea31a61fcd81cbc073588c76e60edd5e699d96858","span":{"start":{"line":478},"end":{"line":500},"content_hash":"675b254309be2d640527fff932bc37c45aed6e4ad1e7f3f4509780728f15d450"},"summary":"The words are the policy's now: `RunPolicy::is_overflow` decides, and its default is `prompt_too_long`, whose documentation lists what it is known to miss","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.898200Z","id":"a24183475d1ba37b6ad05b90eff0632b715ec8e2923a13d9f6ae22a1c4854fb4","body":{"detail":"`impl<S: SettingsSource + ?Sized> RunPolicy for S {}` is gone (cad03c26b); `Deps::new` sets `DefaultPolicy` as before, and neither the server nor the Worker is edited. What this alternative weighed is otherwise unchanged: the tick reads the instance's settings from `Deps.settings` and hands them to `RunPolicy::run_settings`, whose answer for `lease_secs` and the concurrency settings is not read (the module documentation, 'Bounds that are not a policy's', says so).","kind":"comment","references":"c77e0a0f9ae66bbfd18b8f89f7728498db43161d4a951127fed3d576127a004f","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":551},"content_hash":"224f6bdf8a6280fe79ed3af3dda906a0dc0113c19a2d91822b3d2a4df8505d0e"},"summary":"The blanket impl is removed: a settings type may now implement `RunPolicy` itself","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.898773Z","id":"faebce942cf812a574ef3cb51715584a0a03fb39056875d3e67ec7994054f985","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs:173 reads 'to `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes` in a loop that builds the whole value' (70e2f2b81). Proposed close: fixed. What remains for the change that moves the tool result behind the seam is to read the size from the tick's limits, as crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:684 does.","kind":"comment","references":"77c3d58e519a128a834099aae1c4cc53f9ea3dd3275d4301d323b3996edb5c71","span":{"start":{"line":173},"end":{"line":173},"content_hash":"8bf31c422ba0ce9e6a8b60d7a4e62f7559fbe7f3a402e5ecc5e6ae98d2773cb1"},"summary":"The sentence now names `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`","tags":["for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.899340Z","id":"dc2840a928c58499df8453fa12401ef7f5dc4a5a43d5dd1bb80a4b0d0929a5f4","body":{"detail":"`RunPolicy::history` answers messages only (`Shown::Messages`); `turn` takes the system text and whether the request is the first from `transcript(&self.steps)` before the policy is asked (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1973-1977), so a rule cannot make a turn store `system` and `user` again. What a policy answers goes through `policy::check_shown` (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:415): on a first turn the first message must be the user's and open with the request the turn added, and then `history::check_shortened` with the steps' system text. Decided per invariant: 1, 3, 5, 6 and 7 bind a policy as they bind `transcript`; 2 too (a policy may leave out or summarise any step, never drop or rewrite the request the run opens with); 4 is relaxed in one case, in `check_shortened` alone: after a turn whose `next` records an `overflow`, the last message may be the user's, with the cut-off answer left out. `check` is unchanged for the core's own rule. Messages that break one end the run `failed` with `policy_history_<n>` and are never sent (`messages_that_break_what_a_turn_relies_on_fail_the_run_unsent`, crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: the request rewritten on the first turn and after it, and the answers left out). A turn that is sent a policy's messages is priced with no cached prefix (`a_turn_on_a_shorter_history_is_priced_without_a_cached_prefix`).","kind":"comment","references":"7323532dcfd01b9b4cb40c4202acc3c514e0a28a70bd087650158b6fc2ec19d6","span":{"start":{"line":171},"end":{"line":245},"content_hash":"05d96467aafafc188db5484966b9845aced8c899d490c67c32ad3dbc71161d49"},"summary":"A policy's messages are now checked before they are sent, by `check_shortened`, which allows one thing `check` does not; and the system text and the first request cannot come from a policy","tags":["for-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.899864Z","id":"9e12f90430e9712dd3be9f3f0dc11e0d79306cb92e151250bc87d3c0ef805108","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_each_bound_and_size_from_the_policys_limits` no longer bans strings or constants. `a_run_with_no_bound_goes_far_past_every_default` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:593) gives a run 5,000 committed turns and 100,000 credits spent by writing them to the test's database (`seed_a_long_run`, :554), advances the clock a year, and under `RunLimits::unbounded` with the sizes raised to 1 MiB sees it take a turn, run a script whose result is over 300 KiB, and finish. A literal `committed >= 1000` added to `must_stop` fails it. Driving the steps was measured first: 1,100 real ticks took 17 s, so they are written, which couples the test to the columns of `bot_steps` and `bot_runs.credits_milli` (as crates/clax-bot/tests/driver.rs is coupled to `artifacts`). What it does not see: a bound above those figures, one on something it does not grow (the bytes of a history, a script's calls), or one in a path it does not take (a stop, a give-up).","kind":"comment","references":"67f38d18aab9d98bda239ee1547666f9951b8a0341b36851939dd8631f6b4dec","span":{"start":{"line":818},"end":{"line":847},"content_hash":"568c959526b330c4080e40006cbea219cc1df10082d35be68cbe6a9134fc774c"},"summary":"Done as recommended: the test keeps only 'reads `limits.*`', and a run holds that the run loop has no bound of its own, by figures written to the database","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.900888Z","id":"73c829456961c0aeb67fd85d02658caa66c8a5f6259bc7b495ef3d2a44f42175","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs, 'Bounds that are not a policy's', says that a driver gives a tick a budget (an hour a round on the daemon, the alarm's on the Worker) which ends a model call that outlasts it, after which the call is made again; and `RunLimits::model_call_timeout`'s documentation says `None` leaves the tick's budget and the client's deadlines. The hour stays a constant of the driver for the reason record 30795868 gives for the driver's waits: it is the driver's, set by what constructs one, and belongs with the change that puts driver housekeeping behind the provider.","kind":"comment","references":"971dba6b4669f95d3bf8abb8dbc247b0208d8f3275d59a75271345d77b7027db","span":{"start":{"line":460},"end":{"line":464},"content_hash":"5cc282f5bfb9a578b981bac0aa1300504fafbb11d61d7297203cc29b9b7d4fdc"},"summary":"Left as it is, and now said where a policy's author reads it: a tick's budget is the driver's, and ends a call whatever the policy answers","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.901487Z","id":"ca5316de317db7dca07b1a2860b0bcf8cc951f2235d55d1274a7d661c2a2238e","body":{"detail":"`tick` reads all of a run's steps (`Store::run_with_steps`, run.rs:1064) and folds `Control` and `clax::Tally` over them on every tick; `transcript` is folded by a tick that takes a turn, before the policy is asked (run.rs:1975). A policy shortens what is sent, not what is loaded: measured in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs, a run of 5,000 written steps ticks, and 1,100 driven ones took 17 s in all. So a run of any length is bounded by the memory and time of what ticks it (the Worker's isolate first). crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs, 'Bounds that are not a policy's', says so. Lifting it means the tick reading a suffix of the steps and a stored fold of the rest, which is the tick's to do when it holds a `RunInfo` and its providers.","kind":"comment","references":"51f5801f749bcbf7dba342c14a8ab76159d1ea6bd28107a48f8ea9035cc64156","span":{"start":{"line":1064},"end":{"line":1066},"content_hash":"86670f07eb2b225e04ae2e8869d2bb5892139643f951eca6c4956f9345a06496"},"summary":"This is the one bound on a run's length that no policy can lift: every tick loads every step, whatever is shown of them","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.902159Z","id":"d09ba996378876159b96864984d0e2e5a75f265c494c4adbaac2205927e5dc70","body":{"detail":"Done: `overflowed` builds the core's projection from `history::transcript(&self.steps)` (item 1's by-hand rebuild is one call and two pushes); a policy's messages are checked (`policy::check_shown`, item 4), with the cut-off answer allowed to be left out of the end (item 1's partial assistant message); a turn on a policy's messages is priced with no cached prefix (item 5); the turn after an overflow is held to the overflow's answer by its hash. Still open: a policy is given messages and not steps (2); it has nowhere to keep a summary, and `payload.shown` names what was sent without holding it (3); `level` only grows (6); an overflowing turn's `next` carries no nudge, so a nudged model is nudged again (7 of reply 70a8db39), now stated in crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs; and every tick loads every step (8, record 06b231ef's thread). New with the check: invariant 2 binds a policy, so a first request that alone overflows the window cannot be shortened and ends the run (`model_error`).","kind":"comment","references":"1be9afa17ed0bb78509a9115a744bf9b2e3a3a229357880a8140146368fa4b76","span":{"start":{"line":1916},"end":{"line":1962},"content_hash":"9a42d31543d19cfad3106eb94639918e0e14159279b472e2a2841a4190eba3ee"},"summary":"With the fold split: what of this list is done, and what is still a compactor's to meet","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.902223Z","id":"9db3e329a3110fb7627e01e8cddb090511f24fa2b01adcc410c8e3e451a76c99","body":{"detail":"`check_shown` holds a policy's messages to invariant 2: the first message is the user's and opens with step 1's stored `user` blocks (on a first turn, the request the turn added). A compactor may leave out or summarise every step after it. Not taken: allowing a summary to stand in for the request too. The request is the person's words, shown whole (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, 'One door to the model': an `Instruction` keeps its standing only when shown whole), and with the system text it is the cached prefix. The cost: a request that is itself longer than the model's window, or that leaves no room once the prompt is counted, cannot be shortened by any policy, and the run ends `model_error` at its first turn (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs has no test of that case).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":406},"end":{"line":440},"content_hash":"8565e024b6fc65b60dc5c8da1f41bdfdd4c605af172d6a2c526ec46f34360b77"},"summary":"Letting a policy drop or rewrite the request a run opens with (invariant 2 of `history::transcript` not held for a policy's messages)","tags":["revisit:a kind whose requests can be longer than a model's window (an attached document, a long brief)","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.902309Z","id":"30ddc762eee46b48645c8669a48b5cae84f10f01a41cd3aadb20cb6b42e8878b","body":{"detail":"`Tick::policy_failed` counts with `Store::note_commit_failure`, the counter `Tick::commit` uses, so two failed commits and one policy failure on one step end the run; the reason is then `policy_error` if the policy is failing on that tick and `internal_error` if not (`Tick::step`, run.rs:1622-1633). It calls `begin_on` before it counts, so each failure bumps the open step's `attempt` and emits a `step_started` event with nothing after it, and a step is opened (a `turn` or `script` row) for a run that may then end `failed` in its place. The wait between failures is `Deps.retry_later` (30 s), with no backoff: three failures end a run in a minute, which is short for an environment whose policy reads a store that is briefly away. The policy's error is stored as `payload.error` and shown wherever a step's payload is; a policy that puts a secret in its error leaks it there. A counter and a bound of the policy's own need a column, which this change could not add (the store was not to be touched).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1682},"end":{"line":1702},"content_hash":"6c6514bb0fc2d72dd59b5a9220ae8313dbc71aeb0b2b9250d590663da07248fb"},"summary":"A failing policy shares the step's counter of failed commits, begins the step each time it fails, and its error text is kept in the step","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T14:58:17.902382Z","id":"14ceea4a1632bfc8e05dc654933448ea88cc2dde9d9127e22affead1f9ae22cc","body":{"detail":"`stop_lead` has a notice for each: 'this Clax could not work out what this run may do', 'this Clax could not put together what to send the model', 'the model took longer to answer than this run allows'. The first two end the run `failed`, the third `stopped`. None is reachable under `DefaultPolicy` (no fixture under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden changes), and none is pinned by a fixture; the run view and the HTTP run JSON show `reason` as they show any. When the endings move behind a provider's `ending`, these reasons go with `End::Failed` and `End::Stopped` and the words become the provider's; whoever first ships such a policy pins them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1158},"end":{"line":1175},"content_hash":"f6b17de838d1f7b45a1754a729c6f1ac5fcddbb4315da020dfa2081fc8ffd459"},"summary":"Three endings a person can see only under a policy that is not the default, with words nobody chose: `policy_error`, `policy_history_<n>` and `model_timeout`","tags":["for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:19:27.109697Z","id":"22e3eb02af9c4f1e44946339b5a8a5cba3a34d503fc04aff107aaab374044ef9","body":{"detail":"The turn a refused request commits has `assistant: []` and `next: {kind: turn, user: [], overflow}`. `check_with` takes it for a paused turn (`answered == Some(false)`), reads `said` (the stored `assistant` blocks) as empty, and its condition `!said.is_empty() && ...` then never fails, whatever the last message is. Invariant 5 lets a `tool_use` in the last message stand ('one the next step answers'). So after such a turn a policy's messages may end with an assistant message, where the core's own projection ends with the user's. Measured (turn with a script, the script, a request refused as too long, and a policy that answers the overflow by leaving out every message that holds a tool result, so `[user: request][assistant: tool_use]`): the answer is shorter, the refused turn commits, the next tick's `check_shown` passes, and the request is sent (3 requests reached the provider where 2 were expected); the run ended `model_error` on the provider's answer. On the stop-reason path the same policy is caught (`policy_history_3`, 2 requests). Invariant 4's words in `transcript`'s documentation (history.rs, 'after a paused turn ... it is the assistant's and ends with that turn's stored `assistant` blocks') say the same wrong thing for a turn that stored no blocks: the projection's last message is then the user's. A real provider refuses such a request, so the cost is one request and a `model_error` ending in place of `failed` (`policy_history_4`); nothing is charged for it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":357},"end":{"line":371},"content_hash":"f870629fc8adff2f5050fc4158b76f207a433058e6b0a6397e3b5052921852cf"},"suggested_fix":"In `check_with`, when the last committed turn asked for a turn with no `user` blocks and stored no `assistant` blocks, require the last message to be the user's, as after an answered turn; and say so in invariant 4.","summary":"After a refused request that goes on, invariant 4 holds nothing of the last message: a policy's history that ends with the assistant's `tool_use` and no result passes `check_shortened` and is sent","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:19:27.109824Z","id":"4449ebcee6f1a83c98d10c21be871fd67d87867af6f55bdc0d4884d91164f9a0","body":{"detail":"`Tick::step` takes a `finish` or `give_up` the last turn asked for before it reads the cancel (`must_stop`), and `Store::begin_step` refuses every kind but `stopped` and `failed` for a run whose status is `stopping` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1225-1230). Measured with the default policy on both commits: a turn that answers `finish`, then `Store::cancel_run`, then a tick by the same driver: the tick answers `Err(a stopping run's next step is its stopped step)`, the run stays `stopping` with one step, and so does a run whose second text-only turn asked for `give_up` (`no_tool_call`). `Driver::settle` tries such a run again every `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (driver.rs), for ever. The window is the time between a turn's commit and the next tick. crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs ('A policy that fails') says a run can always be stopped; this is the case where it cannot, and it does not depend on the policy.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1634},"end":{"line":1648},"content_hash":"cab4806eeed3a4443b636129648dbb9093481d4b2df93207619e54b16ebb8ebf"},"suggested_fix":"In `Tick::step`, when the run is `stopping` (or the tick's cancel is set), stop it (`Stop::cancelled()`) before a `finish` or `give_up` is taken; or let `begin_step` take a `finish` or `give_up` for a stopping run, if a run that had already asked to finish should finish.","summary":"A run cancelled after its model asked for `finish` or `give_up` cannot take any step: the tick fails with `a stopping run's next step is its stopped step`, on this commit and on d1bc352e3 alike","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:19:27.110517Z","id":"6c90c391fa484cd79c0e9865d416b68c9bd42489d426eb4ea81d23b0fa870488","body":{"detail":"(1) A policy whose `limits` fails twice and then answers, with `Store::note_commit_failure` called once on the open step between: the third tick ends the run `failed`, reason `internal_error`, notice 'I could not save my work', though two of the three counts were the policy's and no commit of the run's own work was tried twice. The count stays on the step when the policy recovers (a script's step committed with `failures = 2`, `attempt = 3`, one `script.compute` row), and the next step starts at 0, as for commits. (2) With `max_steps = 0`, `max_run_credits = 0`, 31 minutes on the clock and a policy that always fails: three ticks (`Waiting(Retry(30s))` twice, then `failed`/`policy_error`), no request to a provider and no step taken; so a run passes its minutes cap by at most the waits between failures, spends nothing, and ends `policy_error` where the instance's cap would have said `run_cap`. (3) A policy error of 100,000 bytes is stored whole in the `failed` step's `payload.error` (100,019 bytes). `step_view` (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs:1366) does not copy a `failed` or `stopped` step's `error` into the run JSON, and the run's `bot.run_end` record does not hold it (165 bytes), so today it is read only from the store.","kind":"comment","references":"30ddc762eee46b48645c8669a48b5cae84f10f01a41cd3aadb20cb6b42e8878b","suggested_fix":"Cut the stored error to a few hundred bytes. When a counter of the policy's own is added, name the ending by which kind of failure reached the bound.","summary":"Measured: two failures of the policy and one failed commit of the same step end the run `internal_error`; while the policy fails no turn or script is taken, so no cap is exceeded by work; the error text is stored whole and is not in the run view or the audit","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:19:27.110916Z","id":"2611aa214404b98fb1a6dc4198e06cbbd0f7be15513a0f5149d3726f914138c6","body":{"detail":"With the default policy, or one that answers `Shown::Core`, a refused first request ends the run `stopped`/`model_error` in one step, as before. With a policy that answers the overflow by cutting the request's text in half, the refused turn commits (a step, and step 1's fee), and the next tick ends the run `failed`/`policy_history_2`: not `model_error`, and one step later, because an overflow's answer is checked by the turn that sends it. Three compactors were run through an overflow on the stop-reason and the refused path, each on a run of two scripts, the overflow, a third script and a finish: the oldest tool results' content replaced; the request, a text block summarising what was left out, and the last call and its result; the request and the last call and its result with a cut-off answer kept. All six runs finished, the turn after the overflow was sent what the overflow's ask answered, and later turns at the same level passed too. A compactor needs only the messages to stay within the checks: keep message 0 whole, cut at an assistant message, and keep each assistant message with the user's after it. The request itself is what no policy may shorten; for `clax` it is the thread's or brief's context, and shortening it is a matter for what builds the request (or the design's `to_model`), not for a history. Relaxing invariant 2 later takes nothing from a policy written against it.","kind":"comment","references":"9db3e329a3110fb7627e01e8cddb090511f24fa2b01adcc410c8e3e451a76c99","summary":"What a first request that alone is too long ends as, measured; and three compactors a host would write pass the checks on both paths, so the rule on the request can wait","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:19:27.111348Z","id":"baba8dd478a8e631c846d32c9d3231d20d8676c85fa58cd1eb5203861150c0e1","body":{"detail":"Each scenario's ticks, run row, steps with payloads, replies, `bot.*` audit records, usage rows and request bodies were printed on this commit and on d1bc352e3 and compared as text: identical in all 17, but for a script's measured times (`cpu_us`, `busy_us`, `wall_us`, and the `script.compute` quantity they give). Which cause wins is unchanged: a cancel over every cap and over exhausted credits; exhausted credits and a withheld flag over the step cap; steps over credits over minutes; a `finish` already asked for over the flag and every cap; a step whose commits failed three times over a cancel (`internal_error`). Also the same: a full window (`context_window`), a refusal for length (`model_error`), a cut stream with little time left (`Waiting(Retry)`), and a run with nothing in its way. The order of `Tick::step` is unchanged for a policy that answers (`policy_error` is `None`): the commit-failure bound, a `finish` or `give_up` asked for, `must_stop` (cancel, refusal, steps, credits, minutes), then the step.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":1044},"end":{"line":1105},"content_hash":"b32eef16d451e8dc9798067f81cc16fd459a9f77332025c232d738ecdbebc13d"},"summary":"Under `DefaultPolicy` a tick leaves what it left at d1bc352e3: 17 scenarios, 12 of them with two or more causes of a stop at once, run on both commits and compared whole","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:23.874318Z","id":"196cea2eaac259563b761f0aaaeceba7c3869be959446749a748ccd0334a02d9","body":{"detail":"Tick::affordable prices the request as its JSON bytes / 4 tokens, the part the last turn cached at the cached-input rate and the rest at the input rate, then caps max_tokens to the output the credits left pay for. A request whose tokens run well above bytes/4 (non-Latin text, dense JSON) can still overshoot the person's credits by its input; counting tokens (the Messages API count_tokens endpoint) would bound it.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (96939966), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Since a9ecb07d1 the uncached rest is priced at the cache-write rate, not the input rate.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1812},"end":{"line":1912},"content_hash":"568435353618718cfb89456697b34d2e6f9bb433e66e9031f7e5eb0c519e7f84"},"summary":"A turn's affordable max_tokens rests on a byte-count estimate of its input","supersedes":"88a6c90cee32cba9e3963d66e7bd352a0b372be80da0efff3b35c1936994265e","tags":["credits","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:24.111077Z","id":"8d486d825822a5e3365716c712f0dffec71a41ddfbbc2b330fafe903768ef90a","body":{"detail":"(1) Test the run loop's half of `SurfaceProvider::ending` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 1474-1511): an `Err` counted against the step as a failed commit, the bound (`max_commit_failures`), `End::Failed`'s `Err` logged and the step committed, and every binding's `ending` still called when one fails. The provider cases hold only the provider's half. (2) Make the change to the sources' read on purpose: `Store::web_pages` failing in `finish` (1684 here) fails the tick uncounted today, so the step is begun again without end; behind `ending` under that contract it is tried `max_commit_failures` times and the run ends `failed` (surface.rs, the endings table's row for a notice that cannot be posted). Keep the read out of `ending`, or accept the bound. (3) `begin`'s failure is retried uncounted and `ending`'s is counted (surface.rs 1460-1471, 1490-1505): decide whether the two should agree. (4) Build what delivers `ended` again; the contract allows a repeat with the same kind of end and best-effort details (1513-1540), and keeping the `End` in the terminal step's payload would let a redelivery carry all of it (record b25bed99). (5) Count a `FieldClash` against the step, and reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) as surface names in `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42), so a clash cannot be reached by a name (record f2d3e3a6). (6) The adapter's terminal cases prove nothing about notices: its `ending` and `ended` do nothing (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 186-204), so the provider cases pass on it with no notice posted. The real provider's fixture needs a `noticed` probe that reads the notices and a `lifecycle`. (7) A cancelled script's `stopped` step is charged the per-step lines with no call counted (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673-683; 1567-1573 here): keep or change it knowingly.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1530},"end":{"line":1810},"content_hash":"ecd11657fe1f09c6d8ca151495cda2a6c5712e4f1e1685a5fbbbf00aa0052e7e"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending` and `ended`: what the seam states and nothing holds yet","supersedes":"53c256e6ad20c899f2350f3253e7c9dd569ed2478c4408fc482fcc9a3dfbce64","tags":["seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:24.327415Z","id":"7805554a47cd9f3cd485765ca20affaf97e15bcc536213e9489766c8e83ed097","body":{"detail":"The overflow plumbing is whole for a policy that is a pure function of the core's messages and a level (held by crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs with a fake that drops the oldest tool results). What it does not have:\n\n1. The overflowing answer's blocks stay in the core's projection: `History::of` pushes every turn's assistant blocks (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:626-627), as it does for `pause_turn`, so after a `stop_reason` overflow the next request ends with that partial assistant message unless the policy drops it. `overflowed` rebuilds that projection by hand (`hist.messages`, then the turn's request, then the answer's blocks) because the fold has no way to say what the next tick's projection will be. The projection function should take the committed steps plus one uncommitted turn, so this is computed once.\n2. A policy is given messages, not steps: it cannot tell which step a message came from, so it cannot keep a tool call and its result together by step or summarise by step. The projection should say, for each message or block, the step it came from.\n3. A policy has nowhere to keep a summary: the design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:880-888) lets it send one it keeps, recorded by hash. Here a turn records `payload.shown` (level, bytes, SHA-256) and the overflowing turn `next.overflow.shown_sha256`, which name what was sent but do not hold it, so a run's exact context is rebuilt only by asking the same policy again.\n4. Nothing checks what a policy answers: messages with a tool result and no call, or two of one role, reach the provider, and its refusal stops the run (`model_error`).\n5. Each shorter history changes the cached prefix; the turn after an overflow pays the cache-write rate for all of it, and `affordable` (run.rs:1817) prices it from the last turn's usage, which after a refused request is zero.\n6. `HistoryAsk.level` only grows. A long session that is compacted many times needs the policy to decide what a level means; nothing lowers it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1922},"end":{"line":1968},"content_hash":"9a42d31543d19cfad3106eb94639918e0e14159279b472e2a2841a4190eba3ee"},"summary":"What a real compactor still needs from the run loop, and what the message projection must provide for it","supersedes":"1be9afa17ed0bb78509a9115a744bf9b2e3a3a229357880a8140146368fa4b76","tags":["for-t3","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:24.538671Z","id":"2636f9a88dd39350414c197fcd487b0ae771849b0ec39f92d4fef2715eca0b61","body":{"detail":"Only under a policy that shows less (never under the default, whose fixtures in crates/clax-bot/tests/golden are unchanged): a `turn` step with `assistant: []`, `stop_reason: \"prompt_too_long\"`, zero usage and `next.overflow`; a turn after it with `payload.shown`; and a `bot.turn` audit record with that stop reason and zero tokens. It takes a step number, so it counts toward `max_steps`, and step 1's harness fee is charged on it as on any first step. `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:297-316) shows it as a turn with no text and no tool; the run view and the HTTP run JSON were not looked at. Whoever first ships a policy that shows less checks those readers and pins the shapes as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2341},"end":{"line":2347},"content_hash":"cd4b34db76207885c770671ec43566b4c81e00010452727d08cb7b35988523b8"},"summary":"A refused request that goes on is committed as a turn with no answer, a shape no reader of steps has seen","supersedes":"413675d56b0b340c6f3b2b1aeb3b5316b7199dfeb6901a9cdfe32a5249c7257b","tags":["for-t8","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:24.747191Z","id":"08fb97c68d2eb92af7652dd94028acb6312d913f20ed90d68fe937834316e7b0","body":{"detail":"`overflowed` (run.rs:1911-1948) lets the run go on when the policy's answer to `HistoryFor::Overflow` has fewer bytes than the messages that overflowed, and records `next.overflow.shown_sha256`. The next tick's `turn` asks again with `HistoryFor::NextTurn` at the same level (run.rs:1993-2001) and sends that answer, whatever it is; it is not compared with the recorded hash or size. A policy that answers an overflow with less and the next turn with `Shown::Core` passes the rule each time and is never sent shorter. Measured with such a policy, `max_overflow_retries = None`, `max_steps = None` and a provider that refuses every request as too long: 30 ticks, 30 committed turns, 30 requests with identical messages, the run still `running`, 1 credit charged (step 1's fee). With the default `max_overflow_retries` of 4 the same policy ends after 4 such turns. On the stop-reason path each such call is answered and charged, so only the person's credits end it. The module documentation (policy.rs:32-37, 'cannot make the run loop ask twice') and `RunLimits::max_overflow_retries` ('leaves only the rule that each answer is shorter than the last', policy.rs:118-122) state more than the code holds.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2013},"end":{"line":2049},"content_hash":"d71e81ca81a489902e210bc5af76ee2843deaf8b575c5f2f868bd7926931c317"},"suggested_fix":"In `turn`, when the last committed step's `next.overflow` is set, take the policy's answer only if it is `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` is `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (or at least whose bytes are no more than `shown_bytes`); otherwise end the run as `overflowed` returning `None` does. Then a run's overflows in a row are bounded by its bytes even with no `max_overflow_retries`. Or say in the documentation that the bound is `max_overflow_retries` alone and that `None` trusts the policy.","summary":"The turn after an overflow sends whatever the policy answers then: nothing holds it to the shorter history the overflow's ask answered, so the rule that each history is shorter is kept per ask and not per run","supersedes":"42ad3153c5bb468159940c36b36a6570838fcf8eae4db7597af8061ff788662a","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:24.962337Z","id":"85eb393f3adcadfd78e8d1c811e5f6cb61f321e28c01ca8919bc05e1ffb47bdc","body":{"detail":"Today: `Control::of` and `Tally::of` run when the tick is built (1051-1052), `transcript` in `turn` (1746). The tally is read by a script tick (script.rs 217, 554) and a terminal tick (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) and not by a turn, so a turn tick folds it for nothing: one pass that clones a few strings. A `OnceCell<Tally>` on `Tick` would save that pass and cost a `Send`/`Sync` question on wasm32 and native alike (the tick is borrowed across awaits), for a field that leaves the tick when the provider folds its own. Not worth it before then.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1979},"end":{"line":1983},"content_hash":"ee259796ac8410d3a2e93d3193d750c7263adf945a3f03269d6ff9ba4be0876c"},"summary":"Folding the transcript and the tally lazily, once, behind a cell on the tick, instead of the tally on every tick and the transcript in the turn","supersedes":"39db5cdae10f00447d066be5c7fdeff9c797f7769ae078aaf011722127e186d2","tags":["t3","revisit:a profile of a tick shows the tally's pass, or `Tick::clax` outlives the change that moves the endings behind the provider","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:25.163985Z","id":"03016140072f004d92493d8b2931e6ac627c3e8915fba0dfcab0a6c067607a48","body":{"detail":"`Tick::policy_failed` counts with `Store::note_commit_failure`, the counter `Tick::commit` uses, so two failed commits and one policy failure on one step end the run; the reason is then `policy_error` if the policy is failing on that tick and `internal_error` if not (`Tick::step`, run.rs:1622-1633). It calls `begin_on` before it counts, so each failure bumps the open step's `attempt` and emits a `step_started` event with nothing after it, and a step is opened (a `turn` or `script` row) for a run that may then end `failed` in its place. The wait between failures is `Deps.retry_later` (30 s), with no backoff: three failures end a run in a minute, which is short for an environment whose policy reads a store that is briefly away. The policy's error is stored as `payload.error` and shown wherever a step's payload is; a policy that puts a secret in its error leaks it there. A counter and a bound of the policy's own need a column, which this change could not add (the store was not to be touched).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1688},"end":{"line":1708},"content_hash":"6c6514bb0fc2d72dd59b5a9220ae8313dbc71aeb0b2b9250d590663da07248fb"},"summary":"A failing policy shares the step's counter of failed commits, begins the step each time it fails, and its error text is kept in the step","supersedes":"30ddc762eee46b48645c8669a48b5cae84f10f01a41cd3aadb20cb6b42e8878b","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:25.390013Z","id":"bcc408ac2c9d07229ab3e5abd9ac0befff1a7649c84b50d76744b44ab9bf74cc","body":{"detail":"`Tick::step` takes a `finish` or `give_up` the last turn asked for before it reads the cancel (`must_stop`), and `Store::begin_step` refuses every kind but `stopped` and `failed` for a run whose status is `stopping` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1225-1230). Measured with the default policy on both commits: a turn that answers `finish`, then `Store::cancel_run`, then a tick by the same driver: the tick answers `Err(a stopping run's next step is its stopped step)`, the run stays `stopping` with one step, and so does a run whose second text-only turn asked for `give_up` (`no_tool_call`). `Driver::settle` tries such a run again every `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (driver.rs), for ever. The window is the time between a turn's commit and the next tick. crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs ('A policy that fails') says a run can always be stopped; this is the case where it cannot, and it does not depend on the policy.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1634},"end":{"line":1654},"content_hash":"8741654dd1167b33d55a1b0b7da4048eef070d3390c83c55f21ee32e67f4216b"},"suggested_fix":"In `Tick::step`, when the run is `stopping` (or the tick's cancel is set), stop it (`Stop::cancelled()`) before a `finish` or `give_up` is taken; or let `begin_step` take a `finish` or `give_up` for a stopping run, if a run that had already asked to finish should finish.","summary":"A run cancelled after its model asked for `finish` or `give_up` cannot take any step: the tick fails with `a stopping run's next step is its stopped step`, on this commit and on d1bc352e3 alike","supersedes":"4449ebcee6f1a83c98d10c21be871fd67d87867af6f55bdc0d4884d91164f9a0","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:25.596370Z","id":"284d87ffedcd24ef630d0674eedb19c25c8f17d5e6fe4683b05ab58326781aef","body":{"detail":"The turn calls `transcript(&self.steps)` once (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1746) and reads `system` (is this the first request, 1747; the system block, 1773) and `messages` (1775). A policy's rule stands at that call. Three things for that change. (1) The design's signature is `fn history(&self, run: &RunInfo, steps: &[Step], why: HistoryFor) -> Result<History>` (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md 864): its `History` is `history::Transcript` here (named so while the reference fold `History` is kept for tests, crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs); the default ignores `run` and `why`. (2) `first` is `committed == 0 || system.is_none()`: a rule that returns no system text for a run whose step 1 stored one makes the turn write the system prompt and the request again and store them in a later step's payload, which changes the cached prefix. The contract says so in words (history.rs 150-153); no type prevents it. Returning the system text apart from the rule's messages (the core reading it from step 1 itself) would remove the case. (3) The contract's other clauses (tool_use and tool_result kept in pairs, order, roles alternating from the user's, stored blocks unchanged) are documentation only: a checker `fn(&[Step], &Transcript) -> Result<(), String>` run on every rule's output in debug builds and in a conformance test would hold them; the default passes whenever the stored steps do.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":171},"end":{"line":247},"content_hash":"09c1fcb7345bb82be474a355051231e5efe5b42227bfafe37bcd91a7d087534b"},"summary":"For the change that adds `RunPolicy`: `transcript` is the default history rule, called in one place, and nothing checks that another rule keeps the contract its documentation states","supersedes":"7323532dcfd01b9b4cb40c4202acc3c514e0a28a70bd087650158b6fc2ec19d6","tags":["t3","for-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:25.807826Z","id":"eb8615ea8ddfd0f8cee6eda0a6ce788c37e6cedcf1c69243030eb62844224420","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface` (203-260) splits this file at its test module and fails on a noun of Clax's surface in any line of the code or its documentation (artifact, thread, draft, version, reply, publish, created, changed, base, tally and their forms, as whole words), on `crate::clax` or `crate::run` in a path, and on any use of `clax_store` but `bot_runs::{Step, StepKind}`. It is a word list: a noun it does not list (`comment`, `file`) or a payload key read through a variable passes, and it holds one file, not `Control`'s readers. A crate for the core's folds would make the compiler hold the imports, but `Step` itself is `clax_store`'s and its payload is untyped JSON, so no arrangement of crates stops `p[\"base\"]`; and the layering (scripts/check-crate-layers.py) would gain a crate for 190 lines. Two mutations show what the test catches: the word `draft` in a doc comment, and `use crate::clax::Tally as _;`, each fail it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":471},"end":{"line":585},"content_hash":"702cf0a3b8aaae14f3f9f842b3bfdc67d4af4ce5cecca106ecc029aaa05b9854"},"summary":"Holding the core's folds free of Clax's nouns by the compiler (a crate of their own, or a module with no access to `clax_store`'s run types) instead of by a test that reads the source","supersedes":"28f383eeeb42af0fce7dcf556cc2883771bcdad3298c6d54a829ac394c94e411","tags":["t3","revisit:the core's run loop moves to a crate of its own, or a step's payload gets a type per surface","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:26.015142Z","id":"39289fd4976f1cb5f087167e8c62b0b007ce976282d02bafd6667d41501cfcb0","body":{"detail":"The turn a refused request commits has `assistant: []` and `next: {kind: turn, user: [], overflow}`. `check_with` takes it for a paused turn (`answered == Some(false)`), reads `said` (the stored `assistant` blocks) as empty, and its condition `!said.is_empty() && ...` then never fails, whatever the last message is. Invariant 5 lets a `tool_use` in the last message stand ('one the next step answers'). So after such a turn a policy's messages may end with an assistant message, where the core's own projection ends with the user's. Measured (turn with a script, the script, a request refused as too long, and a policy that answers the overflow by leaving out every message that holds a tool result, so `[user: request][assistant: tool_use]`): the answer is shorter, the refused turn commits, the next tick's `check_shown` passes, and the request is sent (3 requests reached the provider where 2 were expected); the run ended `model_error` on the provider's answer. On the stop-reason path the same policy is caught (`policy_history_3`, 2 requests). Invariant 4's words in `transcript`'s documentation (history.rs, 'after a paused turn ... it is the assistant's and ends with that turn's stored `assistant` blocks') say the same wrong thing for a turn that stored no blocks: the projection's last message is then the user's. A real provider refuses such a request, so the cost is one request and a `model_error` ending in place of `failed` (`policy_history_4`); nothing is charged for it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":359},"end":{"line":382},"content_hash":"eef794e2ad414b0a427221f6ac45a780ea1828ff30da6f8ecf69d6f2054b031c"},"suggested_fix":"In `check_with`, when the last committed turn asked for a turn with no `user` blocks and stored no `assistant` blocks, require the last message to be the user's, as after an answered turn; and say so in invariant 4.","summary":"After a refused request that goes on, invariant 4 holds nothing of the last message: a policy's history that ends with the assistant's `tool_use` and no result passes `check_shortened` and is sent","supersedes":"22e3eb02af9c4f1e44946339b5a8a5cba3a34d503fc04aff107aaab374044ef9","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:26.231521Z","id":"dd0c359d0b211a55b64ecac210110e0e7de4ad569aca0ac22a9e8bf7636a613c","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:859-861 gives `RunPolicy::settings(&self, run) -> Result<RunSettings>`. Here the tick still reads the instance's settings from `Deps.settings` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1162), because the claim's lease is needed before the run is loaded and the driver reads concurrency with no run in hand (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:214, :392), and then passes them to `RunPolicy::run_settings(run, instance)` and `limits(run, settings)`. That costs no extra read a tick and leaves `Deps.settings` as it was for the server and the Worker. What it gives up: `Deps` carries two things (`settings`, `policy`), and because of the blanket impl at policy.rs:381 a type that is a SettingsSource cannot also implement RunPolicy with answers of its own (the impls would overlap), so an environment that wants both writes two types and passes the second to `Deps::with_policy`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":527},"end":{"line":573},"content_hash":"224f6bdf8a6280fe79ed3af3dda906a0dc0113c19a2d91822b3d2a4df8505d0e"},"summary":"A policy that is the one source of a run's settings (`settings(run)`, as the design draws it), with the blanket impl bridging a SettingsSource to it","supersedes":"c77e0a0f9ae66bbfd18b8f89f7728498db43161d4a951127fed3d576127a004f","tags":["revisit:the tick holds a RunInfo before it claims, or the server's or the Worker's settings type needs limits of its own","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:26.442281Z","id":"6e2998d2e665eacc840e1b9e37b34cd1ff53313ebba1d26639b4be1d17747c34","body":{"detail":"`run_settings`, `limits` and `history` take `&clax_store::bot_runs::Run`, the only description of a run the tick has today (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1180-1181, :1934, :1994). The design's policy is told a `RunInfo` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs:386), which names no artifact or thread. When the tick holds one, these parameters change type: a breaking change for implementers, of which there is one today (`Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs). The three questions the design adds (`accepts`, `to_model`, `delivery`) are not methods yet; each can be added with a default without changing an implementer.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":527},"end":{"line":565},"content_hash":"414d2c8bc944cba69f07ad32b6b18b3d5e1cdb8cc399b58a720e9d97a5811a32"},"summary":"For the change that gives the tick a `RunInfo`: every `RunPolicy` method takes the store's `Run` row","supersedes":"0af362d68652f134f4e1ee339b779a0779f0ba6da3b690ae66808218a487b3d7","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:26.665028Z","id":"1bd4f97aaf24ef73e8ec3c688c7dacce9c5cddd36001f22136298db908ef01ed","body":{"detail":"`prompt_too_long` matches four phrases in the message of a 400 (`prompt is too long`, `context_length_exceeded`, `maximum context length`, `context window`). They are from memory of the Messages, Chat Completions and Responses APIs, not from a recorded answer: crates/clax-sample has no typed error for it (`MessagesError::InvalidRequest` carries only the text, crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:618). A provider that says it otherwise takes the `model_error` stop, which is the behaviour before the policy, so nothing is lost under the default; a compacting policy would not be asked. A phrase that matched a refusal for another reason would ask the policy for a shorter history once per overflow, bounded as any overflow is. The fix belongs in clax-sample: a typed variant set from the provider's error type, with recorded answers as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":500},"end":{"line":522},"content_hash":"675b254309be2d640527fff932bc37c45aed6e4ad1e7f3f4509780728f15d450"},"summary":"A refusal for length is recognised by the provider's words, which no test checks against a provider","supersedes":"224c55652d2e5b065b16ccaea31a61fcd81cbc073588c76e60edd5e699d96858","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:26.888351Z","id":"821957d602898bab36a120a00cbcd6a10ccf97b100a8e4b2bb049c74e2d67e6a","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits::of` sets `model_call_timeout` to the settings' `max_run_minutes` (30 minutes by default). Under the default policy it never binds, since what is left of the run's time is never more. A policy that sets `max_run_minutes = None` and leaves the timeout gets calls of at most the settings' minutes each, on a native driver with no tick budget; before this, the only bounds on a call were the run's remaining time, the tick's budget, and the client's 120 s for headers and 90 s between pieces (crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:1113-1120), which bound a silence and not a call. The design names 'a floor for the model call's own timeout' as work of its own (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:968). `max_overflow_retries` is `Some(4)`: unreachable under the default policy, and a guess for any other. Question for the owner: what one model call may take where no run cap is set (a setting of its own, or the minutes kept as now), and whether a default bound on overflows in a row is wanted at all beside the rule that each history is shorter than the last.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":217},"end":{"line":243},"content_hash":"550cebbd95a87439839eeb2c9110e20facccf5fa7e0900251fcd61fa053b45da"},"summary":"Two defaults chosen here with nothing behind them: the model call's timeout when a policy sets no minutes, and four overflows in a row","supersedes":"21d75d6c0a67a9ece0f341153314b9255642f82bbbe3eea8a571e21eeec2cb97","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:27.095776Z","id":"1836dd088f0c2e4693ba3b845aa166811ee69041ebd31f837fac1a0b8a013bdd","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_no_cap_or_limit_but_the_policys` includes `run.rs` and `script.rs` as text and fails when either names `settings.max_steps`, `settings.max_run_credits`, `settings.max_run_minutes`, `settings.limits()`, the old constants, `at most 64` or `<< 10`, or declares any `const`; and when `run.rs` or `script.rs` no longer reads the `limits.*` fields. A cap read through another binding (`let s = &self.settings; s.max_steps`) passes it, and a file that code moves to is not looked at. When a script's calls move to `clax/*` and the endings to a provider, add those files to the list. `RunLimits::observe_every` has no test of its effect: an attempt's observed entry replaces its last (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1429-1432) and the final figures are recorded whatever the cadence, so only its default and that `run.rs` reads it are held. Keep reading the tool result's size from `Tick.limits.tool_result_bytes` and the sandbox's limits from `Tick.limits.script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":853},"end":{"line":882},"content_hash":"568c959526b330c4080e40006cbea219cc1df10082d35be68cbe6a9134fc774c"},"summary":"The check that the run loop reads no cap of its own is on the text of two files, by name","supersedes":"67f38d18aab9d98bda239ee1547666f9951b8a0341b36851939dd8631f6b4dec","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:27.314949Z","id":"acfd5bd3b9138601c84806505dcb24e3bedb5f6b0fd21d7de75b05f72ed733b6","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits` is `#[non_exhaustive]` with public fields, so outside clax-bot it cannot be written as a literal or with `..`: the one constructor is `RunLimits::of(&settings)`, then assignments (as `Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:104-108 does). `of` answers `Some` for the three caps, `model_call_timeout` and `max_overflow_retries`. An environment that wants a run with no bound writes five assignments; one that forgets `model_call_timeout` keeps calls cut at the settings' minutes (retried, see below), and when a sixth bound is added every such policy gets it set without a change to its source. That is the opposite of the module's rule that a cap the environment does not set is not replaced by one of the run loop's (policy.rs:10-13). Neither the struct nor `of` says that `of` is the way to make one. `HistoryAsk` is also `#[non_exhaustive]` and its `new` is `pub(crate)` (policy.rs:232), so a host cannot construct an ask to test its own `history` outside a tick. What `model_call_timeout` does when it is the bound that fires is not written either: the call ends as a cut stream, which is retried in the tick and then waits `Deps.retry_later`; it is never an ending. Measured with `model_call_timeout = Some(0)`: every tick answers `Waiting(Retry(30s))` and the run stays `running`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":158},"end":{"line":243},"content_hash":"430b729a1705bcbf3369b3d51c1febcab00be07f9b9be9e8eaab8efd8a13fab7"},"suggested_fix":"Add `RunLimits::unbounded(&RunSettings)` (or `none`): every `Option` `None`, the sizes from the settings and the defaults, documented as what a policy starts from when it means no caps, and say on `of` that it is today's bounds. Make `HistoryAsk::new` public. Say on `model_call_timeout` that a call it cuts is tried again and ends nothing.","summary":"A policy can build its limits only from `RunLimits::of`, which sets every bound: one that means 'no caps' must clear each by name, and a bound added later arrives set","supersedes":"e8b13c661b6a75263c1acddeecef931bdbb78309265a503b478cafda2d9b4162","tags":["review","verify-t4","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:27.532458Z","id":"4b4d9e8e3c168da5783eb92809605d305f6921aae78c596b25e851d683b67e6c","body":{"detail":"`check_shown` holds a policy's messages to invariant 2: the first message is the user's and opens with step 1's stored `user` blocks (on a first turn, the request the turn added). A compactor may leave out or summarise every step after it. Not taken: allowing a summary to stand in for the request too. The request is the person's words, shown whole (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, 'One door to the model': an `Instruction` keeps its standing only when shown whole), and with the system text it is the cached prefix. The cost: a request that is itself longer than the model's window, or that leaves no room once the prompt is counted, cannot be shortened by any policy, and the run ends `model_error` at its first turn (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs has no test of that case).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":408},"end":{"line":442},"content_hash":"8565e024b6fc65b60dc5c8da1f41bdfdd4c605af172d6a2c526ec46f34360b77"},"summary":"Letting a policy drop or rewrite the request a run opens with (invariant 2 of `history::transcript` not held for a policy's messages)","supersedes":"9db3e329a3110fb7627e01e8cddb090511f24fa2b01adcc410c8e3e451a76c99","tags":["revisit:a kind whose requests can be longer than a model's window (an attached document, a long brief)","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:32.105246Z","id":"711e7b549a1dab006021b9e553ca12a753e3341892927e1f3c1b0fe4fa23767c","body":{"detail":"(a) Records b25bed99 and f2d3e3a6, cited in (4) and (5), were re-recorded at their lines and are now a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c and 68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10 (the same threads). (b) Two audit-only behaviours of a stop pass every test when changed (record d016f728): `stop` without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (1574-1575 here: no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported), and `unreported_calls` without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (1539: every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record). No ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); pin `p.audit()` for one stopped ending and the cancelled one before these lines move. With several bindings the second matters more: the places of every binding's notices are at or above `NOTICE_SEQ` (`StepCx::notice`), and none should be audited as a script's call. (c) A `stopped` step's requests line is now held (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 249, 287): 1, whatever calls the script made. (d) To test the clash rule in a run loop, a provider is needed that answers a field named `calls` (or another of `PAYLOAD_KEYS`) from `finish` or `ending`: `FakeSurfaceProvider` answers only `fake_set` and `fake_notice`, so give it a way to answer a field a test names. The rule to hold: the attempt counted as a failed commit, the run ended `failed` at the bound, and in the `failed` step the clashing binding's fields left out and the others kept (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, `merge_fields`). `merge_fields` now takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for the step.","kind":"comment","references":"8d486d825822a5e3365716c712f0dffec71a41ddfbbc2b330fafe903768ef90a","span":{"start":{"line":1530},"end":{"line":1810},"content_hash":"ecd11657fe1f09c6d8ca151495cda2a6c5712e4f1e1685a5fbbbf00aa0052e7e"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, and three things about a stop's audit and a clash that no test holds","supersedes":"cdda0b489a97ce3283b420ac5222113bf79d653e558bc4181655400826291580","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:32.339731Z","id":"ba91b7d70fd69889dda122fcf9e704fa712a0d3deec9dc27133a40cc8468b690","body":{"detail":"In `turn`, when the last committed turn's `next` has an `overflow`, the policy's answer must be `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` equals `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (run.rs:2010-2043); the core's projection, or other messages, end the run by `Tick::overflow_ending` (run.rs:1726): `give_up`/`context_window` after the stop reason, `stopped`/`model_error` with the stored provider error after a refusal. So each request after an overflow is shorter than the one that overflowed whatever the policy answers at either ask, and `max_overflow_retries = None` ends. `the_turn_after_an_overflow_is_held_to_the_shorter_history` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) runs the policy of this record (less on the overflow, the core's projection after) with `max_overflow_retries = None` against a provider that refuses every request: steps turn, script, turn, stopped; 2 requests; 4 scripted refusals unused. It runs the same on the stop-reason path, and a policy that answers other messages after the overflow. Mutations, each failing that test: the hash comparison replaced by `is_null()`; the ending for `Shown::Core` after an overflow removed. The module documentation and `max_overflow_retries`' say what the code holds (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs, 'A full context window'). Proposed close: fixed in cad03c26b, with the test for other messages in 72a10983b.","kind":"comment","references":"08fb97c68d2eb92af7652dd94028acb6312d913f20ed90d68fe937834316e7b0","span":{"start":{"line":2013},"end":{"line":2049},"content_hash":"d71e81ca81a489902e210bc5af76ee2843deaf8b575c5f2f868bd7926931c317"},"summary":"The turn after an overflow is now held to the shorter history: it is sent messages whose SHA-256 is the overflow's `shown_sha256`, or the run ends","supersedes":"7cec99abcf40ea3620cad6883a0d93e48b614cfce42b10ecf850ec55457d4792","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:32.556452Z","id":"bf9d4ae46ac6e0db3de2e9d8bd4b48e5266ca126be5852d9f3e84e2fa2fba3ec","body":{"detail":"Done: `overflowed` builds the core's projection from `history::transcript(&self.steps)` (item 1's by-hand rebuild is one call and two pushes); a policy's messages are checked (`policy::check_shown`, item 4), with the cut-off answer allowed to be left out of the end (item 1's partial assistant message); a turn on a policy's messages is priced with no cached prefix (item 5); the turn after an overflow is held to the overflow's answer by its hash. Still open: a policy is given messages and not steps (2); it has nowhere to keep a summary, and `payload.shown` names what was sent without holding it (3); `level` only grows (6); an overflowing turn's `next` carries no nudge, so a nudged model is nudged again (7 of reply 70a8db39), now stated in crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs; and every tick loads every step (8, record 06b231ef's thread). New with the check: invariant 2 binds a policy, so a first request that alone overflows the window cannot be shortened and ends the run (`model_error`).","kind":"comment","references":"7805554a47cd9f3cd485765ca20affaf97e15bcc536213e9489766c8e83ed097","span":{"start":{"line":1922},"end":{"line":1968},"content_hash":"9a42d31543d19cfad3106eb94639918e0e14159279b472e2a2841a4190eba3ee"},"summary":"With the fold split: what of this list is done, and what is still a compactor's to meet","supersedes":"d09ba996378876159b96864984d0e2e5a75f265c494c4adbaac2205927e5dc70","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:32.779517Z","id":"b5017d7be4e9eb710ea94e07f673b1ba5798d9b0aa539d6f99a39a52275e9947","body":{"detail":"`RunPolicy::history` answers messages only (`Shown::Messages`); `turn` takes the system text and whether the request is the first from `transcript(&self.steps)` before the policy is asked (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1973-1977), so a rule cannot make a turn store `system` and `user` again. What a policy answers goes through `policy::check_shown` (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:415): on a first turn the first message must be the user's and open with the request the turn added, and then `history::check_shortened` with the steps' system text. Decided per invariant: 1, 3, 5, 6 and 7 bind a policy as they bind `transcript`; 2 too (a policy may leave out or summarise any step, never drop or rewrite the request the run opens with); 4 is relaxed in one case, in `check_shortened` alone: after a turn whose `next` records an `overflow`, the last message may be the user's, with the cut-off answer left out. `check` is unchanged for the core's own rule. Messages that break one end the run `failed` with `policy_history_<n>` and are never sent (`messages_that_break_what_a_turn_relies_on_fail_the_run_unsent`, crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: the request rewritten on the first turn and after it, and the answers left out). A turn that is sent a policy's messages is priced with no cached prefix (`a_turn_on_a_shorter_history_is_priced_without_a_cached_prefix`).","kind":"comment","references":"284d87ffedcd24ef630d0674eedb19c25c8f17d5e6fe4683b05ab58326781aef","span":{"start":{"line":171},"end":{"line":247},"content_hash":"09c1fcb7345bb82be474a355051231e5efe5b42227bfafe37bcd91a7d087534b"},"summary":"A policy's messages are now checked before they are sent, by `check_shortened`, which allows one thing `check` does not; and the system text and the first request cannot come from a policy","supersedes":"dc2840a928c58499df8453fa12401ef7f5dc4a5a43d5dd1bb80a4b0d0929a5f4","tags":["for-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:33.038637Z","id":"ae39719c12ecbb4df11457f6af1e2f3ab52026a33e3c7cf36525cd81b25c538b","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits::unbounded(&settings)` (policy.rs:230-241) answers `None` for every optional bound with the settings' sizes, and `of` is `unbounded` with the three caps and the overflow bound set, so a bound added to the struct is unset in both until `of` names it; the struct's documentation says how a policy builds one and that a later bound arrives unset in `unbounded`. `HistoryAsk::new` is `pub`. `observe_every` is a `NonZeroUsize`, so 0 is not a value. `model_call_timeout` is `None` in `of`. When a policy sets it and it ends a call, the call is retried as `Deps.retry` allows and then the run stops with reason `model_timeout` and a notice ('the model took longer to answer than this run allows'); a timeout of zero stops the run at its first turn with no call made (`a_model_call_that_outlasts_the_policys_timeout_stops_the_run`, crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: two calls of a provider that never answers, then `stopped`; zero: no request). Still open here: the trait is not for hosts until the tick holds a `RunInfo` (policy.rs module documentation says so).","kind":"comment","references":"acfd5bd3b9138601c84806505dcb24e3bedb5f6b0fd21d7de75b05f72ed733b6","span":{"start":{"line":158},"end":{"line":243},"content_hash":"430b729a1705bcbf3369b3d51c1febcab00be07f9b9be9e8eaab8efd8a13fab7"},"summary":"`RunLimits::unbounded` sets no bound and `of` is built on it; `HistoryAsk::new` is public; what a model call's timeout does when it fires is an ending, and documented","supersedes":"3cfa9cff988a659e507a4d3038945acdb4abd98698395074b88869ed0ca70658","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:33.394138Z","id":"14ffde1c6f9f7aca99393c6482378aff45c6e59897e1366502c0be2dda6cfec3","body":{"detail":"`of` no longer sets `model_call_timeout` (policy.rs:215-226 sets the three caps and `max_overflow_retries` over `unbounded`): under the settings' caps the run's remaining minutes already bound a call, and a policy that drops the minutes cap gets no hidden second use of them. A timeout a policy does set is counted with the tick's retries and then stops the run (`model_timeout`), where it waited without end. `max_overflow_retries` stays `Some(4)` in `of` and `None` in `unbounded`; since the turn after an overflow is held to the shorter history, the rule on bytes ends every run without it, so the default is a bound on cost, not on termination. What is still asked of the owner: whether an instance should have a setting for one model call's longest time where no minutes cap is set (today: none, so the driver's round or the Worker's alarm ends it and it is made again), and whether four is the number.","kind":"comment","references":"821957d602898bab36a120a00cbcd6a10ccf97b100a8e4b2bb049c74e2d67e6a","span":{"start":{"line":217},"end":{"line":243},"content_hash":"550cebbd95a87439839eeb2c9110e20facccf5fa7e0900251fcd61fa053b45da"},"summary":"Done as recommended for the timeout: `RunLimits::of` leaves `model_call_timeout` unset, and a timeout that fires now ends the run. The bound of four overflows in a row is kept. Both remain the owner's to decide","supersedes":"432ac6d7b108bfba7192d0116f56a18ce56745dd0bc4dbbd704c5caeb10141f3","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:33.711371Z","id":"a882f7d33cfd4cf89701b620264cea4121f1e46d153fc8f3023bec74fd24eb90","body":{"detail":"The run loop asks `self.deps.policy.is_overflow(&e)` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, the refusal arm of `turn`); the default calls `prompt_too_long`, so nothing changes under `DefaultPolicy`. `prompt_too_long`'s documentation says the phrases were not checked against recorded answers and names the misses: a provider's error `code` (the client keeps `type` and `message` only), Gemini's refusal, an error sent mid-stream (status 0), and the Messages API's `exceed context limit`. Unit tests hold each phrase alone, in any case, and the status range at 0, 399, 400, 499 and 500 (`each_phrase_alone_says_a_request_is_too_long`, `only_a_refusal_with_a_4xx_status_is_one_for_length`); deleting the `context window` needle or widening the range below 400 fails them. `a_policy_recognises_its_own_providers_refusal_for_length` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) runs a policy whose provider says it in other words. The typed error in clax-sample is still the fix for the default.","kind":"comment","references":"1bd4f97aaf24ef73e8ec3c688c7dacce9c5cddd36001f22136298db908ef01ed","span":{"start":{"line":500},"end":{"line":522},"content_hash":"675b254309be2d640527fff932bc37c45aed6e4ad1e7f3f4509780728f15d450"},"summary":"The words are the policy's now: `RunPolicy::is_overflow` decides, and its default is `prompt_too_long`, whose documentation lists what it is known to miss","supersedes":"c07b3e509a1b407b1bfc3c1593072ebac5678a947099cb69257c37cadda4383f","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:34.038388Z","id":"45fc6ae848a6693b54128a17c775eaeb9bb01258f3e535fa5b1c065a2e20314c","body":{"detail":"`impl<S: SettingsSource + ?Sized> RunPolicy for S {}` is gone (cad03c26b); `Deps::new` sets `DefaultPolicy` as before, and neither the server nor the Worker is edited. What this alternative weighed is otherwise unchanged: the tick reads the instance's settings from `Deps.settings` and hands them to `RunPolicy::run_settings`, whose answer for `lease_secs` and the concurrency settings is not read (the module documentation, 'Bounds that are not a policy's', says so).","kind":"comment","references":"dd0c359d0b211a55b64ecac210110e0e7de4ad569aca0ac22a9e8bf7636a613c","span":{"start":{"line":527},"end":{"line":573},"content_hash":"224f6bdf8a6280fe79ed3af3dda906a0dc0113c19a2d91822b3d2a4df8505d0e"},"summary":"The blanket impl is removed: a settings type may now implement `RunPolicy` itself","supersedes":"a24183475d1ba37b6ad05b90eff0632b715ec8e2923a13d9f6ae22a1c4854fb4","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:26:34.323281Z","id":"2f4b587981379e0eeb9e5b7f98a8876dc798862dd39cb2ad8404d317d7fe88d6","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_each_bound_and_size_from_the_policys_limits` no longer bans strings or constants. `a_run_with_no_bound_goes_far_past_every_default` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:593) gives a run 5,000 committed turns and 100,000 credits spent by writing them to the test's database (`seed_a_long_run`, :554), advances the clock a year, and under `RunLimits::unbounded` with the sizes raised to 1 MiB sees it take a turn, run a script whose result is over 300 KiB, and finish. A literal `committed >= 1000` added to `must_stop` fails it. Driving the steps was measured first: 1,100 real ticks took 17 s, so they are written, which couples the test to the columns of `bot_steps` and `bot_runs.credits_milli` (as crates/clax-bot/tests/driver.rs is coupled to `artifacts`). What it does not see: a bound above those figures, one on something it does not grow (the bytes of a history, a script's calls), or one in a path it does not take (a stop, a give-up).","kind":"comment","references":"1836dd088f0c2e4693ba3b845aa166811ee69041ebd31f837fac1a0b8a013bdd","span":{"start":{"line":853},"end":{"line":882},"content_hash":"568c959526b330c4080e40006cbea219cc1df10082d35be68cbe6a9134fc774c"},"summary":"Done as recommended: the test keeps only 'reads `limits.*`', and a run holds that the run loop has no bound of its own, by figures written to the database","supersedes":"9e12f90430e9712dd3be9f3f0dc11e0d79306cb92e151250bc87d3c0ef805108","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:27:12.274282Z","id":"610c4a4be3a349c07714583b163ee8e0b3018b8485b611c20f82c767a3b5428f","body":{"detail":"`check_with` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs:359-382) now answers '4: after a turn that said nothing, the last message is not the user's' for such a turn, in `check` and `check_shortened` alike, and invariant 4's wording in `transcript`'s documentation says it. `a_history_that_ends_on_an_unanswered_call_after_a_refusal_fails_the_run` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) runs the policy of this record on the refused path: steps turn, script, turn, failed; reason `policy_history_4`; 2 requests reached the provider. `check_holds_the_last_message` holds it on hand-built steps. With the role check replaced by `false`, both fail. The core's own projection keeps the rule (`the_transcript_of_a_run_keeps_what_a_turn_relies_on` passes). Proposed close: fixed in 16e0ba6bf.","kind":"comment","references":"39289fd4976f1cb5f087167e8c62b0b007ce976282d02bafd6667d41501cfcb0","span":{"start":{"line":359},"end":{"line":382},"content_hash":"eef794e2ad414b0a427221f6ac45a780ea1828ff30da6f8ecf69d6f2054b031c"},"summary":"Fixed: after a turn that stored no `assistant` blocks and asked for a turn with no `user` blocks, the last message must be the user's","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:27:12.274899Z","id":"6863712f906ca3f501a5579b73ff721248aba45bf696051a6d5378a4e64de201","body":{"detail":"`Tick::step` reads `run.status == Stopping` before the terminal steps (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1636-1641) and stops as any cancelled run does: a `stopped` step with reason `cancelled`, no notice, and nothing posted of the summary or reason the model meant to send. No store change was needed: `Store::cancel_run` sets `stopping` for a queued or running run, and `Store::begin_step_on` allows a stopping run exactly a `stopped` or `failed` step, which is the one now taken. The driver's cancel token alone (with the run not yet `stopping`) does not take this path; the step the model asked for is then taken, as before. `a_cancel_after_the_model_asked_to_end_the_run_stops_it` (crates/clax-bot/tests/cancel.rs) cancels after a turn whose `next` is `finish` and one whose `next` is `give_up`: each ends `stopped`/`cancelled` in one tick, payload `{reason: cancelled, notice: null}`, no reply. With the check replaced by `false` it fails. No fixture under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden changed. This is the one change on this branch that a person on a Clax with the default policy can see. Proposed close: fixed in 8a191a61f.","kind":"comment","references":"bcc408ac2c9d07229ab3e5abd9ac0befff1a7649c84b50d76744b44ab9bf74cc","span":{"start":{"line":1634},"end":{"line":1654},"content_hash":"8741654dd1167b33d55a1b0b7da4048eef070d3390c83c55f21ee32e67f4216b"},"summary":"Fixed in clax-bot alone: a stopping run takes its `stopped` step before a `finish` or `give_up` the model asked for. A person now sees such a run stop where it never ended","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T15:27:12.276135Z","id":"44148318eca77d89b9377baab40f442715123ee2d38be1f95ab180b6faec4950","body":{"detail":"When the first request is refused as too long, a policy that answers the overflow's ask with the request cut is taken at its word by size (the overflowing step is not committed yet, so the answer is checked by the turn that sends it): the refused turn is committed as step 1, with step 1's harness fee, and its `next.overflow`. The next tick's `turn` then checks the same answer against step 1's stored `user` blocks, finds invariant 2 broken, and ends the run `failed` with `policy_history_2`, unsent (`messages_that_break_what_a_turn_relies_on_fail_the_run_unsent` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs holds the first-turn and later-turn cases of a rewritten request; the refused-first-request case has no test of its own). `model_error` is the ending only when the policy shows no less. Either way no shortened request reaches a provider.","kind":"comment","references":"4b4d9e8e3c168da5783eb92809605d305f6921aae78c596b25e851d683b67e6c","span":{"start":{"line":408},"end":{"line":442},"content_hash":"8565e024b6fc65b60dc5c8da1f41bdfdd4c605af172d6a2c526ec46f34360b77"},"summary":"A correction to the cost stated here: a policy that shortens a refused first request ends the run `policy_history_2` on the next tick, not `model_error`","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.584764Z","id":"a8b58abfc587e3fd1186a479bcfb6d62539abe42be09b2577fba44c62a79a8c6","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs passes `self.limits.script` to the sandbox and `self.limits.tool_result_bytes` to `tool_result_content`. `the_run_loop_reads_each_bound_and_size_from_the_policys_limits` now takes a file's code as what precedes `#[cfg(test)]` followed by `mod tests`: run.rs has a `cfg(test)` field before its tests (`Deps::reference`), and the old split at the first `#[cfg(test)]` cut the file there (the test failed with \"run.rs does not read `limits.max_steps`\" until it was changed). The files a script's calls moved to (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/) read no `RunLimits` and no `RunSettings`: the only `limits` there is a fetch grant's own (`g.limits`, clax/web.rs), and the web surface's sizes (`AUDIT_RESPONSE_BYTES`, `MAX_QUERY_CHARS`) are its constants, so the list of files is as it was. What moves to a provider with the endings is still to add.","kind":"comment","references":"1836dd088f0c2e4693ba3b845aa166811ee69041ebd31f837fac1a0b8a013bdd","summary":"script.rs still reads its sizes from `limits.*` and the test holds it; no file of the `clax` provider reads a bound or a size of a run","tags":["t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.585424Z","id":"18234bb600f902ae7c462b6c40c7b8e1d6c53ad5b4c7390b39f7b6e5b996de32","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs, `Tick::script`: `StepCx::new(info, surface.binding, self.n, s.attempt, &self.steps, claim, &self.opts.cancel)`. With `&[]` in its place, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` (the flags of scripts/lib/bazel-test-flags.sh) fails `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_rebase_is_resumed_from_the_version_rebased_onto`, `golden::scripts::a_second_artifact_is_refused`, `rules::a_brief_run_creates_one_artifact` and 7 more. The tick that makes the tick's `RunInfo` and its providers (the for-t8 half of this record) is not done.","kind":"comment","references":"7c9a3905f1732b8a48521aefa6be644f444df1368edd452048728df2002b0341","summary":"`Tick::script` gives every session `&self.steps` as its history, and a session given none fails tests","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.586267Z","id":"6e58f0fbe689f12ba27b62f8ed3a969b117fca906b21e54d21e180b5eb633bfb","body":{"detail":"`ClaxProvider::begin` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs) takes `base` and `created` from `Tally::of(cx.history)`, loads the draft at `base.unwrap_or(run.base_version)`, and the session refuses a second `artifacts.create` from `created_before` (clax/session.rs, clax/rules.rs). `Tick::clax` is still read by run.rs (the endings: `what_i_did`, the finish rule) and by the reference (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs, for tests). Changed one at a time, with `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` (the flags of scripts/lib/bazel-test-flags.sh): the draft loaded at `run.base_version` fails `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_rebase_is_resumed_from_the_version_rebased_onto`, `providers::claxs_own_provider_passes_the_provider_conformance_suite`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_1` and 4 more; without `created_before` it fails `golden::scripts::a_second_artifact_is_refused`, `rules::a_brief_run_creates_one_artifact`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_3`, `seam::a_script_after_one_that_created_an_artifact_may_not_create_another`. This record's lines are of the file before it was rewritten.","kind":"comment","references":"7564590a4493710cb151474667f770e3c09df7943ba45796371ac003bf16eaed","summary":"Done: the session folds its own tally, and script.rs reads nothing of `Tick::clax`","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.587010Z","id":"56a721982cc27660032a5980e5fbcf212c10750941e5958d41620afac1a28c0f","body":{"detail":"(1) The replay check is the first thing `ClaxSession::web` does (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs); without it `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_3`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_4`, `seam::a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` and 4 more fail. (2) crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/journal.web-disabled.json pins the row for a run that declared no web operation and for one on a host with no source, each crashed and resumed (`golden::web::the_journal_rows_of_web_operations_a_run_lacks_are_pinned`). (3) The divergence check is `StepHost::call` in crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs; `audit.replay-diverged.json` and `script.replay-diverged.json` are unchanged; with the check off `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_that_takes_another_path_stops_where_it_diverged`, `golden::resumed::the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`, `golden::scripts::a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` and 3 more fail. (4) A script's calls are numbered through `CallPlace::in_script` (surface.rs), which `StepCx::place` is too; past `NOTICE_SEQ` the call is answered `no_place`, told and not journaled. Nothing drives a script that far through script.rs: the range is held by `surface::tests::a_scripts_calls_have_the_places_under_the_notices`. (5) crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs, `claxs_own_provider_passes_the_provider_conformance_suite`: `disturb` writes the document the read is of from outside the run; `fail_once` makes `begin` fail as it does for a draft that cannot be loaded (a history whose last step names a base version the artifact lacks), and answers why `ending` cannot be made to fail (it does nothing until the notices move). Twelve cases played, two named not applicable. (6) `ClaxSession::op_ctx` passes `call.place.attempt`; one higher fails `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_1`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_2`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_3` and 3 more. (7) `tool_result_content` starts from the sessions' joined fields (`StepJoin::told`) and trims after; without them `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_rebase_is_resumed_from_the_version_rebased_onto`, `golden::resumed::a_script_that_published_is_resumed_as_it_ran` and 15 more fail. (8) `SurfaceProvider::standing` (default: every operation; `clax`: every operation but the web's), read by `ClaxProvider::info` through `Binding::from_stored`. The bindings are still made from the row's artifact and web grant (`Bindings::clax`), not read from `bot_runs.bindings_json`: that is the change that gives the tick a `RunInfo`. (9) The audit and usage of a script step are the generic step's and the goldens that pin them are unchanged; crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs also compares them with the reference's over twenty runs. From the reply: (d) a replayed call's `ms` of 0 is held by `golden::resumed` (`pin_resumed` reads the stored step) and by `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_is_replayed_after_a_crash`; as 7 it fails `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_is_replayed_after_a_crash`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_rebase_is_resumed_from_the_version_rebased_onto` and 2 more. (e) `StepJoin` (surface.rs) holds the step's two `Merged`, each beside its fields. (f) The fixture says of `disturb`, `staged`, `fail_once` and `lifecycle` that it has one or why not.","kind":"comment","references":"67e0b648578c2cfbe985fa7fbe7dd150274f701ba07a58f1a81bb02d8ef6005f","summary":"Each of the nine, and the four the reply added, as built: script.rs is the generic step and the calls are `clax::ClaxProvider`'s","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.587709Z","id":"b3187cb7cc6cfb4e425f67dae8d04aeccd195cb7eec7088cc406d8f38000f546","body":{"detail":"`tool_result_content` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs) lists the operations of the calls logged before the place that diverged. `audit.replay-diverged.json` did not change (`git diff 2a6389e8d --stat -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden` lists added files only). With the filter as `.filter(|_| false)`, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` (the flags of scripts/lib/bazel-test-flags.sh) fails `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_that_takes_another_path_stops_where_it_diverged`, `golden::resumed::the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_1`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_2`. The same is held for a surface that is not Clax's: `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_that_takes_another_path_stops_where_it_diverged` diverges at its second call and requires `completed: [\"fake.set\"]`.","kind":"comment","references":"983fa5d948f7940d0258cb97a9b56830e35939bea98856942ec2a33aac938bce","summary":"`replay.completed` is built from the generic step's own log, and the fixture that pins it is unchanged","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.589065Z","id":"df3855d4ba824a001643721f9e7f096717689644fe5690b771fb9e859d159683","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::standing(op)` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) answers whether every run of the surface has an operation, true unless the provider says otherwise; `ClaxProvider` answers every operation but `web.*` (`api::declared` with no web shape). The module documentation now describes what the provider does and where (files under crates/clax-bot/src/clax/, by function) in place of line numbers of script.rs. The request is still resolved by run.rs through `BotHost::context` (record c40a8d904e).","kind":"comment","references":"027c6110ff49c2f22afee1ac8c43f73c35ff09002cd86ae525202e2ee77ba74b","summary":"Of the two left open: a surface's standing operations are its provider's to say; who resolves the request at the first turn is still open","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.589742Z","id":"0b1ec433afaac23bc0a42132eb6680ca38922c29edc65ca87b4b1f4ad340fcb9","body":{"detail":"`tool_result_content(&outcome, &calls, join.told(), self.limits.tool_result_bytes)` in `Tick::script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs). The sentence this record was on is now \"to `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`, with the sessions' fields (`published`) and `replay` already in it\". Proposed close: fixed.","kind":"comment","references":"77c3d58e519a128a834099aae1c4cc53f9ea3dd3275d4301d323b3996edb5c71","summary":"The tool result is built in script.rs from the tick's limits, after the sessions' fields are joined","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.590405Z","id":"f640a1310054787f673d22a506579f37ad5411f86a5040f739c9e2a6328173f5","body":{"detail":"With `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` (the flags of scripts/lib/bazel-test-flags.sh): no `replayed` mark fails `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_is_replayed_after_a_crash`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_rebase_is_resumed_from_the_version_rebased_onto` and 7 more; no `unreported_calls` fails `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_that_takes_another_path_stops_where_it_diverged`, `golden::resumed::the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_1` and 1 more; the `bot.script` record listing the run loop's log fails `golden::scripts::a_call_that_was_not_awaited_is_in_the_scripts_record_alone`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_3`; the requests line counting no call fails `golden::resumed::a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls`, `golden::resumed::a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call`, `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_2`.","kind":"comment","references":"20c0b00f2b538abca60743f35e33331c3c108edd8fe9eced2e3a33fcba80f757","summary":"The four are held on the generic step: each change, made to the new script.rs, fails a test","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.591050Z","id":"01f19bcda16b15d5c132b9e88ef9af7d1e90340de8af5862f3c12c82f3e2fb99","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs, `StepHost::call`, asks `CallPlace::in_script(run, step, attempt, seq)` for every call; `StepCx::place` is the same function. From place 1,000,000 a call is answered `no_place` (`call_limit`), told in the step's `calls`, its event and a `bot.call` record, and neither journaled nor handed to a session. A cap on `script.max_calls`, or a clamp where the limits are handed to the sandbox (`self.limits.script`, script.rs), is one line in either place and is not made here.","kind":"comment","references":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","summary":"The run loop's script step now numbers its calls through the check: today's hazard is closed by the backstop, and whether to cap the setting is still the owner's","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.591711Z","id":"2c1f7cb58b4061fe0ac2783ea6187f671257426109180eb26e1248ea1e2b9c36","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/journal.web-disabled.json: the step's `calls`, its journal rows and what the script is told, for a run that declared no web operation and for one that declared both on a host with no source, each crashed before the step's commit and resumed. The same test run against the code before the seam wrote a file that differs from it in the four rows alone: each loses `\"cost_milli\": 0` and `\"web\": {…}` and nothing else changes (`diff` of the two files: four hunks, each those two keys). No fixture held the old row. crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs takes exactly those keys from exactly those rows of the reference's journal before it compares, requires the seam's rows to be `{code, message}`, and compares runs whose first attempt one code made and whose second the other resumed.","kind":"comment","references":"964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb","summary":"The row is as decided, pinned in a fixture of its own, and a run journaled either way resumes the same under the other code","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.593656Z","id":"481638695c7e8aa1a8a37ea0fb07ab614593f8bbe4a685881092f35d8a24a498","body":{"detail":"`pin_resumed` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs) reads the resumed step as stored and requires every call's `ms` to be 0, and `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_is_replayed_after_a_crash` the same for the fake surface. With a replayed call told as 7 ms, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` (the flags of scripts/lib/bazel-test-flags.sh) fails `generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_is_replayed_after_a_crash`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_publish_is_resumed_from_the_published_version`, `golden::resumed::a_script_after_a_rebase_is_resumed_from_the_version_rebased_onto`, `golden::resumed::a_script_that_published_is_resumed_as_it_ran` and 1 more. A cancelled script's stopped step now has its audit records pinned (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/ending.stopped.cancelled-script.*.json), which a stop without `unreported_calls` does not change (no row is left unreported there); (2) and (3) of this record stay for the change that moves the endings.","kind":"comment","references":"5ed5547d0ca5351abc25a5b10e863788dbc3fd9a8c828861f428aa09472c3316","summary":"A replayed call's `ms` of 0 is now held; the two audit-only changes to a stop are still unpinned","tags":["t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.594281Z","id":"b5e22edf401cff79e8fe8ed2c196e4b5acd50e9bfcbea04e7f64c37eca5789df","body":{"detail":"`Tick::clashed` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs) logs the clash, calls `Store::note_commit_failure` and fails the tick with `surface_field_clash`; nothing of the attempt is committed. `generic::a_provider_whose_fields_clash_cannot_hold_a_run_open` (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs) binds the fake under the name `calls`: each tick fails, the open step's `failures` rises by one, and after `max_commit_failures` the run ends `failed` (`internal_error`). Without the count `generic::a_provider_whose_fields_clash_cannot_hold_a_run_open` fails. Still open: reserving those names where a surface is defined, and a clash in a terminal step (no `ending` is called yet).","kind":"comment","references":"68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10","summary":"A clash in a script step is now counted against the step, and a run holds it","tags":["t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.594902Z","id":"659a27996b3f9152357b03c0797957d4931234623980f43a7e6f6f5ef69d49b2","body":{"detail":"`StepJoin` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) holds the payload's fields with their `Merged`, the model's fields with theirs, and the rows; `Tick::script` makes one for each attempt and adds each session's parts under its binding's surface. A run whose one binding is the fake's commits `payload.fake: {fake_set: […]}` and none of `clax`'s fields (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs). `StepParts::draft` is still `clax`'s row in a core type.","kind":"comment","references":"7bec5bda26c3271a11be0b277826ce9602cb21bd58584221a502a1e9209c7c9c","summary":"The script step joins every session's parts through one holder, `StepJoin`","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.595516Z","id":"2f8642e503f993b372eb2954ab3bb2f75a59a609cd7282c48cb0deff5522fca7","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs, `claxs_own_provider_passes_the_provider_conformance_suite`: `db.get` as the read and a write to that document from outside the run as `disturb`; a draft that cannot be loaded as the failing `begin`. `ProviderFixture::fail_once` now answers, for each hook, that it will fail or why it cannot (`FailOnce`), so a provider whose `ending` cannot fail still plays the failing `begin`. Not played against `clax`, each named by the test: the `ending` that fails, and the hooks' record.","kind":"comment","references":"c41498763b8d79f54ca8f7a9adc4377ef367fd447ee48572b4856bfeb634b008","summary":"The suite runs on the product's provider, with a read the fixture disturbs and a `begin` that fails","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596138Z","id":"10fdb09dd18ae2a954f64e66b7c7ed3a31e9893898c37be49ec35ee1d935792a","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs reads neither `Tick::clax` nor anything under `crate::clax` (held by `script::tests::the_script_step_names_nothing_of_claxs_surface`). `StepCx::new` is given `&self.steps`. `Tick::clax` is read by `what_i_did` and `finish` in run.rs, and by crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs, which is compiled for tests alone and goes with the last task of the plan.","kind":"comment","references":"14ecf59970ed1be4f01873ab33fab6e55daa61e74ac3415ba6b7bc03ae4aa6ae","summary":"The script reads of `Tick::clax` are gone; its readers left are the endings in run.rs","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596344Z","id":"1423b9dd5bbadbbbf67996fa3e5c653cecd1c588c35001b5636a395bd9ea4b82","body":{"detail":"`Tick::script` gives `Tick::stop` the joined payload (`StepJoin::payload`) as `extra`, which `stop` lays over `reason` and `notice` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs). The `Merged` inside the `StepJoin` is dropped. `merge_fields` (surface.rs) is written for a surface's `ending` fields to join the object its session made in that same stopped step, which needs that `Merged`. When `ending` is called in `stop`, pass the `StepJoin` (or its payload half) instead of a value, and merge `reason` and `notice` as the core's. `stop` also lets `extra` overwrite `reason` or `notice`; the sessions' fields cannot be named so (`PAYLOAD_KEYS` has `reason`; `notice` is not in it, and a `clax` session answering `notice` would replace the stop's).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":405},"end":{"line":433},"content_hash":"4b54d01f718449383af63ed437e69369bc015a1851ce694a25556696aeaacc8d"},"summary":"A cancelled script's sessions' fields reach the stopped step as one JSON value, so the record of which surface made each name ends there","tags":["t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596452Z","id":"ffaee020ee1d0dcfb70a01c859a6e3f382d9aff3fb049c35e96df6e047c34e2a","body":{"detail":"`script_step` builds `ClaxProvider::of(self.deps).for_run(self.run.clone())` and its `RunInfo` (`ClaxProvider::info`: kind and request from `Run::request` and `Run::thread_ids`, the binding from `Bindings::clax(artifact_id, web)`), not from `bot_runs.kind`, `request_json` and `bindings_json`, which the store writes and `Run` does not carry. `Deps::surfaces` and `Deps::with_surfaces` replace the run's bindings for a test (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs); nothing else sets them. For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: read the stored bindings, hold one provider for each for the tick (or the driver), and delete `Deps::surfaces`, `ClaxProvider::info`, `ClaxProvider::for_run` and its `row` (a provider made without the row reads it by `Store::run`, one read a step; the tick gives its own so a script step reads the store no more than it did). The clone of the row for each script step goes with them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1718},"end":{"line":1743},"content_hash":"ab64eaf4063fb118906fe138e1e7ab931b9ddf1691f39f6d56a3aae2eacaf054"},"summary":"The tick makes a run's one binding from the row's own columns, a provider for each script step, and takes other surfaces from a hook for tests","tags":["t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596511Z","id":"9be023649d8f6bb5cad74f087aaa6f01f76ed32c11bae95b6bdbbc387428612a","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs and script_reference/web.rs (the script step and the web performer as they were, about 1,650 lines); crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs (the comparison); `Deps::reference` and the `cfg(test)` branch that reads it in `Tick::step` (run.rs); `extern crate self as clax_bot` in lib.rs and `test_compile_data = glob([\"tests/common/*.rs\"])` in crates/clax-bot/BUILD.bazel, which let a unit test include the tests' shared support; `Tick::op_ctx` and `Tick::entry` once the notices move too; and `events::with_web_fields` if crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs no longer needs it. The comparison adds about three seconds to `//crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` (four tests side by side, about seventy runs).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1},"end":{"line":19},"content_hash":"74a7058e965b06feaf6aa9635155767e65f9d5e5a4b4993bb9bd9b6b72c6e570"},"summary":"What goes with the reference when a run can have no `clax`","tags":["t5","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596698Z","id":"79495cc8e59069f56399fb493646f3f4ad1e4925f5303b3cdaa964cb0d7535d8","body":{"detail":"Clax's web operations write their journal row, their charge and their `bot.web` record in one transaction (`Store::spend_journaled`). When the entry cannot be made or the transaction fails, nothing was written; the code before the seam answered the script the failure and journaled nothing, so the step run again makes the call again. The seam had no answer for that: `refused` makes the core journal the failure, and `written` said the place has its row. `Answer::unwritten(e)` says it (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs, `unwritten`; the module documentation, \"Who journals a call\"). The other choice: answer `Answer::refused`, which journals the failure, so a store that failed once answers that call the same failure on every later attempt of the step instead of trying again; one constructor fewer, and a change in what a resumed run does after a failed write. No test reaches either (mutating one to the other passes every test).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":936},"end":{"line":937},"content_hash":"531093bc7cd566e79afb8299a88ba2512bdae34e5eb1e1c1b64d6f84556edc14"},"summary":"A failure that neither side journals is now a constructor of the seam, `Answer::unwritten`, for the owner to keep or drop","tags":["t5","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596756Z","id":"69e968a6b0993154b28f964a043f331a11261d8bec2700202991de9b7c5a03d8","body":{"detail":"The reference is `cfg(test)` of the library, so only the library's unit tests can run it; they include crates/clax-bot/tests/common/mod.rs by path to drive whole runs (`extern crate self as clax_bot`, `test_compile_data` in BUILD.bazel). Linking the reference into the library proper would let crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs compare against it, and the server's and the Worker's tests too, at the cost of the old script step and web performer staying in every build until the last task.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":29},"end":{"line":30},"content_hash":"9fea8c0db2d3318b8fdf74f5ee8148b1989a1ca7212a9b1e78e1d8afc5ea95ec"},"summary":"Running the comparison with the reference from the integration tests, beside the proof","tags":["t5","revisit:the comparison must run against the server's host or on the Worker","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:22:01.596801Z","id":"a7e0541e09bff1e2afbc3456c66cf24fb7760e4c7265aab8586f060624ef3f7d","body":{"detail":"A row's `args_hash` by value (two homes mint different IDs, so it is compared as which rows share it, and by runs resumed across the two codes); what a script measured (`ms`, CPU, memory, the bytes of a payload) and the credits charged from it (compute and storage are priced at 0 for the comparison); a lost claim, a sandbox that cannot take the script, a store that fails a write, and a call past `NOTICE_SEQ`; and a `FieldClash`, which the reference cannot have. The scenarios run on the fake host: the server's host is compared with nothing.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":218},"end":{"line":252},"content_hash":"4f609f355adb3fda3e4b5201afa7e9bcbc524cd1bb76c83f68cdc1a69a0b1156"},"summary":"What the comparison with the reference does not hold","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.563282Z","id":"f511a373a836e25244a48a1222fb1873f4e8cc0a6e118120474adc15e059ba21","body":{"detail":"Its script calls `clax.db.update(\"notes\", \"a\", {n: 3})` with three arguments. `db.update` takes four (`opts: IfVersion`, crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts 155), so the call is refused `invalid_args` (\"clax.db.update: argument 4 (opts) is missing\"), nothing catches it, and the script ends there on the reference's path and on the seam's alike: the step's result is `{calls: [threads.read, db.get, db.set, db.update (invalid_args)], error: {kind: host_error}}` (read from the stored step of this scenario, printed by a test added to this file and removed again). `db.delete` is called with two arguments and would fail the same way. So what the name says is compared is not: with the reference changed to never refuse a second publish in one script (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs 306 as `if false {`), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test --test_arg=script_differential` passes all four comparisons. The same script with arguments that hold (`{if_version: doc.version}` for the update, the set's version for the delete), crashed before its commit and resumed, does leave the same on both paths and on the two mixed ones, with `second: [\"already_published\", …]`: run as a twenty-first scenario in a scratch copy of this file. The second publish is held for the product elsewhere (`golden::scripts::the_draft_operations_answers_are_pinned`, `seam::a_replayed_host_read_and_a_second_publish_behind_a_session`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":912},"end":{"line":928},"content_hash":"733afb13b00755fd101c674e2f8b96a2a0008dcac8f1ce32c1d664534dcf9e6e"},"suggested_fix":"Give `db.update` and `db.delete` their `opts`, and have each scenario say what its last script's result must hold (here `second[0] == \"already_published\"` and no `error`), so a script that ends early fails its scenario instead of comparing two equal failures.","summary":"The scenario named for host reads, document writes and a second publish ends at its fourth call: neither path ever reaches the document delete, the list, the publish, the second publish, the reply or the collections","tags":["review","verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.564371Z","id":"57f0d836cbf0f4de45eabaab0ae49d619cc21df6380a685200ebd292c56144a4","body":{"detail":"The change added 24 lines to crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs before `Tick::step` (`Deps::surfaces`, `Deps::reference`, `Deps::with_surfaces`) and 35 after it (`Tick::script_step`), and the table (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 225-236) was not renumbered. Where the table's rows are now: `stop_lead` 1183, `Tick::commit` 1293, `reset_date` 1409, `must_stop` 1444, `what_i_did` 1488, `notify_at` 1519, `unreported_calls` 1538, `stop` 1554, `fail` 1613, the roster on 1690, `finish` 1810, `give_up` 1850, the roster off 1593, 1642, 1846, 1867. `events.rs` 316-326 (`step_summary`) is 340-350. The rows for a script's calls name files and functions and are right; the same would keep these right.","kind":"comment","references":"cd2a3d2bc1b5fdc4eb8294288313d5cb907abf7eaff6dbf36145eda48f171200","span":{"start":{"line":221},"end":{"line":230},"content_hash":"6e8dcb9a9510610a6846486fb62d05388d5e63fee98d6e6f2c3c22de1dcd0d48"},"summary":"After the script step's move every line number the endings table gives of run.rs is wrong: 44 of 44 checked","tags":["verify-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.564745Z","id":"8254766591eb081fd9a88cddc8dc46284087ecfda02425c7dcbfe148fe2992f6","body":{"detail":"Every crashed scenario's first driver runs its whole script (or another whole script) and dies before the step's commit, so no scenario resumes a step whose journal holds only the first calls of its script, and none dies after a commit or in a cancelled step. Run in a scratch copy of this file with the same `Left` and `same`: (1) the driver dies after the draft calls and before the publish; (2) after the publish's row and before the reply; (3) after a search and a fetch were charged, the rest made on resume (a window from the page store, a refused fetch); (4) after three refused writes were journaled, then a reply, a rebase and a publish made on resume; (5) a brief's run, after `artifacts.create`, the second create refused on resume; (6) the scenario of record on lines 912-928 with arguments that hold; (7) a rebase recorded, someone publishes version 3 before the resume: the rebase goes to version 2 again and the publish answers `conflict`; (8) a `db.get` recorded, the document changed before the resume: the first read answers the recorded value and a second the new one; (9) the driver dies after the script step's commit (`CrashPoint::AfterCommit`); (10) a script cancelled once it returned, the driver dies before the `stopped` step's commit (`CrashPoint::BeforeCommit` of kind `Stopped`), and a driver told of no cancel runs the step again from its journal and commits it as a script step; (11) failures of draft operations, replayed. Not driven, by reading: a call past `NOTICE_SEQ` (the reference has no such check); a crash between a source's answer and `Store::spend_journaled` (the code is the reference's, moved: crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs 496-536 against crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference/web.rs 461-512); a lost claim, which differs in the one answer of record 785c9e30. Store entries, counted by a counter added to `Store::with_read`, `with_tx` and `with_write` in scratch copies at 2a6389e8d and at 0ffc5b11a: the same on both for a script step (18 reads and 3 transactions with no call) and for each call (a draft operation 1 and 1, a host read 2 and 1, a reply 3 and 1, a refusal of the run's rules, of the arguments or of check 2 1 and 1, a window from the page store 10 and 1, a fetch that reaches the source 13 and 1, a publish 10 and 1, a rebase 4 and 1).","kind":"comment","references":"a7e0541e09bff1e2afbc3456c66cf24fb7760e4c7265aab8586f060624ef3f7d","span":{"start":{"line":218},"end":{"line":252},"content_hash":"4f609f355adb3fda3e4b5201afa7e9bcbc524cd1bb76c83f68cdc1a69a0b1156"},"suggested_fix":"Keep (1) to (5), (7) and (8) as scenarios here: a `Cut::CrashAfter` whose first script is the first calls of the stored one, and a hook that runs before the resume.","summary":"Eleven crash points the scenarios do not reach, each run the reference's way, the seam's and both mixed ways: all leave the same","tags":["verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.565139Z","id":"61e1a09a7f24b62f872fc81d0e75b99961daae4055259a189347da79c508d6f0","body":{"detail":"It is used in two places, crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs 509 (the entry could not be made) and 534 (`Store::spend_journaled` failed), which are the two places the code before the seam journaled nothing (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference/web.rs 464-472, 506-510). Journaling those failures instead would be a change a person can meet: a fetch whose transaction failed once (a busy store, `page_exists`) would answer that failure on every later attempt of its step, where today the step run again fetches. What it leaves open: `Answer::unwritten(e)` is `Journal::Written` with a failure, the same value as `Answer::written(Err(e))`, so `Answer::journal` says a row was written when none was, and the provider cases cannot tell a provider that uses it for a call that did write. A variant of its own (`Journal::Unwritten`) would let `Claim::record` and the cases say what they mean: `Written` has a row, `Unwritten` has none and no effect.","kind":"comment","references":"79495cc8e59069f56399fb493646f3f4ad1e4925f5303b3cdaa964cb0d7535d8","span":{"start":{"line":936},"end":{"line":937},"content_hash":"531093bc7cd566e79afb8299a88ba2512bdae34e5eb1e1c1b64d6f84556edc14"},"summary":"A recommendation for the owner's question: keep `Answer::unwritten`, and give it a `Journal` of its own","tags":["verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.565201Z","id":"de5b2bc3d03fbc1ac53df8eb1f42693395a84a4f16e67efaeb0b696f7b6edab1","body":{"detail":"`Binding::from_stored` builds a run's operations from the surface's file, the stored grant and this rule each time a script step is run (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs 102-116, called from `Tick::script_step`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1718-1745). The rule has no kind: a surface whose kinds have different operations (the fake's file gives its kind an `@allow` list, crates/clax-bot/src/fake.d.ts) cannot say so. And the rule is read from the running build, not from what the run was bound with: a binding keeps the hash of its surface's definition (`Binding::hash`) so that a run keeps the surface it was made with, but a build whose provider answers `standing` otherwise gives a run made before it other operations on its next step. For `clax` both are harmless today (one rule, in `api::declared`). For the change that reads `bindings_json`: either store a binding's whole set of operations when the run is made (then nothing asks the rule again, and `standing` goes), or take the rule from the kind's `@allow` list in the definition the binding's hash names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1703},"end":{"line":1710},"content_hash":"1cbf1004aead22a54a08156e5af92245b149741e90f805f06f1793aa4d050399"},"summary":"`SurfaceProvider::standing` is asked of the provider's code, per operation and for every run alike: it cannot say what differs by kind, and a provider that changes it changes what runs already made may call","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.565233Z","id":"fbb4ff52cd118c24b2d86e0defd7af5c60a4c4a473734c565c4c940814b14830","body":{"detail":"`the_script_step_names_nothing_of_claxs_surface` fails on a comment that says \"thread\" or \"version\" and passes code that reaches Clax's surface by any other word (a helper in another module, a field named otherwise), as the list in crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs did for limits. crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs is the test of the meaning: with `ClaxProvider::begin` and `ClaxProvider::undeclared` changed to panic (in a scratch copy), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration --test_arg=generic::` passes its five tests, so no session of Clax's is opened and no call is answered by Clax's provider on that run's script steps. What of Clax's still runs there: `Tick::script_step` makes a `ClaxProvider` and its `RunInfo` for every script step before it takes the given surfaces (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1718-1722: four `Arc` clones, a clone of the run's row, the `clax` binding built and thrown away); and outside the script step the turn's prompt and request (`prompt::system_prompt`, `BotHost::context`), the roster (`BotHost::working`), `Tick::clax` (the tally) and every ending's notices on the run's threads.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":477},"end":{"line":510},"content_hash":"ad301d2d14ec7b32681352a9e7d0435d9386b6f2c73c6258aa032a84c2f9a4bc"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the word list; keep the three assertions on imports and `deps.host`/`deps.web` if a guard on the file's text is wanted, and let tests/generic.rs say the rest.","summary":"The test that script.rs names nothing of Clax's is a list of eight words searched in the file's text, comments included; what it means to hold is held by the run whose one surface is the fake's","tags":["review","verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.565257Z","id":"712f1b155ac7a82cddeb0c8ed9333b12bb6fc99f695a3c4feb4e03173f5f325a","body":{"detail":"`join.add` runs before the cancelled branch, so a provider whose parts clash fails the tick there (`Tick::clashed`) though the script was cancelled. Nothing of the attempt is committed. When the cancel is the run's own (`RunStatus::Stopping`), the next tick's `must_stop` stops it plainly (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, `stop_plain`): the `stopped` step has `reason` and `notice` alone, without `title`, `code`, `outcome`, `calls` or any session's fields; the script's compute is not charged (never twice: the abandoned attempt charged nothing); and the step's journal rows get a `bot.call` record each from `unreported_calls`, with no `bot.script` record. When the cancel was only the tick's token, the next tick runs the script again from its journal. With `clax` alone no clash can happen: its session answers `published`, `replied`, `base`, `changed`, `created` and, to the model, `published`, none of which is in `PAYLOAD_KEYS` or `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS`, and it is the first and only surface joined. No test drives a clash in a cancelled step (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs clashes in a step that commits).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":410},"end":{"line":415},"content_hash":"8d6d2fe68399853a5f606fc3c6d073ef51ee6c198b5668cee2dacfa1c24efd8d"},"suggested_fix":"For the change that moves the endings: in a cancelled step, treat a clash as the `failed` step does (log it, leave that binding's fields out, stop with the rest), so that what the script used is charged and its calls are told.","summary":"A clash in a cancelled script's step abandons the stop: the run is stopped by the next tick with none of the script's fields, records or compute","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T16:52:12.565285Z","id":"813737c3ead93c782337df20efec265dadf56d7054e3c10f288def0d3415e46d","body":{"detail":"Each made alone, with `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all` (464 tests pass with each). (1) script.rs 411, the sessions finished and joined in reverse order: every run has one surface, so nothing tells the order of two sessions' rows or which is blamed for a clash; a run with two bindings would. (2) script.rs 214-215, the size test of `tool_result_content` made on the text without the sessions' fields: the result can then pass `tool_result_bytes` by the bytes of those fields (`\"published\":{\"version\":2},`), and no fixture has a result that close to the limit after a publish (`script.logs-cut-to-fit.json` and the comparison's scenario of logs that do not fit are not within those bytes of it). (3) script.rs 327, a lost claim not checked before the sessions are finished: the commit is refused by the store all the same (`run_not_claimed`), and the tick waits `NotOurs`; no test loses a claim in a script. (4) clax/web.rs 534, a web call whose transaction failed answered `Answer::refused` (the run loop then journals the failure) instead of `Answer::unwritten`: no test makes `Store::spend_journaled` fail (record 79495cc8). (5) script.rs 148, a call past the step's places answered without being told: nothing drives a script to place 1,000,000. (6) surface.rs, `dispatch` journaling the core's own refusal again on a replayed call: `Store::record_bot_call` keeps the row that is there, so nothing changes. Of these only (2) and (4) can reach a person or a model with one surface: (2) as a result a few bytes over its bound, (4) as a failure answered again on every later attempt of a step. The other 66 changes made the same way each fail a test: 20 of the run loop's and 19 of the provider's that the earlier verification made on the code before the seam, 9 in run.rs, and 18 more on the generic step; two more in run.rs that pass are of a stop's audit records (record 5ed5547d).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":185},"end":{"line":231},"content_hash":"e4f7b2bbdc9c4ac4c51c429da8f5e4644dcbaa0efb2991722745d9fddef57f46"},"suggested_fix":"(2): a script that publishes and returns a value sized so that the result with `published` is one byte over the limit and without it under. (4): a test host or store hook that fails one `spend_journaled`, the step crashed and resumed, on whichever answer the owner chooses for 79495cc8. (1) and (5) belong to the change that gives a run a second binding and to the cap on `script.max_calls`.","summary":"Six changes to the generic script step and the seam that pass every test of clax-bot, and what each would take to hold","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:16.696429Z","id":"68302f8af7de5c11a09c77923dd5523239be81f235db5d613a4ab0e7d7a4c3d1","body":{"detail":"Live step_done events carry the step's charge from Store::commit_step; the canonical events rebuilt from committed steps read payload.credits_milli, which the payload never has (the charge is computed at commit), so a client that reconnects sees credits only in run_credits_milli. The store could return the step's charge with the steps (a sum of its usage rows) for the replay.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":399},"end":{"line":410},"content_hash":"34ff05f6f3e85c32427c32135770237712eccad0757f384b6ab5900b617140d8"},"summary":"A replayed step_done carries no credits for its step","supersedes":"ca3998787245b12150aa8aeda83f2044691a20c0ba99cbf17aec33c0cff97ff8","tags":["events","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:16.925597Z","id":"f63d8cbf0ac8346d4b65a74c7f520ca47325d8dd7cb720503eb25831c0f104bb","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_no_cap_or_limit_but_the_policys` includes `run.rs` and `script.rs` as text and fails when either names `settings.max_steps`, `settings.max_run_credits`, `settings.max_run_minutes`, `settings.limits()`, the old constants, `at most 64` or `<< 10`, or declares any `const`; and when `run.rs` or `script.rs` no longer reads the `limits.*` fields. A cap read through another binding (`let s = &self.settings; s.max_steps`) passes it, and a file that code moves to is not looked at. When a script's calls move to `clax/*` and the endings to a provider, add those files to the list. `RunLimits::observe_every` has no test of its effect: an attempt's observed entry replaces its last (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1429-1432) and the final figures are recorded whatever the cadence, so only its default and that `run.rs` reads it are held. Keep reading the tool result's size from `Tick.limits.tool_result_bytes` and the sandbox's limits from `Tick.limits.script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":853},"end":{"line":884},"content_hash":"e710994ec671760b115bfe3aab707c1a067e130d79e947327c9c8d75a4028ac6"},"summary":"The check that the run loop reads no cap of its own is on the text of two files, by name","supersedes":"1836dd088f0c2e4693ba3b845aa166811ee69041ebd31f837fac1a0b8a013bdd","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:17.160348Z","id":"432635154c6c4741aaadae65400db13b88af228ae81c1e1ae5b2101364b3ac65","body":{"detail":"A run's history keeps the blocks its provider produced: Gemini turns hold a `signature` field on text and tool_use blocks (crates/clax-sample/src/messages/gemini.rs), and Chat Completions turns hold thinking blocks with an empty signature (openai.rs). RunSettings is read before every tick, so after an admin switches api to anthropic an open run sends these to the Messages API, which refuses the unknown field and the unsigned thinking; the run ends model_error. Switching between openai and gemini drops or mistranslates nothing fatal.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (fa547d92), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":92},"end":{"line":95},"content_hash":"02de2c3a46cb7f229c289416253894e26660ad5d5d58decf7b59d8237bf0879c"},"suggested_fix":"Record the API a run started on (its row, like its model) and keep it for the run, or strip foreign fields when the Messages API is the target.","summary":"Changing the API while runs are open sends one API's blocks to another","supersedes":"992b91f3f23b0bef51a2453b2bb00061c6b90dd0dce6ac87a0982d6841caab93","tags":["providers","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:17.392374Z","id":"fe3251997ba49a1fbb037aba3defa58b24c61cf85d602e1f0efa72ef9c0d46b6","body":{"detail":"Spec section 8: locally a value in config.toml or the environment overrides the stored one. from_store reads Store::bot_setting and leaves per_person_concurrency, lease and renewal at their defaults; the daemon's SettingsSource must apply HomeConfig::bot (including bot.per_person_concurrency and enabled) on top, re-read per tick.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (2f8fe438), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Reply 67173b9e on the original proposed a close: the server lays [bot] over the database inside the store (Store::set_bot_overrides), fixed in git:bf3458ec.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":159},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"ea413dee711a57207ddc291eb3872d8297bd0a023361d0b7c7a7930cf7dd7878"},"summary":"RunSettings::from_store reads the database only: the [bot] config overrides are the server's to lay over it","supersedes":"11d784a7d996c267b6afb3d0e461eb033154f9252865b8e485764fd6851e12dd","tags":["wiring","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:17.617383Z","id":"969bc09b395720454619b0fd8e0244917452ef750b83e5fed4793e0cc2ca7d17","body":{"detail":"`tools(self.limits.script.max_result_bytes)` puts the sandbox's limit on a returned value in the words the model reads (`policy::bytes_text`: `64 KiB` for a whole number of KiB, else `<n> bytes`). Under the default it is `RunSettings::limits()`, whose `max_result_bytes` is always `Limits::default()`'s 64 KiB, so the text cannot change. A policy that answers another size on a later tick of one run changes a tool's description mid-run: the model was told another number, and the cached prefix is lost. docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:94 calls a declaration that changes during a run a failure. `RunPolicy`'s documentation states the rule (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:338-341); a fix that enforces it stores the limits the model was told with the run's first turn and reads them back.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":606},"end":{"line":624},"content_hash":"3b2367aa813c553eb10e0edf87e1fc8636d7bbea89c603409598e06ccd642d1b"},"summary":"The `run_script` description is built from the policy's answer on every turn, and nothing holds that answer still for a run","supersedes":"255eaa44f458f83bd44240c7c128ecc7182fb11e5dfaaf2028f812067dd22cb3","tags":["for-t9","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:17.842748Z","id":"c14faea8da5f2f22fbf50555235c7a3a1f44340569c9ec686a12dfe17907d848","body":{"detail":"usage_rows stores cost as ceil(qty x rate / base) of an integer rate (charge_in, crates/clax-store/src/credits.rs). For qty <= base the share is hit exactly; above it the rows' costs may sum to slightly less than the billed cost. Exact sums would need the store to take a billed cost per row rather than a rate.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (47f96906), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":803},"end":{"line":873},"content_hash":"c540eaa6b294efa7579537cb080ac1566d4ec86f91dd306da0d9f3122c457fc4"},"summary":"bill() sets an integer micro-USD-per-million rate, so a row of more than a million tokens can fall short of its billed share by under qty / 1 000 000 micro-USD","supersedes":"1faec5e0c1488f74bc3286e703616c654e702dce9fcc95b6b154eeaad68d93f4","tags":["pricing","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:18.067897Z","id":"e69f7a7dbc7706c1f8319d5841c4dbc23c1a605e9d0acdf7fd69f3cca8e6f036","body":{"detail":"Without a rate the commit fails unknown_cost_line (charge_in). Dropping the rows would hide the tokens from the person's usage; a new RateSource needs a store migration (usage_rows.rate_source CHECK). The row charges 0 either way, being waived for an own key.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (123e4f52), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":875},"end":{"line":898},"content_hash":"68d3664e4bfe1d629fe1a91f1b256044aaf9f5db75d72f780f9435cdbc170c94"},"summary":"token_rate gives an own-key turn on a model with no rate a waived zero rate sourced 'default', rather than dropping its token rows or adding a RateSource for it","supersedes":"a1eeefe74d2fe03e1b1b43545497a72bf3194c9d823bd77bfb9e746544cece8a","tags":["pricing","revisit:the store gains a rate source for waived or unpriced rows","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:18.294079Z","id":"fbb1bce3e3b9a8fa14284e199a9a3fa12e58ae99276a71e2b3acf4dbe4dc1bbc","body":{"detail":"With infra_lines on (the Worker), each step charges do.requests = 1 + its host calls and toolpath.storage = the payload's JSON bytes for 30 days. Neither is read from Cloudflare's billing: spec section 13 lists this as a known gap. The payload holds the script's code and outcome, so storage scales with output, not with what the journal keeps.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (6f3e5c2c), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1001},"end":{"line":1020},"content_hash":"209d50573adc6aa561185d99669f36da87ff8d0d43beac8cdb8692b1060c2e1e"},"summary":"Durable Object requests and Toolpath storage are estimated per step","supersedes":"36290fbb3bccc41816464fca62b5e15cfcb05f8b57444153c76979bf7f460e28","tags":["credits","gap","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:18.521995Z","id":"76e37d278388077153da4f5a0c483e13434bb712a5c76f2d073b4602e11f4ba8","body":{"detail":"Each scenario's ticks, run row, steps with payloads, replies, `bot.*` audit records, usage rows and request bodies were printed on this commit and on d1bc352e3 and compared as text: identical in all 17, but for a script's measured times (`cpu_us`, `busy_us`, `wall_us`, and the `script.compute` quantity they give). Which cause wins is unchanged: a cancel over every cap and over exhausted credits; exhausted credits and a withheld flag over the step cap; steps over credits over minutes; a `finish` already asked for over the flag and every cap; a step whose commits failed three times over a cancel (`internal_error`). Also the same: a full window (`context_window`), a refusal for length (`model_error`), a cut stream with little time left (`Waiting(Retry)`), and a run with nothing in its way. The order of `Tick::step` is unchanged for a policy that answers (`policy_error` is `None`): the commit-failure bound, a `finish` or `give_up` asked for, `must_stop` (cancel, refusal, steps, credits, minutes), then the step.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":1073},"end":{"line":1134},"content_hash":"b32eef16d451e8dc9798067f81cc16fd459a9f77332025c232d738ecdbebc13d"},"summary":"Under `DefaultPolicy` a tick leaves what it left at d1bc352e3: 17 scenarios, 12 of them with two or more causes of a stop at once, run on both commits and compared whole","supersedes":"baba8dd478a8e631c846d32c9d3231d20d8676c85fa58cd1eb5203861150c0e1","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:18.744764Z","id":"ab015010025d1e85c0cb59b6670f64187179cdbb10437f7b96e74de9ef050ddb","body":{"detail":"tick reads run_with_steps and History::of parses each payload (assistant blocks, script code and outcome, the first turn's system prompt) to rebuild the messages, so a run of N steps does O(N) work and reads O(total payload) per tick, O(N^2) over the run. Bounded by max_steps (40 by default); a cache of the history keyed by the last committed step would make it O(1).\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (94a4332d), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1093},"end":{"line":1095},"content_hash":"86670f07eb2b225e04ae2e8869d2bb5892139643f951eca6c4956f9345a06496"},"summary":"Every tick rebuilds the whole history from every committed step's payload","supersedes":"51f5801f749bcbf7dba342c14a8ab76159d1ea6bd28107a48f8ea9035cc64156","tags":["perf","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:18.970766Z","id":"a7ee2d9794af062159d9d1922943d0bc1f5dece742b8c4920d208b57eb6908bf","body":{"detail":"The open step is taken out by `position` and `remove`. Three changes here pass every test of `//crates/clax-bot:all`: `rposition`, `swap_remove`, and `steps[i].clone()` (the open step left in). They pass because the three folds filter on `committed_at` themselves and a run has at most one open step, the last (asserted on every tick of the integration suite with a temporary assertion: `steps` all committed, the open step's `n` above the last one's; it held). `StepCx::history` is documented as the run's committed steps (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 606-610) and the proof's adapter relies on it (`history.last()` is the turn that asked for the script, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 614-619). When the tick passes `&self.steps` there, a provider that reads the last step, or counts them, is wrong on a run with a second open step or with the open one left in, and no test says so. Suggested: build `steps` with `retain(|s| s.committed_at.is_some())` after taking the first open one, so the field's documentation (run.rs 1076-1078) is true by construction, and a unit test of that on a list with two open steps.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1095},"end":{"line":1095},"content_hash":"084753e5341f60697fbdc29a18b2db7f4ea34e914a9b1e698c523d833a5a8717"},"summary":"Nothing holds that `Tick::steps` is the committed steps only, which is what `StepCx::history` promises a provider","supersedes":"7c9a3905f1732b8a48521aefa6be644f444df1368edd452048728df2002b0341","tags":["t3","for-t5","for-t8","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:19.196210Z","id":"b80d08e93f13794d143adae7c0a09f2f4e66bedde4ea834588da258c69dd1e11","body":{"detail":"`tick` asks `run_settings` and `limits` after the claim and before `Tick::step`, so before `must_stop` (run.rs:1506-1509) reads a cancel. An `Err` from either is the tick's error: `Driver::settle` logs it, releases the run and tries again after `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (30 s, driver.rs:345-348), for ever. Measured with a policy whose `limits` fails: the tick answers `Err`, the run stays `running` with no step and no notice; a person who stops it gets a run that stays `stopping`, since no tick reaches `must_stop`. An `Err` from `history` in `overflowed` on the stop-reason path (run.rs:2261-2263) comes after the provider answered: the turn stays open, the answer's usage is written as absorbed rows (4 rows in the measurement: the instance's cost, no credits), and the next tick calls the model again with the same full window. While the policy keeps failing that is one absorbed full-window call every 30 s, bounded only by the run's minutes if it has any, and not by the person's credits. No policy fails under the defaults (`DefaultPolicy` answers `Ok`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1096},"end":{"line":1110},"content_hash":"4c690f85ffcedfb7a41ea8a477fd9a0122e80e955f9d4d2164e2805ce3a33a1f"},"summary":"A policy that fails leaves a run running that cannot be cancelled, and after a full window drops an answer the instance paid for, again on every retry","supersedes":"cac7184b575b4d4e37564f63a28423abaa60604b5ad760b7db6f98ce7d1c5ffb","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:19.420629Z","id":"9647b192ba2cb7be8b1f393d3558f9ae9fa273b4f374501bb5ab91fb13c18df7","body":{"detail":"The tick holds three things of the committed steps: `hist: history::Control` (the core's: `next`, `nudged`, `committed`, `last_usage`; the field kept its name so `must_stop`'s cap read at 1356 is unchanged), `clax: clax::Tally` (1075) and `steps` (1078), the steps as read, in order, without the open one. After the script reads (script.rs 217, 554) and the ending reads (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) move to the provider, `Tick::clax` has no reader and goes with its import (run.rs 38); from then on run.rs names no field of the tally. `StepCx::new`'s `history` argument (surface.rs 635) is `&self.steps`. `history.rs` is held free of the surface's nouns by its own test (`history::tests::the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface`, which reads the file's source); run.rs is not, and cannot be until this change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1148},"end":{"line":1155},"content_hash":"2ae6de85c19dd03475f998c6f0194121ddd96a1252b92afaf294ca3888d7216f"},"summary":"For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: `Tick::clax` is the one field of the tick that is Clax's own fold, and `Tick::steps` is what `StepCx::history` borrows","supersedes":"14ecf59970ed1be4f01873ab33fab6e55daa61e74ac3415ba6b7bc03ae4aa6ae","tags":["t3","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:19.647785Z","id":"23d65d691e9eaf1df171a4838fa33cd0857d29d69241d8b9ffa8f5ae8773cd7b","body":{"detail":"`stop_lead` has a notice for each: 'this Clax could not work out what this run may do', 'this Clax could not put together what to send the model', 'the model took longer to answer than this run allows'. The first two end the run `failed`, the third `stopped`. None is reachable under `DefaultPolicy` (no fixture under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden changes), and none is pinned by a fixture; the run view and the HTTP run JSON show `reason` as they show any. When the endings move behind a provider's `ending`, these reasons go with `End::Failed` and `End::Stopped` and the words become the provider's; whoever first ships such a policy pins them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1187},"end":{"line":1204},"content_hash":"f6b17de838d1f7b45a1754a729c6f1ac5fcddbb4315da020dfa2081fc8ffd459"},"summary":"Three endings a person can see only under a policy that is not the default, with words nobody chose: `policy_error`, `policy_history_<n>` and `model_timeout`","supersedes":"14ceea4a1632bfc8e05dc654933448ea88cc2dde9d9127e22affead1f9ae22cc","tags":["for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:19.882670Z","id":"c1ae5826a5abc5ca7508f8ae0097569f420d50bd84f3e6e41b0c46b5474b81f0","body":{"detail":"`must_stop`, `minutes_stop` (run.rs:1318-1324) and the credits stop in `affordable` format `<n> steps`, `<n> credits` and `<n> minutes` from `Tick.limits`, which a policy may answer differently from `RunSettings` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: `limit of 1 steps`, `limit of 5 minutes`). `End::Stopped`, which is to carry the limit's value to a provider's `ending`, takes it from the same place; a run with no cap never produces one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1467},"end":{"line":1490},"content_hash":"871e5b277342c83d4789681df670bedca35eb2161beb7735b4564f49f13cf7df"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings: a cap's value in a notice comes from the policy's limits, not the settings","supersedes":"4c1e8f1e82731d30059d0b2412b31972970093ea388968295e6fb0ca67325711","tags":["for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:20.108679Z","id":"e90edb8f44a99a927577e54edf0eb9e60abbb51ec9c371c8436a7e31f22d19a3","body":{"detail":"`what_i_did` (1374-1396) reads `clax.scripts`, `clax.changed` and `clax.published`; `finish` reads `clax.replied` (1574). They are the only readers of those four `clax::Tally` fields (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/tally.rs 21-37), so when the notices and the posting rule move into the provider's `ending`, it folds `Tally::of(cx.history)` there and these reads go. `scripts` is in the tally although a script step is the core's and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md (\"Where the nouns are read\", Core) lists it with the core's fields: its one reader is the notice's wording (\"I ran 2 scripts\"), and `history::Control` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs 86-99) holds only what the tick decides by. A provider that is not `clax` and wants the count folds it from the steps' kinds. Held: `clax: Tally::default()` at run.rs 1052 fails 25 integration tests, `golden::endings::a_notice_counts_the_scripts_that_ran` among them; 1574 without `replied` fails `golden::endings::finish_posts_no_summary_on_any_thread_when_the_run_replied_on_one`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1493},"end":{"line":1515},"content_hash":"fd90d2fab6f4133c8026975093ec060384b8880c38ff5d1fa161cea5cf6e66bf"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending`: `what_i_did` and the finish rule read `Tick::clax`, and the count of scripts is in the tally, not in the core's fold","supersedes":"38dc74dfbba7144044d9ca40032e91fbc767198fda69faa3f82faf364540fcde","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:20.346472Z","id":"e6464e3085eb823a4ca608059cf6e687b7bd735c48abcffc0595ea9729aaafab","body":{"detail":"(1) Test the run loop's half of `SurfaceProvider::ending` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 1474-1511): an `Err` counted against the step as a failed commit, the bound (`max_commit_failures`), `End::Failed`'s `Err` logged and the step committed, and every binding's `ending` still called when one fails. The provider cases hold only the provider's half. (2) Make the change to the sources' read on purpose: `Store::web_pages` failing in `finish` (1684 here) fails the tick uncounted today, so the step is begun again without end; behind `ending` under that contract it is tried `max_commit_failures` times and the run ends `failed` (surface.rs, the endings table's row for a notice that cannot be posted). Keep the read out of `ending`, or accept the bound. (3) `begin`'s failure is retried uncounted and `ending`'s is counted (surface.rs 1460-1471, 1490-1505): decide whether the two should agree. (4) Build what delivers `ended` again; the contract allows a repeat with the same kind of end and best-effort details (1513-1540), and keeping the `End` in the terminal step's payload would let a redelivery carry all of it (record b25bed99). (5) Count a `FieldClash` against the step, and reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) as surface names in `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42), so a clash cannot be reached by a name (record f2d3e3a6). (6) The adapter's terminal cases prove nothing about notices: its `ending` and `ended` do nothing (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 186-204), so the provider cases pass on it with no notice posted. The real provider's fixture needs a `noticed` probe that reads the notices and a `lifecycle`. (7) A cancelled script's `stopped` step is charged the per-step lines with no call counted (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673-683; 1567-1573 here): keep or change it knowingly.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1559},"end":{"line":1874},"content_hash":"d9487a2baab8ac6eaf475b59fe79440b83b15543166d316908da0d6de1c7b84c"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending` and `ended`: what the seam states and nothing holds yet","supersedes":"8d486d825822a5e3365716c712f0dffec71a41ddfbbc2b330fafe903768ef90a","tags":["seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:20.580626Z","id":"85a58cbbf7fb070cbd357f081b1642ca18313495e9325b95840169919e7c469e","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone, passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`: (1) 1570 as `5` (the requests line of every `stopped` step, a cancelled script's among them, counts five calls: crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 160-164 says it counts none). (2) 1574-1575 without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (a stop writes no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported). (3) 1539 without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record of its own). (1) is billed where `RunSettings::infra_lines` is on (the hosted Worker, crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs 310); crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (219) holds the line for a turn, a script and a `finish` step and has no `stopped` step. (2) and (3) are audit only: `Pins::ending` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 568-592) pins a terminal step's payload, replies, writes, notices and roster, and not the run's audit records. Also unpinned, and seen by no one: a call answered from the journal is logged with `ms` 0 (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 as 7 passes all 189; the goldens replace every `ms` by a name, pins.rs 123).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1576},"end":{"line":1583},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"suggested_fix":"Extend `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` with a run stopped by a cap after a script and a script cancelled after a call, each with its `stopped` step's requests quantity; add `p.audit()` to what one stopped ending and the cancelled ending pin.","summary":"No test holds what a `stopped` step is charged on the requests line, nor its audit records: the count of calls, the `bot.call` records of a stop, and that a notice's journal row gets none","supersedes":"5ed5547d0ca5351abc25a5b10e863788dbc3fd9a8c828861f428aa09472c3316","tags":["review","seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:20.815630Z","id":"f865f85f50495a2ff14397644883bac80fcd2022f45f3c2dcd10f3dd20ac6157","body":{"detail":"`Tick::step` takes a `finish` or `give_up` the last turn asked for before it reads the cancel (`must_stop`), and `Store::begin_step` refuses every kind but `stopped` and `failed` for a run whose status is `stopping` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1225-1230). Measured with the default policy on both commits: a turn that answers `finish`, then `Store::cancel_run`, then a tick by the same driver: the tick answers `Err(a stopping run's next step is its stopped step)`, the run stays `stopping` with one step, and so does a run whose second text-only turn asked for `give_up` (`no_tool_call`). `Driver::settle` tries such a run again every `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (driver.rs), for ever. The window is the time between a turn's commit and the next tick. crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs ('A policy that fails') says a run can always be stopped; this is the case where it cannot, and it does not depend on the policy.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1663},"end":{"line":1683},"content_hash":"8741654dd1167b33d55a1b0b7da4048eef070d3390c83c55f21ee32e67f4216b"},"suggested_fix":"In `Tick::step`, when the run is `stopping` (or the tick's cancel is set), stop it (`Stop::cancelled()`) before a `finish` or `give_up` is taken; or let `begin_step` take a `finish` or `give_up` for a stopping run, if a run that had already asked to finish should finish.","summary":"A run cancelled after its model asked for `finish` or `give_up` cannot take any step: the tick fails with `a stopping run's next step is its stopped step`, on this commit and on d1bc352e3 alike","supersedes":"bcc408ac2c9d07229ab3e5abd9ac0befff1a7649c84b50d76744b44ab9bf74cc","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:21.052568Z","id":"aa103f11cb0a0e7bd80a027a7f34ecca349e3e3953a7f750a182fe79cb7fb58d","body":{"detail":"`Tick::policy_failed` counts with `Store::note_commit_failure`, the counter `Tick::commit` uses, so two failed commits and one policy failure on one step end the run; the reason is then `policy_error` if the policy is failing on that tick and `internal_error` if not (`Tick::step`, run.rs:1622-1633). It calls `begin_on` before it counts, so each failure bumps the open step's `attempt` and emits a `step_started` event with nothing after it, and a step is opened (a `turn` or `script` row) for a run that may then end `failed` in its place. The wait between failures is `Deps.retry_later` (30 s), with no backoff: three failures end a run in a minute, which is short for an environment whose policy reads a store that is briefly away. The policy's error is stored as `payload.error` and shown wherever a step's payload is; a policy that puts a secret in its error leaks it there. A counter and a bound of the policy's own need a column, which this change could not add (the store was not to be touched).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1752},"end":{"line":1772},"content_hash":"6c6514bb0fc2d72dd59b5a9220ae8313dbc71aeb0b2b9250d590663da07248fb"},"summary":"A failing policy shares the step's counter of failed commits, begins the step each time it fails, and its error text is kept in the step","supersedes":"03016140072f004d92493d8b2931e6ac627c3e8915fba0dfcab0a6c067607a48","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:21.289455Z","id":"2e0b25fac243b682a52f79e121045ccad01dc9e61167cdb6dc625422854c73fc","body":{"detail":"`script_step` builds `ClaxProvider::of(self.deps).for_run(self.run.clone())` and its `RunInfo` (`ClaxProvider::info`: kind and request from `Run::request` and `Run::thread_ids`, the binding from `Bindings::clax(artifact_id, web)`), not from `bot_runs.kind`, `request_json` and `bindings_json`, which the store writes and `Run` does not carry. `Deps::surfaces` and `Deps::with_surfaces` replace the run's bindings for a test (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs); nothing else sets them. For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: read the stored bindings, hold one provider for each for the tick (or the driver), and delete `Deps::surfaces`, `ClaxProvider::info`, `ClaxProvider::for_run` and its `row` (a provider made without the row reads it by `Store::run`, one read a step; the tick gives its own so a script step reads the store no more than it did). The clone of the row for each script step goes with them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1723},"end":{"line":1748},"content_hash":"ab64eaf4063fb118906fe138e1e7ab931b9ddf1691f39f6d56a3aae2eacaf054"},"summary":"The tick makes a run's one binding from the row's own columns, a provider for each script step, and takes other surfaces from a hook for tests","supersedes":"ffaee020ee1d0dcfb70a01c859a6e3f382d9aff3fb049c35e96df6e047c34e2a","tags":["t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:21.523275Z","id":"ad443fc1842802f93626ba2ff89f9f0ef6dc144d03b92d5b8f3500a369384cdc","body":{"detail":"Tick::affordable prices the request as its JSON bytes / 4 tokens, the part the last turn cached at the cached-input rate and the rest at the input rate, then caps max_tokens to the output the credits left pay for. A request whose tokens run well above bytes/4 (non-Latin text, dense JSON) can still overshoot the person's credits by its input; counting tokens (the Messages API count_tokens endpoint) would bound it.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (96939966), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Since a9ecb07d1 the uncached rest is priced at the cache-write rate, not the input rate.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1876},"end":{"line":1976},"content_hash":"568435353618718cfb89456697b34d2e6f9bb433e66e9031f7e5eb0c519e7f84"},"summary":"A turn's affordable max_tokens rests on a byte-count estimate of its input","supersedes":"196cea2eaac259563b761f0aaaeceba7c3869be959446749a748ccd0334a02d9","tags":["credits","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:21.760521Z","id":"e0a4c40f67410b0400e49c2b50fc36e851ea951cdcd531ad18715d78af773bef","body":{"detail":"The overflow plumbing is whole for a policy that is a pure function of the core's messages and a level (held by crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs with a fake that drops the oldest tool results). What it does not have:\n\n1. The overflowing answer's blocks stay in the core's projection: `History::of` pushes every turn's assistant blocks (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:626-627), as it does for `pause_turn`, so after a `stop_reason` overflow the next request ends with that partial assistant message unless the policy drops it. `overflowed` rebuilds that projection by hand (`hist.messages`, then the turn's request, then the answer's blocks) because the fold has no way to say what the next tick's projection will be. The projection function should take the committed steps plus one uncommitted turn, so this is computed once.\n2. A policy is given messages, not steps: it cannot tell which step a message came from, so it cannot keep a tool call and its result together by step or summarise by step. The projection should say, for each message or block, the step it came from.\n3. A policy has nowhere to keep a summary: the design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:880-888) lets it send one it keeps, recorded by hash. Here a turn records `payload.shown` (level, bytes, SHA-256) and the overflowing turn `next.overflow.shown_sha256`, which name what was sent but do not hold it, so a run's exact context is rebuilt only by asking the same policy again.\n4. Nothing checks what a policy answers: messages with a tool result and no call, or two of one role, reach the provider, and its refusal stops the run (`model_error`).\n5. Each shorter history changes the cached prefix; the turn after an overflow pays the cache-write rate for all of it, and `affordable` (run.rs:1817) prices it from the last turn's usage, which after a refused request is zero.\n6. `HistoryAsk.level` only grows. A long session that is compacted many times needs the policy to decide what a level means; nothing lowers it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1986},"end":{"line":2032},"content_hash":"9a42d31543d19cfad3106eb94639918e0e14159279b472e2a2841a4190eba3ee"},"summary":"What a real compactor still needs from the run loop, and what the message projection must provide for it","supersedes":"7805554a47cd9f3cd485765ca20affaf97e15bcc536213e9489766c8e83ed097","tags":["for-t3","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:21.989193Z","id":"3bb4a9c526a4a5a4069ecbb170ff17a38b9b17d1d37f13d5ce8723b3adb23e28","body":{"detail":"Today: `Control::of` and `Tally::of` run when the tick is built (1051-1052), `transcript` in `turn` (1746). The tally is read by a script tick (script.rs 217, 554) and a terminal tick (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) and not by a turn, so a turn tick folds it for nothing: one pass that clones a few strings. A `OnceCell<Tally>` on `Tick` would save that pass and cost a `Send`/`Sync` question on wasm32 and native alike (the tick is borrowed across awaits), for a field that leaves the tick when the provider folds its own. Not worth it before then.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":2043},"end":{"line":2047},"content_hash":"ee259796ac8410d3a2e93d3193d750c7263adf945a3f03269d6ff9ba4be0876c"},"summary":"Folding the transcript and the tally lazily, once, behind a cell on the tick, instead of the tally on every tick and the transcript in the turn","supersedes":"85eb393f3adcadfd78e8d1c811e5f6cb61f321e28c01ca8919bc05e1ffb47bdc","tags":["t3","revisit:a profile of a tick shows the tally's pass, or `Tick::clax` outlives the change that moves the endings behind the provider","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:22.224469Z","id":"b9153bfa181aec4a3015792c364a499b0645747af8ed91207f86182431ab8385","body":{"detail":"`overflowed` (run.rs:1911-1948) lets the run go on when the policy's answer to `HistoryFor::Overflow` has fewer bytes than the messages that overflowed, and records `next.overflow.shown_sha256`. The next tick's `turn` asks again with `HistoryFor::NextTurn` at the same level (run.rs:1993-2001) and sends that answer, whatever it is; it is not compared with the recorded hash or size. A policy that answers an overflow with less and the next turn with `Shown::Core` passes the rule each time and is never sent shorter. Measured with such a policy, `max_overflow_retries = None`, `max_steps = None` and a provider that refuses every request as too long: 30 ticks, 30 committed turns, 30 requests with identical messages, the run still `running`, 1 credit charged (step 1's fee). With the default `max_overflow_retries` of 4 the same policy ends after 4 such turns. On the stop-reason path each such call is answered and charged, so only the person's credits end it. The module documentation (policy.rs:32-37, 'cannot make the run loop ask twice') and `RunLimits::max_overflow_retries` ('leaves only the rule that each answer is shorter than the last', policy.rs:118-122) state more than the code holds.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2077},"end":{"line":2113},"content_hash":"d71e81ca81a489902e210bc5af76ee2843deaf8b575c5f2f868bd7926931c317"},"suggested_fix":"In `turn`, when the last committed step's `next.overflow` is set, take the policy's answer only if it is `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` is `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (or at least whose bytes are no more than `shown_bytes`); otherwise end the run as `overflowed` returning `None` does. Then a run's overflows in a row are bounded by its bytes even with no `max_overflow_retries`. Or say in the documentation that the bound is `max_overflow_retries` alone and that `None` trusts the policy.","summary":"The turn after an overflow sends whatever the policy answers then: nothing holds it to the shorter history the overflow's ask answered, so the rule that each history is shorter is kept per ask and not per run","supersedes":"08fb97c68d2eb92af7652dd94028acb6312d913f20ed90d68fe937834316e7b0","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:22.452403Z","id":"fe8455bfacab11a0ae0b22dd4cb9cbc4cd481153f93367d0dae60985897adcb0","body":{"detail":"Only under a policy that shows less (never under the default, whose fixtures in crates/clax-bot/tests/golden are unchanged): a `turn` step with `assistant: []`, `stop_reason: \"prompt_too_long\"`, zero usage and `next.overflow`; a turn after it with `payload.shown`; and a `bot.turn` audit record with that stop reason and zero tokens. It takes a step number, so it counts toward `max_steps`, and step 1's harness fee is charged on it as on any first step. `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:297-316) shows it as a turn with no text and no tool; the run view and the HTTP run JSON were not looked at. Whoever first ships a policy that shows less checks those readers and pins the shapes as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2405},"end":{"line":2411},"content_hash":"cd4b34db76207885c770671ec43566b4c81e00010452727d08cb7b35988523b8"},"summary":"A refused request that goes on is committed as a turn with no answer, a shape no reader of steps has seen","supersedes":"2636f9a88dd39350414c197fcd487b0ae771849b0ec39f92d4fef2715eca0b61","tags":["for-t8","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:22.680878Z","id":"8580a5884a779aa2965bd2e60e74ac63f9e1b960038f2aaae2f8ca313d2e4238","body":{"detail":"A record cannot be moved to another file, so the one on crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs:147:148 (same summary, detail and tags) carries it from here on; answer there.","kind":"comment","references":"7564590a4493710cb151474667f770e3c09df7943ba45796371ac003bf16eaed","summary":"The code this record is on moved to crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs 147-148, and the record is recorded again there with the same text","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:22.681496Z","id":"705d2a6bfdef693a15ea1a5433ea8d13b6752fba7193951616b1ebf4f1b65599","body":{"detail":"(1) Keep the replay check first in the web session: a web call given its entry is answered from it before check 2 (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451; surface.rs row 130); crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` holds the adapter to it. (2) Pin the new `web_disabled` journal row, `{code, message}`, in a journal fixture for a run that did not declare the web and for one on a host with no source (surface.rs 132-144; record 0d124949). (3) Write the divergence check: `Stepper` (seam.rs 532-581) asserts on a diverged replay instead of answering `replay_diverged`, and `dispatch` only asserts in a debug build that the entry it is given is of its call; `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins today's audit of one. (4) Refuse a call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the calls are numbered (458-462 here; `StepCx::place`, `no_place`), which today's code does not. (5) Give the real provider's conformance fixture a `fail_once` and a `disturb`: the adapter's fixture has neither (seam.rs 1836), so the cases for a failing `begin` and `ending` are not played against `clax`, and its read is never disturbed. (6) Pass `CallPlace::attempt` to `OpCtx` (seam.rs `ClaxSession::op_ctx`, 232): the fake host now records the attempt it is given and the proof compares it, and the server's host reads it. (7) Join every session's `tool_result` fields before the result is trimmed (surface.rs 170-175). (8) Say where a surface's standing operations come from: `Binding::from_stored` takes a rule the stored binding does not hold, and the proof hard-codes `clax`'s (seam.rs 81-99; `declared`, crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 253, is private). (9) The audit and usage of a script step are pinned for today's path only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, scripts.rs): `replayed`, `unreported_calls`, the calls a `bot.script` record lists, the requests line; the proof builds none of them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":131},"end":{"line":177},"content_hash":"d54b23bc18c24b23a7a586e1b23f03dd91c52cca3cafeca7a039022ceb2e22bb"},"summary":"For the change that moves this file behind the seam: what crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs and its tests could not do without it","supersedes":"67e0b648578c2cfbe985fa7fbe7dd150274f701ba07a58f1a81bb02d8ef6005f","tags":["seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:22.914337Z","id":"1700fa7df6ecc36e89530f0457205f55b5795c7097f0a4018ee7dbcbb702e74d","body":{"detail":"`Tick::script` gives `Tick::stop` the joined payload (`StepJoin::payload`) as `extra`, which `stop` lays over `reason` and `notice` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs). The `Merged` inside the `StepJoin` is dropped. `merge_fields` (surface.rs) is written for a surface's `ending` fields to join the object its session made in that same stopped step, which needs that `Merged`. When `ending` is called in `stop`, pass the `StepJoin` (or its payload half) instead of a value, and merge `reason` and `notice` as the core's. `stop` also lets `extra` overwrite `reason` or `notice`; the sessions' fields cannot be named so (`PAYLOAD_KEYS` has `reason`; `notice` is not in it, and a `clax` session answering `notice` would replace the stop's).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":416},"end":{"line":450},"content_hash":"28bfc79111b6888af7d82695d6e870cf306dc717b908ac52bb3bb557fe65372d"},"summary":"A cancelled script's sessions' fields reach the stopped step as one JSON value, so the record of which surface made each name ends there","supersedes":"1423b9dd5bbadbbbf67996fa3e5c653cecd1c588c35001b5636a395bd9ea4b82","tags":["t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:23.148643Z","id":"1921358d98ef50a6e98245973f50e12dc17baaf5bb4fce37b1b31d004b479bea","body":{"detail":"`join.add` runs before the cancelled branch, so a provider whose parts clash fails the tick there (`Tick::clashed`) though the script was cancelled. Nothing of the attempt is committed. When the cancel is the run's own (`RunStatus::Stopping`), the next tick's `must_stop` stops it plainly (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, `stop_plain`): the `stopped` step has `reason` and `notice` alone, without `title`, `code`, `outcome`, `calls` or any session's fields; the script's compute is not charged (never twice: the abandoned attempt charged nothing); and the step's journal rows get a `bot.call` record each from `unreported_calls`, with no `bot.script` record. When the cancel was only the tick's token, the next tick runs the script again from its journal. With `clax` alone no clash can happen: its session answers `published`, `replied`, `base`, `changed`, `created` and, to the model, `published`, none of which is in `PAYLOAD_KEYS` or `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS`, and it is the first and only surface joined. No test drives a clash in a cancelled step (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs clashes in a step that commits).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":421},"end":{"line":432},"content_hash":"21cf03ceeb0004bd56a91a2ae0a7f248659f7309c6597bc964aad9f5b1b16759"},"suggested_fix":"For the change that moves the endings: in a cancelled step, treat a clash as the `failed` step does (log it, leave that binding's fields out, stop with the rest), so that what the script used is charged and its calls are told.","summary":"A clash in a cancelled script's step abandons the stop: the run is stopped by the next tick with none of the script's fields, records or compute","supersedes":"712f1b155ac7a82cddeb0c8ed9333b12bb6fc99f695a3c4feb4e03173f5f325a","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:23.378395Z","id":"aa0b8a0516626f5b2d66affd7c29279f3522f8d105f823ebac2c55fdbb8b1976","body":{"detail":"`the_script_step_names_nothing_of_claxs_surface` fails on a comment that says \"thread\" or \"version\" and passes code that reaches Clax's surface by any other word (a helper in another module, a field named otherwise), as the list in crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs did for limits. crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs is the test of the meaning: with `ClaxProvider::begin` and `ClaxProvider::undeclared` changed to panic (in a scratch copy), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration --test_arg=generic::` passes its five tests, so no session of Clax's is opened and no call is answered by Clax's provider on that run's script steps. What of Clax's still runs there: `Tick::script_step` makes a `ClaxProvider` and its `RunInfo` for every script step before it takes the given surfaces (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1718-1722: four `Arc` clones, a clone of the run's row, the `clax` binding built and thrown away); and outside the script step the turn's prompt and request (`prompt::system_prompt`, `BotHost::context`), the roster (`BotHost::working`), `Tick::clax` (the tally) and every ending's notices on the run's threads.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":495},"end":{"line":580},"content_hash":"329d20a67cc5c62fc88eb31f7aa6ca224b4ace3a693da0ef7b0aabcf0264538a"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the word list; keep the three assertions on imports and `deps.host`/`deps.web` if a guard on the file's text is wanted, and let tests/generic.rs say the rest.","summary":"The test that script.rs names nothing of Clax's is a list of eight words searched in the file's text, comments included; what it means to hold is held by the run whose one surface is the fake's","supersedes":"fbb4ff52cd118c24b2d86e0defd7af5c60a4c4a473734c565c4c940814b14830","tags":["review","verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:23.611611Z","id":"fd9a7d74f9b2a6ec59bebd2e2fbfc0778792c84a5c92e9afd404ffeafdbd429c","body":{"detail":"Three tests make a divergence: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` (258) and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 write a journal row for another call at place 0 by hand, and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (273) runs another script for the first attempt (`Another`, 243) whose first call is `files.read` where the stored script's is `files.list`. A script's clock and random numbers are fixed for its step and its first call is made before any answer, so a real second attempt always makes the first attempt's first call: a real divergence is at place 1 or later, after calls that were answered from the journal. What that leaves unpinned: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` (the model is told `completed: []` whatever was replayed) passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`; so does no fixture hold a step whose audit has a `replayed` `bot.call`, a `replay_diverged` one and an unreported row together. The mechanics `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins (a `bot.call` with `attempt` and `seq` for each row the committing attempt did not reach, the first attempt's reply standing) are those of a real run; its `diverged_at: 0` is not. `completed` is what tells the model which calls of the script took effect before the replay stopped, and it is built from the run loop's own log of calls (506-511), which is rewritten when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":202},"end":{"line":210},"content_hash":"e391760fc9a5584d216200be123e59818b2d56d8862ff29f885cfc44500a5f2a"},"suggested_fix":"Give `Another`'s first-attempt script the stored script's first call (`files.list`) and let it differ from the second call on: the fixture then pins `completed: [\"files.list\"]` in the step's result, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows. Add the step's `result` to what the fixture pins.","summary":"Every diverged replay in the tests diverges at call 0, which one script cannot do: `replay.completed` is never pinned with a call in it, and no fixture has replayed, diverged and unreported calls in one step","supersedes":"983fa5d948f7940d0258cb97a9b56830e35939bea98856942ec2a33aac938bce","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:23.846224Z","id":"cbb0c726ae817bf886249afeea6e361c2b49ac2f3b121ce01446c02b487ef0c0","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone to this file, passes all 175 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (the 7 of tests/seam.rs among them): (1) 649 as `if false {`: no `bot.call` record says `replayed`. (2) 685 removed: no `bot.call` record for a journal row the committing attempt did not reach (`unreported_calls`, run.rs 1533-1546). (3) 635 built from `st.calls` instead of `outcome.calls`: the `bot.script` record lists the calls that reached the host and not the sandbox's. (4) 705 as `0`: the `do.requests` line (run.rs 1080) counts no call. For contrast, 648 without the target fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned` and two more, 148 (`Event::Call` without its target) fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned`, 679 without the events fails `cancel::a_cancel_mid_script_charges_what_it_used_and_keeps_no_draft`, and 701 without the compute line fails five tests. The proof in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs builds no audit record and no usage (`Stepper`, seam.rs 471-517), and the resumed fixtures pin the step, the journal and the host's writes only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 50-57). All four are the run loop's own part of a script step (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 151-156, 168-178), which is rewritten around `Answer` and `CallNote` when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":380},"end":{"line":398},"content_hash":"8e2108caceedecce26166f0d7b74d4fd0b2b84bf1b5f40cc6af693beb2859e7a"},"suggested_fix":"Before this file moves: add the run's audit records and the step's usage rows to what crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs pins (a resumed step has replayed calls), pin the audit of a diverged replay whose first attempt went further than its second, pin a script with a call the sandbox refused (`script.error.call-limit.json`'s script) with its `bot.script` record, and assert the `do.requests` quantity of a script step with `infra_lines` on.","summary":"No test of clax-bot holds the `replayed` mark of a `bot.call` record, the `bot.call` records of calls no committed attempt reported, which list the `bot.script` record names, or the call count of the infra usage line","supersedes":"20c0b00f2b538abca60743f35e33331c3c108edd8fe9eced2e3a33fcba80f757","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:24.084581Z","id":"344c09a61b87cbf930686b0c68ad22f5ae752f0df5832b459a84885ba2e44eea","body":{"detail":"The reference is `cfg(test)` of the library, so only the library's unit tests can run it; they include crates/clax-bot/tests/common/mod.rs by path to drive whole runs (`extern crate self as clax_bot`, `test_compile_data` in BUILD.bazel). Linking the reference into the library proper would let crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs compare against it, and the server's and the Worker's tests too, at the cost of the old script step and web performer staying in every build until the last task.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":53},"end":{"line":54},"content_hash":"9fea8c0db2d3318b8fdf74f5ee8148b1989a1ca7212a9b1e78e1d8afc5ea95ec"},"summary":"Running the comparison with the reference from the integration tests, beside the proof","supersedes":"69e968a6b0993154b28f964a043f331a11261d8bec2700202991de9b7c5a03d8","tags":["t5","revisit:the comparison must run against the server's host or on the Worker","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:24.323783Z","id":"31959bf00e6d2b5b118991cf4c2a8b3de116ff72d6b4fa3d2ef4576d39483e8d","body":{"detail":"A row's `args_hash` by value (two homes mint different IDs, so it is compared as which rows share it, and by runs resumed across the two codes); what a script measured (`ms`, CPU, memory, the bytes of a payload) and the credits charged from it (compute and storage are priced at 0 for the comparison); a lost claim, a sandbox that cannot take the script, a store that fails a write, and a call past `NOTICE_SEQ`; and a `FieldClash`, which the reference cannot have. The scenarios run on the fake host: the server's host is compared with nothing.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":359},"end":{"line":393},"content_hash":"4f609f355adb3fda3e4b5201afa7e9bcbc524cd1bb76c83f68cdc1a69a0b1156"},"summary":"What the comparison with the reference does not hold","supersedes":"a7e0541e09bff1e2afbc3456c66cf24fb7760e4c7265aab8586f060624ef3f7d","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:24.555567Z","id":"5141f40de93683e6bdca4afa21227f5668f4f3586d4d484698ae4cddb6ecf5a1","body":{"detail":"Its script calls `clax.db.update(\"notes\", \"a\", {n: 3})` with three arguments. `db.update` takes four (`opts: IfVersion`, crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts 155), so the call is refused `invalid_args` (\"clax.db.update: argument 4 (opts) is missing\"), nothing catches it, and the script ends there on the reference's path and on the seam's alike: the step's result is `{calls: [threads.read, db.get, db.set, db.update (invalid_args)], error: {kind: host_error}}` (read from the stored step of this scenario, printed by a test added to this file and removed again). `db.delete` is called with two arguments and would fail the same way. So what the name says is compared is not: with the reference changed to never refuse a second publish in one script (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs 306 as `if false {`), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test --test_arg=script_differential` passes all four comparisons. The same script with arguments that hold (`{if_version: doc.version}` for the update, the set's version for the delete), crashed before its commit and resumed, does leave the same on both paths and on the two mixed ones, with `second: [\"already_published\", …]`: run as a twenty-first scenario in a scratch copy of this file. The second publish is held for the product elsewhere (`golden::scripts::the_draft_operations_answers_are_pinned`, `seam::a_replayed_host_read_and_a_second_publish_behind_a_session`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1188},"end":{"line":1192},"content_hash":"ade7b24913adff1b70e97e3726ccb82c3bc9de80268aba538aa1f0da55a8fa46"},"suggested_fix":"Give `db.update` and `db.delete` their `opts`, and have each scenario say what its last script's result must hold (here `second[0] == \"already_published\"` and no `error`), so a script that ends early fails its scenario instead of comparing two equal failures.","summary":"The scenario named for host reads, document writes and a second publish ends at its fourth call: neither path ever reaches the document delete, the list, the publish, the second publish, the reply or the collections","supersedes":"f511a373a836e25244a48a1222fb1873f4e8cc0a6e118120474adc15e059ba21","tags":["review","verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:24.789940Z","id":"3e081cf41696fb0dff8c11c6913499d49acfb1e09c41494f8b401e1e6e4538e4","body":{"detail":"Read top to bottom as that change's author. (1) Row 3 (52): a call refused `no_place` is \"unjournaled\"; it does not say whether it is in the step's `calls`, the events and the `bot.call` records, and counted on the requests line, as a `replay_diverged` answer is (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs it). (2) `no_place`'s code is `call_limit` (973), the name of the sandbox's `ErrorKind::CallLimit`, but it is a `HostError` a script can catch, in other words (\"call N is past the 1000000 calls one script may make\") than the sandbox's uncatchable end (\"the script made more than its N host calls\", crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs 1019): one name for two things the model can meet. (3) 138-140 still says a refused web call's row \"is never read by this code (the refusal is made again…)\", which row 130 now contradicts for the declared half: it is read, by `from_entry`. (4) Two rows of `Answer`'s table (793-796) claim a call answered from its entry: `of` (\"any call answered from its entry\") and `written` (\"a call answered from an entry the provider wrote\"). The journal of a replayed call is not read, so either works, but only `written` may carry `charged(n)`; say that the constructor of a replayed answer matters for the charge alone. (5) A `FieldClash` is counted as a failed commit (253-256): not said for the `failed` step itself, where an `ending`'s `Err` is logged and its fields left out (1503-1505), nor whether the other bindings' parts of that attempt are kept. (6) Nothing says which of a provider's answers the core turns into `Event::Call`'s `web` field (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 153): `CallNote::extra` has no named field. (7) `Binding::from_stored`'s rule for `clax` is given as \"`declared` in api.rs, which is private\" (341-343): on branch `claxbot-surfaces` it is `pub(crate)`. (8) Still open in their own records: where another surface's standing operations come from, and who resolves the request at the first turn.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":49},"end":{"line":60},"content_hash":"81612a585e3a5c26464b4f71be5ce0f5c2b146d9ce00cb3da72f409328cfd02c"},"summary":"What the module documentation leaves the change that moves script.rs behind the seam to ask","supersedes":"027c6110ff49c2f22afee1ac8c43f73c35ff09002cd86ae525202e2ee77ba74b","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:25.026252Z","id":"82fe334e7dec7714c02652ffe6fe771c04b5a00afb5ca31c3ff867e94d244027","body":{"detail":"Row 3 has the core check the arguments \"against the surface's file\" and then find the binding (`route`). With one surface that is `api::check_args` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs:215), which reads Clax's own definition; with two, which file to check against is known only once the call is routed, so the order is route, check, `Binding::has`, session. `route` (267-275) answers `None` for a run with no `clax` binding calling an operation with no surface prefix (the module's own test: `route(only_fake, \"files.read\")` is `None`), and the table does not say what the script is answered or whether it is journaled; crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:497 `expect`s. And neither `SurfaceProvider` nor `Binding` leads to a `clax_surface::Definition`: `Binding::hash` is `None` for every stored binding of version 1, and `FakeSurfaceProvider::definition` is an associated function, not a trait method. A run with one fake surface and no `clax` needs the definition for the argument check, for the declaration the model reads and for the sandbox's operation list.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":55},"end":{"line":57},"content_hash":"94bd8511cac826a8622355fb4dde761368b798650c6384f299def3799605b0a6"},"suggested_fix":"Write the order as route, then check against that surface's file, then `has`; say a call no binding takes is answered `unknown_op` and journaled by the core as `Outcome`; and add where a definition comes from (a `definition()` method on the provider, or a registry by `Binding::hash`) before the tick holds providers.","summary":"The step table checks a call's arguments before it finds the binding, says nothing of a call no binding takes, and no part of the seam gives the core a surface's definition","supersedes":"2150012049c01958468bbd7369226bc93f4b476f5123cf539beeb16160c52cf7","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:25.276217Z","id":"26e4dbe046d9ee62861718b633c7302a32d2889c4ffddc50d707e5a29cdb279c","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:521-528 refuses `web_disabled` (`clax.<op> is not available to this run`) in two cases: the run did not declare the operation, or it did and `Deps::web` is `None` (`Some(s) if declared => s, _ => …`). Both journal `{code, message, cost_milli: 0, web}` alone, with no `bot.web` record (`Finish::undeclared`, 149-154, and `write`, 471-479). Behind the seam only the first is `SurfaceProvider::undeclared`'s: in the second the binding has the operation, so `perform` (surface.rs 568-586) hands the call to the session, which must refuse it itself, in the same words, uncharged, uncounted and unaudited, and journal it (`Journal::Written` with today's form, or `Journal::Outcome` with the core's). crates/clax-bot/tests/web.rs:698 (`a_host_without_a_source_refuses_web_disabled`) holds the script's code for that case and nothing of its journal row. The row above (98) says a session's web call is journaled `Written`, always, with a `bot.web` record in `spend_journaled`: this refusal is the exception.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":150},"end":{"line":151},"content_hash":"42ef63a3af7dff3456985e00ee6c79168132eb1211fbb0f4e5f5dac678d8f792"},"suggested_fix":"Say in row 99 that it is the undeclared half, and add the no-source half to row 98: refused by the session before check 3, journaled without a `bot.web` record. Whatever is decided for the undeclared row's form (record 0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e), decide the same for this one, so one message does not leave two row forms.","summary":"The table's row for an undeclared web operation covers half of script/web.rs 515-528: a declared operation on a host with no web source takes the same branch and is the session's","supersedes":"7d04f0095704fa5edfeff8956cbbe39a4bed4a4555cf5842ff6b714be3495963","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:25.536452Z","id":"9e196b6392b39c663b446e33b951d4f1cb59da6e4f82283923cd16237fe74657","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451 answers any web call whose place is in the journal from its entry (`from_entry`), before `web_fresh` runs check 2 (512-529). For row 125 (the run did not declare the operation) making the refusal again is the same answer: `Run::web` does not change. For row 126 (declared, `Deps::web` is `None`) it is the same only while the host still has no source. A first attempt on a host with no source journals `web_disabled`; when the step is run again on a host that has one, today's code answers `web_disabled` from the entry, and a session written as row 126 says (check 2 made again, the entry not read) passes check 2 and runs checks 3 to 9, reaching the source for a fetch, before `Store::spend_journaled` (web.rs 497) finds the place taken. The adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs (421) cannot show this: it has no source on any attempt.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":150},"end":{"line":151},"content_hash":"42ef63a3af7dff3456985e00ee6c79168132eb1211fbb0f4e5f5dac678d8f792"},"suggested_fix":"Row 126, Replayed: answered from the entry, as every web call the session is given with one is (web.rs 449-451); `from_entry` reads the core's `{code, message}` row as it reads today's (305-306 default the two keys). Keep `if let Some(e) = replay` first when `script/web.rs` moves into the session.","summary":"The two `web_disabled` rows say a replayed call is answered again without its entry being read; today's code reads the entry first, and for the declared half the two differ when the host gains a web source between attempts","supersedes":"41ee8d5502ae029d59b32f2ce0686b5b14d12d7ef4395a4edcdba40265bae284","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:25.805424Z","id":"e0236ad44637140e869b5dbd5720922378bf5072502f00454e614820acf34468","body":{"detail":"The constant in crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs became a policy value (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:110, default `DEFAULT_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, the same 100 KiB). The change that moves the tool result's building behind the seam reads it from the tick's limits and corrects this sentence.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":212},"end":{"line":215},"content_hash":"f2c8378f4257c509c46f211aa8c3b0b7ed506e76ffe70058cd94ec789bf74ea5"},"summary":"This line names `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, which is gone: the size is `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`","supersedes":"77c3d58e519a128a834099aae1c4cc53f9ea3dd3275d4301d323b3996edb5c71","tags":["for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:26.095032Z","id":"a878f820543320de6f0dbeb2dc7182289e9686328aa9b9455c48a341465eeb23","body":{"detail":"The split removed run.rs 533-653 (the `History` section) and added 12 lines further down, so what the tables call 1681-1719 (`finish`) is at 1572-1610, 1549-1590 (`stop`) at 1440-1481, 1451-1480 (`must_stop`) at 1342-1371, 1483-1504 (`what_i_did`) at 1374-1396, 1509-1529 (`notify_at`) at 1405-1420, and so on through the endings table (207-216) and wherever else the file cites run.rs. The two citations of the fold itself were corrected to name `clax::Tally` (surface.rs 123, 213). The plan's measurements (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md) and the open records on run.rs cite the old lines too (`qualifier review crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs` lists the drifted ones). The change that edits run.rs next for the endings will move them again; recite then, or cite by function name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":246},"end":{"line":255},"content_hash":"1431e72efff9ae47e59e0226c316ba52cfcee6f534e1087d404bf7d3eca4681e"},"summary":"The module documentation cites run.rs by line, and every citation past run.rs 533 is now about 110 lines too high","supersedes":"cd2a3d2bc1b5fdc4eb8294288313d5cb907abf7eaff6dbf36145eda48f171200","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:26.404768Z","id":"3c2d69c93d6a00d036f042a76c8ca1964489fe58c0a406dc7e02d8d3ebd7d0bc","body":{"detail":"Clax's web operations write their journal row, their charge and their `bot.web` record in one transaction (`Store::spend_journaled`). When the entry cannot be made or the transaction fails, nothing was written; the code before the seam answered the script the failure and journaled nothing, so the step run again makes the call again. The seam had no answer for that: `refused` makes the core journal the failure, and `written` said the place has its row. `Answer::unwritten(e)` says it (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs, `unwritten`; the module documentation, \"Who journals a call\"). The other choice: answer `Answer::refused`, which journals the failure, so a store that failed once answers that call the same failure on every later attempt of the step instead of trying again; one constructor fewer, and a change in what a resumed run does after a failed write. No test reaches either (mutating one to the other passes every test).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":968},"end":{"line":969},"content_hash":"6a1d163bf5d2ef9bd84c336fa4d8564736fe9ff19283aa701fd6e2f5c7274b13"},"summary":"A failure that neither side journals is now a constructor of the seam, `Answer::unwritten`, for the owner to keep or drop","supersedes":"79495cc8e59069f56399fb493646f3f4ad1e4925f5303b3cdaa964cb0d7535d8","tags":["t5","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:26.714134Z","id":"06a96d62b78e3f5c3290bde0529f5f562f840acbb3f318a51b6cce4573e8de00","body":{"detail":"`Binding::from_stored` builds a run's operations from the surface's file, the stored grant and this rule each time a script step is run (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs 102-116, called from `Tick::script_step`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1718-1745). The rule has no kind: a surface whose kinds have different operations (the fake's file gives its kind an `@allow` list, crates/clax-bot/src/fake.d.ts) cannot say so. And the rule is read from the running build, not from what the run was bound with: a binding keeps the hash of its surface's definition (`Binding::hash`) so that a run keeps the surface it was made with, but a build whose provider answers `standing` otherwise gives a run made before it other operations on its next step. For `clax` both are harmless today (one rule, in `api::declared`). For the change that reads `bindings_json`: either store a binding's whole set of operations when the run is made (then nothing asks the rule again, and `standing` goes), or take the rule from the kind's `@allow` list in the definition the binding's hash names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1743},"end":{"line":1750},"content_hash":"1cbf1004aead22a54a08156e5af92245b149741e90f805f06f1793aa4d050399"},"summary":"`SurfaceProvider::standing` is asked of the provider's code, per operation and for every run alike: it cannot say what differs by kind, and a provider that changes it changes what runs already made may call","supersedes":"de5b2bc3d03fbc1ac53df8eb1f42693395a84a4f16e67efaeb0b696f7b6edab1","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:56.524078Z","id":"6a474bd7feeb2d3d41623948ee863d51cb8c9cf47a50053e5a0a4d1ced40f185","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_each_bound_and_size_from_the_policys_limits` no longer bans strings or constants. `a_run_with_no_bound_goes_far_past_every_default` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:593) gives a run 5,000 committed turns and 100,000 credits spent by writing them to the test's database (`seed_a_long_run`, :554), advances the clock a year, and under `RunLimits::unbounded` with the sizes raised to 1 MiB sees it take a turn, run a script whose result is over 300 KiB, and finish. A literal `committed >= 1000` added to `must_stop` fails it. Driving the steps was measured first: 1,100 real ticks took 17 s, so they are written, which couples the test to the columns of `bot_steps` and `bot_runs.credits_milli` (as crates/clax-bot/tests/driver.rs is coupled to `artifacts`). What it does not see: a bound above those figures, one on something it does not grow (the bytes of a history, a script's calls), or one in a path it does not take (a stop, a give-up).","kind":"comment","references":"f63d8cbf0ac8346d4b65a74c7f520ca47325d8dd7cb720503eb25831c0f104bb","span":{"start":{"line":853},"end":{"line":884},"content_hash":"e710994ec671760b115bfe3aab707c1a067e130d79e947327c9c8d75a4028ac6"},"summary":"Done as recommended: the test keeps only 'reads `limits.*`', and a run holds that the run loop has no bound of its own, by figures written to the database","supersedes":"2f4b587981379e0eeb9e5b7f98a8876dc798862dd39cb2ad8404d317d7fe88d6","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:56.864913Z","id":"3dfac3f4ae89dfd4bdb939cb54f880ce395071078954dedf7f6faf06bb7ad655","body":{"detail":"`tick` reads all of a run's steps (`Store::run_with_steps`, run.rs:1064) and folds `Control` and `clax::Tally` over them on every tick; `transcript` is folded by a tick that takes a turn, before the policy is asked (run.rs:1975). A policy shortens what is sent, not what is loaded: measured in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs, a run of 5,000 written steps ticks, and 1,100 driven ones took 17 s in all. So a run of any length is bounded by the memory and time of what ticks it (the Worker's isolate first). crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs, 'Bounds that are not a policy's', says so. Lifting it means the tick reading a suffix of the steps and a stored fold of the rest, which is the tick's to do when it holds a `RunInfo` and its providers.","kind":"comment","references":"ab015010025d1e85c0cb59b6670f64187179cdbb10437f7b96e74de9ef050ddb","span":{"start":{"line":1093},"end":{"line":1095},"content_hash":"86670f07eb2b225e04ae2e8869d2bb5892139643f951eca6c4956f9345a06496"},"summary":"This is the one bound on a run's length that no policy can lift: every tick loads every step, whatever is shown of them","supersedes":"ca5316de317db7dca07b1a2860b0bcf8cc951f2235d55d1274a7d661c2a2238e","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:57.214959Z","id":"122f7300c7af2c31d9594dde71408ef938ba194e350775c4ca33666ab9caa1a4","body":{"detail":"`tick` keeps the error and builds the `Tick` on the instance's settings and `RunLimits::of` them (run.rs:1067-1081). `Tick::step` then takes a `finish` or `give_up` the model already asked for, reads the cancel and the refusals (`must_stop`, which applies no cap while the policy's limits are unknown), and only then answers the error: `Tick::policy_failed` (run.rs:1687) begins the step, counts one failure on it with `Store::note_commit_failure` (the counter a failed commit uses, bounded by `RunSettings::max_commit_failures`, 3 by default), and waits `Deps.retry_later`; at the bound the run ends `failed` with reason `policy_error`, the policy's error in `payload.error`, and a notice on its threads. An `Err` from `history` before a model call takes the same path. An `Err` from `history` when asked on an overflow is 'no shorter history': the overflowing turn is committed with its usage and the run ends as it does under the defaults, so nothing paid is discarded and the model is not called again. Tests in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: `a_policy_that_keeps_failing_ends_the_run_failed_at_the_bound` (settings, limits and history each: wait, wait, failed; no request; the instance's cap of 0 steps stops nothing), `a_run_whose_policy_fails_is_still_cancelled`, `a_policy_that_recovers_lets_the_run_go_on`, `a_finish_the_model_asked_for_is_taken_though_the_policy_fails`, `a_policy_that_fails_on_an_overflow_ends_the_run_with_the_answer_kept` (one request, 4 charged token rows, `context_window`; `model_error` on the refused path). Mutations that fail them: the error dropped in `tick`; the bound off by two; the cap check left on while the policy fails. Proposed close: fixed in cad03c26b.","kind":"comment","references":"b80d08e93f13794d143adae7c0a09f2f4e66bedde4ea834588da258c69dd1e11","span":{"start":{"line":1096},"end":{"line":1110},"content_hash":"4c690f85ffcedfb7a41ea8a477fd9a0122e80e955f9d4d2164e2805ce3a33a1f"},"summary":"A policy's error is no longer the tick's: the cancel is read first, the failure is counted against the step as a failed commit is, and the run ends `failed` (`policy_error`) at the bound; an error on an overflow keeps the paid answer","supersedes":"a9510f0066d886ae21dfd64299eb7ebc2d1d0e1763adb5bd6017475f43611dd7","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:57.562375Z","id":"97d76fe100d4a3992be9eb7d0dd80032bf8a0e6893f20cc1f82946eb052abc06","body":{"detail":"(a) Records b25bed99 and f2d3e3a6, cited in (4) and (5), were re-recorded at their lines and are now a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c and 68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10 (the same threads). (b) Two audit-only behaviours of a stop pass every test when changed (record d016f728): `stop` without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (1574-1575 here: no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported), and `unreported_calls` without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (1539: every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record). No ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); pin `p.audit()` for one stopped ending and the cancelled one before these lines move. With several bindings the second matters more: the places of every binding's notices are at or above `NOTICE_SEQ` (`StepCx::notice`), and none should be audited as a script's call. (c) A `stopped` step's requests line is now held (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 249, 287): 1, whatever calls the script made. (d) To test the clash rule in a run loop, a provider is needed that answers a field named `calls` (or another of `PAYLOAD_KEYS`) from `finish` or `ending`: `FakeSurfaceProvider` answers only `fake_set` and `fake_notice`, so give it a way to answer a field a test names. The rule to hold: the attempt counted as a failed commit, the run ended `failed` at the bound, and in the `failed` step the clashing binding's fields left out and the others kept (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, `merge_fields`). `merge_fields` now takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for the step.","kind":"comment","references":"e6464e3085eb823a4ca608059cf6e687b7bd735c48abcffc0595ea9729aaafab","span":{"start":{"line":1559},"end":{"line":1874},"content_hash":"d9487a2baab8ac6eaf475b59fe79440b83b15543166d316908da0d6de1c7b84c"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, and three things about a stop's audit and a clash that no test holds","supersedes":"711e7b549a1dab006021b9e553ca12a753e3341892927e1f3c1b0fe4fa23767c","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:57.918749Z","id":"f390377d15c948feb95d1c84b715f2fb978b76628b1abe3e11f297c7ab82be83","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, with `infra_lines` on: `a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call` (249: `max_steps` 2, a turn, a script of five calls, then the cap: the requests line is 1, 6, 1) and `a_cancelled_scripts_stopped_step_counts_no_call_on_its_requests_line` (287: a script cancelled once its second call is answered: 1 for the turn and 1 for the script's step committed as `stopped`). With line 1570 as `5`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails both and passes without the change. Not done: (2) a stop without `unreported_calls` and (3) a notice's row getting a `bot.call` record still pass every test, since no ending fixture pins the run's audit records (`Pins::ending`, crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs); nor is a replayed call's `ms` of 0 pinned (the goldens replace every `ms` by a name). Adding `p.audit()` to an ending fixture changes bytes that are pinned, which this branch was not to do; both are in the reply on record 264e99a6.","kind":"comment","references":"85a58cbbf7fb070cbd357f081b1642ca18313495e9325b95840169919e7c469e","span":{"start":{"line":1576},"end":{"line":1583},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"summary":"A `stopped` step's requests line is held for a cap stop after a script and for a script cancelled after two calls; the two audit-only changes are still unpinned and are passed to the change that moves the endings","supersedes":"9edf1df3ea36aa0a90b0a8ba224f3317fa594890367ef8373b118dc6851f38c7","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:58.291573Z","id":"9d4e5dbc4520f497939b9d3e48b84846f115b1ac1b510c0aa3896325415df340","body":{"detail":"`golden::resumed::a_stopped_steps_requests_line_counts_no_call` and `a_cancelled_scripts_stopped_step_counts_no_call_on_its_requests_line` fail with the change. Read against the code: a turn charges `1 + 0` (`step_usage`, 1080), a script step `1 + st.calls.len()` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 702-708: 6 for five calls), a cap stop is a new step charged `1 + 0` here (1567-1573), which gives 1, 6, 1; a cancelled script's step is begun again as `stopped` under the same number (1558) and charged here with no call counted, which gives 1 for the turn and 1 for step 2. Still passing every test when changed, and audit only: 1574-1575 without `unreported_calls`, 1539 without the `NOTICE_SEQ` filter; and crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 (`ms` of a replayed call).","kind":"comment","references":"85a58cbbf7fb070cbd357f081b1642ca18313495e9325b95840169919e7c469e","span":{"start":{"line":1576},"end":{"line":1583},"content_hash":"73cb3bc82e271ca4fb590934a71e35719b0aa0ac35b8da64656e0f6a2b71d177"},"summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: line 1570 as `5` fails both new tests, and their quantities are what this file charges","supersedes":"e458c75676b7fd998f94f76011a3deca94fcb0c8305a95f9d0fb368eca49d4eb","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:58.629318Z","id":"d6f74b21759a7aae405049417654b3711658f1e1568caf847f66594bbf9d9901","body":{"detail":"`Tick::step` reads `run.status == Stopping` before the terminal steps (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1636-1641) and stops as any cancelled run does: a `stopped` step with reason `cancelled`, no notice, and nothing posted of the summary or reason the model meant to send. No store change was needed: `Store::cancel_run` sets `stopping` for a queued or running run, and `Store::begin_step_on` allows a stopping run exactly a `stopped` or `failed` step, which is the one now taken. The driver's cancel token alone (with the run not yet `stopping`) does not take this path; the step the model asked for is then taken, as before. `a_cancel_after_the_model_asked_to_end_the_run_stops_it` (crates/clax-bot/tests/cancel.rs) cancels after a turn whose `next` is `finish` and one whose `next` is `give_up`: each ends `stopped`/`cancelled` in one tick, payload `{reason: cancelled, notice: null}`, no reply. With the check replaced by `false` it fails. No fixture under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden changed. This is the one change on this branch that a person on a Clax with the default policy can see. Proposed close: fixed in 8a191a61f.","kind":"comment","references":"f865f85f50495a2ff14397644883bac80fcd2022f45f3c2dcd10f3dd20ac6157","span":{"start":{"line":1663},"end":{"line":1683},"content_hash":"8741654dd1167b33d55a1b0b7da4048eef070d3390c83c55f21ee32e67f4216b"},"summary":"Fixed in clax-bot alone: a stopping run takes its `stopped` step before a `finish` or `give_up` the model asked for. A person now sees such a run stop where it never ended","supersedes":"6863712f906ca3f501a5579b73ff721248aba45bf696051a6d5378a4e64de201","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:58.996687Z","id":"0705c9fa2f432ac0dddfd2c07f6040212958d04b8888a25dcfc92e0bb8b62a31","body":{"detail":"Done: `overflowed` builds the core's projection from `history::transcript(&self.steps)` (item 1's by-hand rebuild is one call and two pushes); a policy's messages are checked (`policy::check_shown`, item 4), with the cut-off answer allowed to be left out of the end (item 1's partial assistant message); a turn on a policy's messages is priced with no cached prefix (item 5); the turn after an overflow is held to the overflow's answer by its hash. Still open: a policy is given messages and not steps (2); it has nowhere to keep a summary, and `payload.shown` names what was sent without holding it (3); `level` only grows (6); an overflowing turn's `next` carries no nudge, so a nudged model is nudged again (7 of reply 70a8db39), now stated in crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs; and every tick loads every step (8, record 06b231ef's thread). New with the check: invariant 2 binds a policy, so a first request that alone overflows the window cannot be shortened and ends the run (`model_error`).","kind":"comment","references":"e0a4c40f67410b0400e49c2b50fc36e851ea951cdcd531ad18715d78af773bef","span":{"start":{"line":1986},"end":{"line":2032},"content_hash":"9a42d31543d19cfad3106eb94639918e0e14159279b472e2a2841a4190eba3ee"},"summary":"With the fold split: what of this list is done, and what is still a compactor's to meet","supersedes":"bf9d4ae46ac6e0db3de2e9d8bd4b48e5266ca126be5852d9f3e84e2fa2fba3ec","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:59.347462Z","id":"a213a2daee47aedcee3eefd38e677a24fb09163790b205656e663272edf4405b","body":{"detail":"In `turn`, when the last committed turn's `next` has an `overflow`, the policy's answer must be `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` equals `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (run.rs:2010-2043); the core's projection, or other messages, end the run by `Tick::overflow_ending` (run.rs:1726): `give_up`/`context_window` after the stop reason, `stopped`/`model_error` with the stored provider error after a refusal. So each request after an overflow is shorter than the one that overflowed whatever the policy answers at either ask, and `max_overflow_retries = None` ends. `the_turn_after_an_overflow_is_held_to_the_shorter_history` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) runs the policy of this record (less on the overflow, the core's projection after) with `max_overflow_retries = None` against a provider that refuses every request: steps turn, script, turn, stopped; 2 requests; 4 scripted refusals unused. It runs the same on the stop-reason path, and a policy that answers other messages after the overflow. Mutations, each failing that test: the hash comparison replaced by `is_null()`; the ending for `Shown::Core` after an overflow removed. The module documentation and `max_overflow_retries`' say what the code holds (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs, 'A full context window'). Proposed close: fixed in cad03c26b, with the test for other messages in 72a10983b.","kind":"comment","references":"b9153bfa181aec4a3015792c364a499b0645747af8ed91207f86182431ab8385","span":{"start":{"line":2077},"end":{"line":2113},"content_hash":"d71e81ca81a489902e210bc5af76ee2843deaf8b575c5f2f868bd7926931c317"},"summary":"The turn after an overflow is now held to the shorter history: it is sent messages whose SHA-256 is the overflow's `shown_sha256`, or the run ends","supersedes":"ba91b7d70fd69889dda122fcf9e704fa712a0d3deec9dc27133a40cc8468b690","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:14:59.691289Z","id":"a190d6bc780b53ea3603e3e7cc0752fef1928a9938118268814a157db25eb6dd","body":{"detail":"(a) Record 0d124949, cited in (2), was re-recorded at its lines and is now 964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb (same thread, still `status:needs-decision`). (b) Line references in this record are of the seam before commit 30c3673b2; the items are found by name: `no_place` and `StepCx::place` for (4), \"What the rows above do not show\" for (7), `Binding::from_stored` for (8). (c) For (3): `audit.replay-diverged.json` now pins a replay that diverges at its second call, with what the model is told (`replay.completed`), a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows; the divergence check written behind the seam must leave that fixture as it is. A refusal for `replay_diverged` or `no_place` is told in the step's `calls`, the events and a `bot.call` record and counted on the requests line, and is not journaled (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs row 3). (d) A replayed call is logged with `ms` 0 (477-481 here): no test holds it, since the goldens replace every `ms` by a name (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 123); pin it, or keep it by reading. (e) `merge_fields` takes a `Merged` the caller keeps for a step's payload and another for what its model is told: one of each for each step, made before the first session's parts are merged. (f) The real provider's conformance fixture now has to say of `disturb` and `lifecycle`, as of `staged` and `fail_once`, that it has one or why not (`Maybe`), and the caller names every case not played.","kind":"comment","references":"705d2a6bfdef693a15ea1a5433ea8d13b6752fba7193951616b1ebf4f1b65599","span":{"start":{"line":131},"end":{"line":177},"content_hash":"d54b23bc18c24b23a7a586e1b23f03dd91c52cca3cafeca7a039022ceb2e22bb"},"summary":"Corrections and additions to this list: the records it cites by their first IDs, `merge_fields`' new argument, and two more things to carry","supersedes":"337f324df19cad4267fdc33ec363b56bbc44323b6521fae4408aae69f1cdad08","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:00.033280Z","id":"177481aca2b9b1069509565d6e8ffb157b2d324bfaf5cb4e952b2050fe5d5c74","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (364): the first attempt's script (through `Another`) now makes the stored script's first call, `files.list`, then `files.read`, `threads.reply` and `artifact.get`; the second attempt is answered `files.list` again and diverges at call 1. The fixture gained `result` (the step's `tool_result`, parsed): `replay: {completed: [\"files.list\"], diverged_at: 1, why}`. Its audit now has a `bot.call` that says `replayed` (`files.list`), the diverged `threads.reply` (`ok: false`), and a record with `attempt: 1` and `seq` for each of rows 2 and 3. Only that fixture was regenerated (`git diff --numstat 9da7cb463 HEAD -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden`: audit.replay-diverged.json 82 added, 44 removed; no other fixture). With line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)`, `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` fails that test and passes without the change. `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 still diverge at call 0 by a hand-written row; they are left as they were.","kind":"comment","references":"fd9a7d74f9b2a6ec59bebd2e2fbfc0778792c84a5c92e9afd404ffeafdbd429c","span":{"start":{"line":202},"end":{"line":210},"content_hash":"e391760fc9a5584d216200be123e59818b2d56d8862ff29f885cfc44500a5f2a"},"summary":"`audit.replay-diverged.json` is now of a replay that diverges at its second call and pins what the model is told: `completed: [\"files.list\"]`, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows in one step","supersedes":"50b70258ea1b41cb26991b2d4d741b95bca0fc04e94e588692288b49a62b6318","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:00.381736Z","id":"6fcc64d20e8917d1baf364843833b37095f9ad052a37b6a2fc5a25440fd0dbd7","body":{"detail":"`bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (191 tests) with the change fails `golden::resumed::the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`. The fixture's diff from 9da7cb463: the first attempt's rows are `files.list`, `files.read`, `threads.reply`, `artifact.get` at places 0 to 3 (the reply's key moves from place 1 to 2); the committing attempt's `files.list` is `ok` and `replayed`; the divergence is at call 1 (`threads.reply` where the journal has `files.read`); the unreported rows are places 2 and 3; and `result` is new. No other fixture changed. A divergence after a call answered again is what a run can produce; the two older tests that write a row at place 0 by hand (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs 258, crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200) still show one it cannot.","kind":"comment","references":"fd9a7d74f9b2a6ec59bebd2e2fbfc0778792c84a5c92e9afd404ffeafdbd429c","span":{"start":{"line":202},"end":{"line":210},"content_hash":"e391760fc9a5584d216200be123e59818b2d56d8862ff29f885cfc44500a5f2a"},"summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` now fails the fixture's test, and every change to `audit.replay-diverged.json` follows from the first attempt's new order of calls","supersedes":"7e8f6d59a8ca3da9d3e644b7ad4085250bd52816303a853036344363e505589a","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:00.722427Z","id":"07c1ae862ee4b1d85314c6c9c820e365b8c4171cf994dd6168b672a6cd959747","body":{"detail":"(1) `replayed`: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `left` (67) now pins the run's audit records, the drafts given to publish and the usage rows beside the step, journal and writes, and `pin_resumed` (100) requires a resumed step to have `bot.call` records that say `replayed` and an uncrashed one to have none. `resumed.publish.json` and `resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json` gained lines and lost none (`git diff --numstat 2230ac3c9 HEAD -- crates/clax-bot/tests/golden`: 406 and 478 added, 0 deleted). script.rs 649 as `if false` fails four tests of `golden::resumed`. (2) `unreported_calls`: `audit.replay-diverged.json` (resumed.rs 273) is a run whose first attempt made four calls and died before its commit, and whose second diverges at call 0; it pins a `bot.call` record with `attempt` and `seq` for each of the three rows the second attempt did not reach. The first attempt's other path comes from a sandbox that runs another script than the one stored (`Another`, 243), since a script is deterministic across attempts. script.rs 685 removed fails that test. (3) which calls the `bot.script` record lists: `audit.script.call-limit.json` pins the record of the call-limit script, as this record asked, but the sandbox does not count the call over its limit, so that pin does not tell the two lists apart; `audit.script.call-not-awaited.json` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs 194) does: a call the script did not await is in the record's `calls` and in what the model is told (`script_finished`), with no `bot.call` record, journal row or place in the step's `calls`. script.rs 635 built from `st.calls` fails that test. (4) `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (resumed.rs 219), with `infra_lines` on: the `do.requests` line is 1 for a turn and 6 for a script of five calls. script.rs 705 as `0` fails it. Each change was made alone and reverted; `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests) passes without it.","kind":"comment","references":"cbb0c726ae817bf886249afeea6e361c2b49ac2f3b121ce01446c02b487ef0c0","span":{"start":{"line":380},"end":{"line":398},"content_hash":"8e2108caceedecce26166f0d7b74d4fd0b2b84bf1b5f40cc6af693beb2859e7a"},"summary":"All four are pinned for today's path; each of the four changes now fails a test","supersedes":"6ef44e052a89d285e23fbf01a5b45a0257ad038c8578ac511793e54cb330674f","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:01.056288Z","id":"4891a2b54ef13af2f4e73a2529a57ff3212dd9b0eb41c351b8723c6a2c2ebe7c","body":{"detail":"Against `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests): 649 as `if false` fails four tests of `golden::resumed`; 685 removed fails `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned`; 635 from `st.calls` fails `golden::scripts::a_call_that_was_not_awaited_is_in_the_scripts_record_alone`; 705 as `0` fails `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls`. `resumed.publish.json` and `resumed.rebase-publish-fetch.json` at 2230ac3c9 and at 3b700b895, parsed and compared: every key and value of the old file is in the new one unchanged, and the new ones add `audit`, `drafts_published` and `usage`. What is still unpinned of a script step and a stop is in the records on crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 505-513 and crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1566-1573.","kind":"comment","references":"cbb0c726ae817bf886249afeea6e361c2b49ac2f3b121ce01446c02b487ef0c0","span":{"start":{"line":380},"end":{"line":398},"content_hash":"8e2108caceedecce26166f0d7b74d4fd0b2b84bf1b5f40cc6af693beb2859e7a"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: each of the four changes now fails a test; the two fixtures that existed gained keys and lost or changed none","supersedes":"5769ee458939ea7bc7223e4a322d15370b7574ee0c2fa2809bdac95b6bbcfb2f","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:01.334500Z","id":"4b2c9b9e0522b1267112247a92ec80431ec0ed53e793d99852897d768b1cb49b","body":{"detail":"Every crashed scenario's first driver runs its whole script (or another whole script) and dies before the step's commit, so no scenario resumes a step whose journal holds only the first calls of its script, and none dies after a commit or in a cancelled step. Run in a scratch copy of this file with the same `Left` and `same`: (1) the driver dies after the draft calls and before the publish; (2) after the publish's row and before the reply; (3) after a search and a fetch were charged, the rest made on resume (a window from the page store, a refused fetch); (4) after three refused writes were journaled, then a reply, a rebase and a publish made on resume; (5) a brief's run, after `artifacts.create`, the second create refused on resume; (6) the scenario of record on lines 912-928 with arguments that hold; (7) a rebase recorded, someone publishes version 3 before the resume: the rebase goes to version 2 again and the publish answers `conflict`; (8) a `db.get` recorded, the document changed before the resume: the first read answers the recorded value and a second the new one; (9) the driver dies after the script step's commit (`CrashPoint::AfterCommit`); (10) a script cancelled once it returned, the driver dies before the `stopped` step's commit (`CrashPoint::BeforeCommit` of kind `Stopped`), and a driver told of no cancel runs the step again from its journal and commits it as a script step; (11) failures of draft operations, replayed. Not driven, by reading: a call past `NOTICE_SEQ` (the reference has no such check); a crash between a source's answer and `Store::spend_journaled` (the code is the reference's, moved: crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs 496-536 against crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference/web.rs 461-512); a lost claim, which differs in the one answer of record 785c9e30. Store entries, counted by a counter added to `Store::with_read`, `with_tx` and `with_write` in scratch copies at 2a6389e8d and at 0ffc5b11a: the same on both for a script step (18 reads and 3 transactions with no call) and for each call (a draft operation 1 and 1, a host read 2 and 1, a reply 3 and 1, a refusal of the run's rules, of the arguments or of check 2 1 and 1, a window from the page store 10 and 1, a fetch that reaches the source 13 and 1, a publish 10 and 1, a rebase 4 and 1).","kind":"comment","references":"31959bf00e6d2b5b118991cf4c2a8b3de116ff72d6b4fa3d2ef4576d39483e8d","span":{"start":{"line":359},"end":{"line":393},"content_hash":"4f609f355adb3fda3e4b5201afa7e9bcbc524cd1bb76c83f68cdc1a69a0b1156"},"suggested_fix":"Keep (1) to (5), (7) and (8) as scenarios here: a `Cut::CrashAfter` whose first script is the first calls of the stored one, and a hook that runs before the resume.","summary":"Eleven crash points the scenarios do not reach, each run the reference's way, the seam's and both mixed ways: all leave the same","supersedes":"8254766591eb081fd9a88cddc8dc46284087ecfda02425c7dcbfe148fe2992f6","tags":["verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:01.655055Z","id":"bafdada589a0599253c8638d71328b3442e48afbe87d527538f7b4bb87c5849c","body":{"detail":"(1) Row 3 (surface.rs 52) and `no_place` (978-992): both of the core's unjournaled refusals, `replay_diverged` and `no_place`, are told as any call's answer is (the step's `calls`, a `call` event, a `bot.call` record, no target) and counted on the requests line, as crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs `replay_diverged`. The proof's `Stepper` logs a `no_place` refusal in its `calls` to agree (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, `Stepper::call`); nothing else in this crate numbers a script's calls through the seam yet. (2) `no_place`'s documentation says it is a backstop under the sandbox's code, an error a script can catch where the sandbox's limit ends the script, reached only while `script.max_calls` may exceed `NOTICE_SEQ`; the code is kept. (3) 136-141: a row of either form answers the same through `from_entry`. (4) `Answer`'s table has one row for each constructor and a paragraph after it (810-815): a replayed answer is journaled by no one, and its constructor matters for the charge alone. (5) 257-262, `merge_fields`'s errors and `ending`'s: in the `failed` step a clash is logged, none of that binding's fields are merged, the other bindings' are kept, and the step commits. (6) `CallNote::extra` (763-768): the field named `web` is what the core puts in `Event::Call`'s `web`. (7) 350-352: `declared` \"is not part of the crate's public interface\", true whether it is private or `pub(crate)`. Also from the same reading: `ended` asks a provider to remember a run only until it has been told `ended` once (1644-1649), with no read of the run's row.","kind":"comment","references":"3e081cf41696fb0dff8c11c6913499d49acfb1e09c41494f8b401e1e6e4538e4","span":{"start":{"line":49},"end":{"line":60},"content_hash":"81612a585e3a5c26464b4f71be5ce0f5c2b146d9ce00cb3da72f409328cfd02c"},"summary":"Seven of the eight are answered in the text; where another surface's standing operations come from and who resolves the request stay in their own records","supersedes":"d7a5a34052453fc3313ed3690dfaabade13278ea28e89659e5d8f780c5e24ac6","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:01.965929Z","id":"b6f72d8b37dc2ae57638553f0271a3c3cbc320b9f2d025b858d7a3ebf4791dab","body":{"detail":"(1) Where another surface's standing operations come from and who resolves the request at the first turn are still open in their own records. (2) After a merge with branch `claxbot-surfaces` (tip 1b2d5f9e7 merges with no conflict and `//crates/clax-bot:all`, `//crates/clax-surface:all`, `//crates/clax-commands:all`, `//bazel/wasm:check`, `//scripts:crate_layers` and `//scripts:no_unsafe` pass on the merged tree), `declared` is at crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 221 and `pub(crate)`: the records that cite api.rs by line (216-218, 253) point elsewhere, and the sentence at 349-353 here stays true.","kind":"comment","references":"3e081cf41696fb0dff8c11c6913499d49acfb1e09c41494f8b401e1e6e4538e4","span":{"start":{"line":49},"end":{"line":60},"content_hash":"81612a585e3a5c26464b4f71be5ce0f5c2b146d9ce00cb3da72f409328cfd02c"},"summary":"Read again at 1473f04ce: the seven are answered in the text; two things are left for the change that moves script.rs to settle","supersedes":"30dc704478ff63b9f17bf30dac1466c069d66f66e505683930dc8388d7ee4361","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:02.294811Z","id":"c714b17cd4bf255b6b85d8411fc775525edfb8d87339fa1fe9ffa160152f35d6","body":{"detail":"surface.rs 130, Replayed: answered from the entry before check 2 (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451), `from_entry` reading the core's `{code, message}` row as it reads today's. Row 129 says why making the refusal again is the same for an operation the run did not declare. The adapter's session answers a web call given its entry before any check (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `ClaxSession::web`, 421-465) and has something standing for a web source (`ClaxProvider::web`, 105) that counts the calls that got past check 2. `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` (1608): attempt 1 on a host with no source journals `web_disabled`; attempt 2 on a host with one answers the same from the entry; through the seam and through today's run loop (`Deps::web` `None` for the driver that dies, the fake source for the one that resumes) no call reaches the source, the step's `calls` are equal, and there is one journal row, of attempt 1. With the session's replay check turned off (`call.replay.filter(|_| false)`) that test fails. Each change was made alone and reverted; `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (189 tests) passes without it.","kind":"comment","references":"9e196b6392b39c663b446e33b951d4f1cb59da6e4f82283923cd16237fe74657","span":{"start":{"line":150},"end":{"line":151},"content_hash":"42ef63a3af7dff3456985e00ee6c79168132eb1211fbb0f4e5f5dac678d8f792"},"summary":"Row 130 now says a journaled call is answered from its entry, as every web call given one; the adapter does so, and a case holds it when the host gains a source between attempts","supersedes":"8f5e73bbc0e751c64891dc2b2319ba8d69d6b1fb2c09eec896f89f57ce876042","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:02.614578Z","id":"af623b78ea2af6428eb9f9a18198b0ee9850052f3eaddb06551e3399ae414266","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 435 changed to skip the entry when the host has a source fails `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` alone of the 12 seam tests. One sentence of the paragraph under the table was not brought along: crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 138-140 still says a row \"is never read by this code (the refusal is made again…)\".","kind":"comment","references":"9e196b6392b39c663b446e33b951d4f1cb59da6e4f82283923cd16237fe74657","span":{"start":{"line":150},"end":{"line":151},"content_hash":"42ef63a3af7dff3456985e00ee6c79168132eb1211fbb0f4e5f5dac678d8f792"},"summary":"Verified at 3b700b895: the adapter answers a web call from its entry before check 2, and the case fails when it does not","supersedes":"a9f287e80c1625c37e9b38c26e86cf93e2c031820d3987addedde0185b04152b","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:02.953474Z","id":"fd01010ab7dad689dc9a0ef0bc128b737ce00e66d2976fab3c8e232ff2ab5bab","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs:173 reads 'to `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes` in a loop that builds the whole value' (70e2f2b81). Proposed close: fixed. What remains for the change that moves the tool result behind the seam is to read the size from the tick's limits, as crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:684 does.","kind":"comment","references":"e0236ad44637140e869b5dbd5720922378bf5072502f00454e614820acf34468","span":{"start":{"line":212},"end":{"line":215},"content_hash":"f2c8378f4257c509c46f211aa8c3b0b7ed506e76ffe70058cd94ec789bf74ea5"},"summary":"The sentence now names `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`","supersedes":"faebce942cf812a574ef3cb51715584a0a03fb39056875d3e67ec7994054f985","tags":["for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:03.276311Z","id":"cb0ab2680daee25cc2da60115504529612bc3714767b0f56a80bac866a00760f","body":{"detail":"The change added 24 lines to crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs before `Tick::step` (`Deps::surfaces`, `Deps::reference`, `Deps::with_surfaces`) and 35 after it (`Tick::script_step`), and the table (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 225-236) was not renumbered. Where the table's rows are now: `stop_lead` 1183, `Tick::commit` 1293, `reset_date` 1409, `must_stop` 1444, `what_i_did` 1488, `notify_at` 1519, `unreported_calls` 1538, `stop` 1554, `fail` 1613, the roster on 1690, `finish` 1810, `give_up` 1850, the roster off 1593, 1642, 1846, 1867. `events.rs` 316-326 (`step_summary`) is 340-350. The rows for a script's calls name files and functions and are right; the same would keep these right.","kind":"comment","references":"a878f820543320de6f0dbeb2dc7182289e9686328aa9b9455c48a341465eeb23","span":{"start":{"line":246},"end":{"line":255},"content_hash":"1431e72efff9ae47e59e0226c316ba52cfcee6f534e1087d404bf7d3eca4681e"},"summary":"After the script step's move every line number the endings table gives of run.rs is wrong: 44 of 44 checked","supersedes":"57f0d836cbf0f4de45eabaab0ae49d619cc21df6380a685200ebd292c56144a4","tags":["verify-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:03.608587Z","id":"2a9c97f61b9cc0e5d96dddd8e5724e11f0f35ea22043a04acf35ebb8bec397ff","body":{"detail":"As reply 432832ac recommends. (1) Cap the setting under `NOTICE_SEQ` (1,000,000) where it is validated (crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 80, 226-229: `script.max_calls` is a `Positive`, 1 to 1,000,000,000,000). (2) Clamp a value already stored above the cap where `RunSettings` reads it (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 175-176), so an instance that set one before the cap is not left with it. (3) Keep `StepCx::place` answering `None` from `NOTICE_SEQ` up and `no_place` as the backstop: its documentation (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 978-992) now says a model should never meet it once the setting is capped, and that it shares the sandbox's code `call_limit` while being an error a script can catch. None of the three is done on this branch: bot_settings.rs and run.rs were not to change here. The owner's to decide.","kind":"comment","references":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","span":{"start":{"line":740},"end":{"line":756},"content_hash":"c450e4f73b4a874088e783bfda5ec4962ff9bb05742b171d6d83656a156bc7fb"},"summary":"The recommendation on record for the owner: cap `script.max_calls` under `NOTICE_SEQ`, clamp a stored larger value where `RunSettings` reads it, and keep `place`'s refusal as a backstop","supersedes":"9e3764d4ad3cc129debb08d9781771061d40d1388514ccc0ea4b9c63e699fec4","tags":["seam","fix-round","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:03.931980Z","id":"5478637c1921a4b57435bc8aae4faca852d55f7811d80206974dea75970e8374","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs passes `self.limits.script` to the sandbox, and `RunLimits::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:131) fills it from `RunSettings::limits()`, so under the default policy `max_calls` is the setting's value and its range is untouched. A policy written in code may answer any `max_calls`, including one at or over `NOTICE_SEQ`, without passing through the setting. So a cap on the setting alone would not close this for a policy; a clamp where the run loop hands the limits to the sandbox would. The decision stays the owner's.","kind":"comment","references":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","span":{"start":{"line":731},"end":{"line":749},"content_hash":"ac6172417eb77e75da3a8a046cc2306d468700a33e06b01c8fee3a9594291038"},"summary":"A policy can now set a script's `max_calls` with no range at all: `RunLimits::script` is the sandbox's `Limits`, passed as the policy answers it","supersedes":"068dcc18b06f6a27689f04f68145a1c0c3581e24498f7f4f3c139e592dbccad7","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:04.256173Z","id":"9b904765b13a5f81bee142e01546e1038a3bae43ef08e230bba8b9cab1cc850c","body":{"detail":"It is used in two places, crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs 509 (the entry could not be made) and 534 (`Store::spend_journaled` failed), which are the two places the code before the seam journaled nothing (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference/web.rs 464-472, 506-510). Journaling those failures instead would be a change a person can meet: a fetch whose transaction failed once (a busy store, `page_exists`) would answer that failure on every later attempt of its step, where today the step run again fetches. What it leaves open: `Answer::unwritten(e)` is `Journal::Written` with a failure, the same value as `Answer::written(Err(e))`, so `Answer::journal` says a row was written when none was, and the provider cases cannot tell a provider that uses it for a call that did write. A variant of its own (`Journal::Unwritten`) would let `Claim::record` and the cases say what they mean: `Written` has a row, `Unwritten` has none and no effect.","kind":"comment","references":"3c2d69c93d6a00d036f042a76c8ca1964489fe58c0a406dc7e02d8d3ebd7d0bc","span":{"start":{"line":968},"end":{"line":969},"content_hash":"6a1d163bf5d2ef9bd84c336fa4d8564736fe9ff19283aa701fd6e2f5c7274b13"},"summary":"A recommendation for the owner's question: keep `Answer::unwritten`, and give it a `Journal` of its own","supersedes":"61e1a09a7f24b62f872fc81d0e75b99961daae4055259a189347da79c508d6f0","tags":["verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:04.564303Z","id":"e38b52488bce692c32c9a476186bb564385684db954a8f5957dfca4c18c61aca","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields(into, merged, core, surface, fields)` (surface.rs 1307) takes a `Merged` (1247) that the core keeps for a step's payload and another for what its model is told. The join arm applies only when `merged` says the name under the surface's name was made by that surface; any other value there, an object too, is a `FieldClash`. No list of `clax`'s field names is kept, so nothing has to stay complete. Unit tests: `a_surface_named_as_a_field_of_claxs_clashes_whichever_is_merged_first` (2481) holds the case of this record (`published` over `{version: 2}` clashes and is not merged into), the other order (the host surface first, then `clax`: the clash is `clax`'s, for the same name), and an object the core put under a surface's name; `a_field_that_takes_a_name_of_the_cores_or_one_taken_is_the_providers_error` (2390) holds `clax` and another surface each answering one of its own fields twice, and that a refused merge leaves both the fields and `merged` as they were. With the guard removed (`if own || true`), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` fails `a_surface_named_as_a_field_of_claxs_clashes_whichever_is_merged_first` and passes without the change.","kind":"comment","references":"4bba8ddd91c12a845fcef7887f62cc5cfba0cc927b8e7cbc29b01effdbbf99d7","span":{"start":{"line":1334},"end":{"line":1546},"content_hash":"3f30b6954c8a178be711ec96680d41f0d4dbeff975d585941b89e68c70ca39f1"},"summary":"Fixed by tracking: the caller holds which surface made each top-level name (`Merged`), and a surface's fields join only the object its own earlier merge made","supersedes":"b0ffa31367f2be290021a75f8f629269e13a5f1c9495ec13812828cac6d0f0c9","tags":["seam","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:15:04.855158Z","id":"f87b6852ef6c3ca6840319649e7a456b844d50edaf85f832826fa08af727cc24","body":{"detail":"Read against `merge_fields` (1307-1353): a name at the top is the core's (refused by the `core` list or by being there), `clax`'s (recorded as made by `clax`), or a surface's own object (recorded under the surface's own name, which only that surface's first merge writes). A surface joins only a name `Merged` says it made, so `published` over `clax`'s object clashes, and merged in the other order `clax` clashes on the same name: the verdict is a clash either way and only the binding blamed differs. A refused merge changes neither value (`clash_of` in this file's tests holds both). What the type does not prevent: `Merged` is not tied to the `Fields` it describes. A fresh `Merged` with fields already merged into gives a clash that is not one; a `Merged` kept from another step, or the payload's used for what the model is told, says a surface made a name that something else put there, and the join arm then adds to that object. The documentation (1239-1245) says one is kept for the payload and one for what the model is told; nothing checks it.","kind":"comment","references":"4bba8ddd91c12a845fcef7887f62cc5cfba0cc927b8e7cbc29b01effdbbf99d7","span":{"start":{"line":1401},"end":{"line":1447},"content_hash":"350380081d0348c4c49a815597eef4cbd0740e64e32c2a2e8218d0782d232b53"},"suggested_fix":"When a run loop first calls this, hold the fields and what made them in one value (a struct with both, whose `merge` takes the surface and its fields), so they cannot be separated or reused.","summary":"Verified at 1473f04ce: no order of merges puts one surface's fields in another's object or over a core name; `Merged` and the fields it describes are still two values the caller must keep together","supersedes":"2a8ec75d373e99d8265ebdb2be16757242377e4fdc2cf97c60d6c237e97eb482","tags":["seam","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.551930Z","id":"f2da8e9a3269c21d2cce1e999da25c0618ccd8b2af52c623f5a149b55355a0bb","body":{"detail":"The script is `READS_WRITES_AND_A_SECOND_PUBLISH` (crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs): `db.set` is given `{}`, `db.update` `{if_version: doc.version}` and `db.delete` the set's version, and the scenario requires `second[0] == \"already_published\"`, version 2 and a last call of `db.collections`. Each `Scenario` now has `last_call` (the last entry of the last script step's `calls`, with its code when it failed), `error` (the kind it means to end with, or none) and `holds` (facts of the stored steps), checked by `Scenario::went_as_meant` on the reference's run and on the seam's, so a script that dies early on both paths fails. With crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs 306 as `if false {`, `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all` (the flags of scripts/lib/bazel-test-flags.sh) fails `script_differential::a_script_step_through_the_seam_leaves_what_the_reference_leaves_2`. The other nineteen, each step printed once and read: eighteen went as their names say. One did less than its name: \"logs and a result that do not fit\" had its logs cut by the sandbox at its own 32 KiB, so the result was 34 KiB and the run loop's cut of the logs never ran; its first script now returns 64,000 bytes and logs lines that double when written as JSON, and the scenario requires fewer log lines than the sandbox kept, the truncation line last, `published` in the result and a text within the bound. Its second script's result is cut as text (102,415 bytes: the bound and the 15 bytes of the marker, as `script.result-cut-to-fit.json` pins).","kind":"comment","references":"5141f40de93683e6bdca4afa21227f5668f4f3586d4d484698ae4cddb6ecf5a1","summary":"The scenario reaches its second publish now, every scenario states how its last script ends, and the reference changed to allow a second publish fails","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.552691Z","id":"cae5a0a978e03b4a432f261be8379ff577efab1d50f2695b57360559b33339b4","body":{"detail":"`Journal::Unwritten` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs): no one journals, the place has no row. `Answer::unwritten` takes a `HostError` alone, so a value cannot be unwritten. The provider cases (crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs, `Attempt::says_truly`) require of every call made without an entry that an answer made as written has a row at its place and one made as unwritten has none; and a new case, `an_unwritten_failure_leaves_no_row_and_is_made_again` (`ProviderFixture::unwritten`), that such a failure changes nothing and that the next attempt finds no entry and is answered afresh. Played on the fake (`FakeSurfaceState::unwritten_next`); not on `clax`, whose fixture cannot make the store refuse a web transaction (it takes the ID of the driver that holds the run, which the cases mint). For `clax` the run loop is driven instead: `web::a_web_call_whose_transaction_fails_is_made_again` has the run come to keep a page of the URL while the source fetches it, so `Store::spend_journaled` answers `page_exists`; the script is answered that failure, no row is left, and after a crash the fetch is made again and answered from the page store. Changed one at a time: that failure answered `Answer::refused` fails `web::a_web_call_whose_transaction_fails_is_made_again`; the run loop journaling an unwritten answer fails `providers::a_case_is_left_out_only_when_the_fixture_and_the_caller_both_say_so`, `providers::a_read_that_nothing_moves_does_not_play_the_case_of_the_replayed_read` and 2 more; the fake answering its failure for the run loop to journal fails `providers::a_case_is_left_out_only_when_the_fixture_and_the_caller_both_say_so`, `providers::a_read_that_nothing_moves_does_not_play_the_case_of_the_replayed_read` and 1 more; `unwritten` as `Journal::Written` fails `providers::a_case_is_left_out_only_when_the_fixture_and_the_caller_both_say_so`, `providers::a_read_that_nothing_moves_does_not_play_the_case_of_the_replayed_read` and 2 more. Whether to keep the constructor at all is still the owner's.","kind":"comment","references":"3c2d69c93d6a00d036f042a76c8ca1964489fe58c0a406dc7e02d8d3ebd7d0bc","summary":"`Answer::unwritten` is kept and is `Journal::Unwritten` now; the provider cases hold what each journal says; a test makes the web transaction fail","tags":["t5","fix-round","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.553052Z","id":"bf41c7f1734aa404320c2e8637f5a48879be612a3ad598b9f1e04b8fc3cd6d89","body":{"detail":"(1) Sessions in reverse: the order is stated (the surface that declared the run's kind first, the others as bound: `Tick::script`, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs; step table rows 2 and 7) and held by `generic::the_kinds_surface_is_joined_first_and_the_others_in_the_order_bound` (Clax's provider beside the fake bound as `published`, given in the other order): reversed, or left in the order given, it fails `generic::the_kinds_surface_is_joined_first_and_the_others_in_the_order_bound`. (2) The size test without the sessions' fields fails `script::tests::a_result_is_cut_to_its_bound_with_the_sessions_fields_in_it`, which builds a result at its bound with `published` in it. The code measured the whole text already; no bound was passed there. One path does pass its bound, and did before the seam: a result with no logs left to cut is cut as text to the bound and then given the marker `… [truncated]`, 15 bytes more (`script.result-cut-to-fit.json` pins 102,415 bytes for a bound of 102,400); it is recorded on those lines. (3) A lost claim not checked before `finish`: `StepSession::finish` now says it may be called by a driver that no longer holds the run (a claim can lapse unnoticed), that it writes nothing, and that the store's refusal of the commit is the guard; step table row 8 says the same. (4) The failed web transaction answered `refused` fails `web::a_web_call_whose_transaction_fails_is_made_again`. (5) `no_place` untold and (6) the replayed core refusal journaled again are left: the first needs a script of a million calls, the second changes nothing (`Store::record_bot_call` keeps the row that is there).","kind":"comment","references":"813737c3ead93c782337df20efec265dadf56d7054e3c10f288def0d3415e46d","summary":"Of the six: three now fail a test, one is stated in the contract, two stay as equivalents","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.553405Z","id":"659799d17b63d08b479125443a73fea9d8408c173edfc1b270a835447d136e42","body":{"detail":"`Tick::script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs): the clash is logged and counted against the step (`Tick::clashed`), that binding's fields are left out (`StepJoin::add` joins nothing of parts that clash), and when the script was cancelled the stop is taken in the same tick with the run loop's own fields, the script's `bot.script` and `bot.call` records and its compute. `generic::a_clash_in_a_cancelled_step_does_not_keep_the_run_from_stopping` binds the fake as `calls`, cancels the script, and requires `Done(Stopped)`, a stopped step with `failures` 1 and the keys `calls`, `code`, `notice`, `outcome`, `reason`, `title`, one `bot.script` record and a `script.compute` row on step 2. Abandoning the stop fails `generic::a_clash_in_a_cancelled_step_does_not_keep_the_run_from_stopping`; not counting the clash fails `generic::a_clash_in_a_cancelled_step_does_not_keep_the_run_from_stopping`. The module documentation of surface.rs says it beside the `failed` step's rule.","kind":"comment","references":"1921358d98ef50a6e98245973f50e12dc17baaf5bb4fce37b1b31d004b479bea","summary":"A clash in a cancelled script's step no longer abandons the stop","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.553758Z","id":"f5feaac3c12d8a7125051fdfc34e71f9fcb006e56afcb9d05dc4e16c2d89a2fd","body":{"detail":"What changed beside it: a binding whose parts clash in a cancelled step is left out of that value and the stop is taken (record 712f1b15). The `Merged` still ends where `Tick::stop` takes `extra`, for the change that calls `ending` there.","kind":"comment","references":"1700fa7df6ecc36e89530f0457205f55b5795c7097f0a4018ee7dbcbb702e74d","summary":"Unchanged by the cancelled clash's fix: the stopped step is still given the sessions' fields as one value","tags":["t5","fix-round","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.554114Z","id":"ade97fef4142538598af939fcf4b47278417aa58ab8574b6d54563ac3883989f","body":{"detail":"`script::tests::the_script_step_reads_no_module_of_claxs_surface` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs) keeps the import assertions (`crate::clax`, `crate::draft`, `crate::check`, `crate::web`, `deps.host`, `deps.web`) and its documentation names crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs as the test of the meaning. What of Clax's still runs in a run whose scripts reach the fake alone (the provider and binding `Tick::script_step` makes and drops, the prompt, the roster, the notices) is the change that gives the tick its run's bindings: record ffaee020.","kind":"comment","references":"aa0b8a0516626f5b2d66affd7c29279f3522f8d105f823ebac2c55fdbb8b1976","summary":"The list of words is gone; the test holds which modules the file reads and says where the meaning is held","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.554459Z","id":"fbc2524a8249417bb3d5c99e2f855f708d575995237ccdbd9f74d3a9894c111a","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::standing` still has no kind and is still asked of the running build on each script step (`ClaxProvider::info`). Both choices this record gives remove it: store a binding's whole set when the run is made, or take the rule from the kind's `@allow` list of the definition the binding's hash names.","kind":"comment","references":"06a96d62b78e3f5c3290bde0529f5f562f840acbb3f318a51b6cce4573e8de00","summary":"Left for the change that reads a run's stored bindings: nothing here changed `standing`","tags":["t5","fix-round","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.554807Z","id":"a07f336f4e810ce2050d54dc549ae6d80aca815af000df05d009ea5f69aba388","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs, after the twenty: the driver dies after the draft calls and before the publish; after the publish's row and before the reply; after a charged search and fetch; after three refused writes were journaled; a brief's after `artifacts.create`; a rebase recorded and a version published before the resume (`[2, \"conflict\"]`); a document read recorded and the document changed (`[{n: 1}, {n: 9}]`); after the script step's commit (`Cut::CrashAfterCommit`); a script cancelled on return whose driver dies before the stop, resumed with no cancel (`Cut::CancelOnReturnThenCrash`: committed as a script step, attempt 3); and draft operations that fail, replayed. A step resumed mid-script is made by a first attempt that runs the first calls of the stored script (`Cut::CrashAfter`). Each is run the reference's way, the seam's and both mixed ways, thirty scenarios in six tests side by side: `//crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` takes about 7 s (it was about 5.5 s with twenty in four).","kind":"comment","references":"31959bf00e6d2b5b118991cf4c2a8b3de116ff72d6b4fa3d2ef4576d39483e8d","summary":"Ten of the eleven crash points are scenarios of the comparison now; the eleventh was the corrected scenario","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.555155Z","id":"55a4ca1e4fa80bed2f7aa9dd184d916a997a278ff066a93e5fcdf6f17c6cea93","body":{"detail":"Every citation of the table (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, \"Today's endings, behind the two hooks\") and `step_summary`'s (`events.rs` 340-350) was mapped from the lines of 2a6389e8d to the file now and printed beside the first and last line of its range. The citations past `Tick::step` were already six lines low at 2a6389e8d (`finish` was cited at 1745 where it began at 1751), so those were moved six more: `finish` 1815-1853, `give_up` 1855-1874, `stop` 1559-1600, `fail` 1618-1649, `must_stop` 1449-1490, `what_i_did` 1493-1514, `notify` 1519-1539, the roster on 1695 and off 1598, 1647, 1851, 1872. The rows for a script's calls name files and functions; these still name lines, and move again with any edit above them.","kind":"comment","references":"a878f820543320de6f0dbeb2dc7182289e9686328aa9b9455c48a341465eeb23","summary":"The endings table is renumbered against run.rs as it is, each range read against its sentence","tags":["t5","fix-round","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:16:38.555201Z","id":"7ff1e47fa30c79a7e4c1c556ac9f09d49a2cbbd1a82ce24d3e4bf4c83e8c6896","body":{"detail":"`tool_result_content` cuts the text to `max` bytes (`RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`) at a character boundary and appends `… [truncated]`, so what the model is sent is up to `max + 15` bytes. It did so before the script step went through the seam (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs, the same lines), and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/script.result-cut-to-fit.json pins it: `content_bytes` 102415 for a bound of 102400. Cutting to `max` less the marker would keep the bound and changes that golden. The path with logs keeps the bound (`script::tests::a_result_is_cut_to_its_bound_with_the_sessions_fields_in_it`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":218},"end":{"line":223},"content_hash":"86b5a5b98fa3360e64d19d5bdb570df4e400a172ae4666879ebf8cd1a65544c0"},"summary":"A result with no logs left to cut is 15 bytes over its bound: the text is cut to the bound and the marker is added after","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T17:45:38.106290Z","id":"a74b2f63d6c444a2f772fd94a55a6ca0c5ada5bf1c0921a86b06a267a7650ae4","body":{"detail":"`bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test --test_arg=script_differential` with crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs 306 as `if false {` fails one comparison; of the twenty changes to the reference only a replayed call's `ms` passes (scrubbed as measured, and held on the product by `golden::resumed`). The journal at each crash, printed in a scratch copy: the ten scenarios that die mid-script die where they say (three draft calls; those and the publish; a search and a fetch; three refused writes; one `artifacts.create`; one `draft.rebase`; one `db.get`; after the commit with no open step). `Scenario::went_as_meant` (205), tried on the first scenario with its script replaced: a `throw` after the first call fails on the error's kind; a last call that is refused and caught fails on the last call; a middle call (the publish) refused and caught passes when the script returns the fact `holds` looks for without having it from the call. So it tells an early end and a refused last call, and a refused middle call only through `holds`. The thirty scenarios' `holds` each read a value a call returned; the web scenario asserts six of its nine outcomes (not the fifth to seventh refusals' codes). The transaction that fails in crates/clax-bot/tests/web.rs `a_web_call_whose_transaction_fails_is_made_again` (`page_exists`, a refusal `Store::spend_journaled` documents) leaves the same on the reference: the same source wrapper run both ways in a scratch copy, whole `Left` compared.","kind":"comment","references":"5141f40de93683e6bdca4afa21227f5668f4f3586d4d484698ae4cddb6ecf5a1","span":{"start":{"line":1188},"end":{"line":1192},"content_hash":"ade7b24913adff1b70e97e3726ccb82c3bc9de80268aba538aa1f0da55a8fa46"},"summary":"Verified at 65a77a602: the scenario reaches its second publish, the reference changed not to refuse it now fails the comparison, and what `went_as_meant` can and cannot tell","tags":["verify-t5","verified","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.881890Z","id":"b85839d6cb9058c99745bab70aa77609b029f7f2d32ef49582227d8c8917a98f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs and crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs read no `RunLimits` and no `RunSettings` cap: `must_stop`, `minutes_stop` and `affordable` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs) format `<n> steps`, `<n> credits`, `<n> minutes` from `self.limits` into `End::Stopped::limit` (`capped`), and the provider prints the string it is given. ending.rs reads `settings` only for the per-step usage lines (`step_usage`). The list of files `the_run_loop_reads_each_bound_and_size_from_the_policys_limits` reads is unchanged.","kind":"comment","references":"f63d8cbf0ac8346d4b65a74c7f520ca47325d8dd7cb720503eb25831c0f104bb","summary":"The endings moved and no file they moved to reads a bound: the cap's value is formatted in run.rs from `limits` and carried in `End::Stopped`","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.882233Z","id":"8081675a68443c21be43cc37af897f952157cabee60ac0470932c63700225dbc","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs, `lead`: `policy_error` is 'I stopped: this Clax could not work out what this run may do.', `policy_history_<n>` is 'I stopped: this Clax could not put together what to send the model.', `model_timeout` is 'I stopped: the model took longer to answer than this run allows.', each followed by what the run did (`what_i_did`). The first two end the run `failed`, the third `stopped`. `clax::ending::tests::a_stop_has_the_notice_of_its_reason_and_two_reasons_have_none` now holds the three strings, so a change of wording is a change of that test; crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs drives each ending. No golden under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden holds them. For the owner: keep these words, or give the three others.","kind":"comment","references":"23d65d691e9eaf1df171a4838fa33cd0857d29d69241d8b9ffa8f5ae8773cd7b","summary":"The three endings' words moved as they were to `clax`'s provider; nobody chose them yet","tags":["t7","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.882599Z","id":"8c035bc8a75d40d9d6973f7ae948b67b3a2e2dadedbef51c5d0292601b1f4480","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs: `must_stop`, `minutes_stop` and the credits stop of `affordable` build the stop with `capped(format!(\"{max} steps\"))` and its like from `self.limits`; crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs `notice` writes 'this run reached its limit of {limit}'. A policy's own caps reach the notice (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: 'limit of 1 steps', 'limit of 5 minutes', passing), and `generic::a_run_of_the_fake_alone_is_stopped_by_a_cap` holds the `End` a provider is told: `limit: Some(\"1 steps\")`. A `run_cap` with no limit, which the run loop never makes, is given the lead of an unnamed stop.","kind":"comment","references":"c1ae5826a5abc5ca7508f8ae0097569f420d50bd84f3e6e41b0c46b5474b81f0","summary":"`End::Stopped::limit` is made from `Tick.limits`, and the provider prints what it is given","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.882952Z","id":"50e55890aedf35cc1e5295abfe6f4f6ec05e2b5e98b6a41d8d086b165cd52868","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs `run_ending` folds `Tally::of(cx.history)` once an ending and reads `replied` for the rule and `scripts`, `changed`, `published` for `what_i_did`. crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs folds no tally on a tick: `Tick::clax` is `#[cfg(test)]`, read by the references (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs, crates/clax-bot/src/ending_reference.rs). Held: with the rule inverted, `golden::endings::finish_posts_no_summary_on_any_thread_when_the_run_replied_on_one` and the endings comparison fail; the count of scripts in a notice is held by `golden::endings::a_notice_counts_the_scripts_that_ran` and `clax::ending::tests::what_a_run_did_counts_its_scripts_and_names_its_edits_and_its_version`.","kind":"comment","references":"e90edb8f44a99a927577e54edf0eb9e60abbb51ec9c371c8436a7e31f22d19a3","summary":"`what_i_did` and the finish rule are the provider's, folded from `Tally::of(cx.history)`; `Tick::clax` is a field of the library's test builds alone","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.883302Z","id":"65cd81891dcf5626c15758a1caba72c96d33ae2c9e921d0d1a3f15a18223043f","body":{"detail":"(1) The run loop's half of `ending` is crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs `Tick::end` and is held by crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs: `an_ending_that_fails_is_counted_and_bounded_and_the_other_binding_is_still_asked` (an `Err` counted once an attempt, the step open, the other binding asked on each attempt, `failed` at `max_commit_failures`, the `failed` step committed without the failing binding's fields and with the other's, both told `ended`), `an_ending_that_fails_once_is_asked_again_and_writes_nothing_twice`, `a_clash_in_a_terminal_step_is_counted_and_the_failed_step_commits_without_it`. (2) The sources' read is in `clax`'s `ending` and its failure is counted: `endings::sources_that_cannot_be_read_are_a_counted_failure_of_the_finish_step`, `endings::sources_that_stay_unreadable_end_the_run_failed` (crates/clax-bot/tests/endings.rs), and the comparison names it as its first exception (`ending_differential::unreadable_sources_are_counted_through_the_seam_alone`). (3) `begin` uncounted and `ending` counted were left to differ: see the record on crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs tagged `status:needs-decision`. (4) `ended` is told again when a driver starts, for the runs a provider says it still awaits (`SurfaceProvider::awaiting_ended`, `ending::redeliver`, `Driver::start`); the `End` is rebuilt from the run's row and its terminal step (`ending::stored_end`), not kept whole in the payload, which is pinned. (5) A clash is counted (`Tick::end`; `Tick::clashed` in script.rs), and the names are refused where a host's surface is defined (`clax_surface::STEP_FIELDS`, crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs), held equal to the run loop's lists by `surface::tests::the_names_refused_a_hosts_surface_are_the_names_a_step_keeps_fields_under`. (6) The real provider's fixture has a `noticed` probe and a `lifecycle` (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs). (7) A cancelled script's `stopped` step is charged as before, the per-step lines with no call counted: `Tick::end` passes 0 calls to `step_usage`, and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs holds the line.","kind":"comment","references":"e6464e3085eb823a4ca608059cf6e687b7bd735c48abcffc0595ea9729aaafab","summary":"Each of the seven, as built","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.883662Z","id":"6642253bf8809b37c04a6930934fe04d1442caf5600727193b8a4ad33507a8d3","body":{"detail":"Every ending golden has a companion, `net.ending.*.json` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs, `Pins::ending_net`): the run's `bot.*` audit records, usage rows, whole journal and every roster call. `golden::endings::a_stop_records_the_calls_of_a_script_that_never_committed_and_not_its_own_notice` stops a run on the step of a script whose driver died before its commit. Measured before the endings moved (run.rs at 7deb94e24): `stop` without `unreported_calls` failed that test; `unreported_calls` without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` failed 13 tests of `golden::endings`. After the move the same two changes (crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs `Tick::end`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs `unreported_calls`) fail the same tests.","kind":"comment","references":"85a58cbbf7fb070cbd357f081b1642ca18313495e9325b95840169919e7c469e","summary":"A stop's audit records are pinned, and both changes now fail tests","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.884022Z","id":"4deeeb1e599ff3bc1da16e647b86e33a9fe0ae078b863a5c51095fed99a41efa","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs hands `Tick::end` an `Ending` whose `join` is the step's `StepJoin` and whose `core` holds the run loop's fields; crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs joins each binding's `ending` fields to it with `StepJoin::add` and builds the payload with `StepJoin::payload`, so a surface's `ending` fields join the object its session made (`generic::a_fake_surfaces_script_that_is_cancelled_stops_the_run`: `fake: {fake_set, fake_notice}`), and `reason` is merged as the core's. `notice` is `clax`'s field and no longer the run loop's: a session of `clax` that answered `notice` would clash with its own `ending`.","kind":"comment","references":"1700fa7df6ecc36e89530f0457205f55b5795c7097f0a4018ee7dbcbb702e74d","summary":"The cancelled script's `StepJoin` now reaches the terminal step whole","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.884383Z","id":"dd13c6e9bbbdafd5cca56d17be97522da67d6497c4139f347f5084069a504618","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs `Tick::end`, `Ending::cancelled_script`: the attempt is counted once, for a session's clash and an `ending`'s alike, that binding's fields are left out, and the step commits with the script's compute and records. `generic::a_clash_in_a_cancelled_step_does_not_keep_the_run_from_stopping` binds the fake as `calls`, whose session's fields and `ending` fields both clash: `failures` is 1 and the stop is taken. The trait's documentation of `ending` says it (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs).","kind":"comment","references":"1921358d98ef50a6e98245973f50e12dc17baaf5bb4fce37b1b31d004b479bea","summary":"An `ending` that fails or clashes in a cancelled script's stop is treated as its sessions' clash is: counted, left out, and the stop taken","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.884740Z","id":"e93b36ac88774fd10710a79826b677843e7e749eae7e11cc85c9a860d79b20f1","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, \"A run's end, through the two hooks\": each row names `Tick::end`, `Tick::working`, `must_stop` and the functions of crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs, and `step_summary` is cited as `events.rs` without lines. `grep -n '([0-9]\\{4\\}' crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs` finds nothing. The measurements of docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md still cite run.rs by the lines it had when the plan was written.","kind":"comment","references":"a878f820543320de6f0dbeb2dc7182289e9686328aa9b9455c48a341465eeb23","summary":"The endings table no longer cites run.rs by line: it names files and functions","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885091Z","id":"17779326627d497f8b1031b31ee391a12b2f510b1ea33f2a10a948b54aa22ee3","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs: `STEP_FIELDS` (the run loop's fields of a payload and of what a model is told, and `clax`'s) and the refusal 'is a reserved name: a host's surface may not be named as a field of a run's step'. `Reserved` and its message are unchanged. Tests: `tests::examples::a_hosts_surface_named_as_a_field_of_a_step_is_refused` (the `.d.ts` notation, every name), two rows of the manifest's refusals (crates/clax-surface/src/tests/manifest.rs: `calls`, `published`), and in clax-bot `surface::tests::the_names_refused_a_hosts_surface_are_the_names_a_step_keeps_fields_under`, which fails when `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` or `CLAX_FIELDS` and that list differ. `endings::a_steps_fields_are_the_run_loops_and_the_ones_listed_as_claxs` holds `CLAX_FIELDS` to what the provider answers. A test can still bind the fake under such a name (`Deps::with_surfaces`), which is how the clash rule is driven.","kind":"comment","references":"68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10","summary":"The names are refused where a host's surface is defined, in both notations","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885181Z","id":"24bb1ffa01441de739ce0a1613735af2313b76f77c175b83b12b10110237f0e1","body":{"detail":"`Deps::info`, `Deps::providers` and `Tick::bound` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs) build `ClaxProvider::of(deps)` and a run's one binding from the row's columns; they are the only places crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs and script.rs get a provider from, so reading a run's stored bindings replaces these three. `Driver::start` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs) calls `ending::redeliver` and then `BotHost::start_waiting`: for `clax` the first already starts the follow-ups of every thread whose last run ended (`ClaxProvider::awaiting`, crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs), and the second stays for a thread with a follow-up queued and no run row; when the driver's housekeeping goes behind the provider, `start_waiting` goes there or goes. `redeliver` gives the kind's result to the provider whose surface's name is the kind's first word, by the names `Deps::providers` answers.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":373},"end":{"line":396},"content_hash":"c7c693b91e08ce17c8dd152c28d02289c96bf4c09a3f4dff6be45dc550381db5"},"summary":"For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: the run loop still makes `clax`'s provider itself, for a tick and for a starting driver, and a starting driver still calls `BotHost::start_waiting`","tags":["t7","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885214Z","id":"cc3b395f99175b2b9f8a02d2e7d2cd4412f2c4ab540afbbff4609e325cc5ec75","body":{"detail":"`BotHost::working` is called by `clax`'s provider alone (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs, `show_working`, `run_ended`) and by the reference (crates/clax-bot/src/ending_reference.rs; `Tick::working` here under `cfg(test)`). `Deps.host` is read by `ClaxProvider::of` (the provider's host: `plan_reply` for notices, `plan_write`, `plan_publish`, `read`), by the first turn's request context (`self.deps.host.context`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, `Tick::turn`) and by `Driver::start` (`start_waiting`). With the reference go: crates/clax-bot/src/ending_reference.rs, crates/clax-bot/src/ending_differential.rs, the two `cfg(test)` branches here, `Tick::clax`, `Tick::entry` and `Tick::op_ctx` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, `cfg(test)`, read by the two references), and `Deps::reference`. crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs declares the tests' shared `common` module `pub(crate)` because the endings' comparison uses it too.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":146},"end":{"line":166},"content_hash":"98b095eb8a5a9836216cc6ccba631924f761deee6b9d0b21c45e305ad9cb3abe"},"summary":"For the change that deletes `BotHost::working` and `Deps.host`: what still reads them, and what goes with the endings' reference","tags":["t7","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885239Z","id":"fd33196c29123b7541fb2354d59796723b857ac70a3f8e0598df6c9d37283160","body":{"detail":"A column on `bot_runs` set once each provider's `ended` answered `Ok`, and a starting driver telling the providers of every ended run without it. It would redeliver `ended` for any provider, whatever it remembers, and would turn `clax`'s roster off after a crash for a run with no follow-up queued. Not taken: it is a migration and a write after every run's end, and `clax` needs neither: the follow-ups are its own durable record of an end not acted on (`ClaxProvider::awaiting`, crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs), and a roster record lapses by itself 120 seconds after its last renewal and is kept in memory only (crates/clax-core/src/working.rs 3-12). As built the provider says which runs it awaits (`SurfaceProvider::awaiting_ended`), and a provider that says none is told once, by the tick that committed, or not at all when that tick died.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":334},"end":{"line":334},"content_hash":"6e6ef426cd8a8fb1603bc59edfc585dfc3223b87412a8cc8e4c4701dd4a9ffd7"},"summary":"The run loop remembering, in the run's row, that every provider was told `ended`","tags":["t7","revisit:a provider that must act on every run's end and keeps no record of its own of the runs it has not acted on","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885264Z","id":"0424b00610e3ea6f9616f3fa4e141242f20e214ed9c661ba9d6cd5c1b24dcc60","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::begin`'s failure fails the tick and is not counted against the step (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs, `Tick::script`; a draft that cannot be loaded always held its step open), while an `ending`'s is counted here and ends the run `failed` at `max_commit_failures`. So a store that cannot be read holds a script step open for as long as the run's caps allow and ends a `finish` after three tries. Counting `begin` too would change what a run whose draft cannot be loaded does today (it would end `failed`, with a notice); leaving `ending` uncounted would let a provider hold a run open. Which of the two a `begin` should be is the owner's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":223},"end":{"line":233},"content_hash":"dfaeeb3baf2998756f1eda1a55f7d3d23bc098ce2e6853efb146a71eb3310c43"},"summary":"A `begin` that fails is retried without a count and an `ending` that fails is counted: left to differ, for the owner","tags":["t7","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885332Z","id":"5bd35594163fc30711fe82064adef60d107c767d0f34e5276ff5fee3d49f6435","body":{"detail":"Thirteen runs, each plainly and with the driver dying at three points, the died ones resumed four ways: 13 × (2 + 3 × 4) = 182 runs. Not among them: a stop after a script whose driver died (held by `golden::endings::a_stop_records_the_calls_of_a_script_that_never_committed_and_not_its_own_notice`, a golden written before the endings moved and unchanged after); a cancelled script's stop (crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs drives it both ways, since one flag, `Deps::reference`, selects both references); credits spent mid-run, a rejected key, no key, no flag, an unpriced model and the time and credit caps (each has an `ending.*.json` and a `net.ending.*.json` golden written on the code before the move); the three endings of a policy that is not the default; and follow-ups started, which the fake host does not do (crates/clax-server/tests/api_claxbot_run.rs holds them, unchanged and passing). The store's reads were not counted by a hook, which the store does not have: the calls that reach the host are compared (`reached_the_host`, `roster`).","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":560},"end":{"line":560},"content_hash":"7208f707d80dc909b75247a38db8b8ce188d2631500a677e4c3dd2223291d327"},"summary":"What the endings' comparison does not drive","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.885695Z","id":"5a6fe4be0a6f8bfeb94f5f2891dbfda4529b84564d44c9feb2b701ae7a9c8cec","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::awaiting_ended` (default: none) is asked of each provider by `ending::redeliver` (crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs), which `Driver::start` calls (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs); each run named that has ended is told `ended` with the end rebuilt from its row and terminal step (`stored_end`). Nothing new is stored: the provider is what remembers. `clax` names the last run of each thread with a follow-up queued and no run at work (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs, `awaiting`); the fake names the runs it was told are ending and not that they ended. Held: `generic::a_provider_is_told_a_run_ended_when_the_tick_that_ended_it_died` (a tick dies after its `give_up` commit, no provider is told; a starting driver tells the fake once, with `End::GaveUp` as stored; a second start tells no one) and `endings::a_run_whose_tick_died_after_its_end_is_ended_for_its_surface_when_a_driver_starts` (`clax`: the host is told to stop showing the run, and the notice is on the thread once). With the call taken out of `Driver::start` both fail. Before this, a tick that died after its terminal commit left the roster on until the record lapsed and the follow-ups to `BotHost::start_waiting`; a test's crash hook was not such a death, since the run loop called `working(false)` after it.","kind":"comment","references":"3932a279e840d0a976a806d10b61fc0ad3c51b21ab3400541c54f555f7e345e9","summary":"`ended` is told again when a driver starts, for the runs a provider says it still awaits the end of","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.886049Z","id":"6939988d2c3f8bcff99c9c00455119e1c7cfb0e1f33af336d48e4dcf018b145a","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs `Tick::end`: every binding's `ending` is called in each attempt whatever another answered; an `Err` or a clash is counted once for the attempt (`Store::note_commit_failure`) and fails the tick; `Tick::step` ends a step that reached `max_commit_failures` as `failed`; in the `failed` step and in a cancelled script's stop the failure is logged, that binding's fields are left out and the step commits. Tests: crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs `an_ending_that_fails_is_counted_and_bounded_and_the_other_binding_is_still_asked`, `an_ending_that_fails_once_is_asked_again_and_writes_nothing_twice`, `a_clash_in_a_terminal_step_is_counted_and_the_failed_step_commits_without_it`. An error that says the driver lost its claim (`run_not_claimed`, `step_mismatch`) is not counted: the tick waits as not its own.","kind":"comment","references":"750264730300120087ed2b176f6033e541597a035a67d87dbbfb477738a3112c","summary":"The run loop's part of `ending`'s failure contract is built and held","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T18:44:21.886402Z","id":"cce6f37731ed8edf29422752b1db39713d6db083b3ca081221cc61b918165153","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs: `Tick::clax` is `#[cfg(test)]` and folded only there; the run loop's endings read nothing of it (crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs), and `clax`'s provider folds `Tally::of(cx.history)` in `begin` and in `ending`. The tick holds its run's `RunInfo` and providers lazily (`Tick::bound`, a `OnceLock`), made from the row by `Deps::info` and `ClaxProvider::of`: what is left for the change that reads a run's bindings is to make that the tick's own state.","kind":"comment","references":"9647b192ba2cb7be8b1f393d3558f9ae9fa273b4f374501bb5ab91fb13c18df7","summary":"`Tick::clax` is no field of the run loop: it is kept for the library's test builds, where the two references read it","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:49:39.462926Z","id":"587b5411f450991be1729a1ecfe488996b441227e47e80f453934c5490f4b947","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"969bc09b395720454619b0fd8e0244917452ef750b83e5fed4793e0cc2ca7d17","summary":"`tools` now also takes `finish`, whose description and schema come from the run's kind (crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs, `Finish::tool`) and not from the policy: they hold still for a run, since `SurfaceProvider::definition` is the same for as long as the provider lives (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, its doc). `run_script`'s size is still read from `self.limits` on every turn, so this concern stands as recorded.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:49:39.463126Z","id":"9bb28c7d908018d96898dc79162329e5b2cad3aeff7cbdf016d61f73b3efd45e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/tests/finish.rs and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/finish.rs run `fake.verdict` (crates/clax-bot/src/fake.d.ts) with `Deps::with_surfaces(..).with_kind(\"fake.verdict\")`; `Deps::info` lays the kind over the one `ClaxProvider::info` reads from the row (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs, `info`). When the tick reads a run's bindings from its row and `Deps::surfaces` goes, the kind needs the same: a stored kind on the row, or these tests lose their only way to make a run of a kind that is not `clax`'s.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":295},"end":{"line":299},"content_hash":"24c11e55a2f94b8ece58e549958855b3a1ed2aa0dd240a298b8d5cf8b9d89218"},"summary":"Tests make a run of a host's kind only through `Deps::with_kind`: a run's row keeps a `RequestKind`, so `RunInfo::kind` is otherwise always `clax.*`","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:49:39.463232Z","id":"01eee5be4354cbc8e54417d1bd1cffc96518446d26b7b342261c36f7aec3afff","body":{"detail":"`Done` is `{summary: string}` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts) and `check_result` refuses a field the type does not list, so `{\"summary\": \"done\", \"extra\": 1}` would be `invalid_result` under the typed rule. Today's runs finish with it, and an input without a string `summary` is answered \"The input of finish does not match its schema; nothing ran.\" with no count (tests/golden/answer.tool-input.finish-not-its-schema.json). Both are kept for `clax` so that no clax.dev run changes: tests/finish.rs `a_finish_of_claxs_kind_takes_a_summary_beside_keys_its_type_lacks` and `a_finish_of_claxs_kind_without_a_string_summary_is_answered_without_a_bound` hold them. Moving `clax` onto the typed rule changes what a model is told and adds a bound; the owner's call.","kind":"waiver","span":{"start":{"line":124},"end":{"line":128},"content_hash":"8780b7a116b71f9208a6b9f43658a1fb0a91fe3881eb5c35751191e68409986f"},"summary":"A `finish` of Clax's own kinds is read as before kinds had types: keys beside `summary` are ignored, which `Kind::check_result` would refuse","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:49:39.463294Z","id":"22692226f2cb450092ecff698fa8566f4b696215d09cc2037194e3f0b97c2ef4","body":{"detail":"In a row, a model that puts any other step between two refused results (a script, a turn the nudge answers, a call of another tool) starts the count again, so `RunLimits::max_invalid_results` never gives it up; the run's own caps (`max_steps`, credits, minutes) still end it. A count over the run would fold every turn's `next` in `Control::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs), which other changes are editing; the count in `next` needs nothing folded and is held by tests/finish.rs `a_step_that_is_not_a_refused_finish_ends_the_row`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":96},"end":{"line":96},"content_hash":"bf638ed8ee5e2858acb5cde68136af9bfa40d39bdc57bdeec8e26384cc384a4e"},"summary":"Count the results a run's kind refused over the whole run, rather than in a row in the turn's `next` (`invalid_results`)","tags":["revisit:a run of a host's kind is seen alternating refused results with other steps until a cap ends it","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:49:39.463374Z","id":"3b2fcf61d9e1f2f96be4349165e447b24aa3c3561ec6b8b85e730c3bb8572b37","body":{"detail":"The terminal step of a kind that is not `clax`'s keeps the checked value as `result` and has no `summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs, `ending`); `step_summary` now passes `result` on beside `summary`, but the run panel's finish step reads `summary` (its fixture: web/e2e/claxbot.spec.ts line 30). Until a host's runs are shown there, a finished host run's step reads as empty.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":350},"end":{"line":353},"content_hash":"4b219b37669ad2e6c501874edbf01998a04a3d9fdd7b5a20d08acce01570d0e5"},"summary":"A run of a host's kind finishes with `summary: null` in its `step_done`, and nothing in the run panel shows its `result`","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:49:39.463541Z","id":"289b758be918c16d548167502c7fb7ddebe0ae7295e4af9c5f76268f0fca7637","body":{"detail":"`finish`'s description is `Ends the run: the work is done.` followed by the doc comment of the kind's result interface, and its input schema is `Kind::result_schema`, which writes a field's doc comment as its `description` (crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs, `Finish::tool`; crates/clax-surface/src/manifest.rs). So editing either changes every request a thread or brief run sends, which the `request.*.json` and `request.*.wire.json` goldens of crates/clax-bot/tests/golden pin byte for byte.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":222},"end":{"line":225},"content_hash":"c4dd781a32f5bafd2b7904b3d502e1f4a70a91737ad658b3c3a44f700817f3c2"},"summary":"`Done`'s doc comment ends the description of `finish` for Clax's kinds, and a doc comment on `summary` would be a `description` in the tool's schema","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:04:44.328647Z","id":"5ddda6960398118315c3715cc7cdae52a9e012188cf75d1d5845b6144eb31317","body":{"detail":"`Rule::Undeclared` gives `finish` the schema `{\"type\": \"object\"}` and refuses every call with `the run's kind <k> is declared by none of its surfaces` (lines 133-135), counted as a refused result. Nothing a model does can finish such a run, so the turns are spent for nothing and the end's code names the model's result rather than the run's set-up. The run could instead fail at its first step with a code of its own. It is not reachable today: a run's row keeps a `RequestKind` (`thread`, `brief`, both declared in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts), and only `Deps::with_kind` makes another. `Finish::of` also looks for the kind's definition only among the run's bindings; a registry that holds the kind's provider apart from the bindings should give that provider's definition here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":87},"end":{"line":93},"content_hash":"e69eac703185bdfef7cf8f7125aea89159528d6ca42bc2053a40a0d701c1270e"},"summary":"A run whose kind none of its surfaces declares is offered a `finish` no value passes, and gives up `invalid_result` only after the model has been refused `max_invalid_results` + 1 times (never, under `RunLimits::unbounded`)","tags":["t9-review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:04:44.328676Z","id":"2c66963724b4bd96162219e7ab9232969ed16ee30f818c96db4c3e6c5e72d0a0","body":{"detail":"`Kind::check_result` checks the value as a script holds it (crates/clax-surface/src/value.rs:348, `check_held`): a whole handle `{id, media_type, bytes, sha256, ...}` is checked for its fields and types, not that the ID names stored bytes this run may read or that `sha256`/`bytes`/`media_type` are theirs. A script's value has handles Clax made, but `finish`'s input is the model's text, so a result's handle can name any ID with any claimed digest, and `ending`/`ended` of the kind's surface receive it as checked. No kind declares `Content` in its result yet (crates/clax-bot/src/fake.d.ts, the shell file in crates/clax-surface/src/tests). Before one does: resolve each handle in a finished result against the store (and the run's reach), or refuse `Content` and `Image` in a kind's result.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":130},"content_hash":"53dedd915abf16fed3dbdcb153b75488708d3859f73b80643384868d381c6b2f"},"summary":"A kind's result is checked for shape only: a `Content` or `Image` in a model's `finish` is whatever handle the model wrote, which the kind's surface is then given as the run's result","tags":["t9-review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.892605Z","id":"ecc38e4a8d75e51385294deb1731e0cf4be2704ae1813d7d8e556bfa3926e9a7","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"Registration and authority\": 'It lasts while its registrant is connected, and a short while after') names no figure. `HubLimits::lease_ms` defaults to 300,000. Dropping a registration frees its definition and makes `serve` answer `unknown_registration`; its calls and ended runs stay in the store until their own deadlines, and a call with no deadline (a policy that sets none) for a host that never comes back stays for ever: nothing here ends it. Whether a registration's end should settle its calls (`unavailable`, or `unknown_outcome` for one that was sent) is the design's sentence 'with no one to answer, their calls to it are `unavailable`' read strictly; it is not done, because a host that goes on as its registration answers them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":414},"end":{"line":416},"content_hash":"abb93e40561b986aa06f1fa69008d2753aadd2299090dadadb4c986375ef1dd5"},"summary":"A registration is kept five minutes after it stops being connected; the design says 'a short while': for the owner to set","tags":["status:needs-decision","claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.892702Z","id":"21d343fac2be4321c1397e2c94c8d3e82352ae260a4c679d844f5a505e133e8d","body":{"detail":"`Hub::answer` passes on an error whose code is lowercase letters, digits and `_` and no longer than the definition's `name_bytes`, and turns any other into `invalid_result`. It does not look at `Op::throws`. So a host can throw `unavailable`, `expired`, `unknown_outcome` or `invalid_result`, which the script cannot tell from Clax's own, and a code its definition (and so the model's declaration) never named. Holding errors to `throws` would refuse hosts that have a general failure to report unless the format gains codes every operation may throw.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1393},"end":{"line":1394},"content_hash":"d905ca89993c93c3a8613e7ce09f51bf6d85f061a9dd56ed07f790e485d365e3"},"summary":"A host's error is thrown to the script under any well-formed code, not only the codes the operation declares with `@throws`: for the owner to decide","tags":["status:needs-decision","claxbot-surfaces","protocol","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.892990Z","id":"ea0562956be8128a8fa5dbc80b1bddf24738af32bbc0bfc01d68bc770163fa18","body":{"detail":"A run keeps its definition by hash (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"A run\"), and `PendingCall` carries no hash. When a host goes on as its registration with a changed definition (`Hub::register`), an answer to a call made before the change is checked by the new one: an operation that is gone settles as `invalid_result`, and a result type that changed is checked as changed (tests: `a_registration_gone_on_with_keeps_its_serves_and_checks_by_its_new_definition`). Checking by the run's own definition needs the definitions kept by hash (the store) and the hash on the call; `clax bot surface diff` is the design's answer to whether the new host can serve the old runs at all.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1382},"end":{"line":1386},"content_hash":"2c7ad9adc1a565cd624e576e69ba76b738c88eb27687da4a275c4ee57de2b57d"},"summary":"An answer is checked against the definition its registration has when the answer arrives, not the one the run was made with","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.893120Z","id":"e4a8a5c0c131c5adc277c63cbe47216a16d534c88cb2daee714050467510faea","body":{"detail":"`Pending` is written so that `clax-store` implements it method for method. Beyond opening, accepting, answering, expiring and listing calls and recording, listing and acknowledging ends, the hub needs: `sent_calls` (whether a call was sent decides `unavailable` against `expired` or `unknown_outcome` at its deadline, so it must outlast a restart: a sent write reported as `unavailable` would say nothing happened); `count_calls` and `count_ends` (the queue limits, without reading every call's arguments); and `expire_calls` takes the function that makes each call's failure (`unanswered`), so the wording and codes stay with the rules and the settling stays one transaction. `open_call` is where a key is held to one call (`OpenedCall::Same`, `KeyReused`), `answer_call` refuses at or after the deadline. A store whose methods differ needs an adapter, or this trait changed to match. The module names only `clax_surface`, `clax_core::audit::sha256_hex` and JSON, so that it can move below the routes (`clax-http`) as it is.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":274},"end":{"line":276},"content_hash":"8505adf54651218117a56476c8771b655b70ee5136b9ada29132f805a9de95a5"},"summary":"What the store must implement for the hub is this trait: three of its methods go beyond open, accept, answer, expire and list","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.893217Z","id":"a04733e7274baa58fbdf8979653fd7d3504c94a136e96dd1669ee46d2fb9f367","body":{"detail":"`withheld` looks at each operation's parameters and result. A kind's request, message and result are not looked at: the design's own example (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces/shell.d.ts`, `Triage.clip?: Image`) has a kind that can be started with an image. Until content is carried, whatever starts a run of a host's kind has to refuse a request that holds a `Content`, or the kind; `Registered::withheld` has no way to say a kind is withheld (its entries are `{op, reason}`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":919},"end":{"line":922},"content_hash":"211d864913d981bc103f8ff9fc1282be92540ecffb3f3c55ba0cb379abfa66c5"},"summary":"Only operations are withheld for naming `Content` or `Image`; a kind whose request or result names one is not","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.893560Z","id":"adc215a5e7d8d16e5808b3b68fdf5b58410ddab2746d33b603eeddc02ae6adb2","body":{"detail":"The design's table (\"Deadlines, retries and what a call may promise\") says of `@once` alone that a call 'is not sent again once the provider has accepted it'. Here `accepted` stops the sending of any call: the host said it has it. The cost: a host that accepts a call and then loses it (a restart) is not sent it again, and the call ends at its deadline as `unknown_outcome` (`expired` for a read), where sending it again would let a host that keeps its promise of a key perform it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":56},"end":{"line":59},"content_hash":"b9038b866c23a85cb7b5c175715cd8d0bbba1f36c9a7794eaf1a4c7551f1a413"},"summary":"Sending an accepted call again unless its operation is `@once`","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","revisit:hosts lose accepted calls across their own restarts and runs end unknown_outcome for it","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:38:58.352774Z","id":"f9ee04c5450ddb7e21262ab87631e9f6500700204f7aa328eb35e525b358404c","body":{"detail":"Each was made alone in this file and `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all` passed: (1) asking the bindings' `ending` in reverse order (line 186, `surfaces.iter().rev()`), against the documented kind's-surface-first order; (2) not counting a cancelled script's session clash with no `ending` failure (line 230 without `| (None, Some(true))`); (3) a wrong end rebuilt for redelivery (`stored_end`, line 303: a finished run's summary taken from `reason`, a stopped run rebuilt as `End::Failed`); (4) `stored_end` answering a stop for a run that has not ended, so `redeliver` (line 334) would tell `ended` of a running run named by `awaiting_ended`. None reaches a person today: a Clax run has one binding, `ClaxProvider::run_ended` ignores the end and result, and `ClaxProvider::awaiting` names only a terminal last run of a thread with no queued, running or stopping run (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs `active_on_in`). They become observable with the first host's provider.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":184},"end":{"line":364},"content_hash":"6e4f45d02f679b7501d6a48874e951123acb4878b87129bd7a269995f9686674"},"suggested_fix":"In crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs: two fake bindings whose `ending` hooks are recorded in order; a cancelled script whose sessions clash, pinning the step's `failures`; a fake that awaits each kind of end, pinning the `End` and result it is told on a driver's start; a fake that names a running run, pinning that it is not told.","summary":"Four rules of the run loop's end that no test holds, each observable only once a run has a second provider","tags":["review","test-gap","for:T8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:22:07.589452Z","id":"e1aef121d773ac61ae56042278d71814d4bbbc427348c6b3b11e1109e759dcfb","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"5ddda6960398118315c3715cc7cdae52a9e012188cf75d1d5845b6144eb31317","summary":"Left as it is, for the change that gives the tick a run's bindings from its row (T8 of docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md): it is the place to find the kind's definition (`Finish::of` takes it from the bound providers today) and to refuse a run whose kind none of its surfaces declares when the run starts, not after `max_invalid_results` refusals. `Deps::with_kind` (record 9bb28c7d) goes with it.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:22:07.589876Z","id":"b7efa40842091258b3ec84192551855d9598c3653c5da3098b31da3f41475c2e","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"2c66963724b4bd96162219e7ab9232969ed16ee30f818c96db4c3e6c5e72d0a0","summary":"Left for the content step: a `Content` or `Image` the model writes in its `finish` is checked for the shape of a whole handle only (`Kind::check_result`, as `check_held`), and nothing yet resolves it against the store or the kind's surface. Whoever lands the content step owns checking that the handle names stored bytes the run may give.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.305335Z","id":"09f8ab5bce8fa4bd01d6e5b474fd9f0d4c5fa904632874d1f39b70ebf3b993ea","body":{"detail":"`Hub::register` refuses a named ID only when the hub holds it under another name (`RegisterError::UnknownRegistration`). Once the hub does not hold it (its lease ended, or the daemon restarted), any ID of `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,256}` is made a registration of whatever name the request is for. Shown with a scratch test against this module: register `desk` as `r1`, enqueue `desk.lamps`, let the lease end, register `shelf` with `registration: \"r1\"`: Ok; the next `serve` of `shelf` is sent the call with `op: \"desk.lamps\"` (the prefix is not stripped, `Hub::due`), and its answer settles it `invalid_result` (\"not an operation of the definition its host now serves\"). Registering `desk` with `registration: \"my-constant-id\"`, an ID nothing issued, is Ok too. So a host written against docs/contract.md (\"Going on as a registration\") may invent a constant ID, two hosts may share one, and a call can reach another surface's host. Narrowing this after hosts exist breaks them. The fix needs the store to keep each issued ID with its surface name (the `Pending` trait has no registrations today): resume only an ID the daemon issued, for the same name, else `unknown_registration`; draw IDs with 128 bits of randomness. Related: 4e794bfd23 (the owner's decision on resuming at all).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1086},"end":{"line":1099},"content_hash":"7ecfe76f661a24e46a443b4b22f902a5cffb2347b0ca41b5a8edab76dec42d46"},"summary":"Going on as a registration takes any well-formed ID, one no daemon issued and one of another surface once the hub has dropped it","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","protocol","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.305528Z","id":"f85124ccd6e57799ca4d31f7fa75b494220b6a8f12ba05aac3319ca8c756d054","body":{"detail":"`Hub::open` records each ID it took in `taken` and ignores any later answer naming it, `accepted` and `retry_after_ms` included. A host that says `accepted` when a call arrives and has its result by the next serve sends `[{id, accepted: true}, {id, result}]`: scratch test against this module, the call stays open and accepted, so it is not sent again, and the run is thrown `unknown_outcome` at its deadline (or waits for ever with none) though its host answered. docs/contract.md says \"a second answer for one ID\" is ignored, which a host reads as a second *result*. Suggested rule: within one serve `accepted` and `retry_after_ms` never block a later `result` or `error` for the same ID; only a second settling answer is ignored.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1306},"end":{"line":1328},"content_hash":"7880a3f09bc44dbbe441fee5e73bdfbefd6b5a8c5cff7abfcaadcecafeb4f9ef"},"summary":"`accepted` and then a result for one call in one serve: the result is ignored and the call is never sent again","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","protocol","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.305702Z","id":"e1d0ecc6ffb13f4f8ebab145380a7a9e1e8a1feb4796e9b2600df337a56592d7","body":{"detail":"A registration is dropped only `connected_ms + lease_ms` (315 s) after it was last served, and `register` refuses a new one while `registrations_per_name` (8) or `registrations` (32) are held. Scratch test against this module: eight registrations of `desk` at 0 ms that never serve; a ninth is refused `TooManyRegistrations` at 10 ms, 60 s, 200 s and 315,000 ms, and taken at 315,008 ms. A host that crash-loops and registers anew on each start (docs/contract.md tells a host given `unknown_registration` to register again, and says nothing of keeping its ID), or any other holder of the daemon token, does this. Suggested: when a limit is reached, drop the oldest registration of the name (or of all) that is not connected, instead of refusing; its calls and ends stay in the store, and its host can still go on as it by ID.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1139},"end":{"line":1157},"content_hash":"b047376111db6cbe0a2fbd0204dcabebe1186f58ec786983bdfec050e9b4262e"},"summary":"Eight registrations that are never served lock the name out for five minutes and fifteen seconds","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.305824Z","id":"b697ffd0eb43d0e6867e7b8af8cb73f9dbd07a52bd038c5e502e83ee911d7fa0","body":{"detail":"`NewCall::deadline` and `PendingCall::deadline` are `Millis`, and the store lane keeps them across restarts so a call that waits can be answered a day later (docs/contract.md, \"Calls that wait\"). A monotonic clock (`Instant`) starts again with the process, so a stored deadline means another instant after a restart. The clock has to be one that survives a restart: milliseconds since the Unix epoch, with the hub's clamp (`Hub::advance`) absorbing a step back. Say so on `Millis` before the store and the routes pick a clock.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":135},"end":{"line":139},"content_hash":"ec4cc539eb67e40443cd972f267ca409f1ff07fad3b17f95bfc4b02aceeb7a15"},"summary":"`Millis` is \"the caller's own monotonic clock\", but deadlines go into a store that outlasts the process","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.305887Z","id":"faeb89d8d18ad44b1f01a17f7b2907cb2f5ad2b8399c02b657dc2f9e17daf421","body":{"detail":"1. `Hub::next_wake` leaves deadlines to \"the store's to tell\", and `Pending` has no method that tells it: without one the daemon must sweep `Hub::expire` on a fixed interval to wake runs whose host never serves again. A `next_deadline() -> Option<Millis>` fits SQL (`MIN(deadline)` over open calls). 2. `Hub::ready` and `Hub::close` call `calls_for`, which answers every open call with its arguments: up to 256 calls of up to 1 MiB each for every `ready`, which a route calls on every wake of every open serve. `ready` needs only IDs, `sent` and `accepted`; a summary method keeps the arguments in the store until `close`. A serve answer can also be 64 deliveries of 1 MiB: a byte bound beside `deliveries_per_serve` keeps it under what a host and the route take. 3. A `serve` evicted by the fifth open (`serves_per_registration`) is only dropped from `Reg::holds`: `Hub::ready` still answers false for it and `Hub::close` answers it as any other, so the route holds it its whole wait and cannot tell it was evicted (scratch test: five opens, `held().serves == 4`, the first still not ready, and a new call then delivered by all five). Either `ready` answers true for a serve not in `holds`, or the eviction rule goes. 4. The wake hook is called under whatever lock the route holds the hub with; the route must take its `Notified` before it checks `ready`, or a call enqueued between `open` and the wait is missed until the wait ends.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":274},"end":{"line":340},"content_hash":"7b493d4ad1e0ba30da6de9be37759862239fde2fab14627aefc0ab8052f84560"},"summary":"What the store and routes will need of `Pending` and `Hub` that is not here: the next deadline, a due list without arguments, and word of an evicted serve","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.305941Z","id":"5b68b239cbeac170ed9294695ff4a42c9caf58bba09aace07113a73eb66695ed","body":{"detail":"`MemoryPending::open_call` compares arguments only while the call is open (`outcome.is_some() || held.args == call.args`), because settling empties `args`; the `Pending::open_call` and `OpenedCall::Same` docs hold the store lane to that. Scratch test against this module: `desk.notify(\"one\")` under key `k`, answered, then `desk.notify(\"two\")` under `k`: `Ok(\"c1\")`, the first call's outcome. docs/contract.md (\"Keys\") says another call under the key is refused, `key_reused`. Under a replayed step the arguments are the same, so the difference shows only for a script that is not deterministic, which is when `key_reused` matters. A store can keep a digest of the arguments when it drops them. Also `Hub::enqueue`'s full-queue path finds only open calls, so a settled call's key answers `QueueFull` there.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1796},"end":{"line":1808},"content_hash":"627629a8a6049e5ef41c6c9b56be341804c70777be885788df396addc10929c7"},"summary":"A settled call's key with other arguments answers the settled call, where the contract says `key_reused`","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.307258Z","id":"1a13fed5776fa5d5eb589040062d5e9bf25e38fda12bd0ea723a261ae432246b","body":{"detail":"Tightening this after hosts exist rewrites errors they rely on, so it is cheaper now. The codes the core gives meanings to (`expired`, `unknown_outcome`, `invalid_result`, `key_reused`, `unknown_op`, `invalid_args`, `internal_error`) say things about delivery a host cannot know; `unavailable` (nothing happened) is one a host can truthfully say. Anything else undeclared settles as `invalid_result`, as a result of the wrong type already does, keeping the host's code and message in the message.","kind":"comment","references":"21d343fac2be4321c1397e2c94c8d3e82352ae260a4c679d844f5a505e133e8d","summary":"Recommendation: hold a host to its `@throws` plus `unavailable`, and settle any other code as `invalid_result` with the code in the message","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.878014Z","id":"c7e3eccdb961e0395926194b02220ededfc1fbc29ce9e1d5dde93ad8f3894eb8","body":{"detail":"`Hub::register` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) now takes the store: a new registration's ID (`fresh`, checked by `is_registration_id`: 32 lowercase hexadecimal digits) is recorded with its surface's name (`Pending::issue_registration`) before it is answered, and a request that names one is taken only when the hub holds it under that name or `Pending::registration_name` gives that name; anything else is `unknown_registration` (404). The reviewer's case is `only_an_id_this_daemon_gave_a_registration_of_the_surface_is_gone_on_with` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs: `shelf` naming `desk`'s ID, an ID nothing issued, `my-constant-id`, each refused while the hub holds the registration and after it has dropped it, and a `shelf` serve sent nothing of `desk`'s. The fixture's script has both refusals (docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json, steps at 7 and 9 ms). The requirement on `fresh` (128 bits from a source fit for secrets) is on `Hub::register`.","kind":"comment","references":"09f8ab5bce8fa4bd01d6e5b474fd9f0d4c5fa904632874d1f39b70ebf3b993ea","summary":"Done: only an ID issued for the same surface is gone on with, and IDs are 32 hexadecimal digits the caller draws","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.878817Z","id":"c9d7aad7a5fa56a66fc04461273dddc82cbc504f8aa08c89954da29a1dbb1bae","body":{"detail":"`Hub::enqueue` opens a call with its registration's hash; `Hub::answer` reads the definition by the call's hash from the hub's own (`Hub::definition`: every registration's, and up to `HubLimits::definitions` others, the rest read from `Pending::definition_text` as the canonical manifest). An answer whose call's definition the store does not have is not taken (`ServeError::Storage`), so the host sends it again. Tests in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs: `an_answer_is_checked_by_the_definition_its_call_was_made_under` (an old `ask` takes a string and a new one a number after the same registration changed its definition; an operation the new definition lacks is still answered), `after_a_restart_…` (the definition is read back from the store by a hub that never registered it).","kind":"comment","references":"ea0562956be8128a8fa5dbc80b1bddf24738af32bbc0bfc01d68bc770163fa18","summary":"Done: a call keeps the hash of its definition, and its answer is checked by that","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.879176Z","id":"dd42a040c09541673d9f20592c4cf8e178f78468f72d081b0dea51bc858d239d","body":{"detail":"`Hub::open` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) now remembers only the calls an answer of the request settled; anything after a result or an error for a call is ignored, and `accepted` and `retry_after_ms` block nothing. Test `accepted_and_retry_after_do_not_keep_a_result_in_the_same_serve_from_settling` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs; the fixture's script has `[accepted, result, error]` for one call in one serve (docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json, step at 49 ms); docs/contract.md, \"Serving\", \"Answers are taken in their order\".","kind":"comment","references":"f85124ccd6e57799ca4d31f7fa75b494220b6a8f12ba05aac3319ca8c756d054","summary":"Done: `accepted` and `retry_after_ms` do not keep a later result or error for the same call from settling it","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.880282Z","id":"c1045cd078a3abf227976dbf4f8730966304a0ca7a8d76e823a2108190b6b20b","body":{"detail":"Documented on `Millis` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs), with the clamp kept for a clock that steps back (`Hub::advance`); test `a_clock_that_goes_back_is_read_as_where_it_was` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs, and the generated sequences now give the hub instants earlier than it has seen.","kind":"comment","references":"b697ffd0eb43d0e6867e7b8af8cb73f9dbd07a52bd038c5e502e83ee911d7fa0","summary":"Done: `Millis` is milliseconds since the Unix epoch","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.880633Z","id":"5aef7625569f9afff2e41d8ecd973d8bcb51d7612b0822729ed869ddb73de8cd","body":{"detail":"`Hub::register` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) drops the oldest unconnected registration of the name for `registrations_per_name`, and of any name for `registrations`, and refuses (`too_many_registrations`, 429) only when every one that could go is connected. What a dropped registration had stays in the store and its host goes on with it by ID. Test `at_a_limit_the_oldest_registration_that_is_not_connected_makes_room` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs: refused at 15,000 ms while the oldest is still connected, taken at 15,001. The generated sequences hold each registration to the same rule.","kind":"comment","references":"e1d0ecc6ffb13f4f8ebab145380a7a9e1e8a1feb4796e9b2600df337a56592d7","summary":"Done: at a limit the oldest registration that is not connected makes room","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.880983Z","id":"cf90c3cb4aa8989517f2ad85e63c85cfafcc714e76bf39f9656c2abcb4c73536","body":{"detail":"`OpenedCall::Same` now requires the same arguments whether or not the call is settled, and the `Pending` trait says a store that drops a settled call's arguments keeps a digest of them; `MemoryPending` keeps what a call was opened with. The queue limit moved into `Pending::open_call` (`OpenedCall::Full`), which answers for a key it holds before it looks at the limit, so a settled call's key no longer answers `QueueFull`. Test `a_key_is_one_calls_for_as_long_as_the_store_holds_the_call` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs.","kind":"comment","references":"5b68b239cbeac170ed9294695ff4a42c9caf58bba09aace07113a73eb66695ed","summary":"Done: a key is one call's for as long as the store holds the call, settled or not","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.881331Z","id":"dd66e6f17e9aae912b7e7be91f7e26337fb6358e5cc6009a1da7f3fca3e39756","body":{"detail":"1. `Pending::next_deadline`, and `Hub::next_wake(store)` gives the earliest of it and the hub's own instants. 2. `Pending::pending_for` answers `PendingSummary` (each open call without its arguments, each end without its result, with their sizes); `Hub::ready` and `Hub::open` read only that, and `Hub::close` loads whole only what it sends (`calls_for(registration, ids)`, `ends_for`). `HubLimits::deliveries_bytes` (8 MiB) bounds one answer beside `deliveries_per_serve`, with one delivery always sent. 3. `Hub::ready` answers `Ready::{Wait, Deliver, Ended}`: `Ended` for a serve a later one replaced, whose wait is over, or whose registration is gone, and `Hub::close` answers such a serve with nothing; `Serve::until` gives its instant. 4. The `Wake` type's documentation says the hook runs under the hub's lock and that a serve listens before it first asks `ready`. Tests in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs: `one_serve_more_than_a_registration_may_have_open_ends_the_oldest`, `a_serve_is_answered_with_no_more_bytes_than_the_limit_and_one_delivery_at_the_least`, `calls_and_ends_do_not_wait_for_ever`.","kind":"comment","references":"faeb89d8d18ad44b1f01a17f7b2907cb2f5ad2b8399c02b657dc2f9e17daf421","summary":"Done: the next deadline, a summary without arguments, word of an ended serve, and where the hook is called","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.881686Z","id":"362b5bd629d9363c92ed0a3f0f2822e90bbc9574332b4b006b9d49ebe1e4a2bc","body":{"detail":"`Pending` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs): `issue_registration`, `registration_name`, `keep_definition`, `definition_text`; `open_call(call, limit, now)` (key identity and the queue limit in one change), `pending_for`, `calls_for(registration, ids)`, `sent_calls`, `accept_call`, `answer_call`, `expire_calls`, `next_deadline`; `record_end(end, limit, now)`, `ends_for(registration, ids)`, `acknowledge_end`, `expire_ends(before)`. `count_calls` and `count_ends` are gone (the limits are `open_call`'s and `record_end`'s). `NewCall` and `CallSummary` carry the definition's hash, a deadline that is always set (`HubLimits::call_ms`, 24 h, for a call given none) and the size of the arguments.","kind":"comment","references":"e4a8a5c0c131c5adc277c63cbe47216a16d534c88cb2daee714050467510faea","summary":"The trait as it now reads: sixteen methods","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.882041Z","id":"15ee0f21d48eb3e4c376c390f95716998d589f88225039b510a19a86b1db16cb","body":{"detail":"`Hub::answer` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) passes on an error whose code the operation declares in the call's own definition, or `unavailable` (retryable); any other, the core's codes among them, settles as `invalid_result` with the host's code and message in its message. Test `a_host_throws_what_its_operation_declares_or_unavailable_and_nothing_else` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs; fixture steps at 57 and 59 ms; docs/contract.md, \"Serving\", `error`. The decision is still the owner's to confirm.","kind":"comment","references":"21d343fac2be4321c1397e2c94c8d3e82352ae260a4c679d844f5a505e133e8d","summary":"Built as the reply recommends: a host throws its operation's `@throws` codes or `unavailable`","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.882385Z","id":"d8636656845b8934aece5145dbe7e1b0179cfab22043d6c9f30ac8fe33da4b87","body":{"detail":"A call given no deadline gets `HubLimits::call_ms` (24 h) from when it is made, and an unacknowledged end is forgotten after `HubLimits::ended_ms` (seven days), both through `Hub::expire` (test `calls_and_ends_do_not_wait_for_ever` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs). At a registration limit the oldest unconnected registration makes room. The lease's own figure, 300,000 ms, is unchanged and still the owner's to set.","kind":"comment","references":"ecc38e4a8d75e51385294deb1731e0cf4be2704ae1813d7d8e556bfa3926e9a7","summary":"Two of its parts are answered: nothing waits for ever, and a full table no longer locks a name out","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.882735Z","id":"eb7518369a116c8dd1849b84a70cfd74f676eb42ca70de249a1f956906f21a05","body":{"detail":"`withheld` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) asks `Definition::run_operations` for each kind (nothing else narrowing) and lists the operations it gives `clax_surface::Withheld::Content`, so there is one rule; an operation no kind allows, which no run has in any case, is no longer listed. `RunOperations::request_content` is what whoever starts a run reads to refuse a kind whose request holds content; the registration's answer still has no way to say so.","kind":"comment","references":"a04733e7274baa58fbdf8979653fd7d3504c94a136e96dd1669ee46d2fb9f367","summary":"The rule is now `clax_surface`'s own, which also says what a kind's request holds","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.530357Z","id":"c10af962bd711eac884da467a5495c0e4c3ab9f7cfbc49e9a314349b1debbfd3","body":{"detail":"`Deps::info`, the `Deps::providers` function and `Tick::bound` are deleted. `tick` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs) reads the row with `Store::run_with_steps_as_made` and calls `Providers::bind` (crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs), which makes the `RunInfo` from `bot_runs.kind`, `request_json` and `bindings_json` and takes each binding's provider from `Deps.providers`; `Tick::surfaces` holds the result. `Driver::start` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs) calls `ending::redeliver` and then `SurfaceProvider::driver_started` on each registered provider; `ClaxProvider::driver_started` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs) is `BotHost::start_waiting`. `redeliver` iterates `Deps.providers` by registered name. Held by `registry::tests::the_run_loop_names_nothing_of_claxs_but_what_is_listed` and `generic::a_starting_driver_tells_each_registered_provider`. What is left: `Deps::new` registers `ClaxProvider` over `Deps.host` through `Providers::register_clax`, the one place the run loop's side names it.","kind":"comment","references":"24bb1ffa01441de739ce0a1613735af2313b76f77c175b83b12b10110237f0e1","summary":"The three are gone: a tick binds its run from the row through the registry, and a starting driver tells each registered provider, from which `clax`'s calls `BotHost::start_waiting`","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.530753Z","id":"22b8cb59a111dfb27bf4124eeb6aaf93acf807a45fabbe2500cff7f11f6cb8bb","body":{"detail":"The tick asks the policy after it has bound the run (`Providers::bind`), with `Tick::surfaces.info`. `policy::a_policy_is_told_the_run_as_the_run_loop_knows_it` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs) holds that each of the three is told the `RunInfo` of the run its tick is of, with two runs in the store. No host implements the trait. `accepts`, `to_model` and `delivery` are still not methods.","kind":"comment","references":"6e2998d2e665eacc840e1b9e37b34cd1ff53313ebba1d26639b4be1d17747c34","summary":"`run_settings`, `limits` and `history` take `&RunInfo`; the one implementer outside the crate's defaults, `Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs, changed with them","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.531090Z","id":"9f6db327e10e5961b16fb82193f4de711c9047c8d337b03f7bbb4be4c940398e","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"dd0c359d0b211a55b64ecac210110e0e7de4ad569aca0ac22a9e8bf7636a613c","summary":"Still not taken: the policy's methods now take the `RunInfo` the design's `settings(run)` would, and the settings source stays what a tick reads first","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.531417Z","id":"3cb196f91ec127696c7139368178df540b99d1e8f542b4dcfaba54b2b8bd34ca","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs folds the tally where it read the field (two places). `registry::tests::the_run_loop_names_nothing_of_claxs_but_what_is_listed` (crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs) reads the source of run.rs, driver.rs, ending.rs and script.rs before their tests, without comment lines, and fails on `crate::clax`, `ClaxProvider`, `artifact_id`, `base_version`, `thread_ids`, `RequestKind`, `run.request`, `run.web`, `deps.web`, `.context(`, `.start_waiting(` or `.plan_`, and on any line naming the deps' host other than the six it lists.","kind":"comment","references":"9647b192ba2cb7be8b1f393d3558f9ae9fa273b4f374501bb5ab91fb13c18df7","summary":"`Tick::clax` is deleted: the two references fold `clax::Tally::of(&steps)` themselves, and run.rs imports nothing of `crate::clax`","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.531747Z","id":"a2c93b3397adf8a1dbaebd33f3813f39a84bab13f0b5562fc96149be310884a8","body":{"detail":"`ClaxProvider::for_run` is reached through the seam (`SurfaceProvider::for_run`, crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs): `Providers::bind` asks each registered provider once a tick for the provider that tick holds, and `clax` answers one holding the tick's row, so a step reads the row no more than it did. The row is cloned once a tick, where it was cloned once for each tick that took a step. `ClaxProvider::row` still falls back to `Store::run`: the provider registered in `Deps.providers` holds no row and is the one a starting driver asks (`awaiting_ended`, `ended`, `driver_started`). The tests that gave a run other surfaces register a provider (`Deps::with_provider`, `Deps::with_providers`) and write the run's `bindings_json` (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs, `Fake::run_bound`).","kind":"comment","references":"2e0b25fac243b682a52f79e121045ccad01dc9e61167cdb6dc625422854c73fc","summary":"The tick reads the stored bindings and holds a provider for each; `Deps::surfaces`, `Deps::with_surfaces`, `ClaxProvider::info` and `ClaxProvider::of` are deleted; `for_run` and `row` stay","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.532074Z","id":"795350f582b92b030f1f7df8c09b1b13f3a266698192e5d8606c0ec13cc9ae4f","body":{"detail":"`Providers::bind` (crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs) makes each `Binding` with `Binding::from_stored(stored, provider.definition(), provider.standing)` once a tick, so the rule is still the provider's code and still applies to runs already made. Version 1 keeps `granted` only; the change that stores a run's operations (bindings version 2) is the one that can drop `standing`. The fake now answers it (`fake.hidden` is not standing), where its test binding left the operation out by hand.","kind":"comment","references":"06a96d62b78e3f5c3290bde0529f5f562f840acbb3f318a51b6cce4573e8de00","summary":"`standing` is left where it was: reading a run's stored bindings (version 1) did not move it to bind time","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.533080Z","id":"0ee86efd0f3fecfcdbf29b073b6dfe6b238975de03476aeb78802fdcf1d64e4d","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"30795868a45fb7ce9e76dfe33c0371a0dbbe38c2ca967e4d358dcaee60ec969f","summary":"Left as constants: nothing asked for another value, and the driver's start changed only in whom it tells","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.533433Z","id":"7720b2b2ba1d61879806584aad437a9477b85df6d7f7fbda0c09544fcb1308c1","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"971dba6b4669f95d3bf8abb8dbc247b0208d8f3275d59a75271345d77b7027db","summary":"Left as it is: the tick's budget is unchanged","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.533787Z","id":"a37e3f20632253bbe4ded64acc03474cdd8e79168948f035d43e27a02dcec127","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"acfd5bd3b9138601c84806505dcb24e3bedb5f6b0fd21d7de75b05f72ed733b6","summary":"Not changed: only the type the policy's methods are told a run by changed","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.534146Z","id":"38f925d77216dc08772f65afaacbb1302b850cf4a68e0eac12bfaf8a2a645c9b","body":{"detail":"Before: `Store::claim_run`, `Store::run_with_steps` (one statement for the row, one for the steps), `Store::next_step_n`, then the step's own reads. After: the same, with `Store::run_with_steps_as_made` in place of `run_with_steps`: the row's statement selects three more columns of the same row. `ending::redeliver` reads each awaited run the same way, once, as before.","kind":"comment","references":"ab015010025d1e85c0cb59b6670f64187179cdbb10437f7b96e74de9ef050ddb","summary":"Still so. A tick now also parses the run's `bindings_json` and `request_json` and clones its row once, and reads the store no more than it did","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.534502Z","id":"f3afead1b8b786bf36716e7c8d92c08a2e9aaca3569e023a4b572c444a01ffc9","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"a7ee2d9794af062159d9d1922943d0bc1f5dece742b8c4920d208b57eb6908bf","summary":"Not changed: `Tick::steps` is built as it was","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.534852Z","id":"048ee67ab529cb8b3e86add3f14965eacf857843e5dc2b7e57b732e856a50851","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"aa103f11cb0a0e7bd80a027a7f34ecca349e3e3953a7f750a182fe79cb7fb58d","summary":"Not changed for a policy that fails. One failure now comes before the policy is asked: a row whose bindings or request cannot be read fails the tick, uncounted (recorded on crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs)","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.535207Z","id":"e03a097a1149c807bfe20d2c9da2e7b909cc753874f90da67c55ed801cd784ea","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"fe8455bfacab11a0ae0b22dd4cb9cbc4cd481153f93367d0dae60985897adcb0","summary":"Not changed: the turn's commit and its readers were not touched","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.535578Z","id":"db3ce4a9333ec6fe364ffe3e2679948210cb4686e525f26861c1b3e00f706734","body":{"detail":"`dispatch` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) asks `SurfaceProvider::unavailable` after `route` and before `check_args`. With the check removed, `generic::a_binding_with_no_provider_answers_unavailable_and_takes_no_part_in_the_end` fails (the calls are answered `unknown_op`). script.rs itself did not change.","kind":"comment","references":"813737c3ead93c782337df20efec265dadf56d7054e3c10f288def0d3415e46d","summary":"One change to the seam's `dispatch` since: a call routed to a binding whose provider is away is refused before its arguments are checked","tags":["t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536720Z","id":"147b288a82cf9f4d31610c78c18d68321b1944f2f2429c5fa05ee72a1e7088d4","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"cc3b395f99175b2b9f8a02d2e7d2cd4412f2c4ab540afbbff4609e325cc5ec75","summary":"After the tick holds its providers: the request's context and a starting driver's `start_waiting` are `clax`'s provider's too, and what still reads `Deps.host` and `Deps.web` is listed on crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs (`Providers::register_clax`)","tags":["t8","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536763Z","id":"d311ae86405d00caa50c3cfef6652ba4f3514fb97718f52804108254f31abc43","body":{"detail":"`Deps.host`: `Deps::new`, `Deps::with_web` and `Deps::without_web` hand it to `Providers::register_clax` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs), which is the only reason the run loop's side names `ClaxProvider`; `Tick::working`'s reference branch (crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs, `deps.host.working`, `cfg(test)`); crates/clax-bot/src/ending_reference.rs (`plan_reply`, `working`); crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs (`read`, `plan_write`) and script_reference/web.rs (`context`). `Deps.web` (now `pub(crate)`): the same three functions of run.rs, the `Debug` of `Deps`, and script_reference/web.rs. `BotHost::context`, `working` and `start_waiting` are called by `clax`'s provider alone outside the references (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs, clax/ending.rs, clax/web.rs). `api::Performer`: crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs (the table and `OpSpec::performer`), host.rs (`FakeBotHost`'s check of an operation), script_reference.rs. When whoever builds a `Deps` registers `clax` itself (`Providers::register`), `Deps::new` loses `host`, `register_clax`, `with_web` and `without_web` go, and the six lines `registry::tests::the_run_loop_names_nothing_of_claxs_but_what_is_listed` lists become none.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":130},"end":{"line":141},"content_hash":"afebcc81982b876cfc76e8c8704b99a3998d7435aede317ce46fff0c9093077e"},"summary":"For the change that deletes `Deps.host` and `Deps.web`: what still reads them, `BotHost::working`/`start_waiting`/`context` and `api::Performer` after the tick holds its providers","tags":["t8","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536798Z","id":"8f1682a4329e40976d0422df5cc4a8ebedebf64da2177b47ea49e12ce8ceac08","body":{"detail":"`Driver::start` calls `Store::sweep_zero_version_artifacts` before it tells the providers (`SurfaceProvider::driver_started`). It names no `BotHost` method and no module of `clax`'s provider, so the test of the run loop's source passes; the noun is still Clax's. It can move into `ClaxProvider::driver_started` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs), which would then need the lease the sweep is given (`RunSettings::lease_secs`), or stay with what builds the daemon's driver. Left because the order of the sweep and the repeats of `ended` is today's and nothing asked for the move.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":226},"end":{"line":226},"content_hash":"33919012cb238e6d46f7612fe3c0a5a80217a4b5aee383dcedfd245a9c316151"},"summary":"A starting driver still sweeps artifacts that have no version, which is `clax`'s housekeeping, by a call of the store","tags":["t8","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536853Z","id":"6a48c3ca9751a010cfc7cef844c15a12c19a04163982c0814c6d32967ec4f89d","body":{"detail":"`system_of` answers `system_prompt(RequestKind::Thread, shape)` for a kind that is not `clax.thread` or `clax.brief`, and `request_of` renders the request the kind's surface resolved (`SurfaceProvider::request`) in the request section of crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md, which says the request's strings are data except those marked as the person's, and marks none. Only a test makes such a run today (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs writes the row's `kind`); `generic::a_fake_runs_first_turn_is_sent_the_request_its_surface_resolved` pins both texts. The change that sends the standard prompt (marking a request's `Instruction` strings, a registration's prompt file) replaces both branches here, and must do so before the store can make a run of another surface's kind. For `clax` kinds the two are `system_prompt` and `request_message` over the same values as before (the goldens under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden and src/*.golden did not change).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":418},"end":{"line":463},"content_hash":"ab3d59c032705cf1ae443fe76bc890c2e89b460effa20d35df1fa364873ed610"},"summary":"A run whose kind is not Clax's is sent the system prompt of a `clax.thread` run and the standard file's request section with nothing marked as the person's words","tags":["t8","for-3a-15","for-3a-03","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536883Z","id":"108955fbe294ff04c089bc4ceb02f50fef02b2028105af8e693df88708280bd8","body":{"detail":"`Providers::bind` answers `bindings_invalid` (`Bindings::parse`: another version, a malformed value) or `request_invalid`, and `tick` returns the error before the policy is asked and before `Tick::step`, so nothing is begun and `Store::note_commit_failure` is never called. Every row made so far parses (migration 64 and `Store::create_run` write version 1). The change that writes bindings of another version, read beside version 1, decides what a build that cannot read a run's bindings does with the run; `generic::a_run_is_known_by_its_stored_kind_request_and_bindings` holds today's answer (the tick fails and no step is made).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":154},"end":{"line":155},"content_hash":"909de731b592a826970d53ddfc30e11752fe3dc7c992af9db107bba2a6f6174c"},"summary":"A run whose stored bindings or request cannot be read fails every tick before any step, uncounted: it cannot be ended, cancelled included","tags":["t8","for-3a-03","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536906Z","id":"d4ac1b6c06efbe7aaacc8ab320a7ca219560a173989b56ab9a975f3e38b38342","body":{"detail":"A stored binding of version 1 keeps no definition (`hash` is null), so `Unavailable::definition` is an empty one, the binding has no operations, and `dispatch` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) answers `unavailable` (retryable) for any name routed to the surface, a name the surface never had included. The core journals the refusal as an outcome, so the step run again answers it from the journal even when a provider has been registered since. For the registry that follows: a registration's adapter and the definition read by hash give the binding its operations (then `unknown_op` and `invalid_args` come first again, and `unavailable` is for a call no provider accepted), and the retrying the design gives the run loop decides whether an `unavailable` is journaled at all. A run whose kind's surface has no provider stops `request_gone` at its first turn (`generic::a_run_whose_kinds_surface_has_no_provider_stops`): whether it should wait instead is for the same change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":293},"end":{"line":293},"content_hash":"5e83239ea75f5c70fa8383e311b777f564e9a5986088072ed1331dcd78152986"},"summary":"A binding with no provider has no definition: its calls are refused before their arguments are checked, the refusal is journaled, and nothing sends a call again","tags":["t8","for-3a-13","for-3a-14","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.536996Z","id":"97edc3e416f19ef543b791616af6d9ad1c3954a53cf68e4a3624f831b2fcaa04","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::for_run(&Run)` exists so `clax` reads its own columns (artifact, threads, request kind, base version, agent grant, web) from the row the tick read instead of reading it again; a provider outside the process ignores it. It goes when those columns leave `Run` for a row of `clax`'s own, or when `clax` reads them from its binding's scope and the request. `SurfaceProvider::audit_subject` answers `clax_core::audit::AuditIds` (artifact, thread, session, question, call, origin): a host's surface can name none of its own objects, so a run with no `clax` binding is filed on no object and found by its actor's `on_behalf_of`, which is what the design's \"filed on the person\" is here (`generic::a_runs_audit_records_are_filed_on_the_subject_its_surface_names`). `Store::commit_bot_step_on` takes the subject; `bot.run_start` (`Store::create_run`), `bot.cancel` (`Store::cancel_run`) and the web calls' records (crates/clax-store/src/bot_web.rs) are still filed by the store from the row's artifact.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1849},"end":{"line":1849},"content_hash":"817595088e043d2f1a6b0b56319447bf9ec9588fd4d973132ffc1a968ee2072d"},"summary":"Two of the seam's new hooks carry Clax's shapes: `for_run` is handed the store's `Run` row, and `audit_subject` answers `AuditIds`, which has places only for Clax's objects","tags":["t8","for-3a-13","for-3a-14","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:07:27.537166Z","id":"871a2360c5908500c5cfd96d48403048d2134eca6ea0b64128b6ca5f58188355","body":{"detail":"Taken instead: `Deps.providers` maps a surface's name to an `Arc<dyn SurfaceProvider>`, `Deps::new` registers `ClaxProvider` over the host it is given, and `Deps::with_web` and `Deps::without_web` register it again; `Deps.web` became `pub(crate)` so that writing it without registering is a compile error outside the crate (eight lines of crates/clax-bot/tests changed, one of them in tests/golden/web.rs, which is test code and no fixture). A provider that wants the tick's row answers one from `SurfaceProvider::for_run`. The sources would have kept those lines and made every provider's construction depend on `Deps`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":285},"end":{"line":285},"content_hash":"285181eade0b1b65fa0ba1493d388d4a6010a146c90bf84d783129bed60fd1dc"},"summary":"A registry of sources that make a provider from the deps at each tick, so that `Deps.web` could stay a public field a test writes","tags":["t8","revisit:Deps.web and Deps.host are deleted and clax is registered by what builds the deps","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.597418Z","id":"7ca30c191b6356eef4297655941c93484928f09f7f17afe128c857a155193757","body":{"detail":"`Providers::bind` (crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs) asks the registry's `Resolver` for a stored binding whose surface has no provider registered by name, and `crate::outside::Registrations` answers an `OutsideProvider` (crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs) for one that names a registration. Its `definition()` is the one the broker keeps under the binding's hash (the registration's own for a binding with no hash), so `dispatch` answers `unknown_op` and `invalid_args` before the session is asked, and `unavailable` is what the session answers when the call cannot be sent (`outside::a_host_that_is_away_is_unavailable_and_a_wrong_answer_is_an_invalid_result`). A registration that was never issued, or one not held whose binding keeps no hash, has no definition: `OutsideProvider::unavailable` answers and the call is refused before its arguments are checked, as `Unavailable` does (`outside::a_registration_that_is_gone_takes_no_call_and_its_runs_request_stands`). Still as this record says: a call that could not be sent is answered `Answer::refused`, which the core journals, so the step run again answers it from the journal and sends nothing. Whether an `unavailable` is journaled at all is for the change that gives the run loop its retries (the design's \"Retrying is the run loop's work\"). A run whose kind's surface has no definition still stops `request_gone` at its first turn: `OutsideProvider::request` answers the stored request, but `prompt::request_of` (called in crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, `Tick::turn`) needs the kind from the definition.","kind":"comment","references":"d4ac1b6c06efbe7aaacc8ab320a7ca219560a173989b56ab9a975f3e38b38342","summary":"A binding that names a registration is served by the outside provider with the definition kept under its hash, so its arguments are checked first again; `unavailable` is still journaled and nothing sends a call again","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.597791Z","id":"6a2bc205a6afd79a91caf1a3210760506c145dc5cbb0b8a339831c45d17ebb06","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::for_run(&self, row: &Run, binding: &BindingAsStored)` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs): a provider registered by name that serves several registrations chooses by the binding's registration and hash; `ClaxProvider` ignores the binding, and the fake does not implement the hook. The binding's operations are then taken from the provider answered (`Binding::as_stored`: the list the row keeps when it keeps one, else the provider's definition and standing rule). It is no longer asked for a run that has no binding of its kind's surface: that run's kind is served by the provider registered under the name (`Providers::of`). `BindingAsStored::of` reads `registration` and `operations` from the binding's own JSON object beside what `StoredBinding` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs) reads, since version 1 has no accessor for them; when the store reads a version that holds them, that function takes them from the store's type and the second parse of `bindings_json` in `Providers::bind` goes. `audit_subject` still answers `AuditIds`, and an outside provider names no subject.","kind":"comment","references":"97edc3e416f19ef543b791616af6d9ad1c3954a53cf68e4a3624f831b2fcaa04","summary":"`for_run` is now given the stored binding (`BindingAsStored`: surface, hash, registration, scope, granted, operations); `audit_subject` is as it was","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598123Z","id":"58449e9cb3aaa20d5d87df5bfb5d60ed378712c4a1e078e3eda2311d566c7a1d","body":{"detail":"`Providers::bind` still returns `bindings_invalid` from `Bindings::parse` for any version but 1, uncounted. It now also reads `registration` (a text) and `operations` (a list of names) from each binding's JSON object when they are there (`BindingAsStored::of`, crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs), which `Bindings::parse` ignores as unknown fields, so a row of version 1 written by hand with those members is served by its registration (crates/clax-bot/tests/outside.rs builds its runs so). What a build does with a run whose bindings it cannot read is still for the change that writes another version.","kind":"comment","references":"108955fbe294ff04c089bc4ceb02f50fef02b2028105af8e693df88708280bd8","summary":"Unchanged: bindings of another version still fail every tick before any step. The members a later version adds are read beside version 1 when a row has them","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598504Z","id":"cb4e8d44597370c166f01dbd034f7e2d9615d3107ed57c9bc7febf1738ada9e4","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::call` hands a waiting answer (`Answer::is_waiting`) to `not_parked`, which throws the script `unavailable`, unjournaled. The step that parks: (1) implements `Host::ask` on `StepHost` and answers `HostReply::Waiting` here, recording the place, operation and arguments' hash of the call, and does not call `told` (a waiting call is no call of the step's `calls` yet); (2) when the sandbox's outcome is `ErrorKind::Parked`, drops the sessions without `finish`, as a lost claim does, commits nothing and parks the run on that place. What it can rely on: `dispatch` and `perform` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) return a waiting answer only for an operation tagged `@waits` whose call came with no entry, and have journaled nothing; the provider has already waited as long as the policy lets a script sit (`Delivery::park_after`, crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs) inside `StepSession::call`, so the core adds no wait and drops no future; the outside provider's broker opened the call in its store, with the place and under the claim, before the call was sent (`CallBroker::send`, crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs), so an answer can already have arrived when the park is attempted, and the park must treat a place that is journaled by then as due at once. The step run again makes the call again: with the entry when the store journaled the answer, and with no entry otherwise, in which case the outside provider sends the same key, the broker answers the same call's ID, and a settled outcome comes back at once (`broker::tests::a_call_is_sent_with_its_key_and_deadline_and_its_checked_answer_comes_back`). `generic::a_call_that_waits_is_thrown_unavailable_until_the_step_parks` and `outside::a_call_that_waits_is_held_by_its_host_and_journaled_by_no_one` pin today's refusal and change with it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":178},"end":{"line":189},"content_hash":"23f1e0cb9e16b58f1347803f87cfc594b1a1999dd8a0ad5854a8c9e32bef429f"},"summary":"The one place the script step turns a waiting answer into `unavailable`: what the step that parks replaces, and what it can rely on","tags":["for-3a-12","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598544Z","id":"4823fe079a8ec0cf52fca4582fa443e2576bdaeeeb2f3e6119156d05283c262b","body":{"detail":"`HubBroker<S>` (crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs) is the one broker; the daemon gives it a store that outlasts the process in place of `MemoryPending`. That store implements `bot_surfaces::Pending` and `BrokerStore`. (1) `open_placed` is the only way the broker opens a call: it is given `Placed` (the claim's driver, the run, the `CallPlace` with step, attempt, seq and key, the arguments as the script passed them and their `host::args_hash`, and whether the operation waits) beside the hub's `NewCall`, and writes the pending row under that driver's claim before the hub wakes the host; a claim that is lost is an error, which the script is thrown as `unavailable` (retryable) and the core fails to journal. `Pending::open_call` is never called through the broker (`broker::tests`' store panics in it). (2) `settled` answers a call's outcome, read and left in place, for as long as the call's step is open: a store that journals answers at the call's place answers what that row holds, in the form `surface::recorded` reads. The outside provider then answers `Answer::of`, and the core's write keeps the row that is there. (3) A call opened again with its key for the same registration, run, operation and arguments answers `OpenedCall::Same` with the first ID, settled or not: a step run again after a crash relies on it not to reach its host twice (`outside::a_hosts_calls_are_not_sent_again_when_a_crashed_step_is_run_again` holds it over `MemoryPending`). (4) `record_end` for a run whose end the registration has acknowledged should change nothing; `MemoryPending` forgets an acknowledged end and would record it again. (5) `Hub::enqueue_under` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) reads a definition by hash through `Pending::definition_text` when the registration has registered another since, so definitions are kept for as long as a run bound with them is live. (6) The hub's instants are `Millis` since the epoch and the store's are timestamps: the broker's `BrokerClock` and the store agree on one clock.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":243},"end":{"line":272},"content_hash":"a2a63b5ca7a661eb2db2513c472c2818b0ed6d7fb9c27ed49777a5fdb2cc28d4"},"summary":"What a store's implementation of `BrokerStore` must hold to for the daemon's broker","tags":["for-3a-17","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598575Z","id":"a1682df3764a342f2059b278805cc7a58e51ba4a54452a225ae001a4df25c4d0","body":{"detail":"`SkipClock` is for tests: a wait moves it. The daemon needs a `BrokerClock` whose `now` is the store's clock in milliseconds and whose `until` sleeps on `clax_rt::time` (it may return early; `HubBroker::outcome` reads the clock again). The routes of the host protocol (`PUT /api/bot/surfaces/<name>`, `…/serve`) call `Hub::register`, `Hub::open`, `Hub::ready` and `Hub::close` inside `HubBroker::with`, which holds the broker's lock for the closure and then wakes whoever waits in `CallBroker::outcome`, so an answer a `serve` brought reaches its run without a timer (`broker::tests::an_answer_reaches_whoever_waits_for_it_without_the_clock_moving`). The hub's `Wake` hook is called under that lock and must not call the broker. Nothing but `outcome` calls `Hub::expire`: with no run waiting, calls past their deadline are settled only when a `serve` opens, so the daemon needs a pass at `Hub::next_wake`, which is also what journals a parked run's deadline. Wiring: `deps.providers.set_resolver(Arc::new(outside::Registrations::new(broker, deps.policy.clone())))`, with the same policy the deps hold, since `RunPolicy::delivery` is asked through it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":196},"end":{"line":203},"content_hash":"7fa8a5482deeb7ea4e5d0e666ee4f04f83bd7f516ea17e1cdf25b4f3ceb7e2cf"},"summary":"No `BrokerClock` for a daemon is written, and the routes reach the hub only through `HubBroker::with`","tags":["for-3a-17","for-3a-20","for-3a-21","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598604Z","id":"6cc3f96ffe22ede13258e0d5fcfdc78d8d5ce7cdc4d30b81388f00e296fb9620","body":{"detail":"`OutsideProvider::ended` records the end with the broker (`CallBroker::ended`, which is `Hub::ended`), after the terminal commit. The provider lives for one tick and the registry's resolver is not asked when a driver starts, so it has no `awaiting_ended`: once recorded, delivery until acknowledged is the hub's and the store's. But a tick that dies between the terminal commit and this call, or a broker that refuses the end (`EndedError::QueueFull`, `ResultTooLarge`, `Storage`), leaves the host untold, and nothing repeats it. Writing the end in the terminal step's transaction (the store's part, for each binding that names a registration) closes both; the call here then finds the end recorded and changes nothing. Also: the kind's result is given only to the binding of the surface that declared the kind (`ending::result_for`), so a registration bound to a run of another surface's kind is told the end without a result, as the design says.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":379},"end":{"line":395},"content_hash":"aae32f94eb900c26b455359b77fe7aa009a9293504de62ce9797f37e73950ee6"},"summary":"An outside provider's `ended` is the only time a run's end is recorded for its host: a failure there is logged and never made up for","tags":["for-3a-16","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598637Z","id":"32b82e53ba6fb919cdb814ab607a51df8c608d236aee1b99d9ec30172550d803","body":{"detail":"`RunSurfaces::operations` lists, for each binding in the order bound, every operation of its provider's definition (not the run's own set, so that a call to one the run lacks is refused by the run loop in its surface's words: `web_disabled`), and for a binding whose provider takes no call, the operations the row keeps for it. Three things follow. (1) `dispatch`'s refusal `no_such_operation` for a call no binding takes (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) can no longer be reached from a sandbox: the script gets a `TypeError` from its own `clax` instead (`generic::a_runs_scripts_are_given_the_operations_of_its_surfaces`); the case was dropped from `generic`'s `WORK` script. (2) A binding served by `Unavailable`, or by an outside provider whose registration is gone, gives its scripts no operation unless the row keeps the binding's `operations`: a script that calls it gets a `TypeError`, not `unavailable` (`generic::a_binding_with_no_provider_answers_unavailable_and_takes_no_part_in_the_end` stores the list to reach the refusal). Bindings that store their operations make this whole. (3) Tests that register one fake under two surface names give a run each operation twice, which `validate_ops` refuses: such a run ends `failed` (`surface_ops_invalid`) at its first script step. The tests that do so today take no script step.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":352},"end":{"line":354},"content_hash":"743ee6f9d0cd80fd2d837b69367b4036deca6348cb63d1f9d0d082fa36bf34ae"},"summary":"A script's `clax` now has every operation of each bound surface's definition, so no script can name an operation no binding takes, and one definition bound under two names fails the run","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:35:40.598740Z","id":"c78388acb7c9d105613c5da22a931563a0e548a800ec7e0537cda46ce68a8cb4","body":{"detail":"`Providers` holds the providers registered by name (Clax's own, from `Deps::new`) and one optional `Resolver` (`Providers::with_resolver`, `set_resolver`). `Providers::bind` asks the resolver for each stored binding whose surface has no provider registered by name, with the run's row, its kind and the binding as stored (`BindingAsStored`: surface, hash, registration, scope, granted, operations); `outside::Registrations` answers an `OutsideProvider` for a binding that names a registration, and `Unavailable` serves what it does not. So nothing is registered after `Driver::start`, and a name unknown when the deps were made is served. `SurfaceProvider::driver_started` and `awaiting_ended` (`Driver::start`, `ending::redeliver`) still reach only the providers registered by name. A resolved provider lives for one tick and is owed neither: an outside provider's `ended` records the run's end with the broker, whose store keeps it and whose hub delivers it with each `serve` until the host acknowledges. `generic::the_kinds_surface_is_told_of_the_end_first_and_names_the_subject_first` holds the order of two bindings in the terminal step and which names the run's audit subject; `outside::a_registration_that_is_gone_takes_no_call_and_its_runs_request_stands` holds that a provider registered by name is asked before the resolver.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":79},"end":{"line":91},"content_hash":"6e861961601df8592939d96c94c0ab8c7a014530e7cf996de9dd0a15d2ddf491"},"summary":"Surfaces registered while a driver runs are served through one resolver the daemon gives the registry, not by registering each: providers by name stay fixed when the deps are made","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.782689Z","id":"3c6f2851580bc631841f90e83e883e3cf79052735722be1185e992a1d9b945e1","body":{"detail":"Commit 6ef4d0b59. `prompt::system_of(run, surface, bound, own)` renders crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md, or `own`, the prompt file registered with the kind's surface, with the kind's paragraph, `{rules}`, one `clax_surface::declaration` over `bound` (the kind's surface first) and each other binding's rules under `{surfaces}`; for `clax.thread`/`clax.brief` it is `system_prompt` as before. `prompt::request_of(run, surface, resolved, own)` lists the JSON Pointers `Kind::instruction_places` gives before the request (crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/standard.rs, `instructions`, `fence`). crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs (`Tick::turn`, the first-turn branch) passes the run's bindings with their providers' definitions and `own: None`: nothing carries a registration's prompt file to the run loop yet. A failure of `system_of` (two bindings putting one name on `clax`, an unreadable file) ends the run `stopped` with `request_gone`, as a request that cannot be resolved does. Tests: crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/live.rs; `generic::a_fake_runs_first_turn_is_sent_the_request_its_surface_resolved` (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs) now pins the real texts. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:all //bazel/wasm:check //scripts:crate_layers //scripts:no_unsafe` passes. Left for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"6a48c3ca9751a010cfc7cef844c15a12c19a04163982c0814c6d32967ec4f89d","summary":"Both branches are replaced: a run of a kind that is not Clax's is sent the standard prompt (or its surface's registered file) over all its bindings, and a request message that lists the person's words before the request","tags":["for-3a-15","claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.784341Z","id":"352948af2c6f73b7f16a51f128feea1eb55f92469b3c90ee90aba17fdb893904","body":{"detail":"Commit 6ef4d0b59. crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md: the kind's paragraph (`{kind}`) is the paragraph after the first line, the rules list is `{core.data}`, `{core.images}`, `{core.finish}`, then `{rules}`; the request section is `{instructions}`, an empty line, and `{fence}json` / `{request}` / `{fence}`. Two request-section slots are new in crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs: `{instructions}` (at most once, on a line before `{request}`'s) and `{fence}`. A host's own prompt file must have `{instructions}` (`prompt::check_prompt_file`). The decision this record asks for is the owner's; the standard file's wording is taken as recommended in the reply on ddbcd266.","kind":"comment","references":"d85c7b72a248400637b754a1e59f1358b901713eb69f6f94038bdb906520c360","summary":"The standard prompt now reaches runs of a host's kind, `{rules}` as the earlier replies built it; its request section is `{instructions}`, then the request in a `{fence}` block","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.785237Z","id":"28961728c7d4a87189120ca14d94a18e4e82416f1c4f52ff9ab026beea0370c3","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs `system_pieces_of` (commit 6ef4d0b59): the prompt file first (`prompt:clax`, `prompt:standard`, or `prompt:<surface>` for a registered file), then each slot that became something, named by the slot. So a run bound to two surfaces still has one `declarations` piece and one `surfaces` piece, not one per binding as the design says. The run loop does not call it, and the store column for pieces is not on this branch.","kind":"comment","references":"cc5bd07672cc495489d77443179ef3d990167b040e809af1abe26d2799a1f556","summary":"`system_pieces_of` gives the pieces of a host's run too, by the same slot-by-slot rule; nothing stores them","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:deferred","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.786045Z","id":"629b90afa34bf6e00f24c429439c1f2630230c272da2a7e15ef437b4e686b526","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs `render_other` and `request_of` (commit 6ef4d0b59) answer an error naming the surface and kind before `Prompt::system`/`Prompt::request` are called; crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/live.rs `a_kind_or_a_request_the_surface_does_not_have_is_refused`. `Prompt::system` and `Prompt::request` still panic when called directly. Left for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"0f7d6f9bc4dae0250bacecabe054f425804a06373a32d9e11f1567ee70f5980b","summary":"The run loop's path no longer reaches either panic for a host's kind: `system_of` and `request_of` refuse a kind the surface lacks before rendering","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.786184Z","id":"2a87725b44527c7e8958a8431933d8fa30a297ba92f1bce746af74df9d98429b","body":{"detail":"The design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"The prompt\") says a run records each piece's name and hash. `system_pieces_of` returns them for any run; crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs (`Tick::turn`, first turn) sends `system_of`'s text and records no pieces. The store's `pieces_json` column is on the store lane's branch, not here; the step that lands it calls `system_pieces_of` with the same arguments as `system_of` and stores the result.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":620},"end":{"line":637},"content_hash":"275f5b2b006c9cb13d23dedde3445ab68938be0dc36582599d9f98a3053fb3a2"},"summary":"A run's prompt pieces are computed by `system_pieces_of` and stored nowhere: the run loop does not call it","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.786270Z","id":"7cd500a3f7c8099b43100ca789209c084ab7f8c44b4f82768afd09746a444ef0","body":{"detail":"`wire::RegisterRequest.prompt` is stored unread. crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs (`Tick::turn`) calls `prompt::system_of` and `prompt::request_of` with `own: None`, so a surface's registered file is never used; `RunSurfaces`/`RunInfo` carry no prompt. docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json (lines 29 and 651) registers `desk` with `\"prompt\": \"Keep the desk tidy.\\n\"`, which `check_prompt_file` refuses ('line 1: no `--- system ---` section', held by crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/live.rs `a_hosts_prompt_file_is_checked_when_it_is_registered`). When registration calls the check, that fixture needs a prompt file (sections, `{declarations}`, `{surfaces}`, `{rules}` for desk's own rules, `{instructions}`, `{request}`), or a registration whose prompt is refused needs a refusal in the fixture.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":466},"end":{"line":481},"content_hash":"125b74a2afb70d2b6289e9b3390e5b24564084194f67aaefd4908cbe8c9e4a7b"},"summary":"Nothing calls `check_prompt_file` at registration, and nothing hands a registered prompt file to the run loop, which passes `own: None`; the contract fixture's registered prompt is no prompt file and would be refused","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.786340Z","id":"67ba2958f43aab6f4406760e9a5530c161f30f956a9e6fb75d3439ae2e8a10b5","body":{"detail":"`crate::api::dts` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs) wraps Clax's declaration in a comment saying a failure rejects with a `HostError` whose `code` and `retryable` say why, the script's clock and seeded `Math.random`, and ends with `declare const console`. `render_other` gives `{declarations}` `clax_surface::declaration` alone, so crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.shell.triage.golden says nothing of errors or the clock, though `{core.code_mode}` names `console.log`. The surface-neutral parts of that comment could be the core's (a `{core.…}` paragraph, or a head the declaration always has).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":539},"end":{"line":539},"content_hash":"dbc706f451749ffe2729950d12ea55e5cb712589b1c2534acbb2a6ea4a9dcabf"},"summary":"A host's run is not told how a failed call rejects, nor declared `console`: Clax's own declaration has both around it, the standard prompt's has neither","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.shell.triage.golden","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:36:43.786385Z","id":"25ca9d1d3585e61bb96f78be9ae75159814869239298ffdaedc6b5b7ede3c245","body":{"detail":"The kind's paragraph and shell's rules come from docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces/shell.d.ts. `threads.open` and `runs.wait` are Clax operations that do not exist yet (the file's own comment says so); `shell.screenshot` returns an `Image` and is withheld while Clax stores no content (`Definition::run_operations`, `Withheld::Content`). The golden renders a run with the operations the kind and the default scope grant: parts, windows, settings.get and ask. A host's words naming operations a run lacks are the host's to fix; nothing warns of it (`clax bot surface check` notes only the `@allow` patterns that match nothing).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":4},"end":{"line":20},"content_hash":"d3b5720ccf930618fcfc874e846c249cedb34e1bab5175af274e90007c8b32ed"},"summary":"The shell.triage prompt tells the model to use `clax.threads.open`, `clax.runs.wait` and `clax.shell.screenshot`, none of which the run is declared","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:26:11.267867Z","id":"7e2f537bc58f5ee0ad6e33c678fb3bfb2374798155ffdf8dda085f814105ac14","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"9bb28c7d908018d96898dc79162329e5b2cad3aeff7cbdf016d61f73b3efd45e","summary":"`Deps::kind` and `Deps::with_kind` are gone: a run's kind is now its row's stored `kind` (`RunAsMade::kind`, read by `Providers::bind`, crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs). crates/clax-bot/tests/finish.rs (`Typed::run`) makes a `fake.verdict` row with the fake's `bindings_json` and registers the fake with `Deps::with_providers`, as crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs does for `fake.job`. Proposed close.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:26:11.268566Z","id":"3b14ab0752db71f3d4f67b31b984f7dd50c3db9ef4650876c0e23ea59ec3e24f","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"5ddda6960398118315c3715cc7cdae52a9e012188cf75d1d5845b6144eb31317","summary":"Since the registry: `Finish::of` takes the definition of the provider the run's binding resolves for its kind (`RunSurfaces::kind_provider`). A run whose kind's surface has no provider, and a run whose kind its surface's definition lacks, both stop `request_gone` at the first turn, before the model is asked, because the request cannot be rendered (`prompt::request_of`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs `turn`). crates/clax-bot/tests/finish.rs `a_run_of_a_kind_its_surface_does_not_declare_stops_at_its_first_turn` holds the second case, so a run no longer limps to `invalid_result`. The stop's code is the request's, not one naming the kind; the step's `error` names it. Proposed close, or keep it open for a code of its own: the owner's call.","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:26:28.870666Z","id":"565466d7faa19a8c1381e9780992f23652c9db4e3e0ad3cc0f6418950f4f06d6","body":{"detail":"`Providers` is a plain list held in `Deps.providers`, and `Driver` holds the deps behind an `Arc` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs). `Providers::of` answers `Unavailable` for any name not registered beforehand, with no fallback. `Driver::start` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:240) calls `driver_started` on each provider listed at that moment. A provider of surfaces registered at run time under names not known when the deps were made (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, 'A runtime registration is a lease', 'Registration, unavailable (§6) | Yes: at run time') has two ways in: interior mutability in `Providers` (and an answer to what `driver_started` means for a provider registered after start), or a fallback resolver (`Fn(&str) -> Option<Arc<dyn SurfaceProvider>>`) that `of` asks after the list. Either changes `Providers` and the public field `Deps.providers`, so the change that adds the first such provider decides it. Nothing on clax.dev is affected: `clax` is registered by `Deps::new`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":50},"end":{"line":54},"content_hash":"24d2decfd64dd03e5821e7713c6047668f9848d74103d20aa9a79593ae7bde3e"},"summary":"The registry is fixed when `Deps` is built: no provider can be registered while the driver runs, no surface name unknown then can be served, and `driver_started` reaches only the providers registered when `Driver::start` runs","tags":["review","t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:26:28.872180Z","id":"c3586ed57b81d934b8ee3ca6186d24c3e0fad39886a740d093028f2bbe690920","body":{"detail":"`Providers::bind` (crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs:154-170) asks `for_run(row)` once for each stored binding, knowing only `binding.surface()`, then takes that binding's operations from the answered provider's `definition()`. The `StoredBinding` (its `hash` and `scope`) is not passed, and `Run` does not hold `bindings_json` (it is in `RunAsMade`). The design says a run is served by the registration that bound it, chosen by definition hash, so a provider of many registrations of one name would have to re-parse the row's bindings itself, or could not tell two bindings apart. Adding the stored binding to the signature (`for_run(&self, row: &Run, binding: &StoredBinding)`) costs one implementer (`ClaxProvider`) now and is a breaking change of a public trait later.","kind":"comment","references":"97edc3e416f19ef543b791616af6d9ad1c3954a53cf68e4a3624f831b2fcaa04","span":{"start":{"line":1849},"end":{"line":1849},"content_hash":"817595088e043d2f1a6b0b56319447bf9ec9588fd4d973132ffc1a968ee2072d"},"summary":"`for_run` is also not given the stored binding it is asked for, so a provider serving several registrations of one surface name cannot choose the one that serves this binding","tags":["review","t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:26:28.872528Z","id":"d40ac91db153011d996c3cd89d159df1f6263f350c9d4a878a6e179db5630f96","body":{"detail":"Three mutants, each alone, each passing //crates/clax-bot:all: (1) `surfaces.sort_by_key(...)` at line 254 deleted: the script step sorts again (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:282), so only the terminal step's order (`Tick::end`, crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs: who is asked `ending` and joined first, told `ended` first) loses it, and no test ends a run whose kind's binding is not stored first; (2) `.rev()` before `find_map` at line 197-200, so the last binding naming a subject wins: no test binds two surfaces that both name one; (3) in `Driver::start` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:236-255) the `driver_started` loop moved before `ending::redeliver`: `generic::a_starting_driver_tells_each_registered_provider` counts calls, not their order, which the doc of `SurfaceProvider::driver_started` states. None changes a `clax`-only run (one binding, one provider).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":242},"end":{"line":256},"content_hash":"e8da72dfed8c811d43e1c8e3eade370bfe248691169fe0f4d64e7fe8954b32a3"},"summary":"The order of a run's bindings is not held by a test where it matters: with the kind's sort removed from `RunSurfaces::surfaces`, with the subject taken from the last binding, or with `driver_started` called before `redeliver`, every test of //crates/clax-bot:all passes","tags":["review","t8","tests","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:26:28.872695Z","id":"f7b88ea05641ea33b34d3c10c4cde4d9c88fdd1bee569fc5bb43eccb13534eda","body":{"detail":"`register_clax` (line 373) builds a new `ClaxProvider` from the deps' fields and registers it unconditionally. `tests/generic.rs` calls `with_providers` after `deps_with` (which calls `with_web`), so its registries hold no `clax`; the reverse order would silently register it. `Deps.store`, `Deps.host` and `Deps.clock` are public fields: a caller assigning one after `Deps::new` leaves the registered provider on the old value. The server (crates/clax-server/src/bot.rs:1177-1189) and the Worker (crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs:439-450) build `Deps::new(...)` then `with_web`, which is correct. Re-registering only when `clax` is registered, and the fields going private or rebuilt-on-change when `Deps.host` is deleted, would close both.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":356},"end":{"line":381},"content_hash":"a61fba12fef99e8b27329a3e3997b98333cb585974c89bed01a88c4c3b597c78"},"summary":"`Deps::with_web` and `without_web` register `clax` again whatever the registry holds, so after `with_providers` with no `clax` they add it back; and a `Deps.store`, `host` or `clock` set after `Deps::new` is not seen by the `clax` provider already registered","tags":["review","t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:48:52.636520Z","id":"fa5ab467338019e9a17bdcdaa48fd55d9e2acb7143b69a23016fb2c4a4378551","body":{"detail":"Commit 89141ca47. crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/standard.rs `declaration_head(surface_note, waits)` and `CONSOLE`; crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs `dts` builds Clax's head from them with `CLAX_HEAD_NOTE` (what files.*, draft.*, db.* and threads.* change), and the eight src/prompt.system.*.golden and src/api*.d.ts.golden are unchanged. `prompt::declaration_of` gives a host's run the head with no surface note, its operations and `console`. A run with an `@waits` operation is also told 'A call marked (may take long) is awaited like any other: the script goes on when it is answered, however long that takes.' (no Clax operation waits, so no Clax run is told it). Held by crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/live.rs `a_hosts_run_is_told_how_a_call_fails_and_how_a_long_one_is_awaited` and the re-pinned src/prompt.shell.triage.golden, src/prompt.desk.tidy.golden and src/prompt.standard.golden. The head's list of codes (`invalid_args`, `replay_diverged`) does not name `unavailable`, `expired` or `unknown_outcome`, which a host's calls can be thrown (crates/clax-surface/src/wire.rs); adding them changes Clax's head too. Left for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"67ba2958f43aab6f4406760e9a5530c161f30f956a9e6fb75d3439ae2e8a10b5","summary":"Done: every run's declaration has the core's head on how a call fails and ends with `console`; Clax's head is the core's with Clax's own note, byte for byte","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:48:52.637016Z","id":"9891c6072a37873313ddb7cf3831d3f876a16910597951fa706f7094364e0128","body":{"detail":"Commit 89141ca47. `Hub::register` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) calls `prompt::check_prompt_file` after the definition reads and refuses with `invalid_prompt` (400, `clax_surface::wire::CODE_INVALID_PROMPT`, documented in docs/contract.md, \"Registering\" and the refusal table). docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json registers `desk` with `$file:desk.prompt.md`, a small prompt file, and its script has a registration with `\"prompt\": \"Keep the desk tidy.\\n\"` refused `invalid_prompt` at 6 ms; crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/live.rs `a_hosts_prompt_file_is_checked_when_it_is_registered` holds both. Still to do: the registry's `RunSurfaces` carries no registration's prompt, so crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs (`Tick::turn`, first turn) calls `prompt::system_of(info, kind.definition(), &bound, None)` and `prompt::request_of(info, kind.definition(), &resolved, None)`. The step that carries it passes the registration's text (`Registration::prompt` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) of the registration that bound the run's kind's surface) as `own` to both calls, the same value to each, and to `system_pieces_of` when pieces are stored.","kind":"comment","references":"7cd500a3f7c8099b43100ca789209c084ab7f8c44b4f82768afd09746a444ef0","summary":"Registration now checks the prompt file and the fixture carries a valid one; the run loop still passes `own: None`","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:54:27.341747Z","id":"4df55afb2088839af3922fe9df78d86b0bb06cdd66d4e2d1fd4b33832a376162","body":{"detail":"Bindings version 2 is read beside version 1 (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs, Bindings::read). Any other version is BindingsError::Unsupported { version }, and text that is not bindings is BindingsError::Invalid(message); Bindings::parse, which Providers::bind calls, maps both to `bindings_invalid` as before, so nothing here changed behaviour. RunAsMade::bindings() returns the typed error: a caller that matches Unsupported can end the run as failed (a later build wrote it and no retry will read it) instead of returning a tick error on every tick. That change is the run loop's and is not made.","kind":"comment","references":"108955fbe294ff04c089bc4ceb02f50fef02b2028105af8e693df88708280bd8","summary":"The store now tells bindings of a version it does not read from malformed ones (BindingsError::Unsupported), through Bindings::read and RunAsMade::bindings; Providers::bind is unchanged and still fails the tick","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:54:27.342439Z","id":"5fc13c456fb30a8335cbf1ab11862c1a778b372a12cedb78df3be24f855dc8aa","body":{"detail":"A version-2 binding (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs, StoredBinding::bound, written by Store::create_run_of) holds `operations`, every operation the run has of the surface as it was made, and `registration`, the registration that serves it. StoredBinding::operations() is None for version 1, whose operations are still the provider's standing rule and `granted`; `clax` runs are still written as version 1. Binding::from_stored still filters the definition by `standing` and `granted()`, which for a version-2 binding is empty: until it takes `operations()` when present, a version-2 binding gets only what its provider's `standing` says.","kind":"comment","references":"06a96d62b78e3f5c3290bde0529f5f562f840acbb3f318a51b6cce4573e8de00","summary":"Bindings version 2 stores a binding's whole set of operations (StoredBinding::operations), and its registration; Binding::from_stored does not read them yet","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:54:51.238099Z","id":"cbe039140881f2a56ca1a2e91af6671d4c84ba5256d98f16c74265900f80ab65","body":{"detail":"`Tick::end` (crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs) asks each binding's provider `end_for(run, end, result)` and passes what they answer to `Store::commit_bot_step_on` as `ends`, so the run ends and its end is kept for its host in one transaction. `OutsideProvider::end_for` answers one when its broker's store is the run's (`CallBroker::commits_ends`, true for `broker::stored::StoredPending`) and the registration is one the broker found; `ended` then calls `CallBroker::wake` and records nothing, however often it is told. With the broker in memory (`MemoryPending`) nothing changed: `ended` records the end. Held by `outside::answered::in_the_store` and `outside::conformance::in_the_store`. Still open: a registration the broker could not find when the provider was made (never issued, or not held with no hash on the binding) gets no end in the commit, and `ended` asks the hub, which refuses an ID that was never issued; and a run that ends because its bindings could not be read (`Providers::bind_or_unreadable`) has no provider to name an end, so its host is not told.","kind":"comment","references":"6cc3f96ffe22ede13258e0d5fcfdc78d8d5ce7cdc4d30b81388f00e296fb9620","summary":"With a broker over the run's store the end is now kept by the terminal commit: `SurfaceProvider::end_for` names it and `ended` only wakes the hub","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:54:51.238887Z","id":"78945a2f76ebb402114742f496d6ce51e6ca1b0f0545f58f6c1c69255c13d55a","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker/stored.rs maps each `Pending` method to one of the store's and implements `open_placed` (`Store::open_call` under `Placed::driver`'s claim, with the step, attempt, seq, key, arguments' hash and `waits`), `settled` (`Store::pending_call`, then the `bot_calls` row of its place, read with `surface::recorded`) and `commits_ends` (true). Points 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 of this record hold of it and are played by every case of crates/clax-bot/tests/outside.rs under `in_the_store`. Point 4 is moot where the end is kept by the terminal commit (the outside provider no longer records it in `ended` then); `Store::record_end` itself still keeps a new end after an acknowledged one, by design of the store (a run continued and ended again).","kind":"comment","references":"4823fe079a8ec0cf52fca4582fa443e2576bdaeeeb2f3e6119156d05283c262b","summary":"The store's implementation is written: `broker::stored::StoredPending`, over `clax_store::Store`","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:54:51.239555Z","id":"8b5a944e03a36c355f60aeea20fb354654d6f697605ea8b12427711158e384fd","body":{"detail":"`tick` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs) binds with `Providers::bind_or_unreadable`: for `BindingsError::Unsupported`, `BindingsError::Invalid` and a request that is not JSON it holds the run with no binding and the reason (`RunSurfaces::unreadable`), and `Tick::step` ends the run `failed` with `bindings_unsupported`, `bindings_invalid` or `request_invalid` right after the check for a cancel, which still stops the run as `cancelled`. `Providers::bind` itself still answers the error, under those codes, for its other callers. `generic::a_run_is_known_by_its_stored_kind_request_and_bindings` holds the three codes, the one `failed` step and the cancel. No provider is told of such an end: none could be found.","kind":"comment","references":"108955fbe294ff04c089bc4ceb02f50fef02b2028105af8e693df88708280bd8","summary":"A run whose bindings or request cannot be read now ends `failed` at its next tick, a cancel honoured first","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T21:54:51.240263Z","id":"482c01198881c7074702e7a7a85d3d0177d03d2484f61e5ef512a22d0090f078","body":{"detail":"With bindings of version 2 read by the store (`StoredBinding::registration`, `operations`), `BindingAsStored::of(stored)` takes no JSON and `Providers::bind` parses `bindings_json` once (`RunAsMade::bindings`). `Binding::as_stored(stored, provider)` gives a version-2 binding the operations its row lists, as listed, and a version-1 binding its provider's standing operations and what it was granted. A version-1 row no longer carries a registration: the hand-built rows of crates/clax-bot/tests/outside.rs are of version 2.","kind":"comment","references":"97edc3e416f19ef543b791616af6d9ad1c3954a53cf68e4a3624f831b2fcaa04","summary":"`BindingAsStored::of` now reads the store's `StoredBinding` alone, and a version-2 binding's stored operations are the run's","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:09:01.225528Z","id":"5c883bf81152fb64b99a761e589adf28c3e5d5d55a1266703949dbe950cf72be","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs `serve` wakes the registration's held serves after each `Hub::open` (one more open serve than `serves_per_registration` ends the oldest, which learns of it only by asking `Hub::ready`), and the timer's pass (`Surfaces::expire`) wakes every held serve, since a call its host asked for later may have come due. Test: crates/clax-server/tests/api_bot_surfaces.rs one_serve_more_than_a_registration_may_hold_ends_the_oldest.","kind":"comment","references":"faeb89d8d18ad44b1f01a17f7b2907cb2f5ad2b8399c02b657dc2f9e17daf421","summary":"As the routes use it: the hub does not call its hook for a serve it ended, so the route wakes the registration after every open","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:09:01.225982Z","id":"3f17c230d0ec5560b0455627726530202e76e65f34dea1683d3913f0e64cc23e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/tests/api_bot_surfaces.rs the_fixtures_script_replays_clean_through_a_daemon replays docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json through the routes with the calls sent by `CallBroker::send` under a claim and the outcomes read from the run's journal: no difference. The test's world gives each key of the script its own place in one open step, and writes the daemon's run IDs as the script's names (`run-1`) in the answers it compares.","kind":"comment","references":"e4a8a5c0c131c5adc277c63cbe47216a16d534c88cb2daee714050467510faea","summary":"The daemon's hub is over `StoredPending` inside one `HubBroker` (crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs `Surfaces::new`); the contract fixture's script replays through it over HTTP","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:09:01.227443Z","id":"5c9cff6fc8fa59a3e1a4285b5c55307d3535300ab2f4f140d9a6e3ebc3e21aa4","body":{"detail":"`Hub::advance` drops a hold whose `until` is before now, and `Hub::close` then answers nothing. With a clock that runs, a serve that waited its whole wait is closed a moment after `until`, and a serve that waits for nothing is opened and closed at two readings of the clock: neither is sent the calls that are due and not new (sent, not accepted), which docs/contract.md (\"Serving\") says go out with every serve. Under a clock a test stops this never shows. The routes work around it (crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs `Surfaces::turn`): open, ready and close run under one hold of the broker at one instant, and a serve asked after its wait is over is answered with `Hub::serve` at that instant. Test: api_bot_surfaces.rs a_new_daemon_on_the_home_goes_on_as_a_registration_with_its_calls (a daemon on the system's clock, `wait_ms` 0 and 1). The rule may be better in the hub: close could answer what is due for a serve that lapsed by time alone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1178},"end":{"line":1199},"content_hash":"05f3c2c061106b0eeb5ffddacf6742758daaae8efef8c4fe7e3c2bf97a64d8ec"},"summary":"A serve's hold lapses at the first instant after its wait, so on a running clock a close after the wait answers nothing: a held serve never redelivers","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:09:01.227524Z","id":"98411a8d4a711ee165663a7a54fefbee25ad83c97bc7f4545ffbb76e132e892c","body":{"detail":"`reg.later` holds each `retry_after_ms` instant until a serve is answered with the call (or the call is settled), so once it is due and its host does not serve, `next_wake` answers that past instant every time, and `Hub::expire` changes nothing about it. A timer that sleeps until `next_wake` would spin. The daemon's timer (crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs `run_timer`) waits `STALE_WAKE` (1 s) when a pass left the next wake in the past. `next_wake` could leave out the instants that have come.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1280},"end":{"line":1307},"content_hash":"5e3a1a2e434d681bb1a34bdbe4f5c44ce9c4450a025544013f53333a0c07d713"},"summary":"`Hub::next_wake` keeps answering an instant in the past while a call its host asked for later is due and no serve takes it","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:17:26.562791Z","id":"085b71ffb8c24ca6ffa8964015919e706c5d44347b3ddaee76fb4087e82ce2b1","body":{"detail":"`Hub::advance` no longer drops a hold when its wait is past: the hold stops keeping its registration connected (`Hub::is_connected` counts only holds whose wait is not over) and stays open, so `Hub::close` answers it with what is due at the instant of the close. `Hub::ready` answers `Ready::Deliver` once `now >= Serve::until`, and `Ready::Ended` only for a serve a later one replaced or whose registration is gone. The routes' second serve for one asked after its wait is removed (`Surfaces::turn`, crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs). Test `a_serve_is_answered_with_what_is_due_whether_or_not_its_wait_is_over` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs: a `wait_ms: 0` serve closed a millisecond after it was opened, a held one closed 680 ms after its wait, and one closed 20 s after, each sent the call; the generated sequences close serves at later instants than they were opened at.","kind":"comment","references":"5c9cff6fc8fa59a3e1a4285b5c55307d3535300ab2f4f140d9a6e3ebc3e21aa4","summary":"Fixed in the hub: a serve stays open until it is closed, and is answered with what is due whether or not its wait is over","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:17:26.563203Z","id":"8d6230d5509b77c09e56504d823c0c40ae846a8ea3ab04e826cb5296f431a283","body":{"detail":"`Hub::next_wake(store, now)` filters its own instants (a call asked for later, a registration ceasing to be connected, a lease ending) to those after `now`, so a call its host asked for later that is due and unserved is no longer answered; the store's next deadline is answered as it is, since `Hub::expire` settles it. Test `the_next_wake_is_never_a_past_instant_of_what_only_a_serve_can_take` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs walks `next_wake` and `expire` from the call coming due to its deadline: every instant is after the one asked at. `STALE_WAKE` (crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs) is kept for the one case left, a deadline a pass could not settle because the store failed, and its documentation says so. The oldest end's age is not among the instants: the trait has no method for it, and an end is forgotten by whichever pass next runs.","kind":"comment","references":"98411a8d4a711ee165663a7a54fefbee25ad83c97bc7f4545ffbb76e132e892c","summary":"Fixed in the hub: `next_wake` takes the instant and answers nothing at or before it but an unsettled deadline","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:51:56.072625Z","id":"751b4e818a9f59b8ddef57a6092ca9e8adc95c4703f3c794694d5ff4a2b29245","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::ask` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs) emits the waiting call as a `call` event with the code `waiting`, remembers its place, operation, key, surface and deadline (`Answer::waiting_until`), and answers the sandbox `HostReply::Waiting`; it is not told as a call of the step and not journaled, and no call after it is placed or reaches a session. On `ErrorKind::Parked` with no cancel asked, `Tick::script` drops the sessions without `finish` and `Tick::park` calls `Store::park_run` with the place, `waiting_on` (`surface`, `op`, `key`, `since`, `deadline`) and the deadline as `due_at`: `Park::Parked` is `Wait::Parked`, `Park::Answered` (the answer was journaled first) and a run that went `stopping` leave the tick runnable. `tick` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs) claims no parked run and, once its `due_at` has passed, has the store journal the deadline's failure (`expire_waits`). The driver neither requeues nor claims a parked run and arms its deadline (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs, `settle`, `resume`); `Driver::wake` takes it on. Held by crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs and `generic::a_run_of_the_fake_alone_parks_on_a_call_that_waits_and_goes_on_when_it_is_answered`.","kind":"comment","references":"cb4e8d44597370c166f01dbd034f7e2d9615d3107ed57c9bc7febf1738ada9e4","summary":"The step parks: `StepHost::ask` answers `HostReply::Waiting` at this place and `Tick::park` parks the run on the call","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:51:56.072740Z","id":"3cbb87b4ebd3d3dc99f90db9ce80735b0e3704adfa6ce736b77c7dfc9c494a81","body":{"detail":"`expire_waits` calls `Store::expire_calls` with the hub's rule for a call that was never answered (`broker::stored::deadline_failure`, which is `bot_surfaces::unanswered`), then unparks a run that is parked still. It rests on three things. (1) The call the run waits on is a pending call of the run's own store, which holds for the daemon's broker (`StoredPending`). With a broker that keeps its calls elsewhere (`MemoryPending`, tests) nothing journals an answer for a parked run and nothing unparks it when one arrives: at the deadline the run is unparked with nothing journaled and its step, run again, sends the call again under its key and is answered what the broker kept. A run of a surface with no broker at all (the fake) is unparked by whoever has its answer (`Store::unpark_run`). (2) `Deps::clock`, by which a tick reads whether `due_at` has passed, and the broker's `BrokerClock`, by which the deadline was computed, are one clock in a daemon; crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs moves the two together (`World::pass`), and they differ there by the ten seconds a provider waits before it parks. (3) `Store::expire_calls` ends every call that is due, of any run: the hub's own state for those calls (a call its host asked to be sent later) is dropped the next time the hub reads what is pending. Whoever journals an answer or runs `expire_calls` outside a tick (a `serve`, a timed pass) must call `Driver::wake` for each run it made due: the driver arms only the deadline of a run it settled or resumed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1214},"end":{"line":1224},"content_hash":"b612b30f30164bbd75a094d434bf1e618c0ed5eb8d91ea92437b61d591fdf712"},"summary":"A parked run's deadline is ended by the run loop through the store's pending calls, not through the hub: what that rests on","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:51:56.072793Z","id":"93d61a27c3c97bda6ec95c519293964b4a5bdbbb3b398b765b646dc4fc3ada79","body":{"detail":"Between a park and its answer the audit journal says nothing of the run. Adding the two kinds touches `clax_core::audit` (the kinds and their fields), the toolpath export and its redaction (crates/clax-store/src/toolpath), ten pinned files under crates/clax-core/tests/toolpath, docs/contract.md, and, to be written with what they record, the store's own transactions: `Store::park_run`, `Store::answer_call`, `Store::expire_calls`, `Store::unpark_run` and `Store::cancel_run` each change a run's parking and none takes an audit context or a subject. A record written by the run loop beside `park_run` would not be in its transaction, and an unpark is made by whoever brings an answer, with no run loop there. So the store is where both records belong. What the store keeps meanwhile: `bot_runs.parked_at`, `parked_ms`, `waiting_on` (`surface`, `op`, `key`, `since`, `deadline`) and `bot_steps.parks` (`Store::run_parking`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":703},"end":{"line":760},"content_hash":"f144187ddbb7b8ebd9222547d0c85a656b9480bc721fbf87de8f3537c604fa62"},"summary":"No audit record is written when a run parks or is resumed: `bot.park` and `bot.unpark` are not added","tags":["status:deferred","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:51:56.072829Z","id":"126cfc9b77739eb4e06ec5e768bc641cf257e5e5bafb9d303fdd037a2d900495","body":{"detail":"`Tick::unreached_wait` looks at the rows of the step's journal that this attempt's script did not reach and makes the step end `replay_diverged` when one of them is at the place of a pending call marked `waits` (`Store::pending_call_at`): the answer a run parked for must not go unused in a step that commits as if nothing were wrong (`park::a_replay_that_ends_before_the_call_it_waited_for_diverges`). The general case is left as it was: a crashed attempt that made more calls than the attempt that commits leaves rows the commit does not reach, and the step commits cleanly, each such row told only as a `bot.call` record of a call no committed attempt reported (`Tick::unreported_calls`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs). The fixtures under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden named `resumed.*.json` pin steps of Clax's own runs executed again after a crash; the rule was not widened, so none of them was put at risk. Not covered either: a waited call with no pending row (the fake's `fake.ask`), whose answer is never journaled before the step is run again. Whether every unreached row should be a divergence is the owner's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":673},"end":{"line":695},"content_hash":"2c2b810155ab4a76791051885c95ac250574ad863518a3a6b8e9bb33aecc50b1"},"summary":"A script that ends before a journaled call diverges only when that call is one the run waited for; any other unreached row still commits cleanly","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:51:56.072861Z","id":"bf712dc686e4f2330654e42fabe5018d6b395670b94ae35ad1689466bc34f129","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::may_not_park` refuses the waiting call with `park_limit` when the open step has parked `RunLimits::max_parks_per_step` times (`Parking::parks`, which `Store::park_run` counts) and with `parked_runs_limit` when the person has `RunLimits::max_parked_runs` runs parked (`Store::parked_runs_of`). The refusal is journaled by the run loop at the call's place, so the step run again is refused the same and the host's call stops waiting (the store ends a pending call's wait once its place is journaled); the host was already sent the call and may have asked its person, and its late answer is ignored. The count of a person's parked runs is read outside any transaction with the park: runs of one person that reach a waiting call in the same instant can each pass the check, so the bound can be exceeded by as many runs as the driver ticks at once for that person (`per_person_concurrency`, 1 by default). Neither bound is a setting of the instance yet: `RunLimits::of` gives 8 and 20.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":127},"end":{"line":160},"content_hash":"965eebaf48f9357c6977c77df7dac6748016f92eec5617938984431270508d92"},"summary":"The two bounds on parking are read when a call would wait, and their refusal is journaled","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:51:56.072894Z","id":"cb51a588911b8a31cbc7d290dab20e9496b038480937ab20602d4e755e033e79","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::ask` emits one `call` event with the code `waiting` for the call an attempt parks on and does not add it to the attempt's log of calls (`told`). The log of a parked attempt is dropped with the attempt, so whether the waiting call was in it shows only in the run's live events, which no test of crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs or tests/generic.rs subscribes to for a parked step. A change that called `told` for the waiting call before answering `HostReply::Waiting` (a second `call` event, with the same code) passed `//crates/clax-bot:all`. The event's shape is `Event::Call` as for any call (`op`, `target`, `ok: false`, `code: \"waiting\"`, `ms`); no new event kind was added, so the stream of a run of Clax's own surface, where no operation waits, is as it was.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":211},"end":{"line":296},"content_hash":"1293eaa3c1ceb5004661feebdb9cc0435ce3514b4f1ab6d93757c021be168fef"},"summary":"No test reads what a parked attempt tells of its calls: an attempt that also logged the waiting call as a call of the step passes every test","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:17:12.345331Z","id":"572edc0ece287179a28505d0dd6451c2fb8602e27d59c12b12f3b0c1b144952a","body":{"detail":"Failure: run R ticks; its script parks (`Store::park_run` commits, R is `parked`); before `Driver::settle` runs for that tick, a `serve` journals the answer (`Store::answer_call`, R becomes `queued`) and calls `Driver::wake(R)`, or a person cancels (`Store::cancel_run`, R becomes `stopping`) and `Driver::cancel(R)` runs. The same holds during a tick that found R parked and not late (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1126-1136). `Driver::wake` queues nothing for a run in `active` (driver.rs:137-145), and the `Wait::Parked` arm here reads R, finds it not `parked`, arms nothing and returns. R is `queued` (or `stopping`) in the store and in no queue, delayed list or timer; `spawn_forever` sleeps until another wake. Only `Driver::resume` (a restart) finds it. Shown by a scratch test (a clock that, read while R is parked, answers and wakes, or cancels): after three `run_until_idle` calls R is `Queued` (answer) or `Stopping` (cancel), ticks [Runnable, Waiting(Parked)], `next_wake` None. Fix: in this arm, when the run read is live and not `parked`, push it on the queue (with that change the same scratch tests finish and stop, and every `park::` test passes); or have `wake` mark an active run to be queued again when it settles.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":382},"end":{"line":399},"content_hash":"87adcbc22c69d1c5947068848d44bf4ad425ed0245bf0040f60733ba88111460"},"suggested_fix":"let row = self.deps.store.run(id).ok().flatten(); if row.as_ref().is_some_and(|r| r.status != RunStatus::Parked && !r.status.is_terminal()) { st.queue.push_back(id.to_string()); return; }","summary":"An answer or a cancel that lands while the driver is ticking a parked run is lost: the run stays queued or stopping with no driver entry until the process restarts","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:17:12.345443Z","id":"16e82158a121e2b40c048a4e3f917f1d38f1f8bd9206decd2b16d981d45f2800","body":{"detail":"A parked run whose host is away long enough for its registration to end (the case parking exists for) is run again (unparked by `Store::unpark_run`, a settled sibling call, or the deadline path); `OutsideSession::call` finds no served registration and answers `Answer::refused(provider.gone())`, which the core journals: `{code: unavailable, message: \"the surface's host is gone: its registration has ended. Nothing happened\"}`, retryable. The pending row says `sent_at` was set an hour before. For a write or effect that is the wrong claim: the hub's own rule (`bot_surfaces::unanswered`) gives `unknown_outcome` for a call that was sent, and `Store::expire_calls` uses it for a replaced registration. A script that trusts `unavailable` repeats an effect that may have happened. Seen with a scratch test on crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs's desk (park, pass 3600 s with no serve, `unpark_run`, drive): journal row 1 as above, pending row (sent_at 10:00, accepted_at None, answered_at 11:00). Fix: when the place has a pending row, answer what `unanswered` gives for it (or leave the call to `expire_calls`) instead of `gone()`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":297},"end":{"line":300},"content_hash":"e81e37ec2c017041d0e16af309931dbc380ba47c47f0dea71ccaf5f3d607e096"},"summary":"A waiting call replayed after its host's registration lapsed is journaled `unavailable ... Nothing happened` although the host was sent it","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:17:12.345517Z","id":"c942d7666b333deab566a64e023e542dc8da8d50651cc6613acd894a688cf5a3","body":{"detail":"`OutsideSession::call` computes `deadline = now + wait_limit` on every attempt (crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs:311) and answers `Answer::waiting_until(deadline)`; the store keeps the call's first `expires_at` (`open_call` returns `Opened::Same`). Re-parked after a wake with nothing answered, the run's `due_at` is the new deadline. Scratch test: parked at 10:00 with due_at = expires_at = next day 10:00; after 3600 s and `unpark_run`, re-parked with due_at next day 11:00:10 while the row still expires at 10:00. Without a hub pass that expires the row and wakes the run, the run sleeps up to one more wait limit. Fix: take the deadline from the pending row (the broker knows it) for `waiting_until`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":286},"end":{"line":292},"content_hash":"03a1dc242a6b13acd3428a611f1dac2e05bb036535834eb35e03a115aa000fe3"},"summary":"A run that parks again on the same call takes the attempt's fresh deadline as its `due_at`, not the pending call's, so its due time moves past the call's expiry","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:17:12.345567Z","id":"71e5c71f74ddd90c2cb18517e28a7b4a214c63407c88bcdd92ca89af137e1088","body":{"detail":"The check runs whenever `calls.diverged` is None, whatever `outcome.error` is. A replay that hits the CPU limit before the waited call (limits are wall- and load-sensitive) is told to the model as \"the script took another path when run again\", not as the limit it hit. Narrow it to scripts that returned or threw on their own, or keep the original error and add the divergence to it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":524},"end":{"line":539},"content_hash":"e48cab78c70b27dbc18a66c19f283bba21f721777e75d3734b3547bc8dd156c1"},"summary":"The unreached-wait divergence replaces whatever error the script ended with, including a CPU, memory or call limit, by `replay_diverged`","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:17:12.345686Z","id":"7816e3eaa1e58fff08b29bde5bd351673989861c239a17615e31c4903caf1077","body":{"detail":"`expire_waits` ends every due call of every run, including calls whose registration was replaced (`expire_calls`' second clause), and unparks their runs, clearing `due_at`. Nothing wakes those runs: their driver entry stays at their own `due_at` (none, for a run with no deadline), and a later hub pass that would have woken them finds the calls answered and reports nothing. Bounded by the deadline for a run that has one; a run with no deadline stays queued with no entry until a restart. Consider a per-run expiry here, or returning the runs it made due for the driver to wake. Adds to 3cbb87b4ebd3d3dc99f90db9ce80735b0e3704adfa6ce736b77c7dfc9c494a81, point (3).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1214},"end":{"line":1224},"content_hash":"b612b30f30164bbd75a094d434bf1e618c0ed5eb8d91ea92437b61d591fdf712"},"summary":"A tick's global `expire_calls` makes other runs due without waking their driver entries, and takes those runs from the hub's own pass","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:17:12.345788Z","id":"01db40eecc85dd272cfc62e7187283ab12215d22d7829453a8428095e4ad7ed6","body":{"detail":"Changing `.saturating_sub(self.parked)` to `.saturating_sub(self.parked * 2)` leaves `//crates/clax-bot:integration` and `:clax_bot_lib_test` green: `park::parked_time_does_not_count_against_the_runs_minutes` checks only that a run parked longer than its cap goes on. A run that parks for an hour could then compute for an hour more than `max_run_minutes`. A test that parks, is answered, and then works until the cap, checking that it stops at cap + parked time and not later, would hold it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1305},"end":{"line":1314},"content_hash":"3920f0a4f20b4a3fa7382a6973cd2aa0468f7a4e193474f0750cf770e66d4015"},"summary":"No test catches parked time subtracted more than once from the run's minutes","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:57:43.868865Z","id":"492be5da7773eaca37491c3ebaf783213240e80be4a07bbe2886f93a399bfafe","body":{"detail":"The list is held to `Limits::list` patterns and each to `Pattern::parse` (crates/clax-surface/src/allow.rs:58), which bounds no name's length, where a kind's `@allow` is held to `Limits::name_bytes` (`Pattern::names`, allow.rs:91). Probe, through a `TestServer`: `PUT /api/bot/surfaces/desk` with `settings: {allow: [\"desk.\" + 7 MiB of `a`]}` answers 200 in 295 ms, and the registration's row holds `settings_json` of 7,340,051 bytes. `HubLimits::registrations` says a registration keeps megabytes at the most; with this it keeps up to the 8 MiB of a request, 256 MiB for 32 registrations, and each new registration leaves that much in `bot_registrations` for good. `allow` of a start (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot_surfaces.rs:195) is read by the same rule, and is not kept.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1411},"end":{"line":1424},"content_hash":"a7f2a1d41db23571a8579c02af857ae8b3c13b9e18563c3658be31a00e999cc6"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse (`invalid_settings`, `invalid_request` for a start) a pattern with a name longer than `Limits::name_bytes` or with more names than `Limits::depth` + 2, as `Pattern::names` already measures.","summary":"A pattern of `settings.allow` has no longest length: one of 7 MiB is accepted, held with the registration and written to its row","tags":["security","review","dos","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:57:43.868932Z","id":"9aa839eeb20a81b8428783a478fa02ac97de99d40150bd0c4837cce1f6a22254","body":{"detail":"`bot.surface.registered` is written by `Store::issue_registration` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_surfaces.rs:221), which `Hub::register` calls only for a request that names no registration. A request that names one changes the words sent to every later turn of that registration's runs and records nothing. Probe, through a `TestServer`: 200 requests that go on as one registration of `bench`, each with another definition and another prompt file, add no audit event; afterwards the hub's prompt is the last one sent, and the row's `def_hash` is the first definition's and its `prompt` is NULL (`StoredPending::issue_registration`, crates/clax-bot/src/broker/stored.rs, writes them once). A run's `bot.run_start` record names `started_by` and its bindings keep the definition's hash, but the prompt file a run was sent is kept nowhere (record 2a87725b: the pieces are computed and not stored). So what a host put into the prompts of runs that act as the owner cannot be read back after the fact. The audit actor of both records is what the request's own headers say (`DeferredAudit`, crates/clax-http/src/audit.rs:185: the owner by hand, or the session `X-Clax-Session` names), so two hosts with the token are not told apart by it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1479},"end":{"line":1512},"content_hash":"9d32bcf7ef2da4c6d3af45dd2a060a2438ea09403c258250cce4b9654902a574"},"suggested_fix":"When a registration is gone on with and its definition's hash, its prompt file or its settings differ, update the row and record `bot.surface.registered` with the registration, the new `def_hash` and the SHA-256 of the prompt file; record that hash with the first registration too.","summary":"Going on as a registration replaces its definition, prompt file and settings with no audit record, and its row keeps the first ones","tags":["security","review","audit","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:57:43.869086Z","id":"ea6a7c1ff18d01299821444c104d345290c6a5bf02fbd1c3ad05e463a7d20b1d","body":{"detail":"`HubBroker::with` ends with `changed.notify_waiters()` (line 466), whoever called it and whatever it did: a `serve` that brought nothing, `Surfaces::limits`, `find`, the listing. Each `outcome` that waits then takes the lock and calls `Hub::expire`, which is `Store::expire_calls` and `Store::expire_ends`, two write transactions, on the run loop's async task and not on a store thread. So a host that polls with `wait_ms: 0` in a loop makes every waiting run of every host do store writes at the rate of its polling; the work for each wake is bounded, the rate is the host's to choose.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":557},"end":{"line":590},"content_hash":"c0d1699017f993b6c3351531cff55646b725daf52ba2d007fd599234d9519f8b"},"suggested_fix":"Notify `changed` only from what can settle a call (an answer taken, `Hub::expire` settling one), and let `outcome` read the call's outcome without settling deadlines itself, since the timer and every `open` already do.","summary":"Every use of the hub wakes every run that waits for an answer, and each then settles deadlines in a write transaction under the hub's lock, on its async task","tags":["review","dos","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T22:57:43.869114Z","id":"dec4c5473416196de4393e441ea8df9e636272ea204102c69a7a15a53afb17c0","body":{"detail":"`OutsideProvider` keeps the definition under the hash in the run's bindings and the prompt of `CallBroker::registration` (crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs:484), which is the registration's current one. `Hub::register` checked that prompt against the definition registered with it, not against an earlier one. So going on as a registration with a new prompt file changes the system prompt of its runs between two turns, by whichever host of the registration registered last; and a new file that names a kind or a `{rules.<namespace>}` the run's definition lacks, or lacks one it has, does not read for the run, whose next turn stops it `request_gone` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1856). Read, not probed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":380},"end":{"line":383},"content_hash":"45603fe4b37f2ba46b625ea9643979c83aa8c13a6f6e5b5d06d4bb7006b4ec97"},"suggested_fix":"Keep a registration's prompt file by hash as its definition is kept, name that hash in the run's binding when the run is made, and send the run that file for all its turns.","summary":"A run's turns are sent the prompt file its registration has now, read against the definition the run was made with","tags":["review","prompt-injection","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:36:20.116619Z","id":"cd885e0b1ca3922702cb2571b3acf6aaef2ce0bce931f0d6dd0bd6606432f50e","body":{"detail":"`allow_patterns` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) holds a list to `Limits::list` patterns, each to `depth + 2` names of `name_bytes` at the most, before `Pattern::parse`; a registration's settings and a start's `allow` are both read by it. `check_settings` holds the settings to `HubLimits::settings_bytes` (16 KiB of JSON) and each limit to `\"none\"` or its range (1 to a year of seconds for `call_timeout_s` and `wait_limit_s`, 0 to 1,000,000 for `max_effects_per_run`, 1 to 1,000,000 for `runs.max_steps`, 1 to 525,600 for `runs.max_run_minutes`). docs/contract.md, \"Registering\", says them. Tests: `a_registrations_settings_are_held_to_their_bounds` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs, and `a_setting_out_of_its_bounds_is_refused_and_nothing_is_kept` in crates/clax-server/tests/api_bot_surfaces_bounds.rs (the 7 MiB pattern is 400 `invalid_settings` and leaves no row).","kind":"comment","references":"492be5da7773eaca37491c3ebaf783213240e80be4a07bbe2886f93a399bfafe","summary":"Fixed: an allow pattern, the settings' size and each limit have bounds","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","security","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:36:20.117810Z","id":"17c7e42a4dab3faa77492b074d217d7b0f6bd09541c25eb600a090d0c1676f57","body":{"detail":"`Hub::settles` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) counts the calls settled (by an answer or a deadline) and the ends recorded; `HubBroker::with` (crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs) compares it before and after and calls `notify_waiters` only when it moved, and `HubBroker::ended` goes through it. `outcome` no longer calls `Hub::expire` on each look: it does when the store's next deadline is at or before now, which is the one case a broker with no timer needs it for. Not done: waking only the waiters of the call that was settled (one `Notify` for all is kept). The broker's own tests pass unchanged (crates/clax-bot/src/broker/tests.rs), and `answers_judged_with_the_hub_let_go_settle_as_those_judged_under_it` in crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces/tests.rs holds the count to what was settled; no test counts the wakes of a use that settled nothing.","kind":"comment","references":"ea6a7c1ff18d01299821444c104d345290c6a5bf02fbd1c3ad05e463a7d20b1d","summary":"Fixed: a use of the hub wakes those that wait only when it settled something, and reading an outcome writes only when a deadline has come","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","security","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:36:20.118916Z","id":"11ab1d63fadc52a424b0ab360c3b42838a1f11caf420f35d55fedf8a796bb8e5","body":{"detail":"`Store::change_registration` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_surfaces.rs) gives the row the definition's hash, the prompt file and the settings when any differs, and records `bot.surface.registered` with `changed: true`, `def_hash` and `prompt_sha256` (when there is a prompt file); a first registration's record has `prompt_sha256` too. `Pending::change_registration` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs; it does nothing unless written, and `StoredPending` writes it) is called by `Hub::register_parsed` for every request that names a registration. No new audit kind: the toolpath's goldens stand. Tests: `going_on_with_a_registration_changed_brings_its_row_up_to_date_and_is_recorded` (the store's), `going_on_as_a_registration_with_something_changed_is_recorded_and_kept` in crates/clax-server/tests/api_bot_surfaces_bounds.rs.","kind":"comment","references":"9aa839eeb20a81b8428783a478fa02ac97de99d40150bd0c4837cce1f6a22254","summary":"Fixed: going on with something changed brings the row up to date and is recorded","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","security","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:36:20.120257Z","id":"592b7652f2b474432dcd1c58581e9fe6867b4a39e23d0d653bcf95394c4cd14e","body":{"detail":"Nothing was changed for this record. What it needs, none of it written: prompt texts kept by SHA-256 in the store (a table in the surfaces migration, with its prune beside `prune_surfaces_in`); an optional prompt hash in a version-2 `StoredBinding` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs), set by the start route from `Snapshot::prompt` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs); and `OutsideProvider::prompt` (crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs) answering the text kept under the run's own hash, the standard prompt for a binding without one. Until then the bound on changes (eight a minute) limits how often a prompt can be swapped under a run, and each change is recorded with the prompt's hash.","kind":"comment","references":"dec4c5473416196de4393e441ea8df9e636272ea204102c69a7a15a53afb17c0","summary":"Not done: a run is still sent its registration's current prompt file","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","security","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:43:47.908093Z","id":"b1093ce9da8042e8cd6d59f5ca9044eaa31bbd36dfccd68d59295aea33f4b8d6","body":{"detail":"`Driver::wake` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs) puts a run that is in `active` in `State::again`; `settle` takes the mark and, unless the tick ended the run, queues the run again in place of whatever wait the tick's outcome would have set. `Driver::cancel` goes through `wake`, so a cancel is covered. In the `Wait::Parked` arm a run whose row is live and not `parked`, or that was marked, is queued and the driver woken; only a run that is parked still is armed for its `due_at`. The other arms, for a wake or cancel that landed mid-tick: `Runnable` queued the run anyway and `Budget` put it off to the next call, so nothing was lost; `Stalled`, `Retry`, `NotOurs` and a tick that failed delayed the run by their own wait, so the wake was late by that long and not lost, and now they are queued at once; `Crashed` (a test's hook) left the run to another driver, and is queued again when marked. Held by park::an_answer_that_lands_while_the_parking_tick_settles_is_not_lost, park::a_cancel_that_lands_while_the_parking_tick_settles_stops_the_run, park::a_run_found_answered_when_its_parking_tick_settles_is_ticked_again_unwoken (no wake at all), park::an_answer_that_lands_while_a_tick_looks_at_a_parked_run_is_not_lost (the look-tick; it tries the driver's first five readings of its clock, since which of them is the tick's own depends on the order the driver polls what it waits on) and park::a_run_woken_while_it_is_ticked_is_ticked_again_whatever_the_tick_decided (a tick that stalls). Removing the mark fails the last; removing the not-parked clause fails the unwoken one.","kind":"comment","references":"572edc0ece287179a28505d0dd6451c2fb8602e27d59c12b12f3b0c1b144952a","summary":"Both fixes are in: a wake for a run that is being ticked marks it to be queued again when the tick settles, and the parked arm queues a run it finds parked no longer","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:43:47.908612Z","id":"142ce823b4d387aff149ba74c66f50beb92c31a3566efe520618984cade6c0de","body":{"detail":"`OutsideSession::unsent` (crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs) is what both failures to send now answer through: a provider with no registration, and a `CallBroker::send` that failed (the registration ended, or its host is not connected). When the run's store holds a pending call at the place that has not been answered (`Claim::pending_call`), the answer is `broker::stored::deadline_failure` of it, the hub's `unanswered` rule: `unavailable` (retryable) when it was never sent, `expired` for a read that was, `unknown_outcome` for a write or an effect that was. The core journals it at the place, after which the store no longer counts the call as waiting. With no pending row (a first attempt, or a broker that keeps its calls elsewhere) the answer is as before. park::a_waited_call_whose_host_is_gone_is_answered_by_what_is_known_of_it plays the sequence of this record with a binding that keeps its definition's hash: `unknown_outcome`, not retryable. A binding with no hash whose registration is gone has no definition at all, and its run stops at its next turn for want of its kind (`prompt` and `finish` read the kind's definition): the call is answered the same way first.","kind":"comment","references":"16e82158a121e2b40c048a4e3f917f1d38f1f8bd9206decd2b16d981d45f2800","summary":"A call that cannot be sent and has a pending row that still waits is answered by what is known of that row, not `unavailable ... Nothing happened`","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:43:47.909032Z","id":"309547245f80700ca5c03d47fae7274445c16773d0890361b357d68008526385","body":{"detail":"`OutsideSession::call` answers `Answer::waiting_until` with the pending row's `expires_at` (`Claim::pending_call`), and with the attempt's own deadline only when the store keeps no row for the place (a broker in memory). So `Store::park_run`'s `due_at` is the call's expiry on every park. park::a_run_parked_again_on_the_same_call_keeps_the_calls_deadline: parked, an hour passes, the host goes on as its registration, the run is unparked and parks again with the `due_at` it had, `parks` 2, and its host is sent nothing more. One consequence: with a policy that sets no limit on a wait the row still expires at the hub's own limit (`HubLimits::call_ms`), so such a run is due then and not never.","kind":"comment","references":"c942d7666b333deab566a64e023e542dc8da8d50651cc6613acd894a688cf5a3","summary":"A waiting answer now carries the deadline the pending call was opened with","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:43:47.909421Z","id":"fad97e42781db9b82735f488d4c4c505c933b9265287c380c2ae59374a1de8de","body":{"detail":"`expire_waits` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs) calls `Store::expire_calls_of(run_id, now, failure)` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_pending.rs), which is `expire_calls` for the pending calls of one run, so no other run is made due without its own driver entry, and the hub's pass still finds and reports the others. Store test: bot_pending::tests::a_runs_own_deadline_is_ended_and_no_other_runs. Whoever runs the pass for every run (`Store::expire_calls`, a `serve`'s opening or a timed pass) still has to wake the driver for each run it reports as unparked.","kind":"comment","references":"7816e3eaa1e58fff08b29bde5bd351673989861c239a17615e31c4903caf1077","summary":"A tick now ends its own run's deadlines only: `Store::expire_calls_of`","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:43:47.909806Z","id":"b52f241b142c90c6628578a4c37e7f9be77b7eba19d4f24126753c1a52624083","body":{"detail":"The check in `Tick::script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs) now runs only when the script's outcome has no error and the run has a binding of a surface other than `clax`: a script that hit a limit, threw or failed keeps its own error (park::a_replay_that_fails_before_the_call_it_waited_for_keeps_its_error), and a run whose one surface is Clax's, none of whose operations waits, makes no read of the store for it, crashed or not. A script that throws on its other path is therefore not told it diverged; what it is told is its own error.","kind":"comment","references":"71e5c71f74ddd90c2cb18517e28a7b4a214c63407c88bcdd92ca89af137e1088","summary":"Only a script that ended with no error can be said to have taken another path, and a run of Clax's surface alone is not looked at","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:43:47.910196Z","id":"6140929f18cdc9a087ef824a4974d4243deff769ac0be3d525e01b194a808364","body":{"detail":"park::time_at_work_after_a_park_still_counts_against_the_runs_minutes: a run is parked for twenty minutes, answered, and then thirty-one minutes pass with the run not parked; its next tick stops it `run_cap`. With `.saturating_sub(self.parked * 2)` the run goes on and the test fails.","kind":"comment","references":"01db40eecc85dd272cfc62e7187283ab12215d22d7829453a8428095e4ad7ed6","summary":"A test now fails when parked time is subtracted twice","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.584194Z","id":"3b56abce0701b0b7aab5fb7f66046898eb3e67ea4cf56b479b9ddb28f909c8c2","body":{"detail":"Commit 2b4ceaf85. `step`, `open`, `a_run`, the goldens' steps, `disturbed`, `generated` and `a_written_run` moved to crates/clax-bot/src/history_fixtures.rs (cfg(test), declared in lib.rs), with the two tests of the folds alone (`the_run_loops_folds_read_nothing_clax_keeps_in_a_payload`, `the_transcript_of_a_run_keeps_what_a_turn_relies_on`) and the seeds' check (`the_seeds_hold_every_kind_of_step_and_each_fact_a_fold_reads`). The three changes that record d050941c found only the comparison failed are now unit tests: `history::tests::committed_counts_the_committed_steps_and_not_their_numbers`, `history::tests::only_a_turn_says_whether_the_model_was_nudged`, `clax::tally::tests::a_published_value_of_any_shape_starts_the_replies_over`. What the folds give for the 4000 generated histories is no longer compared with anything: a change to a fold that no unit test, golden or integration test sees now passes. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"aaf6076f0e3307e3dc0c84347325c483d6540cf9f86659fb0b1ee8ea3b25b85a","summary":"The file is gone: the single fold and its three comparisons are deleted, and the hand-built and generated histories are in crates/clax-bot/src/history_fixtures.rs","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.584617Z","id":"b3a8dac3f4908772f8612a8edcc66884b936308805408c00dbba6127c5478fb2","body":{"detail":"Commits 42ccfd7f0 (each scenario's run through the seam written to tests/scenarios/script.<name>.json while the comparison passed) and ebe3d108f (src/script_reference.rs, src/script_reference/web.rs deleted; src/script_differential.rs became src/script_scenarios.rs: each scenario driven once through `tick()`, held to its fixture, to `went_as_meant`, and to the count of `{code, message}` rows of web operations the run lacks, three in all). `Deps::reference` and its branch went in 1e9aefb5b. `extern crate self as clax_bot` and `test_compile_data` stay: the scenarios include tests/common. `events::with_web_fields` stays: crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot.rs calls it. The lost-claim answers are held by tests/web.rs (`a_web_operation_a_run_lacks_is_refused_as_itself_when_the_claim_is_lost`, `..._in_a_step_that_commits`). What is no longer driven: a run begun by one build and resumed by the other; the fixtures hold the journal rows the code before the seam wrote, but nothing resumes a run from them. Eleven one-line changes, each applied alone after the deletion and `bazelisk test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test //crates/clax-bot:integration` run, all fail tests: a refused publish answered `Answer::unwritten` (clax/publish.rs) fails script_scenarios groups 2 and 3, golden::scripts::a_publish_conflict_and_the_rebase_after_it_are_pinned and seam::refused_writes_behind_a_session_are_journaled_and_not_asked_of_the_host_again; default addresses dropped fails the three script_scenarios groups and 5 integration tests; a replayed read sent to the host (clax/replies.rs) fails the three groups, providers::claxs_own_provider_passes_the_provider_conformance_suite, rules::a_script_run_again_sees_its_steps_clock_and_journaled_reads and seam::a_replayed_host_read_and_a_second_publish_behind_a_session; the finish rule inverted (clax/ending.rs) fails 8 lib and 28 integration tests; a notice's place moved by one fails 8 and 36; the roster not turned off fails 4 and 42; `unreported_calls` dropped from a stop (ending.rs) fails golden::endings::a_stop_records_the_calls_of_a_script_that_never_committed_and_not_its_own_notice alone (no scenario of src/ending_scenarios.rs reaches it); a notice placed by attempt, so posted again on each, fails 5 and 7; `ended` told before the commit fails 5 and 51; the draft loaded at the run's base (clax/provider.rs) fails the three groups and 5; a replayed rebase made onto the current version (clax/draft.rs) fails groups 2 and 3, golden::resumed::a_script_that_rebased_published_and_fetched_is_resumed_as_it_ran and seam::a_replayed_rebase_behind_a_session_goes_where_it_went. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"9be023649d8f6bb5cad74f087aaa6f01f76ed32c11bae95b6bdbbc387428612a","summary":"Done: the reference, its web performer and the comparison are gone, and the thirty scenarios are held to fixtures in crates/clax-bot/src/script_scenarios.rs","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.585017Z","id":"c3d6a1746e129baee546f80b14ee5c0df4fb9353fc4d0c9286a17e8bd603954d","body":{"detail":"Commits 1e9aefb5b and 359f0c1f4. The thirteen ending scenarios, plainly and at each of the three cuts, are held to fixtures in crates/clax-bot/src/ending_scenarios.rs (tests/scenarios/ending.*.json, written in 42ccfd7f0 while the comparison passed); a finish whose sources cannot be read twice is held to its two counted failures and a fixture. `BotHost::working` is called by `clax`'s provider alone (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs) and stays, as Clax's host interface. `ending.rs` names no host: `the_run_loops_ending_names_nothing_of_claxs_surface` no longer filters a line out. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"cc3b395f99175b2b9f8a02d2e7d2cd4412f2c4ab540afbbff4609e325cc5ec75","span":{"start":{"line":146},"end":{"line":166},"content_hash":"66e624bff2bcd0a6cad9983dcb1cd14c039e97aed26dd684362ae2a282dc3c50"},"summary":"Done: the endings' reference, its comparison, the two cfg(test) branches, `Tick::entry`, `Tick::op_ctx`, `Deps::reference` and `Deps.host` are gone","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.585406Z","id":"fcaad544b8b3baa6068a490361c8ff89d925ebf17da62989a31a0d36c44fb60d","body":{"detail":"Commit 359f0c1f4: `Deps::new` keeps its signature and hands the host to `Providers::register_clax`; `Providers` keeps the provider it registered (`clax`, cleared when another is registered for the surface) so that `Deps::with_web`/`without_web` call `Providers::set_clax_web`, which registers `ClaxProvider::with_web` of it. After `with_providers` without `clax` they no longer add it back (the first half of record f7b88ea0). No call site in crates/clax-server or crates/clax-worker changed. `registry::tests::the_run_loop_names_nothing_of_claxs_but_what_is_listed` lists two lines (run.rs: `host: Arc<dyn BotHost>,` and the `register_clax` call) and now also catches a line that calls `register_clax(`. Commit ac72d8527: `api::Performer`, `OpSpec::performer` and `PERFORMERS` gone; the table is `Part`/`PARTS` in crates/clax-bot/src/clax/session.rs with its test; the fake host checks an operation against the definition and its access. `BotHost::context`, `working`, `start_waiting` are called by clax/* alone. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"d311ae86405d00caa50c3cfef6652ba4f3514fb97718f52804108254f31abc43","span":{"start":{"line":130},"end":{"line":141},"content_hash":"08117c127ec710f5e84b57a47c58d5b208561d119156aa416cb0d481248f89d0"},"summary":"Done: `Deps` holds no host and no web source, `api::Performer` is gone, and the run loop names the host in two lines of `Deps::new`","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.586176Z","id":"3d4158aa97746a8fd838f390f3c7ceb57604bc34ee47e2febb366d87227c9aa6","body":{"detail":"`Driver::start` sweeps before the repeats of `ended` (`ending::redeliver`), and `driver_started` is called after them, so the move puts the sweep after those repeats. The lease is `Deps.settings`' `lease_secs`, read when the driver starts; `driver_started` takes no argument and the provider holds no settings, and `RunSettings::from_store` reads the database without the server's [bot] overrides (record fe325199, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs), so a provider reading it could sweep with another lease. A move needs `driver_started` to be given the lease or the provider to be given the settings source. Unchanged at 1b6bd5529.","kind":"comment","references":"8f1682a4329e40976d0422df5cc4a8ebedebf64da2177b47ea49e12ce8ceac08","span":{"start":{"line":226},"end":{"line":226},"content_hash":"73007faac82632f69502bdca8256c952132236ce92021496949dfb007ba451bb"},"summary":"Left where it is: moving the sweep into `ClaxProvider::driver_started` changes its order and where its lease comes from","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.586932Z","id":"2d615aa4480cbf6d328e27c431cf3f80b328aaa1184e2dc8c233394028e0a2fc","body":{"detail":"`the_request_message_is_as_written_for_every_shape_of_request` includes a brief request with `brief: None` (`\"brief\": null`) and a thread request with no threads (`\"threads\": []`); still no golden of its own. Commit bfae8df8a.","kind":"comment","references":"3fed56b7530c3d5519b906b0de919d1234f44b8d7bf8d1cec2d815d65ce56d26","span":{"start":{"line":609},"end":{"line":654},"content_hash":"5ba2373c58ecafd87c7165638d65409dc808a1719aa671314a0da03e2f418b72"},"summary":"The two shapes stay held after prompt/reference.rs went: crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/request_words.rs renders both and requires the golden's words and the request's JSON","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
