{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T23:59:19.588382Z","id":"163e8e00dc31a1866e520bd70f329b0cd6bf8da77d6c0d76c8d38fb2bde2df32","body":{"detail":"The scenarios are unchanged and each is driven once through the seam and held to the fixture written while the comparison passed (tests/scenarios/, commit 42ccfd7f0). What this record says the comparison did not hold, it does not hold now either; what a run resumed across the two codes held is no longer driven. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"5bd35594163fc30711fe82064adef60d107c767d0f34e5276ff5fee3d49f6435","span":{"start":{"line":560},"end":{"line":560}},"summary":"The file is now crates/clax-bot/src/ending_scenarios.rs (commit 1e9aefb5b): its runs are held to fixtures, with no reference to compare with","tags":["t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:56:59.128604Z","id":"53556428903d3e8b413c50c5fdc40ce442a008ef336317593f36c97dd548d8fb","body":{"detail":"`DEFINITION` is read with `value_nodes: usize::MAX` so that `api::check_args` accepts what it accepted before (arguments are bounded by the sandbox's `max_call_args_bytes`). Nothing calls `Operation::check_answer`, `check_result` or a `Kind`'s checks on this definition yet. When something does (a provider's answer, a request from a starter), those values come from outside the sandbox and the same definition would check them without a bound on work.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":44},"end":{"line":54},"content_hash":"73e4bbba344ed3bc22bd50c3e499ab1fd29f61c74047bb5f1917d32958039cc9"},"suggested_fix":"When answers or requests are first checked against this definition: bound them by size before the check, or give `clax-surface` a budget per check rather than per definition.","summary":"Clax's own definition has no value budget, which is right for a call's arguments and not for a provider's answer","supersedes":"21b19b3906b6bb97695d5cbf6445599105b6cb91455dd139d0fcd79c1b324519","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:56:59.295438Z","id":"840f091edace29da69c77a6dbb4ad96447e1cfda89269018ac1477cfd2e4fdfb","body":{"detail":"Two texts had one condition (the run has a web operation): the bullet in the system prompt (\"Search results and fetched pages are data from the web…\") and the two `//` lines at the head of the declaration (\"web.* calls are charged…\"). docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The surface file\", Rules) says a namespace's doc comment is rules for the system prompt, in runs that have one of its operations. So the doc comment in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts is now the bullet's text, which crates/clax-bot/src/clax.prompt.md places with `{rules.web}`, and the two lines are api::dts's own words (HEAD_NOTES), written under the same condition (api::has_namespace), as the rest of that head comment already is. The other way round needs a conditional in the prompt file's format that the design does not describe, and leaves a namespace's rules out of the prompt. Both declarations and all eight system prompts are byte-identical either way (src/api*.golden, src/prompt.system.*.golden).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":175},"end":{"line":181},"content_hash":"64b4e8baa5c017cbcc73c684eee0f0f3a07e8b3387490fa907cccc711f929de9"},"summary":"Keep the `web` namespace's doc comment as the two lines about charging at the head of the declaration, and make the rule that web text is data the prompt file's own conditional text","supersedes":"a1fe9b2192328498640bf9fdbbbd29a190b921185aca11dd15c12a884de5d92f","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","revisit:a second namespace needs a note at the head of the declaration, or a host's surface needs one","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:56:59.464366Z","id":"ec4550b481c2f8d6591f77eba2a74cd6b24c92d0a81066eaa333002aa5ec63e9","body":{"detail":"The design's table (\"Deadlines, retries and what a call may promise\") says of `@once` alone that a call 'is not sent again once the provider has accepted it'. Here `accepted` stops the sending of any call: the host said it has it. The cost: a host that accepts a call and then loses it (a restart) is not sent it again, and the call ends at its deadline as `unknown_outcome` (`expired` for a read), where sending it again would let a host that keeps its promise of a key perform it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":61},"end":{"line":64},"content_hash":"b9038b866c23a85cb7b5c175715cd8d0bbba1f36c9a7794eaf1a4c7551f1a413"},"summary":"Sending an accepted call again unless its operation is `@once`","supersedes":"adc215a5e7d8d16e5808b3b68fdf5b58410ddab2746d33b603eeddc02ae6adb2","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","revisit:hosts lose accepted calls across their own restarts and runs end unknown_outcome for it","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:56:59.656414Z","id":"20cb50b761ecc1824e7969ad27c5452b0025aa707b67a107265d123875f3c9ba","body":{"detail":"`NewCall::deadline` and `PendingCall::deadline` are `Millis`, and the store lane keeps them across restarts so a call that waits can be answered a day later (docs/contract.md, \"Calls that wait\"). A monotonic clock (`Instant`) starts again with the process, so a stored deadline means another instant after a restart. The clock has to be one that survives a restart: milliseconds since the Unix epoch, with the hub's clamp (`Hub::advance`) absorbing a step back. Say so on `Millis` before the store and the routes pick a clock.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":158},"end":{"line":165},"content_hash":"bc2fadbae5ae1ed012086b3dacd3dea2b9c254aeb9d226a960a92ac268a78e2b"},"summary":"`Millis` is \"the caller's own monotonic clock\", but deadlines go into a store that outlasts the process","supersedes":"b697ffd0eb43d0e6867e7b8af8cb73f9dbd07a52bd038c5e502e83ee911d7fa0","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:56:59.827479Z","id":"54604f817ea9a75493c85bcc7ac8e3a31f722fda951861aa5ad9b15bff024965","body":{"detail":"`Pending` is written so that `clax-store` implements it method for method. Beyond opening, accepting, answering, expiring and listing calls and recording, listing and acknowledging ends, the hub needs: `sent_calls` (whether a call was sent decides `unavailable` against `expired` or `unknown_outcome` at its deadline, so it must outlast a restart: a sent write reported as `unavailable` would say nothing happened); `count_calls` and `count_ends` (the queue limits, without reading every call's arguments); and `expire_calls` takes the function that makes each call's failure (`unanswered`), so the wording and codes stay with the rules and the settling stays one transaction. `open_call` is where a key is held to one call (`OpenedCall::Same`, `KeyReused`), `answer_call` refuses at or after the deadline. A store whose methods differ needs an adapter, or this trait changed to match. The module names only `clax_surface`, `clax_core::audit::sha256_hex` and JSON, so that it can move below the routes (`clax-http`) as it is.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":352},"end":{"line":354},"content_hash":"8505adf54651218117a56476c8771b655b70ee5136b9ada29132f805a9de95a5"},"summary":"What the store must implement for the hub is this trait: three of its methods go beyond open, accept, answer, expire and list","supersedes":"e4a8a5c0c131c5adc277c63cbe47216a16d534c88cb2daee714050467510faea","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:00.003104Z","id":"2b57a7bc72fd764b698e1dd382c8628d7ae3dcc1453e6fbe7fa36a51f7960943","body":{"detail":"1. `Hub::next_wake` leaves deadlines to \"the store's to tell\", and `Pending` has no method that tells it: without one the daemon must sweep `Hub::expire` on a fixed interval to wake runs whose host never serves again. A `next_deadline() -> Option<Millis>` fits SQL (`MIN(deadline)` over open calls). 2. `Hub::ready` and `Hub::close` call `calls_for`, which answers every open call with its arguments: up to 256 calls of up to 1 MiB each for every `ready`, which a route calls on every wake of every open serve. `ready` needs only IDs, `sent` and `accepted`; a summary method keeps the arguments in the store until `close`. A serve answer can also be 64 deliveries of 1 MiB: a byte bound beside `deliveries_per_serve` keeps it under what a host and the route take. 3. A `serve` evicted by the fifth open (`serves_per_registration`) is only dropped from `Reg::holds`: `Hub::ready` still answers false for it and `Hub::close` answers it as any other, so the route holds it its whole wait and cannot tell it was evicted (scratch test: five opens, `held().serves == 4`, the first still not ready, and a new call then delivered by all five). Either `ready` answers true for a serve not in `holds`, or the eviction rule goes. 4. The wake hook is called under whatever lock the route holds the hub with; the route must take its `Notified` before it checks `ready`, or a call enqueued between `open` and the wait is missed until the wait ends.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":352},"end":{"line":488},"content_hash":"21c06a20e5c4bf826b525237e4ccc34a6c8327d6a0388be72007feb382286a2c"},"summary":"What the store and routes will need of `Pending` and `Hub` that is not here: the next deadline, a due list without arguments, and word of an evicted serve","supersedes":"faeb89d8d18ad44b1f01a17f7b2907cb2f5ad2b8399c02b657dc2f9e17daf421","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:00.172479Z","id":"3794ec355e3e967b92cb0f1fe4cc24f14f97a5c47ee6363e1b2984934c7d94f0","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"Registration and authority\": 'It lasts while its registrant is connected, and a short while after') names no figure. `HubLimits::lease_ms` defaults to 300,000. Dropping a registration frees its definition and makes `serve` answer `unknown_registration`; its calls and ended runs stay in the store until their own deadlines, and a call with no deadline (a policy that sets none) for a host that never comes back stays for ever: nothing here ends it. Whether a registration's end should settle its calls (`unavailable`, or `unknown_outcome` for one that was sent) is the design's sentence 'with no one to answer, their calls to it are `unavailable`' read strictly; it is not done, because a host that goes on as its registration answers them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":590},"end":{"line":592},"content_hash":"abb93e40561b986aa06f1fa69008d2753aadd2299090dadadb4c986375ef1dd5"},"summary":"A registration is kept five minutes after it stops being connected; the design says 'a short while': for the owner to set","supersedes":"ecc38e4a8d75e51385294deb1731e0cf4be2704ae1813d7d8e556bfa3926e9a7","tags":["status:needs-decision","claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:00.344685Z","id":"d6c68c3dd61c6903047f23bed49ecb8c415c8c31a4b3361a35bb299496b70b2d","body":{"detail":"`withheld` looks at each operation's parameters and result. A kind's request, message and result are not looked at: the design's own example (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces/shell.d.ts`, `Triage.clip?: Image`) has a kind that can be started with an image. Until content is carried, whatever starts a run of a host's kind has to refuse a request that holds a `Content`, or the kind; `Registered::withheld` has no way to say a kind is withheld (its entries are `{op, reason}`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1499},"end":{"line":1502},"content_hash":"d3d70405f90b32383a8b132f51a343354d038b13ab5ca222c63559114d8cf526"},"summary":"Only operations are withheld for naming `Content` or `Image`; a kind whose request or result names one is not","supersedes":"a04733e7274baa58fbdf8979653fd7d3504c94a136e96dd1669ee46d2fb9f367","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:00.513451Z","id":"c95a5407170f6e9836ae4b946be6a2e409797353e16d89fb6548295c0f2f3ec9","body":{"detail":"`Hub::register` refuses a named ID only when the hub holds it under another name (`RegisterError::UnknownRegistration`). Once the hub does not hold it (its lease ended, or the daemon restarted), any ID of `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,256}` is made a registration of whatever name the request is for. Shown with a scratch test against this module: register `desk` as `r1`, enqueue `desk.lamps`, let the lease end, register `shelf` with `registration: \"r1\"`: Ok; the next `serve` of `shelf` is sent the call with `op: \"desk.lamps\"` (the prefix is not stripped, `Hub::due`), and its answer settles it `invalid_result` (\"not an operation of the definition its host now serves\"). Registering `desk` with `registration: \"my-constant-id\"`, an ID nothing issued, is Ok too. So a host written against docs/contract.md (\"Going on as a registration\") may invent a constant ID, two hosts may share one, and a call can reach another surface's host. Narrowing this after hosts exist breaks them. The fix needs the store to keep each issued ID with its surface name (the `Pending` trait has no registrations today): resume only an ID the daemon issued, for the same name, else `unknown_registration`; draw IDs with 128 bits of randomness. Related: 4e794bfd23 (the owner's decision on resuming at all).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1826},"end":{"line":1841},"content_hash":"56caa0b4da8ffb1301589dad339b8961507c07839512b8e393880a1cb55c4864"},"summary":"Going on as a registration takes any well-formed ID, one no daemon issued and one of another surface once the hub has dropped it","supersedes":"09f8ab5bce8fa4bd01d6e5b474fd9f0d4c5fa904632874d1f39b70ebf3b993ea","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","protocol","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:00.683213Z","id":"0ee4780e7595b05f6ede50931fe2409b74a2babe4d206bdde57bce968b528860","body":{"detail":"A registration is dropped only `connected_ms + lease_ms` (315 s) after it was last served, and `register` refuses a new one while `registrations_per_name` (8) or `registrations` (32) are held. Scratch test against this module: eight registrations of `desk` at 0 ms that never serve; a ninth is refused `TooManyRegistrations` at 10 ms, 60 s, 200 s and 315,000 ms, and taken at 315,008 ms. A host that crash-loops and registers anew on each start (docs/contract.md tells a host given `unknown_registration` to register again, and says nothing of keeping its ID), or any other holder of the daemon token, does this. Suggested: when a limit is reached, drop the oldest registration of the name (or of all) that is not connected, instead of refusing; its calls and ends stay in the store, and its host can still go on as it by ID.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1866},"end":{"line":1896},"content_hash":"1445ac07abcb4ec3dcd2e4a8a733e3b060d62ccae568944c75400fb79b0c91c8"},"summary":"Eight registrations that are never served lock the name out for five minutes and fifteen seconds","supersedes":"e1d0ecc6ffb13f4f8ebab145380a7a9e1e8a1feb4796e9b2600df337a56592d7","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:00.853470Z","id":"c803bc7a056535058d40a39581d6b0ff101ecd4b53d9ecd5400729aa93451cab","body":{"detail":"`Hub::advance` drops a hold whose `until` is before now, and `Hub::close` then answers nothing. With a clock that runs, a serve that waited its whole wait is closed a moment after `until`, and a serve that waits for nothing is opened and closed at two readings of the clock: neither is sent the calls that are due and not new (sent, not accepted), which docs/contract.md (\"Serving\") says go out with every serve. Under a clock a test stops this never shows. The routes work around it (crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs `Surfaces::turn`): open, ready and close run under one hold of the broker at one instant, and a serve asked after its wait is over is answered with `Hub::serve` at that instant. Test: api_bot_surfaces.rs a_new_daemon_on_the_home_goes_on_as_a_registration_with_its_calls (a daemon on the system's clock, `wait_ms` 0 and 1). The rule may be better in the hub: close could answer what is due for a serve that lapsed by time alone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1601},"end":{"line":1617},"content_hash":"e448fb356f521c22f43c8436b84c46bf35fff55203e4ba00f05556c13faa7873"},"summary":"A serve's hold lapses at the first instant after its wait, so on a running clock a close after the wait answers nothing: a held serve never redelivers","supersedes":"5c9cff6fc8fa59a3e1a4285b5c55307d3535300ab2f4f140d9a6e3ebc3e21aa4","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:01.032681Z","id":"70eac1454a970712917d82d4a4107ad66cf11960579bf15d86cb5237a74031b5","body":{"detail":"`reg.later` holds each `retry_after_ms` instant until a serve is answered with the call (or the call is settled), so once it is due and its host does not serve, `next_wake` answers that past instant every time, and `Hub::expire` changes nothing about it. A timer that sleeps until `next_wake` would spin. The daemon's timer (crates/clax-http/src/bot_surfaces.rs `run_timer`) waits `STALE_WAKE` (1 s) when a pass left the next wake in the past. `next_wake` could leave out the instants that have come.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1701},"end":{"line":1749},"content_hash":"b7606c14df2e396c48e5d17039e55a6f66bdc3e2dce60da6fc1c426981618999"},"summary":"`Hub::next_wake` keeps answering an instant in the past while a call its host asked for later is due and no serve takes it","supersedes":"98411a8d4a711ee165663a7a54fefbee25ad83c97bc7f4545ffbb76e132e892c","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:01.203639Z","id":"8f4f40f6ce6af13935d905c3f95c0d51ae4057b84c53bac71e9bf6d5a6729a66","body":{"detail":"`Hub::open` records each ID it took in `taken` and ignores any later answer naming it, `accepted` and `retry_after_ms` included. A host that says `accepted` when a call arrives and has its result by the next serve sends `[{id, accepted: true}, {id, result}]`: scratch test against this module, the call stays open and accepted, so it is not sent again, and the run is thrown `unknown_outcome` at its deadline (or waits for ever with none) though its host answered. docs/contract.md says \"a second answer for one ID\" is ignored, which a host reads as a second *result*. Suggested rule: within one serve `accepted` and `retry_after_ms` never block a later `result` or `error` for the same ID; only a second settling answer is ignored.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2137},"end":{"line":2164},"content_hash":"fa297fd24be091bf1638c7c2641ca2661d04481ea5c08e73ff2a450d4255a01c"},"summary":"`accepted` and then a result for one call in one serve: the result is ignored and the call is never sent again","supersedes":"f85124ccd6e57799ca4d31f7fa75b494220b6a8f12ba05aac3319ca8c756d054","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","protocol","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:01.373515Z","id":"a4143d009767574e43e729166071f8b624ab16668fcff74c90e4c7795cfc579a","body":{"detail":"A run keeps its definition by hash (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"A run\"), and `PendingCall` carries no hash. When a host goes on as its registration with a changed definition (`Hub::register`), an answer to a call made before the change is checked by the new one: an operation that is gone settles as `invalid_result`, and a result type that changed is checked as changed (tests: `a_registration_gone_on_with_keeps_its_serves_and_checks_by_its_new_definition`). Checking by the run's own definition needs the definitions kept by hash (the store) and the hash on the call; `clax bot surface diff` is the design's answer to whether the new host can serve the old runs at all.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1298},"end":{"line":1334},"content_hash":"3f4f2f06d36326c61b1af7fd4fce4766fc16125a8d299f8e31e6014f256cfc0e"},"summary":"An answer is checked against the definition its registration has when the answer arrives, not the one the run was made with","supersedes":"ea0562956be8128a8fa5dbc80b1bddf24738af32bbc0bfc01d68bc770163fa18","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:01.543106Z","id":"6f50e4336ebbd37aeddd09718e9da59259acdf74298dee5f1ef45fc82c7707fc","body":{"detail":"`Hub::answer` passes on an error whose code is lowercase letters, digits and `_` and no longer than the definition's `name_bytes`, and turns any other into `invalid_result`. It does not look at `Op::throws`. So a host can throw `unavailable`, `expired`, `unknown_outcome` or `invalid_result`, which the script cannot tell from Clax's own, and a code its definition (and so the model's declaration) never named. Holding errors to `throws` would refuse hosts that have a general failure to report unless the format gains codes every operation may throw.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1335},"end":{"line":1349},"content_hash":"119d0f6e1b4e1a7d61aea3148809768d9f59c1acb26ca1e8385e649eed4db264"},"summary":"A host's error is thrown to the script under any well-formed code, not only the codes the operation declares with `@throws`: for the owner to decide","supersedes":"21d343fac2be4321c1397e2c94c8d3e82352ae260a4c679d844f5a505e133e8d","tags":["status:needs-decision","claxbot-surfaces","protocol","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:01.710743Z","id":"473f47590c76d206dd1fe06203404ccc30ae2a5619e1b1253023aeff80702959","body":{"detail":"The list is held to `Limits::list` patterns and each to `Pattern::parse` (crates/clax-surface/src/allow.rs:58), which bounds no name's length, where a kind's `@allow` is held to `Limits::name_bytes` (`Pattern::names`, allow.rs:91). Probe, through a `TestServer`: `PUT /api/bot/surfaces/desk` with `settings: {allow: [\"desk.\" + 7 MiB of `a`]}` answers 200 in 295 ms, and the registration's row holds `settings_json` of 7,340,051 bytes. `HubLimits::registrations` says a registration keeps megabytes at the most; with this it keeps up to the 8 MiB of a request, 256 MiB for 32 registrations, and each new registration leaves that much in `bot_registrations` for good. `allow` of a start (crates/clax-server/src/routes/bot_surfaces.rs:195) is read by the same rule, and is not kept.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1128},"end":{"line":1165},"content_hash":"46281a9858d27e171e6dd629e6e757bcbbcd2992f0f3959565c6888a838f2c0b"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse (`invalid_settings`, `invalid_request` for a start) a pattern with a name longer than `Limits::name_bytes` or with more names than `Limits::depth` + 2, as `Pattern::names` already measures.","summary":"A pattern of `settings.allow` has no longest length: one of 7 MiB is accepted, held with the registration and written to its row","supersedes":"492be5da7773eaca37491c3ebaf783213240e80be4a07bbe2886f93a399bfafe","tags":["security","review","dos","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:01.890065Z","id":"23a0f2c4db36febaba808091017e2b3ceff2de32f32468d6774a60d315356c4e","body":{"detail":"`bot.surface.registered` is written by `Store::issue_registration` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_surfaces.rs:221), which `Hub::register` calls only for a request that names no registration. A request that names one changes the words sent to every later turn of that registration's runs and records nothing. Probe, through a `TestServer`: 200 requests that go on as one registration of `bench`, each with another definition and another prompt file, add no audit event; afterwards the hub's prompt is the last one sent, and the row's `def_hash` is the first definition's and its `prompt` is NULL (`StoredPending::issue_registration`, crates/clax-bot/src/broker/stored.rs, writes them once). A run's `bot.run_start` record names `started_by` and its bindings keep the definition's hash, but the prompt file a run was sent is kept nowhere (record 2a87725b: the pieces are computed and not stored). So what a host put into the prompts of runs that act as the owner cannot be read back after the fact. The audit actor of both records is what the request's own headers say (`DeferredAudit`, crates/clax-http/src/audit.rs:185: the owner by hand, or the session `X-Clax-Session` names), so two hosts with the token are not told apart by it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1897},"end":{"line":1931},"content_hash":"9c1568bc465dab4c95175ef084508efa7a5fa3c2d359e0d457c3603b6cd27501"},"suggested_fix":"When a registration is gone on with and its definition's hash, its prompt file or its settings differ, update the row and record `bot.surface.registered` with the registration, the new `def_hash` and the SHA-256 of the prompt file; record that hash with the first registration too.","summary":"Going on as a registration replaces its definition, prompt file and settings with no audit record, and its row keeps the first ones","supersedes":"9aa839eeb20a81b8428783a478fa02ac97de99d40150bd0c4837cce1f6a22254","tags":["security","review","audit","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:02.070237Z","id":"4f03ba1d63efcb74962187a966065bac193b63be7a8b7ff8f23d46a6b41c3c76","body":{"detail":"`MemoryPending::open_call` compares arguments only while the call is open (`outcome.is_some() || held.args == call.args`), because settling empties `args`; the `Pending::open_call` and `OpenedCall::Same` docs hold the store lane to that. Scratch test against this module: `desk.notify(\"one\")` under key `k`, answered, then `desk.notify(\"two\")` under `k`: `Ok(\"c1\")`, the first call's outcome. docs/contract.md (\"Keys\") says another call under the key is refused, `key_reused`. Under a replayed step the arguments are the same, so the difference shows only for a script that is not deterministic, which is when `key_reused` matters. A store can keep a digest of the arguments when it drops them. Also `Hub::enqueue`'s full-queue path finds only open calls, so a settled call's key answers `QueueFull` there.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2827},"end":{"line":2845},"content_hash":"8f1078c09f91eae21a68e80e085e836d7837ed18ae0202ee5f79478772fc0c5f"},"summary":"A settled call's key with other arguments answers the settled call, where the contract says `key_reused`","supersedes":"5b68b239cbeac170ed9294695ff4a42c9caf58bba09aace07113a73eb66695ed","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:02.263002Z","id":"efb724a544c59ca28f77c24f8b95222116849a9ae7ec89130852e5a3acbd546f","body":{"detail":"`SkipClock` is for tests: a wait moves it. The daemon needs a `BrokerClock` whose `now` is the store's clock in milliseconds and whose `until` sleeps on `clax_rt::time` (it may return early; `HubBroker::outcome` reads the clock again). The routes of the host protocol (`PUT /api/bot/surfaces/<name>`, `…/serve`) call `Hub::register`, `Hub::open`, `Hub::ready` and `Hub::close` inside `HubBroker::with`, which holds the broker's lock for the closure and then wakes whoever waits in `CallBroker::outcome`, so an answer a `serve` brought reaches its run without a timer (`broker::tests::an_answer_reaches_whoever_waits_for_it_without_the_clock_moving`). The hub's `Wake` hook is called under that lock and must not call the broker. Nothing but `outcome` calls `Hub::expire`: with no run waiting, calls past their deadline are settled only when a `serve` opens, so the daemon needs a pass at `Hub::next_wake`, which is also what journals a parked run's deadline. Wiring: `deps.providers.set_resolver(Arc::new(outside::Registrations::new(broker, deps.policy.clone())))`, with the same policy the deps hold, since `RunPolicy::delivery` is asked through it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":215},"end":{"line":222},"content_hash":"7fa8a5482deeb7ea4e5d0e666ee4f04f83bd7f516ea17e1cdf25b4f3ceb7e2cf"},"summary":"No `BrokerClock` for a daemon is written, and the routes reach the hub only through `HubBroker::with`","supersedes":"a1682df3764a342f2059b278805cc7a58e51ba4a54452a225ae001a4df25c4d0","tags":["for-3a-17","for-3a-20","for-3a-21","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:02.437518Z","id":"dbf51165f0f16a31432e3aa91b030785a251e4759ad792e10bee929cbeb489d7","body":{"detail":"`HubBroker<S>` (crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs) is the one broker; the daemon gives it a store that outlasts the process in place of `MemoryPending`. That store implements `bot_surfaces::Pending` and `BrokerStore`. (1) `open_placed` is the only way the broker opens a call: it is given `Placed` (the claim's driver, the run, the `CallPlace` with step, attempt, seq and key, the arguments as the script passed them and their `host::args_hash`, and whether the operation waits) beside the hub's `NewCall`, and writes the pending row under that driver's claim before the hub wakes the host; a claim that is lost is an error, which the script is thrown as `unavailable` (retryable) and the core fails to journal. `Pending::open_call` is never called through the broker (`broker::tests`' store panics in it). (2) `settled` answers a call's outcome, read and left in place, for as long as the call's step is open: a store that journals answers at the call's place answers what that row holds, in the form `surface::recorded` reads. The outside provider then answers `Answer::of`, and the core's write keeps the row that is there. (3) A call opened again with its key for the same registration, run, operation and arguments answers `OpenedCall::Same` with the first ID, settled or not: a step run again after a crash relies on it not to reach its host twice (`outside::a_hosts_calls_are_not_sent_again_when_a_crashed_step_is_run_again` holds it over `MemoryPending`). (4) `record_end` for a run whose end the registration has acknowledged should change nothing; `MemoryPending` forgets an acknowledged end and would record it again. (5) `Hub::enqueue_under` (crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs) reads a definition by hash through `Pending::definition_text` when the registration has registered another since, so definitions are kept for as long as a run bound with them is live. (6) The hub's instants are `Millis` since the epoch and the store's are timestamps: the broker's `BrokerClock` and the store agree on one clock.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":262},"end":{"line":299},"content_hash":"eb1c52c9b726b4fe70a4c1bd1fada77db643165875d9599f99fa912f86b5c17c"},"summary":"What a store's implementation of `BrokerStore` must hold to for the daemon's broker","supersedes":"4823fe079a8ec0cf52fca4582fa443e2576bdaeeeb2f3e6119156d05283c262b","tags":["for-3a-17","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:02.618906Z","id":"f2588c938eb8da4a14565057e5073ad35c9fd89cb6fb6f068e9b3e852eb90efd","body":{"detail":"`HubBroker::with` ends with `changed.notify_waiters()` (line 466), whoever called it and whatever it did: a `serve` that brought nothing, `Surfaces::limits`, `find`, the listing. Each `outcome` that waits then takes the lock and calls `Hub::expire`, which is `Store::expire_calls` and `Store::expire_ends`, two write transactions, on the run loop's async task and not on a store thread. So a host that polls with `wait_ms: 0` in a loop makes every waiting run of every host do store writes at the rate of its polling; the work for each wake is bounded, the rate is the host's to choose.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":564},"end":{"line":601},"content_hash":"e33aea21ddf76adc6bc65e87f06845c33e621a5518bc26206a0f7d36e075b868"},"suggested_fix":"Notify `changed` only from what can settle a call (an answer taken, `Hub::expire` settling one), and let `outcome` read the call's outcome without settling deadlines itself, since the timer and every `open` already do.","summary":"Every use of the hub wakes every run that waits for an answer, and each then settles deadlines in a write transaction under the hub's lock, on its async task","supersedes":"ea6a7c1ff18d01299821444c104d345290c6a5bf02fbd1c3ad05e463a7d20b1d","tags":["review","dos","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:03.152482Z","id":"724e738467d80a61ffe9de655e5dcfbccd01f0768d1896b3cf387c8dc8715467","body":{"detail":"`finish`'s description is `Ends the run: the work is done.` followed by the doc comment of the kind's result interface, and its input schema is `Kind::result_schema`, which writes a field's doc comment as its `description` (crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs, `Finish::tool`; crates/clax-surface/src/manifest.rs). So editing either changes every request a thread or brief run sends, which the `request.*.json` and `request.*.wire.json` goldens of crates/clax-bot/tests/golden pin byte for byte.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":226},"end":{"line":229},"content_hash":"c4dd781a32f5bafd2b7904b3d502e1f4a70a91737ad658b3c3a44f700817f3c2"},"summary":"`Done`'s doc comment ends the description of `finish` for Clax's kinds, and a doc comment on `summary` would be a `description` in the tool's schema","supersedes":"289b758be918c16d548167502c7fb7ddebe0ae7295e4af9c5f76268f0fca7637","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:03.843440Z","id":"c016c62b8399ca96babe5d1bb2865743b9798b1ea6c590e4f8d8aba2758ee9a6","body":{"detail":"`Driver::start` calls `Store::sweep_zero_version_artifacts` before it tells the providers (`SurfaceProvider::driver_started`). It names no `BotHost` method and no module of `clax`'s provider, so the test of the run loop's source passes; the noun is still Clax's. It can move into `ClaxProvider::driver_started` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs), which would then need the lease the sweep is given (`RunSettings::lease_secs`), or stay with what builds the daemon's driver. Left because the order of the sweep and the repeats of `ended` is today's and nothing asked for the move.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":249},"end":{"line":249},"content_hash":"33919012cb238e6d46f7612fe3c0a5a80217a4b5aee383dcedfd245a9c316151"},"summary":"A starting driver still sweeps artifacts that have no version, which is `clax`'s housekeeping, by a call of the store","supersedes":"8f1682a4329e40976d0422df5cc4a8ebedebf64da2177b47ea49e12ce8ceac08","tags":["t8","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.011570Z","id":"3d0d03b0cd19812e4e95200d454bda4214d2c2994a39772ba93760f6d7c859cb","body":{"detail":"Failure: run R ticks; its script parks (`Store::park_run` commits, R is `parked`); before `Driver::settle` runs for that tick, a `serve` journals the answer (`Store::answer_call`, R becomes `queued`) and calls `Driver::wake(R)`, or a person cancels (`Store::cancel_run`, R becomes `stopping`) and `Driver::cancel(R)` runs. The same holds during a tick that found R parked and not late (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1126-1136). `Driver::wake` queues nothing for a run in `active` (driver.rs:137-145), and the `Wait::Parked` arm here reads R, finds it not `parked`, arms nothing and returns. R is `queued` (or `stopping`) in the store and in no queue, delayed list or timer; `spawn_forever` sleeps until another wake. Only `Driver::resume` (a restart) finds it. Shown by a scratch test (a clock that, read while R is parked, answers and wakes, or cancels): after three `run_until_idle` calls R is `Queued` (answer) or `Stopping` (cancel), ticks [Runnable, Waiting(Parked)], `next_wake` None. Fix: in this arm, when the run read is live and not `parked`, push it on the queue (with that change the same scratch tests finish and stop, and every `park::` test passes); or have `wake` mark an active run to be queued again when it settles.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":396},"end":{"line":426},"content_hash":"2818a5022ce5906c0b9c984d5afee1dc03b18b8a365d36e8ce79932ce7ee8979"},"suggested_fix":"let row = self.deps.store.run(id).ok().flatten(); if row.as_ref().is_some_and(|r| r.status != RunStatus::Parked && !r.status.is_terminal()) { st.queue.push_back(id.to_string()); return; }","summary":"An answer or a cancel that lands while the driver is ticking a parked run is lost: the run stays queued or stopping with no driver entry until the process restarts","supersedes":"572edc0ece287179a28505d0dd6451c2fb8602e27d59c12b12f3b0c1b144952a","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.181894Z","id":"3a40d0c3a899663f199a374a51a8b17924d6dadbfec9b6589aff7b8bb1d8a950","body":{"detail":"`spawn_forever` passes `Duration::from_secs(3600)` and `run_until_idle` hands what is left of it to each tick as `TickOpts.budget` (driver.rs:413), which `Tick::available` takes the least of with the run's time. So with `max_run_minutes = None` and `model_call_timeout = None` a call is still cut after at most an hour (less for a tick started late in the round), then tried again from its start. It bounds a call, not a run: the run goes on in the next round. It is the driver's constant, as the waits at driver.rs:39-42 are, and the Worker's is its alarm (`alarm_budget`, crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs:91). Named here because `RunLimits::model_call_timeout`'s `None` reads as 'the client's own deadlines only' (policy.rs:104-107).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":552},"end":{"line":556},"content_hash":"5cc282f5bfb9a578b981bac0aa1300504fafbb11d61d7297203cc29b9b7d4fdc"},"summary":"The daemon's driver gives every tick at most an hour (`run_until_idle(3600 s)`), which bounds one model call whatever the policy answers","supersedes":"971dba6b4669f95d3bf8abb8dbc247b0208d8f3275d59a75271345d77b7027db","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.353177Z","id":"9a975a5887c30c7d9a287ea865a4c1f7222c005ecd4cd325f30a9110a796f72b","body":{"detail":"Deleted in 1e9aefb5b test(claxbot): a run's endings are held to their fixtures, and the endings they replaced go. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"cc3b395f99175b2b9f8a02d2e7d2cd4412f2c4ab540afbbff4609e325cc5ec75","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: The two cfg(test) reference branches of Tick::working and Tick::end that this record pins were deleted in 1e9aefb5b (with crates/clax-bot/src/ending_reference.rs and ending_differential.rs), and Deps.host in 359f0c1f4; Tick::working (ending.rs 147-152) and Tick::end (160-281) remain without them","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.353673Z","id":"1f9be301c72e00e926ce115ce71bafe25af2e91eed229cf73c2778c09aead1a4","body":{"detail":"Each was made alone in this file and `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all` passed: (1) asking the bindings' `ending` in reverse order (line 186, `surfaces.iter().rev()`), against the documented kind's-surface-first order; (2) not counting a cancelled script's session clash with no `ending` failure (line 230 without `| (None, Some(true))`); (3) a wrong end rebuilt for redelivery (`stored_end`, line 303: a finished run's summary taken from `reason`, a stopped run rebuilt as `End::Failed`); (4) `stored_end` answering a stop for a run that has not ended, so `redeliver` (line 334) would tell `ended` of a running run named by `awaiting_ended`. None reaches a person today: a Clax run has one binding, `ClaxProvider::run_ended` ignores the end and result, and `ClaxProvider::awaiting` names only a terminal last run of a thread with no queued, running or stopping run (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs `active_on_in`). They become observable with the first host's provider.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":177},"end":{"line":377},"content_hash":"470e6a689ec290ca2bf9b0b27aebd16a13ec00dadc617003fa9cd8ccc086d280"},"suggested_fix":"In crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs: two fake bindings whose `ending` hooks are recorded in order; a cancelled script whose sessions clash, pinning the step's `failures`; a fake that awaits each kind of end, pinning the `End` and result it is told on a driver's start; a fake that names a running run, pinning that it is not told.","summary":"Four rules of the run loop's end that no test holds, each observable only once a run has a second provider","supersedes":"f9ee04c5450ddb7e21262ab87631e9f6500700204f7aa328eb35e525b358404c","tags":["review","test-gap","for:T8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.532Z","id":"dd1855db226c8157940973002932bbcd66414e6e3b92f6acb5fdd8d30205a47d","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::begin`'s failure fails the tick and is not counted against the step (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs, `Tick::script`; a draft that cannot be loaded always held its step open), while an `ending`'s is counted here and ends the run `failed` at `max_commit_failures`. So a store that cannot be read holds a script step open for as long as the run's caps allow and ends a `finish` after three tries. Counting `begin` too would change what a run whose draft cannot be loaded does today (it would end `failed`, with a notice); leaving `ending` uncounted would let a provider hold a run open. Which of the two a `begin` should be is the owner's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":216},"end":{"line":226},"content_hash":"dfaeeb3baf2998756f1eda1a55f7d3d23bc098ce2e6853efb146a71eb3310c43"},"summary":"A `begin` that fails is retried without a count and an `ending` that fails is counted: left to differ, for the owner","supersedes":"0424b00610e3ea6f9616f3fa4e141242f20e214ed9c661ba9d6cd5c1b24dcc60","tags":["t7","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.704334Z","id":"1e4e1c2de27bdb836d919c74f23cd6f71af61961c1b0a3952b376458624398e9","body":{"detail":"A column on `bot_runs` set once each provider's `ended` answered `Ok`, and a starting driver telling the providers of every ended run without it. It would redeliver `ended` for any provider, whatever it remembers, and would turn `clax`'s roster off after a crash for a run with no follow-up queued. Not taken: it is a migration and a write after every run's end, and `clax` needs neither: the follow-ups are its own durable record of an end not acted on (`ClaxProvider::awaiting`, crates/clax-bot/src/clax/ending.rs), and a roster record lapses by itself 120 seconds after its last renewal and is kept in memory only (crates/clax-core/src/working.rs 3-12). As built the provider says which runs it awaits (`SurfaceProvider::awaiting_ended`), and a provider that says none is told once, by the tick that committed, or not at all when that tick died.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":344},"end":{"line":344},"content_hash":"6e6ef426cd8a8fb1603bc59edfc585dfc3223b87412a8cc8e4c4701dd4a9ffd7"},"summary":"The run loop remembering, in the run's row, that every provider was told `ended`","supersedes":"fd33196c29123b7541fb2354d59796723b857ac70a3f8e0598df6c9d37283160","tags":["t7","revisit:a provider that must act on every run's end and keeps no record of its own of the runs it has not acted on","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending_scenarios.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.893109Z","id":"9fad3a35709d5c10c998ccad5fe7f562faf4422f39e5a79535d76165016a7717","body":{"detail":"Thirteen runs, each plainly and with the driver dying at three points, the died ones resumed four ways: 13 × (2 + 3 × 4) = 182 runs. Not among them: a stop after a script whose driver died (held by `golden::endings::a_stop_records_the_calls_of_a_script_that_never_committed_and_not_its_own_notice`, a golden written before the endings moved and unchanged after); a cancelled script's stop (crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs drives it both ways, since one flag, `Deps::reference`, selects both references); credits spent mid-run, a rejected key, no key, no flag, an unpriced model and the time and credit caps (each has an `ending.*.json` and a `net.ending.*.json` golden written on the code before the move); the three endings of a policy that is not the default; and follow-ups started, which the fake host does not do (crates/clax-server/tests/api_claxbot_run.rs holds them, unchanged and passing). The store's reads were not counted by a hook, which the store does not have: the calls that reach the host are compared (`reached_the_host`, `roster`).\n\nRe-recorded unchanged from record 5bd35594 on crates/clax-bot/src/ending_differential.rs, whose subject moved here.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":536},"end":{"line":536},"content_hash":"5583739a3c4362e3af6dc7f73325099dd62f34a645a73cb16df70c2fe4d131f2"},"summary":"What the endings' comparison does not drive","tags":["t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:04.893834Z","id":"a107a2df1158246d8dd5c3baf85bc298883ad2e5276f7cce073ab3aecb3bac9a","body":{"detail":"Live step_done events carry the step's charge from Store::commit_step; the canonical events rebuilt from committed steps read payload.credits_milli, which the payload never has (the charge is computed at commit), so a client that reconnects sees credits only in run_credits_milli. The store could return the step's charge with the steps (a sum of its usage rows) for the replay.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":405},"end":{"line":416},"content_hash":"34ff05f6f3e85c32427c32135770237712eccad0757f384b6ab5900b617140d8"},"summary":"A replayed step_done carries no credits for its step","supersedes":"68302f8af7de5c11a09c77923dd5523239be81f235db5d613a4ab0e7d7a4c3d1","tags":["events","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:05.074354Z","id":"f4c69a61e9f76498743f143c559ca8e9d281b6ca352562d72c0d5484c53e2e2e","body":{"detail":"`Rule::Undeclared` gives `finish` the schema `{\"type\": \"object\"}` and refuses every call with `the run's kind <k> is declared by none of its surfaces` (lines 133-135), counted as a refused result. Nothing a model does can finish such a run, so the turns are spent for nothing and the end's code names the model's result rather than the run's set-up. The run could instead fail at its first step with a code of its own. It is not reachable today: a run's row keeps a `RequestKind` (`thread`, `brief`, both declared in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts), and only `Deps::with_kind` makes another. `Finish::of` also looks for the kind's definition only among the run's bindings; a registry that holds the kind's provider apart from the bindings should give that provider's definition here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":86},"end":{"line":91},"content_hash":"b6b985da17954ab5b0ab7739862de58e3a4685138ef5e0bda89a83ed96ede9e6"},"summary":"A run whose kind none of its surfaces declares is offered a `finish` no value passes, and gives up `invalid_result` only after the model has been refused `max_invalid_results` + 1 times (never, under `RunLimits::unbounded`)","supersedes":"5ddda6960398118315c3715cc7cdae52a9e012188cf75d1d5845b6144eb31317","tags":["t9-review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:05.242503Z","id":"24681cc5aa28366cbb925ac0389195d2fb27adf695e1b0b53626e74e1717849a","body":{"detail":"In a row, a model that puts any other step between two refused results (a script, a turn the nudge answers, a call of another tool) starts the count again, so `RunLimits::max_invalid_results` never gives it up; the run's own caps (`max_steps`, credits, minutes) still end it. A count over the run would fold every turn's `next` in `Control::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs), which other changes are editing; the count in `next` needs nothing folded and is held by tests/finish.rs `a_step_that_is_not_a_refused_finish_ends_the_row`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":94},"end":{"line":94},"content_hash":"bf638ed8ee5e2858acb5cde68136af9bfa40d39bdc57bdeec8e26384cc384a4e"},"summary":"Count the results a run's kind refused over the whole run, rather than in a row in the turn's `next` (`invalid_results`)","supersedes":"22692226f2cb450092ecff698fa8566f4b696215d09cc2037194e3f0b97c2ef4","tags":["revisit:a run of a host's kind is seen alternating refused results with other steps until a cap ends it","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:05.418404Z","id":"213d6d01940b49e38e5fbc07dae21d859f767821307e080fb491154ea18134f6","body":{"detail":"`Done` is `{summary: string}` (crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts) and `check_result` refuses a field the type does not list, so `{\"summary\": \"done\", \"extra\": 1}` would be `invalid_result` under the typed rule. Today's runs finish with it, and an input without a string `summary` is answered \"The input of finish does not match its schema; nothing ran.\" with no count (tests/golden/answer.tool-input.finish-not-its-schema.json). Both are kept for `clax` so that no clax.dev run changes: tests/finish.rs `a_finish_of_claxs_kind_takes_a_summary_beside_keys_its_type_lacks` and `a_finish_of_claxs_kind_without_a_string_summary_is_answered_without_a_bound` hold them. Moving `clax` onto the typed rule changes what a model is told and adds a bound; the owner's call.","kind":"waiver","span":{"start":{"line":128},"end":{"line":132},"content_hash":"8780b7a116b71f9208a6b9f43658a1fb0a91fe3881eb5c35751191e68409986f"},"summary":"A `finish` of Clax's own kinds is read as before kinds had types: keys beside `summary` are ignored, which `Kind::check_result` would refuse","supersedes":"01eee5be4354cbc8e54417d1bd1cffc96518446d26b7b342261c36f7aec3afff","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/finish.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:05.592929Z","id":"e7cd5ec3a7ec301f69470678aa592ef17c2ffb92498ee8038314eaf03057030e","body":{"detail":"`Kind::check_result` checks the value as a script holds it (crates/clax-surface/src/value.rs:348, `check_held`): a whole handle `{id, media_type, bytes, sha256, ...}` is checked for its fields and types, not that the ID names stored bytes this run may read or that `sha256`/`bytes`/`media_type` are theirs. A script's value has handles Clax made, but `finish`'s input is the model's text, so a result's handle can name any ID with any claimed digest, and `ending`/`ended` of the kind's surface receive it as checked. No kind declares `Content` in its result yet (crates/clax-bot/src/fake.d.ts, the shell file in crates/clax-surface/src/tests). Before one does: resolve each handle in a finished result against the store (and the run's reach), or refuse `Content` and `Image` in a kind's result.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":133},"end":{"line":134},"content_hash":"53dedd915abf16fed3dbdcb153b75488708d3859f73b80643384868d381c6b2f"},"summary":"A kind's result is checked for shape only: a `Content` or `Image` in a model's `finish` is whatever handle the model wrote, which the kind's surface is then given as the run's result","supersedes":"2c66963724b4bd96162219e7ab9232969ed16ee30f818c96db4c3e6c5e72d0a0","tags":["t9-review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/golden.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:05.762260Z","id":"310c7eb834dc09981a4ad9aedc1c7da7f95893d326d926026c949de82b34059b","body":{"detail":"Not taken so that the variable means one thing in the repository: the three other places that read it also test only that it is set (crates/clax-core/src/icon.rs:838, crates/clax-server/src/routes/guards.rs:2307, crates/clax-store/src/toolpath/tests.rs:176). Changing this helper alone would make CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=0 rewrite some goldens and compare others.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":173},"end":{"line":173},"content_hash":"1f927b95e7a46d88ea74a8245c8ddebba2a8e6d29f605b061f92ebc98f6f6122"},"summary":"Only CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 (or a truthy value) turns rewriting on; today any value does, 0 included","supersedes":"d34643f66ce0337a9d109d8fde6fa71105962eb5d9ea1d59247a2047eab390e9","tags":["revisit:the other readers of CLAX_UPDATE_GOLDEN are changed to test its value","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:05.935672Z","id":"e30a99adf790d5dbd8ffe917ff2ee00b42ad7a9fac8d3de4bc741d1c4cfce2bb","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface` (203-260) splits this file at its test module and fails on a noun of Clax's surface in any line of the code or its documentation (artifact, thread, draft, version, reply, publish, created, changed, base, tally and their forms, as whole words), on `crate::clax` or `crate::run` in a path, and on any use of `clax_store` but `bot_runs::{Step, StepKind}`. It is a word list: a noun it does not list (`comment`, `file`) or a payload key read through a variable passes, and it holds one file, not `Control`'s readers. A crate for the core's folds would make the compiler hold the imports, but `Step` itself is `clax_store`'s and its payload is untyped JSON, so no arrangement of crates stops `p[\"base\"]`; and the layering (scripts/check-crate-layers.py) would gain a crate for 190 lines. Two mutations show what the test catches: the word `draft` in a doc comment, and `use crate::clax::Tally as _;`, each fail it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":471},"end":{"line":585},"content_hash":"b8fb8d0fcd18488205265fcd3cf1b62a138ee3dec4d261f9c930171b968625e9"},"summary":"Holding the core's folds free of Clax's nouns by the compiler (a crate of their own, or a module with no access to `clax_store`'s run types) instead of by a test that reads the source","supersedes":"eb8615ea8ddfd0f8cee6eda0a6ce788c37e6cedcf1c69243030eb62844224420","tags":["t3","revisit:the core's run loop moves to a crate of its own, or a step's payload gets a type per surface","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.104617Z","id":"7c643c8f90bb2ffdf6b7ad1b93b5b2aecf7678e728d1e34d9e17d7a3741b1878","body":{"detail":"Deleted in 2b4ceaf85 test(claxbot): the folds stand on their own tests, without the single fold they were split from. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"aaf6076f0e3307e3dc0c84347325c483d6540cf9f86659fb0b1ee8ea3b25b85a","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: crates/clax-bot/src/history_reference.rs (the single fold History::of and its three comparisons) was deleted in 2b4ceaf85; the hand-built steps step/open/a_run moved to crates/clax-bot/src/history_fixtures.rs","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.105128Z","id":"7112b2465af4284cd4af98c714ad23c1912b4fed2d6efc7e300c9e5c85b1f8d8","body":{"detail":"A parked run whose host is away long enough for its registration to end (the case parking exists for) is run again (unparked by `Store::unpark_run`, a settled sibling call, or the deadline path); `OutsideSession::call` finds no served registration and answers `Answer::refused(provider.gone())`, which the core journals: `{code: unavailable, message: \"the surface's host is gone: its registration has ended. Nothing happened\"}`, retryable. The pending row says `sent_at` was set an hour before. For a write or effect that is the wrong claim: the hub's own rule (`bot_surfaces::unanswered`) gives `unknown_outcome` for a call that was sent, and `Store::expire_calls` uses it for a replaced registration. A script that trusts `unavailable` repeats an effect that may have happened. Seen with a scratch test on crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs's desk (park, pass 3600 s with no serve, `unpark_run`, drive): journal row 1 as above, pending row (sent_at 10:00, accepted_at None, answered_at 11:00). Fix: when the place has a pending row, answer what `unanswered` gives for it (or leave the call to `expire_calls`) instead of `gone()`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":327},"end":{"line":330},"content_hash":"951772bab59f73f82b3cfc28051212f127783bf0d1a9b44109315b33ed617dfa"},"summary":"A waiting call replayed after its host's registration lapsed is journaled `unavailable ... Nothing happened` although the host was sent it","supersedes":"16e82158a121e2b40c048a4e3f917f1d38f1f8bd9206decd2b16d981d45f2800","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.292744Z","id":"36ed24e3d0ec67109658d01afa087bf7b4db7ad50fb40ff41b8b5ac5a4590288","body":{"detail":"`OutsideProvider::ended` records the end with the broker (`CallBroker::ended`, which is `Hub::ended`), after the terminal commit. The provider lives for one tick and the registry's resolver is not asked when a driver starts, so it has no `awaiting_ended`: once recorded, delivery until acknowledged is the hub's and the store's. But a tick that dies between the terminal commit and this call, or a broker that refuses the end (`EndedError::QueueFull`, `ResultTooLarge`, `Storage`), leaves the host untold, and nothing repeats it. Writing the end in the terminal step's transaction (the store's part, for each binding that names a registration) closes both; the call here then finds the end recorded and changes nothing. Also: the kind's result is given only to the binding of the surface that declared the kind (`ending::result_for`), so a registration bound to a run of another surface's kind is told the end without a result, as the design says.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":455},"end":{"line":471},"content_hash":"6d434ec59d1f2bc08f200d26f74f29798f30de6e684ab6f69f0c02da0093e97f"},"summary":"An outside provider's `ended` is the only time a run's end is recorded for its host: a failure there is logged and never made up for","supersedes":"6cc3f96ffe22ede13258e0d5fcfdc78d8d5ce7cdc4d30b81388f00e296fb9620","tags":["for-3a-16","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.472548Z","id":"d3e72dfef6064aa5d94fb95e37b12201aff518bbddebbcf75344488763adc7d8","body":{"detail":"`OutsideProvider` keeps the definition under the hash in the run's bindings and the prompt of `CallBroker::registration` (crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs:484), which is the registration's current one. `Hub::register` checked that prompt against the definition registered with it, not against an earlier one. So going on as a registration with a new prompt file changes the system prompt of its runs between two turns, by whichever host of the registration registered last; and a new file that names a kind or a `{rules.<namespace>}` the run's definition lacks, or lacks one it has, does not read for the run, whose next turn stops it `request_gone` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1856). Read, not probed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":421},"end":{"line":424},"content_hash":"45603fe4b37f2ba46b625ea9643979c83aa8c13a6f6e5b5d06d4bb7006b4ec97"},"suggested_fix":"Keep a registration's prompt file by hash as its definition is kept, name that hash in the run's binding when the run is made, and send the run that file for all its turns.","summary":"A run's turns are sent the prompt file its registration has now, read against the definition the run was made with","supersedes":"dec4c5473416196de4393e441ea8df9e636272ea204102c69a7a15a53afb17c0","tags":["review","prompt-injection","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.642314Z","id":"25355510740ded1fa3e8fc2f9d89b7622ea9fc884008081aaad66eced4e8372a","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits` is `#[non_exhaustive]` with public fields, so outside clax-bot it cannot be written as a literal or with `..`: the one constructor is `RunLimits::of(&settings)`, then assignments (as `Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs:104-108 does). `of` answers `Some` for the three caps, `model_call_timeout` and `max_overflow_retries`. An environment that wants a run with no bound writes five assignments; one that forgets `model_call_timeout` keeps calls cut at the settings' minutes (retried, see below), and when a sixth bound is added every such policy gets it set without a change to its source. That is the opposite of the module's rule that a cap the environment does not set is not replaced by one of the run loop's (policy.rs:10-13). Neither the struct nor `of` says that `of` is the way to make one. `HistoryAsk` is also `#[non_exhaustive]` and its `new` is `pub(crate)` (policy.rs:232), so a host cannot construct an ask to test its own `history` outside a tick. What `model_call_timeout` does when it is the bound that fires is not written either: the call ends as a cut stream, which is retried in the tick and then waits `Deps.retry_later`; it is never an ending. Measured with `model_call_timeout = Some(0)`: every tick answers `Waiting(Retry(30s))` and the run stays `running`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":162},"end":{"line":278},"content_hash":"e3911b83790799e58c406486d17ce602cfaced3c6a473b56585f2156e7743f49"},"suggested_fix":"Add `RunLimits::unbounded(&RunSettings)` (or `none`): every `Option` `None`, the sizes from the settings and the defaults, documented as what a policy starts from when it means no caps, and say on `of` that it is today's bounds. Make `HistoryAsk::new` public. Say on `model_call_timeout` that a call it cuts is tried again and ends nothing.","summary":"A policy can build its limits only from `RunLimits::of`, which sets every bound: one that means 'no caps' must clear each by name, and a bound added later arrives set","supersedes":"acfd5bd3b9138601c84806505dcb24e3bedb5f6b0fd21d7de75b05f72ed733b6","tags":["review","verify-t4","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.809644Z","id":"a5c5af59884d408a02af31f27e52551d427951a2243009f887937c75341e892a","body":{"detail":"`RunLimits::of` sets `model_call_timeout` to the settings' `max_run_minutes` (30 minutes by default). Under the default policy it never binds, since what is left of the run's time is never more. A policy that sets `max_run_minutes = None` and leaves the timeout gets calls of at most the settings' minutes each, on a native driver with no tick budget; before this, the only bounds on a call were the run's remaining time, the tick's budget, and the client's 120 s for headers and 90 s between pieces (crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:1113-1120), which bound a silence and not a call. The design names 'a floor for the model call's own timeout' as work of its own (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:968). `max_overflow_retries` is `Some(4)`: unreachable under the default policy, and a guess for any other. Question for the owner: what one model call may take where no run cap is set (a setting of its own, or the minutes kept as now), and whether a default bound on overflows in a row is wanted at all beside the rule that each history is shorter than the last.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":246},"end":{"line":278},"content_hash":"00180cec42b5265c1026f0eacb006a8fafde3edd69a669c5f23b8e2ceef8b14d"},"summary":"Two defaults chosen here with nothing behind them: the model call's timeout when a policy sets no minutes, and four overflows in a row","supersedes":"821957d602898bab36a120a00cbcd6a10ccf97b100a8e4b2bb049c74e2d67e6a","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:06.980270Z","id":"f37f5cd7b013409683b6b5976743359a80da4723a91401e3a24ec57eed9b9bdd","body":{"detail":"`check_shown` holds a policy's messages to invariant 2: the first message is the user's and opens with step 1's stored `user` blocks (on a first turn, the request the turn added). A compactor may leave out or summarise every step after it. Not taken: allowing a summary to stand in for the request too. The request is the person's words, shown whole (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, 'One door to the model': an `Instruction` keeps its standing only when shown whole), and with the system text it is the cached prefix. The cost: a request that is itself longer than the model's window, or that leaves no room once the prompt is counted, cannot be shortened by any policy, and the run ends `model_error` at its first turn (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs has no test of that case).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":443},"end":{"line":477},"content_hash":"8565e024b6fc65b60dc5c8da1f41bdfdd4c605af172d6a2c526ec46f34360b77"},"summary":"Letting a policy drop or rewrite the request a run opens with (invariant 2 of `history::transcript` not held for a policy's messages)","supersedes":"4b4d9e8e3c168da5783eb92809605d305f6921aae78c596b25e851d683b67e6c","tags":["revisit:a kind whose requests can be longer than a model's window (an attached document, a long brief)","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:07.150035Z","id":"987bfc74bcf81fabad6d4224fd53f7242a8357881fdbdaedef34d5a3366af625","body":{"detail":"`prompt_too_long` matches four phrases in the message of a 400 (`prompt is too long`, `context_length_exceeded`, `maximum context length`, `context window`). They are from memory of the Messages, Chat Completions and Responses APIs, not from a recorded answer: crates/clax-sample has no typed error for it (`MessagesError::InvalidRequest` carries only the text, crates/clax-sample/src/messages.rs:618). A provider that says it otherwise takes the `model_error` stop, which is the behaviour before the policy, so nothing is lost under the default; a compacting policy would not be asked. A phrase that matched a refusal for another reason would ask the policy for a shorter history once per overflow, bounded as any overflow is. The fix belongs in clax-sample: a typed variant set from the provider's error type, with recorded answers as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":535},"end":{"line":552},"content_hash":"7b19a5d23e166a634ecee10719de53e566567a2829f509b7bc38ca46789a0b5f"},"summary":"A refusal for length is recognised by the provider's words, which no test checks against a provider","supersedes":"1bd4f97aaf24ef73e8ec3c688c7dacce9c5cddd36001f22136298db908ef01ed","tags":["compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:07.323516Z","id":"26ee232577c298b5d54490eed1345c62129140d6bf1ab584aaea3ef868ffc7d0","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:859-861 gives `RunPolicy::settings(&self, run) -> Result<RunSettings>`. Here the tick still reads the instance's settings from `Deps.settings` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1162), because the claim's lease is needed before the run is loaded and the driver reads concurrency with no run in hand (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:214, :392), and then passes them to `RunPolicy::run_settings(run, instance)` and `limits(run, settings)`. That costs no extra read a tick and leaves `Deps.settings` as it was for the server and the Worker. What it gives up: `Deps` carries two things (`settings`, `policy`), and because of the blanket impl at policy.rs:381 a type that is a SettingsSource cannot also implement RunPolicy with answers of its own (the impls would overlap), so an environment that wants both writes two types and passes the second to `Deps::with_policy`.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":616},"end":{"line":672},"content_hash":"8bb72fe535f25a85fbddbdc84eea705baf594d2ffe8868b77e72245b1026a1b5"},"summary":"A policy that is the one source of a run's settings (`settings(run)`, as the design draws it), with the blanket impl bridging a SettingsSource to it","supersedes":"dd0c359d0b211a55b64ecac210110e0e7de4ad569aca0ac22a9e8bf7636a613c","tags":["revisit:the tick holds a RunInfo before it claims, or the server's or the Worker's settings type needs limits of its own","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:07.502281Z","id":"497002f7af9229cc995bb95b039d082642eb5340f04178654d5eee4ddf79bacf","body":{"detail":"`run_settings`, `limits` and `history` take `&clax_store::bot_runs::Run`, the only description of a run the tick has today (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1180-1181, :1934, :1994). The design's policy is told a `RunInfo` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs:386), which names no artifact or thread. When the tick holds one, these parameters change type: a breaking change for implementers, of which there is one today (`Fake` in crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs). The three questions the design adds (`accepts`, `to_model`, `delivery`) are not methods yet; each can be added with a default without changing an implementer.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":616},"end":{"line":654},"content_hash":"69b766ca6f67f4fd34760a82df60a7785c27a2bca68cd11c6ad576187578510b"},"summary":"For the change that gives the tick a `RunInfo`: every `RunPolicy` method takes the store's `Run` row","supersedes":"6e2998d2e665eacc840e1b9e37b34cd1ff53313ebba1d26639b4be1d17747c34","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:07.673081Z","id":"ae35954bea8bd084f48860cd390b3a4b6efa774df552e91ee53c582678f4d180","body":{"detail":"`the_run_loop_reads_no_cap_or_limit_but_the_policys` includes `run.rs` and `script.rs` as text and fails when either names `settings.max_steps`, `settings.max_run_credits`, `settings.max_run_minutes`, `settings.limits()`, the old constants, `at most 64` or `<< 10`, or declares any `const`; and when `run.rs` or `script.rs` no longer reads the `limits.*` fields. A cap read through another binding (`let s = &self.settings; s.max_steps`) passes it, and a file that code moves to is not looked at. When a script's calls move to `clax/*` and the endings to a provider, add those files to the list. `RunLimits::observe_every` has no test of its effect: an attempt's observed entry replaces its last (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1429-1432) and the final figures are recorded whatever the cadence, so only its default and that `run.rs` reads it are held. Keep reading the tool result's size from `Tick.limits.tool_result_bytes` and the sandbox's limits from `Tick.limits.script` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":961},"end":{"line":998},"content_hash":"851c23e014d44b6fb36e7e2942cc0994bd87a492ef2b4b18d0cc04260da6bc7c"},"summary":"The check that the run loop reads no cap of its own is on the text of two files, by name","supersedes":"f63d8cbf0ac8346d4b65a74c7f520ca47325d8dd7cb720503eb25831c0f104bb","tags":["for-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:07.844653Z","id":"701beb9286af115572c23654499efe58f432b4aba01d6bdb746bac0a8c3a2416","body":{"detail":"`Prompt::has_operation_of` tells a namespace's operations by the prefix `<surface>.<path>.` (or `<path>.` for the surface named `clax`); the last `.` is what keeps `web` from matching `webSearch.query`. Writing `format!(\"{name}.{path}\")` for `format!(\"{name}.{path}.\")` at prompt.rs:215 leaves `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test` passing (155 tests, run at git:05f602913): the test surface `SHELL` in prompt.rs has `windows`, `mail$Box` and `quiet`, none a prefix of another, and Clax's own surface has one ruled namespace. With the dot dropped, a run with only `shell.windowsLog.read` would be sent the rules of `windows`, in `{rules}` and in `{rules.windows}`. The same holds for the `clax` arm at prompt.rs:214, which the system goldens hold only because no namespace of crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts begins with `web`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":118},"end":{"line":129},"content_hash":"28d5a8972a6b860f6c76940a88b0c27df03b18d3098c04c8ed15bd23fdca2e4b"},"suggested_fix":"Give `SHELL` a ruled namespace `win` beside `windows` (or `mail` beside `mail$Box`) and assert in `rules_are_the_surfaces_then_those_of_each_namespace_the_run_has` that a run with only `shell.windows.list` has the rules of `windows` and not of `win`.","summary":"No test holds that a namespace's rules stay out of a run that has only operations of a sibling namespace whose name begins with its name","supersedes":"4b9eece38ede23e8d5fdfebf691bfd79a9a637370a8d2ca44761ab65eb9f9760","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:08.016431Z","id":"5e68242bc7589dab591f7a5f03bc23879b200929590623c8f292a2dbe2cceeb2","body":{"detail":"`Prompt::kind` panics with `the surface `<name>` has no kind `<kind>``. Today the only caller is Clax's own (`render_system` and `request_message`, whose kinds are `RequestKind`'s two, both in crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts, as the system goldens hold). The step that renders a prompt for a registered host's run takes the kind's name from stored data, where a panic would end the process's task instead of failing the run.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":292},"end":{"line":302},"content_hash":"ea8205afcab062bbc634658b7c3f3faaf42f6ca34fc24bf0fb4eaabaed640421"},"suggested_fix":"When a host's run is rendered, resolve the kind once where the run is made (a `&Kind` in `Run` in place of its name), so that rendering cannot fail.","summary":"`Prompt::system` and `Prompt::request` panic for a kind the surface does not have, which is right for Clax's two kinds and not for a run of a host's surface","supersedes":"0f7d6f9bc4dae0250bacecabe054f425804a06373a32d9e11f1567ee70f5980b","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:08.188969Z","id":"4dc2ffd4b44a24bfe8d70c69f3fa35129aa13c53cdaabb5351d053578de952c0","body":{"detail":"A piece is the prompt file (`prompt:clax`) or what one slot became, hashed as substituted (so `declarations` is api::dts without its last line ending). With one binding that is the design's list (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"The prompt\", last paragraph). When a run has a host's surface, `{surfaces}` and `{declarations}` each hold several bindings' text, and the change that stores pieces must decide whether to split them by binding. Nothing stores the pieces yet, and nothing keeps texts by hash: the test `a_system_prompt_is_rebuilt_from_its_pieces` stands in for that store. The request message's opener is covered by the file's hash; the request's JSON is not a piece.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":357},"end":{"line":425},"content_hash":"3ccf3bcd1b3bcebac3160b98c87f891217f0d7f77c4e6a511e803b29a933502e"},"summary":"`system_pieces` gives one `declarations` piece and would give one `surfaces` piece, where the design says a run records each binding's rules and declaration; and it covers the system prompt only","supersedes":"cc5bd07672cc495489d77443179ef3d990167b040e809af1abe26d2799a1f556","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:deferred","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:08.368668Z","id":"5081cb5d9ce41def702f3282c4061992b5ec9e186205fd1e0adba6c8fd610595","body":{"detail":"`system_of` answers `system_prompt(RequestKind::Thread, shape)` for a kind that is not `clax.thread` or `clax.brief`, and `request_of` renders the request the kind's surface resolved (`SurfaceProvider::request`) in the request section of crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md, which says the request's strings are data except those marked as the person's, and marks none. Only a test makes such a run today (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs writes the row's `kind`); `generic::a_fake_runs_first_turn_is_sent_the_request_its_surface_resolved` pins both texts. The change that sends the standard prompt (marking a request's `Instruction` strings, a registration's prompt file) replaces both branches here, and must do so before the store can make a run of another surface's kind. For `clax` kinds the two are `system_prompt` and `request_message` over the same values as before (the goldens under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden and src/*.golden did not change).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":633},"end":{"line":720},"content_hash":"2d37bef893b35fd76c028c30fcc7e2a0cee08adbd24ffd56067ee766b07795df"},"summary":"A run whose kind is not Clax's is sent the system prompt of a `clax.thread` run and the standard file's request section with nothing marked as the person's words","supersedes":"6a48c3ca9751a010cfc7cef844c15a12c19a04163982c0814c6d32967ec4f89d","tags":["t8","for-3a-15","for-3a-03","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:08.544094Z","id":"5bd6231b23d4a472f4195efce41665a7a1a41197402ba99dbfb3dbe5530fce3d","body":{"detail":"`crate::api::dts` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs) wraps Clax's declaration in a comment saying a failure rejects with a `HostError` whose `code` and `retryable` say why, the script's clock and seeded `Math.random`, and ends with `declare const console`. `render_other` gives `{declarations}` `clax_surface::declaration` alone, so crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.shell.triage.golden says nothing of errors or the clock, though `{core.code_mode}` names `console.log`. The surface-neutral parts of that comment could be the core's (a `{core.…}` paragraph, or a head the declaration always has).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":575},"end":{"line":594},"content_hash":"81ec55f69667a8523855a13afab6ad6df958ae1ab0bcc56cc568dd9af1f7a243"},"summary":"A host's run is not told how a failed call rejects, nor declared `console`: Clax's own declaration has both around it, the standard prompt's has neither","supersedes":"67ba2958f43aab6f4406760e9a5530c161f30f956a9e6fb75d3439ae2e8a10b5","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:08.750949Z","id":"6dc9f7dff80a2fd7f35baaf2641d1645dbefb9b8ecaf633eb6500e7e13d0ab9c","body":{"detail":"request_message (prompt.rs:127-150) writes `doc[\"brief\"] = json!(cx.brief)` and an array of cx.threads whatever they hold. a_brief_request always has Some(brief) and a_thread_request has two threads, so a rewrite of the assembly that left out a null brief or an empty threads array (serde's skip_serializing_if on typed structs, say) would keep both goldens byte for byte. Null handling is pinned elsewhere in the same documents (description, url, for, a thread's anchor), which is why this is small. Worth a third golden only if a host can produce either context: check the server's BotHost::context before adding one. (Re-anchored unchanged from record 836022309f0ecc570b8d4339356241b6db86474d8381ad763bca9affa5347db1, issued 2026-11-11T02:18:11.228094Z by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai) on lines 289-333 of the file at git:1abd5e29d: the same text, a_brief_request and the brief request's golden test, is now lines 265-310.)","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":934},"end":{"line":979},"content_hash":"f5b774d706c8f3660078a15025ca27f521cf5e11dabd2d1e3b90c4f66a9633ef"},"summary":"The request-message goldens do not pin a brief request without a brief (`\"brief\": null`) or a thread request with no threads (`\"threads\": []`)","supersedes":"3fed56b7530c3d5519b906b0de919d1234f44b8d7bf8d1cec2d815d65ce56d26","tags":["review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/prompt.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:08.923660Z","id":"6f5db9f0e0e73ae3b26c7335dc1a9e7d6704e68f609740f6475bb266e1c6a07d","body":{"detail":"The design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, \"The prompt\") says a run records each piece's name and hash. `system_pieces_of` returns them for any run; crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs (`Tick::turn`, first turn) sends `system_of`'s text and records no pieces. The store's `pieces_json` column is on the store lane's branch, not here; the step that lands it calls `system_pieces_of` with the same arguments as `system_of` and stores the result.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":653},"end":{"line":670},"content_hash":"275f5b2b006c9cb13d23dedde3445ab68938be0dc36582599d9f98a3053fb3a2"},"summary":"A run's prompt pieces are computed by `system_pieces_of` and stored nowhere: the run loop does not call it","supersedes":"2a87725b44527c7e8958a8431933d8fa30a297ba92f1bce746af74df9d98429b","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:11.103770Z","id":"5ca254da7242942ef9b987283572a9974e6b0547be95c5845f5aedc6a2221539","body":{"detail":"(1) 530-536, \"the refusal was journaled more than once\", cannot fail: the journal holds one row for a `(run, step, seq)` (`Store::record_bot_call`, crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs 1517-1525, keeps the row that is there). A provider that journals a refusal itself and answers `refused` is caught only when its row differs, at 501-506, in words that say the core journaled it. (2) 848-850 copies any failure of the terminal case into `an_ending_that_failed_is_made_again_in_the_next_attempt`, which comes first in the results: with `FakeSurfaceProvider::ended` changed to fail when told twice, the test panics under that name and not under `ending_is_repeatable_inside_the_terminal_step_and_ended_after_its_commit`. (3) No case reads the journal after `ProviderFixture::write`: a provider that answers `Answer::written` and writes no row passes, since the call made again with its key answers the same (414-422). The module documentation of crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs (90-93) calls that safe; the place then has no row for the divergence check or for `unreported_calls`. (4) A case is `NotApplicable` because the fixture left `staged` or `fail_once` as `None`, and the documented way to run the suite (71-75) passes it over: a provider that does stage writes, with a fixture that says it does not, passes. Both tests that run the suite assert the list of cases not played (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs 106-114, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 1569-1582); nothing requires the next fixture to. For the adapter of `clax` the two reasons read \"the fixture cannot make the provider's hook fail\": its `ending` cannot fail at all (seam.rs 165-172), and its `begin` could be made to (146-148 read the store) by a fixture that chose to.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":204},"end":{"line":233},"content_hash":"7b1ec150902f51cd25ccfec71bec6ca9f75119d36aacde536f27e87e35beba2f"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the count at 530-536 or compare the row with the answer whoever wrote it; report the terminal case's failure under its own name only; after the write, require a row at its place and say who wrote it; make `check_provider` take the cases a fixture declares not applicable and fail on any other `NotApplicable`, and give the real `clax` provider's fixture a `fail_once`.","summary":"Four things the provider cases do not hold as their text says: a check that cannot fail, a failure reported under another case's name, a self-journaled write that journals nothing, and a case left out by a fixture with no one told","supersedes":"c6fbafa01696d2c9bbd0c8f406362de71f31779a22f45dad558010655fae3c16","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:11.398272Z","id":"9ef299d3c414e0a9d96bc7653ce83dc2c3572fd495e5f95543d28d6dfdfac7e4","body":{"detail":"Every case's write succeeds: nothing holds that a refusal a session answers is journaled by the core and answered the same when the step runs again (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs, \"Who journals a call\"; today a refused write is journaled, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:422-424). `finish` is only called with `StepEnd::Commit` (257-259), so \"empty after `Cancelled`\" (surface.rs 616-618) is held nowhere, here or in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs. `begin` and `ending` returning `Err` are not played. The case `an_operation_outside_the_binding_never_reaches_the_provider` also hands the operation straight to the session and requires a refusal (353-359): that makes every session check its binding a second time, which `perform` already did, and for Clax's surface it is what keeps check 2 of crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs in the session; say so in the case, or drop the bypass. The only fixture is the fake's (crates/clax-bot/tests/providers.rs); `ClaxProvider` in tests/seam.rs is not run through `check_provider`, so whether today's behaviour passes is unknown: by reading, it would with a host write as `write` (`threads.reply`), not with a draft write, which changes nothing outside the session before the commit (the case requires `state` to change, 301-304).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":535},"end":{"line":537},"content_hash":"e1d6f63de76de103f2fecc3aa0def0762b5cebc3330202f713730f11680d498f"},"suggested_fix":"Add a `refused` call to the fixture (journaled once, answered the same with and without its entry, state unchanged), a case that finishes with `StepEnd::Cancelled` and commits a `stopped` step, and run the suite on `ClaxProvider` when script.rs moves.","summary":"The conformance cases have no refusal by a session, no cancelled step and no failing hook, and are not run against the adapter of Clax's own surface","supersedes":"c41498763b8d79f54ca8f7a9adc4377ef367fd447ee48572b4856bfeb634b008","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:11.631863Z","id":"20a82ceca00c4aafb7c50fc2ba175eb907085e925edb20f74e4b66bc66a585be","body":{"detail":"`Providers` is a plain list held in `Deps.providers`, and `Driver` holds the deps behind an `Arc` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs). `Providers::of` answers `Unavailable` for any name not registered beforehand, with no fallback. `Driver::start` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:240) calls `driver_started` on each provider listed at that moment. A provider of surfaces registered at run time under names not known when the deps were made (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md, 'A runtime registration is a lease', 'Registration, unavailable (§6) | Yes: at run time') has two ways in: interior mutability in `Providers` (and an answer to what `driver_started` means for a provider registered after start), or a fallback resolver (`Fn(&str) -> Option<Arc<dyn SurfaceProvider>>`) that `of` asks after the list. Either changes `Providers` and the public field `Deps.providers`, so the change that adds the first such provider decides it. Nothing on clax.dev is affected: `clax` is registered by `Deps::new`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":96},"end":{"line":104},"content_hash":"97e40f002719a55da4ff8a7eb3c4280db1b1c724ff483fe4376bd5221560efb1"},"summary":"The registry is fixed when `Deps` is built: no provider can be registered while the driver runs, no surface name unknown then can be served, and `driver_started` reaches only the providers registered when `Driver::start` runs","supersedes":"565466d7faa19a8c1381e9780992f23652c9db4e3e0ad3cc0f6418950f4f06d6","tags":["review","t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:11.823327Z","id":"1b63aade0b94e04040101599310cf7799e65b88baa64f6a9cd3d2fcad887975d","body":{"detail":"`Deps.host`: `Deps::new`, `Deps::with_web` and `Deps::without_web` hand it to `Providers::register_clax` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs), which is the only reason the run loop's side names `ClaxProvider`; `Tick::working`'s reference branch (crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs, `deps.host.working`, `cfg(test)`); crates/clax-bot/src/ending_reference.rs (`plan_reply`, `working`); crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs (`read`, `plan_write`) and script_reference/web.rs (`context`). `Deps.web` (now `pub(crate)`): the same three functions of run.rs, the `Debug` of `Deps`, and script_reference/web.rs. `BotHost::context`, `working` and `start_waiting` are called by `clax`'s provider alone outside the references (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs, clax/ending.rs, clax/web.rs). `api::Performer`: crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs (the table and `OpSpec::performer`), host.rs (`FakeBotHost`'s check of an operation), script_reference.rs. When whoever builds a `Deps` registers `clax` itself (`Providers::register`), `Deps::new` loses `host`, `register_clax`, `with_web` and `without_web` go, and the six lines `registry::tests::the_run_loop_names_nothing_of_claxs_but_what_is_listed` lists become none.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":197},"end":{"line":207},"content_hash":"13c3f24bdc49b9ec7246721379ea9911d14087e7066078cd7edc70cc4aa4afcd"},"summary":"For the change that deletes `Deps.host` and `Deps.web`: what still reads them, `BotHost::working`/`start_waiting`/`context` and `api::Performer` after the tick holds its providers","supersedes":"d311ae86405d00caa50c3cfef6652ba4f3514fb97718f52804108254f31abc43","tags":["t8","for-t10","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:12.206720Z","id":"3a17a2b39268cb987177c199a69be755e51518498ceb6532530aaccebef59b3d","body":{"detail":"`Providers::bind` answers `bindings_invalid` (`Bindings::parse`: another version, a malformed value) or `request_invalid`, and `tick` returns the error before the policy is asked and before `Tick::step`, so nothing is begun and `Store::note_commit_failure` is never called. Every row made so far parses (migration 64 and `Store::create_run` write version 1). The change that writes bindings of another version, read beside version 1, decides what a build that cannot read a run's bindings does with the run; `generic::a_run_is_known_by_its_stored_kind_request_and_bindings` holds today's answer (the tick fails and no step is made).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":236},"end":{"line":237},"content_hash":"3ded323fdeb76c09eb1d81605814e5afd35bd5e5e1b3b2dee7ff1df964b84cf1"},"summary":"A run whose stored bindings or request cannot be read fails every tick before any step, uncounted: it cannot be ended, cancelled included","supersedes":"108955fbe294ff04c089bc4ceb02f50fef02b2028105af8e693df88708280bd8","tags":["t8","for-3a-03","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:12.530728Z","id":"a5e62b764b897db4f44d9fdb49aef6bcf3547554bb0fafd256de928b5984aba0","body":{"detail":"Three mutants, each alone, each passing //crates/clax-bot:all: (1) `surfaces.sort_by_key(...)` at line 254 deleted: the script step sorts again (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:282), so only the terminal step's order (`Tick::end`, crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs: who is asked `ending` and joined first, told `ended` first) loses it, and no test ends a run whose kind's binding is not stored first; (2) `.rev()` before `find_map` at line 197-200, so the last binding naming a subject wins: no test binds two surfaces that both name one; (3) in `Driver::start` (crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:236-255) the `driver_started` loop moved before `ending::redeliver`: `generic::a_starting_driver_tells_each_registered_provider` counts calls, not their order, which the doc of `SurfaceProvider::driver_started` states. None changes a `clax`-only run (one binding, one provider).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":393},"end":{"line":407},"content_hash":"e8da72dfed8c811d43e1c8e3eade370bfe248691169fe0f4d64e7fe8954b32a3"},"summary":"The order of a run's bindings is not held by a test where it matters: with the kind's sort removed from `RunSurfaces::surfaces`, with the subject taken from the last binding, or with `driver_started` called before `redeliver`, every test of //crates/clax-bot:all passes","supersedes":"d40ac91db153011d996c3cd89d159df1f6263f350c9d4a878a6e179db5630f96","tags":["review","t8","tests","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:12.802866Z","id":"c342fb4aaa0308089469635305cd702b28feb358e622dd88998492bd3f174ae7","body":{"detail":"A stored binding of version 1 keeps no definition (`hash` is null), so `Unavailable::definition` is an empty one, the binding has no operations, and `dispatch` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) answers `unavailable` (retryable) for any name routed to the surface, a name the surface never had included. The core journals the refusal as an outcome, so the step run again answers it from the journal even when a provider has been registered since. For the registry that follows: a registration's adapter and the definition read by hash give the binding its operations (then `unknown_op` and `invalid_args` come first again, and `unavailable` is for a call no provider accepted), and the retrying the design gives the run loop decides whether an `unavailable` is journaled at all. A run whose kind's surface has no provider stops `request_gone` at its first turn (`generic::a_run_whose_kinds_surface_has_no_provider_stops`): whether it should wait instead is for the same change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":505},"end":{"line":505},"content_hash":"5e83239ea75f5c70fa8383e311b777f564e9a5986088072ed1331dcd78152986"},"summary":"A binding with no provider has no definition: its calls are refused before their arguments are checked, the refusal is journaled, and nothing sends a call again","supersedes":"d4ac1b6c06efbe7aaacc8ab320a7ca219560a173989b56ab9a975f3e38b38342","tags":["t8","for-3a-13","for-3a-14","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.067644Z","id":"6a69227a1b176f7b7871f63dbf0061bd22c4852b13e08a24f4a007d47fd3baec","body":{"detail":"`RunSurfaces::operations` lists, for each binding in the order bound, every operation of its provider's definition (not the run's own set, so that a call to one the run lacks is refused by the run loop in its surface's words: `web_disabled`), and for a binding whose provider takes no call, the operations the row keeps for it. Three things follow. (1) `dispatch`'s refusal `no_such_operation` for a call no binding takes (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) can no longer be reached from a sandbox: the script gets a `TypeError` from its own `clax` instead (`generic::a_runs_scripts_are_given_the_operations_of_its_surfaces`); the case was dropped from `generic`'s `WORK` script. (2) A binding served by `Unavailable`, or by an outside provider whose registration is gone, gives its scripts no operation unless the row keeps the binding's `operations`: a script that calls it gets a `TypeError`, not `unavailable` (`generic::a_binding_with_no_provider_answers_unavailable_and_takes_no_part_in_the_end` stores the list to reach the refusal). Bindings that store their operations make this whole. (3) Tests that register one fake under two surface names give a run each operation twice, which `validate_ops` refuses: such a run ends `failed` (`surface_ops_invalid`) at its first script step. The tests that do so today take no script step.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":434},"end":{"line":436},"content_hash":"743ee6f9d0cd80fd2d837b69367b4036deca6348cb63d1f9d0d082fa36bf34ae"},"summary":"A script's `clax` now has every operation of each bound surface's definition, so no script can name an operation no binding takes, and one definition bound under two names fails the run","supersedes":"32b82e53ba6fb919cdb814ab607a51df8c608d236aee1b99d9ec30172550d803","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.248529Z","id":"aa33efbfdb23e171503a1e6247145cfe3446573181f0b6de94f94d423b335b0b","body":{"detail":"A run's history keeps the blocks its provider produced: Gemini turns hold a `signature` field on text and tool_use blocks (crates/clax-sample/src/messages/gemini.rs), and Chat Completions turns hold thinking blocks with an empty signature (openai.rs). RunSettings is read before every tick, so after an admin switches api to anthropic an open run sends these to the Messages API, which refuses the unknown field and the unsigned thinking; the run ends model_error. Switching between openai and gemini drops or mistranslates nothing fatal.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (fa547d92), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":94},"end":{"line":97},"content_hash":"02de2c3a46cb7f229c289416253894e26660ad5d5d58decf7b59d8237bf0879c"},"suggested_fix":"Record the API a run started on (its row, like its model) and keep it for the run, or strip foreign fields when the Messages API is the target.","summary":"Changing the API while runs are open sends one API's blocks to another","supersedes":"432635154c6c4741aaadae65400db13b88af228ae81c1e1ae5b2101364b3ac65","tags":["providers","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.419999Z","id":"4b1f2e07bee681b28f6673b60d491492c8ff6c565686ff353f828fa2d208ec80","body":{"detail":"Spec section 8: locally a value in config.toml or the environment overrides the stored one. from_store reads Store::bot_setting and leaves per_person_concurrency, lease and renewal at their defaults; the daemon's SettingsSource must apply HomeConfig::bot (including bot.per_person_concurrency and enabled) on top, re-read per tick.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (2f8fe438), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Reply 67173b9e on the original proposed a close: the server lays [bot] over the database inside the store (Store::set_bot_overrides), fixed in git:bf3458ec.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":161},"end":{"line":165},"content_hash":"ea413dee711a57207ddc291eb3872d8297bd0a023361d0b7c7a7930cf7dd7878"},"summary":"RunSettings::from_store reads the database only: the [bot] config overrides are the server's to lay over it","supersedes":"fe3251997ba49a1fbb037aba3defa58b24c61cf85d602e1f0efa72ef9c0d46b6","tags":["wiring","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.625032Z","id":"dd13a18a5678d7e70e00b307019bb8525f2382eb136f994e4fe7746eda22268b","body":{"detail":"Deleted in 359f0c1f4 refactor(claxbot): the run loop's deps hold no host and no web source. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"871a2360c5908500c5cfd96d48403048d2134eca6ea0b64128b6ca5f58188355","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: The field Deps.web this alternative is pinned on was deleted in 359f0c1f4; the web source is now the registry's (Providers::set_clax_web, crates/clax-bot/src/registry.rs)","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.625529Z","id":"70a8890987f09343e04c0c201e7849ac7829959522739e98e821fbd6cafd63b0","body":{"detail":"Deleted in d85e45395 merge(claxbot): T8's registry under the typed finish. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"9bb28c7d908018d96898dc79162329e5b2cad3aeff7cbdf016d61f73b3efd45e","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: Deps.kind and Deps::with_kind were deleted in the merge d85e45395 (T8's registry): a run's kind is its row's stored kind, read by Providers::bind","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.626094Z","id":"0efc0e82108f0e4270154fa26788c1352b7514325e3a7f6a7bd3c43d168e4f01","body":{"detail":"`register_clax` (line 373) builds a new `ClaxProvider` from the deps' fields and registers it unconditionally. `tests/generic.rs` calls `with_providers` after `deps_with` (which calls `with_web`), so its registries hold no `clax`; the reverse order would silently register it. `Deps.store`, `Deps.host` and `Deps.clock` are public fields: a caller assigning one after `Deps::new` leaves the registered provider on the old value. The server (crates/clax-server/src/bot.rs:1177-1189) and the Worker (crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs:439-450) build `Deps::new(...)` then `with_web`, which is correct. Re-registering only when `clax` is registered, and the fields going private or rebuilt-on-change when `Deps.host` is deleted, would close both.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":338},"end":{"line":349},"content_hash":"cc50342d1fa6f5132c76e462d8c7eec18b2d04ac34d67a3f082596c932cab820"},"summary":"`Deps::with_web` and `without_web` register `clax` again whatever the registry holds, so after `with_providers` with no `clax` they add it back; and a `Deps.store`, `host` or `clock` set after `Deps::new` is not seen by the `clax` provider already registered","supersedes":"f7b88ea05641ea33b34d3c10c4cde4d9c88fdd1bee569fc5bb43eccb13534eda","tags":["review","t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.800005Z","id":"b174cc080cc6157d276031eabd981955e816a42ab245e0d094fb33d8c3e1cd29","body":{"detail":"Deleted in d5751d4c0 feat(claxbot): a tick holds its run as stored and the provider of each binding, from a registry. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"24bb1ffa01441de739ce0a1613735af2313b76f77c175b83b12b10110237f0e1","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: Deps::info and Deps::providers, the code this record is pinned on, were deleted in d5751d4c0; the tick binds its run through Providers::bind (registry.rs)","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:13.800587Z","id":"96feba20d96e82ce2496e4b538f0d5754c820c347b63e1c21402f7c0d4076256","body":{"detail":"`tools(self.limits.script.max_result_bytes)` puts the sandbox's limit on a returned value in the words the model reads (`policy::bytes_text`: `64 KiB` for a whole number of KiB, else `<n> bytes`). Under the default it is `RunSettings::limits()`, whose `max_result_bytes` is always `Limits::default()`'s 64 KiB, so the text cannot change. A policy that answers another size on a later tick of one run changes a tool's description mid-run: the model was told another number, and the cached prefix is lost. docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:94 calls a declaration that changes during a run a failure. `RunPolicy`'s documentation states the rule (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:338-341); a fix that enforces it stores the limits the model was told with the run's first turn and reads them back.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":618},"end":{"line":639},"content_hash":"61f842d8435827b36d552abbda389d721abf8fc89a34d936176c233fd5af4f4a"},"summary":"The `run_script` description is built from the policy's answer on every turn, and nothing holds that answer still for a run","supersedes":"969bc09b395720454619b0fd8e0244917452ef750b83e5fed4793e0cc2ca7d17","tags":["for-t9","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:14.003913Z","id":"c6df3c3a2e415a3e04ef6089dbb551533aafae080902f0a4482512acfb6081ba","body":{"detail":"usage_rows stores cost as ceil(qty x rate / base) of an integer rate (charge_in, crates/clax-store/src/credits.rs). For qty <= base the share is hit exactly; above it the rows' costs may sum to slightly less than the billed cost. Exact sums would need the store to take a billed cost per row rather than a rate.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (47f96906), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":815},"end":{"line":885},"content_hash":"c540eaa6b294efa7579537cb080ac1566d4ec86f91dd306da0d9f3122c457fc4"},"summary":"bill() sets an integer micro-USD-per-million rate, so a row of more than a million tokens can fall short of its billed share by under qty / 1 000 000 micro-USD","supersedes":"c14faea8da5f2f22fbf50555235c7a3a1f44340569c9ec686a12dfe17907d848","tags":["pricing","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:14.200568Z","id":"4451025345f0a9e93cd361fe1527994b4441bebf2b51cba5114129851daebdf0","body":{"detail":"Without a rate the commit fails unknown_cost_line (charge_in). Dropping the rows would hide the tokens from the person's usage; a new RateSource needs a store migration (usage_rows.rate_source CHECK). The row charges 0 either way, being waived for an own key.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (123e4f52), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":887},"end":{"line":910},"content_hash":"68d3664e4bfe1d629fe1a91f1b256044aaf9f5db75d72f780f9435cdbc170c94"},"summary":"token_rate gives an own-key turn on a model with no rate a waived zero rate sourced 'default', rather than dropping its token rows or adding a RateSource for it","supersedes":"e69f7a7dbc7706c1f8319d5841c4dbc23c1a605e9d0acdf7fd69f3cca8e6f036","tags":["pricing","revisit:the store gains a rate source for waived or unpriced rows","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:14.399552Z","id":"0437a930a65497babf6c8b3e255ed5478213830da605d71e8c83301bec4946d0","body":{"detail":"With infra_lines on (the Worker), each step charges do.requests = 1 + its host calls and toolpath.storage = the payload's JSON bytes for 30 days. Neither is read from Cloudflare's billing: spec section 13 lists this as a known gap. The payload holds the script's code and outcome, so storage scales with output, not with what the journal keeps.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (6f3e5c2c), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1013},"end":{"line":1032},"content_hash":"209d50573adc6aa561185d99669f36da87ff8d0d43beac8cdb8692b1060c2e1e"},"summary":"Durable Object requests and Toolpath storage are estimated per step","supersedes":"fbb1bce3e3b9a8fa14284e199a9a3fa12e58ae99276a71e2b3acf4dbe4dc1bbc","tags":["credits","gap","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:14.623820Z","id":"9e65c042e9543a1f8e5e162552c9c121497b4c412cbedf60c5d17be4788f63e1","body":{"detail":"Each scenario's ticks, run row, steps with payloads, replies, `bot.*` audit records, usage rows and request bodies were printed on this commit and on d1bc352e3 and compared as text: identical in all 17, but for a script's measured times (`cpu_us`, `busy_us`, `wall_us`, and the `script.compute` quantity they give). Which cause wins is unchanged: a cancel over every cap and over exhausted credits; exhausted credits and a withheld flag over the step cap; steps over credits over minutes; a `finish` already asked for over the flag and every cap; a step whose commits failed three times over a cancel (`internal_error`). Also the same: a full window (`context_window`), a refusal for length (`model_error`), a cut stream with little time left (`Waiting(Retry)`), and a run with nothing in its way. The order of `Tick::step` is unchanged for a policy that answers (`policy_error` is `None`): the commit-failure bound, a `finish` or `give_up` asked for, `must_stop` (cancel, refusal, steps, credits, minutes), then the step.","kind":"comment","span":{"start":{"line":1085},"end":{"line":1172},"content_hash":"869819378c3c6ff5c54ad1244b12d83e4ce5b9d2f8baaa8e8585adeab90e8102"},"summary":"Under `DefaultPolicy` a tick leaves what it left at d1bc352e3: 17 scenarios, 12 of them with two or more causes of a stop at once, run on both commits and compared whole","supersedes":"76e37d278388077153da4f5a0c483e13434bb712a5c76f2d073b4602e11f4ba8","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:14.816702Z","id":"e3faf97eb7f755046ec35e63b9b11cba481d1cce08851edbe938fff82cac645d","body":{"detail":"tick reads run_with_steps and History::of parses each payload (assistant blocks, script code and outcome, the first turn's system prompt) to rebuild the messages, so a run of N steps does O(N) work and reads O(total payload) per tick, O(N^2) over the run. Bounded by max_steps (40 by default); a cache of the history keyed by the last committed step would make it O(1).\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (94a4332d), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1121},"end":{"line":1125},"content_hash":"5b3d5ed35ee9788b1c237507f19f96b32df336f51020fbca09e5748c294743c7"},"summary":"Every tick rebuilds the whole history from every committed step's payload","supersedes":"ab015010025d1e85c0cb59b6670f64187179cdbb10437f7b96e74de9ef050ddb","tags":["perf","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:14.999935Z","id":"2565dfb25fc632fd5297cc841dee29ac4c900e4c284f47750eab7bc1cd311136","body":{"detail":"The open step is taken out by `position` and `remove`. Three changes here pass every test of `//crates/clax-bot:all`: `rposition`, `swap_remove`, and `steps[i].clone()` (the open step left in). They pass because the three folds filter on `committed_at` themselves and a run has at most one open step, the last (asserted on every tick of the integration suite with a temporary assertion: `steps` all committed, the open step's `n` above the last one's; it held). `StepCx::history` is documented as the run's committed steps (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 606-610) and the proof's adapter relies on it (`history.last()` is the turn that asked for the script, crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 614-619). When the tick passes `&self.steps` there, a provider that reads the last step, or counts them, is wrong on a run with a second open step or with the open one left in, and no test says so. Suggested: build `steps` with `retain(|s| s.committed_at.is_some())` after taking the first open one, so the field's documentation (run.rs 1076-1078) is true by construction, and a unit test of that on a list with two open steps.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1125},"end":{"line":1125},"content_hash":"084753e5341f60697fbdc29a18b2db7f4ea34e914a9b1e698c523d833a5a8717"},"summary":"Nothing holds that `Tick::steps` is the committed steps only, which is what `StepCx::history` promises a provider","supersedes":"a7ee2d9794af062159d9d1922943d0bc1f5dece742b8c4920d208b57eb6908bf","tags":["t3","for-t5","for-t8","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:15.262383Z","id":"b680ba019d98f7eb73dece466dc16376d3d84a7d1fca9e30dbde5db1afec6cc5","body":{"detail":"`tick` asks `run_settings` and `limits` after the claim and before `Tick::step`, so before `must_stop` (run.rs:1506-1509) reads a cancel. An `Err` from either is the tick's error: `Driver::settle` logs it, releases the run and tries again after `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (30 s, driver.rs:345-348), for ever. Measured with a policy whose `limits` fails: the tick answers `Err`, the run stays `running` with no step and no notice; a person who stops it gets a run that stays `stopping`, since no tick reaches `must_stop`. An `Err` from `history` in `overflowed` on the stop-reason path (run.rs:2261-2263) comes after the provider answered: the turn stays open, the answer's usage is written as absorbed rows (4 rows in the measurement: the instance's cost, no credits), and the next tick calls the model again with the same full window. While the policy keeps failing that is one absorbed full-window call every 30 s, bounded only by the run's minutes if it has any, and not by the person's credits. No policy fails under the defaults (`DefaultPolicy` answers `Ok`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1129},"end":{"line":1142},"content_hash":"eb9cff496c2fef978519c956ca644cca0e7fc910110b82902e08cb3859c35c6b"},"summary":"A policy that fails leaves a run running that cannot be cancelled, and after a full window drops an answer the instance paid for, again on every retry","supersedes":"b80d08e93f13794d143adae7c0a09f2f4e66bedde4ea834588da258c69dd1e11","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:15.476054Z","id":"d30ff12dfa37b00ade0b839d0bdceef93f649aed451b72b980aea2976a262cbb","body":{"detail":"The tick holds three things of the committed steps: `hist: history::Control` (the core's: `next`, `nudged`, `committed`, `last_usage`; the field kept its name so `must_stop`'s cap read at 1356 is unchanged), `clax: clax::Tally` (1075) and `steps` (1078), the steps as read, in order, without the open one. After the script reads (script.rs 217, 554) and the ending reads (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) move to the provider, `Tick::clax` has no reader and goes with its import (run.rs 38); from then on run.rs names no field of the tally. `StepCx::new`'s `history` argument (surface.rs 635) is `&self.steps`. `history.rs` is held free of the surface's nouns by its own test (`history::tests::the_run_loops_folds_name_nothing_of_a_surface`, which reads the file's source); run.rs is not, and cannot be until this change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1226},"end":{"line":1235},"content_hash":"2f29eccbd4bc0c4520a3cd0cdcedabc2987872b945fd31814495002dab7a7f38"},"summary":"For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: `Tick::clax` is the one field of the tick that is Clax's own fold, and `Tick::steps` is what `StepCx::history` borrows","supersedes":"9647b192ba2cb7be8b1f393d3558f9ae9fa273b4f374501bb5ab91fb13c18df7","tags":["t3","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:15.715983Z","id":"9d10477555bcb26ce2dcc807064753a065b9d442f0ad1c7a33dabda82276dad5","body":{"detail":"`expire_waits` calls `Store::expire_calls` with the hub's rule for a call that was never answered (`broker::stored::deadline_failure`, which is `bot_surfaces::unanswered`), then unparks a run that is parked still. It rests on three things. (1) The call the run waits on is a pending call of the run's own store, which holds for the daemon's broker (`StoredPending`). With a broker that keeps its calls elsewhere (`MemoryPending`, tests) nothing journals an answer for a parked run and nothing unparks it when one arrives: at the deadline the run is unparked with nothing journaled and its step, run again, sends the call again under its key and is answered what the broker kept. A run of a surface with no broker at all (the fake) is unparked by whoever has its answer (`Store::unpark_run`). (2) `Deps::clock`, by which a tick reads whether `due_at` has passed, and the broker's `BrokerClock`, by which the deadline was computed, are one clock in a daemon; crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs moves the two together (`World::pass`), and they differ there by the ten seconds a provider waits before it parks. (3) `Store::expire_calls` ends every call that is due, of any run: the hub's own state for those calls (a call its host asked to be sent later) is dropped the next time the hub reads what is pending. Whoever journals an answer or runs `expire_calls` outside a tick (a `serve`, a timed pass) must call `Driver::wake` for each run it made due: the driver arms only the deadline of a run it settled or resumed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1183},"end":{"line":1192},"content_hash":"865e31cb85d725a89b606b33a99f90733eecb9381ec94b32f1e04c1aa8f2c41e"},"summary":"A parked run's deadline is ended by the run loop through the store's pending calls, not through the hub: what that rests on","supersedes":"3cbb87b4ebd3d3dc99f90db9ce80735b0e3704adfa6ce736b77c7dfc9c494a81","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:15.997174Z","id":"ac318c09365337cf5e7bde7da44c0d5a6bcfb89c9b646bd7ca0872ed5bc47903","body":{"detail":"`expire_waits` ends every due call of every run, including calls whose registration was replaced (`expire_calls`' second clause), and unparks their runs, clearing `due_at`. Nothing wakes those runs: their driver entry stays at their own `due_at` (none, for a run with no deadline), and a later hub pass that would have woken them finds the calls answered and reports nothing. Bounded by the deadline for a run that has one; a run with no deadline stays queued with no entry until a restart. Consider a per-run expiry here, or returning the runs it made due for the driver to wake. Adds to 3cbb87b4ebd3d3dc99f90db9ce80735b0e3704adfa6ce736b77c7dfc9c494a81, point (3).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1183},"end":{"line":1192},"content_hash":"865e31cb85d725a89b606b33a99f90733eecb9381ec94b32f1e04c1aa8f2c41e"},"summary":"A tick's global `expire_calls` makes other runs due without waking their driver entries, and takes those runs from the hub's own pass","supersedes":"7816e3eaa1e58fff08b29bde5bd351673989861c239a17615e31c4903caf1077","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:16.262545Z","id":"43e3b7447049a8ee98c17b5d9f02471f6dd1fdb77a59f723d0770697040ee960","body":{"detail":"Changing `.saturating_sub(self.parked)` to `.saturating_sub(self.parked * 2)` leaves `//crates/clax-bot:integration` and `:clax_bot_lib_test` green: `park::parked_time_does_not_count_against_the_runs_minutes` checks only that a run parked longer than its cap goes on. A run that parks for an hour could then compute for an hour more than `max_run_minutes`. A test that parks, is answered, and then works until the cap, checking that it stops at cap + parked time and not later, would hold it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1274},"end":{"line":1283},"content_hash":"3920f0a4f20b4a3fa7382a6973cd2aa0468f7a4e193474f0750cf770e66d4015"},"summary":"No test catches parked time subtracted more than once from the run's minutes","supersedes":"01db40eecc85dd272cfc62e7187283ab12215d22d7829453a8428095e4ad7ed6","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:16.529827Z","id":"3bde110169a1410ba245e9c689012eebeffeae00ef3cad0e3ed14b30745a2bc4","body":{"detail":"`must_stop`, `minutes_stop` (run.rs:1318-1324) and the credits stop in `affordable` format `<n> steps`, `<n> credits` and `<n> minutes` from `Tick.limits`, which a policy may answer differently from `RunSettings` (crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs: `limit of 1 steps`, `limit of 5 minutes`). `End::Stopped`, which is to carry the limit's value to a provider's `ending`, takes it from the same place; a run with no cap never produces one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1490},"end":{"line":1509},"content_hash":"d6672897d017a2fa2cd797c714c5f26be4943d67f9a5331280ebe7a5be09290b"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings: a cap's value in a notice comes from the policy's limits, not the settings","supersedes":"c1ae5826a5abc5ca7508f8ae0097569f420d50bd84f3e6e41b0c46b5474b81f0","tags":["for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:16.727729Z","id":"5ed4ede7744056e25af7f00f49715922b419d3147896a8a0f80e19cc4d40aa10","body":{"detail":"(1) Test the run loop's half of `SurfaceProvider::ending` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 1474-1511): an `Err` counted against the step as a failed commit, the bound (`max_commit_failures`), `End::Failed`'s `Err` logged and the step committed, and every binding's `ending` still called when one fails. The provider cases hold only the provider's half. (2) Make the change to the sources' read on purpose: `Store::web_pages` failing in `finish` (1684 here) fails the tick uncounted today, so the step is begun again without end; behind `ending` under that contract it is tried `max_commit_failures` times and the run ends `failed` (surface.rs, the endings table's row for a notice that cannot be posted). Keep the read out of `ending`, or accept the bound. (3) `begin`'s failure is retried uncounted and `ending`'s is counted (surface.rs 1460-1471, 1490-1505): decide whether the two should agree. (4) Build what delivers `ended` again; the contract allows a repeat with the same kind of end and best-effort details (1513-1540), and keeping the `End` in the terminal step's payload would let a redelivery carry all of it (record b25bed99). (5) Count a `FieldClash` against the step, and reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) as surface names in `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42), so a clash cannot be reached by a name (record f2d3e3a6). (6) The adapter's terminal cases prove nothing about notices: its `ending` and `ended` do nothing (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs 186-204), so the provider cases pass on it with no notice posted. The real provider's fixture needs a `noticed` probe that reads the notices and a `lifecycle`. (7) A cancelled script's `stopped` step is charged the per-step lines with no call counted (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673-683; 1567-1573 here): keep or change it knowingly.\n\nRe-recorded unchanged from record e6464e30 on crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, whose subject moved here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":160},"end":{"line":281},"content_hash":"4e6754cbab1255e9cf1d7cf5c082f7f5aba681c62cca450c1a3077d0bf566384"},"summary":"For the change that moves the endings behind `ending` and `ended`: what the seam states and nothing holds yet","tags":["seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:16.727766Z","id":"19a39ed890e52b97d9d94eb2fa360fccc68613473f94a27893dd5818fa944408","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone, passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`: (1) 1570 as `5` (the requests line of every `stopped` step, a cancelled script's among them, counts five calls: crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 160-164 says it counts none). (2) 1574-1575 without `self.unreported_calls(reported)` (a stop writes no `bot.call` for a journal row no committed attempt reported). (3) 1539 without `c.seq() < NOTICE_SEQ` (every notice a stop posts gets a `bot.call` record of its own). (1) is billed where `RunSettings::infra_lines` is on (the hosted Worker, crates/clax-worker/src/bot.rs 310); crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` (219) holds the line for a turn, a script and a `finish` step and has no `stopped` step. (2) and (3) are audit only: `Pins::ending` (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/pins.rs 568-592) pins a terminal step's payload, replies, writes, notices and roster, and not the run's audit records. Also unpinned, and seen by no one: a call answered from the journal is logged with `ms` 0 (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 477-481 as 7 passes all 189; the goldens replace every `ms` by a name, pins.rs 123).\n\nRe-recorded unchanged from record 85a58cbb on crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, whose subject moved here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":230},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"60d22ce3eab4067af8e78a992da08789ed65bd33160f9c851f6a3aea9ebe0004"},"suggested_fix":"Extend `a_script_steps_requests_line_counts_its_calls` with a run stopped by a cap after a script and a script cancelled after a call, each with its `stopped` step's requests quantity; add `p.audit()` to what one stopped ending and the cancelled ending pin.","summary":"No test holds what a `stopped` step is charged on the requests line, nor its audit records: the count of calls, the `bot.call` records of a stop, and that a notice's journal row gets none","tags":["review","seam","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:16.728533Z","id":"e4f58aa8632ee8055bf9109f3a6c67f1c7ef4c83c8ca0745d38338773c9ac5c8","body":{"detail":"`Tick::step` takes a `finish` or `give_up` the last turn asked for before it reads the cancel (`must_stop`), and `Store::begin_step` refuses every kind but `stopped` and `failed` for a run whose status is `stopping` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs:1225-1230). Measured with the default policy on both commits: a turn that answers `finish`, then `Store::cancel_run`, then a tick by the same driver: the tick answers `Err(a stopping run's next step is its stopped step)`, the run stays `stopping` with one step, and so does a run whose second text-only turn asked for `give_up` (`no_tool_call`). `Driver::settle` tries such a run again every `NOT_OURS_WAIT` (driver.rs), for ever. The window is the time between a turn's commit and the next tick. crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs ('A policy that fails') says a run can always be stopped; this is the case where it cannot, and it does not depend on the policy.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1553},"end":{"line":1578},"content_hash":"ed3d642289f12d04764b66866b640ce38d57536ae2f3dbf683fdb90b1d41b14c"},"suggested_fix":"In `Tick::step`, when the run is `stopping` (or the tick's cancel is set), stop it (`Stop::cancelled()`) before a `finish` or `give_up` is taken; or let `begin_step` take a `finish` or `give_up` for a stopping run, if a run that had already asked to finish should finish.","summary":"A run cancelled after its model asked for `finish` or `give_up` cannot take any step: the tick fails with `a stopping run's next step is its stopped step`, on this commit and on d1bc352e3 alike","supersedes":"f865f85f50495a2ff14397644883bac80fcd2022f45f3c2dcd10f3dd20ac6157","tags":["review","verify-t4","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:16.919313Z","id":"0c8047a5b3a58156e15ee2a65f5f323e73c275de10551d2478238af5b5436b46","body":{"detail":"`script_step` builds `ClaxProvider::of(self.deps).for_run(self.run.clone())` and its `RunInfo` (`ClaxProvider::info`: kind and request from `Run::request` and `Run::thread_ids`, the binding from `Bindings::clax(artifact_id, web)`), not from `bot_runs.kind`, `request_json` and `bindings_json`, which the store writes and `Run` does not carry. `Deps::surfaces` and `Deps::with_surfaces` replace the run's bindings for a test (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs); nothing else sets them. For the change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers: read the stored bindings, hold one provider for each for the tick (or the driver), and delete `Deps::surfaces`, `ClaxProvider::info`, `ClaxProvider::for_run` and its `row` (a provider made without the row reads it by `Store::run`, one read a step; the tick gives its own so a script step reads the store no more than it did). The clone of the row for each script step goes with them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1613},"end":{"line":1617},"content_hash":"e2ea55ca5d85680e8390f2a849fa23b1e33ddfccedb068e8e5d35caf0ffab3d4"},"summary":"The tick makes a run's one binding from the row's own columns, a provider for each script step, and takes other surfaces from a hook for tests","supersedes":"2e0b25fac243b682a52f79e121045ccad01dc9e61167cdb6dc625422854c73fc","tags":["t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:17.150942Z","id":"be6f353bc4096b96294ef224c1cb6c65c2fd7b1e13fa44f0a71844d931967bfe","body":{"detail":"`Tick::policy_failed` counts with `Store::note_commit_failure`, the counter `Tick::commit` uses, so two failed commits and one policy failure on one step end the run; the reason is then `policy_error` if the policy is failing on that tick and `internal_error` if not (`Tick::step`, run.rs:1622-1633). It calls `begin_on` before it counts, so each failure bumps the open step's `attempt` and emits a `step_started` event with nothing after it, and a step is opened (a `turn` or `script` row) for a run that may then end `failed` in its place. The wait between failures is `Deps.retry_later` (30 s), with no backoff: three failures end a run in a minute, which is short for an environment whose policy reads a store that is briefly away. The policy's error is stored as `payload.error` and shown wherever a step's payload is; a policy that puts a secret in its error leaks it there. A counter and a bound of the policy's own need a column, which this change could not add (the store was not to be touched).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1624},"end":{"line":1644},"content_hash":"6c6514bb0fc2d72dd59b5a9220ae8313dbc71aeb0b2b9250d590663da07248fb"},"summary":"A failing policy shares the step's counter of failed commits, begins the step each time it fails, and its error text is kept in the step","supersedes":"aa103f11cb0a0e7bd80a027a7f34ecca349e3e3953a7f750a182fe79cb7fb58d","tags":["for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:17.528965Z","id":"c0806661ad3e3447ba53aebaa6709d28e69d0fa36b3a462a07fbab72788281da","body":{"detail":"Tick::affordable prices the request as its JSON bytes / 4 tokens, the part the last turn cached at the cached-input rate and the rest at the input rate, then caps max_tokens to the output the credits left pay for. A request whose tokens run well above bytes/4 (non-Latin text, dense JSON) can still overshoot the person's credits by its input; counting tokens (the Messages API count_tokens endpoint) would bound it.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from its original record by mailto:alex@kesling.co (96939966), after crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs moved. Since a9ecb07d1 the uncached rest is priced at the cache-write rate, not the input rate.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1665},"end":{"line":1759},"content_hash":"540fd5386534671db4b7db84966a2b79b36b118e79dd336b5905bec71cf6960b"},"summary":"A turn's affordable max_tokens rests on a byte-count estimate of its input","supersedes":"ad443fc1842802f93626ba2ff89f9f0ef6dc144d03b92d5b8f3500a369384cdc","tags":["credits","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:17.784115Z","id":"9ad0943f39869a65c33c3e99d2375e4cb1b2302a0c31e6b8796d9a66d2c2ee17","body":{"detail":"The overflow plumbing is whole for a policy that is a pure function of the core's messages and a level (held by crates/clax-bot/tests/policy.rs with a fake that drops the oldest tool results). What it does not have:\n\n1. The overflowing answer's blocks stay in the core's projection: `History::of` pushes every turn's assistant blocks (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:626-627), as it does for `pause_turn`, so after a `stop_reason` overflow the next request ends with that partial assistant message unless the policy drops it. `overflowed` rebuilds that projection by hand (`hist.messages`, then the turn's request, then the answer's blocks) because the fold has no way to say what the next tick's projection will be. The projection function should take the committed steps plus one uncommitted turn, so this is computed once.\n2. A policy is given messages, not steps: it cannot tell which step a message came from, so it cannot keep a tool call and its result together by step or summarise by step. The projection should say, for each message or block, the step it came from.\n3. A policy has nowhere to keep a summary: the design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:880-888) lets it send one it keeps, recorded by hash. Here a turn records `payload.shown` (level, bytes, SHA-256) and the overflowing turn `next.overflow.shown_sha256`, which name what was sent but do not hold it, so a run's exact context is rebuilt only by asking the same policy again.\n4. Nothing checks what a policy answers: messages with a tool result and no call, or two of one role, reach the provider, and its refusal stops the run (`model_error`).\n5. Each shorter history changes the cached prefix; the turn after an overflow pays the cache-write rate for all of it, and `affordable` (run.rs:1817) prices it from the last turn's usage, which after a refused request is zero.\n6. `HistoryAsk.level` only grows. A long session that is compacted many times needs the policy to decide what a level means; nothing lowers it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1769},"end":{"line":1815},"content_hash":"0f7ecb1f53573ab9f05099e1c4238b483ffbc14e6345a13dd6daaa0feb7f6648"},"summary":"What a real compactor still needs from the run loop, and what the message projection must provide for it","supersedes":"e0a4c40f67410b0400e49c2b50fc36e851ea951cdcd531ad18715d78af773bef","tags":["for-t3","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:18.011153Z","id":"16991ecff1fe4ccc62df1233a865f656987c5bd236ad0a2ebdbfc46bf041ac2b","body":{"detail":"Today: `Control::of` and `Tally::of` run when the tick is built (1051-1052), `transcript` in `turn` (1746). The tally is read by a script tick (script.rs 217, 554) and a terminal tick (run.rs 1374-1396, 1574) and not by a turn, so a turn tick folds it for nothing: one pass that clones a few strings. A `OnceCell<Tally>` on `Tick` would save that pass and cost a `Send`/`Sync` question on wasm32 and native alike (the tick is borrowed across awaits), for a field that leaves the tick when the provider folds its own. Not worth it before then.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1826},"end":{"line":1830},"content_hash":"ee259796ac8410d3a2e93d3193d750c7263adf945a3f03269d6ff9ba4be0876c"},"summary":"Folding the transcript and the tally lazily, once, behind a cell on the tick, instead of the tally on every tick and the transcript in the turn","supersedes":"3bb4a9c526a4a5a4069ecbb170ff17a38b9b17d1d37f13d5ce8723b3adb23e28","tags":["t3","revisit:a profile of a tick shows the tally's pass, or `Tick::clax` outlives the change that moves the endings behind the provider","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:18.238579Z","id":"54aaba01bd7f33dbbdce87eb2161a16320e2382353ba9f9ac5eebfc0d35e1602","body":{"detail":"`overflowed` (run.rs:1911-1948) lets the run go on when the policy's answer to `HistoryFor::Overflow` has fewer bytes than the messages that overflowed, and records `next.overflow.shown_sha256`. The next tick's `turn` asks again with `HistoryFor::NextTurn` at the same level (run.rs:1993-2001) and sends that answer, whatever it is; it is not compared with the recorded hash or size. A policy that answers an overflow with less and the next turn with `Shown::Core` passes the rule each time and is never sent shorter. Measured with such a policy, `max_overflow_retries = None`, `max_steps = None` and a provider that refuses every request as too long: 30 ticks, 30 committed turns, 30 requests with identical messages, the run still `running`, 1 credit charged (step 1's fee). With the default `max_overflow_retries` of 4 the same policy ends after 4 such turns. On the stop-reason path each such call is answered and charged, so only the person's credits end it. The module documentation (policy.rs:32-37, 'cannot make the run loop ask twice') and `RunLimits::max_overflow_retries` ('leaves only the rule that each answer is shorter than the last', policy.rs:118-122) state more than the code holds.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1860},"end":{"line":1900},"content_hash":"44c9c41abc1c1816e00bd6911ef65afcd19f52221f4d76f29f9903dd8428e261"},"suggested_fix":"In `turn`, when the last committed step's `next.overflow` is set, take the policy's answer only if it is `Shown::Messages` whose `messages_sha256` is `next.overflow.shown_sha256` (or at least whose bytes are no more than `shown_bytes`); otherwise end the run as `overflowed` returning `None` does. Then a run's overflows in a row are bounded by its bytes even with no `max_overflow_retries`. Or say in the documentation that the bound is `max_overflow_retries` alone and that `None` trusts the policy.","summary":"The turn after an overflow sends whatever the policy answers then: nothing holds it to the shorter history the overflow's ask answered, so the rule that each history is shorter is kept per ask and not per run","supersedes":"b9153bfa181aec4a3015792c364a499b0645747af8ed91207f86182431ab8385","tags":["review","verify-t4","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:18.490699Z","id":"7032eefba9f3323842509348277aa76166ce24c82b09c814c8d83b1432813f1e","body":{"detail":"Only under a policy that shows less (never under the default, whose fixtures in crates/clax-bot/tests/golden are unchanged): a `turn` step with `assistant: []`, `stop_reason: \"prompt_too_long\"`, zero usage and `next.overflow`; a turn after it with `payload.shown`; and a `bot.turn` audit record with that stop reason and zero tokens. It takes a step number, so it counts toward `max_steps`, and step 1's harness fee is charged on it as on any first step. `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:297-316) shows it as a turn with no text and no tool; the run view and the HTTP run JSON were not looked at. Whoever first ships a policy that shows less checks those readers and pins the shapes as fixtures.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2184},"end":{"line":2190},"content_hash":"cd4b34db76207885c770671ec43566b4c81e00010452727d08cb7b35988523b8"},"summary":"A refused request that goes on is committed as a turn with no answer, a shape no reader of steps has seen","supersedes":"fe8455bfacab11a0ae0b22dd4cb9cbc4cd481153f93367d0dae60985897adcb0","tags":["for-t8","compaction","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:18.746720Z","id":"348e73852929e55fd9c3932a47949760c959ffa4ddf033362aa86d386e5c94b1","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::may_not_park` refuses the waiting call with `park_limit` when the open step has parked `RunLimits::max_parks_per_step` times (`Parking::parks`, which `Store::park_run` counts) and with `parked_runs_limit` when the person has `RunLimits::max_parked_runs` runs parked (`Store::parked_runs_of`). The refusal is journaled by the run loop at the call's place, so the step run again is refused the same and the host's call stops waiting (the store ends a pending call's wait once its place is journaled); the host was already sent the call and may have asked its person, and its late answer is ignored. The count of a person's parked runs is read outside any transaction with the park: runs of one person that reach a waiting call in the same instant can each pass the check, so the bound can be exceeded by as many runs as the driver ticks at once for that person (`per_person_concurrency`, 1 by default). Neither bound is a setting of the instance yet: `RunLimits::of` gives 8 and 20.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":162},"content_hash":"965eebaf48f9357c6977c77df7dac6748016f92eec5617938984431270508d92"},"summary":"The two bounds on parking are read when a call would wait, and their refusal is journaled","supersedes":"bf712dc686e4f2330654e42fabe5018d6b395670b94ae35ad1689466bc34f129","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:19.005066Z","id":"aef2cf56ac65a8b214ac25dc5519089d67e0a9e45c5aa1dc409fc27abdec5ff1","body":{"detail":"(1) Keep the replay check first in the web session: a web call given its entry is answered from it before check 2 (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451; surface.rs row 130); crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs `a_web_refusal_is_answered_from_its_entry_when_the_host_gains_a_source` holds the adapter to it. (2) Pin the new `web_disabled` journal row, `{code, message}`, in a journal fixture for a run that did not declare the web and for one on a host with no source (surface.rs 132-144; record 0d124949). (3) Write the divergence check: `Stepper` (seam.rs 532-581) asserts on a diverged replay instead of answering `replay_diverged`, and `dispatch` only asserts in a debug build that the entry it is given is of its call; `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins today's audit of one. (4) Refuse a call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the calls are numbered (458-462 here; `StepCx::place`, `no_place`), which today's code does not. (5) Give the real provider's conformance fixture a `fail_once` and a `disturb`: the adapter's fixture has neither (seam.rs 1836), so the cases for a failing `begin` and `ending` are not played against `clax`, and its read is never disturbed. (6) Pass `CallPlace::attempt` to `OpCtx` (seam.rs `ClaxSession::op_ctx`, 232): the fake host now records the attempt it is given and the proof compares it, and the server's host reads it. (7) Join every session's `tool_result` fields before the result is trimmed (surface.rs 170-175). (8) Say where a surface's standing operations come from: `Binding::from_stored` takes a rule the stored binding does not hold, and the proof hard-codes `clax`'s (seam.rs 81-99; `declared`, crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs 253, is private). (9) The audit and usage of a script step are pinned for today's path only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs, scripts.rs): `replayed`, `unreported_calls`, the calls a `bot.script` record lists, the requests line; the proof builds none of them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":200},"end":{"line":299},"content_hash":"b596aa0d37ced766d27922094a6983b3c0d492e245038695d417b779c528a295"},"summary":"For the change that moves this file behind the seam: what crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs and its tests could not do without it","supersedes":"705d2a6bfdef693a15ea1a5433ea8d13b6752fba7193951616b1ebf4f1b65599","tags":["seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:19.271828Z","id":"9f2b2479ab44cb0e845c0b4b162da2390842169a71c5265e0ffe95f65917ec8c","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::call` hands a waiting answer (`Answer::is_waiting`) to `not_parked`, which throws the script `unavailable`, unjournaled. The step that parks: (1) implements `Host::ask` on `StepHost` and answers `HostReply::Waiting` here, recording the place, operation and arguments' hash of the call, and does not call `told` (a waiting call is no call of the step's `calls` yet); (2) when the sandbox's outcome is `ErrorKind::Parked`, drops the sessions without `finish`, as a lost claim does, commits nothing and parks the run on that place. What it can rely on: `dispatch` and `perform` (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs) return a waiting answer only for an operation tagged `@waits` whose call came with no entry, and have journaled nothing; the provider has already waited as long as the policy lets a script sit (`Delivery::park_after`, crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs) inside `StepSession::call`, so the core adds no wait and drops no future; the outside provider's broker opened the call in its store, with the place and under the claim, before the call was sent (`CallBroker::send`, crates/clax-bot/src/broker.rs), so an answer can already have arrived when the park is attempted, and the park must treat a place that is journaled by then as due at once. The step run again makes the call again: with the entry when the store journaled the answer, and with no entry otherwise, in which case the outside provider sends the same key, the broker answers the same call's ID, and a settled outcome comes back at once (`broker::tests::a_call_is_sent_with_its_key_and_deadline_and_its_checked_answer_comes_back`). `generic::a_call_that_waits_is_thrown_unavailable_until_the_step_parks` and `outside::a_call_that_waits_is_held_by_its_host_and_journaled_by_no_one` pin today's refusal and change with it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":262},"end":{"line":296},"content_hash":"92f90f485eff7b01c274a072f45a1367ffc774b409d90fab299766a14339b7f2"},"summary":"The one place the script step turns a waiting answer into `unavailable`: what the step that parks replaces, and what it can rely on","supersedes":"cb4e8d44597370c166f01dbd034f7e2d9615d3107ed57c9bc7febf1738ada9e4","tags":["for-3a-12","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:19.559230Z","id":"97e77755aa647986c52b62439588f1090b90311994957389fe9d8b911b8b3039","body":{"detail":"Each made alone, with `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:all` (464 tests pass with each). (1) script.rs 411, the sessions finished and joined in reverse order: every run has one surface, so nothing tells the order of two sessions' rows or which is blamed for a clash; a run with two bindings would. (2) script.rs 214-215, the size test of `tool_result_content` made on the text without the sessions' fields: the result can then pass `tool_result_bytes` by the bytes of those fields (`\"published\":{\"version\":2},`), and no fixture has a result that close to the limit after a publish (`script.logs-cut-to-fit.json` and the comparison's scenario of logs that do not fit are not within those bytes of it). (3) script.rs 327, a lost claim not checked before the sessions are finished: the commit is refused by the store all the same (`run_not_claimed`), and the tick waits `NotOurs`; no test loses a claim in a script. (4) clax/web.rs 534, a web call whose transaction failed answered `Answer::refused` (the run loop then journals the failure) instead of `Answer::unwritten`: no test makes `Store::spend_journaled` fail (record 79495cc8). (5) script.rs 148, a call past the step's places answered without being told: nothing drives a script to place 1,000,000. (6) surface.rs, `dispatch` journaling the core's own refusal again on a replayed call: `Store::record_bot_call` keeps the row that is there, so nothing changes. Of these only (2) and (4) can reach a person or a model with one surface: (2) as a result a few bytes over its bound, (4) as a failure answered again on every later attempt of a step. The other 66 changes made the same way each fail a test: 20 of the run loop's and 19 of the provider's that the earlier verification made on the code before the seam, 9 in run.rs, and 18 more on the generic step; two more in run.rs that pass are of a stop's audit records (record 5ed5547d).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":307},"end":{"line":353},"content_hash":"e4f7b2bbdc9c4ac4c51c429da8f5e4644dcbaa0efb2991722745d9fddef57f46"},"suggested_fix":"(2): a script that publishes and returns a value sized so that the result with `published` is one byte over the limit and without it under. (4): a test host or store hook that fails one `spend_journaled`, the step crashed and resumed, on whichever answer the owner chooses for 79495cc8. (1) and (5) belong to the change that gives a run a second binding and to the cap on `script.max_calls`.","summary":"Six changes to the generic script step and the seam that pass every test of clax-bot, and what each would take to hold","supersedes":"813737c3ead93c782337df20efec265dadf56d7054e3c10f288def0d3415e46d","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:19.819587Z","id":"7ee48c37ff2bc99e77e49f4e93f1756a28f47b32c67756e74bc2014969ce45be","body":{"detail":"Three tests make a divergence: crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/scripts.rs `a_replay_that_diverged_is_pinned` (258) and crates/clax-bot/tests/rules.rs 200-230 write a journal row for another call at place 0 by hand, and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs `the_audit_of_a_replay_that_diverged_after_a_longer_first_attempt_is_pinned` (273) runs another script for the first attempt (`Another`, 243) whose first call is `files.read` where the stored script's is `files.list`. A script's clock and random numbers are fixed for its step and its first call is made before any answer, so a real second attempt always makes the first attempt's first call: a real divergence is at place 1 or later, after calls that were answered from the journal. What that leaves unpinned: line 509 as `.filter(|_| false)` (the model is told `completed: []` whatever was replayed) passes all 189 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration`; so does no fixture hold a step whose audit has a `replayed` `bot.call`, a `replay_diverged` one and an unreported row together. The mechanics `audit.replay-diverged.json` pins (a `bot.call` with `attempt` and `seq` for each row the committing attempt did not reach, the first attempt's reply standing) are those of a real run; its `diverged_at: 0` is not. `completed` is what tells the model which calls of the script took effect before the replay stopped, and it is built from the run loop's own log of calls (506-511), which is rewritten when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":324},"end":{"line":332},"content_hash":"e391760fc9a5584d216200be123e59818b2d56d8862ff29f885cfc44500a5f2a"},"suggested_fix":"Give `Another`'s first-attempt script the stored script's first call (`files.list`) and let it differ from the second call on: the fixture then pins `completed: [\"files.list\"]` in the step's result, a `replayed` record, the diverged call and the unreported rows. Add the step's `result` to what the fixture pins.","summary":"Every diverged replay in the tests diverges at call 0, which one script cannot do: `replay.completed` is never pinned with a call in it, and no fixture has replayed, diverged and unreported calls in one step","supersedes":"fd9a7d74f9b2a6ec59bebd2e2fbfc0778792c84a5c92e9afd404ffeafdbd429c","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:20.036018Z","id":"fa1afdc8af5430edbc42327aedecf1208cc877fc596f9c09656061a09cae26a3","body":{"detail":"`StepHost::ask` emits one `call` event with the code `waiting` for the call an attempt parks on and does not add it to the attempt's log of calls (`told`). The log of a parked attempt is dropped with the attempt, so whether the waiting call was in it shows only in the run's live events, which no test of crates/clax-bot/tests/park.rs or tests/generic.rs subscribes to for a parked step. A change that called `told` for the waiting call before answering `HostReply::Waiting` (a second `call` event, with the same code) passed `//crates/clax-bot:all`. The event's shape is `Event::Call` as for any call (`op`, `target`, `ok: false`, `code: \"waiting\"`, `ms`); no new event kind was added, so the stream of a run of Clax's own surface, where no operation waits, is as it was.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":213},"end":{"line":298},"content_hash":"1293eaa3c1ceb5004661feebdb9cc0435ce3514b4f1ab6d93757c021be168fef"},"summary":"No test reads what a parked attempt tells of its calls: an attempt that also logged the waiting call as a call of the step passes every test","supersedes":"cb51a588911b8a31cbc7d290dab20e9496b038480937ab20602d4e755e033e79","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:20.247896Z","id":"7186133c2089ddf6229b1fc070eba14bf0ac69678cab055a9b7182d8b2ec76a5","body":{"detail":"`tool_result_content` cuts the text to `max` bytes (`RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`) at a character boundary and appends `… [truncated]`, so what the model is sent is up to `max + 15` bytes. It did so before the script step went through the seam (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs, the same lines), and crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/script.result-cut-to-fit.json pins it: `content_bytes` 102415 for a bound of 102400. Cutting to `max` less the marker would keep the bound and changes that golden. The path with logs keeps the bound (`script::tests::a_result_is_cut_to_its_bound_with_the_sessions_fields_in_it`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":340},"end":{"line":345},"content_hash":"86b5a5b98fa3360e64d19d5bdb570df4e400a172ae4666879ebf8cd1a65544c0"},"summary":"A result with no logs left to cut is 15 bytes over its bound: the text is cut to the bound and the marker is added after","supersedes":"7ff1e47fa30c79a7e4c1c556ac9f09d49a2cbbd1a82ce24d3e4bf4c83e8c6896","tags":["t5","fix-round","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:20.459490Z","id":"92f6ab4804ef9b8e7d1f084517b69935c20c93a8225effb21bd447259b7d320b","body":{"detail":"`OutsideSession::call` computes `deadline = now + wait_limit` on every attempt (crates/clax-bot/src/outside.rs:311) and answers `Answer::waiting_until(deadline)`; the store keeps the call's first `expires_at` (`open_call` returns `Opened::Same`). Re-parked after a wake with nothing answered, the run's `due_at` is the new deadline. Scratch test: parked at 10:00 with due_at = expires_at = next day 10:00; after 3600 s and `unpark_run`, re-parked with due_at next day 11:00:10 while the row still expires at 10:00. Without a hub pass that expires the row and wakes the run, the run sleeps up to one more wait limit. Fix: take the deadline from the pending row (the broker knows it) for `waiting_until`.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":288},"end":{"line":294},"content_hash":"03a1dc242a6b13acd3428a611f1dac2e05bb036535834eb35e03a115aa000fe3"},"summary":"A run that parks again on the same call takes the attempt's fresh deadline as its `due_at`, not the pending call's, so its due time moves past the call's expiry","supersedes":"c942d7666b333deab566a64e023e542dc8da8d50651cc6613acd894a688cf5a3","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:20.670607Z","id":"3bce73e83e8971ab2226540ea0dd8c9b144bfcd1d7654b81e08c850b8c4d4e18","body":{"detail":"Each change below, made alone to this file, passes all 175 tests of `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration` (the 7 of tests/seam.rs among them): (1) 649 as `if false {`: no `bot.call` record says `replayed`. (2) 685 removed: no `bot.call` record for a journal row the committing attempt did not reach (`unreported_calls`, run.rs 1533-1546). (3) 635 built from `st.calls` instead of `outcome.calls`: the `bot.script` record lists the calls that reached the host and not the sandbox's. (4) 705 as `0`: the `do.requests` line (run.rs 1080) counts no call. For contrast, 648 without the target fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned` and two more, 148 (`Event::Call` without its target) fails `golden::runs::a_thread_run_is_pinned`, 679 without the events fails `cancel::a_cancel_mid_script_charges_what_it_used_and_keeps_no_draft`, and 701 without the compute line fails five tests. The proof in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs builds no audit record and no usage (`Stepper`, seam.rs 471-517), and the resumed fixtures pin the step, the journal and the host's writes only (crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs 50-57). All four are the run loop's own part of a script step (crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs 151-156, 168-178), which is rewritten around `Answer` and `CallNote` when this file moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":563},"end":{"line":581},"content_hash":"8e2108caceedecce26166f0d7b74d4fd0b2b84bf1b5f40cc6af693beb2859e7a"},"suggested_fix":"Before this file moves: add the run's audit records and the step's usage rows to what crates/clax-bot/tests/golden/resumed.rs pins (a resumed step has replayed calls), pin the audit of a diverged replay whose first attempt went further than its second, pin a script with a call the sandbox refused (`script.error.call-limit.json`'s script) with its `bot.script` record, and assert the `do.requests` quantity of a script step with `infra_lines` on.","summary":"No test of clax-bot holds the `replayed` mark of a `bot.call` record, the `bot.call` records of calls no committed attempt reported, which list the `bot.script` record names, or the call count of the infra usage line","supersedes":"cbb0c726ae817bf886249afeea6e361c2b49ac2f3b121ce01446c02b487ef0c0","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:20.882680Z","id":"c0ad69f799c752473499dbc454ed7da3ce717e5f24abeae9191f5a120ed46e42","body":{"detail":"`Tick::script` gives `Tick::stop` the joined payload (`StepJoin::payload`) as `extra`, which `stop` lays over `reason` and `notice` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs). The `Merged` inside the `StepJoin` is dropped. `merge_fields` (surface.rs) is written for a surface's `ending` fields to join the object its session made in that same stopped step, which needs that `Merged`. When `ending` is called in `stop`, pass the `StepJoin` (or its payload half) instead of a value, and merge `reason` and `notice` as the core's. `stop` also lets `extra` overwrite `reason` or `notice`; the sessions' fields cannot be named so (`PAYLOAD_KEYS` has `reason`; `notice` is not in it, and a `clax` session answering `notice` would replace the stop's).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":599},"end":{"line":645},"content_hash":"c0e9fe8bf08268014cd6e67d1aa33e748814f222a8f2a50c9b15c0967195ea37"},"summary":"A cancelled script's sessions' fields reach the stopped step as one JSON value, so the record of which surface made each name ends there","supersedes":"1700fa7df6ecc36e89530f0457205f55b5795c7097f0a4018ee7dbcbb702e74d","tags":["t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:21.084058Z","id":"6044a8618ac1ac81d7a3dcf28ca947623d5beb02dff1c9fc69f0cc58c05f888f","body":{"detail":"`join.add` runs before the cancelled branch, so a provider whose parts clash fails the tick there (`Tick::clashed`) though the script was cancelled. Nothing of the attempt is committed. When the cancel is the run's own (`RunStatus::Stopping`), the next tick's `must_stop` stops it plainly (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs, `stop_plain`): the `stopped` step has `reason` and `notice` alone, without `title`, `code`, `outcome`, `calls` or any session's fields; the script's compute is not charged (never twice: the abandoned attempt charged nothing); and the step's journal rows get a `bot.call` record each from `unreported_calls`, with no `bot.script` record. When the cancel was only the tick's token, the next tick runs the script again from its journal. With `clax` alone no clash can happen: its session answers `published`, `replied`, `base`, `changed`, `created` and, to the model, `published`, none of which is in `PAYLOAD_KEYS` or `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS`, and it is the first and only surface joined. No test drives a clash in a cancelled step (crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs clashes in a step that commits).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":604},"end":{"line":622},"content_hash":"4fcafab5b4558497c570bad6ff4d0d60fc856067c5670f73b3b5f96e4a85d1c3"},"suggested_fix":"For the change that moves the endings: in a cancelled step, treat a clash as the `failed` step does (log it, leave that binding's fields out, stop with the rest), so that what the script used is charged and its calls are told.","summary":"A clash in a cancelled script's step abandons the stop: the run is stopped by the next tick with none of the script's fields, records or compute","supersedes":"1921358d98ef50a6e98245973f50e12dc17baaf5bb4fce37b1b31d004b479bea","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:21.268262Z","id":"2afe3987b8f4e82efb30d032865940227ed2f4bee03990e8ca128a198a9f7c45","body":{"detail":"`the_script_step_names_nothing_of_claxs_surface` fails on a comment that says \"thread\" or \"version\" and passes code that reaches Clax's surface by any other word (a helper in another module, a field named otherwise), as the list in crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs did for limits. crates/clax-bot/tests/generic.rs is the test of the meaning: with `ClaxProvider::begin` and `ClaxProvider::undeclared` changed to panic (in a scratch copy), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:integration --test_arg=generic::` passes its five tests, so no session of Clax's is opened and no call is answered by Clax's provider on that run's script steps. What of Clax's still runs there: `Tick::script_step` makes a `ClaxProvider` and its `RunInfo` for every script step before it takes the given surfaces (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1718-1722: four `Arc` clones, a clone of the run's row, the `clax` binding built and thrown away); and outside the script step the turn's prompt and request (`prompt::system_prompt`, `BotHost::context`), the roster (`BotHost::working`), `Tick::clax` (the tally) and every ending's notices on the run's threads.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":786},"end":{"line":872},"content_hash":"3c684592de87488dd6f0a5b423d0dcecd174765336e1198a135fa347e49d7758"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the word list; keep the three assertions on imports and `deps.host`/`deps.web` if a guard on the file's text is wanted, and let tests/generic.rs say the rest.","summary":"The test that script.rs names nothing of Clax's is a list of eight words searched in the file's text, comments included; what it means to hold is held by the run whose one surface is the fake's","supersedes":"aa0b8a0516626f5b2d66affd7c29279f3522f8d105f823ebac2c55fdbb8b1976","tags":["review","verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:21.461608Z","id":"6ddfcc6e5d51b79307d9e9e140a1be8a76c03e0721dabf400216fddf6c719b76","body":{"detail":"The check runs whenever `calls.diverged` is None, whatever `outcome.error` is. A replay that hits the CPU limit before the waited call (limits are wall- and load-sensitive) is told to the model as \"the script took another path when run again\", not as the limit it hit. Narrow it to scripts that returned or threw on their own, or keep the original error and add the divergence to it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":522},"end":{"line":546},"content_hash":"1067de24d579b1229abdae83c2ec7fc610a19db91513a4cea5eca34d226389ba"},"summary":"The unreached-wait divergence replaces whatever error the script ended with, including a CPU, memory or call limit, by `replay_diverged`","supersedes":"71e5c71f74ddd90c2cb18517e28a7b4a214c63407c88bcdd92ca89af137e1088","tags":["review","claxbot-park","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:21.668069Z","id":"6582136745e4e21e5d647aef0783a145b10df055fd41b1495cf4fd6f6738a778","body":{"detail":"`Tick::unreached_wait` looks at the rows of the step's journal that this attempt's script did not reach and makes the step end `replay_diverged` when one of them is at the place of a pending call marked `waits` (`Store::pending_call_at`): the answer a run parked for must not go unused in a step that commits as if nothing were wrong (`park::a_replay_that_ends_before_the_call_it_waited_for_diverges`). The general case is left as it was: a crashed attempt that made more calls than the attempt that commits leaves rows the commit does not reach, and the step commits cleanly, each such row told only as a `bot.call` record of a call no committed attempt reported (`Tick::unreported_calls`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs). The fixtures under crates/clax-bot/tests/golden named `resumed.*.json` pin steps of Clax's own runs executed again after a crash; the rule was not widened, so none of them was put at risk. Not covered either: a waited call with no pending row (the fake's `fake.ask`), whose answer is never journaled before the step is run again. Whether every unreached row should be a divergence is the owner's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":680},"end":{"line":702},"content_hash":"2c2b810155ab4a76791051885c95ac250574ad863518a3a6b8e9bb33aecc50b1"},"summary":"A script that ends before a journaled call diverges only when that call is one the run waited for; any other unreached row still commits cleanly","supersedes":"126cfc9b77739eb4e06ec5e768bc641cf257e5e5bafb9d303fdd037a2d900495","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:21.861359Z","id":"18756dbe487296e8afafe10830e0190d5f97c10a5b4d23bd3781c625f22bffa7","body":{"detail":"Between a park and its answer the audit journal says nothing of the run. Adding the two kinds touches `clax_core::audit` (the kinds and their fields), the toolpath export and its redaction (crates/clax-store/src/toolpath), ten pinned files under crates/clax-core/tests/toolpath, docs/contract.md, and, to be written with what they record, the store's own transactions: `Store::park_run`, `Store::answer_call`, `Store::expire_calls`, `Store::unpark_run` and `Store::cancel_run` each change a run's parking and none takes an audit context or a subject. A record written by the run loop beside `park_run` would not be in its transaction, and an unpark is made by whoever brings an answer, with no run loop there. So the store is where both records belong. What the store keeps meanwhile: `bot_runs.parked_at`, `parked_ms`, `waiting_on` (`surface`, `op`, `key`, `since`, `deadline`) and `bot_steps.parks` (`Store::run_parking`).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":710},"end":{"line":767},"content_hash":"f144187ddbb7b8ebd9222547d0c85a656b9480bc721fbf87de8f3537c604fa62"},"summary":"No audit record is written when a run parks or is resumed: `bot.park` and `bot.unpark` are not added","supersedes":"93d61a27c3c97bda6ec95c519293964b4a5bdbbb3b398b765b646dc4fc3ada79","tags":["status:deferred","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.044667Z","id":"dfc7ce648b2c7df5b3c53670d453c940217856651dcc5b10aceae8434d3ab8e8","body":{"detail":"Deleted in ebe3d108f test(claxbot): the script step's runs are held to their fixtures, and the step it replaced goes. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"344c09a61b87cbf930686b0c68ad22f5ae752f0df5832b459a84885ba2e44eea","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: The reference this alternative is about (cfg(test) script_reference.rs and the comparison in script_differential.rs) was deleted in ebe3d108f; the include of tests/common/mod.rs by path remains in crates/clax-bot/src/script_scenarios.rs:41-42, but there is no reference left to link into the library","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_scenarios.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.044805Z","id":"ab8985881adf1a13ecdd5853e8c59fea5a5d78c821f727713f6da8474d4a70f8","body":{"detail":"A row's `args_hash` by value (two homes mint different IDs, so it is compared as which rows share it, and by runs resumed across the two codes); what a script measured (`ms`, CPU, memory, the bytes of a payload) and the credits charged from it (compute and storage are priced at 0 for the comparison); a lost claim, a sandbox that cannot take the script, a store that fails a write, and a call past `NOTICE_SEQ`; and a `FieldClash`, which the reference cannot have. The scenarios run on the fake host: the server's host is compared with nothing.\n\nRe-recorded unchanged from record 31959bf0 on crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs, whose subject moved here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":334},"end":{"line":368},"content_hash":"4f609f355adb3fda3e4b5201afa7e9bcbc524cd1bb76c83f68cdc1a69a0b1156"},"summary":"What the comparison with the reference does not hold","tags":["t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_scenarios.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.044871Z","id":"4e3a00593fa27702870882f3ddca5df8665253ece9fd6252eed85737c05a83f1","body":{"detail":"Its script calls `clax.db.update(\"notes\", \"a\", {n: 3})` with three arguments. `db.update` takes four (`opts: IfVersion`, crates/clax-bot/src/clax.d.ts 155), so the call is refused `invalid_args` (\"clax.db.update: argument 4 (opts) is missing\"), nothing catches it, and the script ends there on the reference's path and on the seam's alike: the step's result is `{calls: [threads.read, db.get, db.set, db.update (invalid_args)], error: {kind: host_error}}` (read from the stored step of this scenario, printed by a test added to this file and removed again). `db.delete` is called with two arguments and would fail the same way. So what the name says is compared is not: with the reference changed to never refuse a second publish in one script (crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs 306 as `if false {`), `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test --test_arg=script_differential` passes all four comparisons. The same script with arguments that hold (`{if_version: doc.version}` for the update, the set's version for the delete), crashed before its commit and resumed, does leave the same on both paths and on the two mixed ones, with `second: [\"already_published\", …]`: run as a twenty-first scenario in a scratch copy of this file. The second publish is held for the product elsewhere (`golden::scripts::the_draft_operations_answers_are_pinned`, `seam::a_replayed_host_read_and_a_second_publish_behind_a_session`).\n\nRe-recorded unchanged from record 5141f40d on crates/clax-bot/src/script_differential.rs, whose subject moved here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1119},"end":{"line":1123},"content_hash":"ade7b24913adff1b70e97e3726ccb82c3bc9de80268aba538aa1f0da55a8fa46"},"suggested_fix":"Give `db.update` and `db.delete` their `opts`, and have each scenario say what its last script's result must hold (here `second[0] == \"already_published\"` and no `error`), so a script that ends early fails its scenario instead of comparing two equal failures.","summary":"The scenario named for host reads, document writes and a second publish ends at its fourth call: neither path ever reaches the document delete, the list, the publish, the second publish, the reply or the collections","tags":["review","verify-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.045272Z","id":"4eb691c3f0c6a23741636d2abdd4cfb1b335a96c3d40fb7aeebf12dcfcfb379a","body":{"detail":"Deleted in ebe3d108f test(claxbot): the script step's runs are held to their fixtures, and the step it replaced goes. Left open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"9be023649d8f6bb5cad74f087aaa6f01f76ed32c11bae95b6bdbbc387428612a","summary":"The code this record is about was deleted and nothing took its place: crates/clax-bot/src/script_reference.rs, script_reference/web.rs and script_differential.rs were deleted in ebe3d108f (Deps::reference with them); the record's own reply already says Done","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/standard.prompt.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.045675Z","id":"8606a722365aaeed886f874dd079518e6e2e2290ee361f654567728f461da863","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md (\"The prompt\") lists `{kind}`, `{declarations}`, `{surfaces}` (each *other* binding's rules) and `{core.…}`. It gives no slot for a namespace's rules, which \"The surface file\" says appear only in runs that have one of the namespace's operations. crates/clax-bot/src/prompt/file.rs adds `{rules.<namespace>}`, which a prompt file places by name; prompt.rs (`read`) refuses Clax's file when a ruled namespace has no slot. A surface with no prompt file cannot name its namespaces in the standard file, and its own const's rules have no slot either, so today such rules would reach no prompt. Also, slots `{core.x}` and `{rules.x}` share a namespace with a surface's own slots (`{clax.page_contract}`): a surface named `core` or `rules` would collide (file.rs:229-250 resolves own slots first). The step that registers a host's surface needs to settle: a `{rules}` slot for all of the owner's rules, or the owner rendered under `{surfaces}` too, and reserving `core` and `rules` as surface names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":7},"end":{"line":25},"content_hash":"235aedef8f01e55301a7dd315956ed1b27760083ea3d4d428d81f566130dff21"},"summary":"The standard prompt has no place for the rules of the surface that owns the kind, nor for its namespaces' rules, and the design's slot table names none","supersedes":"d85c7b72a248400637b754a1e59f1358b901713eb69f6f94038bdb906520c360","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.230821Z","id":"b3904aaee12776ddcaf22879c561b8738f97800565bcb3fa7c082998d6acc7ab","body":{"detail":"Read top to bottom as that change's author. (1) Row 3 (52): a call refused `no_place` is \"unjournaled\"; it does not say whether it is in the step's `calls`, the events and the `bot.call` records, and counted on the requests line, as a `replay_diverged` answer is (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 463-474 logs it). (2) `no_place`'s code is `call_limit` (973), the name of the sandbox's `ErrorKind::CallLimit`, but it is a `HostError` a script can catch, in other words (\"call N is past the 1000000 calls one script may make\") than the sandbox's uncatchable end (\"the script made more than its N host calls\", crates/clax-bot-js/src/lib.rs 1019): one name for two things the model can meet. (3) 138-140 still says a refused web call's row \"is never read by this code (the refusal is made again…)\", which row 130 now contradicts for the declared half: it is read, by `from_entry`. (4) Two rows of `Answer`'s table (793-796) claim a call answered from its entry: `of` (\"any call answered from its entry\") and `written` (\"a call answered from an entry the provider wrote\"). The journal of a replayed call is not read, so either works, but only `written` may carry `charged(n)`; say that the constructor of a replayed answer matters for the charge alone. (5) A `FieldClash` is counted as a failed commit (253-256): not said for the `failed` step itself, where an `ending`'s `Err` is logged and its fields left out (1503-1505), nor whether the other bindings' parts of that attempt are kept. (6) Nothing says which of a provider's answers the core turns into `Event::Call`'s `web` field (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 153): `CallNote::extra` has no named field. (7) `Binding::from_stored`'s rule for `clax` is given as \"`declared` in api.rs, which is private\" (341-343): on branch `claxbot-surfaces` it is `pub(crate)`. (8) Still open in their own records: where another surface's standing operations come from, and who resolves the request at the first turn.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":64},"end":{"line":75},"content_hash":"6231d4731cc01d22635f711285cada490dbaac146dfe7932f6f138819fef266a"},"summary":"What the module documentation leaves the change that moves script.rs behind the seam to ask","supersedes":"3e081cf41696fb0dff8c11c6913499d49acfb1e09c41494f8b401e1e6e4538e4","tags":["review","seam","for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.419451Z","id":"d52bcea5e714f95e6d37587b9325999a6a7fb8141e7ac9c34fcead0f8f8e0901","body":{"detail":"Row 3 has the core check the arguments \"against the surface's file\" and then find the binding (`route`). With one surface that is `api::check_args` (crates/clax-bot/src/api.rs:215), which reads Clax's own definition; with two, which file to check against is known only once the call is routed, so the order is route, check, `Binding::has`, session. `route` (267-275) answers `None` for a run with no `clax` binding calling an operation with no surface prefix (the module's own test: `route(only_fake, \"files.read\")` is `None`), and the table does not say what the script is answered or whether it is journaled; crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:497 `expect`s. And neither `SurfaceProvider` nor `Binding` leads to a `clax_surface::Definition`: `Binding::hash` is `None` for every stored binding of version 1, and `FakeSurfaceProvider::definition` is an associated function, not a trait method. A run with one fake surface and no `clax` needs the definition for the argument check, for the declaration the model reads and for the sandbox's operation list.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":70},"end":{"line":72},"content_hash":"94bd8511cac826a8622355fb4dde761368b798650c6384f299def3799605b0a6"},"suggested_fix":"Write the order as route, then check against that surface's file, then `has`; say a call no binding takes is answered `unknown_op` and journaled by the core as `Outcome`; and add where a definition comes from (a `definition()` method on the provider, or a registry by `Binding::hash`) before the tick holds providers.","summary":"The step table checks a call's arguments before it finds the binding, says nothing of a call no binding takes, and no part of the seam gives the core a surface's definition","supersedes":"82fe334e7dec7714c02652ffe6fe771c04b5a00afb5ca31c3ff867e94d244027","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.592771Z","id":"d2f7658b012a1db9a0e04497fb25419d159c82f2f0f3b4ea291780b03c18775d","body":{"detail":"(1) A failed `draft.rebase` is journaled as a failure (script.rs:393) and its entry has no `result.version`, so a replay performs the rebase again against the artifact's current version (241-252): it is not answered from the entry and may answer otherwise. (2) The tool result is trimmed to `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES` with `published` and `replay` already in it (498-530): the trim must run after the sessions' `tool_result` fields are joined, or a script with long logs that published changes bytes; no golden has that pair (script.logs-cut-to-fit.json does not publish). (3) There are two lists of calls: the model is told the sandbox's (`outcome.calls`, 491-495: calls that never reached the host are in it, `script_finished`, `invalid_args`, the call over `max_calls`), while the payload's `calls`, the `bot.call` records and the infra usage line count the host's log (646-663, 702-708). (4) After a divergence no session sees a later call, the step still commits what the sessions hold (615-621, 695-716), and the earlier attempt's rows past the divergence stay in the journal: a publish among them is in no session's parts. (5) The core reads `op`, `ok`, `attempt` and `seq` of every journal row, a provider's own included, for `unreported_calls` (run.rs:1533-1546). (6) A script is cancelled when its error is `Cancelled` or the token is set when it returns (665-669), so a script that returned may still end as `StepEnd::Cancelled`. (7) A stop decided after a turn or script step was begun (script.rs:673; run.rs:2094-2115) begins the same step again as `stopped`: `ending` gets an attempt one higher than the session had. (8) `payload.notice` is the text whenever the stop has one, and `payload.posted` is true whenever the rule held, whether or not a post succeeded (run.rs:1525-1527, 1562, 1683, 1701); the endings table says `null` \"when nothing was posted\". (9) `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:316-326) reads `changed`, `published` and `notice` from payloads: fields the provider returns, read by the core's event. (10) `working` is called before steps that then stop the run in the same tick: no key (run.rs:1659, 1669-1677), the turn's own stops (1838-1847, 1906-1909, 2094-2115), `request_gone` (1857-1873).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":211},"end":{"line":264},"content_hash":"03fcc26a6dd36da40b6a2ce1135727b9c4a6f050effb4f2d8e5ca2db1f7adb44"},"summary":"What crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs and run.rs do that the mapping tables leave out, each to be carried when the code moves behind the seam","supersedes":"90a9c39877e9bfe40d2e6771170d48e3e0251fd09d25740655e113919a95dc3f","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.793064Z","id":"658c8c56ad41ae96b136e951a09e6738ebc9beb781e6364324512d154981ef9e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:521-528 refuses `web_disabled` (`clax.<op> is not available to this run`) in two cases: the run did not declare the operation, or it did and `Deps::web` is `None` (`Some(s) if declared => s, _ => …`). Both journal `{code, message, cost_milli: 0, web}` alone, with no `bot.web` record (`Finish::undeclared`, 149-154, and `write`, 471-479). Behind the seam only the first is `SurfaceProvider::undeclared`'s: in the second the binding has the operation, so `perform` (surface.rs 568-586) hands the call to the session, which must refuse it itself, in the same words, uncharged, uncounted and unaudited, and journal it (`Journal::Written` with today's form, or `Journal::Outcome` with the core's). crates/clax-bot/tests/web.rs:698 (`a_host_without_a_source_refuses_web_disabled`) holds the script's code for that case and nothing of its journal row. The row above (98) says a session's web call is journaled `Written`, always, with a `bot.web` record in `spend_journaled`: this refusal is the exception.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":165},"end":{"line":166},"content_hash":"42ef63a3af7dff3456985e00ee6c79168132eb1211fbb0f4e5f5dac678d8f792"},"suggested_fix":"Say in row 99 that it is the undeclared half, and add the no-source half to row 98: refused by the session before check 3, journaled without a `bot.web` record. Whatever is decided for the undeclared row's form (record 0d124949b9e1c3281e2ad5bc7780698fd6123c0f2643dffc8930ab7cd341c32e), decide the same for this one, so one message does not leave two row forms.","summary":"The table's row for an undeclared web operation covers half of script/web.rs 515-528: a declared operation on a host with no web source takes the same branch and is the session's","supersedes":"26e4dbe046d9ee62861718b633c7302a32d2889c4ffddc50d707e5a29cdb279c","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:22.976521Z","id":"956311c05670b570a6c3408a1a9869d5160899178750d9cbef4f8b89e365abf8","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 449-451 answers any web call whose place is in the journal from its entry (`from_entry`), before `web_fresh` runs check 2 (512-529). For row 125 (the run did not declare the operation) making the refusal again is the same answer: `Run::web` does not change. For row 126 (declared, `Deps::web` is `None`) it is the same only while the host still has no source. A first attempt on a host with no source journals `web_disabled`; when the step is run again on a host that has one, today's code answers `web_disabled` from the entry, and a session written as row 126 says (check 2 made again, the entry not read) passes check 2 and runs checks 3 to 9, reaching the source for a fetch, before `Store::spend_journaled` (web.rs 497) finds the place taken. The adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs (421) cannot show this: it has no source on any attempt.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":165},"end":{"line":166},"content_hash":"42ef63a3af7dff3456985e00ee6c79168132eb1211fbb0f4e5f5dac678d8f792"},"suggested_fix":"Row 126, Replayed: answered from the entry, as every web call the session is given with one is (web.rs 449-451); `from_entry` reads the core's `{code, message}` row as it reads today's (305-306 default the two keys). Keep `if let Some(e) = replay` first when `script/web.rs` moves into the session.","summary":"The two `web_disabled` rows say a replayed call is answered again without its entry being read; today's code reads the entry first, and for the declared half the two differ when the host gains a web source between attempts","supersedes":"9e196b6392b39c663b446e33b951d4f1cb59da6e4f82283923cd16237fe74657","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:23.159662Z","id":"7abeda95fe4b14b48ae549cd848ae62973a576833e07d386d912ff2e6d88afc8","body":{"detail":"The constant in crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs became a policy value (crates/clax-bot/src/policy.rs:110, default `DEFAULT_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, the same 100 KiB). The change that moves the tool result's building behind the seam reads it from the tick's limits and corrects this sentence.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":232},"end":{"line":235},"content_hash":"f2c8378f4257c509c46f211aa8c3b0b7ed506e76ffe70058cd94ec789bf74ea5"},"summary":"This line names `MAX_TOOL_RESULT_BYTES`, which is gone: the size is `RunLimits::tool_result_bytes`","supersedes":"e0236ad44637140e869b5dbd5720922378bf5072502f00454e614820acf34468","tags":["for-t5","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:23.343775Z","id":"bee12374366cf110f2533cfb30fd2a81cf413247c273a369f8ba1dcc1ad41728","body":{"detail":"The split removed run.rs 533-653 (the `History` section) and added 12 lines further down, so what the tables call 1681-1719 (`finish`) is at 1572-1610, 1549-1590 (`stop`) at 1440-1481, 1451-1480 (`must_stop`) at 1342-1371, 1483-1504 (`what_i_did`) at 1374-1396, 1509-1529 (`notify_at`) at 1405-1420, and so on through the endings table (207-216) and wherever else the file cites run.rs. The two citations of the fold itself were corrected to name `clax::Tally` (surface.rs 123, 213). The plan's measurements (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md) and the open records on run.rs cite the old lines too (`qualifier review crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs` lists the drifted ones). The change that edits run.rs next for the endings will move them again; recite then, or cite by function name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":266},"end":{"line":293},"content_hash":"822ca1c873cadcbe13e7a3fc2cf4f987f0c896210bdcab210f3281500095a312"},"summary":"The module documentation cites run.rs by line, and every citation past run.rs 533 is now about 110 lines too high","supersedes":"a878f820543320de6f0dbeb2dc7182289e9686328aa9b9455c48a341465eeb23","tags":["t3","for-t7","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:23.514925Z","id":"08906f1a42b251324c498df8ed674ba026a792bdbc414745cdb5910cf02ef2c6","body":{"detail":"`CallPlace::seq` documents the places from `NOTICE_SEQ` as the terminal step's (418-421) and `StepCx::notice` refuses an index outside its binding's stride, but `StepCx::place(seq)` checks nothing. The setting `script.max_calls` accepts 1 to 1,000,000,000,000 (crates/clax-store/src/bot_settings.rs 80, 226-229). A cancelled script's step is the step its `stopped` notices are journaled under (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 673, run.rs 1558), so with a limit over 1,000,000 a script's call and a binding's notice can ask for one place of one step, and `unreported_calls` (run.rs 1539) leaves out every script call from that place up. The hazard is today's (`NOTICE_SEQ`, crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs 127) and the strides do not widen it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":900},"end":{"line":913},"content_hash":"b4d6915708e80c2cf5116b22fd9bc3be90def108afdb8396533b3915be660239"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse a script's call whose place would reach `NOTICE_SEQ` where the core numbers the calls (or cap `script.max_calls` under it), and have `StepCx::place` answer `None` from `NOTICE_SEQ` up as `notice` does outside its range.","summary":"Nothing but documentation keeps a script's place under `NOTICE_SEQ`: `StepCx::place` takes any `seq`, and `script.max_calls` may be set far above 1,000,000","supersedes":"5e75a26c92c73542d8ec1eb9e5e965bb2d404495fcb965083e06b73e05603062","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:23.702200Z","id":"edd824549725869230a30e3b78f1ff2a3fb1230b5be5b1ead9dd734688a873c7","body":{"detail":"Not taken: a provider with no store (FakeSurfaceProvider, and the wire adapter of the design's \"Providers\": \"its answer is journaled when it arrives\") would have to be handed one, and three of today's four behaviours already leave the entry to the run loop (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:393, 411, 399-401, 422-424). So an answer says who journals it (Journal), and only a write journaled in its own transaction or with its charge is the provider's (Written).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":955},"end":{"line":977},"content_hash":"eba833c93b41b03e36157d6a38cd353cbd21000c27e80540161a46a4ef1afc90"},"summary":"Every provider journals each of its calls itself through the claim, and the core journals only its own refusals","supersedes":"9538cc148e40b7dd0ec2496dbc07f9da7f12f15323b7750e63748abba433e622","tags":["seam","revisit:a second in-process provider needs a journal entry of its own form for a call that is not a write","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:23.879733Z","id":"c33b802ee352367d85058c8e840fd07a7ff29dc1f735ea57170593fd9f2762ca","body":{"detail":"They are the three facts `Event::Call` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs:84-93), a step's `calls` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:654-663) and the `bot.call` record (script.rs:648) take from a performer today, and docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-11-claxbot-surfaces-step2.md:42 lists them as ambiguous. Kept as they are so that no stored or streamed byte moves; a general form belongs to the change that moves the events.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":988},"end":{"line":997},"content_hash":"369c8025344c2f85c272a621782952d084389d5211bfce073e7ec02019be6aa9"},"summary":"`CallNote` holds what `clax`'s operations say of a call (`target`, `cost_milli`, `web`) in a type of the core","supersedes":"88d6fc25aa2b22424cb56bdf4bee779b1242fac4ec5ebe9cc89711b4c45859ff","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:24.055231Z","id":"e76130370922201153c3504c516f8025ddbc94231d8274a9b5c009d8d2e9f28d","body":{"detail":"(1) `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` (1289-1298) is told to answer with `Answer::refused`, but `perform` (968-986) journals its answer as the answer says: one built with `Answer::written` leaves the refusal unjournaled, with no place for the provider to have written it (the hook is given no place and no claim). (2) `charged(n)` with `n` over 0 on an answer the core journals (`of`, `outcome`, `refused`): the core's row keeps no cost, so the attempt that replays the call shows none, and nothing ties the charge to the row. Today a charge is always journaled with its row (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs 491-497); only `charged(0)` goes with a core row (the two `web_disabled` refusals). (3) `Answer::of(Err(e))`, `Answer::outcome(Err(e))` and `Answer::refused(e)` journal the same row (`Claim::record`, 507-531): three spellings of one thing. None is reachable from the adapter in crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1045},"end":{"line":1235},"content_hash":"4a9eb8f2862a240b4ea77930ccdf020d9a4d4f1933e3a043cccbc53ef866a71f"},"suggested_fix":"Have `perform` journal an `undeclared` answer as an outcome whatever its constructor says (or have the hook answer a `HostError` and a note, not an `Answer`); say on `charged` that a call that charged more than 0 is journaled by the provider with its charge, and assert it in `Claim::record`.","summary":"`Answer`'s constructors leave three combinations a provider can build and the core does not catch","supersedes":"014e16a2dd786801ca363b3ff551c137bb9d1b45c8f32e02ca917aa511f481d1","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:24.246589Z","id":"4c8e4b86e50e2128165f92d8200aae68f4a633990abeb329fe21e9ee522498f6","body":{"detail":"Clax's web operations write their journal row, their charge and their `bot.web` record in one transaction (`Store::spend_journaled`). When the entry cannot be made or the transaction fails, nothing was written; the code before the seam answered the script the failure and journaled nothing, so the step run again makes the call again. The seam had no answer for that: `refused` makes the core journal the failure, and `written` said the place has its row. `Answer::unwritten(e)` says it (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/web.rs, `unwritten`; the module documentation, \"Who journals a call\"). The other choice: answer `Answer::refused`, which journals the failure, so a store that failed once answers that call the same failure on every later attempt of the step instead of trying again; one constructor fewer, and a change in what a resumed run does after a failed write. No test reaches either (mutating one to the other passes every test).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1143},"end":{"line":1144},"content_hash":"6a1d163bf5d2ef9bd84c336fa4d8564736fe9ff19283aa701fd6e2f5c7274b13"},"summary":"A failure that neither side journals is now a constructor of the seam, `Answer::unwritten`, for the owner to keep or drop","supersedes":"3c2d69c93d6a00d036f042a76c8ca1964489fe58c0a406dc7e02d8d3ebd7d0bc","tags":["t5","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:24.466202Z","id":"d288cfba27c51c98ded343bcc0af894fd92e6b986913a380016931bf1f599fea","body":{"detail":"`merge_fields` puts another surface's fields under the surface's name and answers `FieldClash` when that name is one of `PAYLOAD_KEYS` (`calls`, `code`, `error`, `outcome`, `reason`, `result`, `summary`, `title`, `tool_use_id`) or is taken. `clax_surface::Reserved::of` (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42) refuses a host's surface only the names `clax`, `runs`, `content` and the namespaces of Clax's own file (`files`, `draft`, `threads`, …). So a surface named `result` or `calls` parses, binds, and then fails each of its steps. A surface named as one of `clax`'s own payload fields (`changed`, `published`, `replied`, `created`, `base`, `notice`, `posted`, `sources`) clashes only in a run that also has a `clax` binding; in a run without one its object lands where `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs 316-326) and `History::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 598-644) read `clax`'s fields. The module documentation (surface.rs 236-238) says a clash \"fails the tick\" and not whether the failure is counted against the step (`Store::note_commit_failure`): uncounted, the step is begun again without end.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1572},"end":{"line":1818},"content_hash":"0d298c7c4ea723001cebaf43cb0cabad5867a0767a8f829f82994a891b4f153d"},"suggested_fix":"Reserve `PAYLOAD_KEYS`, `TOOL_RESULT_KEYS` and `clax`'s payload field names as surface names where a host's definition is parsed, so the clash cannot be reached by a name; and say that a `FieldClash` counts as a failed commit of the step does.","summary":"A host surface named as a field of a step's payload fails every step it adds fields to, at run time; nothing refuses the name when the surface is defined, and what a `FieldClash` does to the step is not said","supersedes":"68d20dccece26629ca6a7ee81f4d3a4a696171f3827876dc210dc592d20bdd10","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:24.705823Z","id":"8c2f4134ed22b4a3f6f52b1ab9d3b8ec575468f614370845ddf75bf717e2bc06","body":{"detail":"The arm at 1233-1238 takes any object at `into[surface]` for the surface's own. `clax` puts `published` at the top of a script step's payload as `{version, url?}` (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs 697) and of what the model is told (502-504). With `into = {\"published\": {\"version\": 2}}`, `merge_fields(&mut into, &PAYLOAD_KEYS, \"published\", {\"x\": 1})` answers `Ok` and leaves `{\"published\": {\"version\": 2, \"x\": 1}}`: a unit test asserting a `FieldClash` there, added to this file's tests and run with `bazel test //crates/clax-bot:clax_bot_lib_test --test_arg=probe_`, failed with `merged into clax's field: {\"published\":{\"version\":2,\"x\":1}}` (the test was removed again). The documentation says otherwise in three places: 258-261 (\"one of `clax`'s field names, clashes on every step\"), 1188-1190 and 1193-1195 (\"taken there by something that is not this surface's object\"). Merged in the other order (the host surface first, `clax` after) it clashes, so the result depends on the order the bindings are merged in, and the kind's surface is first. Before commit c0b76016f every name taken at the top clashed. `published` is the one field of `clax`'s that is an object; `History::of` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 601) and `step_summary` (crates/clax-bot/src/events.rs 321) read it. Nothing refuses the surface name yet (crates/clax-surface/src/check.rs 20-42).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1706},"end":{"line":1718},"content_hash":"764fa766d0e6a49e508d20f366485b2d48a7d04f094bb53958da457a79970b4c"},"suggested_fix":"Tell the surface's own object from another's without guessing: have `merge_fields` refuse a surface whose name is one of `clax`'s payload or tool-result field names (a constant beside `PAYLOAD_KEYS`), or keep which top-level names each surface's merge made. Add the case above to `a_field_that_takes_a_name_of_the_cores_or_one_taken_is_the_providers_error`.","summary":"`merge_fields` adds a host surface's fields to whatever object is under the surface's name, and `clax`'s own `published` is an object: a surface named `published` is merged into it and not refused","supersedes":"4bba8ddd91c12a845fcef7887f62cc5cfba0cc927b8e7cbc29b01effdbbf99d7","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:24.995864Z","id":"7e6242c9c7afa87616809bce299520cd86c04f0d5b41abcfced205f13b68375b","body":{"detail":"`StepParts::payload` fields are flat names because today's are (`changed`, `replied`, `published`, `created`, `base`: crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:695-699, pinned by tests/golden/steps.thread.json), so two sessions answering one name would overwrite each other silently. `StepParts::draft` is the store's `DraftChange` (crates/clax-store/src/bot_runs.rs), a row of `clax` in a core type, because `StepCommit` commits no other kind of row for a provider. Whoever first runs two sessions in one step decides whether a clash is refused or a host's fields are kept under its surface's name; a second kind of row needs the store.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1834},"end":{"line":1856},"content_hash":"44c7b7864cc3432d3a6f0beab0e83a5cde66e17715464de92eea728b5c25c2b3"},"summary":"The parts of several sessions go into one payload and one list of rows with nothing to tell the bindings apart","supersedes":"7bec5bda26c3271a11be0b277826ce9602cb21bd58584221a502a1e9209c7c9c","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:25.278297Z","id":"e0ce345d7e693f25f4cffb0980f4c4e6ada80e4f092fcda0ff5d6b254c1b07b1","body":{"detail":"Today's sessions depend on both. The sandbox sends calls one at a time, in the script's order (crates/clax-bot-sandbox/src/lib.rs:44-45), and `draft.rebase` relies on it: it clones the draft, awaits, and assigns the clone back (crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:254-260), which loses a write made in between. A cancelled script, or a driver that lost its claim, drops the sandbox's future (`watched`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1090-1122) and with it a call at any await, after which `finish(Cancelled)` is called on the same session, or the session is dropped. Neither is in the trait's contract, so an adapter written against it may hold state across an await that a drop leaves half-changed. Separately, \"Not here\" (135-141) puts a call that waits as \"a third answer beside a value and a failure\": `Answer::result` is `Result<Value, HostError>` and `Answer`, `CallNote` are built by struct literal outside the crate (crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs:110-121), so adding it changes every provider that builds or reads one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1875},"end":{"line":1934},"content_hash":"85b7e96fa8893f7af6594348861579e81c5620cd8a7ac3715c601ec427a13a08"},"suggested_fix":"State in `StepSession`'s documentation: calls of a step are made one at a time across all of its sessions, in place order; a call's future may be dropped at any await, and the session must then be as it was before the call or as after it. Make `Answer` and `CallNote` buildable only through constructors (`Answer::of`, `refused`, `journaled`, `naming`, and two for the cost and the shown value), so a waiting answer is a new constructor and not a new shape.","summary":"`StepSession::call` does not say that calls never overlap or that a call's future may be dropped, and `Answer` is a struct of public fields that a third outcome would change for every provider","supersedes":"89a036d325ac22b991c8633ad5c8fefc7869319b90c99965fc5760ff73ccea9b","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:25.516999Z","id":"087f42b8cb640cf9348a3bdcd0f9a14d281e7e4169ff18a35601ed66fbe7313e","body":{"detail":"The design's correction names only the limit's value. Today's `credits_exhausted` notice also says when the week's credits reset, from the tick's clock (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1416-1429, Deps::clock), which no provider is given; a provider's own clock can be in the next week when the tick's was not. So the core passes the instant (`credits_reset`) and the provider words it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":1954},"end":{"line":1978},"content_hash":"3c7a0a47247c4ae2538d7d7119f1cdd442c3e3abcf55435d462b14aabc1178bc"},"summary":"A provider works out when credits reset from a clock of its own, and `End::Stopped` carries the reason and the limit alone","supersedes":"40f9b073276f803c0cd339204add7b8d7f2a35e00b9ddf50848bddf0cc82841e","tags":["seam","revisit:providers are given the run loop's clock","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:25.720115Z","id":"f9c4633750c7809a206f18a645675fa7cd4c99a3a7aea7fafe1803b9887d53f7","body":{"detail":"`BotHost::context` (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:265) is read at the first turn (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1855-1879), and the design's correction says the kind's surface resolves the stored request there. `SurfaceProvider` holds only what a step and a run's end need; a run with no `clax` binding cannot be given a prompt through it. The change that makes the tick hold `RunInfo` and its providers adds it, with the prompt file.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2029},"end":{"line":2037},"content_hash":"9e2dd32f7c9022e3bc7617d3644202dc7b39504edf8f572b4b817940669bf021"},"summary":"The seam has no way for the surface that declared a run's kind to resolve the request at the first turn","supersedes":"c40a8d904e05ee0405351a95e18a7bd0ead214007b6ac8aa764328b667162b46","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:25.912435Z","id":"86e96f3bb8a89b4219fc78bca7b0cd804e866d8e4b912d41b05d28c516a02277","body":{"detail":"Today the web performer refuses it and journals the row itself (crates/clax-bot/src/script/web.rs:515-528, entry_result 276-293). Behind the seam the run's binding does not have the operation (the design's \"A run\": \"An operation outside it is not declared and is refused when called\"), so `perform` asks `SurfaceProvider::undeclared` and the core journals the standard form. What does not change: the script's error (`web_disabled`, the same words, pinned by tests/golden/script.web-refusals.json), the step's `calls` entry with `cost_milli: 0` and `web` (from the answer's note), and what a replay reads (`from_entry`, web.rs:305-306, defaults both to the same values). crates/clax-bot/tests/seam.rs, `refusals_behind_a_session_are_journaled_once_and_answered_again`, holds all four. The other choice keeps the row byte for byte: every `clax` binding lists all 24 operations and the session refuses, which makes the web grant something other than the binding's set. The owner's to choose before script.rs moves behind the seam.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2064},"end":{"line":2074},"content_hash":"e0f4d9b1c266b529067f182fb962771523523d913caed844b4f308a0bebe725a"},"summary":"Once the core refuses a web operation a run did not declare, its journal row is `{code, message}`: today's also holds `cost_milli: 0` and `web`","supersedes":"964e0fd9a32c78196a8ddcd394abb50f25fd72931e3b1e3e2ee7131cf6e469eb","tags":["seam","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:26.124484Z","id":"a124986dc4515d4d4c7dd02139af6d0730d36de158ffb9f404287a1605127c99","body":{"detail":"The design's correction (\"What measuring the run loop changed\", first row) names two hooks, `ending` and `ended`. Today ClaxBot is shown as working before each turn and each script on every tick (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1659), which is neither, and `BotHost::working` is to be deleted. Not taken: a session for a turn would load the draft (Draft::load, crates/clax-bot/src/script.rs:554-555) for a step that makes no call, and a turn would fail when the draft's base version is gone. So the seam has a third hook, which answers nothing.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":2148},"end":{"line":2157},"content_hash":"7e156d157fe54b80fa60724f29b01d13cff487cac04ac15ecbdcedc1f9d2e276"},"summary":"No `working` hook: a session opened for every step, turns included, with the roster shown from `begin`","supersedes":"6b08ec296625f2b7d743472293260034d1342ccf85920d30047447e1f7983aea","tags":["seam","revisit:a parked run's roster must say what it waits on","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:26.302946Z","id":"ada24033544ca82a5f442d53a9db692c2d22e84ea08c12ca3bbc54befbe8e605","body":{"detail":"Not taken: what fails inside today's terminal step and must keep it open is a store read (`Store::web_pages`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1684), which fails the tick with a `CoreError`; a notice that cannot be posted is logged and the step commits (run.rs:1525-1527). So `ending` and `begin` answer `clax_core::Result`, and a provider logs a write it could not make. `ended` answers `Result<(), HostError>` as drawn, since the run has ended whatever it says.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":2177},"end":{"line":2219},"content_hash":"f09ba935815b7a0ffc37a94769c8c5bf9be1427dd22e2001ee835c3328927ab6"},"summary":"`ending` failing with a `HostError`, as the design's sketch of the trait has it","supersedes":"b2fee1f0a5b12399f386fcf2ca1d105b17f5494ea330e9f1ad0045b566c978f8","tags":["seam","revisit:a provider outside the process is given a part in the terminal step","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:26.536878Z","id":"15aadcdd4712b52d5a73b96904d69d16f47afa392da2f20e43c2d6d1c122811b","body":{"detail":"The places from NOTICE_SEQ (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:127) are a step's, not a binding's: today thread `i`'s notice is at `NOTICE_SEQ + i` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1514-1516), and a sources list at `NOTICE_SEQ + 1 + i` (run.rs:1694). With one binding that writes there (`clax`) nothing collides. The change that first gives a run a second provider whose `ending` journals must give each binding a range of its own.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2177},"end":{"line":2219},"content_hash":"f09ba935815b7a0ffc37a94769c8c5bf9be1427dd22e2001ee835c3328927ab6"},"summary":"Two bindings whose `ending` both write would take the same journal places","supersedes":"7ae1abb9c79e92624db665756cc1fd4f714c52e8e8aaec99ea5f43f051f67177","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:26.750393Z","id":"03dce11a0c937196e9a76cd6c347c48b2487ffd39e0436508e69a84b2447a2db","body":{"detail":"The contract is: an error fails the tick and the step is begun again. Today the one such error is `Store::web_pages` in `finish` (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:1684), and a tick that fails that way is not counted (`note_commit_failure`, run.rs:1339, counts commit failures only), so today's run also retries without end; behind the seam the same holds for any provider's error, including one from `ending(End::Failed)`, which is the path a run takes when nothing else works (`fail`, run.rs:1606-1631, logs a notice that cannot be posted and commits). With two bindings, the first's `Err` may or may not stop the second's `ending` from being called in that attempt; each is safe to call again (its writes are journaled), but a binding that always fails then keeps every other binding's `ended` from ever coming.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2193},"end":{"line":2213},"content_hash":"bea50bf39f70fb5c40cdf35cbd04efeda3d8f423dfa4bd0e2eb92574ec0e737b"},"suggested_fix":"Fix the contract before the endings move: every binding's `ending` is called in each attempt, the kind's surface first; an `Err` is counted against the step as a failed commit is, and at the limit the run ends `Failed`; `ending(End::Failed)`'s `Err` is logged and the step commits.","summary":"`ending`'s `Err` keeps the terminal step open with no bound, also for `End::Failed`, and with several bindings nothing says whether the others are still called","supersedes":"750264730300120087ed2b176f6033e541597a035a67d87dbbfb477738a3112c","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:26.985286Z","id":"0959910ae54aa6d0a1075f8c9c36959a78bd2a9d58f910ba37b7a0e602a785b3","body":{"detail":"Today a starting driver makes up for the lost `working(false)` by starting the follow-ups that were waiting (`BotHost::start_waiting`, crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:301-307; crates/clax-bot/src/driver.rs:219-235). The seam has no hook for that, and the design's \"recorded with the run and delivered until the provider acknowledges it, for seven days\" (\"Providers\") is not built. The change that puts the driver's start behind the provider needs one or the other.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2236},"end":{"line":2271},"content_hash":"c9f9f04de70901313e41c298027a5f592c7777fd86a37aca52e776095284c33e"},"summary":"Nothing in the seam delivers `ended` again when a crash falls between the terminal commit and the call","supersedes":"3932a279e840d0a976a806d10b61fc0ad3c51b21ab3400541c54f555f7e345e9","tags":["seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:27.286388Z","id":"f7d0e93431fd09569ec1eeaabef049cd6034decb944f12081901ebdab8346242","body":{"detail":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` (1629-1635) fails a repeat whose `End` is not equal to the first's. A repeat made by the tick that committed has the value in hand; one made after a restart (the case the contract names: a crash between the commit and the call) has only the run's row and the terminal step. `Run::reason` and the payload's `reason`, `code`, `summary` give `End::Finished`, `GaveUp` and `Failed` back, but a `run_cap` stop's `limit` is only inside the notice's text (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1431-1436, 1562) and a `credits_exhausted` stop's reset time is nowhere (1416-1420 computes it from the clock when the notice is written). On the provider's side, \"what the provider did for the run's end the first time, it does not do twice\" has no bound: a provider that is not idempotent by nature must keep every run it was told of for ever.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2236},"end":{"line":2271},"content_hash":"c9f9f04de70901313e41c298027a5f592c7777fd86a37aca52e776095284c33e"},"suggested_fix":"When the endings move: either keep the `End` (and the kind's result) in the terminal step's payload so a redelivery reads it back, or say on the trait that a repeat carries the same status and reason and may lack `limit` and `credits_reset`, and make `lifecycle` compare those. Say on the trait that a provider's `ended` must be idempotent without a record of its own, or how long the run loop may redeliver.","summary":"`ended` is to be told again \"with the same end\", but nothing the store keeps of an ended run can rebuild `End::Stopped`'s `limit` and `credits_reset`, and the contract does not say for how long a provider must remember an end it acted on","supersedes":"a329e202fd42f0cd075593b937414a76078acb0b6d95342866c04c447115f42c","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:27.492866Z","id":"eb15b112be74633d6b06690d61629b8764bf93bb031de70dcca74b20d8b1a723","body":{"detail":"`Binding::from_stored` builds a run's operations from the surface's file, the stored grant and this rule each time a script step is run (crates/clax-bot/src/clax/provider.rs 102-116, called from `Tick::script_step`, crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs 1718-1745). The rule has no kind: a surface whose kinds have different operations (the fake's file gives its kind an `@allow` list, crates/clax-bot/src/fake.d.ts) cannot say so. And the rule is read from the running build, not from what the run was bound with: a binding keeps the hash of its surface's definition (`Binding::hash`) so that a run keeps the surface it was made with, but a build whose provider answers `standing` otherwise gives a run made before it other operations on its next step. For `clax` both are harmless today (one rule, in `api::declared`). For the change that reads `bindings_json`: either store a binding's whole set of operations when the run is made (then nothing asks the rule again, and `standing` goes), or take the rule from the kind's `@allow` list in the definition the binding's hash names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2045},"end":{"line":2052},"content_hash":"1cbf1004aead22a54a08156e5af92245b149741e90f805f06f1793aa4d050399"},"summary":"`SurfaceProvider::standing` is asked of the provider's code, per operation and for every run alike: it cannot say what differs by kind, and a provider that changes it changes what runs already made may call","supersedes":"06a96d62b78e3f5c3290bde0529f5f562f840acbb3f318a51b6cce4573e8de00","tags":["review","verify-t5","for-t8","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:27.712523Z","id":"b9bcdb53423b95e4d929443c468dbb07a132a1197aa3b8332dcd01f9d51a12f7","body":{"detail":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-10-claxbot-surfaces-design.md:333-335 (\"delivered until the provider acknowledges it, for seven days\") and 467-468 (\"`ended` is then delivered again when it next ends\") make `ended` at least once. Today's `BotHost::working(run, false)` is at least once too: `start_waiting` (crates/clax-bot/src/host.rs:301-307) makes up for a lost call at driver start, and the server starts follow-ups from both (crates/clax-server/src/bot.rs:1030-1066). `lifecycle` (1016-1018) fails on a second `ended`, and crates/clax-bot/src/provider_conformance.rs:482-488 calls `ended` once, so a provider whose `ended` is not safe to repeat passes, and a run loop that redelivers fails the fake.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2574},"end":{"line":2640},"content_hash":"1805085475b76b8f2dff5a0dbb28ec28527d139ae097a82859c6f18fa5f9f2fa"},"suggested_fix":"Have the suite call `ended` twice with the same end and require the second to succeed and change nothing the first did not; have `lifecycle` accept repeats after the terminal commit with the same end.","summary":"`FakeSurfaceProvider::lifecycle` and the conformance suite hold `ended` to exactly once; the design delivers it until acknowledged, and today's roster-off is already repeated","supersedes":"656c85a7594652b34817eb7d480c115a55cc1939762e660debfdcc100f562d88","tags":["review","seam","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/surface.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:57:27.914429Z","id":"ed567593b22986c610132ced69d20c80bc50c88b1f7feb5c60825768955bb91c","body":{"detail":"`SurfaceProvider::for_run(&Run)` exists so `clax` reads its own columns (artifact, threads, request kind, base version, agent grant, web) from the row the tick read instead of reading it again; a provider outside the process ignores it. It goes when those columns leave `Run` for a row of `clax`'s own, or when `clax` reads them from its binding's scope and the request. `SurfaceProvider::audit_subject` answers `clax_core::audit::AuditIds` (artifact, thread, session, question, call, origin): a host's surface can name none of its own objects, so a run with no `clax` binding is filed on no object and found by its actor's `on_behalf_of`, which is what the design's \"filed on the person\" is here (`generic::a_runs_audit_records_are_filed_on_the_subject_its_surface_names`). `Store::commit_bot_step_on` takes the subject; `bot.run_start` (`Store::create_run`), `bot.cancel` (`Store::cancel_run`) and the web calls' records (crates/clax-store/src/bot_web.rs) are still filed by the store from the row's artifact.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2101},"end":{"line":2101},"content_hash":"cdee8a744ffbf705e7796d4cbe1e70e098bd058322b910a0200e08178bf14436"},"summary":"Two of the seam's new hooks carry Clax's shapes: `for_run` is handed the store's `Run` row, and `audit_subject` answers `AuditIds`, which has places only for Clax's objects","supersedes":"97edc3e416f19ef543b791616af6d9ad1c3954a53cf68e4a3624f831b2fcaa04","tags":["t8","for-3a-13","for-3a-14","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-bot/src/ending_differential.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:58:24.157984Z","id":"d3d43a8e73355933aa259615e5de62235d4a198e2513528cf11398cc58162fef","body":{"detail":"The new record is 9fad3a35709d5c10c998ccad5fe7f562faf4422f39e5a79535d76165016a7717. This one stays open for the owner to close.","kind":"comment","references":"5bd35594163fc30711fe82064adef60d107c767d0f34e5276ff5fee3d49f6435","summary":"Moved: what this record is about is now async fn run(scenario: Scenario, cut: Cut) -> Left at crates/clax-bot/src/ending_scenarios.rs:536-536, re-recorded there unchanged as record 9fad3a35","tags":["session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
